KANT_TR['reflm/guyer'] = {"reflm:3489":["expresses tautological propositions (*propositiones tavtologicas*).",[]],"reflm:3490":["Tedious pastime is *contradictio in adiecto*.\nEntertaining pastime is tautology.",[]],"reflm:3491":["visible darkness",[]],"reflm:3492":["Over and \n*pia fraus*",[]],"reflm:3493":["In the margin at right \nIllusory contradictions in the government of the world. In the mysteries. |\nIn the riddles. In witty ideas. Which are resolved. A book would be shorter etc. etc. In the writing of a great man the contradictions are held to be apparent.",[]],"reflm:3494":["*identitas et contradictio explicita et implicita*.\nOn M § 13 »patens«:\n*indemonstrata*.\nOn M § 13 »latens«:\n*demonstrata*.",[]],"reflm:3495":["*pia fraus*.",[]],"reflm:3496":["*latens* is *pia fraus*.",[]],"reflm:3497":["Also tautology.",[]],"reflm:3498":["A ground (*ratio*) is something other than the grounded (*rationatum*).\nNothing contains a ground in itself.\nPrior, posterior. *Antecedens* and *consequens*.",[]],"reflm:3499":["Logical *oppositio*, real.",[]],"reflm:3500":["In the margin at right \nThe real ground of actuality is the efficient cause.",[]],"reflm:3501":["Connected (*connexa*) are those of which, one being posited, another is posited.",[]],"reflm:3502":["That, which not having been posited, another is not posited, is a condition (*conditio*).",[]],"reflm:3503":["The grounded having been posited, a ground is not posited, but is required.",[]],"reflm:3504":["ground (*ratio*) either analytic (logical) or synthetic (real ground).",[]],"reflm:3505":["That, which having been posited, another is necessarily posited, is a ground (*ratio*).",[]],"reflm:3506":["Not, the grounded having been posited, is a ground posited.",[]],"reflm:3507":["That, which being posited (if I posit it), another is posited (s determinately) according to a rule, is a ground (*ratio*).\nFrom the grounded to the ground the consequence is not valid.\ncondition: that, which not having been posited, another is not posited.",[]],"reflm:3508":["ideal and real.",[]],"reflm:3509":["the *nexus* is also of the coordinated, not always of the subordinated.",[]],"reflm:3510":["Something, Nothing, no thing.",[]],"reflm:3511":["In the margin at left \nOn possibility. On the something. On the thing.",[]],"reflm:3512":["in a certain, in every regard (*respectu*).",[]],"reflm:3513":["connected (*connexa*) (g mutually) either through agreement or opposition.",[]],"reflm:3514":["In a series (g of the coordinated and) of the subordinated.",[]],"reflm:3515":["Primitive and subaltern ground (*ratio*).",[]],"reflm:3516":["ground (*ratio*) either primitive or subaltern.",[]],"reflm:3517":["a ground (*ratio*) which is itself a grounded (*rationatum*).",[]],"reflm:3518":["On condition in general — limiting.",[]],"reflm:3519":["Over and \nA ground (*ratio*) either suffices for the grounded or [sibim] also for itself; if the former: it is sufficient *secundum qvid*, if the latter: it is *simpliciter*.",[]],"reflm:3520":["logical predicate (*praedicatum logicum*). Determines:",[]],"reflm:3521":["He who is a human being is determined with regard to scholarship, but not as a human being. I.e. not the logical subject human being, but the real, namely the one who is thought through the concept of humanity.",[]],"reflm:3522":["That in which there is a relation (*respectus*) of the ground to the grounded is called the determining (*determinans*).\nWhat is in itself undetermined is determined by another.",[]],"reflm:3523":["logical predicate (*praedicatum logicum*) or determination.",[]],"reflm:3524":["See p. 12.\nOn M § 36 »realitas — negatio«:\n*per se*, not relative to another concept, which is logical, not metaphysical or transcendental.",[]],"reflm:3525":["Absolute and respective concepts.\nsuch as those of ground and of the sequels are respective. Only the respective can be made distinct.",[]],"reflm:3526":["A book in the library\n*deest* — *negatio*: is lacking. What is not in its place. (g *abest*: what is not in its time.)\n*deficit* — when what ought to have been is missing, i.e. a negation opposed to perfection (g Is wanting).\n*abest* — when [it] one knows where it otherwise is (g is not present).",[]],"reflm:3527":["This is a logical respect (*respectus*).\n(s real.)",[]],"reflm:3528":["The essence of a thing (adjectivally).\nA being: *ens*.",[]],"reflm:3529":["peculiar\nFurther from σ-ψ:\nare those whose concepts allow themselves to be reciprocated with the subjects.",[]],"reflm:3530":["From the assertoric to the problematic, but not conversely.",[]],"reflm:3531":["ψ−χ?? M 16.\nFrom the negation of the assertoric (g *antecedens*) not to the negation of the problematic.",[]],"reflm:3532":["*nullo*\nBetween § 59 and § 60 stand the words:\nfor if the upper proposition is true, then from a particular nothing at all can follow.",[]],"reflm:3533":["i.e. from the concept neither of thoroughgoing determination nor of a reality can an inference to existence be made.",[]],"reflm:3534":["»*omni, non tollitur possibilitas*« one cannot say; what does not exist at all, must also not be possible in every aim.",[]],"reflm:3535":["that which contains reality",[]],"reflm:3536":["Thing and its predicates",[]],"reflm:3537":["chimera, fantasy, ideal.",[]],"reflm:3538":["determination (*determinatio*) of a being (*ens*), insofar as it is regarded what is posited or how often something is posited.",[]],"reflm:3539":["The difference of one thing from another through the plurality of what is of the same kind, which is contained in [it] both, is magnitude. The difference of what is of unlike kind is quality.",[]],"reflm:3540":["What is possible through one posited several times is a *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:3541":["better *quanta*; that something is a *quantum* can be cognized absolutely, but how large (*quantitas*), only relatively.",[]],"reflm:3542":["*quantum* and *quantitas*.",[]],"reflm:3543":["In the margin at right, \nTaken substantively.\nThe adjectival is hypothetical.\nFurther likewise in the margin at right, above l. 3, beside M § 74 »*Quicquid — est*«, probably also on »*unitas categorica*«:\nquantitative and qualitative",[]],"reflm:3544":["That of which there are not several of the same kind is single.",[]],"reflm:3545":["Nonsense in which there is method.",[]],"reflm:3546":["On »*Sectio VII. Perfectum.*«, resp. \nThe perfection (*perfectio*) of a being (*ens*) in general is the totality (*omnitudo*) of reality.",[]],"reflm:3547":["they agree (*consentiunt*) either logically, insofar as they do not contradict, or really, insofar as they are not really opposed to one another.",[]],"reflm:3548":["On »*Sectio VII*«:\nperfection taken adjectivally or substantively. likewise unity, truth, possibility.",[]],"reflm:3549":["Contingent in every way (*omnimode contingens*)",[]],"reflm:3550":["Contingent is something either to a thing (i.e. it is not necessary to it), e.g. composition to the elements, or breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3551":["There are contingent beings, namely the beings of all contingent things, e.g. motions.",[]],"reflm:3552":["Unconditioned necessity is both with regard to the outer and inner Best breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3553":["Thoroughly necessary.",[]],"reflm:3554":["Either with regard to one determination or to all: 1. the opposite is internally or absolutely not thinkable, i.e. there cannot be more necessary beings.\nFurther:\ni.e. under the condition under which something necessarily belongs to a thing, this thing cannot be determined in another way; but it can be subjected to another condition. e.g. A bell cannot be other than [inn] internally hollow and open; but the same metal can nonetheless be melted into a rod.",[]],"reflm:3555":["After the opening word of M § 125 Kant wants to have inserted:\n*perdurabilis*",[]],"reflm:3556":["the subject (*subiectum*) is changed, the determinations (*determinationes*) vary, the state (*status*) breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3557":["Beside and \nWhether matter, motion is reality.\nWhether vice is reality (*realitas*) or negation (*negatio*).\nnegations on objects are limitations.\nreality affected with negations (*realitas affecta negationibus*).\nPlurality of reality and degree.\nSpace and time have no degree.\nExtensive, protensive and intensive magnitude.",[]],"reflm:3558":["real opposition (*oppositio realis*)",[]],"reflm:3559":["In the margin below, \nlimitation (*limitatio*).",[]],"reflm:3560":["Occamists (g Thomists) nominalists. Scotists realists.",[]],"reflm:3561":["nominalists and realists.",[]],"reflm:3562":["not 2/3 + 3/4 = 1. On the 21 fourth parts of the city of Paris.",[]],"reflm:3563":["Kant alters the definition at the beginning of M § 157 so that it reads:\nA composite (*compositum*) entirely the same as its parts is a whole (*totum*).",[]],"reflm:3564":["That which cannot exist except either as a part or as a grounded (*rationatum*) or as an accident (*accidens*).",[]],"reflm:3565":["The totality (*totalitas*) either of a *quantum* or of a *compositum* as such. Part of a whole, either of a *quantum* or of a *compositum*.",[]],"reflm:3566":["a *quantum* of quantity — — indeterminate.\nDeterminate is quantity.\n[the assignable parts are]\nA *quantum* is givable (*dabile*), which [as a whole] as complete can be actual. Assignable parts are those which can be clearly cognized in themselves. [whose parts] That in which the multitude of assignable parts is indeterminate (whose parts cannot be given in every way except through the whole), is a *continuum*.",[]],"reflm:3567":["It is false; for what is greater than nothing, of that the part is equal to nothing; therefore nothing is a part of something, which is repugnant.",[]],"reflm:3568":["Absolutely (i.e. in some respect) possible.\nIn a certain respect also possible.",[]],"reflm:3569":["It determines strictly, widely.\n*latitudinarius*: he who admits exceptions.",[]],"reflm:3570":["The more it approaches universality and lacks restrictions.",[]],"reflm:3571":["The intensive magnitude of laws rests on necessity, i.e. the lack of exceptions. No rule without exception, but a law is without exception.",[]],"reflm:3572":["*substratum*, what is stable.",[]],"reflm:3573":["A real subject (*subiectum reale*) is a substance (*substantia*).",[]],"reflm:3574":["An accident can be a logical subject.",[]],"reflm:3575":["Substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*)",[]],"reflm:3576":["If these were true, the state (*status*) of no being (*ens*) would be immutable.",[]],"reflm:3577":["On the heading: »*Sectio VIII. Status*«:\nWhether it is all one to say: a thing has changed, or: its state (at any rate inner) has changed.",[]],"reflm:3578":["Properly it is not a *modus* but the substance that is changed. For that which is changed remains; the change is only the exchange of its determinations.",[]],"reflm:3579":["On the last two sentences of M § 210:\nThe [Human] body (s the lifeless,) effects; the living (g makes) acts (s *operatur*); the free does (s *facit*).",[]],"reflm:3580":["A substance (*substantia*) actuating a substance (g producing) creates*.\n(g preserves for the sake of duration.)\nA substance adding a complement to an acting substance concurs.\n*(s the cause of the origin of a substance creates.)",[]],"reflm:3581":["If all suffering of the substance is properly the determination of the activity of the suffering subject, then no substance can be determined internally itself through God to other and greater accidents (*accidentibus*) than are possible from its nature; otherwise another fundamental force and substantiality would be produced in it, and thus it would be recreated, and not be the identity of the subject.",[]],"reflm:3582":["The inner possibility of a force (s of acting) is the faculty. e.g. Someone can have a great faculty for science, but has as yet no force of acuteness etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:3583":["The internally sufficient ground for it is force; if it is also externally sufficient, then it is living force; if it is externally insufficient, then it is dead force. Dead force for the sake of the hindrance is *conatus*.",[]],"reflm:3584":["Faculty and force,\n*potentia* — *actus*.\nForce acts, faculty does not.",[]],"reflm:3585":["The inner principle of the possibility of acting is the faculty*, e.g. machine**.\n*(g The inner determining ground of the faculty for acting is endeavor (*conatus*). The faculty with an endeavor to act is force. Force, insofar as it is inwardly sufficient for the actuality of the action, but without effect, is dead force; this always proves hindrance. (s Force sufficient inwardly as well as outwardly is living.))\n**(s The faculty together with a determining ground of the same (e.g. an applied lever) is force. The force (thus faculty and determining ground of the same together) [is brings] is by itself sufficient for the actuality of the action. Thus *posita vi* (g *per se*) *ponitur actio*. Only another can conflict with the determining ground [of the] in the force. [This] The state of a force in this conflict is endeavor (*conatus*) and that which contains the ground of the fact that the action does not arise, the hindrance. Every *conatus* is an action, to which however something works in opposition. Hence it is dead force. The living force is not greater than the *conatus*, but brings forth a desired effect.\nNB. For the *conatus* a time is required if it is to become living force. For if the support were taken from a weight and gravity would not further work, the one impression would give no motion.)",[]],"reflm:3586":["To act means: to contain the sufficient ground of the *accidens*.\nThe possibility of the action is the faculty.\nThe inwardly sufficient ground of the action is the force.\nIt is dead if [outwardly] a ground of the opposite is there.\nThe opposed ground is *impedimentum*; if it is another substance: *resistentia*.",[]],"reflm:3587":["Consonant forces: of these one adds to the other something homogeneous, e.g. the attraction of the sun to the moon. Not combined: there the addition is 0. Opposed: –a; they take away just as much, thus the difference of the adding from the taking away is double the amount of the *accessorium*. Hence the danger of diminution is always greater than the gain of the addition.",[]],"reflm:3588":["The substance, insofar as it contains the sufficient ground of a determinate *accidens*, acts; insofar as it contains the ground of accidents in general, has a force. This is either inwardly sufficient or insufficient; in the latter case it is a mere faculty, in the former it is either outwardly insufficient, i.e. a *conatus*, or outwardly sufficient: *vis viva*.\nWith each dead force another opposed one is also dead.",[]],"reflm:3589":["Where force is, there is also action (in lifeless beings), but not therefore immediately effect, because an inner or outer resistance [a] the counteraction of a force is, which cancels the effect of the previous one. In living beings faculty and force are distinguished; in lifeless ones e.g. a rope has as much faculty to bear a load held on it as it exerts actual force of interconnection.",[]],"reflm:3590":["The real relation of a substance as cause to [its] the [there] through it possible effect is called force. This before the effect is called *conatus*; without the same, because a resistance occurs, *vis mortua*.",[]],"reflm:3591":["At the very top of the page:\nBecause all *nexus* is *logicus* or *realis*. so is breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3592":["Origination (*ortus*) is existence that follows non-existence.",[]],"reflm:3593":["Arising and perishing, beginning and ceasing. The latter pertains to *accidentia*.",[]],"reflm:3594":["Substance (*substantia*) as phenomenon (*phaenomenon*) is the perdurable (*perdurabile*).",[]],"reflm:3595":["Above the concluding word of M § 233:\nA composite (*compositum*) spoken of quantitatively, qualitatively.",[]],"reflm:3596":["*simile* of the bow.",[]],"reflm:3597":["tensed.",[]],"reflm:3598":["Space and time have *termini*.",[]],"reflm:3599":["Sameness and difference belong to the relation of comparison.",[]],"reflm:3600":["Identical generically, specifically and numerically.",[]],"reflm:3601":["totally the same (*totaliter eadem*) —\nthe same in number (*numero idem*)\n(g *congruentia*) (g as to the internal —\nin different relations)\nas to quantity —\nthey are also as to quality, because only homogeneous things can be equal,\nas to quality —\nthey can also be as to quantity, and then they would be *congruentia*.",[]],"reflm:3602":["The *terminus* of space is distinguished from *limites*.\nA spherical surface is properly not terminated anywhere, but is terminating.",[]],"reflm:3603":["Because the boundary is a ground of limitations, it is no part, since it adds nothing to the magnitude, but rather cuts off; the cutting off, however, belongs to both, what is cut off; thus the boundary is common.",[]],"reflm:3604":["The quality of the boundary of the extended is the figure.",[]],"reflm:3605":["The community of boundaries. Two points do not touch one another, thus distant.",[]],"reflm:3606":["Two points lie at all times in a line, three points [in a surface and 4 in a corporeal space.], through which no common straight line can be drawn, lie in a surface, and four points, of which through three no straight line can go, lie in corporeal space. Still from these *data* two bodies are possible, namely toward two opposed regions.\nThis means as much as: If one point is given, then from a given distance another point can be given in infinitely many places (g i.e. it lies in a spherical surface). If two points are given, then the third point lies in a circle*. If three are given, then the fourth point lies in two places on opposite sides**. All position of the points can be determined geometrically from certain *data*, but the last position of the fourth point only with regard to our body.\nOne must therefore know the whole corporeal space, in order to draw from a given point with a given distance a line; for this can only be drawn to a determinate point in the surface.\n*(g the third point is determined, but not its place.)\n**(g for then no rotation is possible.)",[]],"reflm:3607":["*Continua* have not only limitations, but also boundaries.",[]],"reflm:3608":["The mathematical concepts are unity, plurality, number, infinity, (the relations) [equality, inequality], composition of a magnitude out of another one or several times or out of its parts, or also out of many magnitudes by synthesis (*per synthesin*), either positive or negative (*vel positivam vel negativam*); hence the idea of equality, inequality and relations. The objects of mathematics are space, time, motion or every other ground estimated either as a whole (*totum*) or as a ground, the former through the many that is in it, the latter through that which is posited by it.",[]],"reflm:3609":["If [magnitudes of] the quality of a magnitude is of the kind that the action [the *coordinatio*] of raising one of them, which has a certain extensive magnitude, intensively to the second degree, is a multiplication, then there are no more than 3 *dimensiones*. But where the magnitude |\nnot intensively, by multiplying (*multiplicando*), but extensively, by adding (*addendo*), grows, there is only one *dimensio* (*dimensionem*). Of things that are simultaneous one can represent the generation of the multitude out of unity as in flux.",[]],"reflm:3610":["It is remarkable that with point, line and surface, when they move (g perpendicularly), the motions of the elements do not fall into one another, but do with the body. It is also not necessary that it happen perpendicularly, if only it is not in the direction in which the motion of the previous elements would meet. The question is: why then do they meet in the fourth dimension, namely that after the generation of a body. Is the ground of this not perhaps situated in the combinations in general?",[]],"reflm:3611":["*qvasi qvies esset accidens* (as if rest were an accident).",[]],"reflm:3612":["Only in phenomena (*phaenomenis*).",[]],"reflm:3613":["*ut quantitas*\nbut forgot to strike out the second word of the text »continuatio«, which with the new definition must of course drop out.",[]],"reflm:3614":["in all parts of time (*omnibus partibus temporis*)",[]],"reflm:3615":["*Ens a se* (being from itself) is not changed (*non mutatur*). For it is in no determination through others, thus not to something other.",[]],"reflm:3616":["*respectus* and *relation*; the former: of comparison, the latter: of influences.",[]],"reflm:3617":["An accident (*accidens*), insofar as it is cause, is not called efficient (*efficiens*).\nSubstance (*substantia*), insofar as its own accident is cause, likewise is not efficient.",[]],"reflm:3618":["either efficient (*efficiens*) or a condition without which not (*conditio sine qva non*)",[]],"reflm:3619":["Buffon seeks *nexus effectivos*, others final ones. Epicurus the former in organization.",[]],"reflm:3620":["On the concluding sentence of M § 332:\nThe causality in the cause is not nobler than the effect (*non est nobilior effectu*).",[]],"reflm:3621":["Estimable (worthy of estimation)\nInestimable (not capable of estimation),\njust as “incorrigible” has two kinds of sense.",[]],"reflm:3622":["objective",[]],"reflm:3623":["Idea. Archetype. Model. Example. Similarity, analogy are different.",[]],"reflm:3624":["Absolute universality of a concept (*universalitas absoluta conceptus*).\nAbsolute universality of a whole (*universalitas absoluta totius*), i.e. the metaphysical universality of things (*rerum universitas metaphysica*). This would prove that there is only one world, that the cause is the highest.",[]],"reflm:3625":["Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:3626":["Respective case (*casus respectivus*). Absolute (*absolutus*).",[]],"reflm:3627":["Nature is taken here only adjectivally.",[]],"reflm:3628":["On the heading »Mundus optimus«:\nThe world as derivative highest good (*summum bonum derivativum*).",[]],"reflm:3629":["[All constraint is either a] All necessitation (g is either a necessitation) against will (g or with will,) either aesthetic or physical, a necessitation of the will itself breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3630":[") M 277'.\nThe action, insofar as it arises from the faculty of desire in such a way that I can become conscious that I am not determined to it breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3631":["Separation of the soul from the body.",[]],"reflm:3632":["Removal (*remotio*) of the *nexus*, either virtual or local.",[]],"reflm:3633":["Separation (*separatio*) of the soul from the body is either local or virtual.",[]],"reflm:3634":["Next to the second-to-last sentence of M § 784:\nMetempsychosis of Michel Angelo, Gallilaei and Newton. (by Nelli in Florence.)",[]],"reflm:3635":["Predicate of God (*Dei praedicatum*): internal\nexternal\n{\nindependence dependence of all on him",[]],"reflm:3636":["Logical perfection (sufficiency, *sufficientia*) or practical.",[]],"reflm:3637":["*nexus* with contingents (*cum contingentibus*)",[]],"reflm:3638":["He is not changeable.\nHis existence is not changeable; persistent.",[]],"reflm:3639":["Because things subsist through God, it seems as if they subsist in him.\nIt is not the soul of the world (*anima Mundi*).",[]],"reflm:3640":["*Causatum maximum*. The highest created good.",[]],"reflm:3641":["As an active being he has nature.",[]],"reflm:3642":["On the underlined word »theologia«\n*ectypa* (ectypal): *e mundanis affectionibus* (from mundane affections). *per reductionem accomodata* (accommodated through reduction): *analogica* (analogical).",[]],"reflm:3643":["The cognition of the good (g of that which is good in the whole (in every actual relation)) from the idea of the whole as a ground of choice is wisdom.\nWhat is to be good in every aim must be derived from the idea of the most perfect whole.\n(g Prudence is the knowledge of the means to ends that are not entirely in our power. Thus with regard to the opinion and freedom of other human beings.)\nThe wisdom of the human being consists in this, that he do without the ends that are not in his power.",[]],"reflm:3644":["Wisdom goes to the good.\nPrudence to the useful.",[]],"reflm:3645":["The faculty of cognizing the relation of all things* to the highest good** is wisdom; to the (g good in appearance) particular: prudence (not of God.).\n(s The suitability of cognition to the highest good and its possibility is either merely theoretical — science, or moral: wisdom.)\n*(s The faculty of the cognition of the [means] best means to the same is wisdom of art; the faculty of the cognition of the end together with the will of the same: moral wisdom.)\n**(s either to itself or to the derived highest good. The former is the ground of the latter, but only in the divine understanding.)",[]],"reflm:3646":[") M 359.\nWisdom in general is the perfection of cognition, insofar as it is suitable to the whole of all ends (either to the physical or moral whole).",[]],"reflm:3647":["Wisdom is the (g subjective) *principium* of the unity of all ends [a pr] according to reason, consequently happiness united with morality.",[]],"reflm:3648":["The highest good has two parts. Which is the condition, which is the conditioned. Morality or happiness?",[]],"reflm:3649":["Whether we can fathom the ends of divine wisdom in nature and whether that be physicotheology? — He is the only Holy — Blessed — Wise.",[]],"reflm:3650":["The divine understanding derives all cognition of the particular from the whole of all possibility and each particular end from the whole of all ends. Everything from itself.",[]],"reflm:3651":["M359':\n[Kl] Skill is the faculty of reason with regard to the means to arbitrary ends, prudence with regard to the sum of all subjective ends.\nWisdom: the faculty of determining each particular end through the whole of all objective ends. |\nM 359: |\nHence »a human being« and »wise« is a contradiction, except in morality as a model.",[]],"reflm:3652":["It is all one whether many or few means lead to the end, if only the end is attained; superfluous means are blameworthy only in the case of human beings, because they hinder his other ends. Yet because the sufficient ground of the means is only in the end, nothing more can be in those than what is required for this.",[]],"reflm:3653":["As means in and for itself (g in the execution) there can surely never be more than in the end, i.e. there is not more in the cause than in the effect. But as means in the intention there can be more than what is required for the end, from error. Yet much can exist besides what fulfills the whole end, and the law of parsimony is not for God.",[]],"reflm:3654":["The measuredness in means.",[]],"reflm:3655":["Not everything is ends or means, at least not everything good in the sequels of certain causes.",[]],"reflm:3656":["Divine wisdom is the ground of the agreement of his choice with itself without exceptions and collision from one plan.",[]],"reflm:3657":["The parsimony of the divine decree in the choice [of the] of that alone which as a means is useful to the highest represented good is no property of divine wisdom. It is the property of the impotent, who, if he does more in one case than what contributes something to any utility, neglects other utility.",[]],"reflm:3658":["*Voluntas obiective antecedens* (will objectively antecedent) is that whose [obiect] motive is what is universal in objects.\n*Voluntas obiective conseqvens* (will objectively consequent) is that whose motive is what is individual in objects.",[]],"reflm:3659":["*Voluntas optima* (the best will) determines [perfectionem] a particular volition (*volitionem particularem*) *in omnitudine* (in totality), i.e. according to the human manner of speaking: in the universal, insofar as it is restricted through particular conditions.",[]],"reflm:3660":["The divine will, insofar as it goes to what is common to the object with others that [nicht ge] he does not will, is called *antecedens*; insofar as it goes to that by which he distinguishes himself from all others, is called *conseqvens*.",[]],"reflm:3661":["*voluntas antecedens* is that which goes to a general concept of an object (as undetermined). *conseqvens*: to the determined concept of the object.",[]],"reflm:3662":["The object of the highest intelligence is the highest good according to cognition and as *autor* according to will.\nThe highest good in the whole and also the relation of each individual to the same:\n1. the relation to the general concept of the good. *voluntas antecedens*.\n2. to the thoroughly determined cognition of this world. *voluntas conseqvens*.",[]],"reflm:3663":["One could still ask whether reward is a matter of justice or of kindness.",[]],"reflm:3664":["On M § 907 Kant has made a sign after the words »bonum morale« underlined by him, to which in the right margin the same sign before the word\n*meritum*\ncorresponds, which evidently is to take the place of the two words of the text. For Kant adds:\nFor whether *bonum morale* in general is merely [Weishei] wise goodness.",[]],"reflm:3665":["*Praemia* (rewards) either are *munera* (gifts): *praemia gratuita* (gratuitous rewards)\n—\n—\n—\n*mercedes* (wages): — — *debita* (owed).\n*moralia* (moral): *sapientiae* (of wisdom),\n*pragmatica* (pragmatic): *prudentiae* (of prudence).",[]],"reflm:3666":["Punitive justice is distinguished from the justice of revenge; the former is exercised by the one whose right, the other, whose person, has been injured.",[]],"reflm:3667":["God does not punish the offense to his person (*laesae maiestatis*), but, because the human being cognizes God only according to the analogy with human beings, the wickedness that the human being manifests, that he [it] would be capable of it in relation to other human beings. [Hence] Otherwise we would confuse ourselves in the infinity of the crime.",[]],"reflm:3668":["Punitive justice belongs to wisdom.",[]],"reflm:3669":["On the first word of M § 910:\n*distributiva vel vindicativa* (distributive or vindicative)",[]],"reflm:3670":["Distributive justice (*iustitia distributiva*) is not instrumental (*instrumentalis*), but final (*finalis*).",[]],"reflm:3671":["Kant alters the first sentence of M § 910 so that it reads:\n*Iustitia dei* (the justice of God) *non est remuneratoria* (is not remunerative), *sed punitiva* (but punitive); *est vel pragmatica* (it is either pragmatic): of the regent, or *moralis* (moral): of the judge, *quia peccatum est* (because sin is).",[]],"reflm:3672":["Whether there be a *Crimen laesae majestatis divinae* (crime of injured divine majesty).",[]],"reflm:3673":["All these properties are the moral [which], chiefly on the ground that God demands the inner of the human heart to him [conformable] and in agreement with his presence.",[]],"reflm:3674":["He demands forbearance among human beings in punishing.\nThe kindness that postpones the punishment of the offender for his improvement.",[]],"reflm:3675":["He punishes according to the order of nature in the whole only after a long time.\nHe punishes only in accordance with the infinite *progressus*.",[]],"reflm:3676":["He [wants] loves impartiality in the judgment of human beings.\nHe does not have favorites, e.g. the Jewish people.",[]],"reflm:3677":["The human being cannot always derive the particular good that God distributes from the general provision; hence he is entitled to regard it as a particular provision and to thank God for it, yet also to believe that in a way unknown to him this is nonetheless general.",[]],"reflm:3678":["He administers equity, i.e. before his court a transgression of equity is a violation of strict right.",[]],"reflm:3679":["He judges from the standpoint of a human being, who is a limited being. The human being can regard many things as an advantage that God gives him for others.",[]],"reflm:3680":["He demands sincerity, which with regard to God commonly is very lacking, in that one affects praises and devotions that one does not feel, from fear pretends to believe much and applies oneself to flattery.\n(s God is open in his revelations necessary to us, not concealed, not enigmatic and ambiguous. He does not lead us into temptation.)",[]],"reflm:3681":["He wants truthfulness among human beings.\n(s one must not say of God what is completely identical with the predicates through which we think his moral perfections.)",[]],"reflm:3682":["*beatus* (blessed). The self-sufficiency in well-being.",[]],"reflm:3683":["He is above the [nature] world as the originator (nature) and as the lawgiver (freedom).",[]],"reflm:3684":["*finis absqve motivis* (end without motives).",[]],"reflm:3685":["*actuatio mundi et regimen* (actuation of the world and government).",[]],"reflm:3686":["On the two last sentences of M § 950:\nThe preservation of the form of the same conforming to the final end in time is called the gubernation: the administration.",[]],"reflm:3687":["*non influxus, qvi est [actio] modificatio, non actuatio substantiae*: not influx, which is [action] modification, not actuation of substance.",[]],"reflm:3688":["A continued beginning is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3689":["*Concursus* (concurrence) to nature or freedom.\n*Providentia respectu legum (illas determinans) est generalis, eventuum est specialis*: providence with respect to laws (determining them) is general, of events is special.",[]],"reflm:3690":["*Non concurrit ad formale actionum* (it does not concur in the formal of actions). *Non est auctor mali secundum directionem ordinariam* (it is not the author of evil according to the ordinary direction).",[]],"reflm:3691":["Permission. Allowance is not permission.",[]],"reflm:3692":["Lawgiver in the beginning,\nRuler in the duration,\nJudge at the end. properly in the infinity of the progress.",[]],"reflm:3693":["who is not subjected to the power of another.",[]],"reflm:3694":["397. E I 689.\nIs the one who [s] binds the creature in accordance with an absolute will, without * taking into consideration their utility in the laws. Thus there are despotic and patriotic laws.\n*(g to take into consideration the agreement of their own will governed through reason, i.e. as machines.)",[]],"reflm:3695":["The will that is determined with regard to the general grounds [of the same] of willing toward a certain action is *antecedens*. That which is determined with regard to the *individui*: *consequens*.",[]],"reflm:3696":["Here God is represented as if he decided about everything possible, which part he would take out for his choice.",[]],"reflm:3697":["*Decretum est voluntas consequens, ergo respectu individui.* (The decree is the *voluntas consequens*, therefore with respect to the individual.)\n*Voluntas antecedens est, cuius caussa impulsiva est tantum generale aliquod, quod omnibus obiectis arbitrii commune est.* (The *voluntas antecedens* is that whose *causa impulsiva* is only something general, which is common to all objects of choice.)\n*Decretum [nullum] de condendo hoc mundo est decretum individui, ergo non est universale. Non datur decretum universale. sed decretum universum sive totale, diversum non a [partial] particulari, sed partiali.* (A decree [none] concerning the founding of this world is a decree of the individual, therefore it is not universal. There is no universal decree, but a *decretum universum* or total, different not from the [partial] particular, but from the partial.)",[]],"reflm:3698":["*est summum bonum mundi, qvatenus opponitur summo bono individui.* (is the highest good of the world, insofar as it is opposed to the highest good of the individual.)",[]],"reflm:3699":["Divine choice is never absolutely unconditioned, but perhaps unconditioned with regard to the moral quality of the person, as despotism; for God's aim [and d] could after all be directed to the best of the world in the whole, and this be the condition.* God cannot sacrifice a (g rational) creature to the world-whole, but can well allow that it sacrifice itself.\n*(g Here it is assumed that God uses the rational creature merely as a means to the perfection of the whole. But there is no moral worth in the world where the rational beings are used merely as means.)",[]],"reflm:3700":["Whether the divine will in itself is determined through motives, or is thought by us merely according to this analogy?",[]],"reflm:3701":["Fate.",[]],"reflm:3702":["*tanquam fine voluntatis* (as by the end of the will)",[]],"reflm:3703":["You ask: [is] who is happier in the world, the virtuous or the vicious? if one examines it, then in the advantages of the [virtuous] wicked there will always be something mixed in that the [vicious] virtuous does not desire and for the sake of which he would not want to exchange his state with the other's; thus he is in fact more content with himself than one thinks. The ills that befall the virtuous do not properly befall virtue, but are common to all. If the general laws were only to be restricted to the relation of the pious and the godless, then say: which are then the pious. Will not one hold this one to be punishable, the other however another |\nto be so, and would then God's justice be cognized by all. Bread is not the reward of virtue, but of diligence. If you finally give the human being everything good, say: are you then content, do you not always desire more, and will God indeed find a goal for your wishes. The true prize of virtue is the inner stillness of the souls, the remaining goods overthrow or corrupt it. Learnedness, posthumous fame, riches: all do not have the true good with them. Thus virtue alone makes true happiness, which finds in abundance as well as in want, in weeping as well as in cheerfulness something that satisfies it. Since virtue thus finds no want, wishing counts for nothing.\nSelf-love, which combines with the love of God and of the neighbor, makes up the happiness of human beings. The greater the love, the more widely extended: the greater the happiness. God begins the love with the whole and extends it to the parts, but love of human beings begins from itself and spreads little by little over the whole. Such a one the earth smiles at from all sides, and the Godhead itself sees its image in his soul.",[]],"reflm:3704":["S. I:\nOutline of optimism.\nOptimism is that doctrinal framework, [which from the presup|position of a highest benevolent wise and omnipotent original being] the [presence] evils of the world [with the concept] from the presupposition of an infinitely perfect, benevolent and omnipotent original being to [unite] justify, in that one convinces oneself that [of such nothing but that] notwithstanding all apparent contradictions, what has been chosen by this infinitely perfect being must nevertheless be the best among everything possible, and ascribes the presence of evil not to the choice of the divine good pleasure, but to the unavoidable necessity of the essential deficiencies of finite things, [which] which, in that without his fault they have been brought through the decree of permission into the plan of creation, are nevertheless turned through his wisdom and goodness so to the best of the whole, that the displeasure that [they they before] the sight of them in particular can arouse is perfectly made good in the whole through the compensation that the divine goodness knows how to arrange, since thus this world among all that were possible through divine power is the best, and that which piecemeal could be better, [the without] it might not through the breaks off.\n[Herr von Leibnitz] One will, if one examines precisely the sketch that Leibnitz gives of his principles, find that this idea expresses his opinion as exactly as possible. One may consider this division into the necessary or metaphysical and the contingent or hypothetically physical and hypothetically moral evils. One must consider his distinction between the antecedent and the consequent will, of which the first [everything without] strives to exclude all evil, the second [through the] includes it in his plan as unavoidable sequel of the [finitude] eternal nature of things, and so one will be convinced of |\nthe [correctness] truthfulness of our depiction. Leibnitz represents the goodness of God, spreading itself without measure, as the pull of a stream, which drives everything that is found within its sum total with equal force to motion, but carries the heavy cargo ships, which possess more natural force of inertia than those of less mass, forward more slowly than these.\nS. II:\nHe represents God as he is ready to reveal himself with the whole infinity of all properties in the works of creation, [but] and as he has a true displeasure at the evils, the sight of which so greatly disquiets the mind of the well-disposed. but he lets them hope, from the goodness, wisdom and power of this Lord sufficiently cognized from other undeniable grounds, that the deficiencies will be compensated in interconnection with advantages and that, [so much] however much the evils may in the end also disfigure the best plan, [it] they nevertheless [not] could not have been prevented in the whole without still greater deviation. To be sure, freely acting beings could have avoided many an evil action to God's great good pleasure; but it was [not in the reference to] an unavoidable necessity, among two evils, of which one is the lack of freedom, the other of the morally best, to choose the least, and there were also in the best plan other hindrances that could move God to omit the arrangement of certain motive causes, which could have prevented some kinds of evil, [to] out of concern for still greater absurdities. In short. It was not possible otherwise, there had to be evil. Thanks be to eternal wisdom, that he permitted the least and brought everything in the whole to his praise out most gloriously. It is true, it seems to me, we do not yet see where the compensation will actually turn out, which is to hold the whole harmless for its |\npartial deficiencies. but [getra] are you not assured that God (g so) is, (g as he) must be, in order to be able to expect everything good from him. In this case you can expect nothing other than, in the sequel, a complete satisfaction, at the least a complete justification of divine justice and goodness.\nS. III:\nComparison of the doctrinal concept of Pope with optimism and preference of the former.\nLeibnitz conceded that the deviations and deficiencies, which as true evils put the well-disposed in distress, were so in fact; but he reserved to himself to excuse the highest wisdom [from] (g which he cognized from other grounds, on account of) the permission. [The best] Thus the properties of God were put in safety for the benefit of those who have insight and docility enough to give assent to the metaphysical proofs of the divine existence. The remaining part of those who [on account of] at the sight of the world would gladly recognize the trace of God. remains in distress. Pope chose a way that, in order to make the beautiful proof of God perceptible to all humans, is the most apt [is] among all possible and that, which just constitutes the perfection of his system, (g even) subjects all possibility to the dominion of an all-sufficient original being, under which things can have no other properties, not even such as one calls essentially necessary, that do not harmonize perfectly to the ex|pression\nof his perfection. He goes through creation piece by piece, primarily there where it seems to lack agreement most; yet he shows that every thing that we would gladly wish away from the plan of greatest perfection is also, considered for itself, good, and that one need not (g beforehand) have a favorable prejudice about the wisdom of the ordaining being [to as] in order to win assent for it. The essential and necessary determinations of things, the universal laws, which are set in relation to one another in a harmonizing plan through no [to them extorted foreign] (g forced) unification, will as it were of themselves accommodate themselves to the preservation of perfect ends. Self-love, which has only its own pleasure as its aim and which evidently seems to be the cause of the moral disorder that we observe, is the origin of that beautiful [property] agreement that we admire. [Wealth can itself] Everything that benefits itself finds itself in the necessity of being useful to others at the same time. The universal bonds, which connect the whole together in an unexplored |\nS. IV: |\nway, make it that the individual advantages always relate to the advantage of other things, and indeed through a natural sequel. Thus a universal law of nature fixes that love [fixed through the drives] which preserves the whole, and indeed through such motive causes as naturally also produce that evil whose sources we would gladly see annihilated.\nCan one, when one sees the essential constitutions in so universal a natural harmony, presume that what so accommodates itself to the excellent whole is at the same time the cause of so evil breaks off.\nOn the universal perfection of the world-framework in the physical and moral understanding.\nThe foremost rule of the perfection of the world is that it be [so] complete in the highest degree, that everything exist that is possible and that neither in the chain of beings nor in the manifoldness of their variation anything be lacking that is only capable of existence; for there is for the world in general no greater defect than the nothing in any of its parts. Hence the [revelation] field of the revelation of the divine power comprehends all genera of finite things and [through] with a kind of richness extends itself down to the deficiencies and loses itself in no other way than through all steps of diminution from the highest degrees of perfection to nothing.\nLet us, on this presupposition, listen with contempt to the complaints of those to whom heaven, in their opinion, has allotted no agreeable lot of perfections. I would like, says one, to have gladly less [over] darkness in the understanding and less vehemence in the sensible inclinations, if I were only so fortunate as to have no [inclination to sin to have] other inclination than that for virtue. If most considered that, when they wish for themselves such properties as presuppose an alteration of their nature, it means just as much as to desire that they not be at all and that in their place another being of the demanded constitution might be present, |\none would go into oneself, for self-love has an abhorrence of annihilation and is rather content with the state as it may be allotted to it than that it breaks off with the side.",[]],"reflm:3705":["p. I:\nDefects of optimism.\nLeibnitz was right to call his doctrinal edifice a theodicy or defense of the good cause of God. For in fact it is nothing other than a justification of God against the imputation that he might perhaps be the author of evil, through the assurance that, so far as it depends on him, everything is good and that it at least is not his fault if [everything] not every thing turns out perfectly as it should turn out according to the wish of the upright.\nThe faults of this doctrinal concept are far too important for one to be able to stop at it. Leibnitz [presents] presents the rules of perfection (g having as end) as they conflict with one another in practice. He regards the exceptions as necessary defects and catches sight of the action befitting the highest wisdom in this, that it makes the choice on the side of the best, just as a seafarer throws a part of his wares overboard in order to save the ship and the rest. It is true, the wisdom and goodness of God triumph here over all reproaches. But what is one to say of the infinity (g and) of the independence. From what causes may the essential determinations of things have that discord which makes the perfections, each of which in particular would increase the satisfaction of God, incompatible in a combination. What kind of [strange] unfathomable strife is it between the general will of God, which aims solely at the good, and the (g metaphysical) necessity [of |\nmetaphysical properties], which will not prepare itself for it with complete agreement (g without exceptions). If the evils, by I know not what kind of necessary fatality, extort their permission from God, without having aroused satisfaction in him [on account of it], they put this most blessed being into a certain kind of displeasure, which can indeed through the justification of innocence on his side be somewhat mitigated, but not quelled. If everything in the whole was good or is still good in the parts, then infallibly the sight from all sides is the source of a true pleasure. Why then must it be so constituted that everything in the parts |\np. II: |\nbe disagreeable, merely in order to arouse satisfaction in the whole. If God abhors the vices and the torments, [why] if he does not desire them but only permits them: why then was it necessary that they must be present, [since they] supposing even that they cannot be excluded, in order not to make room for still greater defects; this excuse serves indeed to acquit God of guilt, but it will never resolve the important doubt why the [necessary propert] essential necessity has something which [against his] conflicts with the general will of God and extorts permission from him, without having acquired his satisfaction. The whole fault rests in this, Leibnitz places the plan of the best world partly in a kind of independence, partly in a dependence on the will of God. [All] All possibility is spread out before God. God sees, considers, examines it; he is steered by the determinations inhering in them, in accordance with their (g particular) perfections, to one side and in accordance with what they make up in combination, to the other side; this comparison occasions his decree. [The world is actually not so because God wants to have it so, but because it cannot be done to make it otherwise |\nto make. The independence of the eternal natures comes first. The dependence consists only in the plan, in which God strives to order them as well in accordance with the rules of the best as their essential determinations permit.]\nThe essence of the world [good] is not [merely therefore good because] solely so for the reason that God wants to have it so [and], but because it was not possible otherwise, only ei breaks off, but still leaves room for 7 lines.\np. III:\nThe second chief fault of [doctr] optimism is that the evils and absurdities that are perceived in the world are excused only from the presupposition of the existence of God, and that one must therefore (g beforehand) believe that there is an infinitely good and infinitely perfect being, before one can assure oneself that the world, which is assumed as his work, is beautiful and regular, whereas the general agreement [and] of the arrangements of the world, if they can be cognized in and for themselves, offer the most beautiful proof of the existence of God and of the general dependence of all things on him. The surest and easiest proof, therefore, of the actuality of an all-sufficient, infinite[ly] good and infinitely wise being, which is cognized from the consideration of the excellent arrangements that the world shows everywhere, is deprived of force by the doctrinal edifice of Herr v. Leibnitz. It seems to me that an Epicurus would answer the one who built on this proof: If the agreement that you perceive in the world seems to prove to you an ordering wisdom [to] as the author, then you must concede that [that part] the world for the greatest part does not depend on it, since everywhere in more than the half it contains in itself absurdities and repugnant deviations. I do not |\naccept your evasion, that of that wisdom which has perhaps ordered some parts [with] to prudent ends it is to be presumed that it has likewise concealed proofs of its oversight in the rest. I would rather therefore conclude thus: if [you] the wise higher cause has not been capable of bringing all things into a plan of concordant beauty, then at least not all things were subject in their properties to its satisfaction, and [that] the eternal fate, which so greatly restricts the [intention] power [of this deity] of the capable cause [only place to ein-] and extorts from it the consent to gross evils, robs it thereby of all-sufficiency |\np. IV: |\nand [in that it weighs it with the rest, under] subjects it to the very same necessity.",[]],"reflm:3706":["p. I:\nIf existence could be counted (g also) among the various predicates that may belong to a thing, as one of them, then certainly no proof could be demanded that would be more cogent and at the same time more comprehensible, in order to demonstrate the existence of God, than the Cartesian. For among all possible things there is one in which [all possible (g the highest) reality that is to be encountered in one] all realities are to be encountered that can be together in one being.[Among these] To these realities, i.e. truly positive predicates, existence also belongs; consequently existence belongs [any] to the most real of all beings on account of its inner possibility. One also objects against this in vain that [all reality only in thought with one another] such a possible thing includes existence only in the understanding, i.e. only as the thing itself is posited in thought, but not [in the real understanding] outside of thought, for in such a way we would have to say of all predicates that belong to a [thing] possible thing: they do not belong to it in fact, but are only posited in it in thought. This latter does indeed take place when one combines arbitrarily with a concept something that is not thereby necessarily posited; e.g. wings are posited in thought to a horse, in order to make a Pegasus, hence [the winged horse] wings belong to any horse only in thought. On the other hand where the connection |\nof a predicate |\np. II: |\nwith a thing is not arbitrary, but is combined through the essence of the things themselves, there it does not belong to it because we think it in it, but it is necessary to think it in it, because it belongs to it in itself. On account of which I cannot say that the equality of the angles with two right angles belongs to a triangle only in thought, but rather in itself. This also does not hinder me from merely thinking such a possible thing; for [I] it is in itself always something, although no one thinks it, and the predicate belongs to it in itself, although no one combines it with it. So it also stands with existence, if it could be regarded (g as) a predicate of things. For it would necessarily belong to that among everything possible [i.e. that thing will necessarily exist] in which all reality is, i.e. [a] a most real being will necessarily exist, and its possibility would include actuality. If, without my thought or that of any thing, existence [no predicate of the] did not belong to the most perfect being, the thought of this being in general would also be false. For if it is correct, it can represent no other predicates than those that also without this thought take place in the thing.\np. III:\nthe question here is not whether more is posited (g in general through an actual thing) than through a not actual one, but whether more predicates are posited in it; now [in a kind good as such insofar as it is considered as existing] in the goodness of God, insofar as it is considered as existing, nothing more is posited than in the goodness that lies in his possibility; but the difference consists only in this, that in the first case the |\nthing itself with what is contained in it, but in the second the *respektus* of this manifold toward one another are posited.\nThe most essential difficulty is probably this, that our use of language, insofar as it seems to express our most exact idea, always designates existence like a predicate, which has the look of stemming from the immediate constitution of our concept. I say through an existing thing: existence belongs to a thing [I] etc: I consider the existence of things in particular and distinguish it from possibility. This constitution of our judgments deserves very much to be weighed. That is not philosophizing, when one merely seeks to demonstrate that something is a delusion, a deception of the understanding, but one must also learn to see how such a deception would be possible. This illusion is itself a [true] actual appearance in the nature of our mind, and I must either be able to explain it, or I have cause to fear that my judgment, which gives out [this] one or another opinions as a delusion, may itself be such an illusion.\np. IV:\nI ask now: if existence is no predicate of things, how can I then make use of the expression existence at all; for this distinguishes something in the thing from the thing itself, which can be attributed to it. e.g. [This] To a certain thing actuality belongs. Because one and the very same thing can be posited in different ways, this positing itself seems to be a mark of the difference, which one could attribute to a thing as a predicate or separate from it. But it is certain that this difference comes down merely to how this thing is posited with everything belonging to it, and not: what is posited in it. According to the difference of the way in which the thing is now posited, something different is also thereby posited: namely through its predicates in relation to the subject merely relations and |\npossibility, [through the] through the thing, insofar as it is posited absolutely, the thing itself. [But because we that through which we] We therefore do not distinguish the possible from the actual through a predicate which belongs to the one more than to the other.\nThe concept of an existing thing can never be changed into a judgment of which the thing would be the subject and existence the predicate.\nBut something existing must be the subject and everything that belongs to it its predicates.",[]],"reflm:3707":["P. I:\nPreparation\nOn the certainty and uncertainty of cognition in general.\nUncertainty is either an uncertainty of the matters or of insight. Although the objects of cognition are in themselves certain as to that which they are, so [so] one can nevertheless attribute uncertainty to these matters, insofar as from that [Din] which one cognizes in them and which is therefore given, nothing is established about the rest, which one seeks, however perfectly one may cognize these data, however one wishes. If in a mixture of three different metals (g whose specific heaviness is given, in addition) the weight of the whole lump and its hydrostatic relation to water is given, so [ist] one may cognize these data as one wishes, [so] it always remains uncertain hereby in what relation they are mixed with one another, and indeed because the cognized pieces |\nare in themselves of such a kind that this relation remains undetermined from them. This kind of uncertainty, which one could call the objective, must be encountered in the cognition of every cognition that is limited. He who does not cognize everything, from whose pieces of cognition something (g according to its constitution) must remain unsettled, may also make comparisons with this cognition as he wishes. [Es kommt nur d]\nIf one now cognizes [diese Er] that from certain data that one knows, certain other [Best] pieces are undetermined, so [ist] insofar no error can take place in our cognition, and the same is therefore not (g objectively) uncertain. If the apparent diameter of a star is known, but the distance unknown, so [ist] it remains uncertain what the true magnitude of the star is, although [woferne man nur nicht weiter ge] from this uncertainty alone no error can arise. Likewise if in the observation |\nP. II: |\nthe angle is once known, that an error of two seconds cannot be noticed, then everything that depends more or less on such a magnitude is undetermined from the observation, and, if one cognizes this as such, then insofar no error can take place.\nSince uncertainty consists in the possibility of erring, i.e. of making a judgment that is false, all grounds of this possibility become either negative or positive are, Namely: they consist either in this, that grounds for a (g certain) true [cognition] judgment are lacking, or, that there are positive grounds to judge, which judgments nonetheless are not in conformity with the constitution of the matters. The former ground in itself is not sufficient to understand the possibility of error thereby. For from the fact that I do not know certain things it does not yet follow that I could make a false judgment. For presupposing that I cognize that I would have to know these pieces in order to judge, I would, [da] if I find that I do not know them, not want to judge and would be safeguarded from all error. (g Bey) The astronomer, as long as he knows of a comet indeed the apparent diameter, but not the [entfernung] parallax, is insofar objectively uncertain how far and how large it is, but it is impossible that he can err in this, as long as |\nhe is conscious that he cannot judge. This [Ungewishei] ignorance of certain given pieces is also not even then a ground of the possibility of erring in itself, if the human being is not at all conscious of this indeterminacy; for as long as there is no ground that occasions him to judge [so] in a matter for which he does not know enough, he is safeguarded from all judgment. Hence the common man is safeguarded from many errors; for, since with regard to most things, after which the scholars strive so eagerly, he is unconcerned, even if he hears the questions that are raised, and most questions never come into his thoughts, there is nothing that should occasion him to judge. |\nP. III: |\nAccordingly one sees that uncertainty in the subjective sense (g still) presupposes a certain ground [ist] to make judgments. If to this the uncertainty in the objective sense, of which one is however not conscious, is added.\nHence with every drive to make a judgment, if the consciousness of the objective uncertainty is added to it, error is impossible, as one can see in geometry. The greater the desire to judge or the habit of judging is. We have cited objective uncertainty only insofar as it is taken together with the [subiektivischen] inner grounds to judge, in order to make the subjective possibility of erring comprehensible. But even if this desire is the same, the grounds of distinction of the true from the false are nonetheless either hidden and, in proportion to the agreements that a false cognition has with a true one, small, or not. (Since we infer from analogy, many agreements can be a ground of the errors, e.g. reason of the animals.) In the case in which one has many but not all grounds of distinction, error is possible only through an ignorance of the indeterminacy of the cognition from these grounds and of the inclination to judge.\nCertainty must be just as possible in philosophical cognition as in mathematical and, as regards the degree toward conviction, be sufficient for it. One cannot say that one certainty is greater |\nthan the other; nothing is ever more certain than that which is certain. But as for the way, it concerns not certainty, but clarity.\n(s All uncertainty is a possibility of erring. To err means to make a false judgment. The possibility of erring is either under a potential or an actual condition. The first is understood in such a way that one could err if one wanted to judge; the second: that there is also from actual grounds a conditioned possibility to judge, where it is nonetheless possible to err. The potential possibility of erring rests on the constitution of the matters: namely, since from that which one cognizes of them it is not determined what belongs to them or not; the actual condition of uncertainty is the ground to judge, in which a potential uncertainty nonetheless sticks. This ground to judge [liegt entweder in der] sticks in the [Mang] apparent similarity of the procedure in a false cognition with that in a true one. As firstly: Where I do not notice that something is forgotten in the marks of the thing, there nothing is forgotten. 2. Things that are similar in certain pieces are similar in others. 3. What belongs to many belongs to all.\nIn the way of designating: since signs of [einerley] similar matters are regarded as one and the same.)\n(s The uncertainty of that of which different causes are possible. Existence of bodies.)\nOn pp. I and II Kant had left below the text a margin of about 4 cm across the whole width of the page. On p. I he subsequently filled this free space with additions, and indeed, as the content proves, still within phase γ. The additions show small differences in handwriting and ink. They are printed in the probable order of their origin.\n(s Means of mathematics: to take the *medium* from many.)\n(s Preliminary judgments.)\n(s On the philosophical opinions and the hypotheses,\non the noticing of the grounds why an opinion is uncertain.)\n(s Uncertainty is of twofold kind: either where one finds in the matter itself no sufficient grounds of assent, or where, notwithstanding all one's conviction, one is apprehensive of erring from the expectation of similar cases in reckoning out.)\n(s Uncertainty is either a possibility of erring under the condition that one would judge of that for which there are no data, or a conditioned possibility to judge in that for which one has no data.)\n(s Immediately certain propositions: before one makes a concept distinct, whereby one however begins this.)\n(s Immediately certain proposition: many thinking [persons] cannot bring forth a thought.)\n(s Immediately certain proposition can only be, if without the predicate the subject cannot be thought, not if it seems one cannot deny it.)",[]],"reflm:3708":["Only rational concepts can be explained, empirical ones never. The concept of the latter serves only to designate the subject of synthetic judgments, but never for *analysis*.",[]],"reflm:3709":["*Prolegomena Metaphysicorum*.\nIn our entire cognition some cognitions must lie at the ground of others. |\nAt the ground of many concepts lie other concepts, and at the ground of many judgments other judgments. At the ground of the most common concept of a clock lies the concept of time, of a motion, and of measurement. Whoever names the word friendship supports himself on the concepts of love, of honesty, etc etc. It is the same way with judgments. Whoever says that envy is a vice, grounds himself secretly on many hidden judgments: that love of one's neighbor is a duty, that that which is opposed to a duty is a vice, that envy is a disfavor and opposed to love, etc etc. One can call the ground-concepts *notiones fundamentales*, the ground-judgments *judicia fundamentalia*. Those ground-concepts that [themselves] do not in turn presuppose others are called *notiones primitivae* (first ground-concepts) and judgments of such a kind *judicia primitiva* (first ground-judgments). But something can be regarded as a first either simply or in relation to something else. Something is a *cognitio absolute primitiva* insofar as no other at all lies at its ground; but it is *respective primitiva* insofar as it either is not within the powers of a subject to cognize its ground [of a certain rational aim not ge], or is not in conformity with a certain rational aim. In agreement with the concept of the good and evil much lies at the ground, which even the philosophers cognize clearly, but the common man certainly cannot penetrate so far into his concepts, and for him they are *notiones primitivae*. It is just so in common cognition with the concepts of space and of time. Some of these ground-concepts can be *notiones primitivae* with regard to the entire human understanding, even though *in sensu absoluto* they may be only *derivativae*, e.g. Simultaneous, being one after another, etc. These one can call *notiones* [*absolute*] *primitivas* *in sensu subjectivo*. Whether they are so also *in sensu objectivo*, the human being cannot settle. In just the same way there are *judicia in sensu subjectivo prima* with regard to some human beings, e.g. proverbs; or with regard to all, e.g. *principium contradictionis*.\nIf we now call all cognitions that lie at the ground of others *principia*, then there are *respectus* with regard to breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3710":["All (g first) principles are either formal or material. The first contain the ground how the concepts in the judgment are to be considered in relation. The second contain the *medium terminum*, by means of which they are to be considered with one another in this relation.\n1. The way in which the judgments are to be compared is either through affirmation or negation. An affirmative judgment represents that a predicate is identical with the subject (is its mark). A negating one, that the predicate contradicts the subject (is an opposite of its mark). The universal formal principle of the former can thus only be this, which indicates the essence of affirmation: *cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum* (to every subject belongs a predicate identical with it), and of the second that which contains the essence of a negation: [*cuilibet subjecto*] *nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum* (to no subject belongs a predicate opposed to it). The first is the proposition of identity, the second that of contradiction. From these principles alone nothing at all can be proved. For to prove means to judge mediately or to infer. But every syllogism is a judgment by means of an intermediate mark (*nota intermedia*, *medius terminus*,). But the named propositions contain no *medium terminum* for others. All propositions that stand immediately under these two are unprovable; all that stand mediately under them are provable.\n2. Universal judgments that contain the *medium terminum* for others are their material grounds; and, if they stand immediately under the two supreme *principiis formalibus*, they are material first principles. E.g. A body is composite, stands immediately under the proposition of identity and contains the *medius terminus* for the proposition: a body is alterable; e.g. Everything composite is alterable, a body is composite, therefore it is etc etc. By contrast the proposition: no space is without places, stands immediately under the proposition of contradiction etc:",[]],"reflm:3711":["*Nihil negativum* is entirely and completely nothing. *non A* is also nothing and in itself not thinkable, but everything else can be thought. Now were the *nihil negativum* merely the nothing of a certain concept, it would be A–A\n=0, hence one and the same with a *privation*; hence the *nihil negativum* is P–P = 0×P.",[]],"reflm:3712":["The mark of the absolutely necessary being cannot be the contingent in the existence of the other things, for then this necessity is only *hypothetica antecedentis*; thus not in this, that it is regarded as a last ground of everything that is there, but that it is a ground of everything in general, both what is there and what is possible; for since possibility in general is certainly necessary, then that which contains the ground is also so.",[]],"reflm:3713":["Onund \n*Determinatio* (determination) of a being (*entis*) (s of anything), insofar as it can be considered what is posited, is *qualitas* (quality).\n*Determinatio* of a being (s of anything), insofar as it can be considered how often something is posited, is *quantitas* (quantity).\nA one in which there is quantity is a *quantum*.\nA positing of the same made several times is a *multitudo* (multitude).\n(s absolute unity is not a *quantum*.)\nIn any *quantum* whatever there is a multitude, and that in which there is a multitude is a *quantum*.\nTo cognize a multitude distinctly by successively adding one to one is *numerare* (to number).\nA multitude distinctly cognized by numbering is a *numerus* (number).\nA *quantum* in which how often the one is posited is in itself undetermined (s which happens in that which is nothing but the mere possibility of coordination), is a *continuum*.\nA *quantum* in which how often the one is posited is in itself determined, is *discretum*.",[]],"reflm:3714":["Every *caussatum* is *in se contingens*; it is asked whether every *absolute contingens* is a *caussatum alterius*. The answer is this: the *contingentia* can be seen into only through this, that the *existentia* of a thing, without being a *caussatum*, is undetermined.",[]],"reflm:3715":["*Arbitrium* is either *liberum* or *brutum*. In the former one is conscious of the *contingentia interna* and *externa* of the action.",[]],"reflm:3716":["P. I:\nA cognition of reason that has no other *principia* than empirical concepts can only be a critique; one can understand the universal (e.g. the touching) only from the particular, and the universal rules can only be abstracted from the particular ones of practice. So it is also with the doctrine of the probable.\nTruth has objective marks; but the certainty that in every case these objective ones are to be encountered, can only have subjectively, i.e. the agreement of the cognition with itself.\nA universal concept is empirical if it cannot be understood otherwise [than] if one cognizes its *concretum*. e.g. One can get no concept of water through all definitions, [nor] than if one has seen it. Above all, all concepts of beauty, of simplicity, of the laughable are comprehensible only in their *concreto*. Hence the rules here too cannot occasion practice, but this makes the rules.\n––––––––––\nWith regard to the assessment of the moral the question is not: whether it is agreeable, but: good. We cognize therein indeed a con|nection\naccording to possibility with happiness, but it does not rest thereon. The principles of moral assessment should not be grounds of the inclination to the good, [i.e.] for this belongs to feeling. The most vicious human being, however small the moral inclination to the good may be in proportion, nonetheless holds it for the best. The *principia* of this judgment are to be established.\n––––––––––\nAll sciences and arts relate either to the culture of healthy reason or not. In the latter case there also prevails a complete lack of taste. The age of healthy reason and of the arts and sciences is only the age of honor; this is found only in republics and monarchies, thus not in the feudal constitution, where far too great an inequality prevails.\n(g The *principium* of identity and of contradiction are for reason what space and time are for the senses. Universally: the *principium* of agreement (be it of sameness or of difference). The *principium* of highest reality is parallel to the sum total of all sensations.)\nPerfection is: what constitutes the worth of the person, happiness: what constitutes the worth of the state.\n(The proposition of sufficient ground is grounded on this, that one can cancel everything in and for itself, thus, in order to posit something, something else must be posited, with which this canceling conflicts. This connection, however, is either according to laws of reason or of the senses.)\nThis proposition, however, only wants to say that nothing can be cognized according to reason without a ground.\n––––––––––\n(g If we want to posit something through reason, we need a ground. I.e. the positing a priori is through a ground, unless it were intuition.)\nIn the proposition: everything that is has a ground, lie the two: 1. something else, 2. whereby the sequel is determined, the former *dependens*, the latter *contingens*.\nP. II:\nAll ideas of metaphysics are analytic except of space, time and force.\nThe analysis rests on the propositions of identity and of contradiction. All these and other propositions, however, are grounded on this, that the understanding posits nothing absolutely, but only insofar as it is necessitated thereto through a condition either analytically or synthetically.\nThe idea of the possible. The idea of All or of a whole.\nMetaphysics is not a philosophy about the objects, for these can be given only through the senses, but about the subject, namely its laws of reason.\nWe have laws for the use of our reason a posteriori; these cannot be applied to concepts, but only to objects of experience.\nMetaphysics thus treats only the subject dogmatically, the object however with regard to synthetic judgments problematically.\nAll judgments are either logical, which enunciate the respective being, or real, which enunciate the absolute. All being in [itself] itself (position) is an existence either respective (g *hypothetice*) or *categorice*.\nUniversality is understood either synthetically and then holds only of the cases of experience of the synthesis or analytically and holds according to reason of concepts.\nTo determine the boundaries of reason belongs something positive: first, to show the domain of the cognitions of reason, and something negative: namely the limitations, finally also the quality of the boundaries, as it were the figure.\nThe cognitions are of twofold kind: either those that go to objects that are given, or: to the concepts of the form, how reason considers each object. The latter are merely subjective and can also alone have a universality of reason.\nIn synthetic judgments I represent to myself an identity for the sake of the predicate, but not the predicate for the sake of the identity.\nThe basic concepts of analysis are: possibility, impossibility, necessity, contingency, unity etc etc.; of synthesis: space, time and force.\nNot all *conceptus superiores* are *abstracti*, but only those that arise a posteriori analytically; those a priori are not *abstracti*. These *abstracti* are empirical or rational.\n===NOTES===\n- 'S.' (Seite) rendered 'P.'; the Academy sigla '(g' and the brackets are carried over unchanged; '[sich]' in 'an sich [sich] selbst' is a bracketed repetition, rendered '[itself] itself'.\n- Ausübung = practice; Beurtheilung = assessment; Urtheil = judgment; Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung = combination (not in this passage); Folge = sequel; Zustand = state.\n- Gegenstand (Gegenstände) and Object/obiect both = 'object'; Grenze = boundary, Schranke = limitation; Umfang = domain; Zufälligkeit = contingency.\n- 'kann nur subiective haben' has no stated object in the German; kept as 'can only have subjectively'. 'Aufheben/Aufhebung' rendered 'cancel/canceling' (not in glossary).\n- 'Satz des zureichenden Grundes' = 'proposition of sufficient ground' (Satz = proposition); 'Sätze' of identity and contradiction likewise.\n- 'es müste denn Anschauung seyn' rendered 'unless it were intuition'. 'tractirt' rendered 'treats'; 'enunciiren' kept as 'enunciate'; 'Qvalitaet' = quality.\n- 'Wissenschaft(en)' = science(s); 'Gesunde Vernunft' = healthy reason; 'Lehnsverfassung' = feudal constitution.",[]],"reflm:3717":["27:\nP. I:\nThe *principium* of the form of all experiences are space and time.\nThe *principium* of the form of all judgments of pure reason: identity and contradiction.\nThe *principium* of the form of all judgments of reason a posteriori: ground and force.\nComparison, derivation, taking together.\nPossible is contradistinguished: 1. from the [Wiederst], whose concept contains a conflict. 2. From the determined or true. 3. From the actual.\nIn all empirical judgments the basic formula is: [everything] In every thing that which contains the ground of a sensation is also the same subject that contains the ground of the other. The synthesis is one of taking together or of derivation.\nAll necessity and contingency that we can represent to ourselves is conditioned. The unconditioned is thought problematically. Absolutely contingent (e.g. free action) and absolutely necessary can both not be thought.\nAll relation is either of combination or of comparison or of taking together.\nAll combination (in the real sense): space, time and force.\nAll definitions are grounded on the concept of the essence; hence we can define nothing but pure reason- and arbitrary concepts.\nAll abstract cognitions have first grounds a posteriori.\nThe universal judgment that has arisen not through abstraction a posteriori and thus empirically is not abstract, but a pure judgment of reason.\nIn metaphysics the grounds of cognition must be taken a priori, although the objects a posteriori.\nIf the grounds [of that] concerning that which must be, from that |\ntaken be, what happens, then they are a posteriori. The synthesis is twofold: of the coordinated and of the subordinated.\nThe synthesis of reason or experience.\nP. II:\nBesides the proposition of sufficient ground this also holds: all analysis [is presupposes a synt] conversely includes the possibility of a synthesis. Accordingly in every series of subordinated things there must be a first, because otherwise no complete synthesis and thus also no analysis would take place.\nThe synthetic principle of the understanding is breaks off.\nThe mathematical analysis is always a philosophical synthesis, only where I think the whole before the parts; but if I think the parts before the whole, then it is a mathematical synthesis[in which]. all synthesis rests on coordination and happens through understanding. The *analysis philosophica*, however, on subordination and happens through reason. The ground is no part of the sequel, nor conversely.\nIn all images of things I coordinate nothing but immediate marks and partial representations.\nab represents the action of coordination, ac however that of subordination.\nThe soul has three dimensions. the sense and its clarity, the understanding and its form as well as matter (i.e. extensiveness) of coordination and reason, that\nis height of subordination.\nWhether it is not also so with the relations to feeling?\nBecause all negations serve only to prevent errors and to demonstrate the not knowing: so metaphysics is a very useful science, not insofar as it extends knowledge, but prevents errors. One learns that which Socrates knew.\nTaste goes to the appearances and not to the sensation, consequently to the constitution of the object, insofar as it is an object of the senses, and is thus as a cognition subject to universally valid rules.\nThe sensible is a perfection, and confusion is not essential to it, but distinctness of coordination can take place.\n–––––––––––\nMathematics shows the greatest dignity of human reason, metaphysics however the limitations and its peculiar vocation.\nIt belongs throughout to everything beautiful that I need only understanding and as little reason as possible for it; for it is to please in the appearance. Hence the coordination.\n–––––––––––\nRemark: that the rules of the beautiful be given through experience, because one can think no beautiful shape of a rational being without a human figure.\n===NOTES===\n- '[Wiederst]' is a bracketed (deleted) fragment, left in German as printed; not completed, since the reading is only a fragment. Likewise '[von dem]', '[alles]', '[ist setzt eine synt]', '[in der]' are carried over in English as bracketed.\n- 'The synthetic principle of the understanding is breaks off': German 'ist bricht ab', where 'bricht ab' is the editors' note; the text is cut off.\n- 'from that | taken be, what happens': German 'von dem | hergenommen seyn, was geschieht'; the | falls in the middle of the clause, and the awkward word order is kept.\n- 'nur da ich das Gantze vor den theilen denke': 'da' can be 'where' or 'since'; rendered 'where', ambiguity left.\n- 'ihre eigenthümliche Bestimmung': 'ihre' could refer to reason or to the limitations; Bestimmung (determination; vocation) rendered 'vocation'.\n- 'Verbindung' = combination; 'Zusammennehmung' = taking together; 'Vergleichung' = comparison; 'Ableitung' = derivation; 'Verhältnis' = relation; 'Schranken' = limitations; 'Folge' = sequel.\n- 'Gegenstand' and 'obiect' both = 'object'; 'Erkentnis' = cognition; 'Wesen' = essence in the definitions sentence, 'rational being' in the last remark; 'Wissen' = knowledge, 'Wissenschaft' = science.\n- 'reine Vernunft- und willkührliche Begriffe' is kept as a hyphenated suspension, ambiguous in attachment as in the German; 'im realverstande' rendered 'in the real sense'.",[]],"reflm:3718":["After »dunckele« (l. 32) Kant inserts:\nindeed clear, but still in part confused.",[]],"reflm:3719":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nNext to »ebenso nothwendig — doch ich muß« (l. 15-18):\nIf the real sequel were contained in the real ground and thereby posited according to the rule of identity, it would at all times be at the same time with it. All changes are therefore possible only through the real relation of the grounds to their sequels, and the logical grounds are therefore distinguished from the real ground not through the limitations of my cognition, but in themselves.",[]],"reflm:3720":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nAfter »widersprechen« (l. 38) Kant inserts:\nbut certainly conflict: namely that the one cancels the sequel of the other. The contradiction is the taking-together of two *contradictorie* i.e. *logice* opposed predicates in one subject, and its result is *nihil negativum*: a and non a at the same time. The real conflict is the combination of two real grounds in one subject, one of which cancels the sequel of the other, and its result is\n=0, which is no *nihil negativum*.",[]],"reflm:3721":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nNext to l. 25-29 (»widerstreiten — princip.«) Kant made a bracket ({) and to the left of »zwar — entweder« (l. 26-30) added:\nThat both positions can be removed does not yet make out any [contradiction out] conflict out, and a contradiction is not here at all, but the conflict is possible only through this, that besides the sequel of a=x and the sequel of b=y, when both are taken together, a third, namely 0, is still possible.",[]],"reflm:3722":["Not Possible =0P. Impossible –P. In the former I need only not think something as blitri; in the latter I must cancel the possibility. To this belongs an actual thought. But were it only the cancellation of a possibility, it would again be 0.",[]],"reflm:3723":["One must distinguish between the *possibile*, the *non possibile* and the *impossibile*. If A is thought, it is possible; if nothing besides non A (which happens in the signification of an empty term), it is not possible; if possibility is cancelled, it is impossible. Impossibility is negative possibility.",[]],"reflm:3724":["Onand \n*Possibilitas* (possibility) is *positio respectiva* (respective positing) conforming to the principle of contradiction. Truth (affirmative) is respective positing according to the principle of identity. *Existentia* (existence) is *positio absoluta* (absolute positing). From respective positing |\nto absolute the consequence is not valid. But from respective positing taken generally to absolute the consequence is valid, since, unless something were absolutely posited, no respect at all would be possible.",[]],"reflm:3725":["Absolute necessity is either logical: on account of the principle of contradiction, or real: not on account of the principle of contradiction.\nThe former is the necessity of judgments, or the necessity of the relation of predicate and subject.\nThe latter is the necessity of beings. 1. God is omnipotent. 2. God is. The latter cannot be cognized through the contradiction of the opposite (g in se). The opposite of existing is not-being. But not-being alone does not contradict itself. Existence is not a predicate, therefore its opposite is not a predicate opposed to anything.",[]],"reflm:3726":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum«:\nThere is given no simple accident, because there is given no simple action, but it is always a *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:3727":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum« and \nQuantity is either of a composite or intensively such, i.e. degree. A greater degree is not the whole of the lesser ones, but a ground, i.e. a double degree, which can be the cause of two equal degrees; but in order that in the same |\nsubject there reside grounds of an effect that equals the effects of two substances taken together, there is required 1. a ground for each effect, 2. a principle of uniting these grounds. E.G. An *intellectus* (understanding) understanding twice as much.\nWhatever is composite, because before composition whatever is given is simple, necessarily consists of simples, but not therefore is every *quantum* composite and a whole.",[]],"reflm:3728":["We can just as little think (g total) absolute contingency through reason as absolute necessity, but experience shows it in the case of what happens.",[]],"reflm:3729":["There is among the substances of the universe a *commercium* (commerce) through established harmony; this is either harmony internally established (*influxus physicus*) or externally established; the latter is either externally preestablished harmony or externally occasionally established harmony.",[]],"reflm:3730":["If each of the existing substances were necessary, no connection obtains among them, [if] i.e. if there were plainly no God, the variety of all substances would not constitute a world. If God were the creator in common of certain substances, another creator of others, [plur] more worlds [would be] would be possible, not a single one. If there is one author of all, all his caused things necessarily constitute a single whole.\nThe system through which, therefore, since several substances exist, their relation and *commercium* seem necessarily to follow, is *influxus physicus*.\nThe system through which, therefore, since all depend on one, *commercium* and universal harmony is necessary, is preestablished harmony. [In this all influx]\nThe system through which, therefore, since all depend on one, [not yet necessar] *commercium* is only possible and in itself is contingent, so that each mutual dependence is established arbitrarily, is *occasionalismus*.\nHere, however, it is to be noted that Leibniz's preestablished harmony is a system feigning for itself the contingency of the *commercium* of substances, even with one author posited, and the correspondence of one with another instituted arbitrarily. In true harmony, however, all harmonic acts occur according to a rule; in preestablished harmony and occasionalism [su] however [are] the divine act inducing harmony is nothing but singular, and the diversity of these consists only in time.",[]],"reflm:3731":["The question: why something is, presupposes the thought that it is possible that something also is not. This thought, however, [grounds itself] |\ngoes [either] either to the existence of the substances or of the relations of them. As for the latter, that kind of composition which [itself] could not be understood from the necessary existence of the substances is a proof of the [existence] contingent existence [of them] of this composition. But as for the existence of the substance itself, the question why it is grounds itself on the inner possibility of thinking its not-being.\nThere are only three kinds of concepts of things: first an absolute one from the inner determinations, second a respective one from the relation to possibility, third a relative one from that to the actuality of other things. From the first concept no absolute necessity can be proved, for each thing with all its inner predicates can be cancelled without contradiction. From the third also not, because this is only a *necessitas hypothetica conseqventiae*. Thus there remains the second, that possibility itself stands in such a relation to actuality that it includes it.\nThe absolute necessity of a thing must be able to be cognized without any determinate condition. [This condition is]\nHow do we cognize absolute possibility? Data for it must always be given to us.\nIf I say: a certain predicate is impossible for a thing, then I say: it [wiede] does not belong to it, i.e. it contradicts it, though not what I think in it, but what belongs to it. (It is asked whether I can say: it contradicts the remainder of a thing, besides its opposite). What is therefore impossible, that contradicts something; what is absolutely impossible contradicts everything, namely the contradiction rests on no particularly determinate condition.",[]],"reflm:3732":["A concept, of whose possibility nothing is established, remaining when the condition is removed under which alone its possibility may be judged, is prob|lematic.\nThus the absolute necessity of a being, the simple element of a body, an action determined by no ground.",[]],"reflm:3733":["(g The thing,) What contains the ground of the inner possibility of things is absolutely necessary. Conversely (g the thing) that is absolutely necessary contains the ground of the (g inner) possibility of things; (g for) even if without the same things were internally necessary, there would be no impossibility at all in its not being. (g Or because [a thing] the opposite of a thing cannot contradict itself, so it must conflict with some other.)\nWhat contains the ground of an inner possibility contains the ground of all. For we want to posit that something possible still remained over, then this would not agree with some possible, that is: it would contradict something possible; but what contradicts some possible is impossible. (g The necessary being thus contains the ground of all possibility.)\nIn all possibility the formal and the material are distinguished. The ground of the first is the *principium contradictionis* (principle of contradiction); of the latter some existence, which contains the ground of reality.\nAccordingly the most real being contains the ground of all possibility (s not a particular ground of the inner possibility of things and another not.) and is absolutely necessary.\nThe necessary being is all-sufficient (s the most perfect *transscendentaliter*), i.e. the most real as a ground.\nThe necessary being is a single one. For if we posit several, then each of them could be canceled, yet so that all the others remain over; but because, if something necessary is canceled |\nthe impossible arises, it would be possible that something possible would be impossible. Moreover each of them would be a sequel of the others: *regressus in infinitum curvilineus* (curvilinear regress to infinity).\nThe necessary being is a simple one. For if a whole is necessary, then the parts must be so too; but then there would be many necessary beings.\nThe world is therefore no necessary being.\nThe necessary being is the highest good, for among all the possible the *bonitas* (goodness) is a reality, without which all existence in respect of value is equal to nothing. Thus it contains the ground of everything good. But the good consists in the relation of things, (g to beings), which have cognitions and feeling, [the] and a being that on that account contains the ground of the things.\nThe necessary being has the most perfect understanding and will.\nThe necessary being is therefore a person, which contains the ground of all existence through understanding and will. I.e. it is a God.",[]],"reflm:3734":["*Vnicitas* (uniqueness) follows either from the highest reality or from absolute necessity.\nEither necessary existence is concluded from the notion of the most real, or the greatest reality from the absolute necessity of existing.\n[Maxima] Each principle is either taken from experience or from mere notions. The former obtains if 1. of the author of the world the great perfection and then absolute necessity is concluded, 2. if it is concluded breaks off?\n. . . . . .\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . concludes\nEither from contingent existence to the necessary or from perfect existence to the most perfect.",[]],"reflm:3735":["logical necessity,\nreal necessity.\n(s impossibility.\nin the logical understanding,\nin the real understanding.)",[]],"reflm:3736":["On the column title of M 333: »Exsistentia Dei«:\nFirst of all the question offers itself to the inquiry: what is the ground of absolutely necessary existence, or in what manner the opposite of the existence of something can be absolutely necessary.\n1. The existence of contingent things as caused does not constitute the principle of absolute necessity; but rather this is the ultimate ground of the possibility of the existence of several contingent things.\n2. No other existence at all contains the ground of absolute necessity, for about contingent things it has already been said, about the condition of necessary existence there is now the question.\n3. Therefore the proper mark (*nota*) of necessary existence will have to be found in its relation (*respectus*) to possibility, which likewise is necessary.\n4. Impossibility consists in contradiction; [will be] it is namely properly, if [nege] you posit what was to be negated, or vice versa. e.g. It is impossible that a triangle be [parallelo] quadrilateral. Which also sounds thus: the triangle is not quadrilateral, which expresses a contradiction. For let |\nA be non-B; then A, which is B, would be B non-B, which is impossible and contradictory.\n5. The opposite of no existence contradicts itself. Contradiction is between subject and predicate, existence is not a predicate, therefore non-existence contradicts no subject in itself.",[]],"reflm:3737":["It is the question whether the power of God is greater than his counsel and, since the former goes to all [*in nexu* possible] things, whether the latter does not also go to them; in this case everything possible is actual. But if it seems that much possible is not actual, then we represent to ourselves as possible that of which the conditions are nevertheless impossible.",[]],"reflm:3738":["All analytic judgments teach what is in the concepts, but thought confusedly; the synthetic, what is supposed to be thought as combined with the concept. In all judgments the concept of the subject is something (g a), that I think in the object x, and the predicate is regarded as a mark of a in the [synthetic] analytic judgments or of x in the synthetic.\n|–a—|—x——|\nall analytic judgments are rational and conversely. all synthetic judgments are empirical and conversely. The first rational principles (*principia rationalia prima*) of matter [or prior ones of form] are [iud] elementary principles; synthetic principles, if they were at the same time rational, would be called axioms; but, since such are not given, the analogues of the rational in mathematics are so called. In philosophy there are given no synthetic principles except *a posteriori*, i.e. empirically, and analytic principles *a priori*, i.e. elementary propositions, both material.\nI can compare notions, thinking their relation, either according to the rule of the understanding: empirically (g and synthetically), or of reason: rationally and analytically, or according to the rules of the analogue of reason, i.e. |\nof imagination and wit. Crusius ascribes many of his principles to this.\nLocke seems to have scented out the distinction of synthetic and analytic judgments in his inquiry concerning man.",[]],"reflm:3739":["If I deny something of a subject, it happens in accordance with the principle of contradiction. This negation together with the affirmation is impossible.",[]],"reflm:3740":["Something opposed to nothing\n{\nnegative, privative or negative.",[]],"reflm:3741":["Material principles of judgments are notions\nformal ones\nthe principle of identity\nor of contradiction\nof inferences of reason the material principles are notions\nformal ones\n*dictum de omni et nullo* (the saying about all and none)\n(s The logical principle of judgments. The real principle of concepts.)",[]],"reflm:3742":["(g There are material, there are also formal basic concepts.)\nAnalytic, synthetic principles (*principia analytica, synthetica*).",[]],"reflm:3743":["All mathematical concepts are synthetic.",[]],"reflm:3744":["There are synthetic propositions from experience, thus *principia prima synthetica* (first synthetic principles); of the like kind are also (g the definitions of mathematics) the axioms of mathematics about space.\n*Principia rationalia* (rational principles) can not at all be synthetic.\nAll empirical propositions are synthetic and conversely.\nAll rational propositions are analytic.",[]],"reflm:3745":["From the principle of identity and of contradiction there follow not only propositions, but necessary ones.",[]],"reflm:3746":["The *principia formalia* (formal principles) are only the first grounds of analytic or rational judgments.",[]],"reflm:3747":["All *principia* of human cognition are either formal or material (*vel formalia vel materialia*). Those contain merely the relation of the concepts in the judgments: either the logical or metaphysical. These of the things and are synthetic. (s Either of the analytic and are called logical and are valid for every understanding, are objective (only not conversely); or of the synthetic and are called real, and, because they are stated universally without senses, they are either principles of the form of the understanding or of sensibility [and are valid in both ca]; in the first case they are merely subjective laws. In the second they are objective only under the hypothesis of sensibility; but if the hypothesis already lies in the subject, they are objective.)",[]],"reflm:3748":["Metaphysical basic concepts. Elementary concepts. Systematic basic concepts.",[]],"reflm:3749":["One can connect concepts with one another, in order thereby to make a greater concept out of them (synthetic); or one can think concepts as connected with one another, in order to cognize that which is in them.\nThe concepts of causes are synthetic and thus empirical.",[]],"reflm:3750":["All *principia primitiva* (primitive principles) are either elementary propositions and ana|lytic\nor *axiomata* (axioms) and are synthetic. Difference of an analytic and a synthetic proposition in general. The rational are analytic, the empirical synthetic, likewise the mathematical.",[]],"reflm:3751":["Not positing and canceling is different.\nOf predicates contradictorily opposed, to posit each of the two at once is impossible through the principle of contradiction.\nOf predicates contradictorily opposed, to cancel each of the two is impossible through the principle of the excluded middle (*principium exclusi medii*).\nOf predicates contradictorily opposed, to think neither of something is possible: rule of abstract notions, which are indeterminate; that the latter is possible is evident from this, that, if they are not material, there is no contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3752":["[*respectus*]\nWhoever thinks a ground also thinks the sequel and conversely: it is a concept of *respectus* (relation). Since no simpler *respectus* can be thought, the concept of it is also inexplicable. One can nevertheless prevent through a judgment that ground and sequels not be regarded as entirely one and the same: so that it is said: *posita ratione ponitur aliud* (the ground being posited, something else is posited); or: *ratio et rationatum sunt diversa* (the ground and what is grounded are different).",[]],"reflm:3753":["The ground (*ratio*) is either of positing or of canceling.\n(g The relation (*respectus*) is either logical: of concepts (of identity),\nor real: of things (really diverse).)\nThe relation of a positing ground is *nexus*, of a canceling one is *oppositio* (opposition).\nThe relation of a logical ground, either of positing or of canceling, is analytic — rational;\nThe relation of a real ground, either of positing or of canceling, is synthetic — empirical.",[]],"reflm:3754":["A relation (*respectus*) is either of *nexus* or of opposition, each either logical or real.\nThe former on account of identity [post] or contradiction, the latter not through them.\n(s Logical ground and what is grounded (*rationatum*) are always simultaneous, real ones after one another.)\n(In logical opposition, with one posited the other is cancelled.)\n(In real opposition, with one posited what is grounded of the other is cancelled.)\n(s In the former the *nexus* is cognizable analytically, in the latter synthetically. The former rationally, the latter empirically.)\nIn the former one or the other is a negation;\nin the latter each of the two is something positive.\nIn the former (s with both posited) what is grounded is *nihil negativum* (s or impossible);\nin the latter (s with both posited) what is grounded is *nihil privativum* =0.",[]],"reflm:3755":["The relation of the logical ground to the sequel is a judgment.\nThe relation of the real ground is a concept.\nVarious [Grundb] primitive concepts of real grounds.\nThe possibility of a real connection in primitive grounds cannot be seen rationally.",[]],"reflm:3756":["The logical of possibility is contradistinguished from truth, the real of possibility from the possibility of actuality.\nThe relation of identity and contradiction thought negatively, i.e. since one concept is not identical with the other, also does not conflict with it, is the logical or formal of mere possibility, the real of possibility is at the same time the material of the same, and our concept of the same extends as far as the simple of our sensations, likewise as far as the primitive relations (*respectus reales*) that we come to know through experience.\nThe logical of possibility is a relation of comparison, the logical of truth a relation of connection.\nThe cognition of the logical relation is rational, the [zweyte] real empirical.\nGround and sequel are at all times different, but either (g only) logically, where the sequel is only a partial concept of the ground (consequently where it is wholly one and the same with it, it is also no sequel), or really, where it is not thought as belonging to the ground as a partial concept, but as wholly different from it.\nAll connection of the logical ground with the sequel is an affirmative judgment, in which the predicate is the sequel and the subject is the ground. In real connection this is never so; although the sequel can be a predicate of the cause, yet none of the ground is in it. Logical connection and conflict can be cognized analytically and thus rationally, the real not otherwise than empirically. Since everything empirical teaches only what customarily tends to be with or after one another (except in the properties of space), the question arises whether we |\nin this counter-relation do not distinguish the ground from the sequel through time.",[]],"reflm:3757":["That whose position is necessarily (according to universal laws) [etwa] combined with the position of something else is called its ground.",[]],"reflm:3758":["The possible (*possibile*), insofar as it is what is grounded (*rationatum*), is connected *a priori*; insofar as it is ground (*ratio*), is connected *a posteriori*. What is neither ground nor what is grounded is plainly unconnected. The series of all possibles subordinated to one another has a terminus *a priori* and *a posteriori*, and cannot [esse] again be connected *osse* with others.",[]],"reflm:3759":["Several connected things make a series. A series of coordinated [connexorum] things can be contained under the idea of all, a series of subordinated things not likewise. The little word *simul* (simultaneously) embraces everything.\nThe first grounds in the series of coordinated parts of a body are simple.",[]],"reflm:3760":["reality and negation are distinctions between what is absolutely or meta|physically\nposited or negative and that which is said to be such only logically, in which there is nothing but a relation either of position or of negation.",[]],"reflm:3761":["A universal and merely possible concept is not determined *omnimode*. But a single concept is, and yet can be merely possible.\nExistence cannot be a predicate, for otherwise a thing as existing would be cognized only through a judgment and by means of the understanding. But thus we cognize the existence of things through sensation.",[]],"reflm:3762":["The respective position of the predicates of a being toward one another in conformity with the principle of contradiction contains less in itself than their absolute position at the same time; therefore from the former to the latter the consequence does not hold.",[]],"reflm:3763":["Fictions can be possible [in sensu absolu]; but if one occupies oneself merely with their possibility, then an ideal way of thinking arises from it.",[]],"reflm:3764":["(s In what there is quantity, there is a quantum.)\nQuantity is the position of the same made several times. It is pure: in which what has been posited is not asked at all, but is something in general, as in arithmetic. Quantity is *in concreto*: in which what has been posited is determined, i.e. in which some quality is given for one.",[]],"reflm:3765":["(s Unity is conditioned or unconditioned.)\nOne perhaps only wanted to say through the 4 sections that the concepts of the one, the true, the perfect can be applied to all things in general.\nEvery thing is not many: signifies transcendental unity.\nEvery thing contains much that is nevertheless one and the same with one*: the metaphysical (g can be greater or lesser unity).\nEvery thing is true, i.e.: it cannot be denied among all possible concepts and agrees with other things: transcendental.\nEvery thing contains truths: metaphysical, the *principium* of the elementary propositions. (g some things contain indeed possibility, but not enough metaphysical truth, i.e. conditioned possibility.)\nEvery thing is perfect in what it is: transcendental.\nEvery one has reality: metaphysical, it is comparatively perfect.\n[Das] The absolute unity, truth and perfection consists in the possibility that comprises all-sufficiency in itself, the possibility in every respect and all reality.\n*(s in the circle there is more unity than in the polygon. The unity of the conic sections.)",[]],"reflm:3766":["Unity is either absolute (g categorical) (s numerical), which is opposed to multitude, or hypothetical, insofar as it belongs to several things conjoined\n|\nunity either by coordinating the whole or by subordinating: unity of the ground and also unity of what is grounded (in machines, in the aim).",[]],"reflm:3767":["All necessity is either logical or real. The former is cognized rationally, the latter empirically. The opposite of the latter is called impossible for the reason that it never happens; the opposite of the former never happens for the reason that it is impossible.",[]],"reflm:3768":["That whose opposite is possible cannot for that reason be called contingent, if by the opposite is understood something other, which is non-A. For [loco] besides the substance whose determination is A, there can be another, which is determined through non-A; e.g. thinking is not contingent to man for the reason that another can also be without thinking; [aut] at least this contingency is only logical, not real, because in this man it is perhaps necessary. Nor is a substance contingent whose determinations can succeed one another.",[]],"reflm:3769":["Opposite the numeral of M § 109:\nThe possibility without existence of the same thing is not one and the same with the possibility of its non-being. I can represent to myself comets that are not; but to represent to oneself that the sun is not could be hypothetically impossible.",[]],"reflm:3770":["The (g absolute) possibility can be cognized only from the *nexus* with everything possible. e.g. whether in a triangle the square of one side can be equal to that of the two others cannot be cognized from a triangle |\nin general, but from its concept connected with the right angle.",[]],"reflm:3771":["A thing exists necessarily whose opposite of existence is impossible. Yet for that reason the opposite of its determinations is possible. For one does say of alterable things that the same things successively have all sorts of determinations. If the opposite of the determinations were at the same time the opposite of the thing, then no thing would be alterable. Indeed, also (g in) an altered thing these alterations themselves can be necessary, for the logical opposite of a determination consists in this, that instead of A in the same time non A takes place; but if [statt] non A follows upon A, then this is [potentialiter] *disparatum*, *non oppositum*.\nThe change of a thing or alteration is what agrees very well [mit] together and where both necessarily follow upon one another. Only the thing must not cease to be, because then there would also be no succession, consequently it is the real opposite.",[]],"reflm:3772":["Reality is (g its concept is) το *esse*;\nNegation is\nτο *non esse*.",[]],"reflm:3773":["Negation is a predicate. A being (*ens*) is the subject. A merely negative being would be something in which there is nothing.",[]],"reflm:3774":["Opposite and \nReality is either relatively positing or relatively cancelling. Diversity is only from relation.\nThe negation of one of the opposites is the position of the other.",[]],"reflm:3775":["A being (s transcendentally) that is maximal is the most real, and generally, that in which there is not synthetic or extensive quantity, but intensive; for in the former the synthesis is never terminated. (s every minimum or maximum is such only relatively. e.g. diameter, periphery with respect to the area.) (s The comparative quantity of the universe: universality.)",[]],"reflm:3776":["Opposite and \nConcepts of the (g absolute) maximum and minimum in mathematical (g and ideal) synthesis are deceptive concepts; but in the real synthesis of qualities they are possible and are equated with allness, but in repeating the same no absolute allness is given.",[]],"reflm:3777":["several things in a being are either to be counted or to be weighed.\nthe minimum of ground is the moment.",[]],"reflm:3778":["negative determinations are not accidents.",[]],"reflm:3779":["The empirical concept of substance is identity amid all varieties.",[]],"reflm:3780":["inherence is not an accident.",[]],"reflm:3781":["In any accident there is regarded, first, the relation of real dependence on the [subject] substance, second, the logical relation as of a predicate toward the subject. If it is only the former, what is grounded does not for that reason inhere in the ground, as Spinoza wrongly maintained of the world in relation to God. If it is only the latter, the subject will be only logical, not a substance, and the predicate a determination, not an accident.",[]],"reflm:3782":["All properties that the poets personify are *phaenomena substantiata*.\n===NOTES===\n- *Eigenschaften* = properties (not in the glossary; *Beschaffenheit* is the glossary's 'property').\n- *phaenomena substantiata* left in Latin.",[]],"reflm:3783":["The *accidentia* are not particular things that inhere in the *subiect*, but *praedicate* of a *subiect*, i.e. ways how the subiect exists. The concept of inherence is a logical expedient, because there one can represent all things in the form of the predicates.\n===NOTES===\n- *Arten* = ways; *Hülfsmittel* = expedient (literally an auxiliary means; *Mittel* in the glossary = means).\n- *Begriff* = concept; *inhaerentz* rendered 'inherence'; *subiect*, *praedicate* are Kant's Latinate forms in German text.\n- *vorstellen* = represent.",[]],"reflm:3784":["*Utrum* [s] *accidens* be something additional to substance (g as color) and properly inhere in it, or only the positive form of the substance itself and its actual determination? E.g. motion inhering in a body.\n===NOTES===\n- *accessorium* = something additional; *forma*, *determinatio* (glossary: determination) kept close to the Latin.\n- 'ct' in 'positiva ct actualis' is printed as given; probably 'et' (doubtful reading), translated 'and'.\n- [s] is a fragmentary deletion, carried over untranslated.",[]],"reflm:3785":["Three things are to be thought: substance, accident, the *respectus* of each. This, however, is either of the accident and [is] inherence; or of the substance and [is] this again either of the potential ground and is force, or of the actuating and is action.\n===NOTES===\n- *rationis* = ground (*Grund*); *vis* = force; *actio* = action.\n- 'of the actuating' (*actuantis*) has no noun supplied; it is left open whether substance or ground is understood.\n- *respectus* left in Latin.",[]],"reflm:3786":["Just as in logic subject, predicate and *respectus* are distinguished, and hence [predicate] the *respectus* is not a predicate, so too in real matters. Force is not an accident, but a *respectus* of the substance as of the ground [resp] toward the accidents.\n===NOTES===\n- *rationis* = ground; *vis* = force; *realibus* = real matters (literally 'real things').\n- [resp] is a fragmentary deletion, carried over untranslated.",[]],"reflm:3787":["The *respectus* of a *pars* and of its *complementum* *ad totum* must [mutually] be *mutuus* and *homogeneus*; thus a sequel cannot be a part of the ground and belong with the ground as a part to the same whole. The thought is not a part of the human being, but his sequel.\n===NOTES===\n- *Folge* = sequel (glossary); *Grund* = ground; *Theil* = part.\n- *Gehören* is capitalized in the source.",[]],"reflm:3788":["The mode of composition is not the essence of the composite, but of the composition.\nComposition is form. The parts, matter.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin passage: *modus compositionis*, *essentia*, *compositio*, *forma*, *partes*, *materia* rendered by their English cognates.",[]],"reflm:3789":["The question is whether in (g in) a *compositum substantiale* there is no substance, but only substances, and whether only the plural of it is possible. A *totum syntheticum* is one whose [The] composition is grounded, as to possibility, on the parts, which can be thought even without [these] all composition. A *totum analyticum* is one whose parts, as to their possibility, already presuppose the composition in the whole. *Spatium* and *tempus* are *tota analytica*, the bodies *synthetica*. A *compositum ex substantiis* is a *totum syntheticum*. A *totum analyticum* is neither a *compositum* of substances nor of accidents, but a *totum* of possible relations.\n===NOTES===\n- *Zusammensetzung* = composition; *Theile* = parts; *möglichkeit* = possibility; *gründet* = is grounded.\n- 'which can be thought even without [these] all composition' follows the German 'ohne [diese] alle zusammensetzung'; the construction is kept as it stands.\n- [The] is a fragmentary deletion, carried over as printed.",[]],"reflm:3790":["Either space contains the ground of the possibility of the compresence of many substances and their relations, or these contain the ground of the possibility of space.\n===NOTES===\n- *compraesentz* = compresence; *Grund* = ground; *Möglichkeit* = possibility.\n- 'these' (*diese*) could refer to substances or to relations (or both); left ambiguous as in the German.",[]],"reflm:3791":["Division is either logical, insofar as various things are regarded as contained under some higher notion. Mathematical, insofar as several are distinguished as parts of the same whole (ideal division). Physical, insofar as it is a matter of parts in such a way that the real nexus, through which they constitute the same whole, is removed (real division).\n(s A *monas* is not mathematically divisible, since the several discernible things in it are not parts, but *rationata*.\nLogical division is distinguished from analysis; the latter is division of the notion [pr] and metaphysical; the former division of the sphere of a notion, and, the less a notion is metaphysically indivisible, the greater the sphere.)\n===NOTES===\n- *notio* = notion; *nexus* kept; *rationata* left in Latin (things grounded).\n- [pr] is a fragmentary deletion, carried over untranslated.\n- *prior*/*posterior* rendered 'the former'/'the latter'; the order of the two members is as in the Latin.",[]],"reflm:3792":["A *momentum* is an element of the ground. Therefore the *momentum* of free descent on an inclined plane is different. *Momenta* in the fact.\n===NOTES===\n- *rationis* = ground; *in facto* rendered 'in the fact' (the phrase is terse; sense uncertain).\n- *momentum* left in Latin.",[]],"reflm:3793":["(s The quantity of one as a whole is regarded extensively.\nThe quantity of one as ground is regarded intensively.)\n[Die W] There is in a kettle full of boiling water more warmth than in a spoonful of it, but not greater. Two donkeys pull a wagon with more speed, but not with greater.\nIf many individuals cannot be so combined that they give a greater degree — — breaks off.\nMore virtue, greater virtue; more welfare, greater welfare.\n===NOTES===\n- *mehr* / *größer* = more / greater; *Grad* = degree; *verbunden* = combined; *Wärme* = warmth; *Geschwindigkeit* = speed.\n- 'breaks off' (*bricht ab*) is the editors' remark, carried over; the sentence is incomplete.\n- [Die W] is a fragmentary deletion.",[]],"reflm:3794":["Infinity relates to the absence of an end (*limitis*), either in adding: and then it is the greatest, or in |\ntaking away: and then it is not for that reason the greatest. The former is, as it were, like a line to which none can be added. The second like one from which a certain line can never be so taken away that it would hit the end.\n===NOTES===\n- *Unendlichkeit* = infinity; *Ende* = end; *limitis* is Kant's Latin gloss.\n- 'a certain line' (*eine Gewisse Linie*) is as in the German; 'it' in 'hit the end' (*sie*) may refer to either line; left ambiguous.",[]],"reflm:3795":["It is not yet established whether the greatest reality in a being is compossible simultaneously or in a successive series.\n===NOTES===\n- *realitas* = reality; *compossibilis* = compossible; *series successiva* = successive series (*Abfolge*/succession not used since the German is absent).",[]],"reflm:3796":["The extended, impenetrable, inert is matter.\n===NOTES===\n- *Extensum impenetrabile iners* is neuter adjectives; the English leaves them as substantives.",[]],"reflm:3797":["Opposite and\nThe existence of a being, upon which all duration follows, is the beginning.\nThe existence of a being, which follows upon all duration, is the end.\nBeginning and end are the bounds of duration, the one *a parte ante*, the other *a parte post*. Beginning and end are *instantia* (instants).\n===NOTES===\n- 'Opposite and' (*Gegenüber und*) is a fragment of uncertain connection; carried over as it stands.\n- *termini* = bounds (glossary *Grenze*); *instantia* left in Latin, plural of the neuter noun.\n- *succedit* = follows upon (*Folge*/sequel not used since the German is absent).",[]],"reflm:3798":["Useful is what is mediately agreeable, i.e. is a |\nmeans to happiness; for the pleasures are the elements of happiness, perfection consists of others.\n===NOTES===\n- *Glükseeligkeit* = happiness; *Vergnügen* = pleasures; *Vollkommenheit* = perfection; *Mittel* = means.\n- 'consists of others' (*besteht aus andern*) is left as ambiguous as the German.",[]],"reflm:3799":["If the concept of the world signified the whole of all possible things, namely those that are possible in interconnection with the all-sufficient ground, it would be more fruitful.\nOn cosmology. 1. *commercium* of the substances.\n===NOTES===\n- *Begrif* = concept; *bedeutete* = signified; *Zusammenhang* = interconnection; *allgnugsamen* = all-sufficient (the glossary's *zureichend* = sufficient is not the word used).\n- *gantze* = whole; *Möglichen Dinge* = possible things.",[]],"reflm:3800":["The question is twofold: whether to a given world another* can be *surrogari*, i.e. substituted, or whether to a given world another may be added. The former is permitted through contingency (unless it is supposed that it must have a totality simply such); the latter is not permitted because they would be joined in time, either in the same [or] part of it or in different ones.\n*(s *alius* is either *per oppositionem* or *per additionem*.)\n===NOTES===\n- *totalitas* = totality (glossary *Allheit*); *contingentia* = contingency; *surrogari* kept in Latin.\n- [vel] (here '[or]') is a deletion, carried over.\n- The asterisk is Kant's own footnote sign.",[]],"reflm:3801":["121. E II 1736.\nM 121':\nThe mathematical law of continuity is: Space and time are continua.\nThe physical law of continuity is: just as between any points placed outside one another in a simultaneous series [there is space] there lies a continuous series of points, so also between two states succeeding one another there lies a continuous series of states, [just as] and in diversity a continuous series of degrees of diversity. E.g. a motion's direction is not changed except through the continuous interposition of intervening degrees of directions. Thus perhaps also not the state of mind.\nThe cosmological law of continuity is: Between any different species there lie all possible intermediate degrees, and there is a continuous series of degrees of diversity.\nLogical law of continuity.\n(s No sequence of two states is immediate, otherwise a moment would be common to them: *simultaneorum oppositorum contradictio*.)\n===NOTES===\n- *status* = state (*Zustand*); *species* = species; *gradus* = degrees; *Augenblik* = moment.\n- 'Motus non mutatur directio' is grammatically irregular in the Latin; rendered 'a motion's direction is not changed'.\n- 'Logical law of continuity.' stands without predicate; nothing is supplied.\n- The sign E II 1736 / M 121' are Academy apparatus, carried over.",[]],"reflm:3802":["Cause and caused do not belong to the same whole, because then the cause would suffer externally from itself; therefore the cause of the world is an extramundane being.\n===NOTES===\n- *causatum* = caused (literally 'the caused'); *ens* = being; *extramundanum* = extramundane.\n- *pateretur* rendered 'would suffer'.",[]],"reflm:3803":["Every Spinozist is an egoist. The question is whether every egoist is necessarily a Spinozist.\n===NOTES===\n- *spinozista*, *egoista* rendered with their English cognates.",[]],"reflm:3804":["The absolute perfection of the world does not consist in the multitude, order, variety of substances, but in the greatest [sense] and purest sense of pleasure. Hence the rules of perfection of the so-called physico-mechanical sort do not collide with the pneumatic ones, since in those no perfection inheres.\n===NOTES===\n- *perfectio* = perfection; *multitudo* = multitude; *sensus* = sense; *voluptas* = pleasure.\n- *illis* ('in those') could refer to the physico-mechanical or the pneumatic rules; the Latin is ambiguous and left so.",[]],"reflm:3805":["Rules of the perfection of the world of spirits and the natural never stand in collision, for the latter has no absolute perfection.\n===NOTES===\n- *Vollkommenheit* = perfection; *Geisterwelt* = world of spirits; *die letzte* = 'the latter', ambiguous in the same way as the German as to whether it means the natural world.",[]],"reflm:3806":[") (ι1?) M 147'. E II 768.\nThe distinction between the *influxus idealis* and *realis* makes no different system, but is merely a correctly determined concept of *influxus* in general. But because all influxus is ideal, it can be concluded from this that it becomes possible to think a *harmonia* of substances in their self-activity in no other way than insofar as they depend on one.*\nSpace and time are the first relations which all things thereby get, and the first grounds of the possibility of a whole of the world.\n(g The theory of the *influxus idealis* is not a [ground of a] particular system, but a ground of the abolition of the *influxus physicus*. Then [still the possibility] must be shown how a *harmonia* under the condition of *influxus realis* is possible. Then the possibility [of an influxus] of a *harmonia praestabilita* or rather its necessity must be shown.)\n===NOTES===\n- *Selbstthätigkeit* = self-activity; *Weltganzen* = whole of the world; *Beziehungen* = relations; *Begrif* = concept.\n- The asterisk after 'one' is in the source; no footnote text is given in the passage.\n- ') (ι1?)' is an apparatus sign of doubtful reading, carried over unchanged.",[]],"reflm:3807":["Rational beings in other worlds (s theurgy). *spiritus astrales*. *angeli*. Influence on the spirit of man. Citation of departed souls or still living ones, haunting.\nPoltergeists. (s idol. Fetish. Talisman. Amulet. sympathy.)\nRiebezahl. (g kobold.) *incubi*, *succubi*. Delrio.\nChangeling. Vampire. (s Subterranean beings.)\nWater-nixies. Mountain manikins. Ghost-seeing.\nCitation. *Pactum*. *Magia*. *obsessio*. Exorcism. Ventriloquist.\n===NOTES===\n- *Citation* (twice) kept as the literal cognate; it may mean 'summoning'.\n- *Geist* = spirit (here 'spirit of man'); *Geistersehen* = ghost-seeing.\n- *Bauchredner* = ventriloquist; *Wechselbalg* = changeling; *Riebezahl*, *Delrio* are proper names.",[]],"reflm:3808":["The primordial being (The [first] independent), highest, supreme When. (Ground.) The being of all beings (of all). The supreme ground of everything.\nThat it is no sequel, consequently a first,\nThat it is a (g first) ground,\nThat it is a ground of everything.\n===NOTES===\n- *Urwesen* = primordial being; *Wesen* = being; *Folge* = sequel; *Grund* = ground.\n- 'When' (*Wenn*) is printed as given and is doubtful (perhaps a misreading); nothing is emended.",[]],"reflm:3809":["The possibility of a thing is twofold: either 1, that what is there be combined in a certain way according to laws which already obtain in them by virtue of their own properties. e.g. If wood is there, a house of wood is possible, or also that from forces an effect flow. 2, The possibility where nothing is there; because then no matter is there for anything, so too the concept of no thing can arise. Thus the concept of possibility is indeed logice before actuality according to given concepts, but realiter it does not precede everything actual. Thus [that] is a being, which contains the matter for all possibility and with which the possibility is to be distinguished from actuality, just as absurd as, if one takes space for an object, one cannot say it is possible.\n===NOTES===\n- *Möglichkeit* = possibility; *Wirklichkeit* = actuality; *Materie* = matter; *Gesetze* = laws; *Kräften* = forces; *Eigenschaften* = properties.\n- The last sentence ('Thus ... is a being ... just as absurd as ...') is syntactically incomplete in the German and is kept so.\n- *Gegenstand/obiect* here is 'obiect' = object.",[]],"reflm:3810":["Possibility is seen logically only through [pr] judgments, in which there is no connection, also no conflict.\n===NOTES===\n- *eingesehen* = seen (into); *Urtheile* = judgments; *Verknüpfung* = connection; *wiederstreit* = conflict.\n- 'no connection, also no conflict' follows the German literally; [pr] is a fragmentary deletion.",[]],"reflm:3811":["All negations are limitations. Limitations in general are possible only through the unlimited. Accordingly the unlimited is that where|by\neverything else is possible. The possibility of things *ascendendo* occurs through comparison, *descendendo* it is seen through derivation.\n===NOTES===\n- *Schranken* = limitations; *Unbeschränkte* = the unlimited; *Möglichkeit* = possibility; *Vergleichung* = comparison; *ableitung* = derivation.\n- *wo|durch* is split by the page/line sign; carried over as 'where|by'.",[]],"reflm:3812":["In the Wolffian proof one can indeed cognize the independence, but not the (g inner) necessity; for his existence is necessary only for the sake of the world.\nFrom the necessity he infers the *omnitudinem realitatum*. — — If that is correct, [that] then he would not have needed to substruct an experience.\nIt is strange that one wants to prove the existence of an absolutely necessary being from something other than from the necessary. For if one understands what absolutely necessary signifies, and cognizes that the concept is something, then the proof is done. If one does not know this breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3813":["It is to be proved, 1. that an absolutely necessary being is possible. 2. That it is *realissimum*. The absolute necessity of the thing must be derived from concepts [and not from] and not from the relation with other existing things.\nIt does not consist in this, that the opposite contradicts itself.\nNo opposite of existence contradicts itself; only of propositions.\nIt must therefore be necessary in connection with the possible. Not as a sequel, but as a ground.\nPossibility is a *respective* *position*. Much can indeed be posited according to possibility, even though it is not posited absolutely; but without that anything at all exists, there is no possibility.\n(g All simple possibilities contain the material ground of possibility; their possibility does not rest on the principle of contradiction.)",[]],"reflm:3814":["Opposite the number of M § 815:\nAll judgments are logical or real. The latter are of existence and, if they concern absolute necessity, cannot be cognized through the principle of contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3815":["God contains the material ground of all possibility, and herein lies at the same time, according to the *principio contradictionis*, the formal ground of the same.\nThat God contains the ground of the being of things is no *perfectio operativa*, but *qviescens*.\nThe divine all-sufficiency is either *omnisufficientia interna vel externa*.",[]],"reflm:3816":["God is [first] represented as the being of the highest perfection, as a ground, namely as a ground of all perfections in the ontological, second in the cosmotheological sense.",[]],"reflm:3817":["According to the analogy of empirical concepts breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3818":["analogy of the one born blind with regard to colors and tones. The cognition of the attributes of God with regard to the moral is dogmatic and positive, with regard to the theoretical it is [analo] critical and negative. That one not err is the foremost. Thus the theoretical cognition that we need is very simple.",[]],"reflm:3819":["Moral attributes. The goodness of the creator, The holiness of the lawgiver, The justice of the judge. } }\n}All-sufficiency of duration, of power, of [omniscience according to] cognition according to. Eternity, omnipotence, omniscience.",[]],"reflm:3820":["That there is an original being.\nwhether there are more original beings?",[]],"reflm:3821":["That there can be only one most perfect being is clear.",[]],"reflm:3822":["The highest cause is the author of the world through freedom.\nThe world is a being from another, but not from several, but from one; not through nature, but through freedom; therefore the cause of the world is author and God; but that God is at the same time *omnisufficiens* and unique cannot be concluded from this.",[]],"reflm:3823":["Whether there are comparative gods, whose right with respect to human beings is absolute and the subjection of human beings natural. This is denied. For there is not given |\nnatural subjection, except under the will of him who cannot be obligated, therefore not under a creature.",[]],"reflm:3824":["Independence and omnisufficiency are the two *principia cognoscitiva* for determining the attributes of God.",[]],"reflm:3825":["*Cognitio prototypa*: cause of things.\n*Cognitio ectypa*: the caused.",[]],"reflm:3826":["God cognizes everything in that he cognizes himself.\nThe human being cognizes himself in that he cognizes other things.",[]],"reflm:3827":["One could ask whether all will also presupposes a representation of the good in the object and whether he who wills the highest good could not will still more than is good; the last will. If the ground of willing does not need a *causam moventem* in the object, but rests in the nature of the subject, then breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3828":["The motive of God is only analogically related to ours; no ground of his action is in the object; rather, because he |\nconsiders himself as the cause and as the most perfect cause, he is the author of the best.",[]],"reflm:3829":["The concepts of substances, insofar as they are abstracted, are only respective: hence bodies are substances insofar as they are the subjects of the inherence of their accidents and inhere in no other known subject; but they would not be called substances insofar as they in turn have another subject [be], of which they are merely the effects.",[]],"reflm:3830":["That will with which a small (g cognized) good agrees more than a greater one is a worse will. The proof is conducted here *a posteriori* that no better world is possible. It would have to be the case [that] of the infinite series of better worlds that no best will be possible. Indeed, every possible will would be infinitely evil. It is the proof of optimism under the hypothesis: there is a God, more evident than even the proof of the divine existence. Indeed, it is still more necessary, or rather one needs the idea of God only for that reason. Only the idea of the *maximum* can teach me what I have to expect from God.\nIt is asked whether not even only a single world is possible.\n(s One cannot represent to oneself any existence at all (in agreement with reason) which would be equally possible with non-being. This is also to be regarded as a difficulty for the rational concept of freedom.)",[]],"reflm:3831":["Whether it is possible to create a spontaneous substance or one of a given degree of spontaneity.",[]],"reflm:3832":["Right at the top of the page:\nOne will sooner say: infinite space is in God, than: God is in infinite space.",[]],"reflm:3833":["Naturalism is either of method or dogmatic naturalism; the latter is either of conservation or of origin, and this is atheism. Naturalism of method is either propaedeutic or universal; the former is Epicurus's and quite rational, the latter is atheism, negatively such.",[]],"reflm:3834":["Synthesis is either philosophical or mathematical and just so also analysis; philosophical synthesis is the combination through a ground.",[]],"reflm:3835":["Nothing according to the concept or according to existence.",[]],"reflm:3836":["On the inner margin\nMaterials and form belong to impossibility, not to the non-possible.",[]],"reflm:3837":["It is exactly the opposite when an explanation includes a circle, with that when it involves a *contradictio*.\nWith what is necessary we do not ask why it is. Why a triangle has 3 sides. But indeed: why there are three sides.",[]],"reflm:3838":["Contingent is that whose non-being is possible. Being is taken here regardless of time, whether it be the present, past or future. Non-being is the complete sublation and thus from all time. The alterations themselves can be necessary; and if they are contingent, then the persistence of the thing is possible.\nThe opposite of a predicate at another time [signifies] proves that the subject in and for itself is undetermined with regard to the same. It does not prove that it is possible that the determination was not in the previous time. The alterations themselves, if they are necessary, since none of the successive determinations takes place without the opposite of the previous one being at the same time, prove not so much the contingency as the limitations, i.e. the nothing, which follows the something with equal steps.",[]],"reflm:3839":["That magnitude which can be the ground of another only through diminution or separation is *extensiva*. That which is a ground without diminution is *intensiva*.\nThe magnitude of matter and form.\nThe degrees of possibility.",[]],"reflm:3840":["The elapsed infinite, which has a beginning, is impos|sible.\nIf everything future is a merely mathematical repetition, then the future infinity is to be regarded as possible. But if the future is determined from the previous or otherwise, it makes up a whole that is thoroughly determined through some ground, and its infinity is no more comprehensible than that of the past and present time.",[]],"reflm:3841":["There is no necessary completeness of the possible coordination, thus also no (g necessary) bounds of a whole in which the quality of the unities is undetermined. (g all bounds are conditions of a whole of contingent magnitude.) Hence every whole is only contingently determined with regard to the bounds, and the possibility of still more parts is the empty. Hence, because the elapsed time makes up a whole, it has bounds, before which a time goes before. The future, which makes up no whole, has no bounds. The (g multitude of) things in the present time makes up a whole, which has bounds, and the world is thus in absolute empty space. In contrast every series of subordinated things necessarily has a bound *a priori*, and the *universitas* itself goes to all possible, not merely actual members, and the first of things can be seen through reason.",[]],"reflm:3842":["In the concept of everything together there must be a particular ground of the taking-together of everything in the thing itself. If now each member is thought synthetically to the others, then the present past time is that which takes them all together, because it is itself a whole. [In the analytic] If the parts are given analytically from the whole, then the *completudo* of the *analysis* with the breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3843":["The relation of cause to effect is no relation of identity; consequently there is also neither similarity nor equality between cause and effect, but *conformitas*. From the effect the cause is to be cognized and also to be termed. *talis est notio causae qva talis, qvalis oritur e notione causati* (such is the notion of cause as such as arises from the notion of the caused).\nWe know every thing of the world only as cause, but in the cause only the causality of the effect, thus only the effects, and thus not the thing itself and its determinations, through which [sie] it brings forth the effect.",[]],"reflm:3844":["*Effectus plenus* is measured not in *serie subordinatorum*, *sed coordinatorum*, that is not through the series of effects from the effects, e.g. a ball's force through the series of balls that it can move mediately. From this one sees that it is always only the same effect that endures in time, but in different conditions; thus the effect that arose immediately always endures and neither increases nor diminishes.",[]],"reflm:3845":["102'. E II 719.\nTo the concluding word of M § 333 Kant adds: aliqva, and then continues on p. 102':\nbut the question is: what do we cognize sooner. Do we cognize sooner that something is an effect, and thus has a cause, or that something is a cause and thus has an effect. The former. For that |\nsomething is an effect, what happens, is posited by reason only *per aliud*; we cognize this *nexus* only *a posteriori*. If we were to cognize causes beforehand, the *nexus* would be cognized *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:3846":["The immediate *bonitas* is for the understanding the greatest, primarily because it is necessary and universal; the mediate usefulness is smaller, because it pleases only contingently and under circumstances.",[]],"reflm:3847":["If many causes agree together to one effect, then they are connected among one another *a posteriori*; from this one concludes that they are so also *a priori*, i.e. that there is a common cause of them. Are breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3848":["For reason the data are not what is essential, but form.",[]],"reflm:3849":["The concept of matter also goes to something suffering, hence the substantial forms are active forces: entelechies. Spirits in their republic cannot be called the matter of the whole, because they are not suffering, but are combined self-actively and constitute their whole.",[]],"reflm:3850":["*forma dat esse rei* (form gives being to the thing). For what is essential of the thing can be cognized only through reason; but now all matter of cognition must be given through senses; thus the essence of things [merely], so far as they are cognized through reason, is the form.",[]],"reflm:3851":["Matter is at all times regarded as suffering, and form depends on activity, likewise all activity goes to nothing further than form. Meanwhile corporeal matter as substance according to Leibniz is an *entelechia*, i.e. something active. Our cognition is according to the data merely suffering, and the form rests on self-activity.",[]],"reflm:3852":["*Forma dat esse rei* (form gives being to the thing). *nam per intellectum solummodo formam cognoscimus* (for through the understanding we cognize only form). *Materia continet data, e qvibus varia oriri possunt diversimode determinando; ideoque determinatum esse alicuius rei pendet a forma* (Matter contains the data, from which various things can arise by being determined in different ways; and therefore the determinate being of some thing depends on form). *Forma est differentia specifica* (Form is the specific difference).",[]],"reflm:3853":["On the 1st proposition of M § 381 (107¹⁸f.):\nFor otherwise all members of the series would be *causata* of another member of the series. [Also wü] Thus there would be a member of the series that would be something other than all members of it.",[]],"reflm:3854":["If the present (our) world is the [allervo] only existing one, then it is also the most perfect whole from a single original being. But if there are several worlds, then one must well be the most perfect among all possible ones, but perhaps not ours.\nThe idea of the most perfect world is as necessary to us as that of the most perfect cause; indeed this is properly necessary to assume for the sake of that.",[]],"reflm:3855":["Life is the faculty of beginning a state (its own or another's) from an inner *principium*. The former is not a complete life, in that that whose state is alterable itself requires something external as cause. Bodies do have an inner *principium* for acting on one another (e.g. interconnection), also for retaining an externally imparted state, but not for beginning it from themselves. Thus all alteration, all origin proves a first beginning and therefore freedom. [Also] But because the beginning can be the first comparatively, namely according to mechanical laws. e.g. If a carrion stirs the dog, then a motion begins in it, which, because it was not produced by the smell according to mechanical laws, but was caused through the arousing of desire. In animals, however, this is just as much an external necessitation as in machines; hence they are called *automata spiritualia*. but in the human being in every case the chain of determining causes is cut off, and therefore one also distinguishes |\nthe immaterial as a *principium* of life from the material. In the human being the spirit is free and wills the good; the animal is an *automaton*; if this spirit were only always effective in the animal and did not alternate with its forces, we would find more proofs of freedom.\n(s *libertas est independentia a necessitatione externa* (freedom is independence from external necessitation).\n*libertas est vel originaria vel derivativa* [*haec vel*] (freedom is either original or derivative [this either]). *Arbitrium autem est vel sensitivum vel intellectuale; illud est vel brutum vel liberum* (but *arbitrium* is either sensitive or intellectual; the former is either brute or free).)",[]],"reflm:3856":["To be determined is quite permissible with freedom, but not passively, neither through the way objects affect, nor through a supreme producing cause. I can say: in this moment I am free (g *liber aut devinctus* (free or bound)) and unbound to do what pleases me; but it is nevertheless unavoidably necessary that I act thus. It is a law of self-activity, which makes the opposite impossible. Can one also with regard to the (g moral) evil be determined just so from free resolve. No! one can be determined to it only passively or not at all, because the pure will nevertheless always remains and thus cannot be bound at all, but its activity does not always exert itself.",[]],"reflm:3857":["Freely acts the substance that, not externally determined, brings forth something that was not before, and this freedom is opposed to inner or outer natural necessity. From free choice it acts, so far as the causality of the action lies in the liking, which is not passive. The difficulties concern only the first idea of |\nfreedom, and it is incomprehensible in the necessary being as well as the contingent. but from different grounds, because the former can begin nothing, but the latter can begin nothing first. The first degree of (g independence is the) self-activity [is]: that of a substance in general; the second degree: the independence in acting from all external determining causes; the third degree: the independence from its own nature.\nThus the negative is properly incomprehensible, the positive of the motives is comprehensible.",[]],"reflm:3858":["In all actions the ground, the causality, (g as to matter) lies in nature; as to form, however, either merely in nature, e.g. attraction, or in another faculty, which inwardly directs the forces of nature. The latter is freedom.",[]],"reflm:3859":["Freedom is (g a) practically necessary fundamental concept.\nThe first necessary is not to be comprehended, because there is no ground of it; the first contingent is also not [of] to be comprehended, because a necessitating ground must be there. The former, because it is necessary although it has no ground, the latter, because it is contingent although it has one.",[]],"reflm:3860":["For freedom, 1. *spontaneitas simpliciter talis* (*automaton*) (g *independentia a causis subiective necessitantibus* (*stimulis*))  is needed, so that [das arb was von dem subiect] the determinations can be attributed to the subject as its action. 2. The faculty of *arbitrium*\n|\n*intellectualis*, that it can be imputed to it as a *factum*. Because everything that happens, according to laws of the faculty of desire, presupposes a satisfaction, the *complacentia*, which is independent of the subjective necessitation, must be intellectual, and the former thus presupposes the latter.\nThe greatest difficulty lies in this: how a subjectively unconditioned choice can be thought (g *est obiective hypotheticum*) in (g according to) the *nexus causarum efficientium sive determinantium* or, if one departs from this, how the imputability of actions is possible.",[]],"reflm:3861":["On aversation.\nThe natural and practical possibility[has] is the one and the same breaks off.\nThe first question is: how one is to comprehend the causality [a] of freedom, whether it breaks off.\nIn the case of a contingent (g acting) being the causality can be either necessitated or dependent.\nWhether the generation (g genesis) of the action is *aliunde* determined or *spontanea*.",[]],"reflm:3862":["One often says: The human being has talents, he also has no evil heart. He could become useful. It lies only in the willing. One should believe this circumstance could easily be changed; but still it does not happen (g Negligent). If he now still has an evil heart: will it then still be able to happen?\nIs the will evil in evil human beings, or is it good, but weak. The means of making the will good are moral; of making it strong, pragmatic. If one seeks to practice resoluteness, steadfastness in resolution, resignation. In some it seems not possible to bring forth a good will (although correct power of judgment, i.e. preliminary will), in others not a strong will.",[]],"reflm:3863":["(g sensuality)\nFreedom from brutality (*spontaneitas practice talis*)\nFreedom from fatality (*transscendentalis*)\n*actus arbitrii vel originarii vel derivativi.*",[]],"reflm:3864":["Liking is a desire without any necessity of an efficient cause; consequently a pure liking can be necessary for the sake of the *bonitas* of the thing and is nevertheless free from inclination and breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3865":["Freedom is really a faculty of subordinating all actions of choice to the motives of reason.",[]],"reflm:3866":["The *stimuli* [move] do not necessitate our choice objectively, i.e. what one ought to do is independent of all sensible stimulations.",[]],"reflm:3867":["No one counts among freedom the faculty of being able to desire what is abominable (evil).\nWe have, then, sensible cognitions, sensible pleasure and displeasure and sensible appetites. The faculty according to motives of reason is freedom. The possibility of willing with consciousness what is disapproved by reason is the weak will; the [Nei] skill of willing evil is the evil will.",[]],"reflm:3868":["The faculty of actively willing the cognized good that is in our power is freedom; but [not] it does not belong to it just as necessarily the faculty of willing the cognized evil, the prevention of which is in our power. This too is not really a faculty, but a possibility of suffering. Evil actions do indeed stand under freedom, but do not happen through it.",[]],"reflm:3869":["The true ends with regard to things are subordinated to the idea of the good; but with regard to the person the idea of what is personally good is subordinated to the end, not that which the person has, but toward which the nature of the person is set up and which one knows through observation.\nOf all things there is a twofold ground of their nature: the cause and the end. The end seems just as much a ground of existence, namely of the worth of the existence, as the efficient cause.",[]],"reflm:3870":["M 283':\n(s pathological is what is in accord with the determination of the force according to impressions.)\n(s Practical is everything that belongs to the action according to rules, or everything that is connected with determining rules of actions. *Necessitatio est vel pathologica vel practica;* |\nM 283: |\nthe last can be objective, the first not.)\nM 283':\nSomething is considered practical in general insofar as it is weighed according to the laws of free choice. If it happens according to the rules of good choice, then it is moral, hence practical and moral |\npossible or necessary. To practical philosophy belong only understanding and a physical feeling, to the moral understanding and a moral feeling. A human race would be possible even without the latter.",[]],"reflm:3871":["Opposite M § 723 (13638-1371), probably \nAs regards the inner necessity [of the distinction] in the faculty of desire, we see ourselves compelled to desire something not merely through feeling (*stimuli*); but we also see ourselves compelled, [our this our appetites] to put ourselves in the place of a being that is subject to no such appetites or feeling, and from merely general concepts of satisfaction to look at our actions themselves, and we feel ourselves compelled to look at ourselves with abhorrence or satisfaction. Since now this abhorrence itself goes to the appetite, it yields a more excellent law than the appetite.\nWhy are we compelled the abhorrence vo breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3872":["The free will is, as it were, isolated. Nothing external determines it; it is active without suffering. The *motiva* are only objects that harmonize with the inner law of its activity.* The good moves only a good will, i.e. it is only the effect [according], which is possible according to laws of the same. Whether it is now determined by moving causes or by *causis efficientibus* (of the Deity), the subject is hung on a foreign cause by means of a chain, and its actions are only derived, the good as well as the evil.\nFreedom consists in the faculty of being able to act independently of external determining grounds according to intellectual choice. [Of it] Under this stands all sensibility. Hence |\nwe ourselves think hastinesses or a series of obscure representations, which are the causes of errors, as subjected to our choice. The actions that happen according to mere rules of sensibility. We must distinguish in the human being the animal, i.e. him according to the laws of sensibility, and the spirit: according to laws of reason. His choice as animal is actually always determined through *stimuli*; but his will is nevertheless free, insofar as his reason is capable of altering these determinations of choice. Why reason at times does not do so and the human being does not act according to the intellectual will is unknown. Precisely hence, however, it happens that the human being considered as spirit blames himself as animal, is in contradiction and strife with himself, wishes other inclinations for himself, but often loses himself in sensibility. The one spiritual use is to order the animal sensations and to direct means according to animal ends. The other is to consider the ends themselves *intellectualiter*.\nHow is it possible that one can blame oneself, if the self is not, so to speak, twofold? For otherwise one can indeed pass no other judgment about oneself than what is taken from oneself and also agrees with itself.\n*(s Not the objective, but subjective grounds necessitate *actualiter*.)",[]],"reflm:3873":["The (g contradictory) opposite of the [things] thing is to be distinguished from the opposite of its relation; the latter is the opposite of the judgment.\ne.g. a divisible body; its opposite is: *Corpus* ×0 + *divisibile* ×0 =0 *nihil privativum*. This opposite never contradicts itself.",[]],"reflm:3874":["Space and time are mere possibilities, whose opposite, i.e. no space and no time, is impossible. (There is no space, there is no time). Now space and time are grounded on something actual, therefore something actual is absolutely necessary.",[]],"reflm:3875":["*Cognita absoluta necessitate alicuius cognoscitur eiusdem existentia.* (The absolute necessity of something having been cognized, the existence of the same is cognized.)\nAbsolute necessity is not cognizable externally through what is caused, but internally from the idea of it.\nAn existent, with which removed all possibility is removed, is absolutely necessary.\nSome possibility (g internal) having been removed, all possibility is removed; for with that removed the absolutely impossible is thought; but if there were still some thinkables, it would be possible that something be impossible. Otherwise: If some existent were absolutely necessary, another existent likewise and so |\non: one of the existents could be removed with the existence of the others saved, and therefore possibility would reach only as far as some existence touches.\nOn the notion of possibility, in which one can abstract from existence, in relation to the possibility, in which one cannot abstract from this, e.g. space, time.\nImpossibility is either of repugnance or of negation; the former is always given, if the relation of certain given notions is impossible; the latter insofar as there is not anything givable to think, i.e. possible.",[]],"reflm:3876":["That the possibility of something does not contain the ground of its existence. Nor existence that of its possibility. But that in the necessary possibility and existence are the same. That nothing contains the ground of itself.",[]],"reflm:3877":["The cause of substance through necessity of nature is emanative (system of emanation).\nThe cause of substance through freedom is creator.\nThe absolute necessity of the substances of the universe with the contingency and dependence of the state breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3878":["Kindness is the divine will in relation to the state (g the possible happiness) of the rational creature. Holiness is the divine will as a ground of the rules of the human. Justice is the (g wise) goodness of the divine will in the relation to the prescription of holiness, *virtualis*.",[]],"reflm:3879":["The world cannot be compared as to time with any being outside the world. It has a beginning, but not with regard to God an origin, but a dependence. I can compare the duration of the world with its parts as an hour with minutes, but not with any thing that is distinguished from the world. Sooner could indeed each thing in the world have been, but not the world itself.",[]],"reflm:3880":["The world, according to that which in it is a work of God, is the best among all possibles. For this is the [practical] representation of the greatest practical perfection of God. The representation of the greatest perfection of the world on this side includes in itself all hopes that I can make for myself from the existence of God, as an identical proposition. And therefore this proposition is also a principal proposition. But from the side of freely acting beings one cannot assert it just so. If one wanted to say: God has power to make the free beings act as it pleases him, then this would be contrary to their freedom; or: he foresaw what they would do in all circumstances, and chose the best conditions, then this foreseeing would prove the necessary connection of the actions with these conditions, which are posited by God.\n===NOTES===\n- Dasein = existence; Vorstellung = representation; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Verknüpfung = connection; setzen = posit (gesetzt).\n- Hauptsatz rendered 'principal proposition' (Satz = proposition); 'Moglichen' (sic, without umlaut) = possibles.\n- 'conditionen' (Latinate spelling) and 'Bedingungen' are both rendered 'conditions'.\n- 'Wesen' = beings; 'Macht' = power; 'behaupten' = assert.",[]],"reflm:3881":["Insofar as the occurrences of the world are supposed to arise from the nature of the same, they all happen according to [communal] universal laws, because nature [ohn] (if no miracle happens) always remains the same and thus the alterations are in accordance with a communal *principium*. Now all things of the world, each of which has its particular |\nnature, are [dependent] on one another and one state dependent on another; thus no occurrence happens merely in relation to one state, but all states of the thing, namely to its nature and at the same time according to the laws of the nature of other connected things. E.g. A human being perishes at sea according to laws of the nature of the water, of the winds and of the shore and not merely in relation to his moral constitution. Now it is impossible that the occurrences, as individually intended [von], should coincide by chance with all things of the world, e.g. the human being's vicious deed and winds, each of which happens according to the order of the nature of the *subiect*; hence they could then not follow from nature and according to one and the same laws.\nOne is accustomed to answer the opinion of some [von der] that God directs his direction not[vor] to *individua* but to the universal, that the universal is thought only through separation from the *individuis*. Regarding this it is to be noted that the universal is either the logically universal ([oder] the common mark of many) or the *realiter* universal. [Jenes] E.g. [setzt] suppose: a machine is designed for many individual effects [z.E] like the duck of Vaucanson, then I can say: [ihre] the provision of the artisan is directed to the universal of the animal actions, namely to the logically universal or to everything; but if the arrangement of the machine is made so that it is designed not at all for any individual ones, but indeterminately for a ground of all of them, then the preparation is a universal real ground. e.g. thus a barometer is a universal air-measurer, and the feet of a jumper are not arranged for the individual dances but for walking and dancing in general.\n===NOTES===\n- Begebenheit = occurrence; Zustand = state; Gesetz = law; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Merkmal = mark (nota); Wirkung = effect; Grund = ground.\n- Einrichtung/eingerichtet = arrangement/arranged; Zurichtung = preparation; angelegt = designed.\n- 'Natur' = nature throughout; 'Wunder' = miracle; 'absonderung' = separation.\n- 'ohn' is a doubtful abbreviated reading, left as printed in brackets; 'nicht[vor]' is printed without a space in the source; 'subiects' is rendered *subiect* (Latinate spelling kept); 'Lasterthat' = vicious deed.\n- 'ei Barometer' is evidently a slip for 'ein'; 'sie' in 'daher könnten sie' is left unresolved.",[]],"reflm:3882":["It is something other: to concede a particular providence, and to have insight into the marks of the same in a particular case or also to understand the particular rules of the application of the goodness and wisdom of God. He who pretends to cognize a particular divine aim in an occurrence, asserts that he has insight into the grounds of the divine decrees, through which an occurrence necessarily had to turn out thus and not otherwise. But then precisely the same insight would also have to suffice to predict this occurrence from the known rules of the divine decrees: Just as he has no insight into the causes of eclipses [der sie nicht] insofar as they are determined to a certain point of time, who cannot announce them in advance.\n===NOTES===\n- Absicht = aim; erkennen = cognize; einsehen/Einsicht = have insight into/insight; Begebenheit = occurrence; Grund = ground; behaupten = assert; bestimmt = determined.\n- Rathschlüße rendered 'decrees' (not in the glossary).\n- The last sentence is syntactically awkward and has a bracketed deletion; the construction is kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:3883":["*Ratio* is that through whose concept (i.e. universal) (g not according to the laws of appearances) something else is posited, and hence that by which something else is universally determined; from the *rationatum* (g predicate) to the *ratio* (g subject) the consequence does not hold, except indeterminately or particularly.\n===NOTES===\n- The Latin technical terms *ratio* and *rationatum* are left in Latin.\n- 'phaenomenorum' rendered 'appearances' (Erscheinung); *conseqventia* rendered 'consequence'.\n- 'g' is the Academy Edition's siglum for a later layer of addition and is carried over unchanged.",[]],"reflm:3884":["The basic concepts are not determinations, hence also not the essential pieces.\nThe logical essence is hypothetical or absolute; the former, that which contains the whole (g primitive) concept that we have of the thing, the second, that which expresses the possible concept.\nThe real essence signifies the subject that is unchanged and determinable. Everything that is has a real essence that always remains, and the rest are determinations.\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen rendered 'essence' here (glossary: being; essence); Begriff = concept; Bestimmung = determination; bedeuten = signify.\n- Stüke rendered literally 'pieces'; 'wesentlichen Stüke' left unexplained.\n- 'den Gantzen ... Begrif' (accusative, sic) in the second paragraph is kept as awkward; the antecedent of 'das zweyte' is left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:3885":["*ab esse ad posse* (from being to being able) the consequence holds, which is empirical. The *posse* concluded [posse] through the principle of contradiction is rationally cognized. We cognize the possibility of a real nexus empirically.\n*a non esse ad non posse* (from not being to not being able) the consequence does not hold, but it is nevertheless an imperfect empirical conclusion.\n===NOTES===\n- *conseqventia* = consequence (Folgerung); *conclusum/conclusio* = concluded/conclusion; *cognoscimus/cognitum* = cognize/cognized.\n- 'nexus' kept in Latin (glossary: Zusammenhang = interconnection; nexus); *principium contradictionis* rendered 'principle of contradiction'.\n- The repeated bracketed [posse] is a deletion/insertion in the apparatus, carried over as is.",[]],"reflm:3886":["Whether magnitude might not be something that is relative in itself in relation to that which is the greatest and contains the data for everything possible, e.g. the triangle in infinite space. [Da] This is the absolute magnitude, which we cognize by the fact that something can still be greater, and not by the fact that it can be diminished, i.e. contains parts. The latter is the relative magnitude.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; erkennen = cognize; Teil = part; Raum = space; 'Moglichen' (sic) = possible.\n- 'Großeste' rendered 'greatest'; 'Verhältnis' rendered 'relation'.\n- 'Ob nicht ... sey' is kept as a dependent clause without a main clause, as in the German.",[]],"reflm:3887":["Perfections simply are [necessary agreements], that which agrees together to the inner worth of things. Hence only rational beings can have perfections; e.g. understanding is distinguished from beauty and happiness.\n*in sensu respectivo* [either the perfection of], where nothing is lacking to one either *in respectu* to something positive in general or *in respectu* to a determinate concept: form of perfection.\n(g the [inner] worth itself is the *bonitas*, depends on the matter of perfection.)\n===NOTES===\n- Vollkommenheit = perfection; Verstand = understanding; Materie = matter; Form = form; Begrif = concept; Vernünftige Wesen = rational beings.\n- 'bestimmt/determinirt': 'determinirten' rendered 'determinate'; *bonitaet* rendered as Latin *bonitas*.\n- 'schlechthin' rendered 'simply'; the sentence 'was zu dem innern Werth ... zusammenstimt' is kept literal and ambiguous in its dative construction.\n- The second paragraph is syntactically incomplete in the German ('da einem ... nichts fehlt').",[]],"reflm:3888":["The [schl] inwardly necessary is simply necessary (*omnimode*); but we cannot have insight into the inner necessity on account of the omission of the determinate conditions. Yet our concept of necessity also permits that of a ground and indeed universal ground, and this one also understands in the idea of thoroughgoing necessity; but this is only a concept of relation in the subordination of the ideas and logically conditional, serves also only to distinguish the *omnimode* necessary from that which is *restrictive tale* (such restrictively).\n===NOTES===\n- notwendig/Nothwendigkeit = necessary/necessity; Grund = ground; Idee = idea; Begrif = concept; einsehen = have insight into; verstehen = understand.\n- 'bestimten' rendered 'determinate'; 'durchgangigen' = thoroughgoing; 'Beziehungsbegrif' = concept of relation.\n- *omnimode* and *restrictive tale* kept in Latin; the parenthetical gloss is a literal rendering.\n- The antecedent of 'diesen' and 'diese' is left as ambiguous as in the German.",[]],"reflm:3889":["*Realitas in sensu substantivo* is a perfection in the metaphysical sense.\nThe highest reality does not consist in everything being in it, but through it as a ground; for the maximum of reality is not synthetically possible or through coordination, but lesser degrees are only through limitation of the greatest possible. Now the highest reality is that which cannot be limited; thus that which is the measure of all things and in which the reality of all things lies, is only the sequel of the *ens summum*.\n===NOTES===\n- Realität = reality; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Grad = degree; Grund = ground; Folge = sequel (glossary); synthetisch = synthetic.\n- 'in metaphysischem Verstande' rendered 'in the metaphysical sense' (Verstand here not the faculty).\n- Einschränkung rendered 'limitation' (as is Schranke in the glossary); 'durch ihr' (sic) rendered 'through it'.\n- 'Maas' = measure; 'also ist diejenige ... nur die Folge' is kept in its awkward form.",[]],"reflm:3890":["Every universal concept contains that which is common to many things |\n(g in which many are one and the same), and is indeterminate with regard to the differences.\n[Through it one can think several things, and it contains many under itself]\nA particular concept is more determined, namely with regard to the differences. (g Of the thoroughly determined.)\nIt contains [many] the particular ones under itself and is *latior*; the particular ones are *conceptus angustiores*. It is called *superior* with regard to the particular ones, which are *inferiores*.\nIt [holds less in itself] is contained in the lower ones and contains less in itself.\nLogical subordination. *Conceptus summus* (g least determined), *infimus*, most determined (g *singularis*). *Sphaera conceptus*. *Logica divisio*.\n*Progressus* through synthesis, *regressus* through analysis.\nIn the series of logical subordination there is a member that has higher ones above itself and lower ones in itself: *conceptus communis* [subord:] (s *subalternus*); if it is considered only in its *superiori*, then it is represented *in abstracto*; in its *inferiori*: *in concreto*; most *in abstracto* or *in individuo*.\n*conceptus communis* [inferior] (s *subalternus*) is with regard to its *inferioris genus*, with regard to its *superioris species*. *Genus*, which with regard to its *superioris* is *species*, is *subalternum*; that which it is not: *summum*. *Species* which contains only *individua* under itself, *species infima*. *Continuitas formarum logicarum*. Between two concepts logically subordinated to one another there is always a middle one.\n*Subordinatio realis*. *Omnitudo realis*. In place of the determinations of the universal concept, limitation of the *sphaera* of the highest ground, *negationes*. In logical divisions one limits the *sphaera*, in real determinations one limits the reality. The *conceptus* (g logically) *inferior* contains fewer determinations in itself, the really inferior fewer *positiones*, until *conceptus infimus*, |\n*realitas evanescens* is or *negatio pura*. Nonetheless the *subordinatio realis* is also only according to possibility and thus *Metaphysica*, of which the *causalis* in existence is called *physica*.\n*Omnitudo realis* [bedeut] is opposed to the *particulari negationi*.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriff = concept (conceptus); Bestimmung = determination; Einteilung = division; Realität = reality; Daseyn = existence; Einschränkung = limitation; Grund = ground; Merkmal not present.\n- 'bestimmt' (more determined) and 'Bestimungen' (determinations) are the same root; 'Besondere' = particular.\n- Siglum g and s (later layers) and brackets and | are carried over; 'Sphaera' kept as Latin *sphaera*.\n- 'Er [hält weniger in sich] ist in den niedrigen enthalten' has a bracketed deletion: the sentence is kept as it stands; 'Metaphysica' capitalized in the source and italicized here as a Latin term.\n- 'bedeut' is an incomplete deleted/inserted word, left in brackets; the final line is an incomplete grammatical construction in the source.",[]],"reflm:3891":["Origin is a moment upon which the whole existence follows; the passing away (end) the moment that follows upon the whole existence. Thereby it is shown first that the origin is an existence, second a *terminus* of duration.\nA whole arises from something when the existence of the parts precedes the form, and from nothing when nothing of the parts precedes the whole.\n===NOTES===\n- Ursprung = origin; Daseyn = existence; Dauer = duration; Form = form; Teil = part; Vergehen = passing away.\n- 'Gantzen' rendered 'whole'; 'folgt' = follows; *terminus* kept in Latin.\n- 'entspringt ein gantzes' is rendered with 'whole' as the subject; the German is not further disambiguated.",[]],"reflm:3892":["Whether there is a *spatium absolutum* (absolute space) or *tempus absolutum* (absolute time) would mean as much as whether one could annihilate everything [else] lying between two things in space and yet the determinate empty gap would remain, and whether, if [between the beginning] a whole year motions and alterations in general ceased, the following could not begin, so that an empty between-year would have elapsed. We do not resolve these difficulties, but answer our opponents through *retorsion*, because their apprehension has precisely these difficulties.\n*Ratio sive principium status est tempus et quidem coniunctionis omnium, qvae pertinent ad eundem statum.* (The ground or principle of the state is time, and indeed of the combination of all that pertain to the same state.)",[]],"reflm:3893":["It is absolutely impossible that a thing be in many places at the same time, but not that it be in succession.\nIt is only conditionally [necessarily] impossible that many substances be in one and the same place, namely those that are impenetrable.",[]],"reflm:3894":["*Cosmologia* (cosmology) is either empirical (physics and psychology) or rational.\nThis considers either the material, i.e. [subject] constitutive things (matter and the immaterial), or the form. The latter is treated here.\n[Empirical] rational cosmology belongs to metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:3895":["Whether the *ens realissimum* (most real being) is at the same time absolutely perfect (g *summum bonum*) is a question; for *respective sive externe* (relatively or externally) it is certainly the most perfect. The *interna perfectio* (internal perfection) (*bonitas*) presupposes the feeling of pleasure.",[]],"reflm:3896":["Nothing is opposed to something even in the logical sense; in the metaphysical sense it is opposed to the thinkable. The former is nothing respectively such (none of those that are contained under some notion). The latter is the opposite of those that are contained under any notion at all.",[]],"reflm:3897":["A ground is sufficient either among coordinates (s *ratio completa*, complete ground) or among subordinates.\nIn the former one no longer asks the »why« of the sequel, in the latter of the ground.\n( s This stands under no higher ground, that is no part of a larger ground. The *terminus* of the subordinated grounds is *ratio primitiva* (primitive ground). The *terminus* of the coordinated grounds is *completudo* (completeness). )\n(s The whole of the series or of the ground.)",[]],"reflm:3898":["In *conceptibus complexis* (complex concepts) the consequence from *posse* to *esse* (from being able to being) does not hold, but in simple ones.\nFrom the form of possibility one cannot infer to actuality, but surely from the matter.",[]],"reflm:3899":["All unity is either of comparison or of connection. The first is insofar as something is one and the same with much else. The second, insofar as much is combined in one ground.\nThe unity of the taking-together (addition): is mathematical, or of derivation: this is philosophical.",[]],"reflm:3900":["Everything is necessary. either conditioned (g i.e. through a ground) or unconditioned.\n[Everything] Among all grounds there must be a first ground, this is absolutely necessary.",[]],"reflm:3901":["The synthesis of reality consists merely in the separation from the |\nsome greatest through negations, because negations presuppose reality; consequently therein is the difference of the [gan] composite and the simple.",[]],"reflm:3902":["Besides substance and accident one can still think something positive, namely the *respectus* (relation). Every *respectus* (g of the same) is threefold: that of substance (empirical): space, of the *accidentium* (accidents): time, of substance to the *accidentibus*: force.",[]],"reflm:3903":["Substance alone is a thing or [something] object.",[]],"reflm:3904":["*Status* (state) is substantial coexistence with accidents.\nThe substantial is immutable; for since it contains the first ground of the accidents and hence of the changes, if it were changed, this change would be an accident, not substantial. The first ground, however, is immutable for this reason, that, since there is no other ground of change, it would change itself, i.e. a and not-a would be determined at the same time in the same *ens* (being).",[]],"reflm:3905":["On M 369:\nThe whole of the world must not be confused with the whole state of the same. |\nFor the accidents are not parts of the world. Consequently I can say: the world arises little by little, in that one part after another is created.",[]],"reflm:3906":["( s Necessity and contingency in the world. Nature (s with ground) and freedom and the perfection of both: happiness and morality. Blind necessity, blind contingency. Happenstance. Supreme *principium* of the rationally necessary, the natural necessity [of the rationally contingent] and freedom; both are contained in the supreme being. )( s Nature and freedom, fate and chance. { Connection. *saltus* (leap). Order. *)\n*( (g this concerns occurrences, consequently what happens in the world; for the origin of the same is no occurrence.) )\nAll occurrences of the world happen either through blind necessity:\n*bruta necessitas* (brute necessity);\n*Fatum* or through fate — destiny — decree of the supreme cause;\n*per libertatem* (through freedom) or through freedom of the acting beings in the world;\n*casus* (chance) or not through fate or freedom;\n*fortuitum* (the fortuitous) or from no cause at all.\nBlind necessity (s — Rational)\nPredetermined. Destiny.\nThrough freedom (s Contingency.)\nwithout will — — — Chance\nblind happenstance. (s Natural chance.)\n*Nihil per saltum, casum et fatum accidit.* (Nothing happens through a leap, chance and fate.)\n( s In the series of everything contingent the first is the free action. In the series of everything necessary the first is absolute necessity. Boundaries. )\n(s *qvalitas mundi* (quality of the world); *mundus non constat vacuo* (the world does not consist of void).)\n(s Every transition is through all possible intermediate members.)\n(s That which is in itself contingent, which yet necessarily [is] happens without ground, is blind necessity.\nThat which is in itself dependent, which there [is] happens without all[ground] necessity, is blind chance.)\n(s The necessity in the world without any ground is the blind one.\nThe contingency in the world without any ground is the blind one.)",[]],"reflm:3907":["( s (g negative: One does not speak of divine reason.) Understanding. No sensibility. (*non affectus*, not affected.) intuitive, not discursive: goes to the possible and the actual equally strongly (g sees everything in itself). No difficulty regarding future time. (g *Perfectiones qviescentes et operativae* (quiescent and operative perfections).) [Will] Pleasure and displeasure from itself, blessedness. Will. Freedom. Independence from incentives (g *analogon* of motive); yet no *absolutum decretum* (absolute decree), but conforming to the goodness of the object. Moral motive: justice. Benevolent aim. )\nAll great properties that I say of God from the arbitrary idea of the same are only *expositions* of the *hypothesis* that I assume. But those that I draw from the work (these, however, are only practically perfect) hit upon a real being given through actual documents.\n1. Existence[of God] of the *ens originarium* (original being): against atheists (s dogmatic and skeptical). Existence [of God] of the *intelligentia summa* (highest intelligence): against deists.\n(s real condition of all possibility. *Necessitas* (necessity). *immutabilitas* (immutability). *impassibilitas* (impassibility). *Immaterialitas* (immateriality). *Infinitudo* (infinity).)\n[2. Originary: against Epicureans] (Existence of the highest person: against fatalists.)\n3. Immutable and impassible (*immutabilis et impassibilis*): against anthropomorphists (g that its concept is purely intellectual).\n4. Substantiality (*substantialitas*): against Spinoza.\n(s Not a single substance (g simple): against Spinoza (so that the world would inhere in it as an accident); not a single substance (g composite) as the complex of all, i.e. το παν [as if God were an accident inhering in the world]. God is an *ens extramundanum* (extramundane being).)\n5. That it is an extramundane being: against Stoics, because they contended for a soul of the world.\n6. Uniqueness (*unicitas*): against polytheists.\n7. Highest perfection (*summa perfectio*) (s highest good (*summum bonum*)): against Manichaeans.\n8. Simplicity (*simplicitas*). (s Infinity: against anthropomorphists.)\n9. Eternity (*aeternitas*). (s 10. Causality (*causalitas*) with respect to the world.)\n10. Omnipresence (*omnipraesentia*). (s Extramundane being (not *anima mundi*, soul of the world): against Stoics.)\n11. Omnipotence (*omnipotentia*). (s Intelligence: against deists.)\na. Personality (*personalitas*): against deists through intellectual concepts of the consciousness of itself, not insofar as we are affected, but act. (s The transcendental, natural and moral theology. God as Holy One.)",[]],"reflm:3908":["(s a)\na: Against Manichaeans.\n(s a 2,)\n3. All-sufficiency (*omnisufficientia*). As cause of the universe, even of the possibles.\n4. Immutability (*immutabilitas*).\n5. Impassibility (*impassibilitas*) (s Spirituality (*spiritualitas*): against deists, theism, but not anthropomorphism.)\n(s c)\n6. Unity (*vnitas*). (s Sanctity (*sanctitas*): against pagans.)\n(s d)\n7. Simplicity (*simplicitas*).\n8. Immateriality (*immaterialitas*).\n9. Intelligence (*intelligentia*). Living God.\n(s a 3. Infinity (*infinitudo*) [ads] internal — relative, all-sufficiency [respect]. the conditions are 9 and 10.)",[]],"reflm:3909":["Transcendental concept of God.\nGod as the highest being. (s Defense against atheism.)\nMetaphysical-psychological:*\nGod as the highest intelligence. (s Defense against deism. Which properly attacks anthropomorphism.)\nMoral:\nGod as the highest good. (s Defense against Epicureanism.)\n(s He can be no [comp] rightful lawgiver of morals, if he is not at the same time that of nature, and indeed in the whole completeness. For if he does not have fate fully in his power, he cannot command absolutely. But for that he must be creator; this is necessary to speculation, but not to religion.)\nα. Morality (*moralitas*) against Epicureans. (s Moral theology, theological morals.)\n*(s we can cognize God only psychologically according to the analogy with the forces of nature, which contain the complete causality of perfection. For we have no *data* of God, but only *data* of his relation to the world, so we cognize in the same only s breaks off.)",[]],"reflm:3910":["I know of God only the condition of his adequate relation to the world, and indeed this relation is similar to that of the things of the world, because he is the *principium* of the series; but these properties are not similar to the properties of the things of the world. We can quite well ignore that which has no such relation. For it does not belong to us.",[]],"reflm:3911":["God is perfect through his nature, but he is good through his will. The ideal of a good will is realized in God or through God.\nWhat is good there, its existence has a worth. Perfection contains the *completudinem* (completeness) of the *datorum* (givens) for something from whose worth we abstract.",[]],"reflm:3912":["The world has a beginning, i.e., a state that is no sequel from another state: *terminum a priori*; *non est aeternus a parte ante*. For if every state of the world were a sequel from another state of the world, then all states of the world would have another state of the world before them; thus a state of the world would be distinguished from all states. which *contradictorium*.\nFrom the beginning of the world up to the present time no eternity has elapsed: *mundus non creatus est ab aeterno*.\nThe world could not have been created earlier; but we could well have existed later.\nWe cannot represent distinctly how the causality of a necessary cause begins. For we always think to ourselves that it had not been before. But at the beginning of all things we must indeed think a relation of the same *a parte post*, but none *a parte ante*, thus no relation of the world to a previously elapsed time.",[]],"reflm:3913":["[consensus vel opposit]\nAll combination is either one of combination or of comparison. The former is either of agreement or of conflict.\nThat of combination is either analytic (of the part with the whole and thus also with itself) and is logical; or synthetic: of the parts among one another, of the [parts] combined unities among one another.",[]],"reflm:3914":["There is nothing in the [human soul] metaphysics that is empirical, except what concerns the general laws of thinking in the human soul.\nHence the synthetic principles also hold not as rational, but as subjectively general; and it is not possible that the understanding begin from concepts and merely compare the concepts analytically, unless some rules of synthesis lie at the ground.",[]],"reflm:3915":["[The relation of magnitudes is the ground of an ideal combination, in that I from a]\nThere are comparisons that are not connections, e.g., of identity, diversity. These are ideal connections or conflicts.\nComparisons of magnitude.",[]],"reflm:3916":["The comparison of concepts according to the law of the imagination is not to be wholly rejected. Things that are always encountered combined with one another give a presumption that they are combined according to the law of the understanding. Hence the inference from induction. According to the law of wit the inference from analogy.",[]],"reflm:3917":["(s All cognition has two *termini*, *a priori* and *a posteriori*; hence *principia prima* *a priori* or *a posteriori*. [If ab] The *principia absolute prima a priori* are all indemonstrable; thus metaphysics contains only *principia comparative prima*.)\n(s All pure ideas of reason are ideas of reflection (*discursivae* and no *intuitus*, as Plato asserted). Hence thereby also not objects are represented, but only laws for comparing the concepts that have been given to us through senses.)\nAll cognition has grounds and among these the first. The first are either those that naturally precede in arising, or those [out of which] in which they ultimately dissolve and from which they can be put together again: *a priori* or *posteriori*. Grammatic: empirical, rational.\n(s empirical or intellectual principles)\nThe first grounds of cognition from behind are experiences.\nThe science of arriving at the highest grounds *a priori* is metaphysics. This science is resolving. Basic concepts, basic judgments.\nMethod of nature.\nConditioned indispensability of this science for higher judgments of reason.\nHistory. Names.\nOrder of the disciplines.\n( g Grounds of one's own insights\nforeign [Eins] testimonies proverbs )",[]],"reflm:3918":["The *territorium* of metaphysics. Parts.\nIts function determines its worth. Is not the satisfac|tion\nof the desire for knowledge, but to determine the boundaries, partly positive, partly negative.\nIt is a science of the ends of our cognition. Comparison with mathematics.\n[history. Aristotle.]",[]],"reflm:3919":["(s The understanding connects concepts because one includes the other; the *imagination*, because one accompanies the other; wit, because one is similar to the other. With negative judgments it is just so. e.g. Because we combine contact with the immediate effect, we negate attraction.)\nThe law of the understanding is not put into practice otherwise than with a great mixing with the law of the *imagination*.\nIt is not enough to have grounds for a cognition; one must also slowly accustom oneself to it. (Friction). It is not enough to cancel grounds; one must also for a time resist habit, gradually to hold back the same former judgment even without grounds.",[]],"reflm:3920":["With all judgments of the understanding the situation is as follows. (If (g any) something x [which] can be cognized through a representation a [is compared with another concept b, either that it the representation b], then a is a mark of something x, [but] the cognition of x through a, however, is a concept. Thus extension, motion, ignorance etc etc. is a mark of something x). If any something x, which is cognized through a representation a, is compared with another concept (g b), either that it include or exclude this, |\nthen this relation is in the judgment. This judgment is (g thus) either the cognition of agreement or of conflict, so that in the [concept of] thing x, which I know through the concept a, either b is contained as a partial concept and thus x [also], which is cognized through a, can also be cognized through b, or that [through] x annuls the concept of b.\nIn all judgments the matter and the form are to be considered. That the former are the concepts of the subject (y+a)\n= x. and of the predicate b. second the form, which among the logicians is called the concept of combination (copula). (One can represent every concept in relation to all others; those that it includes stand toward it in the relation of affirmation, those that it excludes, in negation.) x is called the possible concept of a thing, a the representation through which I think it. All form of judgments is either affirmation or negation. The first represents the relation in which the concept of the thing y+a includes the mark b and thus is identical with it according to its part. The negation consists in this, that the concept of the thing y+a is the opposite of the mark b, and in the representation of the annulment the negation consists.",[]],"reflm:3921":["The predicate is no part (g concept) of the subject, but a representation of the [whole] subject through a partial concept. The understanding cognizes something at all times through a clear or obscure judgment, in that it resolves something into its predicates. All [signs] our concepts are marks drawn from sensation. Sensation itself is no object of the understanding, but the marks of the same; hence e.g. the concept of man is nothing other than the representation of something that has the predicates into which we can resolve the concept of a man. Hence in every judgment the subject is in general Something =x, which, cognized under the mark a, is compared with another mark. Hence it is also no wonder that we cognize no subject |\nbefore all predicates, except merely the I, which nevertheless is no concept, but an [sensation] intuition. Hence through the understanding we cognize in bodies not the proper subjects, but the predicates of extension, solidity, rest, motion etc.. The cause is: through our senses only the relations of things can reveal themselves, and we can represent the absolute or subject only from ourselves. The idea of substance comes properly from the *repraesentatione sui ipsius*, insofar as we represent to ourselves that something is different from us, and predicates cannot be thought without subject and without a last subject, [hinzu k] the constant predicates then together are called the subject.\nThrough a predicate I do not represent to myself a part of the thing or have a concept of the part, but represent to myself the object itself and have of it a partial concept, hence also designation through mathematical signs is impossible. Let y+s be the thing itself, which is represented under the concept s, and its predicate p. then y+s–p would =0, consequently y+s=p would be.",[]],"reflm:3922":["M III:\nMaterial principles seem to be: what [arises] happens must have a ground. Every successive series has a beginning*. Propositions of this kind implicate themselves; for since the beginning is an arising [is] or has happened, so there would have to be, insofar, in turn a ground of it. The idea of freedom indicates an arising without a preceding determining ground. The nature of our understanding brings it with it that, according to its rule, nothing (g contingent) is thinkable without connection with grounds, and that a sequence (according to time) without ground and a happening of something without connection with its ground cannot be thought, because then the understanding would be entirely unusable.\nM IV:\n*(g and every series of subordinated things has a first. †)\n†(s It is hard to think at all in the series of all things a beginning to which everything else succeeds, other than as creation. Likewise it is impossible to think an elapsed infinite series. The idea of using All of an infinite series seems to be false, and yet the necessity of the *causa prima* is grounded on it, because otherwise everything would be *causatum*.)\nM III:\nAnother synthetic principle is: what thinks there is only a simple subject. Every thing must (not absolutely, but respectively to another) be at some time, either simultaneously or one after another.\nIt does not follow that what must be judged according to the laws of our understanding is true, if it concerns such things as our understanding, according to the arrangement of its use, is not determined to judge.\n[It would be no philosophy] We borrow from the corporeal appearances the law of sufficient ground; but if we want to make it general [so] and apply it to things that are elevated above the idea of our understanding, then we mix the idea of the for us absolute unintelligibility with inner impossibility.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsätze = principles (Grundsatz); Gesetz = law; Verknüpfung = connection; Folge = sequence; Erscheinungen = appearances.\n- denklich = thinkable; unbrauchbar = unusable; Unverstandlichkeit (sic) = unintelligibility; Unmoglichkeit (sic) = impossibility.\n- Signs (g, (s, †, * and the bracketed deletions/insertions are carried over as in the source; the placement of the asterisk after Anfang follows the text.\n- Kant's first sentence has no verb agreement issues but the bracket [entsteht] is a deletion/insertion mark; its status is not clear from the passage.\n- irgendwann = at some time (could be read as 'somewhere' in a sloppier sense; the German is temporal).",[]],"reflm:3923":["Some principles are analytic and concern the formal of distinctness in our cognition. Some are synthetic and concern the material, as are the arithmetical, geometrical and chronological ones; likewise the empirical ones. But there are still principles that concern the use of reason in *synthesis* in general. The nature of our reason, however, has this law, that it cognizes things not immediately but mediately; hence it can expect everything that happens only according to a ground, and what is determined by no other ground is unreasonable to it.\nMatter is lifeless is a rational and not an empirical judgment, [it] because one also [assumes but] perceives much life in matter, but distinguishes this from the material properties. Against this: matter has attractive forces, is empirical.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsatz = principle; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Erkenntnis = cognition; erkennet = cognizes; Vernunft = reason; Urtheil = judgment; Kraft = force.\n- unvernünftig rendered 'unreasonable'; the German could also be 'contrary to reason'.\n- Eigenschaften and (elsewhere) Beschaffenheit are both rendered 'property'; here only Eigenschaften occurs.\n- warnimt = perceives (Wahrnehmung); the bracketed 'annimt aber' is a deletion in the source.\n- Allgemein/analytic classification of the empirical ones: 'imgleichen die empirischen' left elliptical as in German.",[]],"reflm:3924":["From the imperfect idea of substance it follows that one can hardly represent to oneself that which is and acts merely through foreign force, hardly as a different substance. Spinozism.\n===NOTES===\n- substantz (sic) = substance; Vorstellung = representation; Kraft = force.\n- schweerlich ... schwerlich: both rendered 'hardly'; the repetition is in the German.\n- 'as a different substance' could attach to 'represent' or to 'that which is and acts'; left as in German.",[]],"reflm:3925":["The *synthesis* is either one after another,[or] i.e. of the subordinated, or next to one another: of the coordinated. From the latter arise the judgments about the whole and its parts.\n===NOTES===\n- neben einander = next to one another; nach einander = one after another.\n- Ganzen = whole; Theile = parts; Urtheile = judgments.\n- [oder] is a bracketed deletion/insertion; its status is not clear.",[]],"reflm:3926":["How do empirical and synthetic judgments become general.\nDo we not perhaps have, besides the *principiis formalibus* of the rational propositions, also *formalia* of the synthetic and empirical ones. Likewise does one not have just so *principia formalia* of real connection as of logical.\n===NOTES===\n- allgemein = general; Sätze = propositions; Realverknüpfung = real connection (Verknüpfung = connection).\n- Urtheile = judgments.\n- 'imgleichen hat man nicht eben so' is awkward and left so; 'just so ... as of logical' is elliptical in the German.",[]],"reflm:3927":["Of the empirical fundamental concepts through abstraction there are countless ones that cannot be further resolved into their elements, although one can show the determination in its cause. Of the rational fundamental concepts, in which solely and alone the sensed properties of things let themselves be explained, there are, in the case of the outer objects: space, time, motion. In the case of the [outer] inner: A 1. immediate representation of the present, of the past, of the future. 2, comparison, distinction and sameness. 3. relation (logical) of connection and of conflict. 4. consciousness, judgments, inferences. B 1. feeling. pleasure, displeasure. 2. in relation to the judgment of the understanding or of the senses. C. desire etc etc. [fundamental concepts that are common to both sensations] Through the nature of the understanding, not *abstrahendo* but *iudicando*, arise fundamental concepts of *synthesis*. Existence, possibility, unity, substance, *accidens*, relation, [of the lo] *respectus realis*, *logicus*, necessary, contingent. Whole, a part. Simple, composite, ground, sequence, force, cause.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundbegriffe = fundamental concepts; Gegenstände = objects; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Schlüsse = inferences; Gefühl = feeling; Empfindungen = sensations.\n- Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Zufallig = contingent; Einfach = simple; Folge = sequence; Grund = ground.\n- Verhältnis = relation; 'relation' (Latin/German form) also appears in the list as written; Wiederstreit = conflict; einerleyheit = sameness.\n- Bestimung (sic) = determination. Begierde = desire.\n- Brackets: [äusse] deleted before 'innern'; [der lo] and the bracketed clause on 'beyden Empfindungen' are as in the source; the last may be a marginal insertion or deletion, not clear from the passage.",[]],"reflm:3928":["The *principia* of the form of all analytic judgments (g of the existential judgments of things absolutely or of the predicates relatively) are the proposition of identity and of contradiction, and [the subjective] the proofs are conducted in that through the *analysin* of the given concept the identity or opposition with the predicate is shown. The *principia* of the form of synthetic judgments are: what is at all times combined with a known part of the possible concept of a thing belongs along with it as a part to this concept etc etc. The proposition: every body is impenetrable, is analytic, because [the b] not only does the body not let itself be thought without impenetrability, but because it lets itself be thought solely through impenetrability; this mark belongs as *pars* to the *notion* of body. But: every body is inert, is a synthetic proposition; for inertia is *compars* with the concept [of the b] of that which is thought under the expression body, to a whole concept, which is necessarily combined with those partial concepts that belong in the *notion* of body. If one had the whole concept, of which the *notions* of the subject and predicate are *compartes*, then the synthetic judgments would transform into analytic ones. It is asked how far something arbitrary is here.\nSo much for the *nexus logicus*. As regards the *nexus realis*, the *principia materialia* of it are the experiences, the formal principles are: everything that happens has a determining ground, and secondly: everything has a first ground. These principles are both synthetic, the former for the use of our reason, the latter for the *terminus* of this use. For according to the former we look, in the series of causes determining one another, always to the higher grounds, and according to the latter we admit that this series is bounded. But it is just as impossible to represent to oneself a series of subordinated grounds that has no |\nbeginning, as to conceive how it begins. Meanwhile the proposition: everything that happens has a determining ground, this proposition, which makes an infinite series necessary, is the *principium* of the form of all our judgments of reason about real connection. But the proposition: every series of subordinated things and every successive series has a first, is a synthetic proposition, which is abstracted more from the boundaries of our understanding than from the object of cognition.\nThe first in the series of causes is always free choice. That this has no determining ground is an empirical proposition, but insofar uncertain; however its certainty depends on this, that otherwise there could be no first at all.\nOn the possibility of change, i.e. of arising and ceasing in general.\n===NOTES===\n- Satz = proposition; Grundsatz/principia = principles; Begriff = concept; Merkmal = mark; Theilbegriffe = partial concepts; Ausdruck = expression; Erkenntnis = cognition; Vernunft = reason.\n- Grentzen/begräntzt = boundaries/bounded (Grenze); Willkühr = choice; Veränderung = change; Gewisheit = certainty.\n- Latin kept in italics: analysin, pars, notion, compars, compartes, nexus logicus/realis, principia materialia, terminus, principium (notion appears in Latin form in the source).\n- 'Es frägt sich, wie weit hier willkührliches sey' = 'It is asked how far something arbitrary is here'; the sense of 'willkührliches' is left open.\n- | marks a page/line break in the middle of 'keinen | Anfang'. Bracketed [the subjective], [the b], [of the b] are deletions/insertions as in the source; the one before 'Beweise' is unclear.",[]],"reflm:3929":["Properly the representation of all things is the representation of our own state and the relation of one representation to the other according to our inner laws. The impossibility of separating concepts or the combination of them without all laws of our understanding are merely subjective; likewise also the possibility. We cognize the possibility of free actions only empirically; in rational understanding it conflicts with the laws of using our understanding. *Analysis sine termino* [is] makes the *synthesis* impossible. Hence according to the laws in the use of our understanding we posit a ground in everything that happens, but [according] just for that reason the *synthesis* is impossible (just this applied to the *analysin* of the body *sine termino*, where no *synthesis* is impossible). Of the first relations according to laws of our understanding no further ground can be given. (g The *analysis completa* led [in all actions] from the many to unity, in *serie successivorum* to the *principium*, in *simultaneis* to *monas*.)\nThe *axiomata* (*synthetica*) of philosophy go solely to the relation, which can be cognized only subjectively according to laws of our understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- Vorstellung = representation; Zustand = state; Verhältnis = relation; Gesetze = laws; Verstand = understanding; Verbindung = combination; erkennen = cognize; setzen = posit.\n- 'without all laws of our understanding' (ohne alle Gesetze) is left as in the German; it may be read as qualifying 'separating' and 'combination' or only the latter.\n- 'rationalem Verstande' = 'in rational understanding'; the German is not clear whether Verstand here means faculty or sense.\n- Brackets [ist], [nach], [in allen Handlungen] are deletions/insertions as in the source; 'Analysis sine termino [ist] macht' is as it stands.\n- Latin terms kept: analysis completa, serie successivorum, simultaneis, monas, axiomata, synthetica.",[]],"reflm:3930":["Some concepts are abstracted from sensations, others merely from the law of the understanding, to compare the abstracted concepts, [and] to combine or to separate them. The origin of the latter is in the understanding, that of the former in the senses. All concepts of such a kind are called pure concepts of the understanding, *conceptus intellectus puri*. To be sure we can set these activities of the understanding in motion only on the occasion of the sensible sensations and become conscious of certain concepts of the general relations of abstracted ideas according to laws of the understanding; and so Locke's rule also holds here, that without sensible sensation no idea becomes clear in us; but the *notiones rationales* arise indeed by means of the sensations and can also be thought only in application to the ideas abstracted from them, but they do not lie in them and are not abstracted from them. Just as in [Mathem] geometry we do not borrow the idea of space from the sensation of extended beings, although we can make [it gl] this concept clear only on the occasion of the [corporeal] sensation of corporeal things. Hence the idea of space is a *notio intellectus puri*, which can be applied to the abstracted idea of mountains and of casks.\n[Wissensch] Philosophy about [everything that] the concepts of the *intellectus puri* is metaphysics. It relates to the remaining philosophy as the *mathesis pura* to the *mathesis applicata*. The concepts of existence (g reality), of possibility, of necessity, of ground, of unity and plurality, of the whole and of the parts (g All, None), of the composite and the simple, of space, of time, of change, (g of motion,) of substance and of accident, of force and of action, and everything that belongs to ontology proper, is in relation to the remaining metaphysics like (g general) arithmetic in the *mathesis pura*.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriff = concept; Verstand = understanding; Empfindung = sensation; Gesetz = law; Raum = space; Daseyn = existence; Kraft = force.\n- *Idee* rendered 'idea'; *Wesen* = 'beings'; *Veränderung* = 'change' (not in glossary); *Vielheit* = 'plurality'; *Handlung* = 'action'.\n- 'Verheltnißen' (sic) = relations; 'bewust werden' = become conscious (Bewußtsein).\n- 'ihn gl' is an unfinished deleted reading, carried over as it stands; 'Mathem' and 'Wissensch' are abbreviated deletions.\n- 'der Handlung' pairs with Kraft; 'g' marks later insertions (Academy siglum), kept.",[]],"reflm:3931":["The first principia of empirical philosophy are propositions of experience that are general, from which one descends to the lower ones. Those of the rational are pure grounds of reason. In empirical philosophy one proceeds analytically, in the rational synthetically.\nWe can give grounds *in sensu subiectivo* for synthetic principles, but not *in sensu obiectivo*. Only for abstracted concepts can we give grounds through the *analysin obiectivam*, but for rational ones only through *analysin subiectivam*. Or rather: for rational propositions we can give grounds only through *analysin subiectivam*. E.g. for the proposition that a being exists necessarily, because namely our thoughts of a possibility without any actuality are [contradictory] void, since all possibility must be borrowed from something actual.\nProper metaphysics or pure philosophy consists of ontology.\n===NOTES===\n- Satz = proposition; Grundsatz = principle; Grund = ground; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Möglichkeit = possibility; Vernunft = reason.\n- The e.g.-sentence has no main clause ('Von dem Satz, daß ...'); left as a fragment as in the German.\n- 'von rationalen' leaves the noun unexpressed in the first sentence ('the rational'), as in German.\n- 'nichtig' = 'void'; the bracketed 'widersprechend' is a deleted reading.",[]],"reflm:3932":["The ideas and rules of reason are also used in the relation of empirical concepts, and this is their natural and correct use; but then they are grounded on *iudicia empirica primitiva*, which are general only through induction. But precisely these judgments of reason, insofar as they are pure, are supposed to be general in themselves. The *philosophia pura* is not, like the *mathesis pura*, certain in itself.\n===NOTES===\n- Gebrauch = use; Urteile = judgments; rein = pure; an sich (selbst) = in itself.\n- 'in dem Verhältnisse empirischer Begriffe' kept as 'in the relation of empirical concepts' (singular, as in the German).\n- 'sie sind alsden' ('then') takes 'sie' = ideas and rules; possible ambiguity retained.",[]],"reflm:3933":["In all identity of concepts [that not] two concepts agree in one, i.e. [a concept is common in both. In every judgment] a [mark] concept belongs to two; in every judgment two concepts belong to one thing. The thing that I think through the concept A, I think the very same |\nalso through the concept B. is a judgment (g of connection). The concept that I think in A, I also think in B: is a judgment of comparison.\nIn identity I compare two subjects that have one and the same predicate.\nIn unity I compare two predicates or concepts that have one subject.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriff = concept; Urteil = judgment; Verknüpfung = connection; Einheit = unity; Merkmal = mark (the bracketed 'Merkm' is deleted).\n- '[die nich]' is a truncated deleted reading ('nich' for 'nicht'), carried over.\n- The sentence 'ist ein Urtheil' has no expressed grammatical subject beyond the preceding clause; left as in German. The | is the Academy line/page break.\n- 'einerley' = 'one and the same'.",[]],"reflm:3934":["To the unthinkability of divine properties belongs not only his presence in all places of space at once, but also in all points of time at once. i.e. that he can represent to himself an eternity wholly.\n===NOTES===\n- Undenklichkeit = unthinkability (a rare word; literal).\n- Eigenschaften = properties; Örter = places; Raum = space; Zeit = time; vorstellen = represent (Vorstellung).\n- 'gantz' could modify 'eternity' or 'represent'; left as ambiguous as the German.",[]],"reflm:3935":["The proposition: all occurrences have sequels (each one), and all together in turn have sequels, are not one and the same.\nAll rational synthetic propositions are subjective, and conversely only the analytic are objective. *principia identitatis et contradictionis*.\nThe synthesis of reason (g rational) or of experience (g empirical).\nThe first is either of coordination: whole and parts, number and unity, or of subordination: ground and sequel.\n[Empirical] The second of coordination according to space and time.\n===NOTES===\n- Begebnisse (sic, for Begebenheiten) = occurrences; Folge = sequel; Zahl = number; Einheit = unity; Grund = ground.\n- 'Der Satz ... sind' has a plural verb after a singular subject; kept as in the German.\n- 'Gantze' = whole; 'Obiectiv' = objective.\n- 'g' = later insertion (Academy siglum); the bracketed 'Empirisch' is a deletion.",[]],"reflm:3936":["Everything is considered either *distributive* or *collective*. The former indicates a general concept, insofar as it is a common mark of the lower ones; the second indicates the taking-together of |\nmany. Since now the collection of everything cannot always occur, the *omnitudo collectiva* is sometimes imagined.\nThe *principia subiectiva*, when they are weighed objectively, contradict themselves. E.g. Everything has a ground, contradicts this proposition: nothing is necessary through itself etc etc.\n===NOTES===\n- gemeinschaftlich = 'common' (gemein also 'common'); Merkmal = mark; Satz = proposition; Grund = ground; Widerspruch = contradiction.\n- 'Zusammennehmung' rendered 'taking-together'; 'eingebildet' = 'imagined' (cf. Einbildungskraft).\n- 'wiedersprechen sich' can be 'contradict themselves' or 'one another'; the reflexive is ambiguous in the German.\n- 'betrachtet' = 'considered' (Betrachtung); 'erwogen' = 'weighed'.",[]],"reflm:3937":["One is supposed to think something as absolutely necessary, because everything that is, is necessary, but not everything can be hypothetically necessary. But one can think nothing as absolutely necessary. One must think the world as bounded, but one also cannot think the boundaries.\nThose synthetic propositions that by their nature do not cancel [through] the same conditions of comprehensibility that they posit, are valid objectively, as of the *commercio substantiarum*.\n===NOTES===\n- notwendig = necessary; Welt = world; Grenze = boundary (begrentzt = bounded); Bedingung = condition; Begreiflichkeit = comprehensibility (begreifen = comprehend); setzen = posit; gelten/gültig = valid.\n- 'Man soll sich ... denken' = 'One is supposed to think'; 'soll' could also be 'ought'.\n- 'als vom' = 'as of the'; the sense of 'als' is left open.\n- '[durch]' is a deleted reading; 'schlechthin' = 'absolutely'.",[]],"reflm:3938":["Besides the determinations without which objects cannot be, there are in our reason still conditions without which we cannot think certain objects through reason, even if these conditions are not determinations of the objects themselves. These *conditiones* are therefore subjective, and their concepts signify nothing in the object. All synthetic judgments of pure reason are accordingly subjective, and the concepts themselves signify relations of the actions of reason to itself.\n===NOTES===\n- 'obiecte' (Latinate) and 'Gegenstände' both = 'objects' (Object / Gegenstand).\n- Bestimmung = determination; Bedingung = condition; bedeuten = signify; Urteil = judgment; rein = pure; Verhältnis = relation; Handlung = action.\n- 'an dem Gegenstande' rendered 'in the object'; 'an' is open (in/on/of).",[]],"reflm:3939":["Logic treats the objective laws of reason, that is, how it ought to proceed. Metaphysics the subjective ones of pure reason, how it proceeds. In both reason is the object, for the (g rational) form is in every science of philosophy and mathematics. That reason, according to its laws, wants to treat the laws according to which it ought to think, brings it about that it avails itself of the rules that it first wants to invent. Hence come the tautologies and rules that are without practice.\n===NOTES===\n- Gesetz = law; Vernunft = reason; Form = form; Wissenschaft = science; Obiect = object; erfinden = invent.\n- The sentence 'Die metaphysik die subiectiven ...' has no verb in the German; left elliptical.\n- 'macht, daß sie sich ... bedient' = 'brings it about that it avails itself'; the antecedent of 'sie' (reason) is kept as a pronoun.\n- 'Tavtologien' = tautologies; 'seyn' (subjunctive-looking) rendered 'are'.",[]],"reflm:3940":["In all cognition of reason only relations are to be considered, and these are either given (sensibly or through the nature of reason) or fabricated. But we can fabricate no relations, (g of) whose possibility we can be convinced, except according to magnitude through repetition in the science of number. To fabricate with regard to the quality of the relations and thus (g first) to fabricate concepts of reason is just as impossible as first sensations of the senses.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkenntnis = cognition; Größe = magnitude; Zahl = number; Wissenschaft = science; möglich = possible; Empfindung = sensation; überzeugt = convinced (Überzeugung = conviction).\n- dichten = 'fabricate' (distinct from erfinden = 'invent' elsewhere).\n- 'qvalitaet' = 'quality'; 'Ansehung' in 'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'.\n- 'von' in '(g von) deren' is an insertion replacing/adding to the relative; carried over.",[]],"reflm:3941":["Metaphysical concepts go 1. merely to the relation of coordination: (g *absolutum et relativum*, whole, part, *continuum*, *discretum*) (g many) some, all (g the first, last, a single one); 2. or of subordination in the logical understanding: general or particular; 3. to subordination in the real understanding: ground, sequel, cause, effect. From this arises the concept of the first cause, of the last sequel, of the cause of everything, of some. 4. to existence: necessary, contingent (g possible); 5. Substance. (g Subject, predicate) simple, composite, *actio*, *passio* (g *vis*, *receptivitas*), *spontanea*, [*coacta*] *iners*. (g Whole of substances. World.)\nTime and space.\n| Duration, moment, eternity, beginning, end. Change, persistence. | Place, extension, point. | (g Space and time:) Motion, rest. Omnipresence.\nEffect in space: filling, force, mass.\nEffect in time: change, creation, annihilation.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstande/realverstande: 'im logischen Verstande', 'im realverstande' rendered literally with 'understanding' (Verstand); the idiomatic sense is left open.\n- Folge = sequel; Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; Grund = ground; Dasein = existence; zufällig = contingent; Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Ausdehnung = extension; Ort = place; Zeit/Raum = time/space; Welt = world; Kraft = force.\n- 'go to' renders 'gehen auf'; the grammar of items 1–5 (genitive 'der subordination' vs. 'auf ...') is irregular in the German and kept so.\n- 'Erfüllung' = 'filling'; 'Veränderung' = 'change'; 'Augenblik' = 'moment'.\n- The | marks are Academy line/page breaks; '[coacta]' is a deletion.",[]],"reflm:3942":["True is a cognition that agrees with the constitution of the object. Because through the idea of space the representation of outer objects is only possible, all axioms of space, and what is derived from them, agree with the object, likewise all relations of concepts according to the rule of identity. For the ideas then agree among one another. Yet since the metaphysical concepts of ground, substance etc. are not properly representations of the objects, in that even the most perfect sense cannot sense these in any thing and without these relations the things altogether, although not through our reason, can be represented: so these concepts are not objective; therefore in the axioms of them everything is subjective. Hence, if they are (g as) falsely regarded as objective, neither truth nor falsity holds of them. In general, if one wants to find the concept of cause, one will find, apart from the relations of connection according to ideas of time, no explanation that does not include a circle, and it seems nothing else.\nThe surest proof that they are not objective is that they stand in evident contradiction.\n===NOTES===\n- Beschaffenheit = constitution; Vorstellung = representation; Sinn = sense; Grund = ground; Verknüpfung = connection; Erklärung = explanation; Beweis = proof; Widerspruch = contradiction; Zeit = time; Raum = space.\n- 'obiecten'/'obiecte' = objects (Object); 'Warheit' = truth.\n- 'nur Möglich ist': 'only' may modify 'possible' or 'through the idea of space'; kept ambiguous.\n- 'sie scheint nichts anderes': 'sie' may refer to the concept (Begriff is masculine, so more likely Ursache/Erklärung); rendered 'it', ambiguity preserved.\n- 'gilt von ihnen' = 'holds of them' (gelten).",[]],"reflm:3943":["(g Metaphysic is the ground of the form of all philosophy.)\nThe utility of metaphysic [firstly in the] one can see best in the actual (g natural) use of it.\nThis utility is positive, to increase the insights;\nnegative, to prevent false metaphysic;\nin both parts the insight is objective, whereby one seeks to acquire the manifold objects of cognition, or subjective, in that one seeks to know the limitations of cognition.\nIt is noble. The subjective can be certain, although the objective is uncertain.",[]],"reflm:3944":["The logical form of our cognitions is to be distinguished from the metaphysical; the first is *analysis*, the second *synthesis*.",[]],"reflm:3945":["Concerning all objects of outer and inner senses we judge rationally, in that we [the laws of] through universal laws of appearances specify ground of them. The first possible laws of appearances cannot be established through experience, but the first grounds of the same rest on pure reason; *physica pura et pnevmatologia*.",[]],"reflm:3946":["M XXXXIII:\nAll pure philosophy is either logical or metaphysical. The former [judges] contains only the subordination of concepts under the *sphaeram* of the others, either immediately: in judgments, or mediately: in inferences. [This] It leaves, however, the concepts themselves, which can be subordinated to one another, undetermined, and does not decide which predicates pertain to things according to laws of pure reason. Hence to cognize the first predicates of things through pure reason is a matter of metaphysic, consequently to find the first basic concepts with which we judge through pure reason, and the principles.\nM XXXXIV:\n(s Metaphysic goes to cognitions solely through reason, logic to all, even empirical ones. All concepts of reason are universal; logic shows only the relation of the universal to the particular in general. Metaphysic, however, the origin of universal concepts, to which all cognition must be traced back, if appearances are to be transformed into concepts.)\nM XXXXIII:\nMetaphysic is therefore a science of the basic concepts and principles of human reason, and not of human cognition in general, in which there is much that is empirical and sensible; logic a science of the relations of universal concepts and propositions in general. Logic borrows concepts and propositions from metaphysic or from some empirical cognition and teaches to make use of them.\nLogic contains the concept of the universal, metaphysic universal concepts of reason (s the former deals with the relation of universal concepts to one another, the second indicates the universal |\nconcepts under which [other things] the concepts of reason of things are contained; the former is logical theory, for logical praxis is tautological, if the cognition of the subject is not presupposed); logic propositions that express the relation of the universal to the particular without predicate and subject, metaphysic however universal propositions. It contains the elements from which all cognition of reason is composed.\nM XXXXIV:\nLogic leaves the particular nature of human reason undetermined and is valid for every reason; metaphysic shows the universal concepts that flow from the nature of human reason, and their particular laws.",[]],"reflm:3947":["All rational cognition contains either the first grounds of that which is an object of merely pure cognition, or the first grounds of appearance. Dogmatic and objective are the cognitions that are valid for everyone and rest on mere concepts.",[]],"reflm:3948":["If the question were whether in empirical sciences new inventions are still to be hoped for, there is no doubt about it, because more experiences give more and new cognitions. If the question is whether in the science of pure reason [more] new inventions are to be expected, it is clear that in mathematic, logic and pure morals many are possible, because according to the rule of identity from universal concepts according to all sorts of arbitrarily given combinations consequences [will] be able to be inferred. But with metaphysic it is entirely different. Here it is not asked what in arbitrarily assumed |\nrelations flows from the universal to the particular according to the rule of identity, but which relations actually lie at the first ground of universal rules. Since now these relations are given neither through the senses in experience, nor through the understanding in an intuiting and singular concept, nothing further can happen than that one dissect one's confused ideas. From this, however, only a science of the subject can arise. Since no object is given here, we would also be able to invent nothing about it through this cutting and dissecting of our ideas.",[]],"reflm:3949":["Under the logical rules stand mathematic and morals (*purae*), under the metaphysical the pure cognition of that which falls into outer and inner sense, consequently the first grounds of reason of outer and inner appearances. In both one must weigh that which is the most universal of the outer and inner senses, according to its first grounds of reason.\nThus there is no philosophy of pure reason except logic and *metaphysica*.\nThe basic concept of logic is that of the universal, insofar as it contains something under itself or not; according to this relation things are thought, but in it they are not represented. The concept of species or genus is not given, but is only the relation according to which *conceptus dabiles* can be compared.\nLogic gives no basic concepts of reason at all, but of the laws according to which we [ourselves] in general make the cognitions distinct through one another. Thus it contains the rules without which we cannot get any distinct cognition of the things; metaphysic, however, the rules without which objects cannot be cognized by us at all. The logical propositions are [willkü] rules which we use arbitrarily as means in order to make cognitions distinct through comparison. Metaphysic shows the marks that are given through the nature of reason (logic only the use of marks in general).",[]],"reflm:3950":["Synthetic [propositions] concepts are space, time and number. [and are objective.]\nIf the concepts are also synthetic, but the propositions are analytic, i.e. according to the rule of identity, then they are objective.\nBut if concepts are subjective, i.e. of no object at all, neither of a given condition of the representation of objects: space and time, nor [of] arbitrary concepts of quantity, then the judgments are nonetheless objective and universally valid, i.e. either universally true or false, [but are] if their form is analytic. But if [then] their form is synthetic, then they are subjective, and objectively they hold only as rules of appearances, consequently as empirical judgments.",[]],"reflm:3951":["Dogmatic *philosophia pura* contains two disciplines.\n1. The rules of the universally valid use of the understanding;\n2. the rules of the universally valid use of the free will.\nIn both judgment is made according to the *nexus logicus* from no data of experience; but in *physiologia transscendentalis* the data from experience are given according to the relations of space and time in such a way that the most universal concept of the object of all outer and inner sensations is taken and the ground of it is sought.",[]],"reflm:3952":["Metaphysic is a science of the laws of pure human reason and thus subjective. Objective pure philosophy |\nhas either analytic *principia* without any axioms of experience or synthetic ones. The former [consists] rests on universal judgments according to the rule of identity and on the subordination of the particular under the universal. Thus the universally valid rules of the understanding and will, consequently *Logica et Moralis pura*. The second has as object the most universal of outer and inner sense and thus contains the pure grounds of reason to which all natural sciences of outer and inner sense can be brought.",[]],"reflm:3953":["The principles of space are objective; but whether a thing is in space or not is subjective, because the concept of space is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3954":["A principal error arises from this, if one [compares] mixes up the *genus* with all *individua* and regards as a sequel according to concepts that which is only a lawlike appearance for the immediate intuiting. Hence the ground is nothing objective. What we call a (g real) ground is only a concomitant appearance for the intuitive concept. And that which we call reason, likewise all its pure concepts, in which we infer from the universal to the particular, have their reality, principally with regard to their basic concepts, only in that they lie in the things and can be abstracted from them. Accordingly our reason is only a supplement to the lack of *intuitus*.\nIf I say, therefore: a principle is subjective, i.e. it contains the conditions under which alone we can judge through our reason according to laws of experience, this does not signify that our reason must accept this law in the objects; for it does not concern the same at all; one can therefore say neither that it is false nor true.",[]],"reflm:3955":["All cognitions are either empirical, insofar as they presuppose sensations, or pure cognitions, insofar as they have no sensation as ground. The latter, namely the pure cognitions, are either [singular] *conceptus singulares* and are called *intuitus puri* or universal [concepts] and are pure concepts of reason. The empirical cognitions are sensation, appearance and empirical concept; from the first the matter of all cognition is to be derived, the second adds the form of intuition to it, the third brings both under a universal concept.",[]],"reflm:3956":["Immediate judgments of experience according to relations of space and time must be distinguished from reflected ones according to relations of reason.",[]],"reflm:3957":["M XXXXVII:\nAll human cognitions can be divided into two main genera: 1. those that arise from the senses [or] and are called empirical; 2. those that are not acquired at all through the senses [in the], but have their ground in the constant nature of the [power of cognition] thinking power of the soul, and can be called pure representations. Since all materials for thinking must necessarily be given through our senses, the matter of our entire cognition is empirical. Just for that reason all pure concepts must go merely to the form of cognitions. Now we have a twofold form of cognitions: the intuitive and rational form. The first takes place only in the immediate cognition of singular things, the second in universal representations; the first I will call intuiting, the second concepts of reason. Now in all empirical cognition one can look first |\nmerely at the matter, and this consists in sensation; second, at the form of appearance [and this consists]; third, at the form of reason in concepts. The form of appearances rests solely on space and time, and these concepts arise through no senses or sensation, but rest on the nature of the mind, according to which the different sensations can be represented under such relations. Hence, if all sensation of the senses is set aside, that of space and time is a pure concept of intuition, and because in it lies everything that the understanding can cognize in experiences, it is a concept of the understanding; and, although the appearances are empirical, it is nevertheless intellectual. Likewise sensations and appearances made universal are not pure but empirical concepts of reason; but if one leaves out all effect of the senses, then the concepts are pure concepts of reason, as: possible, substance etc. Hence all pure concepts are intellectual and intuitive, or rational and [discursive] reflecting concepts. |\nM XXXXVIII: |\nFurthermore all cognitions are either given or fabricated. The matter of cognition cannot be fabricated, thus only the form, and in the form only the repetition; thus all fabrication of reason goes only to mathematics; on the other hand the form that is given in geometry is space.\nM XXXXVII:\n(g Because one can describe only the form of experience, it belongs to the understanding.)\nM XXXXVIII:\nA science of pure reason is possible and also necessary. But it is either philosophy or mathematics; the former weighs what there is, and reason merely which *respectus* there is; the latter how many times one and the same thing is. Space is an object for philosophy as well as for mathematics. The judgments about space that are intuitive are not yet mathematical, nor philosophical. Both assess space not\n|\na priori [through the Ver] from principles of space, which reason cognizes, but from principles a posteriori, i.e. intuitively and singly, though not empirically. The philosophy of pure reason is either dogmatic, and there its object is given through that which all senses have most in common and as it were through the genus of the senses: thus first power of representation and faculty of desire as the objects of inner sense, 2. space and time, hence general mechanics, as the most general of outer senses; hence the dogmatic pure philosophy is theoretical logic, theoretical morals and general natural science. Second, it is critical, hence subjective. It is zetetic, skeptical, problematic.",[]],"reflm:3958":["All cognitions [from experience are either] from experience (g empirical) belong either to sensation and contain the matter of empirical cognition, or to appearance and [comprehend] contain at the same time the form, or to the concept and contain the [what to many objects] universal of different sensations or appearances. Sensation represents singular objects, insofar as they touch the senses, e.g. red, black, sweet, hard, warm etc., [which] consequently only the matter of empirical cognition. The form of objects is thought according to space and time. The form of empirical cognition is that of coordination, that of rational cognition is that of subordination.\nOne may leave out all matter of cognition, consequently everything that touches the senses, then the empirical form of appearances still remains; one may leave this out too, then the rational form remains; |\nand cognitions of the first kind are pure concepts of intuitions, of the second pure concepts of reason.",[]],"reflm:3959":["Metaphysics contains nothing but ontology*, which is falsely considered as a science of things in general *qvoad praedicata vniversalia et disiunctiva*.\n(g for it is no matter, consequently a something in general, the object.)",[]],"reflm:3960":["All cognitions are either sensible cognitions or cognitions of reason. The former are either of sensation breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3961":["All (g cognitions from) experience are called empirical and are either [representations of sensation or of appearances senses or] sensations or appearances or concepts. In the first everything is given through the sense [in the second] and merely the matter for cognition; the second contains in itself the sensations at the same time according to the form of space and time; the third [likewise] the sensations or appearances made universal through reason.\nIf one leaves out the two last actions, sensation remains. If one leaves this out, pure concepts remain: 1. of the understanding: of coordination, 2. of reason: subordination.",[]],"reflm:3962":["All concepts become universal through abstraction, but they a|rise\nnot all from it; *non subtrahendo a sensibus oriuntur, sed abstrahendo*.",[]],"reflm:3963":["Cognitions of reason are either given in the senses as to matter and have only the form of reason, e.g. universal concepts, or they express the form of reason itself; the former are empirical, the latter *notiones purae*.",[]],"reflm:3964":["Metaphysics is a critique of pure reason and no doctrine. Logic is the doctrine 1. of pure, 2. of mixed reason. The application of pure reason to objects that are given through experience is only with the empirical concepts, where one can infer from the universal to the particular.\nThe science of a cognition considered subjectively is critique, of that considered objectively is doctrine.\nThere are certain universal concepts that are given through the nature of reason, according to which others (g and their relation) are thought. [But there are others] E.g. subject and predicate. These are metaphysical. There are representations that are given through the senses and are made universal through reason.",[]],"reflm:3965":["There are pure concepts of intuitions, arbitrary ones of fabrication and universal ones of reason.\nA concept that cannot be regarded as an impression of the senses is pure.\nPrinciples:\n[1. What we do not through intuitions] 1. We cognize everything through judgments that we do not cognize through intuitions.\n2. All judgments of our reason are mediate.",[]],"reflm:3966":["The logical essence is the subjective basic concept and is not valid for all, is also changeable; the real essence is objective; the former goes to the meaning of a word, which admittedly wears down gradually and becomes unanimous through use.",[]],"reflm:3967":["Reason feels the need of a real ground and yet cannot think it according to its own laws; from this it is to be seen that this concept is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3968":["All connection is either of subordination or coordination [the former is either de]. The unity, when a sequel is subordinated to many grounds, is the unity a priori, e.g. in the utility of things. The second: when something is the ground of many, or when many sequels arise from one ground.\nIt is special that, when many grounds agree toward one sequel, one also thinks a common ground for it, e.g. the many things for the utility of the human being, many members on an animal.",[]],"reflm:3969":["Our reason contains nothing but *relationes*. If now these are not given through the relations according to space and time in experience, nor through the repetition and the composition of the one out of many in pure mathematics, then they are no *relationes* that go to objects, but only relations of our concepts according to laws of our reason.",[]],"reflm:3970":["Intuitive concepts of the senses, concepts of the understanding of abstraction, concepts of reason of the [relation] relation, which general cognitions have.\nThe relations of the senses are space and time, those of the understanding: the general and particular, consequently also all judgments and the rules of all syllogisms. Those of reason: the ground and the sequel, namely objective: the ground of things (real ground).\nThe relation of sameness and difference is logical (g likewise the relation of the general and particular), [all] that of repetition is mathematical, the remaining ones are metaphysical — namely the real relations.\nMetaphysics is the critique of human reason, logic is the general doctrine of the same; the first is subjective and problematic, the second objective and dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:3971":["True is that which is valid for every cognition, and thus not merely under the condition of the reason that judges mediately. Accordingly that is true whose opposite contradicts itself, because the opposite cannot even once fall into the senses. But that something be without ground indicates only a subjective condition, and the object could fall into the senses even without this.",[]],"reflm:3972":["The concept of the ground (g of the sequel) contains not only that something that is there is accompanied by something else, but moreover that this relation is general and necessary; for, where such a thing (g b) is, there is such a ground a, and where a [ground] a is, there is the sequel b. Now all real grounds and even the possibility of them are knowable only *a posteriori*; but these indeed show a constant |\naccompaniment, but no generality of connection, consequently the concept ground is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3973":["No other merely arbitrary concepts of pure reason can arise in us than those through repetition, consequently of numbers and magnitude. All [other] concepts of reason, which contain another relation, require that this relation be given through experience or the nature of the understanding and reason; empirical relations, however, presuppose a fundamental-relation-concept. The absolute [can] and simple one cannot invent.",[]],"reflm:3974":["In the use of pure reason a particular confusion arises from the mixing up of these 2 concepts. Everything (*singuli*) has a ground; but everything taken together cannot have a ground, therefore something is without ground. The first is a usable rule of reason for the explanation of appearances; the second a sequel of the *synthesis completa*, which is impossible for our understanding. Likewise: everything that is to be posited through reason is necessary; but everything is necessary under its *hypothesi*.\nAll concepts are either sensible concepts or concepts of reason. The first are either of sensation or of appearance; these have as ground of the form space and time. The second can be found through no *analysin* of experience, although all experience is coordinated to them, and are pure concepts of reason, if through them no object of experience is thought; but if the latter, then they are empirical concepts. E.g. a *genus* is a pure concept, but a stone in general or the *genus* of stone an empirical one.\nThe science of reason of the rules of judging objectively, i.e. of all [concepts] judgments and inferences, insofar as they arise *per analysin*, is logic. The science of reason of [analytic] synthetic cognitions and judgments is metaphysics.\nSpace is no concept of reason, but metaphysics seeks the concept of reason of it.",[]],"reflm:3975":["Because through reason, i.e. through the cognition that is no sensation, only concepts arise, through which it remains undetermined whether the thing is posited or not, something belongs to it that compels us to posit something. Thus the concept of a ground has a subjective origin, and the word: something is posited through another thing or for the sake of another, signifies objectively only: things accompany each other or follow upon one another.\nThe idea of the ground (g cause) arises from experiences.\nTo cognize through reason that something is not or that something is not possible is one and the same; that something is or that something is necessary is one and the same. On the other hand everything appears as contingent that I indeed cognize through senses, but not through reason, that it is.\nThe only concepts of pure reason, through whose relation according to the rule of identity truths can be discovered, are the magnitudes; but these too are arbitrary inventions.",[]],"reflm:3976":["It is according to the subjective law of reason necessary to assume a first action, through which the rest all follows; but it is just as necessary to assume a ground in general of every action and thus no first.\nThere are pure fundamental concepts of intuition or of reflection; the first are the principles of appearance, the second of insight; the first show the coordination, the second subordination. Because everything is represented in time, all our concepts of reason are nevertheless always at the same time thought under the condition of the *phaenomeni*; the conditions of the |\nsecond do not agree with the first. In time no first is possible, and in the ground there is to be one.\nIf the comprehensibility of a [action] thing were to be complete, then we would have to have a first ground; but we also cannot posit a first ground through reason, and so it follows from this that no comprehensibility of an absolute *position* is complete through human reason.\nThe simple concepts of reason, indeed all simple concepts are subjective; the objective ones consist in the agreement of the cognition with itself and are thus composite. Hence the concept of the first is subjective, for it contains the concept of everything.\nAll real relations (space and time excepted) are given through experience (g according to the relations of space and time) and can thus serve for no propositions of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:3977":["All truth consists in the necessary agreement of a cognition with itself. If the cognition that is to agree with itself makes up the form of appearance itself, e.g. space and time, then every judgment [subjec] is objective and either true or false. But if the cognition merely concerns a law of human reason, through which we compare concepts, then it is not at all objective, hence neither true nor false. Hence ground and sequel is no attribute at all of things, which given through mere reason, but is given only through experience. But it is a law of reason to look to this relation; all general rules of reason about cause and effect have no validity at all of objects.",[]],"reflm:3978":["Every objective concept is either an archetype (*archetypon*) or |\nan ectype (*Ectypon*). The former occurs when the concept of the object is thought arbitrarily or at least without it no concept of the objects [mo] and no experience is possible. The latter occurs when the object is given through the senses. The pure concepts of reason, which are given through the nature of reason, are [are] not ectypes, secondly also not archetypes, for they are not thought arbitrarily, but contain the ground of the possibility of all arbitrary ideas of reason. They are also general concepts; consequently they do not contain the form of experiences, and without them are always [representations] immediate ideas of experience possible; consequently they are only condition-concepts, without which a cognition through our reason is impossible, hence not objective.\nAll cognitions of reason have the logical form, but the metaphysical form only those [in] which real fundamental concepts or principles of pure reason presuppose.",[]],"reflm:3979":["M XXXXI:\nA dogmatic science of pure reason has arbitrary ideas as ground, and thinks their relations to itself in logical form. But no relations can be thought arbitrarily except those of repetition, i.e. of numbers, and consequently the science is mathematics; but one can also seek the rule of choice itself, insofar as it is to be generally valid, and this is morals.\nWhere the concepts are given neither through experience nor through the nature of reason, there choice is the ground of them, either according to the form, if no matter (g object) is given at all (but the arbitrary form of concepts is only the mathematical), |\nM XXXXII: |\nor (g pure) choice (g in general) is itself the matter (g the |\nobject) and form for the concept, and the judgments in both are made according to logical form. In mathematics it goes: I want to think to myself these relations, which rules follow from them according to logical form. In morals: I want to think to myself choice itself in relations, which rules hold for it in general. Thus is mathematics the breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3980":["If I want to understand something in nature, then I must not go out of nature with my explanation. But if I want to understand the entire nature, then I must be outside its boundaries.",[]],"reflm:3981":["The pure concepts of reason have no *exemplaria*, but are themselves the archetypes; but the concepts of our pure reason have this reason itself as archetype, are therefore subjective and not objective.",[]],"reflm:3982":["The logical laws of reason contain the rules according to which the concepts are subordinated, one to the *sphaera* of the other, which is called logical subordination. The metaphysical laws are those according to which the concepts are subordinated to one another *realiter*.",[]],"reflm:3983":["All concepts are either archetypes, which [Begr] are grounds of the determinations that pertain to the objects and through which the object itself is determined among all possible ones, or they are copies, which |\nare sequels of the determinations of things. Because through space and time all concepts of experience are Possible, so breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3984":["Everything that happens, i.e. what one is compelled [through the] before experience to cognize as happened, has a ground; but what one wants to happen has no further ground. For every object that is determined through choice has no further ground why it is thought so and not otherwise than this choice. Because something first arises through the will, so just as little can it be asked in the case of arbitrary ideas, which are a ground of actions, as in the case of arbitrary speculative ideas breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3985":["We can posit nothing through reason without subordinating it to another. We can think no subordination without the ground being thought as *antecedens*, hence the entire series being thought *per synthesin a priori completam*.\nNo synthesis is *completa* except only that which is completed by successively progressing (*successive progrediendo*) in a time. If, therefore, a whole is given, then we posit that *regrediendo* a *terminus* is found, from which the *synthesis completa* begins, consequently that a whole is neither divisible to infinity nor composed to infinity. But the *synthesis infinita* (eternity) never gives a whole.",[]],"reflm:3986":["One can assume that the motion of a body is only a successive presence of a great efficacy of impenetrability in space |\nis, where not the substance changes the place, but this effect of impenetrability succeeds in different places one after another, as with sound the air waves. One can also assume that there are no substances at all in space, but a greater or lesser efficacy of a single highest cause in different places of space. From this it would follow that matter would be infinitely divisible.",[]],"reflm:3987":["The concept of God is the only idea that is thoroughly determined through reason and also given as the highest ground of all determination.",[]],"reflm:3988":["[In our judgments we attend either only to the idea of a thing alone and to that through which we think it, or to its relation to others.]\nWe have two kinds of concepts: those that can arise in us through the presence of the thing; or those [which] through which the understanding represents to itself the relation of these concepts to the laws of its own thinking. To the latter belongs the concept of the ground, |\nof possibility, of existence. Hence the principles about the former are objective, those about the latter subjective.\nMetaphysics is a science to have insight into the relation of human reason to the first properties of things.\nAll rational basic concepts are concepts of form, the empirical ones are *principia* of matter. The former are merely subjective, i.e. abstracted from the [action] laws of our thinking, the latter objective, from the representation itself, through which the object is represented. The understanding is applied to experiences only according to laws of the understanding, but the separated idea of the relation of the representation of sensation in general according to laws of the understanding constitutes the pure rational concept. The understanding proceeds according to a natural law when it thinks one and many. This understanding, applied to the sensation of a body [abstrah], abstracts the idea of a whole not from it, but from itself.\nThe *philosophia pura* about the rational concepts is only subjective and never synthetic; the *principia* of the *philosophiae applicatae domestica* are the first laws of experience in combination with the *principiis rationalibus*, insofar as these relate to the *empirica*. The proposition: nothing comes from nothing, and the proposition: everything has a ground, have the same fault, that they are expressed rationally, though they are valid only empirically.\nThe idea of an undetermined freedom cannot be thought at all according to laws of our understanding; but it is not false on that account.\nBy what does one cognize the multiplicity and unity of substances? The representation of I is actually a unity; but is the difference of places a proof of multiplicity?\nIf one only observes breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3989":["A–A=0 signifies an empty representation, for negating is not |\nempty; but whether an empty representation signifies as much as something impossible is a question.",[]],"reflm:3990":["If I first posit something and afterwards cancel it, then 0 (g *rei*), if I posit and cancel at the same time, then [0 *cogitationis*\n. . . 0] impossible. The impossible does not signify merely *conceptum inanem*, whereby nothing at all is thought; for it is possible to think nothing at all.",[]],"reflm:3991":["Through the *principium contradictionis* what is cognized is not the possibility of things, but of the predicates of the same or their relation.",[]],"reflm:3992":["Affirmative judgments serve for cognition of the identity of a thing with others, negative ones for distinction. Hence tautological propositions are ridiculous.\nReason contains nothing but *respectivae notiones*; the senses, since they cognize without reflection, must give something absolute.\nWe must here take great care that reason judges about itself according to its own laws, consequently also not always objectively.",[]],"reflm:3993":["From the nature of our understanding flows the idea of bounds, i.e. of the first and the last.",[]],"reflm:3994":["There are 2 concepts that are not properly predicates of things, but through which the things themselves are thought with all their predicates.",[]],"reflm:3995":["In metaphysics one must, as in optics, take care that the combinations or separations of concepts, whose ground is subjective, are not held to be objective.",[]],"reflm:3996":["There are two principles of the objective laws of reason, which are analytic; two principles that are subjective, and are synthetic. The latter are principles of every [use] real use of reason: the rules how we are to use reason in all real relations, even with the senses. Falsely these are regarded as principles of pure reason, which are objective.",[]],"reflm:3997":["Above and \n*Conceptus ponens*: A; *tollens*: non A; [*deficiens*\n= A×0] *deficiens*\n=0; *conceptus inanis*: to which no object corresponds, e.g. *vis qvaedam ficta* (a certain feigned force). *Conceptus implicans* = A non A (non A + non A). *Ens ponens*: A; *ens tollens*: –A; *ens deficiens*: A–A=0. *realiter oppositum sibi non contradictorium*, and the impossible is different from the not possible.",[]],"reflm:3998":["The proposition can be immediately certain; only its immediacy can be uncertain (g be concealed), as: a whole is greater than a part.\nThere are concepts that are problematic for reason, others that are dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:3999":["Because we form rational concepts only through judgments, but in these the thing is represented through predicates, we represent to ourselves an object of thoughts and its being or not-being particularly as a predicate; if we abstract from this [and the] there still always remains an object of thought, which, insofar as it does not cancel itself, is something possible.\nThe possibility of judgments is contradistinguished from truth and the possibility of things from actuality.\nThe contradictions of pure reason arise from this, because it does not, like empirical philosophy, draw the general propositions from particular ones, but judges generally from (g pure) concepts, but nonetheless needs a *terminus* [*a priori*] (g of subordination or coordination), which contradicts the generality.\nThrough reason we can cognize entirely only the possibility of judgments, but not of things, because to these belongs the presupposition of the possibility of the matter, which must be given through the senses and thus *a posteriori*. According to matter the understanding cannot even invent anything impossible. The falsity of the form in the synthesis can be settled through nothing but through the conflict of the sequels with other certain cognitions.\nPossibility is only a form, but the possible also contains matter.",[]],"reflm:4000":["Some things are valid objectively under an arbitrary condition, others subjectively under a (g through the understanding) given condition. These given conditions are appearances (space and time lie every|\nwhere at the ground). Hence although, if the concepts ground and cause were objective, one of two propositions: There is a first cause or: there is none, would have to be true; but now, since neither of the two is objective, both as subjective laws are true at the same time.",[]],"reflm:4001":["It is difficult to represent how the sequel is after the ground, if this relation is not merely a *phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:4002":["Where there are nothing but real connections, there is no 0, also no alteration. All alterations in the world therefore stem from real oppositions; the last effect of them is therefore 0.",[]],"reflm:4003":["Impossibility of propositions and impossibility of judgments. If I include the condition of impossibility in the concept of the thing, then it is absolute impossibility according to concepts or according to experience.",[]],"reflm:4004":["Possible is every concept in which there lies a *praedicat* that is considered in the subject and does not contradict it; but not every *synthesis* is possible in which there is no contradiction. i.e. real relations are not seen into through the proposition of contradiction.",[]],"reflm:4005":["We can cognize through reason only conditioned possibility. The absolute possibility that we cognize through reason, |\nhappens according to the *principium contradictionis* and concerns propositions into which we resolve the concepts.\nAll absolute possibility must otherwise be given through experience; what is there is possible in itself.",[]],"reflm:4006":["In all elementary representations we can borrow possibility only from actuality, because those are given through these only for thinking.\nIf something happens under certain conditions always in one and the same way and yet has in these conditions no natural ground, then it must have the ground in an aim or in freedom. e.g. if the game always falls on one side. Thus freedom is a first ground and is not understood along with what happens.",[]],"reflm:4007":["Of everything that is there, we must cognize a ground, if we want to cognize through reason that it is*; thus we cannot cognize the absolutely necessary. In everything subordinated we must assume a first, which is thus necessary in itself**; thus it is a dispute of subjective laws.\n*(s thus it is a subjective rule of reason. 2. everything that (g contingent) is an object of experience, (happens), has a ground: is an objective rule of [senses] experience.)\n**(s principle, which we assume only because we otherwise could not see into something a priori.)",[]],"reflm:4008":["Everything that (g comes to be) happens, is through something, is similar to the proposition:\nEverything that comes to be, is from something. From nothing comes nothing, i.e. substance cannot come to be. Which only indicates that our understanding is valid only *in consequentia* and that we must assume in all explanations of nature that substance is at all times. Arising and perishing, coming to be and ceasing are also bound to time and hold only for phenomena.\nIt is also a law of our reason: The supreme cause (one or many) is always, can neither arise nor perish. Also includes the relation of time in itself.",[]],"reflm:4009":["In this demonstration 1. the word nothing in the logical understanding (which signifies as much as the general negation in the judgment and affects the *copula* of a general judgment) is mixed up with the nothing, namely the *remotion* of things. e.g. no thing has a ground, nothing has a ground, and: the nothing is the ground of some things.\nThus the author also did with the *definitio* of the *minimum*.",[]],"reflm:4010":["Everything has a supreme logical ground\nand last logical sequel.\n(g The former the most general concept, which is under none, the latter the single one, under which none is contained.)",[]],"reflm:4011":["Is a necessary hypothesis of healthy reason, thus holds |\nonly in the world and is also the principle of the use of the same in it. Everything has a first ground, is also such a principle, because it begins a posteriori; but to begin a priori from the first ground and to posit it itself, is an action of original reason.",[]],"reflm:4012":["The *principium rationis*, likewise the *principium rationati*, is a rule of healthy reason and thus restricted to the objects of experience. The boundaries of the world of sense are thus also the boundaries of its use. If one goes further and wants to make the validity of these universal, then one errs and brings it into science.",[]],"reflm:4013":["Everything that is there has a ground, is a merely subjective principle of reason and has no relation to the possibility of appearances. What happens there has a ground, signifies that wherein I am passive and observe it. But what I do voluntarily is for me not something that happens. (s Is a conditional proposition of the reality of our |\nrepresentations, which is opposed to the nullity of the same, namely fantasies. If the representation of an object is not to be [given] made by us, but be given, then it must be posited according to [all] a general rule under certain conditions, through which the relation of this object to the representation and the existence of the same is also valid for others. Entirely different is when the representation is made by us (through freedom); for then we are immediately conscious of its reality, i.e. a state in us that we ourselves make can be cognized as a true state, even without ground.\nSomething for what to cognize, understand, see into, comprehend. We cognize that which is given to us, for what; i.e. we give its representations an object through the function of the understanding.\nWhen, where, why. *accidentia* and composition are not things.)",[]],"reflm:4014":["Everything that arises has a ground;\nEverything that perishes also a positive ground.",[]],"reflm:4015":["Everything signifies either the sufficiency (g of the derivation) *a priori* or the completeness of the taking together *a posteriori*; e.g. We can say something in general of all motion, but not cognize all motion together.",[]],"reflm:4016":["The cognition of the logical essence is acquired merely analytically, that |\nof the real essence synthetically. The former is the first subjective basic concept, the latter the first objective. *In conceptibus arbitrariis qva talibus* subjective and objective are one and the same.",[]],"reflm:4017":["*praedicata logica* are concepts, [which] through which I can cognize [can] or can posit certain things. Accordingly all concepts are predicates; they signify, however, either things or their position: the former is a real predicate, the second only a logical one.\nConcepts are indeterminate with regard to many predicates, but things are not. Therefore thoroughgoing determination also depends on actuality and is in existence more than in possibility. This also flows from the fact that through possibility I posit merely the concept, through actuality the thing. If God takes existence from a thing, he takes from it no predicate, but the thing itself (s but not the possibility or [its] the concept of the thing). Whoever denies existence removes the thing with all its predicates.\nExistence can indeed be a logical, but never a real predicate of a thing.",[]],"reflm:4018":["The existential propositions express: how we are to think things, not: what; namely absolute, not respective.",[]],"reflm:4019":["Given is an object, insofar as it is thoroughly determined. If through its concept it is thoroughly given, then it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:4020":["Existence goes only to things, actuality also to their actions.",[]],"reflm:4021":["We can represent to ourselves nothing as possible except what has been given in the senses, as much in its *data* as in its real relations; thus our possibilities are merely modifications of that whose general ground is given through experience.",[]],"reflm:4022":["The proposition that a possible exists in a necessary way signifies as much as: *sublata omni actualitate tollitur possibilitas*; whether this latter proposition is true must be investigated.",[]],"reflm:4023":["If every thing only *in nexu omnimodo* with all determinations of the supreme cause and thus also with its whole effect, but of that being, however, [only] the whole effect only a single one is: then everything that does not actually exist is also not possible *in totum*.",[]],"reflm:4024":["Magnitude is cognized either through composition or through limitation of a given all. The first always requires time.\nWhy is *mathesis* capable of such evident propositions and of such correctness? Origin of its concepts.\nOur faculty to count is (g in itself) unlimited; if it [no] is not limited, it is indeterminate; for it cannot be taken whole. But in the *realissimum* the *omnitudo* precedes the limitations.",[]],"reflm:4025":["*transscendentaliter* something is considered when it is weighed relatively to its (s own) essence* as the sequel; *metaphysice*, when the essence is considered with regard to its sequels as a ground.\n*(s if it is considered respectively to the essence of things in general, it is called metaphysical.)",[]],"reflm:4026":["Transcendental unity.\nMetaphysical unity, truth and perfection. Agreement of one as a ground to the manifold, of the manifold to one as sequel and of the manifold among one another.",[]],"reflm:4027":["Transcendental unity is logical, respectively to the essence of the thing.\nMetaphysical unity is real, respectively to possibility in general.\nThat is the unity of the taking together, where something does not contradict itself.\nThis: unity of connection, where the many is a ground of One and the unity is not arbitrary.",[]],"reflm:4028":["All finite things are defective as things in general (not *transcendentaliter*). One does indeed supplement the imperfection of the other; but because it is another thing, it replaces the imperfection only in the whole.\nThe perfection of a thing respectively to a certain concept or [respectively to] with regard to the concept of a thing in general is the *completudo rationis*, the former the respective, the latter the metaphysical.\nGoodness (*bonitas*) is the ground of perfection, *unum ad quod consentitur*.\nThe goodness of human beings is the moral. Relative goodness is that which is only a means to [absol] something else, which pleases.",[]],"reflm:4029":["Perfection taken *substantive* or *adiective* [betr].\nIn the latter case it is no quality or quantity or relation, but only the *modus* of a determination; *perfectio substantive sumta* is a determination in general, insofar as this *modus* pertains to it; with regard to *entitas* reality is that to which the *modus* of perfection pertains.",[]],"reflm:4030":["Necessity is combined with every categorical position of reason.\nSomething is posited through concepts.\n*Necessarium et contingens* is either absolute or hypothetical.\nLogical or real necessity. This the absolute or hypothetical.\nNominal concept through omission of the condition.\n*oppositum entis sibi non contradicit*. *Conceptus terminator*.",[]],"reflm:4031":["Law of reason, not of the senses:\nEverything that is, is necessary, either simply or conditioned.\nAll necessity is conditioned, but under the condition of the inner determinations of a thing (of that which is inwardly in the thing) or under outer conditions.",[]],"reflm:4032":["Everything that happens is contingent in itself; but because it nevertheless must be necessary by virtue of the above, it is necessary through a foreign ground.",[]],"reflm:4033":["All (g absolute) necessity is either of judgments or of things. The first, as the logical, is at all times a conditioned necessity of the predicate. The necessity of things that we can cognize is at all times conditioned; for in itself we can at all times cancel every thing, because, since we affirm nothing, we also contradict nothing through the negation. The concept of the necessary is nevertheless first a concept given through reason, because through it alone something is determined. Absolute necessity is a boundary concept, because without it there would be no *completudo* in the series of the contingent |\nThis boundary concept, however, is itself problematic and cannot be cognized *a priori* through reason, because it is a *conceptus terminator*, nor can insight into it be had *a posteriori*, for that is not to have insight, nor immediately; it is therefore problematic.\nThe supreme *principium* of everything contingent is the absolutely necessary, and the supreme *principium* (g of possibility) of arising is freedom. It is thought that with regard to a freely acting being every state that is in its power constitutes a *terminus a priori* that is not connected with the previous ones through a natural link. It is itself no link of a larger chain, at least not in accordance with activity.",[]],"reflm:4034":["The actuality of the *omnimode contingentis* (free action) is just as little to be comprehended as that of the *omnimode necessarii*.[The latter] This therefore, because, if the *necessitas* is mediate, the first necessity is not to be understood; that, because [if the *contingentia* is only *respectiva*] the existence of such a thing is not to be comprehended.",[]],"reflm:4035":["All necessity is a necessity of judgments or of things; in the former, if I want to think the opposite, I retain the subject and cancel its identical predicate; in the latter, if I want to think the opposite, I cancel the thing with all its predicates; hence in the former case I can cognize the (g absolute) necessity from the proposition of contradiction, but not in the latter.",[]],"reflm:4036":["Not necessity, but contingency is for rea|son\nincomprehensible. This idea of contingency is either logical (with universal concepts that are undetermined with regard to particular determinations) or consists in this, that I can cancel the thing; but this possibility proves too much.",[]],"reflm:4037":["Inwardly contingent is what, if one abstracts from all conditions, is contingent; absolutely contingent is what (g either has no conditions or) is contingent even with all conditions, because it is the conditions.\nAbsolutely necessary is what is necessary even under necessary conditions and thus outwardly necessary. The inner or outer necessity makes no difference in this concept, but only in the conditions of it.\nNothing is absolutely necessary except what is necessarily connected with an inwardly necessary. Absolutely contingent is what is contingent under all conditions; the like one must negate.",[]],"reflm:4038":["Over and to the column heading »Necessarium et contingens«:\nAbsolute necessity is more than the hypothetical.\nAbsolute contingency is less than the hypothetical,\nbecause absolute possibility is the smallest.\n(g The *omnimoda contingentia*)",[]],"reflm:4039":["The concept of an absolute *necessarii* is a *conceptus terminator* (because we must regard everything contingent [in de] as necessary through a ground and finally the condition must drop away); and since the con|dition\nof understandability drops away, it is not to be seen into according to the laws of reason, in that in the series of possible insights it is something in relation to the sequels and borders on nothing with regard to the grounds. The concept of freedom is also a boundary concept of an absolute beginning. (g Likewise the concept of the simple and in general of the first. All laws of the understanding serve for comprehensibility in the series i breaks off.)\nThe concept of substance is also *conceptus terminator*. Likewise the boundary of the world. Who is, however, *conceptus comprehensor*.\nPossibility without actuality rests on the difference between the self-active representation through concept and the passive one through sensation, since the former remains, even if the latter has been entirely cancelled. In general the possibility of the non-actual is only something that is thought by a certain universal concept.\nAll *conceptus terminatores* are taken from possibility *per synthesin* and always demand a first. Hence *a parte inferiori vel posteriori* there is no *conceptus terminator*. The *terminus* of the series is its first member, but the *conceptus terminator* is the concept through which a first of the series is possible. Of this concept, which is supposed to be perfectly comprehensible through itself, [can nevertheless] such properties must be thought, through which it can in general and without a restriction to be taken from elsewhere and thus as an unrestricted being for itself be the *principium* of the series.\nMorality also has its own *termini a priori* and *a posteriori*: a lawgiver and a reward and punishment according to spiritual laws, thus another world.",[]],"reflm:4040":["The two propositions: a state is possible in which a certain de|termination\nis not, and: it is possible that a certain state not be at all, are different. The opposite of a state is therefore possible either as a particular state or as the not-being of the given. In general the cancellation of what is there is not the same as non-being.\nTo be in a necessary way and to endure necessarily are different concepts. The necessary being cannot be compared with time at all; therefore it can in time neither persist with the same determinations, nor change the same [nor] or vary with regard to them.\nWhat I call unalterable is nevertheless considered under the hypothesis of time; accordingly the *intellectualia* are neither alterable nor unalterable. The beginning of sensibility contradicts itself because then the sensible is considered as bordering on the *intellectuale*.",[]],"reflm:4041":["Contingent is that whose opposite is possible in its place; [zuf] alterable: [whose] that which is possible in connection with its opposite.\nIn all alteration [is] are: 1. *oppositae determinationes, quatenus eidem competunt*. 2. *successio earundem*. The possibility of *mutatio* is not to be cognized from mere contingency. For because it is possible that instead of one predicate there be another, it is not yet to be cognized from this that the subject has the *opposita* one after another. The relation of the real ground to the sequel seems at the same time to contain the idea of succession; for [in de] the sequel is here too something other than the ground, and both can hardly be distinguished otherwise than through *prius et posterius*[erkann]. Whether a simple substance can alter itself in itself? and how through the *compraesentiam* and |\nthe *nexum* of several the alteration is possible? All alterations can be thought only in time.",[]],"reflm:4042":["(s Whether a concept has objective reality?)\n*realitas adiective* taken is opposed to ideality and signifies [relation] the objective; e.g. realists with regard to bodies or idealists.\nLikewise nature taken *adiective* signifies something other than nature simply. (s Magnitudes and the magnitude of a thing.)",[]],"reflm:4043":["*Negatio est vel rationatum rationis tollentis vel deficientis**; *prior est privatio*. *Posterior proprie dici non potest rationatum, quia nihil in ipso ponitur*. (Negation is either the *rationatum* of a canceling or of a deficient reason*; the former is privation. The latter cannot properly be called *rationatum*, because nothing is posited in it.)\n*(s The latter can still be thought as a vanishing ground and thus specifically the same as the *ratione ponente*. e.g. point an infinitely small space, rest as vanishing motion.)",[]],"reflm:4044":["Opposite and \nInsofar as a being is not the most perfect, some deficiencies are necessary, so that certain perfections may be obtained. E.g. abstraction for distinctness. A certain degree of cowardice, laziness, ignorance is very necessary to human nature.",[]],"reflm:4045":["Whether realities may all be of one and the same kind and can be measured through more and less.",[]],"reflm:4046":["The *omnitudo collectiva* (all in one) or totality rests on the *positio simultanea* (simultaneous positing). From the [omnitudine] *multitudine distributiva* (distributive multitude) I can infer to the *unitatem collectivam* (collective unity), but not from the *omnitudo*, because the progression is infinite and not complete. Time is, with regard to potential simultaneity, infinite. Hence we represent space to ourselves as *actualiter* (actually) infinite.",[]],"reflm:4047":["The whole is either of subordination: series (serial sequence), or of coordination: aggregate (heap).\nThe whole, insofar as the parts in it are homogeneous, is a magnitude; but they can also be heterogeneous, and insofar it is not a magnitude.\nThat whole of which no part is an absolute unity is infinitely divisible.",[]],"reflm:4048":["The ground of the unity of coordination: whether it is based on the unity of subordination [and whether not sel], insofar as they all flow immediately from one ground.\nThe matter of the whole is the subject of the conjunction (relation), i.e. the parts. The form of the *coniunctio coordinationis* (combination of coordination). The possibility of this form is cognized either *per intellectum* (through the understanding): through space and time, or *per rationem* (through reason): through *rationem aut causam communem* (a common ground or cause).",[]],"reflm:4049":["The sequels are also combined with the grounds, but not as parts [with] in a whole. The combination of the parts with the whole is [to] according to the rule of identity, but that of the parts among one another is not according to the same.\nOften one has first the idea of the whole or of dispertibility; e.g. universal concept with regard to the classes, space with regard to the parts.\n*Totum ideale aut reale, prius vel logicum vel arbitrarium* (the whole ideal or real, prior either logical or arbitrary).",[]],"reflm:4050":["*Quantitas extensiva vel intensiva* (extensive or intensive quantity). *Gradus* (degree).\nThat which with respect to some *quantum* cannot be thought except intensively, [is] namely as *ratio* (ground). E.g. heat. *Ingenium*. God.\n(s A greater degree of heat cannot be compounded from lesser ones. But the heat-producing force of the rays is infinite and is limited only by the inertia of matter. Hence by combining from weak rays of light (through rarefaction) the intensity of heat can increase.)",[]],"reflm:4051":["»Prima matheseos intensorum principia« (first principles of the mathematics of intensities).\n*Maximum et minimum*: *conceptus deceptores* (deceptive concepts).",[]],"reflm:4052":["Because logical subjects can in turn be predicates, we are |\naccording to laws of reason compelled to think a last subject; this is substance.",[]],"reflm:4053":["Are substances indeed really different from *accidentia* (accidents)? is it not the substance itself that I can think through all its predicates, and does the least indeed remain over, in order to distinguish it from its predicates? Only the succession of some, in that some remains one and the same, seems to confirm the difference.",[]],"reflm:4054":["The concept of substance has, besides the idea of the subject, still the concept of persistence in that which follows upon one another, and of sameness in this sequence, which one therefore calls alterations of the very same thing, along with it. But because [as well] all *accidentia* are variable and the substantial is not known at all, the persistence of the *substantialis* is assumed *precario* (precariously). Even consciousness can flow. Especially if everything endures only through the divine force.",[]],"reflm:4055":["The word predicate indicates no new difference of things; hence every thing or the concept of it can have something predicated of it. E.g. the rational beings are spirits. Thus [if] nothing can exist without being a predicate of other things. The human being is a predicate of a rational earth-dweller.\nThis logical *respectus* can be transformed into a real one, if [it] something exists in another, not in the possible concept of another, and indeed as in the real ground. For a part is in the whole on logical grounds, because the whole is only in part something other than its *partes* (parts); otherwise all *partes* would lie in a whole (not in the |\nplace, so to speak, for all these parts) and the whole would therefore be something other than all the parts together.",[]],"reflm:4056":["Every substance has a force, is an identical proposition. For substance is properly the subject that contains the ground of the accidents and of the effects; consequently the concept of substance arises from the necessity of the force of a subject.",[]],"reflm:4057":["Below and \nThat whose representation is properly [that through which we something] the predicate of the representation of another thing is its determination; and if it is something positive in itself, then it attaches to others (inherence).",[]],"reflm:4058":["60. E II 1058.\nThe concept of substance depends on inner sense. Likewise the *phaenomena* (appearances) also teach us only concomitance or consequence, hence not the *compositio* (composition) or *nexus* (nexus) and the ground, but phenomena of the same. Thus the *axiomata phaenomenorum* (axioms of phenomena) are not to be regarded as *intellectualia* (intellectual things). E.g. to be sure everything that happens has a determining ground and follows upon something else according to a determinate law; but that is no rule for the actual actions that we perceive in ourselves.",[]],"reflm:4059":["All successions are alterations of the same abiding sub|ject.\nSubstances do not flow, but their *status* (states); so reason demands it, so experience shows it; whence this agreement of experience with reason?",[]],"reflm:4060":["If the very same subject x is to be one after another a and *non a*, then the subject must not be altered; otherwise x would first be a and then y *non a*.\nThe substantial is unalterable. For in the succession of the *accidentia* it is always the same, and in *ortus* and *interitus* (arising and perishing) it is not altered.",[]],"reflm:4061":["In every composite all composition can be removed, but not in every *quantum*. In all parts of space parts can be indicated, but not in any part of space can all parts be indicated.",[]],"reflm:4062":["*In omni toto [partes] plura sunt coordinata, non subordinata; ideo ratio et rationatum non faciunt totum.* (In every whole [parts] several are coordinated, not subordinated; therefore ground and grounded do not make a whole.)",[]],"reflm:4063":["*Omne totum est vel compositum ex substantiis vel accidentibus vel relationibus; cum relationes vel sint substantiarum vel accidentium, posterior erit tempus, prior spatium.* (Every whole is either composed of substances or accidents or relations; since relations are either of substances or of accidents, the latter will be time, the former space.)",[]],"reflm:4064":["That in composition through which it distinguishes itself from every other is *modus compositionis* (mode of composition).",[]],"reflm:4065":["That every *compositum substantiale* (substantial composite) consists of simple parts does not signify that through the dissection of the concept of the *compositum* one arrives at simple parts, but: that according to the law of human cognition the material of the composite, i.e. the simple, precedes the form. Thus it proves nothing objectively.",[]],"reflm:4066":["*Omne compositum substantiale constat ex substantiis simplicibus. Qvoniam enim partes sunt substantiae, [possu] existentia earum est subsistentia, et possunt existere, etiamsi non sint invicem compositae; ergo compositio omnis potest abrogari, ita ut tamen omnes partes talis compositi supersint. Abrogata autem omni compositione superstites partes sunt simplices.* (Every substantial composite consists of simple substances. For since the parts are substances, [possu] their existence is subsistence, and they can exist even if they are not composed with one another; therefore all composition can be abrogated, so that nevertheless all parts of such a composite remain. But with all composition abrogated, the remaining parts are simple.)\nThis proof goes only to the *compositum substantiale*, [which] and its possibility can be thought *a priori*, i.e. *synthetice* (synthetically), because then *partes non compositae* (uncomposed parts) constitute the *principia* (principles) of the synthesis through which the idea of the *compositum* arises. But a *compositum accidentale* (accidental composite) is not always of the kind. Time and space have no parts that could also take place without all composition [be].",[]],"reflm:4067":["A [unity] multiplicity we cannot think without (g absolute) unity |\nif the [data as well] whole multiplicity is to be thought through reason. But if it is given as a phenomenon, then the analysis can be *absque termino* (without limit).",[]],"reflm:4068":["Composition is an *accidens*. Because the substantial is thought before all *accidentia*, the parts are thought before all connection.",[]],"reflm:4069":["Opposite and \nThings that exist in the very same time are simultaneous; those that exist at a different one are one after another.",[]],"reflm:4070":["Time is everywhere, or the parts of space are simultaneous. Newton.\nSpace is at all times, this signifies: that time extends over all concepts, outer as well as inner sensations, but not that the concept of space extends over time.\nThat is not the shortest way that goes through the smallest space, but that which can be completed in the shortest time; just as the book is the shortest through which I can comprehend the object in the smallest time.",[]],"reflm:4071":["[All] The concept of time is a single concept. For all [parts of the] different times are one after another, consequently contained in one whole of time. No two years can be at the same time. Time has only one dimension, but existence measured through time has two.\n one after another\nat the same time\nSpace is nothing actual, but a possibility that has its ground in something actual. The possibility of the alterations of things is grounded first on their connection (for a single thing alone would not alter itself), second on their dependence on another; for the being that does not depend on another as to its existence would not be able to alter itself without ceasing.\nOne cannot think to oneself any space, e.g. a cubic foot, without an outer space that surrounds it, and thus no space without contained in the whole. Likewise no two spaces without a determinate distance and position toward one another.",[]],"reflm:4072":["The concepts of the understanding are reflected representations and also have their elements of reflection; space is an intuitive representation.",[]],"reflm:4073":["Concepts are either intuitive or [rational] reflected.\nThe former are either sensitive intuitions (*intuitus sensitivi*) or pure intuitions (*intuitus puri*), insofar as either the matter or the form alone of sensitive representation is present. Reflected concepts (*conceptus reflexi*) are either also empirical or pure; the former are universal concepts containing matter given by the senses, the latter containing only form |\n[conc] containing.[the former The former are intellect] Space and time are concepts of the pure understanding (*conceptus intellectus puri*). Metaphysical notions are concepts of pure reason (*conceptus rationis purae*).\nSpace is either determining (possible) or determined (actual).",[]],"reflm:4074":["Above, beside and \nThe persistence of the existence of all things in One is the *omnipraesentia*, space.\nThe persistence of all existence of each thing in One is the preservation, time.\nIf the whole existence of a thing depends on one, then it is of contingent nature; if only the beginning depends on it, then is [be] the duration of its state contingent.",[]],"reflm:4075":["If all existence depends on one, then both *extensive* and *protensive* breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4076":["The whole existence possible in general is called eternity. The [existence] eternal existence [with], which is properly only the [boundary] continuous bounding of eternity, is in time as it were a continuous beginning in the one *respectu* (respect) and a continuous beginning in the other. Behaves as movement through a line to the extension in this line.",[]],"reflm:4077":["Time and space precede things: that is entirely |\nnatural. Both namely are subjective conditions, [under] under which objects can be given only to the senses. Taken objectively this would be absurd. Hence the difficulty about the place of the world and the time before the world. Yet in absolute time no place is determined without actual things, thus absolute time can furnish no ground of the explanation of the *phaenomena*.",[]],"reflm:4078":["That space is a mere *phaenomenon* and something subjective, but not a representation of the things, is to be seen from this, because relations are represented in it that are yet no effects, but merely grounds of the possibility of effects, and these grounds are themselves no things.",[]],"reflm:4079":["Perhaps the eternity of the world is a consequence of the infinity of the same as of a *quantum simultaneum* (simultaneous quantum); for from a limited number of causes the effects must come to an end, but from an infinite one the combinations and consequences are infinite.\nThe difficulty of representing to oneself a *quantum simultaneum* as infinite rests on the nature of the human understanding, which can think a *totum* (whole), as to its possibility, only synthetically, i.e. *successive addendo unum uni* (successively adding one to one). But the synthesis that is supposed to go to infinity is never complete. On the other hand one can well think an *infinitum successivum* (successive infinite), just because the synthesis has no *termini* (limits). But just |\nfor that reason it is also impossible, according to the nature of our understanding, to think to oneself this successive series as whole and completed in the idea of a being.",[]],"reflm:4080":["Opposite the column heading »Finitum et infinitum«.\nInfinitely great is what in the *synthesis partium dabilium aequalium* (synthesis of givable equal parts) has no last. What in the analysis has no last is infinitely divisible.",[]],"reflm:4081":["(g These propositions show how one mixes up the subjective rule with the objective. It follows only that we could not distinguish such things, [but not] or that the [things] concepts would not much belong to them.)\nOn M § 270, first sentence:\nFor they would be distinguished only by places (g and times). thus properly be only just the same thing at different times and places. For the substance itself must be presupposed before all relation; and if it is the same, then it is one and the same thing.\nThe difference of the [things] places makes no difference of the things themselves, but presupposes them. i.e. [it is the differ] a thing stands in relation with itself when it is in relation with one that is one and the same as it.",[]],"reflm:4082":["For then they would be distinguished only by magnitude, i.e. one would be entirely equal and similar to the part of the other, *quod contra prius* (which is contrary to the former).* The following proposition lies here at the ground.\n*\n(g Likewise, since a smaller magnitude can be transformed into a larger, if (g the lack) of sameness rests only on the circumstance, they could become entirely congruent.)",[]],"reflm:4083":["For equal things must be *homogenea* (homogeneous), hence *totaliter aequalia* (totally equal) must be *totaliter homogenea* (totally homogeneous). (s *totaliter aequalia* here means equal in every relation.)",[]],"reflm:4084":["Rest is the lasting presence of a body in the same place.\nIf in a given place a body did not act, change of place would not be a succession of action. The force of change is not properly thinkable [but rather force], but it is necessary that changeable *accidentia* (accidents) be thought beforehand, whose actuation or removal demands force. Therefore moving force is the ground on account of which a certain unknown force is present successively in different places. But if the force of presence in any place is again called moving, the same is explained through the same. It is evident therefore that in bodies we cognize nothing but determinations of space and time, but do not cognize intellectually the absolute marks (*notae*) of the subjects.",[]],"reflm:4085":["The idea of the world is not arbitrary. For just as I must think a |\npart as finite which is no further a whole, so I must think a whole that is no part.",[]],"reflm:4086":["* A world exists, i.e., a whole that is no part of any actual (possible) whole. From this it can be inferred:\n1. The world is a *compositum substantiale*. Thus it does not consist of necessary beings. Its parts also do not cohere through themselves. [After] Because they are through a cause. Space is the *phaenomenon* of the existence of all things through one. Infinity. The *commercium* of the substances is possible thereby.\n2. Because it has its boundary (s of the subordination and of the coordination) *a priori*, it has an origin. Also a beginning? The beginning of the world is considered only respectively to the world, but not to something outside the world.\n(g 3. On the order of nature. 4. Ladder of beings. *textur, figur, mixtur*.)\nThe whole of the world and of its state.\nIt depends on the highest cause, but is not in *commercio* with it. There are no more worlds outside this one, although in place of this one possible, because there is only one cause. This holds respectively to the things themselves; respectively to the highest cause, however, only one is hypothetically possible.\nWe are thus to infer from a world, which, how great and how perfect it may be, is undetermined, to a highest cause that is infinitely |\ngreater than the ground of proof of its existence. From this it is clear that the existence of this highest and sole cause and its all-sufficiency must otherwise already be known and the existence of the world must give proof only of the existence of a necessary.\nThe composition of a *totius substantiarum* is a proof of contingency;\nThe restriction of every thing is a proof of contingency.\nThe change of every thing is a proof of contingency.\nWhether it cannot be inferred from space as the ground of the possibility of the *commercii substantiarum* according to laws of sensibility to the intuition of a cause valid for all things that can stand in *commercio*. And from infinite time as the ground of the possibility of the duration of things to the eternity and necessity of the cause.\n(g A *compositum* that is no *totum absolute tale* is *tanqvam pars* possible only through the *limitation totius*; thus everything that holds of a thing only as *parte* presupposes the concept of the whole.)\n*(g In every *composito actuali* no part is completely determined as through all the rest together, consequently the thoroughgoing determination goes from all coordinated substances to one, thus it is at all times a part of a *toto absoluto*, i.e., of a world. But in a *composito potentiali* every part is completely determined, because the rest are posited to it and have no influence on the existence of the former; thus the sequence of the series [therefore not com] is not necessarily complete. But if in a series of things following one another there is a last, then there is also a first.)\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen (\"necessary beings\") = beings, not essence; Daseyn = existence; Zufälligkeit = contingency; Grentze = boundary; Beweisgrund = ground of proof.\n- \"von dem Daseyn eines nothwendigen Beweis geben\": \"nothwendigen\" has no noun in the German and is left so; \"Beweis\" is left as the object of \"geben\".\n- \"Dinge durch einen\" and \"Dieses gilt respective auf...\" are left as elliptical as the German; \"Reihenfolge\" = sequence of the series.\n- Doubtful: the bracketed deletions [Nach], [darum nicht com] are rendered as they stand; \"com\" is a broken-off word.",[]],"reflm:4087":["Dependent is a ring in the chain; and that on which others depend |\nand which is itself independent is the uppermost ring. According to laws of gravity it must also depend, because the lower links are a ground of the sinking of the upper ones. Only in itself a thing with its sequels could be without any foreign ground.\n===NOTES===\n- Folgen = sequels (Folge); Grund = ground; an sich selbst = in itself.\n- \"allein\" could be \"only\" or \"but\"; left as \"Only\", which keeps the ambiguity between the adversative and restrictive readings.",[]],"reflm:4088":["Relation of the world to empty intuition outside the world.\nThe world's unity or multiplicity.\n- -\n- -\nContingency or necessity. Dependence.\n- -\n- -\nFiniteness or infinity.\nConnection (g contingency) and unconnectedness.\n(s Composition.* Community.)\nMetaphysical perfection or imperfection.\n(s Natural and supernatural.)\n(s Egoist)\n(s *Partes materiales et immateriales*.)\n( s *partes repraesentativae: vtrum simplices? vtrum spatium repleant?* )\nTogether with the sensitive. Of eternity, of immeasurability, of the beginning, of *continuitaet*.\n*(s *Systemata commercii substantiarum*. If the cause of the harmony is external and merely arbitrary, then the state of the one does not necessarily agree with that of the other, but everything depends on the choice; thus there is no necessary ground, that would be universally valid, for the relation of things; thus they do not make up a whole; or the harmony does not follow sufficiently from the things, is thus no *influxus*.)\n===NOTES===\n- Vielheit = multiplicity; Verknüpfung = connection; Gemeinschaft = community; Anschauung = intuition; Vollkommenheit = perfection.\n- Willkühr is rendered \"choice\" in the second occurrence and \"arbitrary\" for willkührlich; the same German root.\n- The \"- -\" lines are the editors' marks and are carried over unchanged; \"Zusamt dem sensitiven\" is elliptical and left so.",[]],"reflm:4089":["[*Multitudo Complexus*]\nThe multitude of the subordinated is a series. A series is either of the sensitively or of the intellectually subordinated.\n===NOTES===\n- Multitudo = multitude (Menge); Reihe = series.\n- \"Series est vel sensitive vel intellectualiter subordinatorum\" is Latin in the original; the genitive plural is rendered as \"of the ... subordinated\".",[]],"reflm:4090":["Over, beside and \nThe impossibility of a *regressus* in a series of the *successionum* (not *causatorum*) comes from this, that the series is regarded as *absolutum*, completed, and we, who can determine the magnitudes only successively, can nevertheless never cognize such completely. This proves, however, not their impossibility.\nOnly, whether it is a *causatum* of another is not asked thereby at all.\n===NOTES===\n- \"Über, neben und\" breaks off; left unfinished.\n- Großen = magnitudes (Größe); erkennen = cognize; Unmöglichkeit = impossibility.\n- \"Dieses beweiset aber nicht ihre Unmöglichkeit\": \"ihre\" could refer to the series or to the cognition; left ambiguous.",[]],"reflm:4091":["To blind necessity the absolute (s natural) (rational) is opposed.\nTo blind chance freedom (intellectual) is opposed.\n(s both are again the order which makes cognitions of experience possible, thus impossible with regard to the objects of the same.)\n===NOTES===\n- \"wieder\" is given literally as \"again\" (it may stand for \"wider\", against); doubtful reading, not emended.\n- Ohngefehr = chance; Erfahrungserkentnisse = cognitions of experience; Gegenstände = objects.",[]],"reflm:4092":["In the series of marks subordinated to one another there are no two that are the nearest; all differences are divisible to infinity.\n===NOTES===\n- Merkmale = marks (notae); ins unendliche = to infinity; theilbar = divisible.",[]],"reflm:4093":["All change occurs in a time. For between the two |\nmoments of the one state and of the following there is a time, thus the change has occurred in an infinite multitude of moments between both states. We want to posit that the substance persisted for a time in the same state under certain conditions: then it will persist therein eternally under the same conditions.\nBetween two states there are two moments, and between two moments there is a time, and in this time the *subiect* is at all times in another state.\n===NOTES===\n- Veränderung = change; Augenblike = moments; Zustand = state; Menge = multitude; beharren = persist; Bedingungen = conditions.\n- \"in einer Zeit\" could be \"in a time\" or \"in one time\"; left as \"a time\".",[]],"reflm:4094":["Egoism can be refuted only by this, that no change in a single substance alone is possible.\nThe idealist regards all his external (g actual) representations as *spontaneas*. The egoist [as if also no external] all external possible ones.\n===NOTES===\n- Vorstellungen = representations; Veränderung = change.\n- Second sentence is elliptical (\"regards ... all external possible ones\" lacks a second \"as spontaneas\"); the bracketed deletion is carried over as it stands.",[]],"reflm:4095":["Essence is the first logical ground of the inner determinations;\nNature is the first real ground of the inner determinations.\nThe first: the ground of that which in a thing is [constantly] necessary;\nThe second: the ground of that which is possible through a thing.\n(s 1. That through which a thing is possible.\n2. What is possible through a thing.\nWe know nature through experience.)\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen = essence here (the glossary gives \"being; essence\"); Bestimmungen = determinations; kennen = know.\n- real Grund: \"real\" left as printed, in roman type for the adjective; [bestandig] is a deletion rendered as \"constantly\".",[]],"reflm:4096":["The real essence is nature.\n===NOTES===\n- Realwesen = real essence (compound kept literal); Wesen = essence, as in the neighboring Reflexionen.",[]],"reflm:4097":["The necessary of the concept makes up the essence.\nThe necessary of actuality makes nature. is the permanent.\nNature is the *principium* of contingencies.\nWe cognize through experience nature sooner than essence, e.g. the hardness of steel. Essence and nature are both permanent.\nThe first of that which a thing is is called essence.\nThe first of that which happens through a thing is called nature. The essence of bodies consists in impenetrable extension; to the nature of bodies belongs *inertia*. The cognition of nature is grounded on experience or at least contingent relation of the thing itself.\n===NOTES===\n- Wirklichkeit = actuality; Zufälligkeiten = contingencies; Ausdehnung = extension; Erkentnis = cognition; Wesen = essence.\n- \"ist das permanente\" has no clear subject (nature or the necessary); left ambiguous. \"zufallig Verhältnis\" is left ungrammatical as written.",[]],"reflm:4098":["progression.\nThe infinity of the series as such is possible, but not the infinity of the aggregate. The former is an infinite possibility (of the additions), the latter an infinite (actual) taking together.\n===NOTES===\n- Unendlichkeit = infinity; Zusammennehmung = taking together; Hinzuthuungen = additions; progression is Latinate in the original, left as written.",[]],"reflm:4099":["The greatest of an aggregate is impossible; for it would be an *apposition* to which none would be possible.\n===NOTES===\n- Größeste = greatest (superlative of Größe); \"zu der keine möglich wäre\": \"keine\" has no explicit noun (an apposition is supplied only by the Latin word before).",[]],"reflm:4100":["The incompetence of reason to set limitations to sensibility itself under the condition of this intuition, both of the extension and of the division and of both according to time and space. Reason can set limitations to the empirical with regard to the rational [e.g., if one the] and restrict empiricism, e.g., that everything is extended, that before all series in the world another always precedes and there is no first origin; but it cannot restrict sensualism.\n===NOTES===\n- Schranken = limitations; einschränken = restrict; Ausbreitung = extension (spreading); Ausdehnung is not used here, so \"Ausbreitung\" is marked.\n- \"und beyder der Zeit und dem Raume nach\": \"beyder\" refers to extension and division; left as in German.\n- \"sensualismus\" and \"empirismus\" are left in the Latinate form as written; the bracketed deletion is carried over.",[]],"reflm:4101":["In the *harmonia generaliter stabilita* the dancing master is, for example, also suited to walking at the same time instead of dancing; in the *harmonia praestabilita*, however, there is no [ground] general ground at all, but all determination is individual.\n===NOTES===\n- Bestimmung = determination; Grund = ground; Tanzmeister = dancing master.\n- \"generalen\" and \"individuel\" are Latinate adjectives rendered as \"general\" and \"individual\".",[]],"reflm:4102":["Opposite and \n*effectus vel naturae vel libertatis.*\n*actio vel naturalis vel libera.*\n===NOTES===\n- \"Gegenüber und\" breaks off; left unfinished.\n- The Latin lines are left in Latin without translation; no English is supplied beside them in the passage, as they are plain terms.",[]],"reflm:4103":["What is in different places admits of no cognition through an [outer] inner sense, hence no selfhood.\n===NOTES===\n- Örtern = places (Ort); Erkentnis = cognition; Sinn = sense; Selbstheit = selfhood.\n- \"Was in verschiedenen örtern ist\" kept literal; the bracketed deletion [äusseren] is carried over.",[]],"reflm:4104":["*Systema emanationis* (from God or from parents); *systema creationis*.\nMaterialism: *systema* [*propagationis*] of natural generation, psychological *epigenesis*.\n(s Bad manners or naughtiness, e.g. violent heat.)\nWhether everything in the character of the child depends solely on the man or the woman. The system of the *ovulorum* presupposes that, if the woman had also had another man, she would nevertheless have begotten the same children; that of the *animalculorum*: if the man had had another woman, he would have gotten the same children. This is very practical; for in the first case a [Vater] man has very much to look to the constitution of the woman and her *race*, in the second he has not at all to look to it, but only the woman to the *race* of the man. According to *epigenesis* one must look to both: 1. on account of the *alternative*, secondly on account of the mixture. With free *preexistence*, to neither of the two.\nBefore birth was the soul a pure spirit? Did it find itself in spiritual life? Has it already lived on the earth, or does it live in two worlds, the pneumatic and the mechanical at once?",[]],"reflm:4105":["The perpetual duration of the substances, i.e. the equal age of each with the whole world, cannot so well be proved as that it must lie at the ground in the method of philosophizing.",[]],"reflm:4106":["All proofs concerning the immortality of souls* must be conducted so that they hold not merely for some, but for all (g and not merely for a brief existence after death, but for an eternal duration). They can thus not be concluded from the necessity that the world must at all times have spectators, who connect its epochs, because it does not yet follow from that that we, who have once begun to live, must always live.\n1. Not from divine justice. For first, that would hold only of some, whose virtue and vice in this world have not been sufficiently requited. And 2ndly it would prove only a brief existence, which would cease after the requital.\n2. Not from the cognized immortal nature of the soul. Here the grounds of the opposite are preponderant.\n(s not from empirical, but rational grounds.)\n3. Only from analogy with other things of nature. Human Fahigkeiten have no correct relation to the post in this world.\n*(s *Imo* are either derived from the nature of the soul from empirical principles or from concepts *a priori* of thinking beings in general. The first is psychological, the second properly metaphysical;\nor *IIdo* they are teleological, either of the analogy of physical ends or of moral ones. According to the order of nature.)",[]],"reflm:4107":["Life cannot stem from the combination with the lifeless, rather this must diminish a degree of life. Birth can thus be no beginning of life in general, but only of animal life, and death the end of the same.",[]],"reflm:4108":["[Is the soul in general always in this world of sense or]\nThe transition either into another world or other [relations with] region of this one.\nThe 1st question: is the soul after death a pure spirit or still the soul of an animal. The answer is grounded on the decision of the question whether the soul is still, according to the present law of sensibility, in combination with the world, thus also with the corporeal world. Some sensibility will presumably remain. [compar]\n2nd question. Is the other world another region of the world of sense or is it different according to form. Answer: [According to substance] objectively there can be only one world, for all substances except the supreme cause make up a whole; but [according to form] subjectively, i.e. according to the manner in which the subject represents it, there can be another world. And there it is to be presumed that sensibility diminishes and thus the transition from the *mundo sensibili* into the *intelligibilem* goes over *per approximationem*. The *intuitus* is *comparative* intellectual, the [more deu] more the inner sense grows.\nThe *mundus vere intelligibilis* is *mundus moralis*. The principles of whose form hold for everyone, and from the same one can infer God as the *causam mere intelligibilem*; but this *mundus intelligibilis* is no object of intuition, but of *reflexion*. The intuiting of God would at the same time give *intuitum intellectualem* of the world.\nThose who assume an *intuitum mere intellectualem*, which begins naturally after death, assert that the soul after death sees itself in the other world and does not go over thither (g departing of the soul), that it belongs at all times to the *mundo immateriali* as the true substance, that the corporeal world is only a certain sen|sible\nappearance of the world of spirits, that the actions here are *symbola* of the proper character in the intelligible world, and that the virtuous person does not go over into heaven, but only sees himself in it.\n3. If the other world is this very same world of sense, although in another relation, then the soul has at all times a body, either an ordinary (according to the laws of (g ordinary) sensation) *aspectable* body: *Metempsychosis*, or one that is visible only through the choice of the departed soul: *apparitiones*, *spectra*, either through outer or inner influence. *prodigia*.",[]],"reflm:4109":["Hope or fear of the other world.\nA human being who has the resolve of malice and roguery (g unless he fears God and another world) I cannot convince of the existence of both, but only drive this fear into him and thus push into his mind sufficient grounds of the possibility and speculative refutations or counter-grounds of the opposed possibility, but not bring about, either that he know there is another world, or believe it. A human being must have an immediate resolve to be honest, if only he did not put his whole happiness at stake thereby. For then he will wish, hope and believe the [Gl] other world and wise government, and indeed unchangeably and without being shaken.",[]],"reflm:4110":["The analogical proof from the hope of the other world is beautiful in this, that it does not concern itself with the difficulties of speculative explanations, nor engage in theories and ways of explanation that one [nicht] can defend with difficulty, but infers according to the same law according to which one in physics, where the object according to its inner constitution |\nconceals itself, infers from what shows itself externally to what does not discover itself immediately.",[]],"reflm:4111":["It is excellent that on this earth the course of the world does not harmonize with the moral laws, because otherwise no human being himself would know whether he acts from prudence or morality, and pure moral motives could not be felt.",[]],"reflm:4112":["Blessedness is a perfect tranquil gratification, contentment in the consciousness of one's state as a good one. \"Saumseelig\", \"redseelig\" signify an activity ruling in the tranquil state of mind.\nLuck is a contingent acquisition of the agreeable. Happiness is the contentment with luck.",[]],"reflm:4113":["S. I:\n1. Through speculation.\na. (g The natural) original being. Highest and eternal cause. Substratum. Einos. (g Subordinate gods, gods of the land.)\n(g c. Freely acting original being. Life alone can make the first beginning.)\nb, The first mover (g the [architect] author). |\nThe highest spirit. Living God.\nWorld-soul. Spinozism.\n2. From morality. There must be an inwardly present and universal judge.\nThe holy, benevolent and just being.\n––––––––––\nThe greatest perfection 1. As [a] (g the highest) ideal of all good, like a *principium* of the understanding.\n2. As a *principium* of the method, of judging from ends in physics.\n––––––––––\nThe unity of the all of perfection, because only through limitation of the reality that is given to us do we know the manifold of beings.\n––––––––––\nThe idea of God is therefore\n1. The necessary (g *principium*) logical ideal,\n2. The necessary hypothesis of the natural order,\n3. —\n— —\n— —\n—\nmoral\n—.\nThe necessary being, the first, the highest, the being of all beings, the most perfect being, the all-sufficient.\nTotality is something determinate in itself. Is something absolute. The greatest is determined only by its being the greatest among the possible things (hence All); otherwise it is something relative.\nOriginal magnitude is not thought through the *synthesis*. All derived magnitude is only comparative, namely in proportion to the measure.\n––––––––––\n*Theologia est vel rationalis vel revelata (non est empirica), rationalis philosophica vel sensus communis (maxime colenda), philosophica vel transscendentalis vel naturalis (physicotheologia).* (Theology is either rational or revealed (it is not empirical); the rational is philosophical or of common sense (to be cultivated most); the philosophical is transcendental or natural (physicotheology).) One. Almighty. Eternal. Omnipresent. Simple. Unalterable. Impassible.\nOriginal being. The highest intelligence. God.\nS. II:\nThe concept of God is once and for all there; one must [investigate what one] develop it genetically from use, in that one seeks not the sense |\nthat one actually combines with it, but the aim that lies at the ground of all these concepts.\nIn all cognitions that arise long before science and give the occasion for it breaks off.\n1. Fear. Sorcery. Spirits. Priests.\n2. Highest cause.\n––––––––––\n(s At the ground of all our concepts of things lies the concept of reality. A thing, Something. [This] The manifold realities [make the U] must be given to us through senses: reality therefore makes, as it were, the matter of all possibility. The all of reality [makes d] therefore comprehends the possibility of things altogether and is [only] (g the concept of) a single one. The differences of things therefore rest on the determination of the all of reality. Namely [of the position and the Aus] only in the difference of the limitation of the all of reality. Negations are therefore limitations and cannot be thought otherwise than in that in which there are no negations at all. [The concept of the highest reality is therefore a Grundb Grund] With regard to the all-concept of reality, the real in a thing is the (g transcendental) matter, the negations the transcendental form. The highest reality is therefore the substratum [of] our cognition of reason of all possibility, In which either or Through which all possibility is given.)",[]],"reflm:4114":["The concept of God is either a mere ideal of reason (g in relation to everything possible) or [and] given in relation to the world alone. in the latter case it is a concept inferred *a posteriori*, in the first one given *a priori*. Insofar as it has been inferred in relation to the world, it is either needed only [nach] as a practical ground or as a ground of theory.",[]],"reflm:4115":["Alterability in no way proves contingency; the former is *possibilitas statuum diversorum*, the latter *possibilitas oppositorum*; the former is a *determinatio secundum leges sensitivi in cognitione*. We have no marks of contingency at all through reason, just as little as of necessity except the conditioned. The absolute is unknown in all respects.",[]],"reflm:4116":["The proposition that a universal author is necessary says as much as: that it is comprehensible for itself and the opposite absurd in itself, that a being full of universal reason, of the highest power and the best will exist, and that on the contrary it is obscure and not graspable that many things, which alter among each other, or even no thing at all, exist.",[]],"reflm:4117":["The inference from contingent things to a necessary being says only this much, that the existence of alterable things is not (g completely) comprehensible unless a being is assumed at which the question: why? ceases, [Es] i.e. what is necessary for itself. But just as little is anything without conditions comprehensible as necessary existence, as without first conditions the subaltern necessity is comprehensible. That shows that the inference from alterable things to a necessary one does not prove that such a being exists in a necessary way, but that such a concept, if it is possible, is requisite for the explanation; and of this being one can in turn say that its necessity cannot be comprehended without a condition under which it would be necessary.",[]],"reflm:4118":["The existence of God is [the Bedin] connected with the highest maxims in the use of our reason, in regard to the speculative as well as the practical.",[]],"reflm:4119":["Above, beside and \nThe possibility of a thing is only the reality or negation respective to a certain particular concept. The [actuality] reality given absolutely is actuality. All reality must be given absolutely, and so some actuality precedes all possibility, just as space [vor] is not something possible but the ground of all possible figures.",[]],"reflm:4120":["At the lower margin \nWe see through mere concepts that all realities are there and that the possibilities of things are given through their combination and limitation.\nThis *omnitudo realitatum* is in a single being [because otherwise]. Insofar as this is supposed to be a cause of something, this is contingent; but of something contingent something cannot be cause from itself otherwise than through the will.",[]],"reflm:4121":["The moment is existence without any duration. Eternity is an existence with all duration.",[]],"reflm:4122":["Arising, passing away and being altered is only in time. Of what is not in time none of these can be said.\nAlteration is no intellectual predicate at all. Thus not the things but their *phaenomena* alter; but this alteration is itself a *phaenomenon*. (g Things in themselves are constant, and the alterations appearances of their limitations.) What corresponds to the alterations in *intellectualibus* we do not know.\nIt is quite the question whether the concept of substance, which indicates the constancy of something amid the change of determinations, is not only a concept that holds among *phaenomena*; for *intellectualiter* the substance taken together with its determinations cannot be otherwise than one and the same, and inherence cannot be a particular relation of something in the thing to the subject itself.\nTime is also eternal, i.e. the *finita* are, compared with one another through representations, i.e. insofar as one cognizes the other, all in time.",[]],"reflm:4123":["The philosophical cognition of magnitudes determines the same from the idea of the *omnitudo* (g *totum absolutum*) [through]*limitando*, consequently the *omnisufficiens* through the *oppositum* of all *limitationes*.\nMathematical cognition does not begin from the absolute whole, but from the respective, and determines the whole from the parts.\nThe *modus cognoscendi* too is different. The mathematical *per constructionem conceptus secundum intuitionem sensitivam*; the philosophical *per conceptus secundum cognitionem discursivam*.\nIn the concept of God the sensible condition must be omitted. The concept of his magnitude is: as *conceptus definitor, terminator et comprehensor* to infer from the general to the particular or from the all to the part. One must omit the *limitationes* (which are hindrances).",[]],"reflm:4124":["*Cognitio dei non est ectypa: mutuata ab obiectis, sed prototypa: principium obiectorum.* (Cognition of God is not ectypal: borrowed from objects, but prototypal: the principle of objects.)",[]],"reflm:4125":["A necessary being can be the cause of contingent ones only through freedom, for from the necessity of its nature would only the [arise] existence, which is in it.\nThe independence of the divine will, hence without sensibility, and thus greatest freedom.\nThe *bonitas* (goodness) of the same.\nThe all-sufficiency or omnipotence.\nThe inner necessity.",[]],"reflm:4126":["Because God represents the highest good as determined from his own (g free) force, he wills.",[]],"reflm:4127":["(g Free) (g will of reason) (g free)\nHis will is original. All-powerful. Holy. Wise. Benevolent. Just. independent. Because he is pleased with himself as a (g fruitful) ground, [so are therefore the] therefore the greatest perfection is actual through him. He is not satisfied by the things.",[]],"reflm:4128":["No inclination. He needs nothing. His perfection is overflowing with consciousness. That is his willing.\nIn transcendental theology it must be shown that we really think this through transcendental representations. e.g. That the original being has an understanding on account of the possibility of things through ideas, order. That it has a will on account of the efficient cause according to ideas. That the contingent is not determined through the nature of a being necessary in itself, consequently through freedom, that a first beginning is possible only through freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- Vollkomenheit = perfection; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Vorstellungen = representations; Verstand = understanding; Urwesen = original being; Ideen = ideas.\n- wirkende Ursache = efficient cause (literally 'effecting cause'); Zufallige = the contingent (zufällig); bestimmt = determined.\n- The syntax of the last sentence is loose in the German (the 'daß' clauses are left parallel); I have kept it as it stands.",[]],"reflm:4129":["Everything that exists outside God exists only through his will, nothing through the necessity of his nature, except what is in him.\nEverything that exists exists either through physical cause or according to laws of freedom. We can think no beginning without a freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- da ist = exists (rendered the same in both occurrences); Gesetzen = laws; Freyheit = freedom.\n- Nothwendigkeit = necessity.",[]],"reflm:4130":["The will of God goes from the whole to the part (s From the highest good, the good in the whole, to each individual).\nThis is the wise will.\n===NOTES===\n- Ganze/Theil = whole/part; Gute = good (n.).\n- The (s ...) marks the Academy's layer of later insertion; carried over as it stands.",[]],"reflm:4131":["*Voluntas* (will), whose [*motiva*] are not *stimuli*, but *complacentia* (satisfaction) (g *acquiescentia*) of the cause with itself. The *motiva* (motives) are subjectively intellectual.\n===NOTES===\n- The Latin is Kant's; English added beside the terms at first use. 'subiective' = subjectively.\n- *Motiva* is the Latin counterpart of Bewegungsgründe (motives).",[]],"reflm:4132":["The wise decrees are kind and just.\nThe immediate desire for the happiness of others is kindness.\nInsofar as this is in accordance with worthiness (good conduct), justice.\n===NOTES===\n- Rathschlüsse = decrees (not in the glossary; the same word is used in 4134, 4135, 4143).\n- Gütig/Gütigkeit = kind/kindness; Gerecht/Gerechtigkeit = just/justice; Glükseeligkeit = happiness; Würdigkeit = worthiness; Wohlverhalten = good conduct.",[]],"reflm:4133":["Because the will of God goes from the general to the particular, it is at all times just; the human goes from the particular to the particular.\n*Iustitia passiva* (passive justice): by virtue of which someone does what he owes; *activa*: by virtue of which he constrains others to do what they owe. The kindness that is exercised in proportion to conduct belongs to the *activa*.\n===NOTES===\n- schuldig seyn = rendered 'owe' in both places (the German 'was sie schuldig seyn' is literally 'what they are owing').\n- nöthigt = constrains; Verhaltens = conduct; Güte = kindness (cf. Gütig in 4132).",[]],"reflm:4134":["That God is the cause of the world through his nature can be assumed, even though he is not the author.\nThat God is the author of the world, i.e. that it rests on his decree, can be assumed, even though he is not the creator, i.e. has not produced it in time.\nA beginning in the world can be thought, but not a beginning of the world, because for that an imagined time is required. For the world is supposed not merely to be through another, but to become, since it previously was not.\nNor can the *actus* (act) of creation be thought, i.e. where an *actus* begins together with its effect: for in necessary beings nothing begins.\nNor can, even if one assumed this as possible, any motive of freedom be thought, to choose in absolute time.\nThe world is everlasting, not because it is in the whole absolute time, but because this does not take place at all; [but because] since before the world there is no time, the world is at all times.\nEternity is an unrestricted existence of a thing. [Zuf] Thus only the necessary being is eternal. What is in time is everlasting, but not eternal; for in every time it either has been or still is or at least will be.\n===NOTES===\n- Ursache = cause; Urheber = author; Schopfer = creator; Rathschluße = decree; Bewegungsgrund = motive; Daseyn = existence; immerwährend = everlasting; ewig/Ewigkeit = eternal/eternity.\n- '[Zuf]' is a broken-off deleted fragment, left as it stands; '[sondern darum]' is rendered '[but because]'.\n- 'in der Ganzen absoluten Zeit' = 'in the whole absolute time' (literal); 'noch' in the last sentence is left as the bare 'still'.",[]],"reflm:4135":["God has created the world from eternity (g i.e. his decree is eternal *a parte ante et post* (from the side of before and after), or properly without *ante et post*; but this decree is called creation with regard to the beginning of the world, preservation with regard to the continuation), but thereby has not created an eternal world. (g Eternity belongs in the first case to the divine action (s causality), in the second to the product of it, which cannot have such a determination.) For the eternity of God is his duration outside all condition of time, but the eternity of the world is in time. The duration of the world can thus be brought with the duration [of the world] (s of God) into no common measure. (s The phenomenon of a thing is a product of our sensibility. God is author of things in themselves.)\n===NOTES===\n- Schopfung = creation; erhaltung = preservation; fortdauer = continuation; Dauer = duration; Bedingung = condition; Bestimmung = determination; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Dinge an sich = things in themselves.\n- 'Phänomenon' rendered 'phenomenon'.\n- The bracket '[der Welt]' is a deleted reading, carried over as it stands.",[]],"reflm:4136":["What is formed is mere matter. What forms itself, a growth. What forms other things in the world, animal. One does not call stones creatures, because they do not have their form from creation, but plants indeed. The animals are called creatures. Beings acting freely are not called creatures.\n===NOTES===\n- One English word, 'creatures', stands for two German words: Geschöpfe (stones, plants) and Creaturen (animals, free beings).\n- Wesen = beings; Gewachs = growth (also 'plant' in some contexts); thier = animal; Form = form; Materie = matter.",[]],"reflm:4137":["A substance of the world may not be the creator of another, because otherwise it would depend on itself (g in accordance with its whole existence) (on account of *commercium*). (g For things that stand in *commercio* can stand in *commercio* only through that which makes their existence possible as externally dependent.) The *substantia creatrix est extramundana* (creating substance is extramundane).\nThe creator of one substance is at the same time the creator of all, because he places all the rest in dependence on this one. (g There is no community without a common *principium*.)\nCreation is a unity, i.e. several substances did not come to the created ones gradually. For otherwise we would have no rule or unity for the use of our understanding. If e.g. in the growth of a tree substances arose in addition; therefore the *principium stabile perpetuum* (stable, perpetual principle) must also be *invariabile* (invariable) with regard to *qvantitas* (quantity).\n===NOTES===\n- substantz = substance; Gemeinschaft = community (cf. *commercium*); Einheit = unity; Gebrauch = use; Verstand = understanding; Existentz = existence.\n- 'mag nicht ... seyn' = 'may not be'; the German could also mean 'cannot be'. The ambiguity is kept.\n- 'Nach und Nach' = 'gradually'. The sentence beginning 'Wenn z.E.' has no main clause; kept as a fragment.",[]],"reflm:4138":["(g Evil lies only in freedom.)\nNature is good in the whole. 2. Good in each particular order, which has its laws. e.g. even the faults of the female sex, which are natural.\nBut the contingent, i.e. the occurrence of nature, insofar as it is made to deviate from one of the rules of order through the influence of the others, thus insofar as it lies not under a particular rule of predetermination, but merely of possibility, can be evil.\n===NOTES===\n- Zufall (noun) rendered 'the contingent', as zufällig = contingent in the glossary; the German noun is Zufall, not Zufälliges.\n- Begebenheit = occurrence; Vorherbestimung = predetermination; Möglichkeit = possibility; besonder = particular.\n- Böse = evil; Gut = good; stekt = lies (literally 'sticks/is lodged').",[]],"reflm:4139":["If I could assume a most perfect world without a wise author, then everything important in morals could be seen without the existence of God.\n===NOTES===\n- Vollkommenste = most perfect; urheber = author; Daseyn = existence; eingesehen werden = 'be seen' (einsehen, not verstehen or erkennen).",[]],"reflm:4140":["To fear God: to do his commandments from obligation. To love God: to do his commandments gladly.\n}\nVeneration.\n===NOTES===\n- Schuldigkeit = obligation; Verehrung = veneration. The brace is the Academy's sign, carried over.",[]],"reflm:4141":["God proves his honor through the exercise of his moral attributes, i.e. through actions on account of which he is worthy of veneration and adoration, not of high praise. We do not always venerate the one whom we love, nor the one whom we fear, but at the same time. *adoratio* (adoration) is love, fear and admiration at the same time.\n===NOTES===\n- beweiset = proves (Beweis = proof); Eigenschaften = attributes; Verehrung = veneration; Anbetung = adoration; Hochpreisung = high praise.\n- 'sondern zugleich' is elliptical in the German and is left so.",[]],"reflm:4142":["Creation (beginning) springs from freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- Schopfung = creation; Anfang = beginning; freyheit = freedom.",[]],"reflm:4143":["The end of creation is at all times a combination of the world with the divine highest delight, but not as its ground, but as its sequel. Human beings also in moral things depend in part on their end. But if they could feel their own moral perfection also without the exercise of it, then the pleasure would not arise through the action, but this from that.\nThe highest happiness [from t] under the condition in creatures, which lets them regard it as universally good, is the last end. Thus the highest happiness agreeing with morality.\nThus our actions are never directed to the satisfaction of the divine desire. The ends of the actions therefore lie not in God, but in creatures; therefore the honor of God is not the end, but the [motive] ground of good actions.\n===NOTES===\n- Zwek = end; Verbindung = combination; Grund = ground; Folge = sequel; Lust = delight; Vergnügen = pleasure; Begierde = desire; Geschopfen = creatures; Glückseeligkeit = happiness.\n- '[aus d]' is a broken-off deleted fragment (aus d[er/em ...]), rendered '[from t]'; '[Bewegungs] grund' = '[motive] ground' (deleted compound element followed by 'grund').\n- 'unter der condition bey Geschopfen' is obscure and left as it stands; 'letzte' = 'last'.",[]],"reflm:4144":["*Omnipraesentia* (omnipresence) not (g itself) in space, not *localis* (local), but *virtualis* (virtual).\n===NOTES===\n- Raum = space.",[]],"reflm:4145":["Not (g as) world-soul, which reciprocally also suffers.\n*Spatium sensorium omnipraesentiae divinae* (space the sensorium of divine omnipresence): Newton.\n*Intima praesentia fanatice repraesentatur* (the inmost presence is fanatically represented).\n===NOTES===\n- Weltseele = world-soul; leidend = suffering (could also be 'passive'); wechselseitig = reciprocally.\n- The Latin is rendered beside it; the first Latin line has no verb, kept as is.",[]],"reflm:4146":["(s system of pure reason. Not mathematic.)\nMetaphysics is a philosophy of pure reason.\n—\n— —\n— —\n— —\nof the form.\n—\n— —\n— —\n— —\nof the subject and not object.\n(s is the Critique of pure reason.)\n— — of those [Ges] conditions under which alone human pure reason can judge.\n(s *Conceptus vel empirici vel puri* (concepts either empirical or pure))\n===NOTES===\n- Vernunft = reason; rein = pure; Menschliche = human; urtheilen = judge (v.); conditionen = conditions; Form = form.\n- The dashes are the Academy's marks for the lines of the note, carried over; '[Ges]' is a broken-off deleted fragment, left as it stands.\n- 'des subiects und nicht obiects' is kept as it stands (subiect/obiect = subject/object; Object = object).",[]],"reflm:4147":["One can never infer from below upward (for reason infers only from the general to the particular), but only by means of higher rules, under which many particular cases are subsumed, to a subordinated law.\n(s One must regard the *principia* (principles) as heterogeneous (rational).)\n===NOTES===\n- schließen = infer; Vernunft = reason; Gesetze = law (the form 'ein subordinirt Gesetze' is irregular in the German, kept as 'a subordinated law').\n- Ungleichartig = heterogeneous (literally 'of unlike kind'); the parenthesis '(rational)' is Kant's.",[]],"reflm:4148":["Critique is a science not of production, but of the assessment of certain things according to rules of perfection; thus metaphysics a science to assess cognitions from pure reason. Besides logic, which is a [organon] propaedeutic science of all erudition, the erudition of reason contains a critical part of experience and reason and two dogmatic; the first is metaphysics, the second mathematic and morals in pure reason, and physics and psychology in empirical science of reason.\n===NOTES===\n- Beurtheilung/beurtheilen = assessment/assess (glossary: assess; estimation; judgment); Wissenschaft = science; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Erkentnisse = cognitions; Erfahrung = experience.\n- Gelehrsamkeit = erudition; VernunftGelehrsamkeit = 'erudition of reason' (compound, written as one word).\n- '[organon]' and '[phil:]' are deleted readings, carried over; 'critischen Theil ... und zwey dogmatische' (the noun after 'two' is left unsupplied, as in the German).",[]],"reflm:4149":["*Philosophia transscendentalis* (transcendental philosophy).\n*Omnitudo* (allness) *est* (s *disiunctiva* (disjunctive))\n*vel distributiva* (either distributive).\n*Vniversalitas* (universality).*\n*vel collectiva* (or collective).\n*Vniversitas* (totality).\n*Omnitudo collectiva est vel coordinationis vel subordinationis* [*et*] (Collective allness is either of coordination or of subordination); *prioris terminus est mundus, posterioris terminus est ens primum* (the bound of the former is the world, the bound of the latter is the first being).\n*Hinc* (Hence). 1. *Ontologia* 2. *Cosmologia* 3. *Theologia* }\n}\n}\n} *transscendentalis* { s\n{\n{\n{pure philosophy applied to nature in general or this nature. }\n}\n}\n}\n*(g *distributiva* is logical. Each one.\n*collectiva*: real. All together.\nEach [human being is] body is movable; not: all together are movable.\n[Al] Each alteration has an end; not: all together an end.)\n===NOTES===\n- The Latin is Kant's; English added beside. Latin 'terminus' rendered 'bound' (Grenze in the glossary is German; here the Latin word is kept for the sense given). Both *Vniversalitas* and *Vniversitas* are Latinate words that I have rendered 'universality' and 'totality' (Allheit).\n- The braces, 's', and the asterisk marks belong to the Academy's apparatus (layers and the footnote sign), carried over as they stand; the layout of the braces is only approximate to the manuscript.\n- '[et]' and '[Al]' are deleted or broken-off readings; '[Mensch ist]' = '[human being is]' (deleted); the sentence 'Ein jeder [Mensch ist] Korper ist beweglich' is thus awkward, kept so.\n- Veränderung = alteration; Natur = nature; reine philosophie = pure philosophy; angewandt = applied.",[]],"reflm:4150":["All philosophy is either empirical or *pura* (g *intellectualis*).\nThe first is that whose *principia* are borrowed from experience. Hence *psychologia et physica*.\nThe *philosophia pura* is either *theoretica* or *practica*.\nThe *theoretica* has either no object of the senses at all for its object: *transscendentalis*, or it has the universal of all objects of the senses for its object: *metaphysica propria*. The latter consists of the *physiologia, mechanica, pnevmatologia, theologia naturalis*.",[]],"reflm:4151":["Stolpian question: From the attributes which are common to any things whatever constituting this universe, (Yet no others are common, or rather are cognized as common, than that they constitute [this] universe.) to demonstrate that they are not necessary, but created by a necessary being.\n––––––––––\nProf: Pestel, Secretary of the Stolpian institute, before 1 July 1771.",[]],"reflm:4152":["That ontology is nothing other than a transcendental logic (g subjective), but applied metaphysics merely negative, and nothing remains like morals, whose *data* the human will provides, and the *principia formalia* are analytic.",[]],"reflm:4153":["The synthetic judgments of pure reason are not clear through themselves, but only in their application *in conseqventia*, and so cannot be used *in antecedentia*.",[]],"reflm:4154":["Since with the sensible there are matter and form, should there not also be matter and form in the intellectual?\nThe form of sensibility is twofold: space and time.\nThe form of reason is twofold: logical and real.\nuniversal and particular\n| ground and sequel",[]],"reflm:4155":["The metaphysical concepts are first absolute. Possibility and existence. Second respective.\na. Unity and plurality. *omnitudo* and *particularitas*. [Infin]\nb. Boundaries. The first, the last, *infinitum, finitum*.\nc. Combination of coordination. Whole and part, simple and composite.\nof subordination.\n1. subject and predicate.\n2. Ground and sequel.",[]],"reflm:4156":["We can think to ourselves no contingent being that is not necessary in a conditioned way, and we must finally terminate this subordination at a being that is unconditionally necessary, although we have no insight of reason into this absolute necessity, because otherwise we would cognize its existence in itself immediately; and the *ens necessarium* is a concept given through reason and its boundary, but not found within the boundary, hence problematic.\nWe can think nothing that happens without its being contingent, and indeed without thinking of an absolute contingency [to th] of the first ground in arising[so that]. The first beginning takes place only through freedom.\nThus according to rules of (g pure) reason nothing is absolutely contingent, and the supreme causality is necessary; but according to rules of reason that has concepts of appearance as its ground, the causality of the first cause of what happens is contingent. According to rules of morality there must be authors and not mere intermediate causes of occurrences; according to rules of [Pure reason metaphysics] causality there is only one author, who however is outside the world.",[]],"reflm:4157":["The actuality of (g contingent) things requires a necessary *principium*, arising requires the absolute contingency of causality. Thus [the concepts are laws of appearance to the laws of reason] reason cannot be used [a pr] with the principles, which applied to appearances are correct, *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4158":["One can represent a *subiect* only through its *praedicata* and *praedicata* only in their *subiect*. Hence the necessity of representing substances, which is more a subjective necessity of the laws of our understanding than an objective one.\nAll judgments arise in that the understanding pays attention to its own actions or in general to the representations. If these are representations of sensation and of their relations, then they are empirical and correct; if they are representations breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4159":["Of arbitrary [and abstracted] concepts many sorts of *definitiones* can be given, i.e. explanations, from which [all] entirely the same things can follow be derived. and for that very reason [from] of one proposition different proofs. But of self-subsistent concepts of reason only breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4160":["The adverbs and prepositions, likewise (I, you), further the |\npluperfect etc. are inexplicable; philosophy about languages consists in this.\nPiccard: *isagoge in lectionem aristotelis*.",[]],"reflm:4161":["If it were a matter of names of sciences, we would distinguish *Physica, Metaphysica, Hyperphysica* and *hypophysica*. Namely 1. the nature of actual things (g objects of the senses) from experience, 2. of all possible things (g objects) from reason*. 3. That which depends on the cause of nature among actual things. 4. What is actual without having its determining ground in any nature, i.e. chance. (g and *Qvalitas occulta*.) (s and blind necessity.)\nThis division is valid for [under] the determination of the sciences with regard to empirical cognition.\n*(g We cognize through reason the things and their relation, or the relation of concepts. Morals.)",[]],"reflm:4162":["All cognition has either empirical or rational principles; the latter are either logical or real. The logical take the first concepts of things and relations as they are given, |\nand consider only the subordination according to identity and contradiction; the second are first concepts and relations given through the nature of reason.",[]],"reflm:4163":["All sciences of (g pure) reason are either, those which through pure reason consider the rules of cognition in general, or the particular rules of pure reason itself. *Logica. Phaenomologia generalis, Noologia generalis* have merely the rules of general [and not empirical] cognitions, which are given through no experience, as their end. The applied noology to that which is given through experience, although not through grounds of experience, is theoretical: metaphysics, or practical: morals.",[]],"reflm:4164":["*Metaphysica est organon intellectus puri, primo critica.*\n(Metaphysics is the organon of the pure understanding, first of all critical.)\n[*Omnis dubitatio*]\n*Scepticismus est vel dogmaticus vel criticus, prior* breaks off.\n(Skepticism is either dogmatic or critical, the former)",[]],"reflm:4165":["The *terminus* of coordination is the world, of subordination God; thus *cosmologia* and *theologia*. The *omnitudo logica* is in ontology *realis*.",[]],"reflm:4166":["The first part, on the first grounds of our pure cognition of reason of all things (g the supreme *principium cognoscendi*). Ontology.\nThe 2nd part, on the first ground of things: the supreme *principium essendi*. *Theologia Naturalis*.\nOn the first principles of reason with regard to [objects] corporeal nature, i.e. on the possibility of outer appearances *pnevmatologia* or breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4167":["*In philosophia empirica fingere licet, qvia possibilitas principiorum est empirice data; in philosophia pura fingere non licet, qvia fictiones essent purae rationis, h.e. relationes nec e natura rationis nec ab experientia datae ideoque possibilitatis plane arbitrario assertae.*\n(In empirical philosophy it is permitted to feign, because the possibility of principles is empirically given; in pure philosophy it is not permitted to feign, because fictions would be of pure reason, i.e. relations given neither from the nature of reason nor from experience and hence asserted of possibility quite arbitrarily.)",[]],"reflm:4168":["Metaphysics [is a science] has either mere objects of pure reason for its object or [also] objects of the senses, of which it comes to know the grounds and their nature not through the senses, but through pure reason; the former is the general, the latter the particular part (*Metaphysica applicata*) and consists of the *pneumatologia* and *somatologia pura*; before both the *physiologia* of inner or outer sense must go ahead, but not before the *metaphysica universalis*,\n|\nnamely ontology and *theologia generalis*, of which the first considers the supreme *principium* of all cognitions through pure reason, the second the supreme *principium* of all things to be cognized through pure reason. In *ontologia* everything is considered *disiunctive*, in *theologia Naturalis* and *cosmologia* everything *collective*.",[]],"reflm:4169":["Generality (g *Omnitudo*) is either the distributed or the comprehending generality, *distributiva* or *collectiva*. Of this latter it must first be treated in general. (It has its boundaries or not.) The *collectiva* is either of subordination or of coordination. That of the latter gives the concept of the world, the former the concept of the original being as the ground of all sequels subordinated to one another, so that no sequel is encountered among those that stand under one another that does not stand under it.\nThe [univers] *omnitudo distributiva* is either *universaliter* or *disiunctive distributiva*. Ontology treats of the predicates of things which hold *universaliter* or *disiunctive* of all. *Cosmologia* of all together that belong as parts to one and the same whole. *Theologia naturalis* of all together that belong as sequels to one ground.",[]],"reflm:4170":["An empty concept is one that (g to) no object [has] goes; the negative: that goes to the negation of it; the impossible: that cancels itself.",[]],"reflm:4171":["Logical cancelling is as much as: not to posit; the real just as much as: to remove what is there. *prior negatio, posterior ablatio*.",[]],"reflm:4172":["The principles of pure reason are two: 1. from the concepts given through pure reason, but confused, *per analysin*, e.g. substance, whole, necessary etc:; 2. from the concepts given through reason *per synthesin*. The concepts given through reason before all experience, although abstracted on the occasion of experience from the laws of reason, e.g. of the ground, are forms impressed on the ordering understanding for the use of experiences.",[]],"reflm:4173":["What lies in the concept of a necessary being and can be drawn from it *per analysin*, is little; what is combined with it *per synthesin*, i.e. what is required for the necessary being, is much, but can only be cognized *per principia synthetica*.",[]],"reflm:4174":["Everything that happens is in the series of successions [des] and is represented therein. But nothing can be represented in absolute time, rather things are represented only in an order of sequence, insofar as a real connection of them is conceived, through which one member draws the other after it; therefore nothing in a series can be cognized as actual if the transition from what precedes to it is not necessary according to a universal law. I.e. without a ground, although one does not cognize it.\nThe relations of things are in general always represented through real connections, and time is only the form of the appearance, in which these things connected in such a way are intuited. Time brings forth no representations of connected things.\n===NOTES===\n- Verknüpfung = connection; Verhältnisse = relations; Reihenfolge = 'order of sequence' (literal compound); Reihe = series.\n- 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'angeschauet' = intuited (anschauen).\n- 'D.i. ohne einen Grund, ob man ihn gleich nicht erkennt': 'ihn' is left unresolved (Grund).\n- '[des]' is an editorial bracket (deletion), carried over.",[]],"reflm:4175":["The thinking of negation is properly a thinking of the removal of true position, which otherwise could creep into the understanding. A visible lunar eclipse is possible, but not a visible darkness of Milton.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstand = understanding; 'Position' (Latinate) rendered 'position', cf. setzen = posit.\n- 'Dunkelheit des Milton' left literal; the reference is not explained.",[]],"reflm:4176":["In the essence is the sufficiency, as far as coordination is concerned, and the [subordination was] precision (g measuredness), as far as subordination is concerned, the aim. *reductio ad minimos terminos*.\n===NOTES===\n- 'zulenglichkeit' (for Zulänglichkeit) = sufficiency (cf. zureichend); 'Wesen' = essence here; 'Absicht' = aim.\n- 'Abgemessenheit' = measuredness (literal; no glossary entry). 'praecision' = precision.\n- The syntax is elliptical and was left so; the construction is obscure.\n- Bracketed '[subordination was]' and '(g ...)' are Academy apparatus, carried over.",[]],"reflm:4177":["Properly only substances exist. Accidents are only predicates of existing substances. The predicate itself is nothing existing.\n===NOTES===\n- 'existiren' = exist; 'substantzen' = substances; 'accidentien' = accidents (Latinate plural form, not in glossary).",[]],"reflm:4178":["Necessity is either (g as) of the *rationatum* or as of the ground or of independence;\nNecessity is either absolute, i.e. necessary in every respect,\nor hypothetical: necessary only in a certain respect.\nOmnimodal contingency is of that whose opposite is possible in any respect, the mere contingency, the chance. Contingency [omnimod] in external respect omnimodal is freedom.\nA first beginning can be thought only through freedom. Motion is by its nature internally contingent, but, *a certain external nexus being given*, always necessary; freedom is the reverse.\n===NOTES===\n- 'der bloße Zufall' = 'the mere contingency' (Zufall), and 'das Ungefehr' = 'the chance'; Zufall is rendered with the same root as Contingentia/zufällig.\n- '*rationatum*' = what is grounded (left in Latin); 'Freyheit' = freedom; 'Anfang' = beginning.\n- 'Contingentia[omnimod]': the bracket is Academy apparatus, carried over; the Latin sentence is syntactically awkward and was kept so.",[]],"reflm:4179":["Two propositions that stand directly opposed to one another: if something is, then it is necessary through something else (*ratio intelligendi in serie data*, ground of understanding in a given series); if something necessary is, then there is an absolutely necessary being (*ratio complete intelligendi seriem, h.e. terminandi*, ground of completely understanding the series, i.e. of terminating it); according to the first there is no absolutely necessary, according to the second there is. The cause is, because possibility without actuality and yet not without all can be thought.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Ursache' = cause; 'Wirklichkeit' = actuality; 'Wesen' = being; 'Sätze' = propositions.\n- 'nicht ohne alle' is elliptical (all of what is not specified) and left so.\n- English glosses of the Latin in parentheses are mine, added beside the technical terms.",[]],"reflm:4180":["The hypothetically necessary, without an absolutely necessary principle, permits a long use of reason, if one does not think of the boundary, and is therefore better suited to the understanding than the mere chance; but if one denies it (*atheismus assertorius*), this use of reason is nevertheless again made useless, for the whole chain is contingent, because it requires something outside itself, which does not hang further on another link, and this is denied. The concept of the absolutely necessary is thus an essential condition of the use of our reason, but only a presupposition, not however a product of it. For I leave out all condition finally; [ich] what remains can no longer be cognized a priori, i.e. be seen into. I grasp after a higher hold and am in empty space, which is infinite and through itself makes everything possible and bounds it. But this boundary-concept we can cognize sufficiently respectively in relation to the things subordinated to it, namely what is required for it to hold all things through itself. So it is also with freedom, the first beginning of arising. We know well what flows from freedom and its presupposition, and also have need [es] to presuppose it. but no one can comprehend the arising of a free action, because it is the beginning of all arising. I am a substance, in me the predicates terminate, and I am myself none. This self I know with certainty as the *terminus* of my imputations. But I am contingent in all respects and am not the independent of existence, although of inherence etc etc. That without presupposing which [zu] I cannot use my reason (the necessity), is respectively in relation to me the condition of my reason; but that without knowing which I cannot use my reason completely, is merely a boundary for reason.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Grentze/Grenze' = boundary throughout (incl. 'Grentzbegrif' = boundary-concept); 'begräntzt' = bounds; 'Schranke' does not occur.\n- 'erkennen' = cognize; 'begreifen' = comprehend; 'wissen' = know; 'kennen' = know (ich kenne); 'einsehen' = 'see into'.\n- 'Haltung' = hold; 'ringe' = link (literal 'ring'); 'Verneint' = denies; 'Voraussetzung' = presupposition; 'entstehen' = arising.\n- 'respective' left as 'respectively'; in the last sentence 'zu' in '[zu]' is Academy-bracketed deletion, carried over at the position it stands in the text.\n- 'Ich fasse nach einer höheren Haltung': 'nach' read as 'after/for' (grasp after); kept literal.",[]],"reflm:4181":["From succession one cannot yet infer contingency; for the state following thereupon is no *contradictorie oppositum* of the previous one, consequently (g from that) it is not to be cognized that its opposite is possible.\n[absolute] (g internal, internally) unchangeable means either: what by itself alone can never change; but what also cannot change in any combination is simply unchangeable. The internally unchangeable has no such difficulty as the internally changeable for reason.\n===NOTES===\n- 'veränderlich/unveränderlich' = changeable/unchangeable; 'Verbindung' = combination; 'schlechterdings' = simply.\n- '*contradictorie oppositum*' = opposite in the manner of contradiction (kept Latin).\n- 'vor die Vernunft' = 'for reason'; 'schließen' = infer.",[]],"reflm:4182":["*Realitas est vel comparativa vel absoluta.* (Reality is either comparative or absolute.)\n*Realitas phaenomenon non est absoluta.* (The reality of phenomena is not absolute.) e.g. impenetrability.\n*Inter realitates et negationes phaenomena (g non) datur tertium sensuale, sed intellectuale.* (Between realities and negations of phenomena (g not) there is given a third, sensual, but intellectual.) (s between realities of phenomena there is given a third =0.)\n(s The intellectual is that whose concept is a doing.)\n===NOTES===\n- The inserted '(g non)' sits oddly before 'datur tertium sensuale, sed intellectuale'; its placement is doubtful and was left as it stands, so that the sentence remains obscure.\n- 'Thun' = doing; 'Begrif' = concept; 'Intellectuel' kept as 'intellectual'.\n- 'phaenomenon' (as printed) read as a genitive plural.",[]],"reflm:4183":["Every quantum is not on that account a *compositum*, not even |\nonce every *totum*, although it is *divisibile*; but the *divisio* is rather a distinguishing than a separation; hence it is also not possible through composition of the separated. A continuous quantum is not divisible [omne]. The magnitude of an (g intensive) ground proves no composition out of smaller [ausser], but the magnitude of a whole.\nWhat can be divided only by limitation is not possible through composition; hence not space.\n(s The earth is not composed of lands, but divided into them.\nmateria: parts,\nforma: conjunction. Coordination, not subordination.\nQuantity, totality.)\n===NOTES===\n- 'Einschränkung' = limitation (the glossary's Schranke is also 'limitation'); 'Größe' = magnitude; 'Grund' = ground; 'Unterscheidung' = distinguishing; 'Trennung' = separation; 'Zusammensetzung' = composition.\n- 'ein jedes' left as 'every'; 'Every quantum is not ... a compositum' preserves the German ambiguity of scope.\n- The sign | and the brackets [omne], [ausser], (g ...), (s ...) are Academy apparatus, carried over.",[]],"reflm:4184":["Opposite the column heading:\nIncomprehensible is the first of things (*necessarium*) and the first of states (*initium primum: libertas*). Likewise the last, i.e. the outermost of things in the synthesis, that breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- 'unbegreiflich' = incomprehensible (begreifen = comprehend); 'Zustände' = states; 'Dinge' = things.\n- 'das bricht ab' may be an editorial remark rather than Kant's text; left as it stands.\n- The first line is the Academy Edition's locus note.",[]],"reflm:4185":["Several things conjoined make a composite. All conjoined, a whole. Therefore not every composite (e.g. eternity) is a whole, although every whole is a composite. The word 'all' is taken either generically or singularly. Of all numbers we declare generically in arithmetic, and all straight lines we think in geometry generically, but to think all and each singly [sin] is not permitted.\nIn the rational concept of a composite the terminus of analysis is the simple and the synthetic terminus the whole. The former is the terminus *a priori*, the latter *a posteriori*.\nIn order that all be thought distributively, it is necessary that unities be posited simultaneously. And therefore eternity is not a whole.\nIn any whole, therefore, are to be thought: matter, form and totality.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin translated; 'Materia, forma, totalitas' = matter, form, totality (cf. Materie, Form, Allheit).\n- '[sin]' is an Academy bracket (deletion), carried over; 'omnes et singulas' is literally 'all and single ones'.\n- 'vox omnia' = the word 'all' (mentioned, hence quotation marks); 'generice' = generically; 'distributive' = distributively.",[]],"reflm:4186":["Time is the first condition of the possibility of a whole, for coordination demands simultaneity and subordination succession.\n===NOTES===\n- 'conditio' = condition; 'possibilitas' = possibility; 'totum' = whole; 'simultaneitas' = simultaneity; 'successio' = succession.",[]],"reflm:4187":["Those that are simultaneous are equally connected through time as (g those) that are after one another. But time is unique; therefore there is a possibility of the coordination of several depending on one. Coordination of contradictorily opposed things is succession, of non-opposed things coexistence.\nThe ground through which the existence of several pertains to one, namely that they are simultaneous, lies hidden in this, that [all be through] grounds bring about one state of the world; and therefore simultaneity pertains to the unity of the world and attests to a common cause. (time is everywhere.) that changes are possible (in which the idea of succession in time consists), rests breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- 'ratio' = ground; 'caussa' = cause; 'mundi status' = state of the world; 'coexistentia' = coexistence; 'mutationes' = changes.\n- 'connexa' = connected (cf. Verknüpfung, connexio).\n- 'nititur bricht ab': 'bricht ab' ('breaks off') is German and probably editorial; the sentence is cut off, and the object of 'rests' is missing.\n- 'Coordinatio contradictorie oppositorum': 'things' supplied only to render the Latin neuter plural.",[]],"reflm:4188":["Space is no concept of experience: 1. only through it are experiences possible; 2. it is no object of the senses; 3. the immediate principles of it do not have the contingency and particularity of the propositions of experience and are also not appeals to experience. It |\nis also no concept of reason: 1. it is no general, but a single concept; hence all spaces parts of a single one and the principles [not] cognized through immediate intuiting.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Grundsätze' = principles; 'Erfahrungssätze' = propositions of experience (Satz = proposition); 'Gegenstand' = object; 'Sinne' = senses.\n- 'Vernunftbegrif' = concept of reason; 'einzelner' = single; 'anschauen' = intuiting.\n- 'l.' read as '1.'; the last clause lacks a verb ('all spaces parts ...'), kept as it stands.\n- The sign | is Academy apparatus.",[]],"reflm:4189":["With time is the relation of things among one another in regard to existence in general, whether they are simultaneous or after one another, and the magnitude of their duration. They appear all as in one [Dase] whole of existence, yet [mit] of vanishing magnitude in coming-to-be and passing away at once. With space is the relation of things in regard to their community: all as in one whole, which makes all [possible] community possible.\nBecause we cognize not merely the space of the object that touches our senses but the whole space by intuiting, space must not merely arise from the actual touching of the senses but precede it. Meanwhile, because it is nevertheless represented as something actual, it will be the effect of the feeling of omnipresence and time of the dependence of all things in existence on One, just as space the sequel from this to the community of things.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Daseyn' = existence; 'Gemeinschaft' = community; 'Verhältnis' = relation; 'Wirkung' = effect; 'Folge' = sequel; 'Gefühl' = feeling.\n- 'anschauend erkennen' rendered 'cognize ... by intuiting' (participial adverb; could also qualify the whole space).\n- 'von verschwindender Größe' could attach to 'Dinge'/'Daseyns'; left ambiguous. The last clause is elliptical (no verb after 'der Raum') and was left so.\n- 'Object' (obiects) and 'Gegenstand' both = 'object'.",[]],"reflm:4190":["All existence in time is contingent. For it is a perpetual vanishing and beginning; and from the fact that a thing exists it does not follow that it will exist. Necessary existence is the only complete existence.\nExistence in time is to necessary existence as [the present] in space, with regard to extension, an infinite line is to the motion of a point in it.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Daseyn' = existence; 'existiren' = exist; 'zufallig' = contingent; 'nothwendig' = necessary; 'Ausdehnung' = extension; 'vollstandig' = complete.\n- The comparison's construction is obscure (what '[die Gegenwart]' attaches to); kept as in German. The bracket is Academy apparatus.",[]],"reflm:4191":["M 72:\nOf space the rules are: 1. that, since it is only the condition of the appearance of outer things, one must not regard it as the condition of the existence of the things themselves, unless the things themselves must be only appearances; 2. that the predicates of space must not be regarded as predicates of things, but of their appearances, consequently the propositions that express the possibility of things do not hold of space; 3. that space is something entirely true |\nM 72': |\nwith regard to the outer appearances, because it is the condition for them.",[]],"reflm:4192":["Infinity is no objective determinate concept of a magnitude in relation to others, but subjectively a surpassing of a magnitude beyond all that can be stated by us, though not beyond all that can be stated by every understanding.",[]],"reflm:4193":["Over, beside and \nUnder the things the following are kinds of the greatest:\n1. the concept that contains everything [under itself] in its sphere (under itself),\n2. that which comprises everything in itself as a whole,\n3. that which comprehends everything under itself as a ground.\nThe widest concept, the greatest whole, the greatest ground. Logical. Mathematical. Metaphysical: all intellectual sciences.",[]],"reflm:4194":["Beside and \nThe potential infinity, actual. Why the past is also counted with the actual.",[]],"reflm:4195":["Under and \nIn the infinite the difficulty is to reconcile the totality with the impossibility of a *synthesis completae*. Consequently the difficulty is subjective. On the other hand the *potentialiter infinitum* (*infinitum coordinationis potentialis*) is very well comprehensible, but without totality.",[]],"reflm:4196":["The greatest concept, the greatest intuition, the greatest thing are all given; and through their limitation we generate the logical, the sensible and real possibility of all things.",[]],"reflm:4197":["The mathematically infinite is without end according to laws of sensible estimation, the metaphysically infinite is without end in general, i.e. without limitations.\n*Maximum vel respective tale, e.g. Ideale, vel absolute qvoad realitatem* (the maximum, or respectively such, e.g. the ideal, or absolute as to reality).",[]],"reflm:4198":["On and \nThe infinite, the greatest *in sensu absoluto*; that which contains everything. The second is one and the same with the third, if the thing is considered as a ground. The third signifies the most.\nThrough reason the greatest can be seen into only by means of the *consummati*.",[]],"reflm:4199":["That two things cannot be in one place is no objective proposition; rather we call [two] things different only because they are in different places, and we have, in one and the same place through outer sense, no ground of the distinction of different things.\nThe being of a thing in a place can be expressed thus: The place is the ground of something, which signifies as much as: to be in a place is as much as to act externally in a certain relation. Because in absolute space no relation of a thing to others is encountered, a thing can be encountered in no absolute place (without relation of the *actus* to others). but in empty space, which is not filled.",[]],"reflm:4200":["1. That [no] (g all) relation in space [one-sided] (g relative and never absolute) is; e.g. no absolute place, no absolute rest or motion, but at every time motion and rest must refer to some other body;\n2. what follows from that: namely that all motion and rest is reciprocal and equal and that no body, with regard to another that is moved in relation to it, rests relatively to it; [that thus effect and that these motions are opposed]*\n3. That [what the production], if they are thus combined, a common rest follows from it, i.e. that effect and counter-effect are equal to one another.\n*(g That no motion can be generated without producing just as much in the opposite direction (in the mover), because a body moves toward another only as much as this |\nagainst it, or another toward itself as itself toward the other, and because they reciprocally recede from one another or approach. [The] Both bodies taken together will therefore, with regard to the [place] space in relation to which they were considered before the generated motion, remain in one and the same state. I.e. if they rested before the communication, their *systema* will rest, and if they moved, their *systema* will move.)",[]],"reflm:4201":["(s the *totum substantiale absolute completum* (the substantial whole absolutely complete) is the *mundus* (world).)\n(g *contingentia*, i.e. respect to a cause (*respectus ad causam*), flows from composition.)\nTo (g the notion) of the world pertain 1. the material: [subjects] plurality of substances. The egoistic world is not a world. 2. the formal, or the (g real) nexus of substances, insofar as they constitute a whole (g coordination (not subordination under the cause of itself)). 3. [determination] a negatively determined respect: That it not be a part of another whole. or the totality. In every whole there is *totalitas* [absoluta] *respectiva*, in the world *totalitas absoluta*. The earth [is] has *totalitatem*; and if one takes all its parts together, one has *totalitatem respectivam*; but it is itself still a part, so it has no *totalitatem absolutam*.\nThe *terminus* of coordination *per analysin*, i.e. *a priori*, is the simple; the *terminus* of the same *per synthesin*, i.e. *a posteriori*, is the world. The *terminus* of subordination *a priori* is the supreme cause, *a posteriori* the totality of the sequels. Reason cognizes its insight as incomplete that is not enclosed in *termini*. The necessity of the *termini*, however, is not objective. But it also cannot think how a *terminus* is to take place, of coordination as well as of subordination.\nThings that are connected only as [mutually] subordinated do not always admit totality, unless by computing they can be coordinated logically.\n(s The completeness (*completudo*) of a composite as such is totality, and this either absolute or hypothetical; the former either with respect to all possible wholes or with respect to some actual whole.)",[]],"reflm:4202":["The *completum* is a *quantum*, it is not a part of another.",[]],"reflm:4203":["In any (g substantial) whole there is a reciprocal dependence of states among the parts, not dependence [sub] as to the actuality of the substance. For cause and caused are not co-parts of the same whole, because the nexus of subordination differs from the nexus of coordination. The dependence of states, however, consists in this, that the conditions of the inherence of the accidents mutually inhere in the substances.",[]],"reflm:4204":["The real (g real) nexus of coordination is mutual, that of subordination of substances as such is not mutual.",[]],"reflm:4205":["The supreme and all-sufficient cause is that which is subordinated and coordinated to no other.\n(s *independentia* and *completudo*)",[]],"reflm:4206":["The coordination, according to which all [Best] *accidentia* of a thing make up the *totum status*, is a sequel of the subordination under one, namely the *substantiale*. The coordination, according to which all substances make up a whole, is the subordination under an *ens primum*.",[]],"reflm:4207":["Opposite and \nSpace and time are formal grounds of the possibility of a world. Space, time and force alone are capable of magnitude in the world.\n1. From the composition of the world the contingency and the beginning or eternity; [2. From space] likewise the magnitude, infinity.\n2. Alteration of the state. Order of nature. Gradation.\nThe supreme ground of combination is also the formal ground of the possibility of the *commercium*. Expressed sensibly it is space. But space is presumably only the sensible intuition, to which the immediate consciousness (intellectual intuition) lies at the basis, but in it can be found by no dissection.",[]],"reflm:4208":["The effects are *symbola* of the causes, thus space (through which things are nevertheless actually represented as necessarily connected by means of a common ground) a *symbolum* of divine omnipresence or the *phaenomenon* of divine [intuitions] causality. In space possibility is not distinguished from actuality.\nAll sensible representation is a *symbolum* of the intellectual. All (g sensible) representation of the relation of the effect to its cause is the *symbolum*.",[]],"reflm:4209":["An infinite past series of appearances would presuppose an infinite duration of sensibility, hence an appearance whose representation would in turn be appearance.",[]],"reflm:4210":["In time the world is either from eternity (at all time *a parte priori*) or not from eternity, but yet in time (in a part of time *a priori*). Both are false. For the part of time outside or before the world [is] or rather absolute time is indeed a condition of actual, but not of non-actual things, in which their *positus* with regard to the preceding would be determined.\nNo infinite world is possible to represent to oneself. Now, however, the world (*phaenomenon*) and time are only something in the representation. Were I to say: whether an infinite world in itself might not be possible, then the world of sense is nothing in itself. As far as I only want to go (g back), the world is finite; to cognize the world back into the infinite is impossible; thus the world of sense, as far as I go, is always finite. But is world thus to be measured *a parte priori*?",[]],"reflm:4211":["M 121':\n*continuum formarum*.\nThere is a *continuitaet* among the *species*; that is: there is no *species alii proxima*, between which others could not be placed, and thus also no *species infima*. We have *species infimas*, because we do not know the small differences. E.g. gold, of which we know no *species*. Just so with rye and plants. Perhaps they were at first very [from one another] placed according to the law of *continuitaet* and were separated only through the mixing.\n*Non datur species infima* can mean either as much as: no *species infima* is possible, or: it is not actual. e.g. the germs that lie in the human being can bring forth a creature that is perfectly similar to an ape.\nM 121:\nDifferent *species* *arguiren* a difference of that which belongs to substance; but the difference of the states makes no difference of the things, thus also not of the *species*.",[]],"reflm:4212":["If everything is there through One, then the thoroughgoing determination of each thing is a restriction of the world-whole, just as each measured space is a *limitation* of infinite space: i.e. the whole does not arise from the composition of the action, through which parts are brought forth, for thereby a *quantum discretum* comes about, but each part is possible only through the restriction of the power that is in the whole. But a product of nature is connected with another only through the restriction of the general force, which produces them |\nboth, thus [also only] because in the general sufficient force lies the ground for all *species* that are possible between one product and the other only by means of all middle products, of which there are infinitely many, that are contained between one *species* and the other.",[]],"reflm:4213":["In the metaphysical understanding, things of one and the same kind are those from one of which to the other the transition is [only] possible through the alteration of magnitude, be it in a property or in the relation of two. By contrast, things of different kind are those whose rule is already different.",[]],"reflm:4214":["139. E II 1354. 1355.\nM 139':\nThe first (g [inner]) inner ground of everything that belongs to the actuality of a thing is the nature of it.\nAn effect has its ground either in the nature or the freedom of the cause. In the first case the opposite is physically, in the second practically impossible.\nThe first ground of the contingent is not in the nature of any thing.\nEverything that happens through the nature of a thing has a previously |\nM 139: |\ngoing ground and is not the first. The [Zus] thoroughgoing determination of that which belongs to the inner of existence, according to subjective laws: nature, according to objective: freedom.",[]],"reflm:4215":["*dato spatio datur possibilitas compraesentiae plurium*, but the *compraesentia* of several is not possible unless a common principle existing is posited; therefore, space being given, one can conclude to some first being (*ens*). But there is a single space; therefore one concludes to a single first cause. Likewise it is necessary, insofar as it is mere possibility; therefore one concludes to a necessary one.",[]],"reflm:4216":["Every *harmonia* is either generally or singularly established; the former requires that something be a substantial whole.\nGenerally established is either externally or internally established; the latter is *influxus physicus*. Singularly established is either *occasionaliter* or *praestabilita*.\n[In universo est] As regards the special *commercium* of mind and body, the difficulty arises from this, that the substance that we cognize by inner sense is wholly unknown [qvoad] as object of outer sense, and thus no force as general principle of external presence [possit] is given; but to fashion it is not permitted. But this trouble is found also [in] around the actions of bodies, whose principle is established only from experience, not by intuition of reason.\nSpace is a single idea; hence it is established that the possibility of the *compraesentia* of several rests on the existence of a single being (*ens*).",[]],"reflm:4217":["148. E II 1693. 784.\nM 148':\nThe cause of substance is creative, the cause of form architectonic. Therefore [causa ent] an extramundane being (*ens extramundanum*) is not only architect, but creator of the world.\nThe world [est non est par] is a whole that is not a [possibilis] actual part of another whole; if it is also not a part of any possible world, it is the greatest whole. The rational cause of the world is *auctor*. The *auctor* is not an architect. etc etc. The *auctor* of the world is neither coordinated nor subordinated to anyone. God.\nThose [externe] that are not harmonious spontaneously, but agreeing by external and arbitrary artifice, are in apparent, i.e. sympathetic, *commercium*. [Qvorum natura] Those that are connected from a general principle, so that their common nature is the ground of mutual dependence, agree physically or naturally. *Influxus** however is physical either originary or derivative (s *subalternus*). The former would have place if the formal ground is domestic to the substances; the latter, if it is foreign. But the cause foreign to the world is an extramundane being. The *commercium* of the substances of the world therefore occurs through derivative *influxus*, or is natural *commercium* on account of common dependence.\nThe nature of the universe as such (since universal nature is the nature of all substances) consists in conjunction and completeness (on which account it is called *rerum universitas*); therefore breaks off.\nM 148:\n*(s Originarily a substance cannot influence a substance, except that which it sustains, because substances do not suffer from others [from which not], unless so that the *passio* is at the same time *actio*.)",[]],"reflm:4218":["(g If there is a power of choice in God, then it is free power of choice.)\nDivine freedom is very well to be comprehended. The *spontaneitaet* and the *necessitas subiectiva per motiva intellectualia* (which never subjectively necessitate, since they do not consist in the way the subject is affected) are in him the most perfect. Here there is no combination of necessity with contingency. But with human freedom 1. a series of determining grounds of each free action is necessary for the comprehensibility of its origin, but [at the same time] then the *spontaneitas simpliciter talis* is impossible because of the [highest deter] external determining grounds in a contingent thing; but on the other hand [if one it a first beginning with the] for a first beginning unconditioned contingency [necessary] or independent freedom is necessary.",[]],"reflm:4219":["The difficulty of comprehending human freedom lies in this, that the subject is dependent and yet is supposed to act independently of other beings.\nThe necessary being cannot begin to act, the contingent no first beginning.",[]],"reflm:4220":["Freedom is properly only the self-activity of which one is conscious. If something occurs to one, this is an *actus* of self-activity; but one is here conscious not of one's activity, but of the |\neffect. The expression: I think (this object), already indicates that I am not passive with regard to the representation, that it is to be ascribed to me, that the opposite depends on myself.",[]],"reflm:4221":["Freedom is the faculty to bring forth and to effect something originarily. How however *causalitas originaria et facultas originarie efficiendi* can take place with an *ente derivativo* is not to be comprehended at all.",[]],"reflm:4222":["Our *arbitrium* is not sensitive, but the *actus arbitrii* either sensitive or intellectual. For *arbitrium* is not the inclination, but the choice from inclination or reason.",[]],"reflm:4223":["Practical is that nothing which I cannot perceive at all*. Now the transcendental necessitation cannot be perceived by me, but I feel nothing but the law, a force sufficient for it, and the liking; thus the practical is never breaks off.\n*(g For that does not come into reckoning of the motives. Shall I on account of this predetermination be indifferent with regard to the aims of life or proceed otherwise. It is one thing to speculate and another to think practically; the former for explaining, the latter for acting.)",[]],"reflm:4224":["The pathological necessitation is inner and agrees with *spontaneitaet*, but it is nevertheless the condition of a possible external necessitation. The intellectual necessitation is not grounded on passive, but |\nactive *conditiones*; thus this is not opposed to freedom, because it is an independent, but self-made liking. If we have the nearest causes, we need not know the interconnection with the most remote, nor how we come to this force that we have. The laws of motion are always the same, the cause of them may be whichever it will.",[]],"reflm:4225":["10\nSince freedom is a complete self-activity of the will, without being determined by *stimulos* or by anything else that affects the subject, all that matters for it is the certainty of personality: that it namely (g itself) is conscious, it acts from its own choice, [it acts] the will is active and not passive, neither through *stimulos* nor through foreign impressions. Otherwise I would have to say: I am driven or moved to act thus or so, which means as much as: I am not acting, but suffering. If God governs the determinations of choice, then he acts; if the stimuli of things necessarily determine it, then they necessitate; in both cases the action does not arise from me, but I am only the means of another cause.\nIn the world of sense nothing is comprehensible except what is necessitated through preceding grounds. The actions of free choice are *phaenomena*; but their connection with a self-active subject and with (g the faculty) of reason are intellectual; accordingly the determination of free choice cannot be submitted to the *legibus sensitivis*.\nThe question whether freedom is possible is perhaps one and the same with, |\nwhether (g the human being) is a true person and whether the I is possible in a being of outer determinations.\nThe I is an inexplicable representation. It is an intuition that is unchangeable.",[]],"reflm:4226":["(g Freedom does not consist in this, that the opposite could have been to our liking, but only that our liking was not passively necessitated.)\nThe *arbitrium sensitivum* without consciousness is *brutum*.\nThe *arbitrium* is for an action either actively determined or passively determined. In the first case it is nevertheless free, although the *motiva* are *obiective necessitantia*. For it acts [without that] necessarily, not because its subject, according to its suffering attribute, sensibility, is affected by the objects. Human choice is never passively determined. The *arbitrium* that is merely actively determined is a good will. But whether (g although) a good will is in itself determined to good actions (g and free), even if the substance is *causatum alterius*: still the mixture with the *sensitivum* is just as much as if the *rationes ad determinandum* were to become *incompletae*. Therefore the human will is determined to no kind of actions from itself. Its choice is thus a free, to be sure, but undetermined choice (the divine is determined). The *arbitrium brutum* is determined *secundum rationes* [intellectuales] *sensitivas*, the divine *secundum intellectuales*, the human through neither. Its actions could all have occurred according to reason. Hence it is free. But is there then not a determining ground, to be sure not in the choice of the human being in general, but yet in the circumstances and conditions? and if this is not, whence then do the actions actually occur. Answer: All *stimuli* of the sensible choice can nevertheless not make the *active* of the human being into the *passive*. The higher choice nevertheless decides itself; but why it sometimes decides to the side |\nof sensibility, sometimes to that of reason, of this no law can be given, because there is no constant law of both forces.\nIn an active *principio* the state has no influence at all on the subject, neither the past nor the present, to determine its action. The subject is at all times from itself the source of the actions. But why it acts thus and not otherwise.",[]],"reflm:4227":["Were human beings entirely intellectual, then all their actions would be actively determined, but yet free, and would be contingent only with regard to the changeable occasions. These actions could also be imputed to them together with the rewards, even if they were creatures of a higher being. For they would be regarded as self-active principles and as worthy objects of its goodness. Were they entirely sensible, then their actions would be solely passively determined; nothing could be imputed to them, and they would not be capable of any rewards and punishments. Now they are partly sensible, partly intellectual, yet so that sensibility admittedly cannot make the intellectual passive, but the intellectual also cannot overcome the actions other than through a certain measure of preponderance over sensibility. therefore the human being is neither actively nor passively determined; and since sensibility as well as the strength of reason depends on the circumstances, his actions depend partly on the circumstances, partly on the use of his reason and cannot be imputed to him entirely. He is free, if one takes it most exactly, only the possibility of doing something good, in which freedom properly consists. Only whether the action actually arises from this principle or the sensitive one, depends on the *conditiones*. Just as in a game every throw can win, regardless of the preceding and accompanying circumstances.",[]],"reflm:4228":["We see ourselves through the consciousness of our personality in the intellectual world and find ourselves free. We see ourselves through our dependence on impressions in the world of sense and find ourselves determined. Our intuitions of bodies all belong to the world of sense; accordingly the experiences agree with the laws of the same, of determining grounds. but our intellectual intuitions of the free will do not agree with the laws of the *phaenomenorum*.",[]],"reflm:4229":["Freedom does not let itself be divided. The human being is either entirely or not at all free, because he either can act from an active *principio* or depends on conditions.",[]],"reflm:4230":["292. E II 1284. I 356.\nM 292':\nThe pure doctrine of the soul, because it presupposes no *principia* (g principles) from experience, can consist of no others than the following pieces:\n1. [Erke] Absolute consideration of the same from transcendental concepts: Substance. Simple (g single). contingent.* (g whether I or another in me effects the *actus* of choice. if I am a contingent being.) finite. (s imperishable.)\n2. Comparison of the same with other beings (g transcendental concepts), which we know through experience:\na, bodies in general. immaterial;\nb,** other thinking natures of the world. Those which are below or beside or above the human being (*bruta*, *incolae planetarum*, *angeli*).\n3. Connection of the same with the body:\n(g first difficulty: how merely inwardly working forces can have outer sequels, how merely outwardly working forces are supposed to bring forth inner alterations. second: how the union of an immaterial being with a material one is possible.)\na. Possibility of the same. *commercium animae et corporis*. Seat of the soul (g cannot alter it). *Sensorium commune*. (g *ideae materiales*. *materialismus* [subli] *vel subiectivus vel formalis*.) (s *Status animae in diverso statu hominis*. *Sedes animae*.) (s *principium* of life. Freedom.)\nb. Beginning. Birth. before birth.\nc. End. Death. after death (s *metempsychosis*). (g Separation. Ceasing of the dependence on the body. whether it is a new dependence? Place.) (g Resurrection. Sleep of the soul, ethereal body. *Metempsychosis*. Place right after death: in the grave, on the earth, in heaven and hell. pnevmatists.)\n*** The *anima* is a thinking subject depending on the body (sensing). The *animus* is indeed active, but bound to the conditions of the body. The *mens* is active in and through itself.\n4. Connection with other spirits. Appearances: inner, outer.\nM 292: |\n*(s [Its] self-activity (s freedom). My state is not necessary through my nature, but I am free. Higher forces of cognition and of the will. Power over oneself, possibility of morality.)\nM 292: |\n**(s General condition of inner sense: consciousness. 2, Merely an object of inner sense: spirit.)\nM 292':\n***(g Soul. Mind. Spirit. A pain strikes the soul through sensation, it is in the mind (g arises) through reflection and in the spirit through disapproval of itself. †)\nM 292: |\n(† s In the one the sensation displeases, in the second the whole state, in the third the person itself. The first feels ill, the second [is] esteems himself unfortunate, the third worthy of misfortune. (Sometimes before the misfortune is yet actual.) )\nAbove the Rfl., between 46719-23, 4681-5, or in the left margin there stand on M 292' also the following s-additions:\n(s 1. Soul as thinking being: a. Belonging to the world of sense. b. To the intelligible. 2. As dignity.)\n(s *Existentia corporis contra idealismum*.)\n(s NB. I am substance, I am simple etc etc. The I in *sensu stricto* contains all of this.)\n(s I as object of inner sense, as thinking, am not object of the outer. Thoughts are not *phaenomena* in space.)\n(s *bruta* without inner sense, consciousness: *Analoga* of Me.\n*spiritus* – – outer – –\nmere animality, mere intelligence. Humanity (*a priori*).)",[]],"reflm:4231":["In theoretical philosophy the objects are either *obiecte rationis purae* (s *et etiam principia intellectualia*): *philosophia transscendentalis*, or *obiecte sensuum* (s *sed principia* (g *vel*) *intellectualia vel* [sens] *empirica*): Nature. *physiologia*.\n*Physiologia vel sensus interni*. *Psychologia*,\n*externi*. *Physica*;\n*vtraque vel e principiis rationalibus vel empiricis*.",[]],"reflm:4232":["*obiecta rationis purae et principia empirica secundum analogiam*, i.e. a certain philosophical law of proportion, [or] from the given sensible world into antecedents to a term *a priori* (principle): God, or into consequences to a term *a posteriori* (end): the World.\n(g 1. Where the intellectual is the first principle,\n2. Where it is the last sequel.)",[]],"reflm:4233":["Opposite 14116-18:\nFrom immateriality it follows that the soul and body do not act on one another through contacts, indeed through that cannot even be present to one another; that the community of the same with matter must rest on that in matter which is no object of appearance etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:4234":["Opposite 14119-21:\nStrictly all proof of the simple nature of the soul amounts to this, that it is an immediate intuition of itself through the absolute unity I, which is the *singularis* of the actions of thinking! For the proof that was taken from this, that many thinking subjects can make up only one thought, signifies just as |\nmuch as: the I is required for it and thinking is always an action in the *singulari*.",[]],"reflm:4235":["At the very top of the page:\nThe soul is not only simple, but also single in the human being. *anima sentiens, vegetativa, rationalis* (sentient, vegetative, rational soul).",[]],"reflm:4236":["The whole difficulty rests on how a body can bring forth thoughts in the soul and this, through its will, movements. Every effective subject alters in the other the inner state, and this outer relation is *influxus* or outer action, whose *phaenomenon* the breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4237":["No matter is living, consequently death, i.e. lifelessness, |\nwhich follows upon life, is no occurrence that can befall the human body. The human being has two kinds of life: 1. the animal, 2. the spiritual. The latter is personality, taken not merely *potentialiter* but *actualiter*. In the existence of the human soul there are to be considered 1. the existence of the substance; 2. life in general (animal) as a soul; 3. personality, i.e. life as a human spirit. It is asked, therefore, whether it is possible that the human soul, even without a body, be a particular person (at the very least it could then not become conscious of its outer state, because for that it is required that one feel oneself through the very same sense with which one perceives outer things). This question does not contain whether certain interruptions of its personality (such as sleep) take place, but whether it could ever become conscious of itself without a body.",[]],"reflm:4238":["314. E II 1263.\nM 314':\nEither the human being has (g long ago, before he was born) represented something great before his birth, or he will after (g his) death represent nothing at all. (g If his origin is to be thanked to birth — —) One should see the contingency of births. |\nM 314: |\n(g If one wants to dispute their contingency, then all bad kings are Pharaohs, since God, if not producing the wickedness, still promotes it a little, in order to prove his power.) |\nM 314': |\nIf he has a spiritual part that thinks even after the complete decay of the body: why should it not have thought rationally before it put it on. If matter had to help him to think, then it will leave him thoughtless [and] when it is separated from him. But how was this spirit compelled to animate this matter? we do not know the world of spirits, and how matter stands under its command. I do not know how philosophers can be so coy with an incomprehensibility.",[]],"reflm:4239":["315. E II 1306. 1268. 1264.\nM 315':\nIf one can infer from the nature of the grown human being to his eternal duration, then the newborn human being too must let one hope for just this. Thus also the embryo, the *ovulum*, the *ovulum* from the *ovulo*. This claim to eternity cannot depend on the contingent combination with the body; for that perfection which cannot arise without combination with corporeal things cannot also continue without it. Thus human souls have had a spiritual life also before the body; thus animal life cannot decide about their eternal fate.\nThe continuing life of the soul is distinguished from its immortality. The first signifies that it will not die, perhaps on account of particular divine arrangements; the second, that it naturally cannot die. The proofs of the first are moral, the second metaphysical. The enduring life of the soul consists not in the continuing of its substance or of the remaining forces, but of its personality. If it can no longer attain to consciousness, then it is like a tree that has died off, that can no longer put forth sap.\nThe physical of this life is of no significance, because it concerns only the contingent combination with the corporeal world, which is not our natural state; but the moral, which can be encountered only in the soul in accordance with its spiritual nature, hangs together with the spiritual life, and because the moral belongs to the inner worth of the person, it is inextinguishable, whereas fortune and misfortune, since it belongs merely to the fleeting state, after its brief duration loses all worth*. Hence we must esteem this life lightly. We would however have cause to esteem it highly, if the physical of it were the condition of the beginning of the metaphysical. The [life] physical of life remains accordingly a trifle; birth and death are beginning and end of a scene, in which only morality is considerable, and indeed even that only so that [it] one not act contrary to it.\nM 315:\n*(g To grieve over pain, especially the past: is soft or foolish; but over transgressions: fitting and laudable. Hence the latter belongs to our person, thus insofar as we are spiritual beings.)",[]],"reflm:4240":["316'. E II 1277. 1265.\nM 315':\nMatter is lifeless. This proposition does not cling merely to the concept of matter, but, even if the parts of it possessed life, then without a common *principium*, which united the many mediate causes, no unity of life and no determination according to common choice would be possible. The *principium* of life in the human being, the proper subject of his personality, insofar as it animates matter, is called soul, and the life of the human being is an animal life. But if this *principium* of life can live by itself alone, this life would be a spiritual one. Since the lifeless must be no ground of life, but rather a hindrance of it, the pure spiritual life will be the original and self-subsistent life; on the other hand the animal life is derived and restricted. Thus birth is not a beginning of life in general, but of animal life, and a degree of lifelessness, and the complete spiritual life begins after the death of the animal. The other world will represent not other objects, but just the same objects seen otherwise (namely *intellectualiter*) and in other relations to us. And the cognition of things through divine intuiting, likewise |\nthe feeling of blessedness through him is no longer the world, but heaven.\nBut how was the soul combined with this body?, is just as much as if one asks: what is the beginning and cause of sensibility. On this footing the present life is only the appearance and the image of the spiritual, and the vice that we practice here, |\nM 316': |\nwould, if our eyes were opened, present us in our bad character in the spiritual world, and the punishments here would be only weak depictions of those there; death is nothing other than the end of sensibility. Only is\nthe vice not perhaps itself merely an effect of sensibility and must finally cease with the end of all sensibility itself. Nothing can be imputed to a human being except what belongs to his character and to what is peculiar to him. The vices that one practices before another do not always indicate a more vicious character; most are *delicta fortunae*. Take away everything that does not stand in your power: how much can you ascribe to yourselves. Meanwhile in the state of sensibility the character can be vicious and therefore also punishable, which in the intellectual is only [faulty] deficient, as always with a creature that wants to make applications from the part that it knows which fit the whole.",[]],"reflm:4241":["The concepts of God (and of the general subordination under the law of necessity), of freedom (of contingency and independence from the present world) and of the other world (the relation of our freedom and independent nature to God) are the three concepts of reason that have an inner importance, are *connex* among themselves and are the ground of the weightiness of the whole of metaphysics.\nAll cognitions of reason finally have a practical relation. The concepts of freedom are on both sides problems for reason; but we must, with regard to past actions, assume contingency, to present ones necessity, to future ones both at the same time, if we want to act in some way.\n(s We have a natural *instinct* to direct our care, even without reflection, to [zuk] what will go on in the world after our death, just as the butterfly, before it dies, carefully safeguards and provides for its eggs. We become avaricious in old age and gladly want our property to be well applied. We do not want others to think ill of us. We spare the deceased, just as if they could take harm from it. We miss them in society. One brings food to the dead. The miser likes (g that) his money to remain together after death. Through this prolongation everything in the world hangs together. Funeral ceremonies of crude nations.)\n(s Civilized human beings take leave of life as of society, just as if they presumed to see such once again. They shrink either from dying as *poltrons* or from remaining in memory as base ones, or also from offending those who are left behind and making them angry at themselves.)\n===NOTES===\n- Vernunftbegriffe = concepts of reason; Begriff = concept throughout; Vernunft = reason; Erkenntnisse = cognitions.\n- Erheblichkeit (not in the glossary) = ‘weightiness’; Wichtigkeit = ‘importance’. Two German words, two English words.\n- ‘connex’ is Kant’s own Latinate form (connex), kept as such; Zufelligkeit = contingency (Zufälligkeit).\n- ‘etwas handeln’ rendered ‘to act in some way’; the German is loose.\n- ‘solche’ (feminine) in the last paragraph left as ‘such’, with its antecedent unresolved as in the German.\n- ‘(s’ and ‘(g’ are the Academy Edition’s marks for layers of writing; ‘[zuk]’ is an editorial bracket (an apparently abandoned word); both kept as they stand.",[]],"reflm:4242":["The concept of God is a concept of completion, either of speculation with regard to things, or of morality, and arises from the following basic concepts. 1. A necessary being. 2. The highest cause. 3. The all-sufficient being (instead of the most perfect, because |\nthis does not for that reason contain everything). The first concept is sufficient unto itself. The second completes the *series subordinatorum*, the third that of the *coordinatorum*. One can infer from one to the other. 1. The necessary being is the ground of everything, thus also the first cause. 2. The first cause is necessary, thus also the most perfect being. 3. The most perfect being is necessary and the first cause. The first concept ends all questions about existence.\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen = being; Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Ursache = cause.\n- Vollendungsbegrif = ‘concept of completion’ (a compound; no glossary entry).\n- allgnugsame = ‘all-sufficient’ (cf. zureichend = sufficient, a different word); allervollkommenste = ‘most perfect’ (Vollkommenheit = perfection).\n- The sign | is carried over from the Academy Edition at the page/line break.\n- ‘seriem subordinatorum’ is given in the German text as an accusative; the genitive *coordinatorum* is as written.",[]],"reflm:4243":["The idea of God is occasioned: 1. through a need of the understanding (*vide supra*); 2. through a need of the will. This arises from the lack of the highest grounds from which to derive his fates with regard to happiness and his verdict with regard to morality. The former is the strongest incentive, and here that of the averting of evils is the most powerful. One requires an object of worship, from which, because forces do not suffice, wishes, requests and submissions could fetch our happiness. If from this ground of physical needs the concept of a deity is generated, then it is thought at the beginning as terrible. To this a benevolent *principium* is commonly set at the side.\n===NOTES===\n- Idee = idea; Verstand = understanding; Gegenstand = object; Grund = ground; Begrif = concept.\n- ‘seine schicksale … seinen Urtheilsspruch’: the possessive has no clear antecedent (one’s / his / its); ‘his’ kept, ambiguity preserved.\n- ‘Die erstere’ and ‘Diese’ are left unresolved, as in the German; which need each refers to is not settled by the text.\n- Triebfeder = ‘incentive’ (not in the glossary); Kräfte = ‘forces’ (Kraft = force; ‘powers’ is the other possible reading); Glükseeligkeit = happiness; sittlichkeit = morality.\n- Anbetung = ‘worship’; ‘herholen’ = ‘fetch’.",[]],"reflm:4244":["On the necessary being, the original being, the highest and first of all beings.\nEvery thing is in itself thoroughgoingly determined, but not every concept is thoroughgoingly determined. In order, therefore, to cognize a thing wholly it is not enough to cognize what it is, but also what it is not; for if this were left out, then it is undetermined whether |\nbeyond what is cognized in it, more does not yet belong to it. Accordingly a thing’s possibility is thoroughgoingly determined only through the collective possibility of everything, and whoever wants to cognize something wholly must cognize everything. Now of all opposed predicates the negative ones are thinkable only through the *opposita realia* (what is removed there I can know only insofar as I know that which is supposed to be annulled). Accordingly the possibility of all things is given by their being contained in the sum total of infinite reality, and by limitation of the same. There is accordingly a general *principium reale* of all possibilities, and the possibilities are only positions and limitations that have occurred respectively to a certain concept in the *omnitudo realitatum*, which limitations, since they are thought only respectively and the *ens illimitatum* is here laid at the ground absolutely, so the *ens fundamentale* is not itself something merely possible, but what lies at the ground of everything possible; it is therefore posited absolutely, and in it all *respectus* are contained. Accordingly is [the] The actuality taken in general breaks off.\n(g The proposition: from nothing comes nothing, indicates that possibility cannot be thought simply by itself, but presupposes something that is [was]. But that we afterwards can think each thing as merely possible comes from this, that, once we have a determinate concept, we do not ask: whence?, but, in that we consider it as given, annul the thing itself, but yet not everything in general.)\nI assert: the proof of a single original being from the descent of all possibilities is the one that directs all others in human understanding and dwells in each. The old proposition: from nothing comes nothing, signifies nothing other than that the possibilities must lie in something and that things, without there being anything at which they can be thought through determinations, cannot take place at all. Even in the works of human beings all possibilities are merely modifications |\nof something that is, at least of our concepts given through something or other; and with each possibility we represent to ourselves the agreements with everything else, consequently a certain unity of the thinkable.\n===NOTES===\n- durchgängig = ‘thoroughgoing(ly)’ throughout; determiniert / bestimt / Bestimmungen = determined / determinations (bestimmen = determine); unbestimt = undetermined.\n- Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = ‘actuality’ (glossary); Realität/realitaet = reality; Inbegrif = sum total; Satz = proposition; Beweis = proof.\n- erkennen = cognize; kennen = know; Begrif = concept; Verstand = understanding; Einheit = unity.\n- ‘Demnach ist [die] Die Wirklichkeit überhaupt genommen bricht ab’: the sentence is broken off in the manuscript; ‘breaks off’ renders the editor’s remark as it stands in the text, and the bracketed ‘[die]’ is the editor’s deletion. Left as it stands.\n- ‘so ist das ens fundamentale …’: the German construction after ‘welche limitationes, da …’ is anacoluthic; kept so. ‘sich schlechthin gedenken lasse’ rendered ‘be thought simply by itself’ (doubtful nuance of ‘sich’).\n- ‘Abstammung’ = ‘descent’; ‘aufheben’ = ‘annul’; the (g … ) and | marks are the Academy Edition’s.",[]],"reflm:4245":["All things seem to lie in an infinity; for they cannot be undetermined with regard to anything possible, and, since *quoad praedicata affirmantia* they are a part of this infinite, but *quoad negantia* are limited: so (g they) are necessarily in a thoroughgoing relation, and the complete representation of every thing must express this determined or undetermined. Even with a number, e.g. 8, all the rest is excluded from the infinite multitude, notwithstanding that in 10 properly no other possibilities or realities are thought in addition, but only the same action is repeated.\nAll finite things are therefore in accordance with their possibility dependent on a being of all beings, which contains all reality and is independent of all others; for in their thoroughgoing determination they nevertheless relate themselves at every time to the highest reality, which is the [ground] original ground of their possibility.\n===NOTES===\n- Unendlichkeit = infinity; Menge = multitude; Zahl = number; Vorstellung = representation; Bestimmung = determination.\n- ‘bestimt oder unbestimt’ follows ‘diese’; ‘this determined or undetermined’ keeps the German’s unclear grammatical attachment.\n- ‘sie’ in ‘(g sie)’ is the Academy Edition’s insertion mark; ‘[Grund] Urgrund’ = a deleted ‘Grund’ before ‘Urgrund’ (‘original ground’).\n- Verhältnis = relation; ‘beziehen sich’ = ‘relate themselves’.",[]],"reflm:4246":["To things possibility belongs necessarily, thus the possibilities of things have no further ground; but a being is nevertheless necessary, through which possibilities are given. In relation to the same each possibility is only a restriction of the highest reality, and the thoroughgoing possibility is properly the relation to the highest reality in its determination. The original being itself cannot be regarded as possible, but it is the *principium* of possibility. Even the possibility of all fictitious beings rests merely on curtailments. The plurality of things rests on the manifoldness of the curtailments, and what they have that is different are limitations, |\nthus relations to an original being. Accordingly there are not many original beings. The necessity in the existence of this being requires no special proof. For that it is the ground of all possibility and that this is only a derivation from it signifies already as much as: nothing is possible without a *realissimum*; consequently this itself does not belong among the merely possible beings. For otherwise, if one wants to make the possibilities by the way of composition, then a being in which there are many realities is not more possible than that in which there are few, and in the most real of all as the most composite the greatest contingency is.\n===NOTES===\n- Three German words are kept apart: Beschränkung = ‘restriction’, Einschränkung = ‘curtailment’, Schranke = ‘limitation’ (glossary); *limitatio* is not rendered in this passage.\n- Vielheit = ‘plurality’; Menge (not used here) = multitude; manigfaltigkeit = ‘manifoldness’.\n- Urwesen = ‘original being’; Daseyn = ‘existence’; Beweis = proof; bedeutet = ‘signifies’; besondern = ‘special’.\n- ‘das verhaltnis zur hochsten realitaet in seiner Bestimung’: ‘its/his’ has no clear antecedent; ‘its’ is unmarked in the English ‘in its determination’ is not used—‘in his’ avoided; text rendered ‘in its determination’ ambiguously as the German ‘seiner’.\n- ‘in dem allerrealesten’ = ‘in the most real of all’; ‘Möglicher’ = ‘more possible’.",[]],"reflm:4247":["Possibility consists precisely in the actuality of reality, in which through restrictions all things are given; e.g. a triangle is possible through the actuality of space, and [it is] its possibility consists in this, that through restrictions it lies in this space. Thus possibility is therefore a derived concept, not of the actuality of this thing, but of [any] the actuality which contains the first *data* for things in general. In all restricted things possibility (their concept) precedes actuality, because the reality given for things precedes the restriction of the same. But not with the original being.\n*conceptus terminator*.\n===NOTES===\n- Wirklichkeit = ‘actuality’; realitaet = ‘reality’; Beschränkung = ‘restriction’; Raum = space; Begrif = concept; Urwesen = original being.\n- ‘[it is]’ and ‘[any]’ are the Academy Edition’s bracketed deletions, kept in place.\n- ‘Die Moglichkeit besteht eben in der wirklichkeit der realitaet’: the genitive chain is kept as in the German.\n- ‘conceptus terminator’ stands alone as the final line in Latin, as in the source; no English added.",[]],"reflm:4248":["The highest reality cannot be restricted, because [it] would otherwise cease to be the ground of the possibility of all things. Just as space cannot actually be restricted, but the things in space.\nWe therefore cognize the original being and its necessity not from itself, i.e. from the concepts that we have of the inner predicates of a being, in which existence would be included, but relatively from its relation to our concepts of the possibility of all things; it is a boundary concept *a priori*. We assume it without contradiction, we need it for the completion of our reason, we can make no well-grounded objections against it.",[]],"reflm:4249":["The difference of things rests, according to our concepts, on the restrictions of given realities. But it cannot be regarded as the restrictions of different things, because this difference in turn presupposes restrictions; consequently it arises from the restriction of the thing or of its sequels, [through in] whose concept all reality contains. For the realities must be given, because without this one could not think them. The restriction is either of abstraction or of determination.\nThe inferences from this are given from the possibility of cognitions through our reason, not from the matters, consequently the *argument ad homines* holds.",[]],"reflm:4250":["This proof from mere concepts is not complete without confirmation through the empirical proof that the principles of reason agree with experience, although they are not borrowed from it. Conversely an empirical *argument* without this proof of reason is not sufficient for a determinate concept of the original being.",[]],"reflm:4251":["If an object interests us in acting, then [that constitution] that judgment of it is what we would pass on it according to the natural laws of our reason, [whether] although the conditions of our judgment [nothing] contain no data of it, nevertheless practically certain (e.g. of God and the other world) (g i.e. according to rules of prudence and morality).\n(Morally certain is a mere probability, if it concerns an obligation.)",[]],"reflm:4252":["The restricted is regarded as contingent for this reason, that the opposite reality is necessarily presupposed with every possible restriction.",[]],"reflm:4253":["a. The proof: 1. that all possibility must be regarded as a determination of the highest reality and presupposes such a being, is grounded on this, because the *omnimoda determinatio* is possible only through the *omnitudinem* [of the prae] *metaphysicam*, but in this the negative predicates presuppose the *realia*; and since every *ens limitatum* is possible only through presupposition of a *reale*, everything presupposes a *realissimum*; but this is the necessary subordination of the concepts according to the law of the human understanding.\n2. The absolute necessity of a thing is a boundary concept. It cannot be seen through contradiction. That which contains the matter for all possibility is the real ground of it and [its opposite] *realiter* necessary.\nIn itself the necessity of a perfect being is not to be seen better than that of an imperfect one.\nb. The proof *a contingentia* [presupposes] infers from concepts: that namely the restricted cannot be necessary. Consequently it must |\npresuppose the necessity in the *realissimum*. The cause is this, because the general concept of a being that has limitations comprehends many kinds of things under itself; but the unlimited is *conceptus singularis*./\nc. The physico-theological infers from the incomplete acquaintance with the perfection of the world to the highest perfection of God and from there back to the greatest perfection of the world.\nd. The moral from the geometrical, but objective necessity of actions under the moral laws, but also to the necessary assumption of a ruler.\nTo [incomplete] negative proofs, namely that the world is not from itself, belongs [that] that from the *primo motore*.\nThat the *ens realissimum* or *summum* has understanding.",[]],"reflm:4254":["327. E II 1654. 1157.\nM 327':\nThe existence of God can be cognized only through the understanding, because its concept is a highest concept of understanding, [that no relation to] not restricted to objects of experience, but whose *omnisufficientia* is intellectual.\nBut it cannot be cognized from an assumed arbitrary concept, and thus not in itself (unless through immediate intuitions).\nThus only in relation to the world, but that which is valid for every world; for this is only intellectual.\nThe intelligible world is: |\nM 327: |\nwhose concept holds for every world; consequently it contains not physical laws, but objective and moral ones.\nM 327':\nThe intellectual concept of the world is therefore the concept of perfection. The world of understanding is therefore the moral one, and the laws of it hold for every world as objective laws of perfection.\nThus one can infer only from the necessity of the moral laws in this world as in every one to the original and universally valid ground of the essential ends of things, hence to the existence of the most perfect being, and its concept is that which makes us perfect, and therefore practically certain. *Mundus intelligibilis* is the world of rational beings, considered according to objective laws of freedom.\nThe highest *principium* of morality is the agreement of free actions with the original and universally valid laws of the power of choice or with that, |\nM 327: |\nwhich is essentially combined with the ends in the world. Thus the concept of God breaks off?",[]],"reflm:4255":["The proposition of the existence of God is a *hypothesis originaria* (g *tam naturaliter qvam obiective*) both of the comprehensibility, the possibility and actuality of things through the understanding (*theoretica*) and as a *principium* of actions (*practica*). The proof proceeds through a *dilemma theoreticum et practicum*.",[]],"reflm:4256":["M 327':\nIf I deny the existence of God, then I must regard myself either as a fool, if I want to be (or am) an honest man, or as a scoundrel, if I want to be a prudent man. There are proofs *per deductionem contrarii ad absurditatem* or *turpitudinem*. Moral faith presupposes that the necessitating breaks off.\nM 327:\n(s If one denies God, then the virtuous man is a fool and the prudent man a rogue. Prudence and morality can be combined in the practical only if I assume the existence of God.\nWithout this proposition the [laws] moral laws of the *mundi intelligibilis*, which nevertheless command universally, would not hold for the present one and what is universally true would be false in the particular case.)\nM 327':\n(g There are proofs *per deductionem contrarii ad absurdum logicum* and also such *per deductionem ad absurdum morale*: that he who [denies] does not assume it is a [scoundrel] (g fool, visionary,) deceiver; both are\n|\nM 327: |\nnot genetic, but proofs of the necessity of assuming something.)",[]],"reflm:4257":["All negations are limitations; (g for they can be thought only through the *oppositum reale*, just as every line, figure and body is thought only through restriction of space. But why not the numbers. Because the reality for them is regarded not as given, but as arbitrarily invented.) everything restricted is thought as circumscribed in the greater, hence everything restricted in an other. That which is distinguished from everything restricted is unrestricted; everything restricted therefore in the infinite, which is therefore really infinite (without end) and all-sufficient.",[]],"reflm:4258":["Because [we] the [unbe], what we can limit, does not have to be necessary in itself, so the infinity, which as it were contains the |\nmatter for all possibility, is itself given through a higher ground. The *ens summum* is therefore *realissimum* as a ground.",[]],"reflm:4259":["The possibilities seem on the whole to signify nothing other than the relation through the limitations in the given infinite as the matter.",[]],"reflm:4260":["Because all difference of things is only a manifoldness of limitations, on account of the relation that every possible has to all reality, the concept of a highest reality, which is one, lies at the ground of everything.",[]],"reflm:4261":["One can want to prove the existence of God either as a necessary *hypothesis* for the cognition of the existence of other things or as a *dogma a priori*. The latter is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4262":["The cause on account of which we represent to ourselves the (g necessary) existence of a [necessary] most real being as more comprehensible than the necessity of a restricted one, comes from this, because we can think only the restricted things and their negative determinations, in that we limit the more real concept. Accordingly a greater possibility cannot be composed of smaller ones. And the general possibility is no aggregate of all particular possibilities, but these are sequels of that. The restricted thing is that which has some of |\nall possible reality and is thereby distinguished from others. But something distinguishing from everything possible is thinkable only through everything. Duration and the outer relation (g or figure and magnitude) can be cognized only through limiting of infinite time and of infinite space. e.g. triangle.",[]],"reflm:4263":["In order for an *ens realissimum* to be possible, it is not enough that the realities not contradict one another, but that among all possible subjects there be one, which unites them all as a common ground.",[]],"reflm:4264":["All predicates of God concern his existence or his properties. The first are possibility, *omnitudo*, necessity, eternity, independence (relat:), all-sufficiency, omnipresence etc etc. The second: his omnipotence, understanding, will; the almighty, living God. To the understanding belongs: 1. that he is the *prototypon*, that he is independent; 2. that he goes originally to everything.",[]],"reflm:4265":["If from mere concepts an existence is to be inferred. then the necessity of existence must be contained in the mere possibility.",[]],"reflm:4266":[") M 332'. E II 1664.\nIf from the properties of a thing its necessary existence is to be inferred, then either existence itself must be considered as |\na property, or the concept of absolute necessity as one that is the same as the given properties.",[]],"reflm:4267":["The physicotheological concepts of God have only an idea, related to us, of the magnitude of God, which suffices practically, to be sure, but not theoretically. God is wise, but we compare him only through our understanding with our own. God is great: signifies just as much as: how small we are! The absolute concept of d breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4268":["(g we are to infer from the concept of God to the necessity of another world, thus it must not be inferred from this one, because it does not agree entirely with him.)\nThe physicotheological proof gives only an opinion of the existence of God, namely of the existence of an intelligent author a certainty, and yet a belief must be added to it, in order to represent him as most highly perfect. One does not infer from experience to a most perfect world (rather one assumes it to be very imperfect, in order to prove the future world), but only to an intelligent one; of him one believes that whoever can do so much will be able to do everything.\nThe transcendental proof also can be carried out only respectively upon our *hypothesin subiectivam* of reason. Why should the practical one not be able to be carried out from the subjective *hypothesi* of the moral laws, since otherwise *leges morales effectu vacuae* would be, and the virtuous one a fantast, i.e. would not harmonize with this world.",[]],"reflm:4269":["Eternity (infinite duration) as a *phaenomenon* is infinite time. [das Daseyn in] Time is the measure of the duration |\nof finite things, insofar as they appear. Existence in time is the contingency in the appearance [denn das nichtseyn und seine]. The existence of that which is eternal is not infinitely limited by non-being. It is like infinite time itself; but contingent existence is shortened in the following point of time by the previous one and [blos se] changes its place in time more than that it enlarges the duration in the same. (s than only in the foresight. That which one has lived has not enlarged our existence, except only through the present sequels.) It is always the mere boundary of time, which is limited through infinite negation of the past and future.",[]],"reflm:4270":["God determines each thing's cognition from (g and in) the cognition of the whole; thus it is intuitive and an idea. We collect it from part-representations; [dort] the whole goes *originarie* according to possibility before the part.",[]],"reflm:4271":["The world represented sensibly has no first, i.e. no beginning, but certainly through reason; i.e. no first of time, but certainly a first of cause. Both have nothing in common with one another.\nJust so the world, represented sensibly according to magnitude, has no boundaries through empty space, but certainly limitations of reality.",[]],"reflm:4272":["To *auctoribus coordinatis* a *beneficium divisum* is imputed; to *auctoribus plene subordinatis* the same is imputed *pro indiviso* to the *auctori primario*. Hence I cannot impute my happiness to the creature and to God in different parts, but I can directly ascribe it to nature, to my own diligence, and thus to divine providence by means of the order of nature. It is also irreverent to regard God as a mere *causam concurrentem*; he is *solitaria*, but [*per remot*] *superior*.",[]],"reflm:4273":["One represents to oneself in every judgment an analytic unity. Among the parts of a whole a synthetic unity. But this analytic unity one does not always cognize *per analysin*.\nWhere this unity is not conceived in identity, there not a judgment, but a comparison of two judgments through a common mark is made. e.g. The human being is [vernunf] an animal, the horse is an animal.",[]],"reflm:4274":["The disjunctive propositions are comparison of two propositions with one subject and two opposed predicates. If [auch] the subjects are different, while the predicates are opposed, then they are distinguishing propositions. Comparison propositions have two sorts of subject and one predicate.",[]],"reflm:4275":["Analysis of reason. *principium contradictionis, identitatis*; gives objectively valid propositions.\nSynthesis of reason: different laws (*axiomata subreptitia*), subjectively valid propositions.\nThe conditions of our reason, which 1. cognizes objects only mediately and not through intuition, thus the *conditiones*, by means of |\nwhich a cognition of something is possible to it, and the necessity of positing something *primitive* and without *conditiones*.\nIntuitions of the senses (g according to sensible form and matter) give synthetic propositions, which are objective. Crusius explains the real principles of reason [vor] according to the *systema praeformationis* (g from subjective *principiis*), Locke according to the *influxu physico* like Aristotle, Plato and Malebranche from the *intuitu intellectuali*, we according to the *epigenesis* from the use of the natural laws of reason.\n(g The common human understanding, *sensus veri et falsi*, is *Qvalitas occulta*.)\nAntithesis: A method of reason for discovering the *oppositionem* of the subjective laws, which, if it is held *per vitium subreptionis* for objective, is *scepticismus* (*in sensu obiectivo*); but if it is only a [critic des subiects] *propaedeutic*, then it is the *methodus sceptica* for the determination of the subjective laws of reason. *Antithesis subiectiva*.",[]],"reflm:4276":["Pure sensibility. Pure reason. Mixed.\n*ubi, quando.*\n*transscendentalis philosophia.*\nAesthetic is the philosophy of sensibility, either of cognition or of feeling.\n*Logica vel generalis vel* [tran] *speciatim transscendentalis.*\nCategories are the universal actions of reason, through which we think an object in general (for the representations, appearances). Aristotle.\n*Praedicamente* are *modi* of the categories.\n3. Categories. Thesis, synthesis,\nhypothesis.*\n*coordinatio subordinatio*\n(g as well in mere categorical judgments as in all three kinds of judgments)\n*Praedicamenta.*\nThesis: *Possibile, actuale, necessarium cum oppositis.*\n—\n—\nsynthesis: *Qvantitas.*\n10 categories of Aristotle. Pythagoras. 1. *Substantia, Accidens,* 2. *qvalitas,* 3. *qvantitas,* 4. *relatio,* 5. *actio,* 6. *passio,* 7. *qvando,* 8. *ubi,* 9. *situs,* 10. *habitus.*\n*postpraedicamenta: oppositum, prius, simul, motus, habere.*\n*(g The actions of the understanding are either with regard to concepts, from wherever they may also be given, in relation to one another through the understanding, even if the concepts and the ground of their comparison are given through sense;\nor with regard to things, where the [Gegen] understanding thinks an object in general and the way of positing something in general and its relations. Both are distinguished only in this, that in the first the representations, in the others through the representations the things are posited.)",[]],"reflm:4277":["In this world I can become happy only according to the laws of sensibility, and accordingly I depend on the mechanical laws and the [thieris] sensible choice of human beings. (The latter could be unanimous, and then I would also be happy according to merit.)",[]],"reflm:4278":["Reason is bound to the law of thinking everything mediately: *regrediendo*, and yet in the *progressu* it must think something *originarie*.",[]],"reflm:4279":["All propositions predicate *per thesin* (g actually, possibly, necessarily) or *per synthesin* or *analysin*.\nThetic propositions have no predicate and can be cognized only in intuitions. or express the immediate [Vorste] intuition of a thing.",[]],"reflm:4280":["All particular propositions are synthetic, but not all universal ones are therefore analytic.\nIf a particular proposition is analytic, then the particularity is an empty *limitation*.",[]],"reflm:4281":["The *limitation* differs from the *restriction* in this, that the former concerns merely the universality, the latter the necessity of the condition. Reason can limit only through *restrictiones*.",[]],"reflm:4282":["On the *synthesi*; that there are no (g free) hypotheses in metaphysics.\nOn the method, one must not doubt any more what one has once had to admit. In metaphysics one must certainly doubt, if difficulties show themselves. The spirit of the whole is distinguished from the spirit of trifles. subtle and great.",[]],"reflm:4283":["Concerning the principle ask: 1. Is it synthetic or analytic. If it is now the first:\n2. Is it borrowed from general experience or *a priori*.\n3. Is it a principle of sensibility or of reason?\n4. Is it a ground of the possibility of appearances or of real concepts, *principium constitutivum*?\n5. If it is the last: is it a *principium subiectivum* or *obiectivum*?\n6. If it is the first, then it is asked whether a *principium convenientiae* or a *principium concipiendi per se*.",[]],"reflm:4284":["Metaphysics is not science, not erudition, but merely the understanding that knows itself, hence it is merely a rectification of healthy understanding and reason.\nErudition and wide reading is a means of making its doctrines practical through examples. [The Wi other Wi] It serves other sciences for the demarcation of boundaries and holds the human being to his vocation, as concerns the use and the limitations of his reason; it is the logical self-cognition. It is strangely bitter, because it strikes down vain pride and takes away imagined knowledge; it makes our possessions more secure, but to the detriment of the imagined ones, and hinders books from swelling up.\nThe greatest gain is made from it by religion; through it religion is secured in everything that is moral in religion, covered against enthusiasm and unbelief, freed from dependence with regard to scholastic subtlety. It makes it so that our actions can flow from the\n|\nsources of healthy understanding, without having to consult the uncertain and always changeable scholastic brooding.",[]],"reflm:4285":["An object is called only what has a logical quality. e.g. *subiectum* not relatively to other concepts, but in itself. These objects are distinguished from appearances, which can well be brought into logical relations, [sonde] but to which this logical quality does not belong absolutely. If a logical *position* parallels an appearance, which goes to the data of the appearance and not to the relation of one representation to another, then this is objective. e.g. ground, cause.\nOnly through this can we conceive appearances as corresponding to the things, that they be grounds of a possible and universally valid cognition. They can be so, however, if they are in accordance with the logical form.",[]],"reflm:4286":["1. What an object may be?\n2. In what the relation of a determination of the soul to something else consists?\n3. What the ground of the agreement is?\n4. Through what we distinguish what relates to the object and to the subject?",[]],"reflm:4287":["Logical is what consists in comparison; real, what is posited in itself.",[]],"reflm:4288":["Possibility, actuality, and from both necessity are not concepts of objects nor predicates, but placements of the object.\n1. On the power of fiction. The object of fiction is possible. The fiction that cancels itself is no fiction. To every feigning belongs that something be given. 2. The object of sense is actual. 3. The positing through reason is necessary. Hence the possibility of the sensible is thought in space and time. Numbers are the purest fictions, and their possibility is therefore entirely without data possible through understanding, but not the possibility of the qualities, except only through composing and canceling.",[]],"reflm:4289":["On the method of appealing, for the certainty of the metaphysical axioms, 1, to the constant presupposition in all empirical cases and of inferring from it to their abstract generality.\n2. Because with just the same right with which one would deny them, one would have to let the certainty of all remaining cognition vanish.",[]],"reflm:4290":["Whether one cannot, through reason, according to analogy, feign something. *Theologia Naturalis*, because all realities do not contradict one another.\nIt is the question why magnitudes are the only thing of which the |\nhuman understanding makes concepts out of itself, whose possibilities it sees into for itself and in their relations can arrive, through the proposition of contradiction, at certainty and at infinite expansion.",[]],"reflm:4291":["Metaphysics is not the mother of religion, but its bulwark (g against false sophistry). It proves the existence of God not *apodictice*, but *suppletorie*, in that it replaces through reason the lack of morality in unbelief.",[]],"reflm:4292":["The *conditiones* without which the objects cannot be given are objective, although according to laws of sensibility. The *conditiones* without which they (even if they have been given) cannot be cognized (understood) are objective. Those without which they cannot be seen into (cognized through reason)* are merely subjective; but these subjective *conditiones* are objective with regard to the use of reason in experiences [cond] (*leges convenientiae*).\n*(s possibility, necessity)",[]],"reflm:4293":["Philosophy has this peculiarity, that the general thoughts can be set down first, in mathematics, on the contrary, last. Hence in meditation the general *proiet* comes first.",[]],"reflm:4294":["All sciences and arts teach skill, philo|sophy\nalone the principles and rules of the use of all skill according to the ultimate relations of the understanding and of the will, in that it establishes the object itself and its relation to the human being.",[]],"reflm:4295":["How the predicate lies in the subject belongs to metaphysics; how the [unt] subject stands under it, to logic.",[]],"reflm:4296":["5. E II 1055.\nM 5':\nThe proposition: everything has a ground, is a law of reason; but the law: everything that happens is constantly accompanied with something else, is a |\nM 5: |\nlaw of appearances.",[]],"reflm:4297":["Over and \nThe outer possibility is greater than the inner.\nThe hypothetical possibility is smaller than the unconditioned (absolutely, in general, in every aim).\nWhat is possible in every aim is actual.",[]],"reflm:4298":["Possibility: the agreement (*non repugnantia*) with a rule, actuality: the *position* simply, necessity: the *position* according to a rule.\nThe first is thought without being given. The second given, without its being thought. The third given through its being thought.",[]],"reflm:4299":["The relation (of an object) to perception (*perceptio*) is existence; to thinking: possibility; to thinking, insofar as it determines existence: necessity.",[]],"reflm:4300":["A and not-A are *contradictorie opposita* (contradictorily opposed); A and B *disparata*. Between *contradictorie opposita* no third is given (which is neither A nor not-A.)",[]],"reflm:4301":["Reality (*realitas*) is the matter of cognition (*materia cognitionis*)*, whether it be used affirmatively or negatively.\n*(g are *constitutiva* of things, negations only of concepts.)\n(s Does the concept of the simple designate reality or negation?)",[]],"reflm:4302":["Over and \nThe object of the concept is possible, hence through this concept, because it is general, not thoroughly determined. The object of intuition is actual; the object of the intuition, which is the ground of the concepts of the same in general, is necessary.\n(g Intuition itself is no object. Something can be a logical or a real object.)",[]],"reflm:4303":["On M § 54:\nThe *principium contradictionis* is the formal condition, but not *principium* of all possibility.",[]],"reflm:4304":["Because without concept we can think nothing, every object that we are to think must have a relation of agreement to the concept in general, i.e. be possible.\nAn object, insofar as it is given, is thoroughly determined, but not insofar as it is thought.",[]],"reflm:4305":["Mutability (*mutabilitas*) presupposes the existence of things in time, thus also immutability (*immutabilitas*). Hence, because absolute necessity is a concept of reason, *ens necessarium* is neither *mutabile* nor *immutabile*. Thus *mutabilitas* or its *oppositum* belongs only to *sensualibus*.",[]],"reflm:4306":["The inference from changeability to contingency is *metabasis eis allo genos* (a transition into another genus), for I infer from a *sensitivum* to the *intellectuale*.",[]],"reflm:4307":["In a stream without banks we can notice no change of our place nor of the place of the water itself.",[]],"reflm:4308":["First the contingency of the state is not to be confused with the contingency of the thing. The thing remains.\nSecond the changeability of the state not with the contingency of the state.\nThird changeability is a proof of a sensible contingency of the state.",[]],"reflm:4309":["In the margin above \nIt seems that the whole play of changes stems from real oppositions. Since a reality drives the thing out of another state, no motion can be generated without bringing forth just as much [in] opposed motion; thus there are two forces that effect the continuous change of direction.",[]],"reflm:4310":["Only one being is metaphysically perfect, all others have perfections, but are not perfect. The totality is lacking.\nOne does see that realities are considered as grounds for perfection and negations as deficiencies, not merely as not-being, but deficiencies in the totality.",[]],"reflm:4311":["What is opposed to totality is *limitation*.\nWhat is opposed to generality is *restriction*.",[]],"reflm:4312":["*Totum reale* (real whole): in which there is *nexus*.*\n*Totum ideale* (ideal whole): in which there is *coniunctio*.\n*(g this, if it is possible through the addition of parts, is a *compositum*.)",[]],"reflm:4313":["The existence of a thing, insofar as it is determined in time, is its state; the state is either of coexisting or of successive determinations. An original being is in no state.",[]],"reflm:4314":["The whole belongs to magnitude, namely to the *omnitudini*. The *compositum* (composite) belongs to relation and indeed to that of *reciprocae coordinationis*. What is possible through the *coniunctionem* (*mutuam*). A *quantum* is in itself no *compositum*, but its concept can arise *per compositionem idealem*. Hence we speak of *compositis realibus*. The *compositum ex accidentibus* is collective, *ex substantiis* is aggregate.",[]],"reflm:4315":["One can think spaces only insofar as one cuts something out of the universal space. Space precedes things; |\ntherefore it can be no *praedicat* (predicate) of things, but only a law of sensibility, which as the condition of all possible appearances of course precedes all actual ones.",[]],"reflm:4316":["To substance does not pertain the predicate of space and of place, but of the sensible representation. Corporeal parts are comparative substances according to laws of sensibility and *phaenomena substantiata*. The quantity of substance in a body (not of its space) is to be judged from the magnitude of the effect under the same speed. With regard to this nothing is simple. Now the question arises whether with regard to space there is something simple and a determinate alleged number.",[]],"reflm:4317":["All axioms in which the subject has no content of sensibility and which nevertheless restrict it universally through the condition of sensibility are false. They are reduced when [to it] to the subject the condition of sensibility is given back.",[]],"reflm:4318":["The all-concept of time is no universality.",[]],"reflm:4319":["On infinite space and time. Time contains the ground of |\nthe limitations of the existence of things, space of the limitations of their presence, the highest being of the limitations of their reality. Hence all of this is infinite. Here the highest being is thought only sensibly, as it were as the matter for all things.\nTime is what encompasses the existence of all things, in which all things exist, whereby therefore to each its existence is determined relatively to others, when and how long.",[]],"reflm:4320":["Whether space and time empty of sensation, likewise infinite space and time, are *phaenomena*. For *noumena* they are not.",[]],"reflm:4321":["Time is the condition of infinite *apposition*, space of infinite *aggregation*. Both cannot be united, because aggregation, i.e. totality, does not take place with an apposition.\nHere the subjective conditions do not agree with one another.",[]],"reflm:4322":["75. E II 621.\nM 75':\nThe boundaries [find] (g goal, end) are negations which exclude the greater possible addition [end]. The limitations are negations which [the grö the] exclude everything from the given greatest [d eini the remainder] except some. Number is a multitude which has boundaries; a determinate magnitude in space and time arises from the boundaries of infinity. The former is always a whole number, the latter a fraction. In the former diminutions are not thought, the latter is possible only thereby. The definitions arise analytically through the limitations of still greater concepts and are set forth synthetically through the |\nboundaries of the composition of the marks. |\nM 75: |\nThe definition is like a geometrical figure, which encloses a space through the number and magnitude of the sides and through the way of composition of them. The space is the *definitum*. If the figure does not close, then the definition is *latior suo definito*.\nThe lack of enlargement is the boundary (is opposed to a possible greater).\nThe lack from diminution is the limitation (is opposed to an actual greater).\nThe all [the multitude (determinate)]. Some [show] are, others not: indicates limitations.",[]],"reflm:4323":["All alteration is a succession of states; that of the inner state is an alteration of the thing, that of the outer an altered relation.",[]],"reflm:4324":["On the boundaries of a world and of its state according to laws of sensibility and reason.",[]],"reflm:4325":["The connection of a thing with that which is not its cause, hence which is not a subordination, is always a connection of one part with the other into a whole. Accordingly the whole, which itself is no part, is connected with no thing as with its cause, which is therefore outside it; also with no finite being, because this, |\nsince it is not grounded in itself, must again have the ground of its connection in another.",[]],"reflm:4326":["Under and \nThe alterations constitute not parts of the world, but of its state.",[]],"reflm:4327":["Next to the end of M § 358 and the beginning of M § 359:\nThat not merely *multitudo* is a world. That *finitudo* is a sequel of *compositio*.",[]],"reflm:4328":["At the bottom margin \nThe composition proves the contingency (g and dependence), for necessary beings cannot be composed.\nThe totality proves the dependence on One.\nThe composition also proves the *finitudinem*.",[]],"reflm:4329":["The world is finite, because it is a *totum* of beings that limit one another.",[]],"reflm:4330":["The world of sense is only a single one.\nEvery *totum* is *contingens* with regard to the parts.",[]],"reflm:4331":["In time, i.e. the sensible series or the series of the understanding, a first signifies, even if the series were infinite.",[]],"reflm:4332":["Infinity is a concept with regard to sensibility; it is indeed for the understanding, but not for reason. The world (g of the senses) is necessarily infinite, because the senses borrow from themselves no *termini*, except those which consist precisely in this, that the mind is affected by others.",[]],"reflm:4333":["The will of man is free, signifies as much as: reason has a faculty over the will and the other faculties and inclinations. For reason determines itself, and without this all other faculties are determined according to the law of efficient causes and are externally necessary. Reason cannot be determined, i.e. affected; for then it would be sensibility and not reason.",[]],"reflm:4334":["On freedom.\nWe can consider an action of ours either as something |\nthat happens, i.e. as appearance, or as something that ought to happen, i.e. as an intuition of self-activity toward possible effects. In the first case it is [an] subjected to the laws of determining grounds; in the second case it is an intellectual one, in which the subject is not passive; here the *regula rationis determinantis* rules only as a *principium* [cognosc] *comprehensionis* or *logicum*, i.e. that one cannot cognize it *a priori* (except with the perfect understanding) otherwise than through determining grounds. The good understanding has determining grounds, but according to laws of the understanding through intellectual *motiva* (motives); the affected one abandons itself to sensibility.\nSomething happens necessarily, i.e. according to the empirical concept of necessity, which nevertheless admits a contingency according to concepts of reason. The understanding must itself excite sensibility, so that it may determine the action; thus it happens according to laws of sensibility and yet of the understanding. We also know only the nearest determining grounds of sensibility, and according to these the action must always be able to be explained.",[]],"reflm:4335":["It is true: all morality must aim at something useful. But not the utility, rather: the universality of it is what makes it morally good, namely that as a rule alone it is good and without it no universal rule would find place. Even with duties of obligation the action, even if it were harmful, is not to be omitted, and in this consists precisely the obligation.",[]],"reflm:4336":["The actuality of freedom we cannot infer from experience. But we nevertheless have a concept of it only through our intellectual inner intuiting (not the inner sense) of our activity, which can be moved by *motiva intellectualia*, and through which |\npractical laws and rules of the good will itself with regard to ourselves are possible. Thus freedom is a necessary practical presupposition. It also does not contradict theoretical reason. For as appearances [one may the] the actions are always in the field of experience, as objective data they are in the field of reason and are approved and disapproved. Sensibility is here under the laws of the understanding and gives way breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4337":["Contingently arbitrary actions (freedom of men) are those that are determined by no rules. Necessarily arbitrary actions (divine freedom) are those that are determined only according to the rule of good choice.",[]],"reflm:4338":["The freedom of our power of choice from all outer necessitation is clear through experience, likewise the moving force of the intellectual grounds of the good; we can on that account shift the guilt onto no other beings. We can ascribe it to ourselves, even the good that God works in us. Thus morality and religion are *in salvo*.\nBut how does it stand with speculative philosophy concerning the possibility of this freedom? The proposition: everything that happens has a determining ground, i.e. something else through which it is necessitated, is the principle of the alterations of all passive substances (g of all appearances or of that which is given a posteriori; but the actions, to give something a priori, are not comprehended among them.), [but] as the body, also the soul, insofar as it is modified, i.e. in everything that is distinguished from the actions of freedom. Insofar this principle is objective. But as a *principium* of activities it cannot be objective, for there a first beginning must be possible; but in the states of a being as a passive one there is no first (This lies in |\nthe active). Freedom is supposed to be a faculty of beginning a state first. Passive states are mere sequels and belong necessarily to the preceding. I can say in the present moment: for me the whole series up to now is as nothing. I now begin my state as I will. It is thus [only] the difficulty (g not) *secundum* [rationes] *possibilitatem fiendi*, but *cognoscendi*. One cannot have insight into the possibility [of a] of freedom, because one cannot have insight into a first beginning, neither the necessity in existence in general, nor freedom in arising. For our understanding cognizes existence through experience, but reason has insight into it when it cognizes it a priori, i.e. through grounds (that, namely, which is not necessary according to identity, but which is posited *realiter*); now of the first there are no grounds, thus no insight through reason is possible either. That there must be a first (g *natura*) action, which lies at the ground of everything contingent, lies well in reason; but a first *tempore* is not at all to be comprehended, because time itself and what is in it does not depend on reason. Further: that what is determined in its whole existence by another being must end in itself the determining ground of its actions, is not to be comprehended; but only for this reason not, because it is not to be comprehended how it is a substance. Now this is no objection, but a subjective difficulty; for the I proves the end point of the grounds of the actions. I do this [or shows not] does not mean: another works this; and even if I say: I suffer this, it nevertheless signifies the intuition [of my sel] of a subject, [which] what is for itself and suffers.\n===NOTES===\n- Willkühr = power of choice (not in the glossary); Nothigung = necessitation; necessitirt left as 'necessitated'.\n- leidend = passive, leiden = suffer; wirken = work (not in glossary); Folge = sequel; einsehen/Einsicht = have insight into/insight; erkennen = cognize; Daseyn = existence.\n- 'als der Korper, auch der Seele' is elliptical; 'as the body, also the soul' keeps the ambiguity of 'als' (as / as of).\n- 'liegt wol in der Vernunft': 'wol' (wohl) is ambiguous (well / probably / indeed); rendered 'well'.\n- 'der Freyheit' follows the deleted '[eines]' and is carried over as '[of a] of freedom'; the bracketed words ([aber], [nur], [rationes], [oder zeigt nicht], [meiner sel], [was]) are the Academy's deletions, reproduced as they stand; the last two are broken off.\n- 'vor mich' = 'for me'; 'vor sich selbst' = 'for itself'. Latin technical terms left in Latin: *principium*, *secundum*, *possibilitatem fiendi*, *cognoscendi*, *realiter*, *tempore*, *natura*, *in salvo*.",[]],"reflm:4339":["*Philosophia applicata* (applied philosophy) is that whose object is represented through an empirical concept; but an empirical concept, insofar as through analysis it is the ground (*ratio*) of the cognition of the object, has no principles except rational ones.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin passage; *ratio* is left in Latin with 'ground' beside it (it could also be 'reason').\n- 'cognitionis obiecti' = 'of the cognition of the object'; Object/obiectum = 'object'.\n- 'nisi rationalia' rendered 'except rational ones' (principia is neuter plural).",[]],"reflm:4340":["All philosophy (g theoretical) is either empirical (g *Physiologia*, *Psychologia* or *Physica*) or rational (g *Philosophia transscendentalis*). Rational either pure or applied.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin passage with the Latin names of disciplines left in italics; 'g' is the Academy's sign for a later insertion.\n- The final sentence has no verb in the original; none is supplied.",[]],"reflm:4341":["All theoretical philosophy is either pure or applied. The former: whose objects are given only through concepts of the pure understanding (*intellectus puri*); applied: to which objects are given through the senses (*sensus*). Applied is *physiologia*, and this either empirical or rational, each either of inner or of outer sense.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin passage. 'conceptus intellectus puri' = concepts of the pure understanding; *intellectus* = understanding, *sensus* = sense.\n- 'utraqve' = 'each of the two'; the English 'each' follows it.\n- 'Prior' is rendered 'the former'; no noun is supplied.",[]],"reflm:4342":["1. (g transscendental:) That the principle of life cannot die. That life does not depend on matter, although the animal does.\n2. Psychological: That the soul already now works independently of the body.\n3. *theologia moralis*: Divine ends.\n4. Analogy.\n===NOTES===\n- 'although the animal does': the German has 'das thierische' (neuter adjective used as noun, 'the animal [life]'?); no noun supplied.\n- wirken = work; Zweke = ends; Korper = body.\n- 'g' is the Academy's sign for a later insertion.",[]],"reflm:4343":["The proof from analogy requires no experiences that we cannot have, and, in default of the same, no arbitrarily assumed forces.\nIn the analogical proof we must seek out all cases where [the] an incentive is in us that is entirely distinguished from the senses, and a force, indeed even calling, to make itself loose from it. Where life does not reward the effort or is too short for it, and where we are noble creatures and have insight into our own lowliness. Hence the human being has an ideal of perfection in himself that he does not attain.\nThis proof goes to our nature, hence to all; likewise, because it strikes the higher forces, to gradual natural spreading.\n===NOTES===\n- Triebfeder = incentive (not in glossary); Beruf = calling (distinguished from Bestimmung = vocation); Ausbreitung = spreading.\n- 'sich davon los zu machen': the antecedent of 'davon' (the senses / the incentive) is ambiguous; rendered 'from it'.\n- '[die]' is an Academy deletion, kept in brackets.\n- 'trift' rendered 'strikes' (could also be 'concerns').\n- Kraft = force; Vollkommenheit = perfection; einsehen = have insight into.",[]],"reflm:4344":["According to the laws according to which we, with healthy reason, have to infer the existence of certain things, their properties and laws in the world, [kan] it must necessarily be inferred to the existence of a highest, wise being, thus practically sufficient up to accountability. But as regards the condition of the comprehensibility of this highest cause, through which we [unab] want to prove the strength of our reason a priori, and thus according to logical rules of synthetic or dogmatic cognition, this use of reason is precarious. Now no human being can excuse himself that, because he was not able to have insight into something a priori, he also did not assume it, just as he [den Anfang d] also does not have insight into the attraction of gravity a priori and yet assumes it in his actions.\nDeism is that which recognizes no other theology than the transcendental. God as a spiritual being (g intelligence) in distinction from the eternal and necessary nature is called the living God.\n===NOTES===\n- schließen = infer (distinct from folgern = conclude); Eigenschaften = properties; hinreichend = sufficient; Verantwortung = accountability; mislich = precarious; Begreiflichkeit = comprehensibility.\n- erkennen = cognize, but 'recognizes' in 'keine andre theologie erkennt' (glossary allows both).\n- 'muß auf das Daseyn ... geschlossen werden' is impersonal; 'it must ... be inferred to' keeps the German construction.\n- '[kan]', '[unab]', '[den Anfang d]' are Academy deletions, the last two broken off.\n- 'Schwere' (gravity) with 'attraction' (German: attraction der Schwere).",[]],"reflm:4345":["The human being has a faculty of ascending in cognitions up to the Godhead signifies nothing more than: he has a faculty of completing his concepts and of bringing forth an idea of the *maximum*.\n===NOTES===\n- 'maximi' is genitive in the original; rendered 'of the *maximum*'.\n- The first clause stands as subject of 'bedeutet' with no quotation marks, as in the German.\n- Vermögen = faculty; Erkentnisse = cognitions; bedeuten = signify.",[]],"reflm:4346":["The understanding of the original being is *intellectus originarius*; consequently [has e] its understanding is the archetype of things, and it is the cause of things only by means of the understanding, not through mere nature.\n===NOTES===\n- Urwesen = original being; Urbild = archetype; vermittelst = by means of.\n- '[hat e]' is an Academy deletion, broken off.\n- *intellectus originarius* = original understanding.",[]],"reflm:4347":["Not concepts, but ideas (*archetypae*; ours: *ectypae*); i.e. we cognize all perfection only insofar as we represent it as in a being, which thereby becomes the highest idea. In it everything that nature contains becomes a part of the perfection of the whole. The most perfect is only one. The manifoldnesses conceal from us the self-subsisting and original of the archetype. The abstract and general representation arises in this way, from which we leave out much and retain only marks. For us everything is apparence. Platonism. The divine understanding contains every perfection in the archetype, ours in rules.\nThe idea is the unity of cognition, from which the manifold either of cognition or of the object becomes possible. There the whole of cognition [or the] precedes the part, the general the particular; here cognition precedes the possibility of the |\nthings, as e.g. with order and perfection. Everything intelligible is at the same time incomprehensible.\nThat the original being has understanding. Inferred from all-sufficiency, because without this the possibility of every kind of relations, e.g. order [not], through which something in general can be an object of the understanding, would not be possible.\n* No discursive cognition. Intuition. Not sensation, not appearance.\nNo abstraction.\n*(g No sensibility. *materialiter*. None *formaliter*: confusion, obscurity.)\nOur idea is a discursive cognition. Hence it is only *idea informata*.\nThe intuiting is *archetypa*.\nOur cognition of the understanding is discursive. Through it no immediate representations of objects are given, but such are only brought under rules, or that which gives occasion to a rule.\n===NOTES===\n- 'apparentz' (Latinate, not Erscheinung) rendered 'apparence' to keep it distinct from 'appearance' (Erscheinung); 'Vor uns' rendered 'For us'.\n- Mannigfaltigkeiten = manifoldnesses (plural of the abstract noun); das Mannigfaltige = the manifold; Sachen rendered 'things' (Ding also 'thing').\n- 'Die anschauende' = 'the intuiting', no noun supplied; Anschauung = intuition (here lower-case 'intuition' in the German, kept as such).\n- Asterisks in the passage are Kant's own footnote signs, not italics; '[or the]' and '[not]' are Academy deletions.\n- *archetypae*, *ectypae*, *idea informata*, *archetypa*, *materialiter*, *formaliter* left in Latin; Allgnugsamkeit = all-sufficiency; Merkmal = mark.\n- Allgemeine = general.",[]],"reflm:4348":["Our understanding can cognize nothing concrete (it cognizes merely the way of sensibility to dispose according to rules) except that which is in it itself. *Now the good is that which relates necessarily to it itself or to the inner of analogous beings, thus it has cognitions of it a priori. But God cognizes objects in themselves a priori. Consequently they must be given through him, his understanding is therefore *archetypus*.\n*(g of which it itself is the free author, the understanding can cognize a priori; for only the cause through freedom is it through understanding.)\n===NOTES===\n- 'concret' is an adverb/adjective of uncertain force ('in concreto' / 'concrete'); rendered 'concrete' after 'nothing'.\n- 'Art der sinnlichkeit' = 'the way of sensibility'; the parenthesis is syntactically obscure and kept as is.\n- an sich selbst = in themselves; Urheber = author; *archetypus* left in Latin.\n- The asterisks are Kant's footnote signs; the second note's 'it' and 'is it' are as ambiguous as the German.\n- 'Mithin müßen sie' — the antecedent of 'sie' is left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:4349":["Cognition is either sensitive or intellectual; the objects ei|ther\nsensible or intelligible. It [there is for] can be given to us no other world than the sensible; thus every *mundus physicus* (g *materialiter*) is *sensibilis*; only the *Mundus moralis* (g *formaliter*) is *intelligibilis*. Therefore, because freedom is the only thing that is given a priori and consists in this giving a priori; the rule of freedom a priori in a world in general constitutes the *formam mundi intelligibilis*. This leads, according to [laws] grounds of freedom, to the *praesumtion* of the *intelligibilium*: God and a future world, in which everything (g nature) will be in accordance with the moral laws.\nThe *mundus intelligibilis* as an object of intuition is a mere (g indeterminate) idea; but as an object of the practical relation of our intelligence to intelligences of the world in general and God as the practical original being of the same it is a true concept and determinate idea: *Civitas dei*.\n===NOTES===\n- The word-break 'ent|weder' is reproduced as 'ei|ther'; the | and line break stand where they do in the German.\n- '[giebt vor]' and '[Gesetzen]' are Academy deletions; 'vor' rendered 'for'.\n- Latin kept in italics: *mundus physicus* (physical world), *sensibilis*, *Mundus moralis* (moral world), *intelligibilis*, *formam mundi intelligibilis* (accusative: the form of the intelligible world), *praesumtion* (the German form), *intelligibilium* (genitive plural: of intelligible things), *Civitas dei* (city of God).\n- 'Gegenstand der practischen Verhaltnis': the article 'der' does not agree with neuter 'Verhältnis'; left as in the German.\n- Gegenstand = object; Begrif = concept; bestimmte = determinate; unbestimmte = indeterminate; Anschauung = intuition; Urwesen = original being.",[]],"reflm:4350":["The opinion that we must regard all evils as punishments, and indeed, those that already befall us in childhood, as punishments for the sins of the first parents.\nIs God the [origin of] author of evil.",[]],"reflm:4351":["Pains are nothing evil, i.e. they can also subsist together with the highest good in the world without preceding sin. They are means of exercise of freedom and culture.",[]],"reflm:4352":["*inanimata dicuntur res creatae, animata creaturae* (inanimate things are called created things, animate ones creatures). A plant |\nis a divine work, a worm is a creature; for to bring forth life, hence something that is active out of itself, is *creatio per eminentiam* (creation by eminence).",[]],"reflm:4353":["We find in the instincts of animals proofs of divine wisdom, but not in the reason of human beings. Does that not show that our own reason, which is known to itself, but to which the animals are unknown, gives herein the rules of inferring.",[]],"reflm:4354":["On and \nEvery creature stands under the general and particular divine provision, but not every occurrence befalls it through particular direction, but here chance often rules. Namely the provision is *potentiale* direction or the supervision, in which all ends are taken into consideration. but God has not determined the fates of the human being through individual intentions, but left them to the order of nature. e.g. I have a fine stud: I do not arrange every mating, but leave it as I know that they will pair according to general order. For there is herein general provision, but not particular direction. For thereby the human being alone is put in the position to steer his fates himself according to general laws.",[]],"reflm:4355":["On and \nProvidence goes either to the particular through the general or to the general through the particular. The first is the systematic.",[]],"reflm:4356":["A general determination is a fate (e.g. natural death).\nA particular determination is a destiny.\nAn occurrence that is not determined beforehand at all is luck or ill luck.",[]],"reflm:4357":["On the diminution of a miracle through mere direction.\nOn the enthusiastic *concursus* (concurrence) to the holiness of the will; we must ourselves do good.\nGod and the physician. Ebb and flood. Preservation of realms. Life and death.",[]],"reflm:4358":["He who will not subject himself to the laws is a free spirit (libertin); he who will not subject himself to the statutes, a freethinker.",[]],"reflm:4359":["Through the concept of the first one can explain no science. Every whole lets itself be divided into two parts, of which the one is the first relative to the other, e.g. the first centuries. The absolutely first is only a single one, the relatively first is indeterminate.",[]],"reflm:4360":["*Metaphysica est logica intellectus puri* (metaphysics is the logic of the pure understanding), but to *philosophia pura* (pure philosophy) belongs also morals. Because all logic is dogmatic or critical, metaphysics is either dogmatic or critical. and this critique is not merely of propositions, but of human reason, consequently also of the contrary.",[]],"reflm:4361":["*divisio Philosophiae in theoreticam et practicam; theoretica vel ex principiis empiricis vel rationalibus, prior physiologia.* (Division of philosophy into theoretical and practical; the theoretical either from empirical or from rational principles, the former physiology.)",[]],"reflm:4362":["*Metaphysica est philosophia theoretica [pura] intellectualis (oppositum: empirica), et haec vel transscendentalis (g ens summum) vel applicata; posterior vel ad naturam sensus interni vel externi*∗ *vel naturam universam (g summum bonum).* (Metaphysics is intellectual theoretical philosophy [pure] (opposite: empirical), and this either transcendental (g highest being) or applied; the latter either to the nature of inner or outer sense or to universal nature (g highest good).)\n∗(g It is the question: what reason can cognize without all experience. (in mathematics much.) which the conditions, the objects and the boundaries are. Pure reason is dogmatic in morals.)",[]],"reflm:4363":["All our cognition can be distinguished, according to kind, into sensible cognition and cognition of reason.\nAccording to the relation (g of subordination): into *principia et principiata* (principles and what is derived from principles),\nOf coordination: as a part to the entire human cognition.",[]],"reflm:4364":["Theoretical philosophy is either [pura] *rationalis* or *empirica*. The latter is *psychologia* and *physica*. The *rationalis* [is] has either also no object at all from experience [or] and is called *transscendentalis*; or indeed from experience, but *principia* from reason, and is called *metaphysica* (*psychologia rationalis* and *physica rationalis*); finally it has an object of reason, insofar as it is cognized through concepts of experience *per analogiam* (by analogy).",[]],"reflm:4365":["Difference of cognition in the order: *a priori*. *a posteriori*.\n—\n—\n—\nin the kind: empirical or rational.\nThe former are general, the second individual.",[]],"reflm:4366":["Metaphysics is *philosophia pura*. The form of all cognition is merely rational, the matter sensitive; thus metaphysics is the philosophy about the form.",[]],"reflm:4367":["Analysis and *synthesis negativa* (negative synthesis) are very different. A *divisio logica* (logical division) is a *synthesis negativa*: I do not divide the concept, but divide it up (*repartior*).",[]],"reflm:4368":["Our material concepts can never go to anything other than what we have sensed; and thus also our material principles can only be regarded as laws of experience and never be more general. But if one extends the manner of judging up to the concepts of form, then from this there becomes more a law of subjective use.",[]],"reflm:4369":["It is the question whether metaphysics deals with the objects that can be cognized through pure reason, or with the subject, namely the principles and laws in the use of pure reason. Because |\nwe can cognize all objects only through our subject, chiefly those that do not affect us, it is subjective.",[]],"reflm:4370":["By what do I know that *canones* (canons) of reason are not *axiomata* (axioms), i.e. can be used only *a posteriori* and *descendendo* (descending), but not *a priori* and *ascendendo* (ascending). For the subjective laws of reason are rules of the use of the same in application, but the objective ones in explanation.\nAll immediately certain propositions are either 1. fundamental formulas or 2. *axiomata* or 3. *canones* or 4. elementary propositions of analysis or 5. immediately certain propositions of synthesis. The first are the proposition of identity and of contradiction. The second: [Only ground] objective principles of synthesis, space and time. The third: objective principles of *synthesis qvalitativae*. The 4th and fifth: immediately under the principles of the form [and of the] of synthesis as well as of analysis contained material propositions.\n[*Axiomata*] *Formulae primitivae* (primitive formulas) are valid for every cognition.\nThe synthetic fundamental concepts of reason just as the synthetic fundamental concepts of appearances give occasion to axioms, which however both serve only for the use of reason *a posteriori*.\nThe use of the principles *a posteriori*, if one applies them *a priori*, is a cognition of reason of the objects *per analogiam*, [either] e.g. of space, of pleasure and displeasure from God.",[]],"reflm:4371":["The logical relation is merely a relation of subordination, i.e. of the *sphaerarum conceptuum* (of the spheres of concepts), and the logical constitution of reason |\nof concepts consists in this, that they be general [or] concepts or *intuitus* (intuitions).\nThe real relation is that of the coordinations [either according to rules of the], and the rules of the same are either those of reason or of sensibility. To the first belong substance and *accidens*, whereas the logical relation has only subject and predicate. Inherence is a *coordinatio*, agreement a *subordinatio logica*. The concept of the ground. That of the whole or rather of the composite. Possibility and existence are *positiones absolutae* (absolute positings), the former of the concept (Something), the latter of the thing.\nThus are all synthetic concepts (*intellectualiter*). Substance, *compositum* and *ratio*.",[]],"reflm:4372":["All judgments [are] have a logical object, Something in the logical understanding, be it possible or impossible, =A.\nIn these judgments there is either no predicate or there is such a one.\nIn the first case. Either:\nA is — — possible or:\nA is — — actual;\nin the second case.\nA is B.\nIf A contains a subject with predicates, then the first proposition can be expressed logically. E.g. A figure can be enclosed in three sides, i.e. a triangle is possible. But because this presupposes that a figure is possible, otherwise it is only a proposition that expresses the relation of two concepts and not the absolute possibility, one sees well that possibilities must be given. The lack of contradiction does not prove [sinnl] the possibility of the thing and of the *data*, but of the combination. What, then, does the possibility of the thing itself signify? Logical possibility signifies the possibility of the relation A and B or A and non B. A triangle can be oblique-angled. Logical actuality is called truth.\nMetaphysical possibility signifies something objective, an object of thoughts. The non-possibility, of which the thought is empty. The impossible.\nEvery simple thought contains something possible, for one can invent nothing simple. It must accordingly be given through experience or inner law.",[]],"reflm:4373":["All truth consists in the agreement of all (g thoughts with the) laws of thinking and thus with one another. That is the object for us, what and how far it is given to us mediately or immediately through experience. Independently of all experience there are no objects and also no laws of the understanding. (e.g. Substantz: that this concept is something must be concluded from the experience of the persistence of a certain subiect under all circumstances.) We accordingly have concepts, 1. in order to explain phaenomena, 2. in order to have insight into the grounds of the morally good and evil. Everything that is given through these laws, e.g. cognition of the highest good (God), is practically true. The cognition of the very same object breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4374":["Appearances of reason. substantz, spirit etc: concepts of reason according to their form: *Genus*, *species*; according to matter: good and evil; for what is universal in the matter of all sensation is pleasure or displeasure or the equilibrium: 0, *indifferentia*.",[]],"reflm:4375":["We have of the objects of the senses only representations that are appropriate to our *phaenomena*, and of the things themselves (through pure reason), as they are appropriate to the laws of our understanding. |\nEven the concept of God is possible and necessary for us only insofar as it is necessarily in conformity with the laws of the understanding, which we necessarily follow in the (g estimation of the) world. It is therefore still only appearance, namely of the understanding, which borrows its certainty from the necessity of confirming our moral principles, from which all ought must ultimately be derived. [Durch] According to the laws of the understanding in physical appearances we arrive at the idea of a necessary cause, according to those in moral ones we arrive at the idea of a perfect author. One acts absurdly if one does not think to every motion a cause and to every combination a common cause; just as absurd it is to posit the moral rules still above happiness without God. What are appearances of reason? (Subjectively determined universal cognitions.) Hence comes the contradiction of the principles, if one judges according to the rule of reason with regard to the use in the world and afterwards wants to judge *in abstracto* from all such relation.",[]],"reflm:4376":["Apart from the appearance, the being or non-being of the same is also to be noted.",[]],"reflm:4377":["The laws of pure reason can contain the ground of the possibility of objects only relatively to the mind, insofar as they concern such objects whose essence consists in the relation to the soul, i.e. the morally good.",[]],"reflm:4378":["Just as sensibility is a faculty to order things according to rela|tions\nof space and time, so too reason is a law of the coordination of things abstracted from the laws of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:4379":["The concepts and propositions of reason, which *in concreto* are correct with regard to the objects of the world or of the senses, of the actual as well as the possible, lead us, if one goes back, to bounds that cannot be comprehensible according to these very rules; for their usability is valid only within these bounds. Nevertheless we cannot [von] refrain from the [constitution] cognition of that which sets these bounds, [keine andern] insofar as it serves, according to rules of our concrete reason, to explain and to confirm that which is within the bounds. The rest is useless. Thus with freedom the first is incomprehensible.",[]],"reflm:4380":["1. Substance and accidens; 2. Unity and multiplicity; 3. Ground and sequence (cause, effect, combination and separation).",[]],"reflm:4381":["M LIII:\nFor a pure concept of reason, e.g. substance, the predicate impenetrable is too narrow, and for a sensible one (Impenetrable) the pure concept substance is too wide. In the former one must either take sensibility away from the |\nsubiect or add it to the predicate, in the latter conversely. *Praedicatum univocum* or *aeqvivocum*. It is called in the first case: |\nM LIV: |\nA thing in general [stands], thought through understanding, stands under conditions of sensibility, i.e. is *phaenomenon*, in the second: an appearance is *noumenon*. E.g. the world of sense is an absolutely whole. Here the predicate would have to be limited thus: It is a whole that can be determined sensibly; or, with the affection left away from the subject: a world (*noumenon*) is a whole. The proposition: The soul is simple, is transcendent, and I must say either: I, who think the soul, am something simple, or: the soul is something that is not physically separable. In short: an intellectual predicate can belong to an object as *phaenomenon* only insofar as it is sensibly determinable.",[]],"reflm:4382":["There are empirical concepts through which an object is first given and which cannot be experimented on but only dissected. That they want to think the empirical basic concepts (matter) etc etc.",[]],"reflm:4383":["Above, beside and \nWhat is necessary under the (g subjective) conditions under which things appear to us, that is also necessary for things as appearances.\nWhat is necessary under the hypothesis that I arbitrarily invent is objectively necessary everywhere where the case of the hypothesis is given.\nBut what is said of things in general, regardless of the subject, and not under an arbitrary hypothesis, cannot be cognized through the agreements of thoughts with themselves.",[]],"reflm:4384":["In and \nThe mathematical synthesis is not [nicht] an analysis of what we think in the given concept.",[]],"reflm:4385":["The metaphysical concepts are: 1. Possible, 2. Being, (g 2b. Necessary,) 3. One posited to the other (whole), 4. One in the other (substantz), 5. One through the other (ground). The last three are real relations. The unity of the many: a. of the whole, b. the unity of the predicates in one subiect or c. of the sequels through one ground.",[]],"reflm:4386":["The supreme principle of human reason is either: that which expresses the condition under which we alone can cognize things through our reason, or: that under which things alone can be possible; subjective, objective. The principles of acquisition, of genesis, or of the mere cognition of possibility.",[]],"reflm:4387":["1. The matter for true judgment. Possibility.\n2. The *est*. existence.\n3. The combination through concept. Necessity.",[]],"reflm:4388":["*Lockii simplices ideae: cogitatio, Voluptas et taedium, Existentia, unitas, Potentia, Successio.*",[]],"reflm:4389":["(s Something and nothing are formal concepts in metaphysics and signify merely the relation.)\nSomething and nothing in the logical understanding is possible and impossible, i.e. that to which no concept at all belongs: not A, also not non A, which is derived there through contradiction, in metaphysics however from the comparison with the original being. [Etwas oder nichts im met] The possible is something or nothing in the metaphysical understanding as *realitas* or negation. The three absolute concepts as the simplest are: Possible (comes from indeterminate judgments), Existence (from affirmations), Necessary (from affirmation through concepts.) Categories.",[]],"reflm:4390":["The possibility according to matter must through the senses be given |\n. We cognize only the hypothetical possibility and the absolute one of space and time.",[]],"reflm:4391":["Above, beside and \nThe lack (of existence) is =0A. That something does not belong under possibility signifies an empty concept, i.e. a complete lack of concept; but that it is impossible, like a square circle, signifies the cancelation of possibility in general. For with a square circle not merely the possibility of this figure is canceled; for an empty concept contradicts nothing possible, although it is itself nothing; but: to be a circle and also square contradicts the possibility of a circle and also of every other; hence impossible is what contradicts possibility. But to contradict possibility is not of the thing but of the form; consequently however ............",[]],"reflm:4392":["Beside and \n*Nihil in sensu logico opponitur contrarie omnibus* (nothing in the logical sense is opposed contrarily to all). Nothing *logice* (logically) means as much as none.",[]],"reflm:4393":["The rational basic concepts can be brought to a certain number, but not the elementary concepts. Basic concepts of form and basic concepts of matter.",[]],"reflm:4394":["All simple concepts are possible; the impossibility lies only in the composition.",[]],"reflm:4395":["There are material and formal basic concepts, just as there are material and formal principles.",[]],"reflm:4396":["What is posited in itself, exists; what is canceled in itself, does not exist; what is neither the one nor the other, is indeterminate.\nWhat is posited through its idea is necessary; what is canceled thereby is impossible; what is neither the one nor the other is merely possible or in itself contingent.\nPossible is that whose concept is not empty, also not canceled through itself. That it is not to be empty signifies nevertheless that something outside the thought, thus a reality, *respondire* (responds) to it.",[]],"reflm:4397":["In the concept of the understanding the ground signifies that from which the inference to something else holds generally. The possibility of this can indeed be seen in logical, but not in real grounds. Experience, however, gives no true universality, because it gives no necessity. Now we nevertheless take the application of the concept of the real ground merely from experience. Hence the principles can be only empirically general and also have only an empirical significance, namely that with something something else as ground is always connected.",[]],"reflm:4398":["The inner possibility is the smallest, the absolute the greatest.\nAbsolutely possible is, *qvod absque ulla hypothesi* (what without any hypothesis), possible.",[]],"reflm:4399":["A–A=0 signifies no concept at all, but not no thing at all, i.e. a *remotion* of the thing. For A signifies an affirming concept, –A a negating one, both consequently none at all. The impossible is thus no negative concept, but none at all; but if A signifies a thing, –A its real opposite, then 0 = *privativum*. In all concepts something is thought (real), and then the positing or canceling (*actus intellectus: positio vel negatio*).",[]],"reflm:4400":["A thought without an affirming or negating object [the object] |\nrepresents something impossible, in that we neither posit nor cancel anything. (g or rather: it is an empty thought.)",[]],"reflm:4401":["In every negating judgment it is thought that the [predicate] subject is not contained under the predicate; whether because of the contradiction or not, is undecided.",[]],"reflm:4402":["Everything that is considered *transscendentaliter* is weighed respective to the essence, but in the metaphysical understanding absolutely and *universaliter*. In the former the essence is the sequel of the *essentialia*, in the latter the essence with its *essentialibus* is the ground of sequels *in indefinitum*. Hence those propositions are tautological, these however pregnant. For in the former the *essentialia* are given only through the essence and are again components of it.",[]],"reflm:4403":["Absolutely necessary is necessary in every aim, but inwardly necessary is (g it) in itself; consequently such a thing must at the same time include in itself the relation to everything, i.e. be the hypothesis for everything.\nImpossibility is the restricted possibility. The more conditions the possibility is restricted by, the smaller is the domain of this concept, until, if something is [all] in every regard impossible, it is in itself (g outright) impossible. Through mere concepts this can be seen only then, when something contradicts itself. [But the M]",[]],"reflm:4404":["All *commercium* and consequently all *compositum* is, as such, contingent.",[]],"reflm:4405":["Absolute necessity consists in this, that something 1. is no sequel of another, 2. is a ground of everything else.",[]],"reflm:4406":["From the alteration the logical contingency with regard to concepts can be inferred, likewise the inner possibility can be inferred, but not the contingency of the series. The determinations that follow one another are contingent; but the series is necessary; much more is the thing necessary, because it remains, while its determinations alternate.",[]],"reflm:4407":["In the same understanding in which a thing is necessary, it is also unalterable. If a predicate belongs to it outright necessarily, then it is also unalterable with regard to it; if it is in a conditioned |\nway, then it is unalterable under this condition; but the thing itself is on that account not unalterable, but only with regard to its predicates. With regard to existence no thing can be alterable, for not being is no alteration.",[]],"reflm:4408":["The agreement of the concept of an *entis omnimode realis* with the synthetic conditions of thinking [has] seems to have no difficulty, because between *realitatibus noumenis* no conflict can be comprehended, but quite well between *realitatibus phaenomenis*. The latter one expresses through effect in opposite direction.",[]],"reflm:4409":["Opposite and \nEach, all, the All. I have a penny for each poor person, but not for all poor persons, still less for the All. The latter is *completum*. From this All the rest is derived.",[]],"reflm:4410":["That of which no totality is possible is necessarily *incomplet*; so it is a multitude in general. (s [Series.] Progressions.)\nThere are three kinds of *respectus*, therefore also as many *correlata*, and therefore also as many *incompleta*: *rationatum, pars, praedicatum*.\nThe part is, as such, *incomplet*. (All things of the world are *incomplet* [therefore]; the *ens realissimum* is the only *complete* as a ground.) The unity of the whole is yet a ground why something belongs to it as a *complementum*.",[]],"reflm:4411":["The magnitude of a whole is extensive.\nThe magnitude of a ground is intensive or degree.\nThe beginning of a degree (*minimum*) is called *moment*.\n(s Magnitude of weight and degree of gravity.\nDegree of extension, of light and of warmth.\n*Moment* of warming.)\n(s Degree of reality. Generation of a reality from 0, i.e. *moment* of generation.)\n(s The magnitude of motion depends on time and space. The magnitude of speed (in an instant) is the magnitude of the *moment* of motion, through which namely a space can be covered in a time.)",[]],"reflm:4412":["The difference between a logical and a real subject is this, that the former contains the logical ground to posit the predicate, the latter the real ground (something other and positive), and is thus the cause, the |\n*accidens* however the effect. This relation of the cause we draw from our own [Wirk] actions and apply it to that which is constant in the appearances of outer things. But we find in the end everything in the object to be *accidentia*. The first subject is therefore a something through which the *accidentia* are. There arise therefore *iudicia synthetica*. e.g. Something that I know through the constant *accidentia* of impenetrability, that also contains those of attraction. The reality of the *accidens* we judge from the sensation of an object, which is distinguished from that of the subject.",[]],"reflm:4413":["Opposite the end of M § 195 and the paragraph number of M § 196:\nThe unity of the ground, the unity of the whole, the unity of the subject (in the simple).\nThe connection of many (different) things in a whole presupposes the dependence on a ground and flows from it.",[]],"reflm:4414":["One distinguishes the *substantiale* from all its predicates. A thing, however, cannot be distinguished from all its predicates.",[]],"reflm:4415":["All (g real) relations are either of connection or of conflict. The first are either of one through the other (*ratio et rationatum*) or to the other (*partium ad totum*) or in one another (*accidens et substantia*). The unity in all of this is the greatest connection (*subordinatio, coordinatio, involutio*). The boundaries are the first ground, the *omnitudo* of the connected and the last *subiectum*. A substance that contains the first ground of everything that is real, thus |\nwithout dependence, without limitation and without another subject, constitutes the highest *principium* of everything. *Vltimam rerum lineam* etc etc. The logically parallel of the *ente summo* is the *ens entium logicum* (*ens in genere*). It is the highest subject, comprises all things in its *sphaera* and is the highest ground of explanation, which is presupposed in everything as that which expresses its possibility. *Symbola* are the hanging of a part on others ([inherence] *composita in toto*), the depending of a link of the chain on others (dependence), the supporting of a thing on the other (*portitor*) (inherence). On the first ground hangs necessity, on the general infinity, on the [subst] self-subsisting — — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4416":["[The G] The *conceptus terminator* of the *inhaerentia* is *substantiale*\n*dependentia* *ens primum*\ncomposition *simplex*.",[]],"reflm:4417":["*aggregatum plurium se invicem determinantium est compositum reale; non omne totum est compositum.* e.g. *series subordinatorum*.\nIn every *compositum* there is unity of interaction, which proves the community with a third as *principium*.",[]],"reflm:4418":["All unity is relative; whether an absolute one is possible is the question.",[]],"reflm:4419":["A *compositum* from any kind of parts consists of parts that are not composed in the same way, but can quite well consist of parts that are composed of parts of another kind (for the latter is not the *contradictorie oppositum* of the former). For example, a rod consists of feet that are not further composed of feet, but indeed of inches. If therefore the composition is canceled (namely that of a certain kind), then parts remain that are not composed in the same way, but not such as would be composed in no way.",[]],"reflm:4420":["*Qvaestio hic proprie est, utrum compositum reale possit cogitari tanqvam qvantum continuum sive tanqvam discretum; si sit [discretum] continuum, non foret nisi vis qvaedam in spatio vel tempore adstricta.* (The question here is properly whether a real composite can be thought as a continuous *quantum* or as a discrete one; if it is [discrete] continuous, it would be nothing but a certain force confined in space or time.)\nThe whole that is the *totum intelligibile*, whose *phaenomenon* is body, may perhaps consist of simple parts, but not on that account the body, because this whole does not exert simplicity and its sequels in space.\nThe necessity of simple parts rests on this, that they can also exist separated, all together. But the total separation in space is the complete abolition of the relations of space. Thus a body consists of simple parts only insofar as I do not consider these in relations of space, thus not as an *extensum*.",[]],"reflm:4421":["*Corpus non est compositum substantiale, sed phaenomenon substantiatum.* (Body is not a substantial composite, but a substantiated phenomenon.) *[Neque] et (g tam) compositio qvoad spatium qvam divisio sunt phaenomena coniunctionis et divisionis.* (Neither [...] and (g both) composition with regard to space and division are phenomena of conjunction and division.) *Si substantia composita, cuius phaenomenon est corpus, [sit] concipiatur constare e simplicibus, phaenomenon ipsius ideo e talibus non constat.* (If a composite substance, of which body is the phenomenon, [is] be conceived to consist of simples, its phenomenon on that account does not consist of such.) *[Neqve] Praeterea spatium rationem possibilitatis phaenomeni continet; qvicqvid itaque ita praeseus est, ut eius praesentia sit actio in spatio, huius conditionibus adstringitur.* ([Neither] Moreover space contains the ground of the possibility of the phenomenon; whatever, therefore, is so present that its presence is an action in space, is bound by its conditions.)",[]],"reflm:4422":["At the very top of the page:\n*Qvia spatium est phaenomenon, corpora sunt [action] praesentiae externae plurium substantiarum phaenomena.* (Because space is a phenomenon, bodies are [action] phenomena of the external presence of several substances.) *Ergo qvanqvam omnis compositio in toto substantiali, tamen non ideo in externae praesentiae phaenomeno tolli potest.* (Therefore although all composition in the substantial whole, yet it cannot on that account be removed in the phenomenon of external presence.) *Cum spatium externae praesentiae possibilitatis rationem contineat, [possibilitas] necessitas divisionis non ulterius pertingit in composito, qvam per conceptum spatii admittitur.* (Since space contains the ground of the possibility of external presence, [possibility] the necessity of division reaches no further in the composite than is admitted through the concept of space.) *Fac simplex in spatii parte assignata praesens esse: spatium limitaret id obiective, ergo esset obiectivum aliqvid.* (Suppose a simple to be present in an assigned part of space: space would limit it objectively, therefore it would be something objective.)",[]],"reflm:4423":["Opposite the conclusion of M § 244:\nAlterations are actual, means: we actually represent to ourselves the determinations of things according to our law of sensibility in the sequence of time. Alterations are possible only in time, i.e. their representation presupposes the sensible form of time.",[]],"reflm:4424":["Every *quantum* is either a *compositum*, i.e. *syntheticum*, or a *quantum analyticum*. A *compositum* (composite) is that whose parts can be before the |\ncomposition and which comes to be only through composition and combination. Every *compositum* consists of *simplicia* (simples) and is *divisibile* (divisible).\n*Qvantum indivisibile* (an indivisible quantum) is what cannot arise *per compositionem minorum qvantorum* (through the composition of smaller quanta). The magnitude, force of the understanding is no *compositum*. A greater moral perfection neither. The smaller degrees arise only from the limitation of the greatest, and this is the first that is given in itself. Every such *quantum* is *continuum*. A *discretum* is composed.\n(g In all those magnitudes that cannot arise through the composition of the smaller, although these are contained in them, there is a *progressus continuus in infinitum* (continuous progress to infinity). For they [they] themselves exist only through a greatest, and they are limited only by others that are at the same time.)",[]],"reflm:4425":["*Spatium est quantum, sed non compositum* (space is a quantum, but not composite). Because space does not arise in that the parts are posited, but the parts are possible only through space; likewise time. The parts can well be thought particularly *abstrahendo a caeteris* (by abstracting from the rest), but not *removendo caetera* (by removing the rest). and so they can well be discerned, but not separated, and the *divisio non est realis, sed logica* (division is not real, but logical). Because [know whether not] all matter [only as] seems, as to divisibility, to depend on the space that it fills, and it is as divisible as this space, the question arises whether the divisibility of matter is not, just like that of space, merely logical.",[]],"reflm:4426":["Speed does not arise through mere addition of smaller degrees, |\nbut merely through the limitation of the greatest force. Hence the Leibnizian law of the square of speed; for the tortoise A carries itself with one degree of force and speed, carries the second B with just the same degree of force =2. This one, in that it pushes itself forward, also works against the tortoise A with one degree, and this one therefore needs in the direction AB, in order to withstand the counteraction, also one degree of force =3, and B has moreover in the direction AB its own speed =4. The third C [requires] posits therefore first these 4 as presupposed, second in A yet one degree in order to carry it =5, in B also in order to carry it =6, in A a resistance that arises from this =7, further in B a degree from the resistance, in that C pushes itself forward =8, and finally one in C, with which it goes forth, =9. The forces, then, that [one upon the other] each applies are like the odd numbers. A=1. A+B=3. A+B+C=/5=9.",[]],"reflm:4427":["The speed that a spring can give to bodies in just the same space of its stretching-out grows into the infinite. Hence every lasting force, e.g. spring, that works externally, has infinite force. Every inwardly working force is in any case infinite with respect to time, because it [not as] (first it goes to all bodies however great) can work through every time however long in equal degree without diminution.",[]],"reflm:4428":["We can represent the finite *in concreto* to ourselves as possible only through the limitation of the infinite. e.g. a *spatium* of a certain figure. Likewise, since a thing is thought complete only [through] thereby that, if it [laut] has all reality, everything is affirmed of it and hence all being is thought, and if it is an *ens limitatum*, that some is posited in it, but all remaining reality is indeed thought, but denied: so the concept of the finite seems to arise from that of the infinite.",[]],"reflm:4429":["[The infinitely small] In that which is infinitely divisible (*continuum*), there is a *terminus*, but no *finis divisionis*. But what is infinite in the *synthesis* is no *finis* or *terminus* and yet totality. The *synthesis* in the case of the *continuum* has no first beginning and in the case of the *infinitum* no end.",[]],"reflm:4430":["*Infinitum reale est omnisufficiens (in qvo est omnitudo)* (The real infinite is all-sufficient (in which there is allness)). *Infinitum non est mutabile* (The infinite is not mutable); all alterations are limitations of existence.",[]],"reflm:4431":["For the concept of matter extension and impenetrability are enough; for mere matter *inertia* is further required; *materia spontanea (viva)* (spontaneous (living) matter) is not mere matter. How do we come to (g presuppose) that impenetrable extension cannot be self-active apart from a foreign, hence immaterial *principio*? Does that come from this, that we encounter some *impenetrabile iners* and therefore suspect in the case of the *vivo* yet a *principium vitae*?\n(s Resistance takes place only insofar as a body does not have a motion, but rather an endeavor to the contrary.)",[]],"reflm:4432":["The *extensum impenetrabile* is according to its substance matter, according to its form body; to form belong the outer (figure) and the inner structure, (s third the mixture; the figure is an object of mathematics, the structure of mechanics, the mixture of chemistry and forces of physics.)\nThe [mathematical] cognition of corporeal nature is called, as *doctrin*, [*Naturlehre*] *Naturkunde*, as *disciplin*, *Naturlehre*. *Naturlehre* is either rational (*Naturwissenschaft*, science of nature) or empirical, *phaenomena*, (*Naturbeschreibung*, description of nature). *Naturwissenschaft* is either mathematical or philosophical. that one considered breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4433":["If we assume that the difference of all things consists merely in the diversity of magnitude either of the whole or of the parts or also of the motion of these parts, then between all their possible *species* there is a continuous interconnection. Between rest and motion. Between equilateral, isosceles, unequal-sided triangles, the conic sections. Between a surface that moves\ninfinitely little, and moves to an alleged magnitude and makes bodies.\nThis interconnection can also, at least as a law of the understanding, be seen into from the class-division always attempted. For there one presupposes that all things can be regarded as variations of one fundamental being. For if this were not so, then from a concept through the closer determination a necessary (g and determinate) manifold of those contained under it could not be concluded. Or one also represents the unity of the cause, and each product not as subordinated immediately to it, but one by means of the other. Because in the highest cause everything must [a single] be homogeneous and the heterogeneity not a limitation of the parts by the highest cause itself, but of one part by the other. As if from coarse matter gradually plants, from these animals and finally from these the human being had arisen with gradual transition; so that the animal capacities, which con|sist in a very special relation to certain occurring circumstances,\nare gradually arranged for more or the more general and finally entirely for the general and are called reason. One can represent to oneself that either all kinds of animals have arisen through the gradual raising of perfection from the mineral on, *ascendendo*, or through the decrease of a creature that was more perfect than the human being, where in this original animal lay the sources of all possible mechanical formation, which in its products breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4434":["The *saltus* is either according to degree (from a smaller state suddenly to a greater) or according to *qvalitaet* (where, after the series of grounds has ceased, a new one begins *spontaneo*). In the world there is a *continuitaet* of the dynamical series, but not merely as sense- but also as intelligible world. The first especially then has *hiatum*. With regard to the receptivity of the mind there is *continuitaet*; the *facultas* has *spontaneitaet*.",[]],"reflm:4435":["Mere *materie* is *iners* or lifeless; hence what lives of extended beings (animal), [is] has in itself *materie* and a particular *principium* of life: the immaterial. (The inner *principien* of the *phaenomena*, which are cognized only through inner sense.) Hence the immaterial natures and their concept already rest on the distinction of inner and outer sense.",[]],"reflm:4436":["The supreme cause is also the ground of the possibility of the alterations of one and the same world. I.e. that the subject remains, although it exists differently; otherwise the subject would have to perish together with its state and no other arise. The cause of this possibility is therefore the preservation, i.e. the infinite duration of the supreme cause.",[]],"reflm:4437":["*Non mutantur nisi quae sustentantur ab alio.* (Only what is sustained by another is altered.)\n*Qvia omnia subsistunt in alio, spatium est; qvia omnia durabiliter subsistunt in alio, tempus est.* (Because all things subsist in another, there is space; because all things subsist durably in another, there is time.)\nIn alteration there is a unity of manifold states of one and the same *subiect*; this unity rests on the dependence of the substances on One.\n*Qvae pertinent ad eundem mundi statum, sunt simultanea; quae ad diversos sive oppositos, sunt successiva.* (What pertains to the same state of the world is simultaneous; what to different or opposed ones is successive.)\n*Tempus in se continet rationem possibilis mutationis contingentium statuum; nam e contingentia sola non sequitur mutabilitas.* (Time contains in itself the ground of the possible alteration of contingent states; for from contingency alone mutability does not follow.)\n*Non contingentia, sed mutabilitas arguit existentiam ab alio, et qvia tempus est unicum, existentiam ab uno. Simultaneitas etiam arguit existentiam ab uno.* (Not contingency but mutability argues existence from another, and because time is unique, existence from one. Simultaneity also argues existence from one.)",[]],"reflm:4438":["At the very top of the page:\n*Influxus solae substantiae sustentatricis est originarius.* (The influence of the sustaining substance alone is original.)\n*Nullum commercium substantiarum (influxus mutuus) est originarium; omnium est derivativum, qvatenus sustentantur per aliud.* (No community of substances (mutual influence) is original; that of all is derivative, insofar as they are sustained through another.)",[]],"reflm:4439":["One cognizes the course of nature through the senses, and through the (s reason the) order of nature; the greater the reason, the more order one discovers. In the absence of reason everything seems chance (s or blind necessity). Nature is at all times a *principium* of order.\nThe order of nature is distinguished from the order according to rules of perfection (i.e. of a good will, how the things, if they were to please, would have to be). [This is the objective order of the world] The order of nature is thoroughly necessary for the moral order, and the most perfect world will attain this [Gründ] perfection according to the order of nature, because only under this condition is it possible to use understanding.\n(s Freedom also stands under the order of nature.)",[]],"reflm:4440":["There are three kinds of [psych] physiological effects of the human soul.\n1. the [sensation] mere representations, 2. the connection, 3. the comparison.\nThree kinds of cognition [i.e.] and objective differences:\n1. Sensation, 2. form of appearance, 3. concept.\nThree kinds of use of reason:\n1. Inner sense or intuition of oneself and of one's thoughts. 2. Universal representations and the relation of their spheres (logical use). 3. The form of *thesis* and *synthesis*. The use of pure reason contains either absolute or relation|concepts.\nThe former either intuitive, i.e. according to the matter (good), or discursive: according to the mere form (existence).\n(s Animals also have *facultatem diiudicandi* (*iudicium sensitivum*), but not *iudicandi* (*iudicium intellectuale*).",[]],"reflm:4441":["One can regard freedom as if it were the influence of a particular cause, namely of the intelligence, which however does not determine alone, and in turn of sensibility as a particular cause. The first gives as ground of the explanation the character, the second the *naturel* or temperament. Although in each case, whatever the human being may otherwise have done, the opposite is nevertheless possible now (as in a game, where one has often hit): this freedom is nonetheless to be regarded in the whole like nature, and the actions are sufficiently (practically) determined from character and temperament.",[]],"reflm:4442":["The state after death is either a *regeneration* or a liberation; the former either an earthly or a supra-earthly. Either a new animal life of earthly or supra-earthly kind or a spiritual life. And this its relation either in this world or in another world.\nSeat of the soul. Place after death. Separation from the body. Heaven, hell and earth. That belongs both to the world, but not to the world of sense.",[]],"reflm:4443":["The cause on account of which we hope for another life is that the outcome may agree more with morality. This is however brought about by this, that only the means of dissembling vanishes [and]; for thereby good human beings will soon associate with one another and expel the evil and produce happiness among themselves. Not past vices, but the character thereby engrained will constitute the punishment of the future world.",[]],"reflm:4444":["334. E II 1719.\nM 334':\nThat magnitude through which the smaller are alone possible, the magnitude of the universal ground, can be compared with the magnitudes of the sequel or with smaller magnitudes in general only *per analogiam*; it cannot be regarded as the sum of smaller forces; they are not contained in it, but posited through it; thus the all-sufficient ground is a magnitude only *per analogiam*, i.e. only through the relation to the effect.\n(s Proof that there is an *ens realissimum*, in which all things are given; that this is necessary; that it does not exist in time and in space, but yet eternal and omnipresent, all-sufficient.)\n[Possibility, actuality]\nTranscendental predicates.\nUnity: that the concept of God is a *conceptus singularis* and there can also be no other *conceptum singularem* of a being than that which contains *omnitudinem*. For everything is only once. Some a is b, another is *non b*. (g Polytheism, Manichaeism.)\nIs proved by this, that all beings that distinguish themselves do so through their limitations, therefore many beings are always considered in the unlimited. This concept is practically necessary.\n[Einf] Substantiality. Spinoza. *Originaria*.\nSimplicity.\nAll-sufficiency.\n( g Unalterability *impassibilitas* )\nMagnitude. Infinity. *illimitatum*. (Eternity, omnipresence.)\nin existence, in all time and in all places. Eternity and omnipresence.\nin power.\n(s This is not the difficulty, how God is at all, but at no, time, likewise place.)\nMetaphysical properties. In relation to the determinations of the *cosmologia generalis*.\n(s Are taken from the causality with regard to things, because the causality of the contingent lies in freedom and in general the causality of an effect outside it.)\n(s *systema inhaerentiae* (system of inherence): of Spinoza, of emanation, and of creation.)\n(s *Substantia omnisufficiens intellectualis est Deus.* (An all-sufficient intellectual substance is God.))\n(s Independence; *intuitus intellectualis*, *prototypon*; not discursive: intuitive; not reason, not number.)\nIs a spirit, not world-soul. *Anthropomorphismus crassior*.\n1. Understanding. Omniscience. (s Prediction.)\n3. Will. Goodness. Holiness. Justice. Freedom and independence.\n2. Blessedness (s not well-being). *complacentia relativa non est sensus voluptatis* (relative complacency is not a sense of pleasure).\nPhysicotheological predicates (s inner and outer).\nCreation (s causality with regard to) of the world.∗ Of the matter, not merely according to form.\n(s The world is not in time, but the things of the world in it. The world has no beginning, but cause.)\n(s A time of creation cannot be thought.)\n(s That all things of the world are creatures, but not of one another.)\n(g Preservation) Government and providence. (s Not the cause of evil. *Concursus*.)\nFinal end. (s Ordinary, extraordinary direction.) The two propositions: the most perfect cause and the best world with their *consectario* of the other life are inseparably combined.\nGeneral negative determinations. Analogy.\nM 334:\n∗(s signifies *intellectualiter* only the free cause of the substances, *sensitive* the beginning of the duration of the world.)",[]],"reflm:4445":["(s Possibility of such a science. With regard to what is not intuiting.)\nProlegomena. Some sciences contain merely a use of the understanding with regard to various objects of curiosity and of advantage, and logic is the general *Organon* of them, namely the instruction on its use.\nOther sciences have the determination of the understanding itself as their aim, namely both according to its faculty and according to its highest ends, or the objects to which the understanding and the will are restricted by the highest laws of nature. Thus [is] are all other sciences organs of skill, at most of prudence, metaphysics of wisdom.\nThe use of metaphysics with regard to the theoretical is merely negative; it does not open up the cognition of things and is not dogmatic; for where should it get [its] the [propert] cognition of things without senses. Mathematics makes arbitrary concepts of magnitudes as hypothetical conditions, from which sequels can be drawn [A categorical science], through mere repetitions. But with the question of what a thing is, we can invent no concepts and also no relations. They must relate to what is given at least as |\ngrounds of their use. Metaphysics only prevents the false use of reason, which steps out of its limitations and considers the *intellectualia* as objects, since they serve only for the *modo cognoscendi* of the *sensitive dabilium* and at most for the restriction of the same, insofar as it wants to use the *sensitiva* beyond their limitations. Pure reason is dogmatic only with regard to the objects of the will, but with regard to [specu] speculation (merely overseeing) catharctic. In metaphysics there are no hypotheses, because first the possibility of a highest ground of reason would be assumed entirely without rule and because metaphysics precisely wants to set limitations for reason in its pure use.\nAristippus: our judgments about what is agreeable (g likewise all those that express mere appearances) are always true, however the objects may be constituted, because agreeableness signifies something that is in us; but the judgments: this or that object exists, [is] are uncertain, because the objects are distinguished from us.",[]],"reflm:4446":["*Intellectualia* are either theoretical (g *metaphysica*) or practical cognitions (g *moralia*).\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n[dogmatici] Aristotle (g Romans) Scholastics\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\nPlato Ramus\nRoughness of the ...\n(s Critique before the doctrine)\nUnderstanding.\n((s Ideas are in Plato *intuitus*.) s *methodus dogmatica* — *sceptica* *critica*.\ndecartes\nhume )\n*Loci metaphysici Aristotelis* (metaphysical topics of Aristotle)\n*domus conceptuum intellectualium* (house of intellectual concepts).\n( s *ideae connatae* (innate ideas); *acqvisitae* (acquired): *per sensus* (through the senses), *per anticipationes* (through anticipations). )\n(s Natural or supernatural sources of the intellectual cognitions.)\nLocke. Subjective habituation. (s He assumed the *principia fiendi* for the *essendi*.)\n( s (s instead of analytic) *Physiologia intellectus*, where [its phae] the origin of its concepts is explained. Wolf has it not at all. )\nCrusius* *Methodus cognitionis praestabilitae*.\n*vel per epigenesin vel per praeformationem*.\n*(s is not to be used, because we do not know what God has opened up to us.)\n(s All have treated metaphysics dogmatically, not critically.)\n(s Criterion of truth: the taking to be true.)",[]],"reflm:4447":["Plato indicated the origin of the concepts of perfection well. But not of the *notionum*. Plato enthusiast, Aristotle analyst. The objective and subjective principles of the D breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4448":["M VII:\nThe analytic concept of the thing contains what lies in it; the synthetic, what must be attributed to it, insofar as it is to be a certain thing.*\nM VIII:\n(* g *apparentia*. *conceptus*. *notio*. *idea*. The idea that through which *a priori* things are possible according to rules of a perfect will. Images. Concepts. ideas. Plants. Origin: minerals. Ends. )",[]],"reflm:4449":["History of the differences between *sensitivis* and *intellectualibus*.\nEgyptians. Pythagoras. Heraclitus (g Eleatic). Plato (g *ideae innatae*) and Pythagoras made the *intellectualia** into particular objects of possible intuition; his school, the academic [phi] |\nphilosophers, exoterically *sceptice* and esoterically *dogmatice*. *Intuitus intellectualis*, from which everything descends. Aristotle taught the sensitive ones (g categories), but did not remain with them. Epicurus: a philosopher of sensations. [Ep] Aristippus: merely the feeling of the agreeable.\n*(s *intellectualia* either *qvoad obiecta* or *formam cognitionis*)\n(s *intellectualia* either *mystice* or *logice talia*)\n(s the intellectual cognitions, but *circa obiecta sensuum* (about objects of the senses), *neqve per notiones connatas* (nor through innate notions))\n( s *vel per intuitus vel conceptus intellectuales* (either through intuitions or intellectual concepts). The latter either metaphysical\nLocke — Leibniz.\nor physiological\nAristotle and Epicurus. )",[]],"reflm:4450":["Aristotle erred in this, that he made in logic a division of the general concepts through which one can think things: this belongs to metaphysics. Logic has to do with concepts, whichever they may also be, and treats only their relation.",[]],"reflm:4451":["Theosophical and mystical principles.\nPythagoras, the Eleatic school: Parmenides, Plato treated *intellectualia*; Plato *per ideas connatas et intuitivas*, the others *per discursivas*, but the *sensitiva* only as *apparentias*. Aristotle borrowed everything from the senses, but nevertheless treated *intellectualia*, which he held for *sensitiva*.",[]],"reflm:4452":["The true concepts of reason go only to the relation of things in general. The objects are sensitive; only [their] the use of reason with regard to them occurs according to merely intellectual laws; if the objects are intellectual, then this is enthusiastic.",[]],"reflm:4453":["The *Qvaestiones* of metaphysics are all raised by common reason and by our most important ends; it is no *organon* of science, but of wisdom and is useful negatively, to remove the hindrances that oppose themselves to the most important cognition.",[]],"reflm:4454":["In the critique of metaphysics one can make use of two kinds of methods. The first is: to examine the proofs and to seek out their *paralogismos* or *petitiones principii* (petitions of principle). The second: to oppose to a proof another, and indeed an equally convincing one, of the opposite. This latter method is the best. For since the errors of metaphysical inferences consist chiefly in this, that what holds merely of the conditions of sensible cognition is enunciated of the object, a proof can seem so rigorous that one is hardly aware of an error, which one discovers best through a *demonstratio oppositi*.",[]],"reflm:4455":["(g It is discipline of pure reason. Aesthetics: critique of taste.)\nidea of metaphysics: Is it a critique or doctrine: is its procedure zetetic or dogmatic? The question is: what can one [without] through mere reason without all experience cognize (mathematics, morals)? which are the sources, the conditions and boundaries. Transcendental philosophy is critique of pure reason. study of the subject, confusion of the subjective with the objective, prevention.",[]],"reflm:4456":["In transcendental philosophy *notiones* occur, but not ideas.",[]],"reflm:4457":["In the *metaphysica applicata* much is dogmatic. (g in the transcendental everything critical; can something indeed be invented through metaphysics? Yes, with regard to the subject, but not the object.)\n[Only] As critique it has utility. Even if religion and virtue are not grounded on it, but have other sources, it serves to remove the hindrances. Critique of science and *Organon** of wisdom (g which depends more on doing without than on acquiring. Socrates).\n[The Qvaest] It is necessary; the *qvaestiones* are set for it by sound reason and moral concerns. It is indispensable.\n*(g a propaedeutic of the same; morals is an *organon*.)",[]],"reflm:4458":["Of metaphysics, as of an unknown land, on the possession of which we are intent, we have first diligently investigated the (g situation and) approaches. (It lies in the (g region) hemisphere of pure reason;) we have even drawn the outline of it, where this island of [Cognit] of cognition [to the] is connected with the land of experience by bridges, or where it is separated from it by a deep sea; we have even drawn the outline of it and know, as it were, the geography (g ichnography) of the same, but do not yet know what may be encountered in this land, which some have held uninhabitable for humans, others have regarded as their actual settlement. After this general geography of this land of reason we want to take the general history of the same into consideration.",[]],"reflm:4459":["M XI:\nUtility.\nWhat is that which gives the deep investigations of metaphysics their supreme motive and in which the true importance of such a science is to be posited.\n1. It is not the immediate desire for knowledge that it satisfies, thus not as science.\n2. Also not as an *organon* of other sciences, e.g. of natural science.\n3. Thus only as a *propaedeutic* of wisdom. But as such, wherein consist the foremost questions that it resolves, or the important cognitions to which it is supposed to be the key. They are 2. Is there a God, and is there a future life. The answering of these questions is in|turn\nimportant, insofar as it is a ground of our conduct and secures the principles of life.\nBut if the principles of life (g other) have [rules] sources independent of this, |\nM XII: |\nIf in order to support them no science, but only practically sufficient cognition is necessary, but this is given even without [the] strict logical perfection, from [the] practical sources themselves: then this science will be able to serve only for the security of the doctrine of wisdom against all incursions of an apparent reason, which service is very great. Belief in God gives to metaphysical inferences *favorem utilitatis*.\n(s Attack not of the things, but of the assertions: speculations.)",[]],"reflm:4460":["[Analytic part Princ]\nGenetic part: sensibility and reason.\nZetetic:\na. *Analysis*, *Principium Contradictionis*.\nb. *Synthesis*.\nc. *Antithesis*. Skeptical part.",[]],"reflm:4461":["There are 2 supreme practical propositions. 1. What we must do, in order to become worthy of happiness. 2. What ground we have [to believe] to believe practically sufficiently, that one is to become happy, insofar as one is worthy of this happiness. Thus a practically sufficient ground of belief in God and another world. (s All other sciences have only a worth as a means to contingent ends (which to despise would be still more sublime), yet insofar as they cultivate (*excoliren*) the understanding, |\nwhich is the universal *organon* of all ends, they have a worth that is greater than the end.)",[]],"reflm:4462":["The longing for happiness expresses the sum of all inclinations, [as is] in whatever they may consist; hence it is the first [long] or supreme desire. The matter and condition of everything (subjective).",[]],"reflm:4463":["The universal longing of human beings is to be happy; but a [limitation and] condition to which reason (not desire and sensibility) limits this longing is: to be worthy of this happiness.\nIf now the worthiness to be happy is the sole condition of the hope of becoming so too, then the precepts of morality precede those of [prud] prudent self-love. But if these laws are to get their incentives through the hope of becoming happy, then it is necessary that one could at least believe that the world stands under a wise government.",[]],"reflm:4464":["If metaphysics is treated in this way, then it is a hindrance to no science of experience through intellectual *fictiones*, it secures morals |\nagainst false *subtilitaet* and promotes the practical, is advantageous to the beautiful sciences, promotes the inner acquaintance with the human being.\n(s It is the *demarcation* of pure reason and the boundary-guard, in order to prevent that it, by straying beyond its boundaries, confuse itself and disturb religion and morals with its chimeras.)",[]],"reflm:4465":["In the sciences of pure reason the philosophy of the present time is more critical than dogmatic, [more] an investigation of the subject [than] and thereby of the possibility of thinking an object for oneself.",[]],"reflm:4466":["The critique of pure reason a preliminary exercise for metaphysics in theoretical philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4467":["[The speculation] The erudition of reason and the wisdom of reason: the first is speculative and [goes to] is a skill to resolve the *quaestionen* of reason; the second a determination of the worth of these exercises of the understanding and of their boundaries. Philosophy contains the former; the philosopher, who contains the end of philosophy in himself, is the latter. He is not an artist of reason, but a legislator of human reason.",[]],"reflm:4468":["That reason needs discipline. That, if it is not trained, but spreads its branches wildly, it brings forth blossoms without fruits. |\nThat therefore a master of discipline is necessary (not a taskmaster), who governs it. That without this discipline it does not agree with religion and morals, does the big talking and, since it does not know itself, confuses the healthy understanding practiced in experiences.",[]],"reflm:4469":["The skeptical method is the best and the only one, to drive back objections through *retorsion*. Does a universal doubt arise from this? No, rather the presumptions of pure reason with regard to the conditions of the possibility of all things are thereby driven back. Thereby all judgments of healthy reason with regard to the world and the practical get their great repute. Healthy reason or the practical will never let itself be persuaded that there is no God, if only the subtle does not strive to win the rank from it.",[]],"reflm:4470":["The first question is: how concepts [of things] can arise in us that have become known to us through no appearance of the things themselves, or propositions that no experience has taught us.",[]],"reflm:4471":["To philosophy belong many cognitions as means and as [versch] cultivations of our insight, but they do not belong to it as constituent parts. Philosophy itself is the science of determining for all this use of reason its ends and its bounds [to] or circle. The former are a piece of erudition, the latter of wisdom. Yet a sage is one thing and a philosopher another. The latter goes only to the cognition and estimation of the essential ends, the former to the exercise and is practical. The philosophical spirit and the spirit of philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4472":["Empirical (g ground) propositions are at all times synthetic. Universal ones are never empirical. Hence the former are at all times universal only *in tantum*. One makes new propositions from empirical ones *per analysin*.",[]],"reflm:4473":["M XIX:\nIt is the question, how we can represent to ourselves things entirely *a priori*, i.e. independently of all experience (also implicitly), and how we can grasp principles that are borrowed from no experience, consequently *a priori*; how it happens [possible s] that objects correspond to that which is merely a product of our isolating mind and these objects are subjected to the laws that we prescribe to them. That there are such cognitions *a priori*, pure mathematics and metaphysics teach; but it is an investigation of importance, to have insight into the ground of their possibility. That a representation, which is itself an effect of the object, corresponds to it, is well to [grasp] comprehend. But that something that is merely a birth of my brain, relates to an object as representation, is not so clear. Further, that with an impression stemming from objects in me still another is combined, consequently that we connect one representation with the other in accordance with experience, is also graspable. But that we can from ourselves* [connect others with representations can so] connect properties and predicates (g valid) with the represented objects, although no experience has shown them to us as connected, is hard to have insight into. To say that a higher being has already wisely laid such concepts and principles in us, means to destroy all philosophy. It must be sought in the nature of cognitions in general, how a relation and connection is possible, where yet only one of the *relata* is given.\n*(s to be able to say something universal about objects, without needing that they disclose it to us;)\nM XX:\nCognitions of experience are not mere impressions. We must ourselves think something with the impressions, so that such arise. Thus there must yet be actions of cognition that precede experience and through which it is possible. Likewise experiences never give truly universal cognitions, because necessity is lacking in them. Now reason needs for certain cognition universal propositions. Thus certain universal judgments must lie in it still before experience.",[]],"reflm:4474":["The experiences with which we first form transcendental concepts show us not their origin, but only the empirical condition of their arising. For since they are not drawn from experience, so must their source breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4475":["Mathematics has no principles at all from experience, even the *applicata*, but only objects of experience.",[]],"reflm:4476":["M XXV:\nThe category of [Hypothes] synthesis will probably be called: *substantia* (substance) — *Causatum* (g and *independens*), *Compositum* (g and *simplex*).\nThe category of analysis: *Totale* (g *perfectum, completum*) and [*simplex*] *partiale*. (g [*Pars*] *Finitum et infinitum* [(*qvod non est pars*)].) (g *particularitas est infinita*.) (g *Vnum et plura*.)\nThe [Tes] idea of the thesis: *Realitas*.\n(s *qvae, qvalis, qvanta*.)\nOf the synthesis: *Materia et forma*.\nOf the analysis: *qvantitas et qvalitas*.\nIn analysis [also a composition] the whole is earlier than the part, in synthesis the part earlier than the whole.\nM XXVI: |\n*idem et diversum* [(*qvantitas qvalitas*)]: *realitas, negatio consentiens et oppositum* (form of affirmation and negation) *externum et internum* (*qvantitas, qvalitas*) *absolutum et relativum*\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\nComparison\nCombination\nRelation\n{s {\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\nIn judgments the relation 1. Of agreement, fitting-together.\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}",[]],"reflm:4477":["Analytic propositions can be proved from the *principium contradictionis* or *identitatis* (principle of contradiction or of identity), but not synthetic ones; whence, then, do we arrive at these? 1. Empirically. 2. Through pure intuition. 3. Through subjective conditions of the representation of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4478":["The *principium* of all synthetic judgments of pure reason (not of pure intuition) is: everything that contains the conditions without which an apprehension would be impossible is true. The *principium contradictionis* holds of all cognitions insofar as they are [on] regarded as merely possible. I.e. what contradicts the concept that I could have of an object is false.",[]],"reflm:4479":["Formulas are not *axiomata*. Those are logical, these metaphysical; those speak only of predicates and subjects in general, these give one.",[]],"reflm:4480":["To no subject does a predicate belong that contradicts an analytic predicate of the subject. To no subject does a predicate belong that contradicts the synthetic one of the same, at the same time with it.\nWith regard to the synthetic predicates, therefore, every thing is changeable, and the synthesis can be thought as successive determination. The possibility of concepts rests merely on the proposition of contradiction. That of synthesis, on experience.",[]],"reflm:4481":["Because all our simple concepts must be given through the senses (g and cannot be invented), and consequently their object is actual, everything that we think is possible, except that the relation of the concepts can constitute the impossibility. This relation can be logical or real; in the first case it is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:4482":["When I negate, I think something — — affirm, — — —\n}\nboth is *logice* something, but the first *realiter* nothing, both\ntogether *logice* nothing; the zero is *logice* something, *realiter* nothing; *transscendentaliter* nothing: where there are no data for thinking, thus neither affirmation nor negation. But impossibility is merely in contradiction. Thus it is impossible that the transcendental nothing be possible.",[]],"reflm:4483":["At the outer margin, beginning next to the 3rd sentence of M § 18:\nEven to impossibility something given belongs. The first data are not cognized as possible *a priori*, but constitute the condition of all our judgments about possibility, so that only that is possible which agrees with the conditions [of the] *a priori* of empirical cognition. Logical possibility, *principium contradictionis*, is not objective, but of cognition. We cannot think up any possibility of intuition, of reality, of the real relation, of the necessary, except insofar as [it] the principles thereof have been given in experience.",[]],"reflm:4484":["The possibility of that which one cannot cognize determinately *a priori* must be represented according to the analogy of nature, i.e. of the empirical.",[]],"reflm:4485":["Change is given through experience and a problem for reason.",[]],"reflm:4486":["The *opposita* one after another do not *contradicere* each other; consequently the *succedens* is no *contradictorie oppositum* of the *antecedens*, consequently the changes are no proof of the possibility of the *contradictorie oppositum*, thus also not of contingency.",[]],"reflm:4487":["Changeable are either substances or their state, but the change has in these cases different significance; e.g. the shape, the place is changeable, signifies: the substances are changeable with regard to both.",[]],"reflm:4488":["We infer contingency in the world not so much from change as rather from dependence on certain conditions. Afterward, when we take together everything that is necessary only under condition, we infer to the contingency of the whole: *primus motor* (first mover).",[]],"reflm:4489":["Beside and \nOne time contains one state, the other its opposite: is not *repugnans* (conflicting), but *oppositum potentiale* or *disparatum*.\nFrom merely sensible ideas of succession nothing *intellectuale* can be inferred.",[]],"reflm:4490":["*Omnitudo collectiva* (collective allness) is *completudo* (completeness); *distributiva*, *vniversalitas*.\n[*Multitudo completa* (complete multitude) is *omnitudo*.] The universality as against particularity (g exception).\nComplete *vnitas* (unity) in the *coniunctio* (conjunction) of many is *totalitas*. The totality.\n(A complete one is a *totum*.) *Completudo* in the *coniunctio* of many: *Totalitas*. *defectus*.\nIn the whole there is a taking-together: *coniunctio*, in the *compositum* a putting-together.\nA number is no *compositum*; hence a body is *totum reale*, *non ideale*.",[]],"reflm:4491":["The problematic concept of absolute totality, necessity.\nSuch concepts one can indeed think, but not determine and carry out.",[]],"reflm:4492":["*Conceptus substantiae est terminator non entium, sed statuum* (the concept of substance is a terminator of non-beings, but of states).\nThat lies in our reason; now comes the application of the same to the senses. What is the proper subject, namely that which serves the manifold [*prae*] accidents and modifications, the former without distinction of time, the latter in relation of the same, for just the same fixed point?",[]],"reflm:4493":["58'. E II 585.\nM 58:\nOnly three kinds of *respectus reales* (real relations) are possible:\n1. of the sequel to the ground, *dependentiae ab una et causalitatis ab altera parte* (of dependence on the one side and of causality on the other);\n2. of the part to the [composite whole] whole;\n3. of the *accidens* to the substance.\nIn (g all) three unity arises: of subordination*, of coordination and of inherence (g of many accidents in one subject. I is the intuition of a substance.)\nAll three have their bounds:\n1. the *independens* and *absolute necessarium*;**\n2. the *totalitas absoluta* (*completa*. [*Mundus*] *infinitum*), i.e. a synthesis which is not possible [further] greater;\n3. the *substantiale*.\n[Du] The first concept indicates how things belong through one another, the second: to one another, the third: in one another. All three are *termini*. The first [*a priori*] the *necessarium* and its *oppositum*: the absolute or *primum contingens* (*libertas*); the second the *universitatem*: everything taken together, and its *oppositum*: no taking-together, *simplex*; the third the *substantialitas* and its *oppositum*: the mere *relatio*.\nAll three are not to be seen into; the first, because the condition of necessity or, on the other hand, all necessity is lacking; the second, because the *terminus* of the synthesis is lacking, and in the third the predicates are lacking. |\nM 58': |\nAll these relations are only the realized logical ones of the relation of subject and predicate, of the antecedent to the consequent and of the universality of the concept of the subject. Subject and predicate with *est* appended behind signifies *existere*. The *identitas*: necessity. Or only the universality: necessity, the particularity: contingency.\nM 58:\n*(g One can also think a subordinated division.)\n**(g It is not yet clear that the first ground (e.g. freedom) is something necessary.)",[]],"reflm:4494":["All outer appearances are *phaenomena substantiata* (substantiated phenomena), because one treats them as substances.",[]],"reflm:4495":["That something is a subject and no predicate (of the impression, *apparentia*) of another, we can only through the *verbum activum* (active verb) »I«, thus through consciousness, cognizing; therefore *corpora*, *h.e.* (that is) *externe apparentia*, *sunt phaenomena substantiata*, *h.e. permanentia substrata ceteris phaenomenis* (bodies, that is, outer appearances, are substantiated phenomena, that is, persisting substrata for the remaining phenomena).",[]],"reflm:4496":["Around and \nThree *principia*. 1. [Each] In everything actual is the relation of a substance to the accident (*inhaerentia*); 2. of the ground to the sequel (*dependentia*); 3. of the parts and of the composition (*compositio*).\nThere are therefore three presuppositions: of the subject, of the ground and of the parts; and three real *modi*: of *insitio*, of subordination and of *compositio*. Hence also three first *principia*: 1. subject, which is no predicate; 2. ground, which is no sequel; 3. unity, which is not (g in itself) composed.",[]],"reflm:4497":["The community of the boundary of extension is contact or the impenetrability of the contact of matter.\nPoints cannot touch one another.",[]],"reflm:4498":["At the upper margin above the column heading:\nAll composition in a body cannot be removed. |\nFor the body is possible only according to the determinations of space, i.e. [the part] so that each constituent part of the same takes up a space. All composition in a *phaenomeno substantiato* is not possible to remove.",[]],"reflm:4499":["Above, beside and \nThe substantiality of the parts of a body is only respective; namely each part exists without inhering in the other. In themselves, however, they are not substances, but their *phaenomena*. The *phaenomenon* of each substance, however, if it consists in the filling of space, is possible only through the condition of space, namely only according to laws of continuity and not through the composition out of the simple, but at all times as a magnitude, [which] whose parts are all again magnitudes.",[]],"reflm:4500":["The sentence of Leibniz, that bodies are *phaenomena*, offers nothing other than that the idea of body is *sensitiva*, whose intellectual substratum is unknown, i.e. that it expresses nothing but a concept that arises from the relation of unknown substances to the sensitive way of cognizing. If this is held to be intellectual, it is called a *phaenomenon substantiatum*. If this is avoided, however, bodies as *phaenomena* [sunt in] do not consist of simples, i.e. according to the concept of space, in dividing [non terminus] the division is without end. But conceived intellectually it consists of monads. But this thesis does not influence the consequences, i.e. is not a principle of empirical cognition. but only indicates that the nexus of the soul with bodies is not objectively, but only according to the cognitive form, different from the nexus of the soul with simple substances in general. With the sensitive form removed the intellectual remains through |\npre-established harmony. And so both generation and death are nothing but different representative states, and it does not demand an explanation of the nexus with heterogeneous substances, but the heterogeneity of the cognitive form. But space is the formal of *phaenomena*; if it is held for the real conception itself of the nexus of substances, it is called a *phaenomenon intellectuatum*, namely if it is extended further than to the sensitive way of cognizing.\nIf sensitive cognition is extended in its species to infinity, it nevertheless remains sensitive, and bodies will never be found empirically to consist of simples; therefore monads are of no use in physics, and in metaphysics are of negative use, so that it may be guarded against, in holding *phaenomena* for the real constitution of objects, sensitive axioms becoming as it were intellectual.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin throughout; technical terms (*phaenomenon substantiatum*, *phaenomenon intellectuatum*, *sensitiva*) left in Latin. Bracketed [sunt in] and [non terminus] carried over as in the edition, untranslated.\n- *sentitiva* in the last line is evidently a slip for *sensitiva*; rendered 'sensitive'.\n- 'cognitio' = cognition, 'cognoscendi'/'cognoscitivam' = cognizing/cognitive; 'terminus' rendered 'end'; 'status' = state.\n- The sentence 'Sed haec thesis...' has a full stop before lowercase 'verum' in the source; kept.",[]],"reflm:4501":["All composition can be canceled: namely, as much as has been composed can also be separated. Then, however, simple parts remain, if the composition has taken place from simple parts, or parts of magnitude, if it has taken place from such. The simple is opposed to magnitude, and the composite to the separated.\nBut as concerns all parts [des Zus] of magnitude, there is of the *continuum* only *omnitudo collectiva*; every magnitude contains the possibility of a composition, but is not on that account composed.\n===NOTES===\n- aufgehoben = 'canceled' (literally also 'sublated'); Größentheile = 'parts of magnitude'.\n- [des Zus] is an editorial deletion (apparently a broken-off 'Zusammensetzung'); carried over untranslated.\n- *continuum*, *omnitudo collectiva* are Latin in the original.",[]],"reflm:4502":["At the upper margin:\nIf the *phaenomena* of different substances (which are in different places) are really many substances, then all matter consists of sim|ple\nparts. The multitude of these simple parts cannot be infinite, for otherwise they would, by their distancing themselves from one another, increase their extension to infinity; and, if between two there is a finite velocity, the velocity of the surface would be infinite. If they are not substances, but only *phaenomena*, they do not consist of simple parts at all. Accordingly they are divisible to infinity, but no putative part is actually infinite. If the multitude of the parts makes up a finite number of simple substances breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- The Latin parenthesis (*quae sunt in locis diversis*) is translated, being non-technical; Substantz = substance, Menge = multitude, Zahl = number, Ausdehnung = extension.\n- The last sentence is incomplete in the source ('bricht ab' is the editor's note); the construction is left as it stands.\n- 'putative' renders angeblich.",[]],"reflm:4503":["Time and space allow only boundaries, but not of totality. The first beginning and the outermost boundary of the world are equally incomprehensible. For the first is a being and not-being at once, and both seem to indicate an absolute time and space, i.e. something that bounds and yet contains nothing.\n===NOTES===\n- totalitaet (Latin-form word) = 'totality' (cf. Allheit); unbegreiflich = 'incomprehensible' (begreifen = comprehend).\n- 'nicht der totalitaet' is elliptical; 'but not of totality' keeps the ambiguity (allow boundaries not of totality / not allow totality).\n- Grentze = boundary; begrentzt = 'bounds'; 'das erste' rendered 'the first' without specifying a referent.",[]],"reflm:4504":["Time goes beyond everything and is the form of inner sense.\n===NOTES===\n- 'goes beyond' renders geht über; it could also be 'goes over/extends over'.",[]],"reflm:4505":["The doctrines of space and time have the positive: that the propositions that go to them with regard to sensibility cannot be reformed through reason, because they are not things; that they are entirely |\ncertain and evident. But the negative: that they restrict the application to the world of sense.\n===NOTES===\n- 'go to' renders gehen auf (literal); 'they' in 'because they are not things' may refer to the propositions or to space and time; left ambiguous as in the German.\n- Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'; Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'.",[]],"reflm:4506":["With space: the place, the position, the situation, the region. The place is a point; the position is the relation of a place to the places of others; the breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- Ort = place, Lage = position, Stellung = situation, Gegend = region; Verhaltnis = relation.\n- 'the breaks off' follows the source's broken-off sentence ('die bricht ab', editor's remark).",[]],"reflm:4507":["Space is neither a thing itself, nor an actual real relation, through which one thing posits something in the other; consequently it is no concept of the understanding; for that surely has some object, it goes therefore not to the object, but subject, and indeed not the sensation, but the form of the senses.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstandesbegrif = 'concept of the understanding'; Gegenstand and obiect are both 'object' (Gegenstand / Latin-form obiect).\n- The last clause is elliptical ('goes ... not the sensation'); kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:4508":["The *axiomata* of space and time with regard to sensibility are certain and intuitive. The principles: everything is somewhere and sometime, those of shape and place are mere conditions of sensibility.\n===NOTES===\n- anschauend = 'intuitive' (cf. anschaulich); Grundsätze = 'principles'; Gestalt = shape.\n- 'those of shape and place' may attach to the principles or to the axioms; ambiguity kept.",[]],"reflm:4509":["All spaces are simultaneous and all times after one another; in space a thing is not at many places [zugleich], only after one another.\n===NOTES===\n- [zugleich] is an editorial deletion, left in German. 'after one another' renders nach einander (literal).",[]],"reflm:4510":["Are no acquired, but innate intuitions. (s Are *conceptus originarii*, not *derivativi* (*abstrahendo*).)\nSensation\nAppearance\nConcept\nHardness\nShape\nSubstance\nof the body.\nThe play. Alteration without alterable things.\n===NOTES===\n- The passage begins without a subject ('Sind'); the list is a series of separate words as in the manuscript. '(s' is the source's own sign, kept.\n- Anschauungen = 'intuitions'; Veränderung = 'alteration'; Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Empfindung = 'sensation'.",[]],"reflm:4511":["Beside and \nIs space before things? By all means. For the law of coordination is before things and lies at their ground.\nBut can space without things be sensed, or can one remark it only through things? Yes, hence empty space as an object of the senses, e.g. between planets, is impossible.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Neben und' is a locus note (position on the page; the following word is missing in the source).\n- empfindbar = 'can be sensed' (to avoid confusion with sinnlich = 'sensible'); Gegenstand = 'object'.",[]],"reflm:4512":["Beneath and \nPure space is merely the potential relation and is represented before things, but not as something actual. Empty space without filling is possible; but absolute space, as that against which creatures stand in actual relation, is impossible. For no substance is present anywhere without acting, and indeed externally; in absolute space, however, there are not correlates.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Unter und' is a locus note with a missing word, as in the source.\n- 'there are not correlates' keeps nicht correlate; it could be read 'there are no correlates'. Verhältnis = relation; rein = pure.",[]],"reflm:4513":["Space and time give nothing actual yet. Only sensation provides it. Hence real understanding is an activity, parallel to sensation.\n===NOTES===\n- giebts an die Hand is an idiom (literally 'puts it to hand'); rendered 'provides it'. wirklich = 'actual'; real = 'real'.",[]],"reflm:4514":["According to reason time is the *nexus* of *coordinatio*, according to sensibility, however, of *subordinatio*. The first and last is to be specified arbitrarily in space.\n===NOTES===\n- Vernunft = reason, Sinnlichkeit = sensibility. 'The first and last' may be neuter or refer to members of a series; kept ambiguous. willkührlich = 'arbitrarily'.",[]],"reflm:4515":["Space is the ground of the possibility of relations, indeed even of their necessity. Possibility and actuality is not distinguished in space and time. In both the part is possible only through the whole. Both are so connected that space is at all times, i.e. necessarily. Time goes to being in general, space to external being. Both are the only given grounds of synthesis without any inferences.\n===NOTES===\n- Möglichkeit = possibility, Wirklichkeit = actuality; seyn (nominalized) = 'being' (not Wesen); Schlüsse = 'inferences'; Gantze = 'whole'.\n- 'Both are so connected' (verknüpft) = connected; 'is not distinguished' follows the singular verb in the German.",[]],"reflm:4516":["Time and space are the necessary in intuition. Time expresses the whole existence of things.\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn = 'existence'; Anschauung = 'intuition'; drükt aus = 'expresses'.",[]],"reflm:4517":["We cannot use the [Zeitwort] word *est* otherwise than as a time-word. »God is« in contrast to »will be«. The predicates of possible things can be used only *in praesenti*.\n===NOTES===\n- Zeitwort is the German word for 'verb' (literally 'time-word'); the bracketed [Zeitwort] is an editorial deletion, left in German, and the second is rendered literally.",[]],"reflm:4518":["That time is the form of inner sense is to be seen from this, that one can indeed have it in thoughts, but never intuit it as something external like extension. The substances are in space, their state (*accidentia*) in time. All predicates have as copula: *est, fuit, erit*.\n===NOTES===\n- Zustand = 'state'; Ausdehnung = 'extension'; Gedanken = 'thoughts'.\n- 'like extension' may attach to 'intuit' or to 'something external'; kept.",[]],"reflm:4519":["The synthetic propositions of space do not lie in the general concept of space, any more than the chemical propositions of experience about |\ngold in the general concept of it, but are drawn from the intuition of it or found in the intuition of it.\n===NOTES===\n- Erfahrungssätze = 'propositions of experience'; Begriff = 'concept'; Anschauung = 'intuition'.\n- 'desselben' (of it) is rendered 'of it' in each case without specifying the referent.",[]],"reflm:4520":["The first cause of all existence is eternity, and the relation of the existence of contingent things to the all-comprehending existence is time. [Die erste] In so far as this cause of all existence is the cause of all things, this is omnipresence, and the relation of created things to it is space.\nHow does it come about that our sensibility contains determinations in which reason sees its own basic concepts. That is because the sensible form is possible through the same grounds that the understanding needs for comprehension.\n===NOTES===\n- [Die erste] is an editorial deletion, left in German. Daseyn = 'existence'; zufällig = 'contingent'; Bestimmungen = 'determinations'; Grundbegriffe = 'basic concepts'; Begreifung = 'comprehension'.\n- The second paragraph's first sentence has no question mark in the source; kept.",[]],"reflm:4521":["The present concept of the world serves rather to bring the idea of a substantial whole and of an isolated one into order, than to prove thereby the existence of any determinate cause.\n===NOTES===\n- itzig = 'present'; Gantzen = 'whole'; Daseyn = 'existence'; Beweis/beweisen = proof/prove; 'an isolated one' leaves the noun of 'isolirten' unspecified, as in the German.",[]],"reflm:4522":["The world (g the) that can be no part of another whole, is the world in the transcendental understanding. The world that is no part of an actual whole is the world in the physical understanding. Because the [Mögliche] possible is distinguished from the actual only in that in the |\nformer the *conditiones* are not considered in the thoroughgoing determination, a whole world that has no other *conditiones* outside itself than in the absolutely necessary being must also have no other boundaries of actuality than the inner conditions of possibility. From a world in this sense one could infer to a single cause and its all-sufficiency, further to the singleness of this world. But from the world that is not the absolute universe nothing more can be inferred than from a *compositum substantiale* in general. Finally from a mere *multitudo rerum finitarum* no more than from a single *finitum*.\nThe concept of completion is the last in the increase and arises from the concept of all, in the *synthesis* as well as the *analysis*. The boundary concept is what remains over in the diminution of the *conditionum*: e.g. A ground that is no sequel; the absolutely necessary; the absolutely (regardless of all conditions) contingent, i.e. freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- '(g the)' and [Mögliche] are the edition's deletion/insertion marks, kept; 'in transscendentalem / physischem Verstande' uses Verstand in its idiom 'in ... Verstande'; rendered with the glossary word 'understanding'.\n- Bedingung = 'condition' (Latin *conditiones* kept in Latin); Folge = 'sequel'; Wesen = 'being'; Grentze = 'boundary'; Weltall = 'universe'; Allgnugsamkeit = 'all-sufficiency'; einigkeit = 'singleness' (not Einheit); eine einige Ursache = 'a single cause'.\n- Vollendungsbegrif = 'concept of completion'; Grentzbegrif = 'boundary concept'; Zufällig = 'contingent'.",[]],"reflm:4523":["Above and below the heading »Notio mundi«:\nPrivate happiness and religion must in the human being either be derived from a generally valid inner or outer principle, if it is to agree.\n===NOTES===\n- Locus note translated; the lemma »Notio mundi« kept in Latin.\n- 'it' (sie) in 'if it is to agree' may refer to private happiness, to religion, or to both; the ambiguity is kept. 'generally valid' renders allgemeingültig; 'principle' renders princip (Grundsatz = principle also); stimmen = 'agree'.",[]],"reflm:4524":["From our concept of the world only the concept of the relation of each part of a *compositi substantialis* to the whole is brought into order. We can think no effects of a *substantiae* or *compositi* except [under presupposition] according to general rules of the nature of the substance itself or of its cause. In a whole, because the parts stand in *commercio*, the aim at each part in particular cannot subsist with that at the other, yet in such a way that their community |\nthereby agreed, or they have no community at all. The greater the number of the substances, the harder it is to bring out a concord from individual aims, until finally the unity in the concord of the substances in general must follow from the unity of the whole, and the aim at the whole and the nature laid into it for that reason can alone contain the supreme ground of the possibility of the agreement of the aims at the parts. One cannot begin from the aims at each part in order to bring out a clock through their composition. The whole must have a nature which contains general laws, according to which it is merely possible that the [parts] aim of the parts becomes concordant. The unity of the aim at the whole can alone become the ground of the harmony of the part-aims. Even in each single substance the occurrences must flow from it according to general laws, which is its nature. Merely thereby is it a substance, that the general ground of the *accidentien* lies in it itself; otherwise it would always agree with another. Nature is just that with regard to the real determinations and alterations which space is with regard to the *principii* of all possible relations according to laws of sensibility.\n(g *commercium*. Community. *nexus reciprocus*.)\n===NOTES===\n- Begriff = concept; Absicht = aim; Gemeinschaft = community; Begebenheit = occurrence; Zahl = number; Einheit = unity; Grund = ground; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Wirkungen = effects.\n- Latin genitives (*compositi substantialis*, *substantiae*, *compositi*, *principii*, *accidentien*) are kept as written; *commercium* is left untranslated (cf. Gemeinschaft in the closing line); *nexus reciprocus* = reciprocal connection.\n- Zusammenstimmung = concord, Übereinstimmung = agreement, Harmonie = harmony, stimmig = concordant; 'damit stimmete' = 'thereby agreed' (could also be 'accorded with that').\n- 'Realbestimmungen' = real determinations; 'Veränderungen' = alterations. 'Natur' = nature throughout.\n- 'die darum hineingelegte Natur': 'darum' rendered 'for that reason', which is as unspecific as the German.\n- '[unter Voraussetzung]' and '[Theile]' are bracketed words (deleted/inserted per the Academy apparatus), translated within the brackets; the '|' and the 'g' sigla are carried over unchanged.",[]],"reflm:4525":["The world is the absolute whole of possible experience. We can think an absolute world-whole quite well, only not in space and time. The absolute whole in the appearance is a contradiction.\n===NOTES===\n- Welt = world; Erfahrung = experience; Erscheinung = appearance; Wiederspruch (Widerspruch) = contradiction; Raum = space; Zeit = time.\n- 'Weltganze' rendered 'world-whole'; 'absolutganze' as 'absolute whole'.",[]],"reflm:4526":["At the very top of the page.\nA relation (*relatio*) is a determination (*determinatio*) of a being, by which (g or) it can act on another or be acted on by it. A reciprocal active relation, by which it can act as well as be acted on, |\nis possible. A *nexus* is the relation of an actually acting thing to the thing acted on. In any whole there is a *nexus*.\n===NOTES===\n- Wholly Latin; 'qvolibet' = 'quolibet', 'qva' = 'qua' (old spelling).\n- 'patiens' (accusative) rendered 'the thing acted on'; 'agentis' as 'acting thing'. 'Potest' after the | break is the text's continuation of 'qva tam agere qvam pati'.\n- 'Zuoberst auf der Seite' is the editors' locus note.",[]],"reflm:4527":["The general is not always borrowed from the particular. The [math] geometrical properties are not borrowed from the particular determinations of the figures, but these from general space. Just so with the science of number. In the divine will everything is determined according to general rules. And if I cancel the generality of the ground in the *substantia*, then it ceases to be substance. This real generality is the derivation of the partial determinations from the whole as a ground. It is then a standing *principium*. If the things are already given, then cognition goes from the particular to the general. From the ground, however, through which the things are given, the inference goes from the general to the particular, and in the whole from the *universitate* to the *individuo*.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis = cognition; Schluß = inference; Grund = ground; Bestimmung = determination; Zahlwissenschaft = science of number (Wissenschaft = science).\n- '[math]' is a bracketed (deleted) abbreviation, left as it stands; its completion is not certain.\n- *universitate*, *individuo* are in the case forms of the manuscript.\n- 'ein stehend principium': 'standing' kept literally.",[]],"reflm:4528":["Series of the understanding. The All of the same demands a supreme cause.\nSeries of the senses. The series itself always demands something preceding.\nNothing happens in the world *per fatum*, *per casum*, *per saltum*. (g Rules of) order of nature. (s *non existunt* — *non fiunt* — *non connectuntur per saltum*.)\n===NOTES===\n- Verstand = understanding; Sinn = sense (pl. 'the senses'); Ursache = cause; Welt = world.\n- 'Das All' rendered 'the All' (not Allheit, which would be 'totality').\n- *per fatum* = through fate, *per casum* = through chance, *per saltum* = through a leap; the Latin in the 's' insertion: 'do not exist / are not made / are not connected through a leap'.\n- 'Sinnenreihe': 'Sinnen-' rendered 'of the senses'.",[]],"reflm:4529":["The bounds of the appearance cannot appear. Hence no finite world can |\nbe represented to the senses. If I stretch a hand out of the world, then so far the greater world appears; the remaining space does not appear, except insofar as [I it] I put something in. Thus the appearance without object (the empty) is not possible. But an infinite time *a parte ante* is indeed necessary in the appearance, and in the concept of reason mathematical magnitude takes place not at all; but thereby the understanding cannot suffice for the complete *exposition* of sensibility.\n===NOTES===\n- Grenze = bound; Erscheinung = appearance; Gegenstand = object; Größe = magnitude; Vernunft = reason; Verstand = understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; zureichen = suffice.\n- '[ichs] ich' = a deleted 'ichs' ('ich es') followed by 'ich'; rendered '[I it] I'.\n- 'exposition' is the Latinate word in the text (cf. Erörterung); 'dabey' = 'thereby' could also be 'in that case'; 'a parte ante' = from the side of the past.\n- 'ist ... nothwendig' may be read as 'is necessary' (kept).",[]],"reflm:4530":["The *saltus spurius* is attraction at a distance. For here the *intermedia* are not necessarily *causae intermediae*; for the *intermedia* are only places.\n===NOTES===\n- *saltus spurius* = spurious leap; *intermedia* = intermediate things; *causae intermediae* = intermediate causes.\n- Orter (Örter) = places (Ort = place).",[]],"reflm:4531":["Two (g following one another) states are never immediately combined with one another. For two states are in two different points of time. But between two moments there is a time. In which states, then, was the thing during this time? [It is the] The states are the extrema of a line. There is thus an infinite series of intermediate states. (But whether I cannot already consider two states as two successive series, which are immediately connected with one another, just as 2 lines with one another?) All of this cannot be compared with the rational propositions; but the *quaestio* is also not rational, but only: which are the laws and conditions of the appearance. Meanwhile the difficulty is also not from the understanding, but from another law of sensibility, namely to regard a *succession* as a series, whose members can be counted through and which therefore could never be infinite.\n===NOTES===\n- Zustand = state; Zeit = time; Erscheinung = appearance; Verstand = understanding; Gesetz = law; Bedingung = condition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Satz = proposition.\n- verbunden = combined (Verbindung); verknüpft = connected (Verknüpfung); two German words, two English words.\n- 'succession' and *quaestio* are Latinate words in the German text (cf. Abfolge, Frage); 'extrema' = extremities.\n- 'Zeitpunkten' = 'points of time'; 'Augenbliken' = 'moments'.\n- '[Es ist die]' is a bracketed deletion, translated within the brackets.",[]],"reflm:4532":["Different power and honor\nDistributes, by way of steps, the countless hosts,\nWhich, unequally sated with the splendor of the communicated light,\nStand in long order from God to the desolate nothing.\n===NOTES===\n- Verse; the singular verb 'vertheilt' with 'Macht und Ehre' is kept as in the German, which leaves open whether 'Macht und Ehre' is subject or whether 'vertheilt' is a participle ('distributed ... the hosts').\n- 'Stufenart' rendered 'way of steps' (Art = way); 'mitgetheilt' = communicated; 'öde' = desolate (also 'empty').",[]],"reflm:4533":["There are negative or positive oppositions; with the latter [is] the combination of both makes a negative opposition, which is logical. There there is *continuitaet*; to find between magnanimity and avarice the *punctum flexus contrarii*. Another *obiect* is another direction.\n===NOTES===\n- Verbindung = combination; 'obiect' (Object) = object; 'continuitaet' = continuity (in Latinate spelling).\n- '[ist] macht' = a deleted 'ist' followed by 'macht'; the sentence is hard to construe ('bey den letzteren' could refer to the positive oppositions or to a pair).\n- 'zu finden' has no governing verb in the passage (fragment). *punctum flexus contrarii* = point of contrary flexure.",[]],"reflm:4534":["Around M § 393,\nSimple beings (as such) can not at all be parts of the world of the senses. For then they would be parts of the object of outer senses, i.e. of the extended; but the extended does not consist of simple parts. Hence every principle of life must be counted among the intelligible, consequently also the soul. Of the intelligible, however, one knows nothing except the relation of the appearances, for which it is the substratum, in the world of the senses. What it is, therefore, outside of the same (after death) is absolutely uncognizable. Upon this our ignorance is also grounded with regard to all organized beings and beings organizing matter, whose possibility, which rests on a principle of life, can not at all be discerned. Simple beings have no place in the world.\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen = being; einfach = simple; Teil = part; Object = object; Erscheinung = appearance; Welt = world; Ort = place; Materie = matter; erkennen = cognize ('unerkennbar' = uncognizable); wissen = know.\n- 'Sinnenwelt' rendered 'world of the senses'; 'einsehen' (eingesehen) rendered 'discern'.\n- 'Um M § 393' is the editors' locus note ('Um' = around).",[]],"reflm:4535":["At the very top of the page, opposite the column heading and M § 393:\nWhether the world is in empty space or not, (g is no empirical question); for no empirical conditions of determination are possible, whether a space is empty or not (in empty space no outer experience would be possible either). Whether it is possible that all matter be taken away from space through annihilation is an intellectual question; whether through motion, is a physical one, which presupposes whether [the impenetrabi] a body that is moved takes with it all matter that fills the space, or whether one is not immovable.\n===NOTES===\n- Raum = space; Welt = world; Bedingung = condition; Bestimmung = determination; Erfahrung = experience; Materie = matter.\n- '[Die Undurchdringli]' is a broken-off bracketed word (Undurchdringlichkeit, impenetrability); '[unent]' is likewise broken off, not completed.\n- 'ob nicht eine unbeweglich sey': 'eine' has no stated noun; 'one' kept. The parenthesis '(g ist keine empirische Frage)' is a later insertion per the apparatus.\n- 'Columnenüberschrift' = column heading (editors' note).",[]],"reflm:4536":["At the upper edge and next to as well as\nThe question whether the bodies outside me are something actual is answered thus: bodies outside my sensibility are no bodies (*phaenomena*), and thus they are only in the power of representation of sensing beings. Whether something outside me corresponds to these their appearances is a question of the cause of this appearance and not of the existence of that itself which appears. This existence as of an object is the representation of the interconnection with all appearances according to laws.\n===NOTES===\n- The locus note 'Am oberen Rand und neben sowie' breaks off in the source; rendered as it stands.\n- wirklich = actual; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Vorstellung = representation; Erscheinung = appearance; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Gesetz = law.\n- 'existentz' (Existenz) = existence; Dasein is not used here. 'Gegenstand' = object.\n- 'dieser ihren Erscheinungen' = 'these their appearances' (colloquial German kept).",[]],"reflm:4537":["All *commercium* of the substances is* a harmony of their activities. The ground of this harmony can therefore not be in one alone, but in the common ground of everything; the *commercium* breaks off.\n*(s the state of different substances, insofar as they flow into one another *mutuo*: *realis* or *idealis*.)\n===NOTES===\n- Grund = ground; Zustand = state; 'Gemeinschaftlichen' rendered 'common' (cf. Gemeinschaft = community elsewhere).\n- *mutuo* = mutually; *realis*/*idealis* = real/ideal; 'einfließen' = flow into; 'thätigkeiten' = activities.\n- The asterisk and the 's' sign are carried over as in the source.",[]],"reflm:4538":["*sola non successio* of several existing things is not on that account simultaneity.\nThe *commercium substantiarum* is either *absolutum* or *hypotheticum*: the unconditioned community or the conditioned. The former merely through the fact that they exist, or by means of a third. The former is a *systema arbitrarium*.\nThe *hypothetice* established *commercium* is [either *naturalis*] always *a causa extramundana*; but either [*per*] as *ordo naturae* (*mutuae dependentiae substantiarum secundum regulas generales*) or [as] *supernaturaliter*, so that there is really no *commercium naturale*. The first is *influxus physicus*, the other *harmonia idealis vel praestabilita vel occasionalis*.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin glosses: *commercium substantiarum* = commerce of substances; *absolutum*/*hypotheticum* = absolute/hypothetical; *systema arbitrarium* = arbitrary system; *hypothetice* = hypothetically; *a causa extramundana* = from an extramundane cause; *ordo naturae (...)* = order of nature (of mutual dependence of substances according to general rules); *supernaturaliter* = supernaturally; *influxus physicus* = physical influx; *harmonia idealis vel praestabilita vel occasionalis* = ideal, or pre-established, or occasional harmony.\n- 'sola non successio plurium existentium non ideo est simultaneitas' is awkward in the Latin (double 'non'); kept literally.\n- unbedingt/bedingt = unconditioned/conditioned; Gemeinschaft = community; daseyn = exist. 'Jene' is rendered 'the former' both times, as in the German.\n- '[entweder naturalis]', '[per]', '[als]' are bracketed words, translated within the brackets.",[]],"reflm:4539":["Next to and \nA cruder influx (*influxus crassior*) is that according to which a substance flowing into another pours accidents into the other, so that they are not sustained by the patient but are actuated by a foreign force. Such a passion, however, could also be feigned in the system of occasional causes. From the impossibility, however, of such a passion, and because accidents do not inhere except through a proper force, it can be concluded that substances cannot be in *commercio*, unless by the mediation of the same sustaining cause.\n===NOTES===\n- Wholly Latin; 'Neben und' is the editors' locus note, broken off.\n- *patiente* = the thing acted on ('patient'); *passio* = passion (a being acted on); *vis* = force; *accidentia* = accidents; *inhaerent* = inhere.\n- 'poterat confingi' (imperfect) rendered 'could be feigned'; 'qvem'/'qvia' = quem/quia.",[]],"reflm:4540":["Intuition (*intuitus*) is either through inner or outer sense. Pure outer intuition always has the idea of space annexed.\n===NOTES===\n- Wholly Latin; *sensum* = sense, *ideam* = idea, *spatii* = of space.\n- 'purus' rendered 'pure' (rein).",[]],"reflm:4541":["The general grounds of sensible choice are inclinations.\nThe general grounds of rational choice are principles.\n[The concept of freedom is from ei] According to theoretical principles the concept of hypothetically necessary choice, according to practical ones that of absolutely independent choice is more probable.\n===NOTES===\n- Willkühr (Willkür) = choice (not in the glossary); Grundsetze (Grundsätze) = principles; Grund = ground; Begriff = concept; Freiheit = freedom; warscheinlicher = more probable (Wahrscheinlichkeit).\n- sinnlich = sensible; vernünftig = rational (Vernunft = reason).\n- '[Der Begrif der freyheit ist aus ei]' is a broken-off deletion; 'ei' not completed.\n- The last sentence is elliptical ('that of' supplied by English syntax from the repeated 'der Begrif'; the German has only 'nach practischen der absolut unabhängigen Willkühr').",[]],"reflm:4542":["The beginning of effecting must be encountered in the world; everything, therefore, that is not free has no first beginning. Whether a being whose *subsistentia* is *derivativa* (derivative subsistence) could not be independent in its actions? *spontaneitas simplex* (simple spontaneity).",[]],"reflm:4543":["Blind is what one cannot have insight into.\nBlind necessity (fate) (g providence), blind chance.",[]],"reflm:4544":["Everything is necessary either in and for itself or externally. Contingency is only respective. But free actions of contingent substances are supposed to have neither of the two necessities.",[]],"reflm:4545":["At the lower margin:\nThe concept of conditionally necessary power of choice is only a hypothesis of theory and must be assumed in order to explain the free actions as *phaenomena*. The concept of unconditionally free power of choice is a *postulatum practicum*, which everyone actually assumes, and in which he contradicts himself if he demands of others a use of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4546":["At the upper margin:\n*libertas est vel facultatis vel status* (freedom is either of the faculty or of the state) (dogs in a kennel); *itidem est vel interna vel externa; haec est independentia a cogentibus* (likewise it is either internal or external; the latter is independence from the compelling).\n(s *Voluntas non potest cogi, sed arbitrium; hoc autem non simpliciter, sed secundum qvid.* [The will cannot be compelled, but the power of choice can; and this not simply, but in a certain respect.])",[]],"reflm:4547":["Beside and \n*libertas itaqve est vel arbitrii vel potestatis. libertas arbitrii est interna et* [*propria*] *per se talis. libertas potestatis est externe talis. libertas arbitrii est practica* [*vel transsce*] *vel transscendentalis prior est independentia a stimulis, posterior ab omni necessitatione externa. actus arbitrii simpliciter spontanei sunt originarii, secundum qvid sunt derivativi. Necessitatio arbitrii est vel per stimulos et est pathologica, vel per motiva et est practica.*\n*Spontaneitas arbitrii simpliciter talis in ente originario est necessitas actuum arbitrii independens ab omni externo, ergo est libertas.*\n(Freedom is therefore either of the power of choice or of power. Freedom of the power of choice is internal and [proper] such in itself. Freedom of power is such externally. Freedom of the power of choice is practical [or transce] or transcendental; the former is independence from stimuli, the latter from all external necessitation. Acts of the power of choice that are simply spontaneous are original, those in a certain respect are derivative. Necessitation of the power of choice is either through stimuli and is pathological, or through motives and is practical.\nSpontaneity of the power of choice simply such in an original being is necessity of the acts of the power of choice independent of everything external, hence it is freedom.)",[]],"reflm:4548":["Beside and \nFreedom is twofold: either of the power of choice or of power. The first is internal, the second is merely external.\nFreedom of the power of choice (g *arbitrium liberum*) is *independentia a stimulis* and is called practical freedom. Opposed to it stands the *arbitrium brutum necessitatum a stimulis*. (The *arbitrium liberum* [is either] has either *spontaneitatem practicam* or *transscendentalem*.)\nThe *necessitatio arbitrii bruti* is pathological, that of the *arbitrii liberi* is [intellectual] practical. The *causae impulsivae* of the former are stimuli, those of the latter *motiva*. The *actus arbitrii liberi* are either *originarii* or *derivativi*; the former is transcendental, the latter merely practical freedom.",[]],"reflm:4549":["Above and \nFreedom consists in the (g subjective) independence of the power of choice from everything that flows in internally and externally upon our senses. Right is the objective independence of our power of choice from that of others, i.e., the limitation of each power of choice through the condition of reciprocal agreement and the necessity of the actions that flow from these conditions. The wisest and kindest human being, who aimed everything at our best, does not on that account have a right over us.\n(s One must also not transform the title of right into that of kindness and religion. The latter is supposed to serve only for execution.)",[]],"reflm:4550":["Beside and. \nThat something pleases [or] gratifies human beings preeminently, for that he cannot do anything; but that he lets himself be pleased, is to be ascribed to himself.\nHence he who is by natural disposition malicious reproaches himself for it, because the blame falls not on what is innate, but on the lack of the will.\nIt seems that a human being must give an account to the spirit that is in him. He must therefore surely have a faculty to obey its command or not. This latter is freedom. The soul senses, the mind judges [a poster] or assesses *a posteriori*, the spirit *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4551":["In the judgment of actions that have happened, [impute] we nevertheless presuppose that they all have their determining (though not (g yet by others) determined) grounds, and we impute them, even though they may flow from the innate character of the human being; indeed even: we impute them only all the more, partly because one |\nnevertheless knows that everyone has a higher power of choice, under which this character itself stands, partly because everyone nevertheless acts according to his own pleasure and inclination and thus not against his own pleasure, whether he does good or evil, and has insight that according to the rule of the higher will the punishments and rewards fit such a character, and he thus recognizes himself as worthy of them, since he can divest himself of himself, and hence also of his inclinations.",[]],"reflm:4552":["Around M § 770:\nThe question is whether there is an organically forming nature (epigenesis) or only one that forms mechanically and chemically.\nIt seems: a spirit belongs to this, on account of the unity of the relation of all parts, according to their generation, to each individual one. But is there not also in animals and plants a spiritual enlivening being. In such a way one would have to assume an enlivening spirit even in the first chaos, in order to explain the various animals, which now can only propagate themselves.\nOf that breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4553":["It is not clear that the human soul could not be generated from that of the parents, although a simple substance. There is no need for materialism.",[]],"reflm:4554":["Above and \nFirst is the question: whether the human being or merely [the same] the soul will live after death, and in what the identity of the substance and |\nof the person consists. [With the human being] Whether the future life depends merely on the body or the soul. Resurrection. Second, if the problem concerns only the future life of the human soul: whether it lives merely after death (g contingently) or is immortal (g necessarily) (generality, eternity). The first by means of reason from the ends of God, the second from the nature of the soul: a. from particular observation, b. from the nature of a spirit in general.\nGrounds of the problem are either explanation of the *phaenomena* (g speculative) (e.g., appearance of spirits) or the concern of the human being: that of self-love or of morality.\nEpicurus held it necessary for philosophers to decide nothing about it, so that 1. the motives would be purely moral, 2. no superstitious pretense would produce false terrors and hopes. This is grounded on too high an opinion of humanity. [The Stoi] Socrates had considered the future state merely problematically. The Stoics in the beginning also abstracted from it. Without it the order of nature does not agree with the moral order, and well-being and well-conduct are separated. Christian law makes it the main point of view of all motives of this life.",[]],"reflm:4555":["Life does not belong to mere matter. The life appropriate to a certain nature. Freedom. Animal (animalic), spiritual life.",[]],"reflm:4556":["Above, beside and \nLife consists in the inner sufficiency of the *spontaneitas* for representations and desires; but under external hindrances this substance can sleep, although it lives. The body does not contain the condition of the possibility of the life of the soul or even of one of its |\nactivities, but is a hindrance, to the overcoming of which the soul is nevertheless bound. The disposition of the body is a diminution of this hindrance; hence thinking is all the freer breaks off.\n(g because we can carry out the experiences only in the body, they will prove more the dependence than the independence.)",[]],"reflm:4557":["Above and \nI cannot say: I know that there is another life; but: I believe it. This much I know, that no one can prove there is no other life. One also cannot say that one believes there is no other life; but: one can only not believe that there is one. This indicates more a still enduring lack of insight than an insight of the opposite. The hope of another world is a necessary hypothesis of reason with regard to ends and a necessary hypothesis of the heart with regard to morality. It is therefore practically posited on secure grounds, but theoretically obscure and uncertain. Beyond this world one can know only what is incumbent on us in this one.",[]],"reflm:4558":["He who recognizes morality as important, and therefore a holy law and a just judgment together with a kind government, is easily convinced of the future. He to whom this seems to have only a conditioned necessity cannot be convinced at all. For why should the enjoyment of a life that comes to nothing but mere enjoyment be continued even after death?",[]],"reflm:4559":["That there is a being after death that is conscious of all our determi|nations\nof life and ascribes them to itself as past does not yet prove that this being is I. Here it seems that the consciousness must be interrupted, at least yet continued through the dull interim at awakening. The inner sense must endure uninterrupted [although] (according to sensation), although the consciousness according to reflection is interrupted. Thus the constant personality in this life, notwithstanding all changes, proves that the nature of the soul survives the body. Now the I cannot be a suffering sensation. Hence it is a self-active one and does not depend on the body.",[]],"reflm:4560":["Another world either in the cosmographic or cosmological sense, according to region or to kind. The latter either objective or subjective. The transition either gradually or suddenly. Community with this world or not. Ghosts.",[]],"reflm:4561":["Personality is either lasting (continuous) or interrupted. Sleep of the soul.",[]],"reflm:4562":["If in the sequel someone became conscious of himself and also became conscious of all such actions as we remember, would he on that account be I myself? Thus the identity of the person rests not on the agreement of the apperception, but on the continuation of the same, even if it should be among obscure representations.",[]],"reflm:4563":["*Mundus* (world) is either *aspectabilis* or *concupiscibilis*. If it is [sens] as *sensibilis* aspectable, then so far it is also (g sensitive) concupiscible; if it is |\nconsidered as *intelligibilis*, then it is also *intellectualiter* concupiscible, not according to the appearance and feelings, but concepts. *Mundus moralis est intelligibilis qvoad perfectionem* (the moral world is intelligible as regards perfection) and is in *union* with the *sensibilis*, which however is separated by death. With the ceasing of the (g present) sensibility we come, to be sure, out of a *mundo aspectabili* into another, but the *mundus intelligibilis* endures, as well with regard to the substance (*noumenon*) as also with regard to the reciprocal determination of the intelligences.",[]],"reflm:4564":["Theology is either philosophical or revealed; the former is either transcendental or natural, just as religion (practical) is either supernatural or natural. (s Theology is either rational or revealed; the former either philosophical or common. the former either transcendental or natural; he who contends that there is none but the former, is a deist; he who the latter (the highest intelligence), is a theist; he who none but the rational, also a naturalist.)\n(s The natural is either metaphysics or physicotheology.)",[]],"reflm:4565":["Of a most real being it is not more comprehensible that it exists than of a limited one; but from its presupposition it is easier to derive limited beings. Accordingly there is here a neces|sity,\nto assume such a being, for the sake of the grounds of speculative reason. It is not easier to have insight into the necessity of a completely holy will [than the] and the connection of the same with moral perfection than of a will that is limited; but we need it for morality. In such a way both proofs are valid for the subject, which is also enough for the supreme ground of actions.",[]],"reflm:4566":["Unless a most perfect being is proved, I have no determinate concept of its properties. If not only a single original being is determined, I have no concept of the number of them and of their properties. Likewise of the best world as the ground of the judgment of what lies ahead for the rational creatures in the world.",[]],"reflm:4567":["We can derive from the concept of no thing its necessity, but certainly from the conditions of necessity the concept; for necessity is morality.",[]],"reflm:4568":["329'. E II 1236. 1630.\nM 329:\nThe subjective conditions of human reason are the *postulata* of its use and not *axiomata*.\n(g absolutely necessary is the assumption of a thing in the subjective sense, without which I cannot, according to the law [my] of the general human reason, have any concept of the possibilities of things in general.\nThat the sensitive conditions only affect the cognition of the present world serves only for this, that we not set against the concepts of reason (which are needed on other grounds concerning God) dogmatic hindrances.)\nM 329':\n(g This necessity from subjective grounds of the general human reason shows itself also in this, that we can derive the origin of the contingent from a will that acts and chooses in accordance with the cognition of many sorts of possible, the more easily because cognition and will are what is truly active in us and it is also everywhere needed to regard ends as given, so that our actions may be in agreement with the world.)",[]],"reflm:4569":["Everything possible is considered *in respectu* toward everything possible (through either-or) and lies thus as a member of the division in a whole, which contains all *data* for it. Possibility in every aim (absolute, simply possible) requires breaks off.\nThe transcendental proof is a necessary *hypothesis* of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:4570":["All our possibility presupposes *data*, [and the] either for all things with the indeterminacy of each, or a given *principium* of relation with the indeterminacy of a particular one, e.g. In space triangles are possible. The logical relation is the principle of contradiction, that of the *phaenomena* space and time, that of things and their real relations are forces. From the former one cannot infer to the latter. The principle of contradiction is usable only negatively, namely to cognize the impossible through contradiction. but positively: the possible, it presupposes too much, although it is also true thus.",[]],"reflm:4571":["Whether the gospel has so improved theology?\nThe idea of the highest perfection and of a general and immediate ruler is needful from need, but not necessary from reason.",[]],"reflm:4572":["The absolute proposition: if something is God, then it exists, expresses absolute necessity. Hence I cannot say: if something is a sun, then it exists. The absolute proposition that is to express necessary existence must be analytic, hence: if something is absolutely necessary, then it exists.",[]],"reflm:4573":["We cannot infer from the *data* of experience to the existence of any being and its properties, which in respect of magnitude is a mere ideal and surpasses all concepts of experience.",[]],"reflm:4574":["Everything can be possible only through one. For through many together that is not possible which is through one, who unites their forces.\nThe proof from concepts: either of God or of the world in general; the first: from the concept of a most perfect the necessity, and the 2nd: to derive that from this.",[]],"reflm:4575":["The positive concept of God is easy. The negative serves for correction. analogy.",[]],"reflm:4576":["Our cognition of God is for the most part negative, i.e. to determine the prac|tical\nas well as theoretical boundaries of our theological cognition, as also to take heed against errors, as well logically as practically harmful. Presumption, curiosity. For all our cognition of God is only an investigation of what might belong in the ideal of the highest perfection. Now we cannot derive from our idea of the highest perfection everything that pertains to the thing itself; rather the material *principia* must be given through experience, and the form serves only to separate off what contradicts perfection.",[]],"reflm:4577":["M330:\n(g transcendental theology.)\nThe concept terminating all things is that of a necessary being; of an original being or first of all beings, of the highest being: insofar as it has no ground above itself, of the all-sufficient or being of all beings: insofar as it has no ground beside itself, also nothing independent outside itself, consequently of a sole being. It is the question whether of these three concepts one posits all the rest. The first two concepts we need, because we need a *terminus* of subordination. These *qvaestiones* are transcendental and concern merely the subordination of our concepts. The second, of an origin of all things cognized *a priori*, of the constitution of the cause, whether it has understanding or not, is metaphysical and presupposes analogies with known causes. The concept of the original being |\nM 330': |\ngoes to the beings and mere possibilities, that of the cause to actualities, and causality refers at all times to contingencies.\nM 330:\n(g Natural theology: a perfect spirit according to understanding and will. Namely in relation to nature.)",[]],"reflm:4578":["Cosmotheology or physicotheology. The latter is empirical.",[]],"reflm:4579":["God as the highest intelligence: fundamental concept of natural theology. Relation to our concerns and ends. *Conceptus terminator* [*stator*], *comprehensor*, *definitor*; that which determines the limitations of all things; *omnium determinator* in the subordination, coordination and conception, because reason goes only *a universali ad particularia*.\n(g in itself only the all sufficient is determined: *conprehensor*.) What belongs to the concept of God.",[]],"reflm:4580":["The transcendental concept is needed, not the transcendental proof; likewise that one have insight: one can assert nothing heterodox with ground. The necessity of the divine existence [either] as a necessary hypothesis either of the mere concepts of the possible or of the experiences in this world, likewise as hypothesis of morality. The absolute necessity cannot be proved.\n(g The proof of the existence of God is not apodictic, but hypothetical *sub hypothesi logica* and [*morali*] *practica*.)\nThe *hypothesis* which is necessary in relation to the laws of pure reason is *originaria*; that which is necessary in relation to experience is *conditionalis sive relativa*. The first is necessary, the second needed; the former *originaria*, the latter *subsidiaria*, e.g. for the explanation of the order in the world *suppletoria*.",[]],"reflm:4581":["Everything possible stands in a thoroughgoing interconnection. To be possible means properly as much as: to accord. All possibility has *data*, but the form of the same consists in the accordance. Hence thoroughgoing possibility is properly an accordance with everything.",[]],"reflm:4582":["M 330:\nThe need of reason to cognize a highest being is that of a necessary hypothesis of the use of the same: 1. of pure reason. 2. of the empirical, both speculative; 3. of the practical. (s Hence 1. transcendental theology; 2. Natural: cosmotheology, physicotheology; 3. Moral theology. All of this: 1. to determine the concept of the highest being, 2. to demonstrate its existence. |\nM 330': |\nAll this cognition is a belief, as always when we go back to first causes. Transcendental theology alone is deistic; the natural alone is anthropomorphistic; the moral alone is not sufficiently secured against intrusions. Transcendental theology secures against them.)\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen = being; Daseyn = existence; Glauben = belief; Erkentnis = cognition; Gebrauch = use; bestimmen = determine; Hypothesis = hypothesis.\n- Die Bedürfnis: feminine article kept as written. »derselben« (use of the same) could refer to Vernunft or Bedürfnis; left as in German.",[]],"reflm:4583":["[On the existence of God toward the end] *conceptus definitor*: which determines everything through ends; *terminator*: which begins; *comprehensor*: which encompasses everything.\n===NOTES===\n- befasset = encompasses (not begreifen/comprehend); Zweke = ends.\n- »der« is masculine and could be read as the concept or as a person; left ambiguous with »which«.",[]],"reflm:4584":["The practical ground of belief in God can be sufficient; yet speculative ones are nevertheless necessary *in subsidium*, in order to secure the former against false rationalizing.\n===NOTES===\n- hinreichend = sufficient (not zureichend); vernünfteley = rationalizing (vernünfteln).\n- Grund = ground; Glaube = belief.",[]],"reflm:4585":["1. pathological origin of theology; 2. speculative, rationalizing; 3. Moral. There are also such different religions. [The 2nd is deism.] God is in all these cases only a necessary hypothesis of the use of reason with regard to its own happiness or speculation or morality.\n===NOTES===\n- »seiner eigenen«: the masculine/neuter possessive does not agree with Vernunft (fem.); it may refer to Gebrauch. Rendered »its own«, ambiguous.\n- Glükseeligkeit = happiness; Vernünftelnder = rationalizing.",[]],"reflm:4586":["See p. 336. Contingency of nature and *ens supramundanum* make theology necessary.\n===NOTES===\n- Zufalligkeit = contingency (zufällig = contingent).",[]],"reflm:4587":["At the upper margin, \nad p: 330. The idea of a highest being is [a] the necessary presupposition for the completion of our cognition of actual things through reason, as of a first and necessary one as well, and for the sake of all relations: as of the being of all beings, of the being that encompasses everything in itself, [so that] because through it alone something determinate can be cognized. We infer from the existence of God to the greatest perfection of the world and from this to the existence of God. This goes thus. We must assume a supreme understanding and will, in order to explain the contingencies of the world, and both together with power and duration in the highest degree and perfect unity, because without such nothing determinate can be cognized.\n===NOTES===\n- schließen = infer; Verhältnis = relation; Macht = power (not Kraft); Vollkommenheit = perfection.\n- »wirklicher Dinge«: wirklich = actual. The syntax of »als eines ersten und nothwendigen, als auch um…« is left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:4588":["If one wants to prove the existence of God from empirical grounds, there arises the strange circle that we do not indeed trust ourselves to infer from the perfections that we become aware of in the world that it is the best among all possible, but first: that the cau|se\nof the same is the most perfect among all possible, consequently also the effect.\nIf we want to infer the [existence] properties of God from concepts, yet in such a way that we assume some existence through experience, then we infer from this existence to something necessary in itself, just as if this did not have to be clear of itself; and, since this is not clear of itself through the concept that we have of it, we nevertheless infer from this problematic concept [to] to such properties through which it can be only a single one, namely all-sufficiency. Now, however, since the necessity of existence can be inferred only from conditions that can belong to one thing alone, one must also be able to reverse the inference, consequently be able to infer the existence of God from mere concepts without an empirical *principium*.\n===NOTES===\n- schließen = infer (Schlus = inference); eigenschaften = properties (Eigenschaft, not Beschaffenheit); Allgnugsamkeit = all-sufficiency.\n- »vor sich selbst« rendered »of itself«; also »for itself« possible. »Ur|sache« line break kept as »cau|se«.",[]],"reflm:4589":["Above and \nWe have need of the concept of God as a supreme ground of things through freedom. 1. In order to explain the origin of contingent beings, and second: to determine the final ends and hopes of rational beings. The former in the series of speculation, the latter of praxis. The former serves only on account of the beginning of nature. The occurrences of nature themselves must not be explained from it.\n===NOTES===\n- »Über und « is an incomplete locus note, as in the source.\n- Wesen = being; Zweke = ends; Begebenheit = occurrence (Naturbegebenheiten).",[]],"reflm:4590":["Above and \nInfinite reality is the *substratum* of all possibility. The universal foundation. If all negations are limitations, then no thing is possible except through another, which it presupposes, except the *ens realissimum*. [The infinite time of] The time that comprehends everything, the space that contains everything, the all-sufficient thing.\n===NOTES===\n- Schranken = limitations; begreifend = comprehending; enthaltend = containing; allgnugsam = all-sufficient.\n- »ausser« rendered »except« (could also be »outside«).",[]],"reflm:4591":["On the outer margin \nAtheism.\n1. That there is no original being at all: Epicurus.\n2. That it is the world itself or the world a modification of it.\n3. That it is, according to blind fatality, cause of the things.\n===NOTES===\n- Urwesen = original being; »Epicur« rendered Epicurus.",[]],"reflm:4592":["Above, at the outer margin next to and \n∗ In *theologia naturalis* the *ens summum* (g determined) is considered as the cause of nature (g of our world); consequently, since we know causality only through forces that are given to us in experience [so] and these cannot be encountered *proprie* in the highest being (*anthropopathos*), we will be able to think of God only their relation to the effects, which the understanding cognizes, *proprie*, but the way in which they act *improprie*, and, in that we separate off what has been borrowed here from the nature of the things of the world and think the absolute of the same negatively, we must think *theoprepos*; consequently we will in *theologia naturalis* cognize God according to analogy from nature in general. *theologia naturalis vel cosmologia vel physico-theologia*.\nWe infer from this world to another, because it does not agree with the greatest perfection alone; and nevertheless one wants to infer from it to the existence of the most perfect being and afterward from this and its perfections to the other world.\n∗(g all proof of the existence of the *ens summum* is to be conducted only from pure concepts of reason, consequently *a priori* (Wolf). This proof breaks off.)\n===NOTES===\n- Footnote sign written ∗ so as not to collide with italics markup. kennen = know; erkennen = cognize; Kräfte = forces; Verstand = understanding.\n- The position of »von Gott« in »von Gott denken konnen« is retained in the clause order as far as English allows; the syntax of the long sentence is awkward in the original.",[]],"reflm:4593":["On the inner margin \nin natural theology God as cause of this world, consequently merely his relation and he himself according to analogy *per eminentiam*.\n===NOTES===\n- Verhaltnis = relation.",[]],"reflm:4594":["On the inner margin \nCosmotheology is built upon the proposition: the first cause of a contingent thing is so from freedom, not from the necessity of its nature; first mover, teleological.\n===NOTES===\n- Satz = proposition; zufellig = contingent; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.",[]],"reflm:4595":["Natural theology is either cosmotheology or physicotheology.\n===NOTES===\n- None beyond standard terms.",[]],"reflm:4596":["It is not so easy to prove the existence of God theoretically, but easy and natural to believe it.\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn = existence; glauben = believe.",[]],"reflm:4597":["Above and \nTo cosmotheology belongs the relation of God to the world according to causality. 1. [princ] *systema inhaerentiae vel emanationis vel liberae productionis*; the latter *vel tanqvam architecti vel tanqvam creatoris*. 2. *praesentia vel commercii vel conservationis*. The first is *anima mundi*. 3. *duratio non in tempore, qvemadmodum praesentia non in spatio et creatio non in tempore*. Moral theology: *sapientia* (g *benignitas*) *et sanctitas, finis*∗, *summum bonum*, *Optimismus*. *Summa felicitas cum summa dignitate ipsius. Origo mali. decreta omnia non absoluta, sed hypothetica. Gubernatio et idea gubernationem determinans: providentia*.\n∗(g *non est impellens, sed complacentiae immanentis*.)\n===NOTES===\n- Latin left untranslated as technical terms and notes; Kant's spellings (qvemadmodum, etc.) kept. Footnote sign written ∗.\n- »Über und « incomplete locus note, as in the source.",[]],"reflm:4598":["Also in the doctrine of nature the causes are put forward only as necessary |\nhypotheses, but not as absolutely necessary, because another cause of the same *phaenomenon* would still be possible.\n===NOTES===\n- Naturlehre = doctrine of nature (Lehre = doctrine); schlechthin = absolutely; möglich = possible.",[]],"reflm:4599":["Prudence and morality cannot hang together except merely through the hypothesis of a third being, which is powerful and benevolent and at the same time holy and just.\n===NOTES===\n- Klugheit = prudence; Sittlichkeit = morality; Wesen = being; zusammenhängen is related to Zusammenhang (interconnection).",[]],"reflm:4600":["The causality of the entire series of the contingent, hence of the arising and beginning of the same, is not physical, but practical, i.e. from freedom.\n(s Freedom is the supreme condition of the most real being as cause, and the causality in the same is through understanding and will.)\n(s *Primus motor ex libertate. intelligentia.*)\n===NOTES===\n- Reihe = series; realesten = most real; Verstand = understanding.\n- »derselben« in »bey derselben« is ambiguous between freedom and the being; rendered »the same«.",[]],"reflm:4601":["All composition and limitation is contingent; the cause of the same is itself contingent and cannot consist in anything other than a choice.\n===NOTES===\n- Einschrankung = limitation (as does Schranke in the glossary); Wahl = choice; zufallig = contingent.",[]],"reflm:4602":["The [empirical] cosmotheological proof has sufficiency as *principium* of the empirical use of reason with regard to all possible order in the world and also of the first beginning according to the analogy of experiences.\nThereby also only an analogue of our freedom is inferred.\n===NOTES===\n- zulenglichkeit (sic) = sufficiency; Erfahrungen = experiences; schließen = infer.",[]],"reflm:4603":["Causality (g of things) according to the necessity of nature gives substantial unity of cause and effect, because the removal of the latter would remove the nature of the former, hence its existence.\n===NOTES===\n- Aufhebung = removal (aufheben = remove); »ihr« rendered »its«, referring to the former (Ursache).",[]],"reflm:4604":["Analogy with the human understanding.\n*via reductionis* to separate off all sensibility and its (g limiting conditions) limitations.\nbut if we leave out the conditions of sensibility, then all cognition *in concreto* falls away; accordingly we can never think God *proprie* through cognition that is borrowed from nature, but only *secundum analogiam*.\nThe idea breaks off. |\nThe two words stand to the right of the end of § 868; beneath, written in pencil, the word: |\nPlato.\n===NOTES===\n- The last lines are editorial description (Bleifeder = pencil), translated as given; »Die beiden Worte« presumably refers to words not in this passage.\n- Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Schranken = limitations; Bedingungen = conditions; Erkenntnis = cognition.",[]],"reflm:4605":["*Ens originarium non est natura originaria, sed intelligentia originaria.*\n===NOTES===\n- Entirely Latin; left as is.",[]],"reflm:4606":["*theologia transscendentalis*;\n*theologia naturalis*∗: *determinatio per praedicata naturae — psychologica*;\n∗(g *vel ex principiis transscendentalibus vel physicis*)\n*theologia moralis, determinatio et conclusio ex praedicatis et argumentis moralibus*.\n===NOTES===\n- Entirely Latin; footnote sign written ∗.",[]],"reflm:4607":["At the very top of the page, but \n*Acqviescentia in semet ipso est principium omnis caussalitatis entis summi et est beatitudo.*\n===NOTES===\n- »Zuoberst auf der Seite, aber « is an incomplete editorial locus note; Kant's spellings (Acqviescentia, caussalitatis) kept.",[]],"reflm:4608":["Moral theology: God as *summum bonum, legislator et finis*.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin terms kept in Latin.",[]],"reflm:4609":["The *principium* of the inner possibility of a [Wes] thing is the essence. This essence according to (g the determination of) its causality is nature. The condition of nature as a first cause of the contingent is freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen = essence here (glossary: being; essence); [Wes] is Kant's deleted false start before »Dinges«.\n- Bestimmung = determination; Möglichkeit = possibility; Zufalligen = the contingent.",[]],"reflm:4610":["On the outer margin, beginning at the top above the page number, down to \nBeing: *Deus* (g *ut*) *summa possibilitas* (highest possibility).\nNature: *Deus ut summa perfectio*.\nFreedom:\n*summum bonum* (highest good).\n*Perfectiones sunt facultates ad summum finem;\nbonum est intentio summi finis.*\n*summum bonum est unio summae felicitatis cum dignitate sive receptivitate ipsius obiectiva, qvae tanta est, qvanta in felicitate universali practice est determinabilis, h.e. qvantum per consensum practicum cum principiis felicitatis universalibus* licet esse felicem.*\n*\n(g as well in relation to the ends of human nature (g as) of human beings.)\nMoral perfection, insofar as it is the determination of the divine will or is determining with regard to creatures; i.e. [He is the *principium*] His will is just, and he wills that human beings be just. Moral theology is the right one, which makes good.\nGod in this theology in threefold person or quality as the (g holy) lawgiver, as the benevolent ruler and the just judge.",[]],"reflm:4611":["Morality is the agreement of the free power of choice with the end of humanity and of human beings in general, namely with necessary conditions of the universal ends of humanity and of human beings.",[]],"reflm:4612":["Above and \nWe ought to have as much share in universal happiness as we contribute to it. Morality is itself comprehensible only from the idea of such a will.",[]],"reflm:4613":["Luck and fate, in a word: the advantages from nature, can never have the worth that those under correct rules have.",[]],"reflm:4614":["Opposite the concluding sentence of M § 893:\n*Libertas voluntatis* — *Perfectio* vel *naturalis*: *omnipotentia*, *sapientia* (g [*naturalis*] *artificialis*) [*Sanctitas*], vel *moralis*: *Sanctitas*, *benignitas*, *Justitia* = *Sapientia*.",[]],"reflm:4615":["The holiness, benevolence and justice of the divine will. Sum: wisdom.",[]],"reflm:4616":["At the very top of the page. \nThe world considered *intellectualiter* has no beginning; not because it has lasted an infinite time, for then it would be considered *sensitive*, but [it] because in this respect it is not weighed in time at all.\n*Sensibilis mundus* (sensible world) has no beginning, because a first beginning is impossible. He who considers the world *sensitive* cognizes in it no boundaries.",[]],"reflm:4617":["The world, as it begins, cannot be thought according to grounds of reason, and without beginning not according to grounds of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:4618":["Above and \nIf we look into the world as world of sense, then in the *regressu* of appearances we will find no beginning. Now, however, these appearances are not actual things; thus it is properly not said: the world is (g in itself) without beginning, but rather: the appearance (in relation to us) has no beginning. In the appearances there is no absolute boundary of [comp] increase or decrease.\nThe world has an origin, but not as appearance, but rather as being in itself.",[]],"reflm:4619":["Above and \nThe causal relation of the intellectual to the *sensitive* and the determination of sensibility according to merely intellectual principles or conversely cannot be seen into by us at all. e.g. The first ground of composition, the first action through freedom, the origin and beginning of the world.",[]],"reflm:4620":["Causality with regard to substance: creation and preservation;\nwith regard to (g the course of the world) the arrangement: providence and government.",[]],"reflm:4621":["At the upper margin and at the outer side margin \nThe physicotheological concept seeks for the contingent the necessary determinate, the moral for the necessary the necessary, in order to give it reality [the]. It is not necessary to go to the first ground in speculation. But it is necessary, for the sake of duties, to go back to the supreme lawgiver, ruler and judge.\nBelief of reason, which is necessary, is unavoidable hypothesis of a moral being.\nThe subjective grounds of the need of our pure as well as applied reason increase this obligation.",[]],"reflm:4622":["The good is the harmony with the order of nature and of the world by means of freedom. Hence God by means of nature is author of the good and at the same time the one who hinders evil.",[]],"reflm:4623":["The moral *concursus* occurs through the removal of the hindrances of our inner morality and not through determination of our actions.",[]],"reflm:4624":["At the very top of the page. \nGod is the author of the order of nature, freely acting beings in many respects from the course of nature.",[]],"reflm:4625":["Whether the Danube runs toward the east for the reason that the Turks should not flood the realm, and the Fahlun mine was set in the land of Dalecarlia because this is a diligent people?",[]],"reflm:4626":["The analysis of pure reason gives nothing but distinctness in the representations that we already have.\nThe synthetic propositions go to the conditions of judgments through pure reason and are subjective. Thus pure reason cannot otherwise than in application [to that which] to senses teach us to cognize objects.",[]],"reflm:4627":["Metaphysics has neither the utility in use nor the splendor of invention and strength of insight of reason that mathematics has; but its end is a universally human end, and thereby it precedes all theoretical knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4628":["Metaphysics serves science only negatively, to keep off the errors of pure reason; but it is no small service that it the most sublime and dr breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4629":["The fundamental concepts of all our cognition are [the quality (what there is) the quantity (how many times)] 1. being in general (g *qvidditas*), second: how something is, third: how many times it is.\nThat through which things are given is sensation; how they are given, pure intuitions.\nThrough the understanding either things are thought or only their sensible representations. Number requires space and time for its intuiting representation.\nThe logical form is precisely that for the [concepts] representations of the understanding of a thing, which space and time are for the appearances of the same: namely, the former contain the positions to order them. The representation [be], through which we assign to an object its peculiar logical position, is the real concept of the understanding and pure: e.g. Something that I can at all times use only as subject; something from which I must infer hypothetically to a *conseqvens* etc etc.\nBecause our sensations get a determinate position* in space and time, they attain a function among the appearances; but the position in space and in time is determined through the neighborhood of other sensations in the same; e.g. upon the state of my sensations, which have something in common with the previous ones, the other follows; the sensation of a resistance is at the same time in the same space combined with heaviness.\nThrough the determination of the logical position the representation gets a function among the concepts. e.g. *Antecedens*, *conseqvens*. Yet the sensitive function is the ground of the intellectual one.\n*(g a determinate position is distinguished from the arbitrary one.)",[]],"reflm:4630":["Propositions can be cognized *a priori*, but their principles *a posteriori*.",[]],"reflm:4631":["(g Space is a *datum*.)\nThe logical actions are *actus*, through which we posit and order the *data* into representations of things respectively against one another. Thereby the representations get logical functions. The real function [is] consists in the way in which we posit a representation in and for itself; thus it is an action (*a priori*), which corresponds to each *datum* (*a posteriori*) and through which this becomes a concept. These actions are the sources from which the logical are possible. Out of this arise all cognitions, how namely we can grasp *data* and form for ourselves something that is called cognition. In nature no *data* can come before us, except that, if one perceives laws in it, they correspond to the universal ways, according to which we posit something, because otherwise no laws would be noticed or no object at all, but only confused inner changes. Since we can therefore represent objects only through our changes, insofar as they have in themselves something conforming to our [Ge] rules for positing and canceling, so the real functions are the ground of the possibility of the representation of things and the logical functions the ground of the possibility of judgments, consequently of cognitions. For an object is called only that, which breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4632":["Universality is a continuous action, which can afterwards be divided.",[]],"reflm:4633":["How can cognitions be generated in us of which the objects have not yet presented themselves to us. Since the objects must not conform to our cognitions, but these to the objects, it seems they would have to be given to us beforehand at least in their ground-pieces (g according to) |\nGiven, before they can be thought. It is therefore the possibility of a (g each) cognition a priori, which for itself [its sources and its Best] is persistent, without [the Ge] being drawn from the objects themselves, which makes up our first and most important question. A question [which also only its importance also only to understand already a] which to have even merely raised and well understood already has some merit in itself, namely in a part of philosophy which has nothing to thank experience and the senses for. There are indeed in fact [sciences cognit] even entire sciences of this kind. Pure mathematics, which flows away entirely from the pure sources a priori, without taking anything of experience among its grounds, has in this kind incomparable progress and a fortune rightly admired and at times perhaps envied. However there is not lacking [also] (g on the other hand) others which, in that they want to be of just as pure origin, find themselves unceasingly among contradictions. Hence it is good to investigate the sources of cognition a priori [to] in general, without turning to this difference, and only afterwards to make the same comprehensible.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstände / obiecte (Object) both rendered 'objects'; the text's 'obiecte' is Latinate.\n- Grundstücken (g nach): reading and sense of the insertion doubtful; rendered literally as 'ground-pieces (g according to)'.\n- 'welche' in 'welche unsere erste ... Frage ausmacht' could refer to Möglichkeit or Erkenntnis; left ambiguous.\n- Bracketed deletions are fragmentary and broken off ('Best', 'Ge'); not completed.\n- Erkenntnis = cognition; Fortgang = progress; begreiflich = comprehensible; Glück = fortune.",[]],"reflm:4634":["M XXII:\nWe know each object only through predicates that we say or think of it. Beforehand, what of representations is encountered in us is to be counted only among materials, but not as cognition. Hence an object is only a something in general, which we think to ourselves through certain predicates that make up its concept. In every judgment there are accordingly two predicates which we compare with one another. Of which the one, [through which to us the] which makes up the given cognition of the object, is called the logical subject, the second, which is compared with it, the logical predicate. If I say: a body is divisible, then it signifies as much as: Something [which] x, which I know among the predicates that together make up a concept of body, I also think through the predicate of divisibility. x is one and the same with xb. Now a as well as b belongs to x. However |\nM XXIII: |\nin a different way: either that b already lies in that which makes up the concept a, and can therefore be found through dissection of it, or that b belongs to the x without being enclosed in a and comprehended along with it. In the first case the judgment is analytic, in the second synthetic. The case adduced is of an analytic judgment, but the proposition: Every body is heavy, is a synthesis; the one predicate is not involved in the subject, but added to it. Now all analytic judgments can be seen a priori; and what can be cognized only a posteriori is synthetic. Hence the proper empirical judgments are synthetic. But there are yet judgments, [which a] whose validity seems to stand fast a priori, which are nevertheless synthetic, e.g. Everything alterable has a cause; whence does one come to these judgments? [through which ha] whence do we take it, to join to a concept another of just the same object, which no observation and experience shows in it. Nevertheless all proper axioms are such synthetic propositions, e.g. between two points there can be only one straight line. On the other hand the proposition: [Every large is itself If one Every part is klei] Every magnitude is equal to itself, is an analytic proposition. The *principium* or the norm of all analytic propositions is the proposition of contradiction and of identity. It is (if I take them both together) no axiom, but a formula, i.e. a general model of analytic propositions; for it contains no *medium terminum*.\nWe have accordingly judgments a posteriori, which are synthetic, but also judgments a priori, which are nevertheless synthetic and for that reason cannot be derived from any experience, because they contain as well a true universality, hence necessity, as also comprise in themselves nothing but concepts which could not have been drawn from experience. [Whence] These concepts may dwell in us from wherever they will: whence do we take this connection of them. Are they revelations, prejudices etc etc.\nIf certain concepts in us contain nothing other than that through which all experiences are possible on our side, then they can be said before experience and yet with complete validity for everything that may ever occur to us, a priori. They hold then indeed not of things in general, but yet of everything that can ever be given to us through experience, because they contain the conditions through which these experiences are possible. Such propositions will therefore contain the condition of the possibility not of things, but of experience. |\nM XXIV: |\nThings, however, that can be given to us through no experience are for us nothing; therefore we can very well use such propositions as universal in practical aim, only not as principles of speculation about objects in general.\nIn order now to settle what these concepts are that must necessarily precede all experience and through which this alone is possible, which are therefore given a priori and also contain the ground for the judgments a priori, we must dissect an experience in general. In every experience there is something through which an object is given to us, and something through which it is thought. If we take the conditions that lie in the activities of the mind through which it alone can be given, then one can cognize something of the objects a priori. If we take that through which it alone can be thought, then one can also cognize something a priori of all possible objects. For thereby alone does something become for us an object or a cognition of it.\nWe want to investigate the former. That through which an object (g of experience) is given to us is called appearance. The possibility of appearances is, from the side of the human mind, sensibility. In sensibility there is a matter, which is called sensation, and with regard to this and its diversity we are merely passive, and the manifoldness of impressions makes it that we find nothing in us a priori that we would know a priori from ourselves before the impressions. One can represent to oneself no [new] impression of a new kind never in thought |\nrepresent. But the appearances also have a form, a ground lying in our subject, through which we order either the impressions themselves or that which corresponds to them, and give each part of them its place. This [is] can be nothing other than an activity, which is indeed naturally aroused through the impressions, but yet can be cognized for itself.\n(If we posit something in space and in time, then we act; if we posit it next to and after one another, then we connect. These actions are only means to bring about those places; but one can take them particularly; if we posit the same thing several times or in the one action simultaneously the other, then this is a kind of actions through which we posit something in conformity with the rule of appearances, whereby this positing must have its particular rules, which are distinguished from the conditions of the form, as they are to be placed with regard to the appearance.)\n===NOTES===\n- 'x ist einerley mit xb': the text appears to lack an 'a' (perhaps 'xa'); rendered as it stands.\n- 'Every part is klei': the word breaks off; left untranslated; the deletion is incomplete.\n- 'mit begriffen zu seyn' rendered 'comprehended along with'; begreifen = comprehend. Zergliederung = dissection; 'eingesehen' = 'seen' (einsehen).\n- Gegenstand(e) / obiect(e) both 'object'. 'Gedenken' and 'denken' both 'think' (gedenken 'think of' in the first sentence).\n- 'iene stellen zu stande zu bringen': 'stellen' read as noun 'Stellen' (places); it could be read as verb ('to bring about those positings'); left as 'places'.\n- 'Eindruck ... niemals': double negation ('no ... never') kept. 'Verschiedenheit' = diversity; 'Mannigfaltigkeit' = manifoldness.\n- 'vor' = 'before' or 'for' according to context (e.g. 'vor uns nichts' = 'for us nothing'); 'Absicht' = aim; 'Thatigkeit' = activity; 'Handlung' = action.\n- 'principium', 'medium terminum' as in the source (accusative form kept).",[]],"reflm:4635":["Not every object is a thing. That in the representation through which it has its peculiar function represents [the thing itself] the object (real function); its function in judgment is the respective function, through which it has a multi-validity with regard to other representations. The first function goes to what lies in the representation; the second, to what can be cognized thereby.\n===NOTES===\n- 'sache' and 'Ding' are both 'thing' here; 'sache' is the word used in this passage.\n- 'vielgültigkeit' rendered literally as 'multi-validity'; the compound is unusual.\n- 'Dasjenige an der vorstellung' could also be read 'that about the representation'; 'an' rendered 'in'.\n- 'reale function' and 'respective function' kept literally; Object / obiect = 'object'.",[]],"reflm:4636":["Cognitions a priori must go not at all to determinate things (because these are not yet given), but to general representations of |\nthings in general and thus to intuitions (g not sensations, for these are that through which something determinate is given) or to thoughts in general. (One can intuit something, without thinking anything thereby or under it.)\n(g All cognitions come in us through thinking, i.e. through concepts, they are not intuitions.)\n===NOTES===\n- Anschauungen = intuitions (intuitus); 'Anschauen' (verb) = 'intuit'.\n- 'Bestimmtes' / 'bestimmte' = 'determinate' (from bestimmen, determine).\n- 'dabey oder darunter' kept literally: 'thereby or under it'; the antecedent is not fixed by the German.\n- 'Dinge überhaupt' = 'things in general'.",[]],"reflm:4637":["That belonging to our cognitions through which the objects [given] are thought (for space is only the way in which they are given to us), is either the thinking of an object in general: *possibile* (the possible) [it may be given or not], or how it can be given by ourselves in general: *quantitas* (quantity), or how it must be given to us through experience.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Das zu unsern Erkenntnissen gehörige' is left with the participial construction; its predicate ('ist entweder ...') is far removed, as in the German.\n- 'von uns selbst' could be 'by' or 'from ourselves'; rendered 'by'.\n- Gegenstand / Object = 'object'; Art = 'way'; gegeben = 'given'.\n- Latin terms *possibile*, *quantitas* are in the source as written (qvantitas).",[]],"reflm:4638":["All cognition consists in judgments. The judgments may now be immediate or mediate (syllogisms); [a determinate] thinking is called judging. Even the concepts are predicates. Accordingly [cognitions] will concepts, to which no object is given, which however are nonetheless supposed to express the ways to think objects in general, contain in themselves that which in the judgments is thought relatively of two concepts on one another.\nCommonly valid concepts have a logical [function] place [the place] among the concepts. The place in a judgment is a logical function; it is breaks off.\nThe determinate logical function of a representation in general is the [real] pure concept of the understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis = cognition; Urtheil = judgment; Vernunftschlüsse = syllogisms (glossary); Gegenstand = object; Art = way; Stelle = place; Verstandesbegrif = concept of the understanding.\n- Sentence 4 is syntactically broken in the German (\"werden [Erkenntnisse] Begriffe ... enthalten\"); the English keeps the word order and does not repair it.\n- \"Gemeingültig\" rendered \"commonly valid\" (gemein = common).\n- \"bricht ab\" = the editor's remark that the text breaks off; it stands directly after \"sie ist\".\n- Bracketed words are Kant's deletions as marked by the Academy Edition.",[]],"reflm:4639":["That the necessity of mere truths of reason involves a kind of existence is to be judged by the little word is. E.g. One cannot say: all triangles will become three-cornered.\n===NOTES===\n- \"ist am Wortchen ist zu urtheilen\": the first \"ist\" is the copula of the main clause and the second is the word mentioned; I have kept both without quotation marks, as in the German.\n- Existentz = existence (Dasein would also be \"existence\"); Art = kind; Vernunftwarheiten = truths of reason.\n- \"dreyeckigt\" rendered \"three-cornered\".",[]],"reflm:4640":["Cause and effect seems to be for the [phantasy] *praevision*, whole and part for the phantasy, substance and accident for the senses, possibility for the faculty of fiction, actuality for the [intuition] sensation, magnitude on intuition, necessity for the w breaks off.\nIn all these items the constant combination makes the ground of an abstract concept of the understanding. For those forces are combining, and from this arises the possibility of the judgments *a priori* and also of these concepts.\nEverything subjective relates finally to sensation, hence every objective to substance.\nBecause all form (g of the subjective) is grounded on this, that one determination is combined together with the other, inherence is grounded on intuition, the connection of the effect with the cause on after-formation, conversely on fore-formation, whole and part on imagination.\n===NOTES===\n- \"vor\" is rendered \"for\" throughout; it could also be read otherwise (e.g. \"before\"/\"in front of\"), and the German does not decide.\n- Dichtungsvermögen = faculty of fiction; Nachbildung / Vorbildung = after-formation / fore-formation; Einbildung = imagination; Kräfte = forces; Verbindung = combination; Verknüpfung = connection; Wirklichkeit = actuality.\n- \"praevision\" and \"phantasie\" are kept close to the German spelling.\n- \"(g ...)\" is the Academy siglum for a later layer of writing; \"w bricht ab\" = a fragment \"w\" where the text breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4641":["The distinct representation of that which one\n[sees] intuits or which one thinks.\nsynthesis\nanalysis\nOne can still transform a sensible or appearance-concept into a concept of reason.\nDistinctness is opposed to indistinctness, order to confusion. [Through the understanding] The order depends on one's giving each partial representation its place, thus it is not merely the consciousness of this partial representation (distinctness), but also the [Zusam] co- or subordination. The distinctness of the understanding depends on dissection and gets order through the logical places.\n===NOTES===\n- deutlich = distinct; Undeutlichkeit = indistinctness; Verwirrung = confusion; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Erscheinung = appearance; Vernunft = reason; Stelle = place.\n- \"[Zusam] zusammen- oder unterordnung\": the deleted fragment is left in German; the hyphenated \"zusammen-\" is rendered \"co-\".\n- Zergliederung rendered \"dissection\" (distinct from Analyse/analysis, which appears above as a Latinate heading).\n- \"anschaut\" rendered \"intuits\" (cognate of Anschauung = intuition).",[]],"reflm:4642":["Everything that makes *a priori* a law of our representations, [every *actus*] hence the relation of representations to a (g *a priori* established) universal law, is the object. What is supposed to be thought under it presents the thing as it is, i.e. as it is valid for all appearances. But for that reason it must be something determinable *a priori*, because only thereby can we think; for thinking signifies nothing other than determining the representations from the universal.\nThe concepts of the understanding express all *actus* of the powers of the mind, insofar as representations are possible according to their universal laws, and indeed [that which] this their possibility *a priori*.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand/obiect: here \"obiect\" (Latinate) = object; both are \"object\" in this translation.\n- \"Das soll darunter gedacht werden, stellet die Sache vor\": the subject of \"stellet\" is left as ambiguous as in the German (\"What is supposed to be thought under it\" / \"that\").\n- Gemüthskräfte = powers of the mind (Gemüt = mind, Kraft = power).\n- bedeuten = signify; gilt = is valid; Bestimbares = determinable.\n- The final clause (\"und zwar ... ihre Moglichkeit a priori\") is elliptical in the German and is left so.",[]],"reflm:4643":["Transcendental is every pure cognition *a priori*, in which therefore no sensation is given.\n1. The transcendental aesthetic.\n2. The transcendental logic.\n3. The transcendental critic.\n4. —\n—\n—\n—\nArchitectonic.\nWhat one knows only through inner experience, of that one cannot say the predicates of space (hence the soul a spirit).\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis = cognition; kennt = knows (be acquainted with); Empfindung = sensation; Geist = spirit; Raum = space.\n- The dashes and the numbering are carried over as they stand; the list is incomplete in the manuscript.\n- \"Critic\" follows the German \"Critic\" (spelling kept close to the source).",[]],"reflm:4644":["If one uses of a thing in general [what] *a priori* predicates of space and time, then this is a *petitio phaenomenorum*.\nIf one uses of things which one knows only under the appearance of space universal [concepts] real concepts of the understanding (of the kind through which the things themselves are given to the understanding and whose quality, not the magnitude or possibility), then one commits a *petitionem noumeni*. The former is *synthesis subrepticia*, the latter *analysis subrepticia*.\nQuantity does not present a thing itself, but substance, cause, composite do indeed.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; qvantitaet = quantity; qvalitaet = quality (Latinate forms kept distinct in the spelling only).\n- \"dieses ... Jenes\": rendered \"the latter ... the former\".\n- \"deren qvalitaet, nicht die Größe oder Moglichkeit\" is elliptical (no verb); kept so.\n- \"wohl\" rendered \"indeed\"; \"substantia, causa, compositum\" are Latin in the source, rendered here in English as the German nouns above them are glossed.",[]],"reflm:4645":["In all cognitions there is to be noted in the object\n1. the matter and the form of the same, i.e. the quality.\n2. The difference, how it is given.\n3. The quantity.\nIn a judgment the matter is subject and predicate, but the form of each consists precisely in this, that the one is called subject etc etc.\nThe positing is copula: *est*. The quantity concerns both, either that the subject is a universal concept in itself etc etc.\nHere it is not the logical of the judgment but the real of the concepts that is weighed. It is the matter of the concepts.\nTo matter belong objective real data, e.g. cause, inherence, composition.\nMagnitude concerns only the division of the representation, how it can arise from repetition, but not the real composition and the origin from the same with a preceding simple.\n===NOTES===\n- Materie = matter; Form = form; Setzung = positing; Eintheilung = division; Größe = magnitude; qvantitaet = quantity; Einfachen = simple (n.).\n- \"derselben\" (\"of the same\") has an unclear antecedent in the German and is left so.\n- \"Mit vorhergegangenem Einfachen\" is rendered literally as \"with a preceding simple\".\n- \"Erwogen\" rendered \"weighed\"; \"das Logische\" / \"das reale\" kept as substantivized adjectives.",[]],"reflm:4646":["We would have no concept of magnitude at all if we did not repeat one several times; thus time and its intuiting counter-image, space, are here entirely effective.\nMagnitude is for pure intuition in general without data.\nQuality for the difference of sensation in general.\nThe categories belonging under this relate to sensations of the relations, which are posited through the activity of the soul.\nThe position on the faculty of fiction, the [Ans] sensation and the breaks off.\n(g Categories of quantity, quality and position.)\n===NOTES===\n- Zeit = time; Raum = space; Anschauung = intuition; Empfindung = sensation; Dichtungsvermögen = faculty of fiction; Verhältnisse = relations; Größe = magnitude.\n- \"Gegenbild\" rendered \"counter-image\"; \"Anschauendes\" \"intuiting\".\n- \"vor\" rendered \"for\" as in 4640 (ambiguous in the German).\n- \"Die position auf ... die [Ans] Empfindung und die bricht ab\": the text breaks off mid-list.",[]],"reflm:4647":["To the maxims of reason belongs that even that which contains the most distinct relation to ends nevertheless arose according to the order of nature.\nMy onto-theological proof, which is correct in this, that according to the order of nature under the same creator we become aware of relations to ends.\n===NOTES===\n- Beweis = proof; Zwecke = ends; Vernunft = reason; deutlich = distinct; Beziehung = relation.\n- The second sentence has no finite main verb in the German (a fragment); the English is likewise incomplete.\n- \"entstanden sey\" is subjunctive; rendered \"arose\" without further marking.",[]],"reflm:4648":["Across the inner margin:\nMotion is something that happens, thus belongs to the actual appearances and not to the mere sensible form, also presupposes something mov|able\ni.e. changeable with regard to place, which cannot be cognized *a priori*, but presupposes empirical concepts, among them also concepts of the understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- Wirklich = actual; Erscheinung = appearance; sinnlich = sensible; Ort = place; erkannt = cognized; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding.\n- The line break and \"|\" fall inside \"be|wegliches\"; the \"|\" is placed at the corresponding point inside \"mov|able\" (the syllable division differs in English).\n- \"Am Innenrand quer\" is the editor's description of the location (\"across the inner margin\").",[]],"reflm:4649":["Across the inner margin:\nThe understanding presents the things as they are, but not in *intuition*, but in *reflexion*, i.e. not as they are given in general, but as they are thought. That which the appearances of the object for the same sense among themselves breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- *intuition* and *reflexion* are Latinate forms in the source (glossary: Anschauung = intuition; Überlegung = reflection); I keep Kant's own spelling for \"reflexion\".\n- \"unter sich\" could be \"among themselves\" or \"under themselves\"; left in the plainer form, and the sentence breaks off.\n- \"überhaupt gegeben\" rendered \"given in general\".",[]],"reflm:4650":["Across the inner margin:\nM XXV:\nAll *axiomata univoca*, whether of sensibility or reason, [are] can be correct.\nThe *axiomata aeqvivoca*, where either subject is intellectual and predicate sensible or conversely, are reduced, either through the interpolation in the first case or through the restriction in the second.\nM XXVI:\nThe *axiomata* or *postulata rationalia* can become true only in that one does not precisely limit them, but restricts them to the conditions of cognition *a priori* or of comprehension, so that there are properly no *axiomata intellectus puri* at all.\n===NOTES===\n- \"M XXV\", \"M XXVI\" are the editor's locus markers and are carried over unchanged.\n- Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Vernunft = reason; Bedingungen = conditions; Erkenntnis = cognition; \"comprehension\" is Kant's Latinate word (cf. begreifen = comprehend).\n- \"limitirt\" / \"restringirt\" rendered \"limit\" / \"restrict\" (Latinate verbs in German).\n- \"aeqvivoca\" spelling kept as in the source.",[]],"reflm:4651":["Across the inner margin:\nA learned journal ought properly to announce the progress of a science. This has up to now with metaphysical works not been able to hap|pen. Now it can happen, and indeed as regards completeness, distinctness and also precision. Indeed, the completion of the science can also be announced, and indeed not after a very long time.\n===NOTES===\n- Fortgang = progress; Wissenschaft = science; Vollständigkeit = completeness; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Vollendung = completion.\n- In the German, \"ge|schehen\" and a line break stand between \"geschehen\" and \"können\"; the \"|\" is kept inside \"hap|pen\" and the line break is not reproduced because of the English word order.\n- \"nicht nach gar langer Zeit\" rendered literally as \"not after a very long time\".",[]],"reflm:4652":["Across the inner margin:\nI doubted at first whether motion also belongs to the transcendental aesthetic. Now I see that, since it contains something in space that is moved, hence the change of something with regard to relations, it contains not mere sensibility but an intellectual concept.\n===NOTES===\n- Bewegung = motion; Veränderung = change; Verhältnisse = relations; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Begrif = concept; \"transscendentalen Aesthetic\" = transcendental aesthetic.\n- \"enthalte\" is subjunctive (indirect speech) and is rendered \"contains\".\n- \"mit zur ... gehöre\" rendered \"also belongs to\".",[]],"reflm:4653":["Across the inner margin:\nOur understanding does not give materials through which we could cognize a new world, but forms, in order to arrange the present one accordingly.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstand = understanding; Welt = world; Formen = forms; \"materialien\" = materials (Latinate, as against Materie/Stoff).\n- \"die Gegenwärtige\" rendered \"the present one\".",[]],"reflm:4654":["In and \nThe *contrapositum* of the proposition of contradiction is a subjective law. Of thinkability, the supreme *criterio veritatis* of Crusius.\n===NOTES===\n- \"In und \" is the editor's locus note with the lemma(s) missing in the text as given; it is left as it stands.\n- Satz des Wiederspruchs = proposition of contradiction (glossary: Satz = proposition; Widerspruch = contradiction); the usual rendering \"principle\" is not used.\n- *criterio veritatis* is Latin in the dative/ablative form in the source and is kept so; the sentence is a fragment.\n- Denklichkeit = thinkability (not in the glossary); Gesetz = law.",[]],"reflm:4655":["In and \n*Principium identitatis* and *contradictionis* are both called *contradictionis*. *Indemonstrable* judgments stand immediately beneath them; material principles, in which the predicate is determined, are *indemonstrable*.\nIs the *principium* of the judgments and of their possibility.\nThe possibility of things requires a real ground.\n===NOTES===\n- \"In und \" is left as in the source (lemma missing).\n- Urtheil = judgment; Grundsätze = principles (Grundsatz); Realgrund = real ground; Möglichkeit = possibility; bestimmt = determined.\n- The third line has no stated subject (\"Ist das principium ...\"); kept as a fragment.\n- \"indemonstrabel\" is a Latinate adjective in German; rendered with the Latin-derived English word in italics.",[]],"reflm:4656":["In and \nThe most universal object is object in general,\nbeing and not-being, *aliqvid et nihil*.\n(s Something or nothing according to the concept: possible, impossible; or objectively: positive, negative.)\nThe possibility of the predicates or of the things.\nEvery object is either possible or impossible. The former affirming or denying. The — in the logical sense means: not to posit, in the real: to abolish the thing.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand überhaupt and obiect both = object; Möglichkeit = possibility; setzen = posit; bejahend = affirming.\n- \"Das —\": the dash stands where a word is omitted in the source; it is carried over.\n- \"im logischen verstande\" (lower-case) is rendered \"in the logical sense\", not \"understanding\".\n- \"ausheben\" rendered \"abolish\"; \"(s ...)\" is the Academy siglum for a later layer.",[]],"reflm:4657":["Simple concepts can be given only through experience; thus the possibility in their relation cannot be discerned through reason. *Possibilitas synthetica*, *analytica*.\n===NOTES===\n- Einfach = simple; Erfahrung = experience; Vernunft = reason; Verhältnis = relation; Möglichkeit = possibility.\n- \"eingesehen\" rendered \"discerned\" (literally seen into).\n- \"synthetica, Analytica\": capitalization is as in the source.",[]],"reflm:4658":["A necessary being, which is posited not merely under a condition, bu|t\nunder every possible condition, must through its existence have a relation to all possibility.\nIf a contingent thing is not, then it is\n=0A. If a necessary thing is not, then it is =0û, i.e. *nihil negativum*; through the *nihil negativum* everything is always abolished d breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- notwendig = necessary; zufällig = contingent; Bedingung = condition; Daseyn = existence (Dasein); gesetzt = posited; Beziehung = relation.\n- The \"|\" falls inside \"son|dern\" in the source; it is placed inside \"bu|t\" here.\n- \"=0A\" and \"=0û\" are reproduced exactly; the second reading (\"û\") is as printed and I cannot confirm it.\n- \"aufgehoben\" rendered \"abolished\"; the text breaks off after the stray \"d\".",[]],"reflm:4659":["The common proof of the [existence of a] necessary existence of a most perfect being is that, if it did not exist, a perfection, namely existence, would be lacking to it; that it therefore would already contain existence in its concept. I answer: if it did not exist, then it would not be something that would be lacking to the most perfect being, but rather the most perfect being would be wholly lacking. There can indeed be no contradiction when the thing itself is cancelled and nothing remains. The most perfect possible, which does not exist, is the most perfect among all that which is merely possible.",[]],"reflm:4660":["ρ1? φ1?? M 31.\nThe necessity of a *principium* (of the rule) or of a *principiatum*. The first is the necessity of the assumption according to reason.",[]],"reflm:4661":["Next to and \nOnly the possibility of a necessary being needs to be demonstrated, and then its existence is also proved. For a necessary being |\nis (necessarily) there, that is the definition of it. One does not need reality for this. This is to serve only to represent the necessity as possible. But that all reality be, is possible; that none be, is also possible.",[]],"reflm:4662":["Below and \nSubstances among the appearances are only empirically necessary, i.e. they hang together with the whole of the appearances according to time; but they are not absolutely necessary, because they can be cancelled along with all other appearances. Empty space and time.",[]],"reflm:4663":["It is possible that a thing that is at one time is not at another time: expresses alterability.\n(s in the same time signifies contingency.)\nWhat is contingent is therefore not alterable; and what is alterable is not yet contingent.",[]],"reflm:4664":["It is (g a time) possible that something [at a time is] be. It is possible in just the same circumstances of time that it not be; i.e.: It is contingent under just the same hypotheses of time.",[]],"reflm:4665":["That in which there is a multitude determined in itself is a *quantum discretum*.\nThe representation of a determined multitude is *quantitas discreta*.",[]],"reflm:4666":["*De lege continui logica.* The living and dead force (g if it has different measure) cannot be distinguished through motion and rest, because rest is an infinitely small motion or also infinitely small speed. If virtue and vice were distinguished only according to degree, then a certain degree, as the boundary of both, would be both virtue as well as vice. To be sure the boundary of a magnitude can be regarded as something and also as nothing — (signifying); but never the ground for positing something however small as a ground for cancelling it, because these differ in respect of quality |\nor are *disparata*. If gravity were equal at all distances, then the body would be heavy even in the *centro*.\nIs the boundary of the matter of a body [also] determined through a jump or does its density decrease imperceptibly up to a certain distance, where it is vanishing? The continuous in alteration and the boundaries, the beginning and end. everything is as it were related to duration in a motion. All *quanta discreta* are numbers. Are the magnitudes in nature like numbers? and are numbers something from which the natural magnitudes are possible? or conversely are numbers only divisions of already given magnitudes?\n*Lex continui aesthetica.*\n(s The *lex continui* means: two states following upon one another always have something in common, namely their boundary.\nGravity cannot be equal at all distances from the center point.)",[]],"reflm:4667":["All effect and counter-effect of substances distinct from one another is possible only insofar as many make up a unity, without which the state of the one would not concern that of the other; and [the] two opposed determinations conflict with one another only insofar as they are found in one; thus if many substances were not combined into unity, the state of the one would never conflict with the state of the other.",[]],"reflm:4668":["If our representations are not effects of the objects, then they prove their existence in no other way than through revelation.",[]],"reflm:4669":["On the brief expressions that contain much sense. The White Bear. We learn in youth to speak of him. The philosopher speaks of the white bear when he speaks of the state after death. Clergymen have written big books for and against about the white bear. And the frontispiece of such writings should always be the White Bear. Further the sentence: let each one on his hobbyhorse the streets of the city etc etc. That is understanding in the vehicle.\n(g We can let the theologians ride on their hobbyhorses of philology and criticism to their heart's content; but it is intolerable that they compel the common being to sit up behind.)\n(g It is too bad to get so worked up about the White Bear itself to the wildness of a bear.)\nM305:\n(g When two people quarrel over the white bear, I look on calmly, do not mix in among them.)",[]],"reflm:4670":["From the analogy of nature we prove most beautifully the future life. But the ground of proof consists in this, that we develop out of ourselves capacities that exceed our present vocation. Primarily this is shown in the sciences; thus it can already be seen from this that [our] the sciences do not harmonize with our present vocation and are premature developments, which nevertheless, by betraying a talent that should have remained hidden, have at least the use of discovering our future vocation. Meanwhile the sciences indeed give this prospect, but no incentive. They belong to the play of the human race, but not to its ends.",[]],"reflm:4671":["Remark of Kant on the last page of the letter of his brother J.H. Kant of 3 July 1773 (2 X 140f.) in vol. II of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant p. 492:\nAll morality consists in the derivation of actions from the idea of the subject, not from sensation. The idea is universally valid [and contains] as well from the end (g *abstrahendo*) as also from the relation to all (g *combinando*).\nThe sources of all cognition of experience [lie in] are transcendental. They are (g inner) anticipations. [The]",[]],"reflm:4672":["Remark of Kant on the back (address side) of the letter of E.T. von Kortum of 18 Nov. 1773 (2 X 142f.) in the III. Dorpat volume of letters Bl. 27ψ.\nTo the left of the address, crosswise:\nFirst there must be certain titles of thinking, under which appearances in themselves are brought: e.g. whether they are regarded as magnitude or as subject or as ground or as whole or merely as reality (figure is no reality). I will on that account not regard in the appearance, what I will, as subject or, as I will, either as subject or predicate, but it is determined as subject |\nrespectively as ground. What logical function is therefore properly valid of an appearance in regard to the others, whether that of magnitude or of the subject, thus which function of judgments. For otherwise we can use logical functions at will, without making out, also without perceiving, that the object is more appropriate to one than to another. Thus one can think an appearance without bringing it under a title of thinking in general, consequently determining an object for it.\nTo the right of the address, crosswise:\nIn order that the appearances be represented as proper to certain rules or as determined according to them, it is required that they be represented as belonging under one or another function of the same. Thereby they become in a determinate way objects of thoughts; otherwise there is nothing in their relations (for sensations [lass] are not thoughts) that would make them thinkable before the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4673":["Lossow's of 28 Apr. 1774 (2 X 167) in vol. III of the Dorpat collection of letters, leaf 6, 6υ, 7, 7υ.\nP. I. Above the salutation:\n[single. I.e. all representations make in my inner state through the unity of intuition] For as intuition of a single subject [attain all Ge] all objects belong only to a one breaks off?\nBetween salutation and letter, as well as between its first four lines:\nSimultaneously with the sense, [unt] under which they are offered to our mind breaks off?\n1. Time single. [For it is a Sub] Which means as much as: I can cognize all objects only in myself and in the representations found in [it this] my |\nsingle subject [cognize insofar only according to the form of inner intuition immediately cognize.] (g To intuit, and all possible objects of my intuition stand to one another in relation according to the particular form of this intuition.)\n[2. It is infinite]\n2. It is infinite without first and last. For it is the [*datum*] condition of *coordination* through inner sense, which [through] cannot otherwise than through the representations, which are placed [ge] according to it, be determined and limited. Itself the [mere form of the Din recep] receptivity of the ordering together cannot limit.\n3. It is necessary, i.e. it depends on no thing, but lies at the ground of all, is therefore [the first *datum* of possibility] the condition of the inner intuitions and lies at the ground of the possibility of all intuitions.\n4. In it all things and all states of things have their determinate position. For [only through the Ve] they have (g must) through the unity of inner sense their determinate relation to all [possible D] other putative objects of intuition.\n5. It precedes all actual things and can [*a priori*] itself consequently also [the Be] as condition of the objects be cognized *a priori*.\nBetween X2 16722 and 16725:\n(g The inner determinations are not in space.)\nWe cannot get out of the world with these concepts.\n1. If therefore a *subiect* is a thing in general and the *praedicat* is space and time or a concept built upon them as condition, then |\nthe judgment is transcendent. (g Everything is somewhere and sometime.) (g I do not say that it is false; it merely does not infer, *non liquet*. That is [but] namely false, that from this subjective something general and objective should follow.)\n2. If that *subiect* is given only through (g *praedicate* of) inner sensation and the *praedicat* contains a condition of outer sensibility, then it is also transcendent.\nP. II:\nRemark.\nSpace is nothing but the intuition of the mere form even without given matter, thus the pure intuition. It is an individual representation on account of the unity of the subject (g and of the capacity), in which all representations of outer objects (g next to) one another can be placed. It is infinite, because in the capacity to receive there are no boundaries. It is necessary, because it is the first condition of the possibility of the outer representations; consequently it is the ground of the capacity of outer representations, and we cannot represent the opposite to ourselves, because otherwise we would have to have yet [another] higher capacity. It is something actual, which does not depend on the existence of things; [but] for the capacity to intuit does not depend on the existence of things, can therefore be cognized *a priori*.\nSpace is not an object of intuitions (an *obiect* or its determination), but the intuition itself, which precedes all objects and [through which it] in which (g if) the same placed |\nare, the appearance of the same is possible. It is a pure intuition *a priori*. But how is such an intuition possible. [It is] It is nothing other than the consciousness of its own receptivity, to receive representations (impressions) of things according to certain relations among one another.\nThe *spatium absolutum*, this riddle of the philosophers, is quite something correct (g but not real, but ideal), otherwise one would not be able to say anything of it *a priori*, and indeed not through general concepts, but through properties that let themselves be perceived in it through [inner War] immediate grasp. But it is nothing outer, but the condition of the form of all outer representation consisting in the mind itself. It is [no] nothing imagined (*ens imaginarium*). For it is the sole actual condition of the representation of actual outer things. The order of things that are next to one another is not space, but space is that which makes possible such an order or better *coordination* according to determinate conditions. If it is a mere general concept of order, then let one try how much one can derive from it and how one might come *a priori* [to the] upon the necessity of such an order; for to borrow it *a posteriori* is first against ocular evidence, and then it would have only the consequences of an observation, but not of a [Grundbe] ground-representation.\nSpace as an outer representation would also have to have something in the object, through which it would be effected in the mind. For it would be no representation *a priori*. But where there is yet nothing at all, there is also no influence, and the mere form cannot be communicated to us through influence.\nIn general, that there are things that correspond to sensibility the understanding must cognize; thus the ideality of space is nothing further than the distinction of sensibility and of that which is posited through it, from the understanding and what is thought through it. Through the ideality the actuality of bodies |\n(g of certain beings that correspond to it) and of certain properties is not denied, indeed not otherwise than merely negatively thought in it.\nThe ideality of space does not cancel its reality with regard to bodies, i.e. of all [sensi] outer objects of sensibility, and space actually pertains to them, but only distinguishes objects of the senses as such from things in themselves. To a thing in itself no space pertains (g as condition or determination), but every object of outer sense is thought through the condition of space.\nP. III. Under X2 16733-35:\nThe propositions about [R] the properties of absolute space and time seemed strange; one therefore either denied absolute time and made it into an *abstracto* or *empirico*, or one [vorichtete] made time objective and made it into a most real ideal, i.e. a chimera.\nTo the right of X2 16734f., above and below l. 12-16:\nWe have no intuitions other than through the senses; therefore no other concepts can dwell in the understanding than those that go to the *disposition* and the order among these intuitions. These concepts must contain the general and rules. The faculty of rules *in abstracto*: the learned understanding, *in concreto*: the sound. The sound understanding has [therefore vielfelt] in all cases the advantage, where the rule must be abstracted from the cases *a posteriori*; but where it has its origin thoroughly *a priori*, there it does not take place at all.\nP. IV:\nSpace is the condition attaching to our sensibility with regard to all outer appearance, for it is the form of this capacity |\nof the mind to perceive things as outer [to]. Hence bodies are representations that [necessarily at this Bed] are possible only under this condition [and are themselves nothing], and insofar [has] space is certainly something real. [The ideality] But through a body there is thought not an object of cognition in general, but a thing as an object of outer sense. Space goes like predicate thus not at all to a thing in itself, but only as to the object of outer sense. It is the condition not of things, but of the *phaenomenon* [of] of [a] the things, and indeed [wer with regard to the through the outer sense] of the outer sense. Hereby sensibility is only distinguished from the understanding, through which something is represented, not as it is given to us as object of the senses, but is thought independently of that.\nDifferent predicates of space now, which one otherwise regarded as objective, let themselves be explained by this concept with regard to their origin. 1. Space is single, because it is the form of the representations (g of all possible outer objects) in a single subject. 2. Space is infinite. For the capacity [der hinzu], to admit several impressions of outer things, or the receptivity, has in itself no limitations. 3. Space is necessary; for it is that on which the possibility of the senses itself grounds itself.\nThe representation of space is no imagining and which relates itself merely to the subject (g Albefassend), but a [means] condition to represent outer things, and a means to order them. The order is in accordance with the inner form.\nThe omnipresence of space and the eternity of time. That it is present everywhere, i.e. it is itself the condition of all presence, for through it presence is cognized.\nThe former signifies: that we [us] can intuit no thing as present as somewhere in space.\nReason is the faculty of rules *a priori*.\nA rule *a priori* is a rule of reason [and this]. A rule from concepts *in abstracto* is a law. Reason is the faculty of laws. A rule of the *construction* of concepts is called formula. A symbolic formula —\n[Reason considers the unbedin] Concepts of reason are unconditionally valid concepts, thus the all, the first, the [un] transcendent. The unconditioned necessity, the unconditioned *principium* (g the independent *principium*), the unconditioned (g unlimited) all.\n––––––––––\nSpace and time contain the conditions of the rules of appearance, hence all categories with regard to their application lay them at the ground.\n––––––––––\nThe question whether space is something ideal (not imaginary) or real interests different sciences not at all. In mathematics, mechanics and general physics no heed has been taken of it; although Leibnitz as well as Newton (I name them here at the head of the remaining great names), the latter assuming the subsisting reality of the same, the former the adhering, both posit in the application to objects of the world to it, as if [it] both, space and time, [containers] were self-subsisting containers of things, and even if the ideality has been proved by us, it cannot make anything different with regard to such investigations. But there, where these answers become transcendent, it sounds otherwise.\nDifferent things are in different places and conversely: the difference of places proves the difference of things (is already a proposition that concerns the empirical understanding).\nEven if we cannot comprehend many propositions from the concepts of space and time, we must notice that they are not representations of reason, but of intuition and [that we the] breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand and Object (*obiect*, Latin-script spelling) are both rendered \"object\"; Dasein = \"existence\"; Wirklichkeit/wirklich = \"actuality\"/\"actual\"; Realität/real = \"reality\"/\"real\".\n- Vorstellung = representation; Fähigkeit (Fahigkeit) = capacity, Vermögen = faculty; Empfänglichkeit and *receptivitaet* = \"receptivity\"; Gemüth = mind; Wesen = beings; Sinn = sense; Erkentnis = cognition; erkennen = cognize.\n- Not in glossary, rendered consistently: einig = \"single\"; einzeln = \"individual\"; einzig (eintzige) = \"sole\"; Einbildung = \"imagining\", eingebildet = \"imagined\"; verschieden = \"different\"; Eigenschaft = \"property\"; schließen = \"infer\"; Schranke = \"limitation\"; Grenze = \"boundary\"; Augenschein = \"ocular evidence\"; Grundvorstellung = \"ground-representation\".\n- Fragments and doubtful readings left as the edition gives them (untranslated inside brackets): Ge, Ve, D, Din recep, Bed, War, Grundbe, vorichtete, vielfelt, wer, der hinzu, Albefassend, unbedin; \"bricht ab\" is the editor's remark. \"Daß subiect\" (for \"das\"?) is rendered \"that *subiect*\".\n- In item 1 (P. I) the main text has no finite verb after \"Vorstellungen\", because \"erkennen\" is deleted in brackets; \"zu eine\" left ungrammatical. The |-break at \"gestellt | werden\" (P. II) is kept after \"placed\", hence the German order \"placed | are\".\n- Preserved ambiguities: \"kann also a priori erkannt werden\" (subject space or capacity, P. II); \"seiner eignen\" (its/his own); \"wodurch\" in \"von dem Verstande unterschieden, wodurch etwas vorgestellt wird\" (through the understanding / through the distinction); \"ihr\" in \"setzen beyde ... ihr\"; \"daran\" in \"blos negativ daran gedacht\"; \"dieser ... jener\" given as \"the latter ... the former\".\n- \"abstracto oder empirico\" left in the Latin ablative forms as written; \"Phänomenon\" left as *phaenomenon*; \"das\" for \"daß\" in \"das wir\" rendered \"that we\"; \"Z.\" = Zeile, \"l.\"; \"S.\" = Seite, \"P.\".",[]],"reflm:4674":["P. I:\nThe principles of appearance (g in general) are merely of the form, namely time.\nThe *principium* of the exposition of appearances is the ground of the exposition in general of that which has been given. The exposition of that which is thought rests merely on consciousness, that of what is given, however, if one regards the matter as undetermined, on the ground of all relation and of the concatenation of the representations (sensations). The concatenation is grounded (just as the appearance is not on mere [influence] sensation, but in inner principles of the form) not on the mere appearance, but is a representation of the inner action of the mind, to connect representations, not merely to place them alongside one another in intuition, but to make a whole according to the matter. Here there is therefore unity, not [of the] by virtue of that: in which, but: through which the manifold is brought into one, hence universal validity. Hence it is not forms but functions on which the relations of appearances rest. The exposition of appearances is therefore the determination of the ground on which the interconnection of the sensations [derse] in them rests.\n––––––––––\nBy a general concept of a sensible *datum*, in which the reality and at the same time its relation to the sensible condition in general is indicated, we understand the action of determining an object sensibly according to such conditions; e.g. what happens signifies the action of determining something in time according to sequence. Now x is this determinable, which contains the conditions of the determination; a, however, signifies only the action of determining in general. It is therefore no wonder if in x, besides the action of deter|mining, still more is contained, which is expressed through b (g to the concept a still more is contained, which belongs thereto in order to determine it in the mind, i.e. the way to cognize how this is generated or specified in the mind, or what flows from its specification or is combined with it as condition). E.g. in space, besides the general action of constructing a triangle, still the magnitude of the angles, and in inner sense, besides the designation of that [wa] in general which happens, still the conditions under which alone this happening (g as apprehension) can be determined in the mind. These conditions are found in x in intuition through the construction of a, e.g. triangle, but in a real concept through [the subject] the *concretum* of the subject, in which the representation a is posited. Thus the relation that is thought through a is to be determined by the real condition of the subject alone, which consists in the (g function of) relative positing in general and with regard to the *datum* a in particular; since the subjective condition (g x) is supposed to suffice for all these positions, the determination of a, i.e. b, must be a general action, through which the appearance of a is exposited i.e. breaks off.\n(g Only [with regard to] relation do objectively synthetic propositions of appearance hold.)\nIn synthetic propositions what is represented is properly not the relation among the concepts immediately [but] (for this occurs only in analytic ones), but in the [subject given] conditions of their concrete representation in the subject, be it intuition or appearance. This subject contains the conditions of the representation of everything of which we have concepts, and in whose sensibility [the objects must yet] the objective of the same must yet be determined. x always signifies the object of the concept a. But there can be no object except either of pure or of empirical intuiting. As for the latter |\nconcerns, the concept a can go either to a given object of the senses x or to conditions of sensibility, under which an object [of the concept of the], insofar as it merely corresponds to the concept x, must be given and under which alone it can be cognized as conforming to a.\nP. II:\nThat is a proof that space is a subjective condition, because, since the propositions thereof are synthetic and (g thereby objects) can be cognized *a priori*, this would be impossible if space were not a subjective condition of the representation of these objects.\nOn the other hand the synthetic judgments of experience are cognized *a posteriori*, because they are directed immediately to given objects.\nIf now, however, something is to be cognized *a priori* of things not merely according to the form of appearance, but with regard to their remaining constitution etc etc.\n––––––––––\n(g The x drops out, for it is to signify the object that is thought through a; but since b is merely compared with the concept a and is already determined thereby, the rest in x is indifferent; if a is considered adjectively, the proposition is not always general.)\nb must be a determination of a and no analytic predicate. Analytic predicates are identical and tautological. Of the analytic hypothetical judgments. Disjunction, dichotomy. (g Categorical ones are the foundation.) The concept substance and accident gives in itself a synthesis, likewise cause and effect and multitude in a real unity. That now nature, according to the different relations to inner sense, must throughout stand under one of these syntheses.\nx is therefore the determinable (object) which I think through the concept a, and b is its determination (g or way of determining it). In mathematics x is the construction of a, in experience the *concretum*, with regard to an inhering representation or thought |\nin general x is the function of thinking in general in the subject, there then the (g real) concept a [determined] together with everything is determined: 1. through the subject, 2. with regard to succession through the ground, 3. with regard to coexistence through composition.\n(g [The condition through which a gets an object x is enunciated in b but] x is the object. This can be given in construction *a priori*, in the exposition, however (which is quite something other than observation, which has nothing connected [with] *a priori* with a), the conditions can be cognized *a priori* in the subject under which a /in general relates to an object, namely a real one. This object can only be represented according to its relations and is nothing other than the subjective representation (of the subject) itself, but made general, for I am the original of all objects. It is therefore the conjugation as function that makes the exponent of a rule.)\n––––––––––\nReality must be given (g in sensation). Magnitude we can (g according to intuition) construct. The [real] real synthesis is not merely given to us in sensation, nor can it be constructed, but yet lies in appearance neither as intuition nor as sensation. For experience does give us substance, effect and cause, and whole to cognize (although we cannot think the latter *a priori*, namely how, of many things, one reciprocally determines all the others and is determined by them, and can take many together only in thought). These three kinds of concepts go to objects as appearances (possibility etc etc. only as concepts *a priori*); with magnitude I need no sensation, but only time, with real synthesis both sensation in general and time. (Threefold dimension of the synthesis. [We can therefore] How can we then represent to ourselves [the] positions of the postulates of the synthesis *a priori*. They are the three functions of apperception, which are met with in all thinking of our state in general |\nand under which all appearance must fit for this reason, that in it no synthesis lies in itself, if the mind does not add it or make it from the data thereof. The mind is therefore to itself the archetype of [the possibility of] such a synthesis through original and not derived thinking.\n––––––––––\nConcepts give only the outline of objects, namely that, [through which one] what is a sign of their representation. b is at all times compared with the object x by means of a, but x is not always considered merely in the concept a; in the latter case b goes either to the way in which the object a is given *a priori* (g objectively) in intuition, or *a posteriori* in experience, or *a priori*, but [subjectively] in the subjective perception of apperception. This latter goes only to cases of perception, and indeed to the synthesis in it, i.e. to the relation. Apperception is the perception of oneself as a thinking subject in general.\nApperception is the consciousness of thinking, i.e. of the representations, as they are posited in the mind. Here there are three exponents: 1. of the relation to the subject, 2. of the relation of sequence among one another, 3. of the taking-together. The determination of a in these *momenta* of apperception is the subsumption under one of these *actus* of thinking; one (g cognizes it as determinable in itself and thus objectively, namely) the concept a, when one brings it under one of these general actions of thinking, by means of which it comes under a rule. Such a proposition is a *principium* of the rule, thus of the cognition of appearance through the understanding, whereby [it] it is considered as something objective, which [not] is thought in itself independently of the singularity in which it was given.\n===NOTES===\n- Square brackets, (g ...), | and / are carried over as in the source; \"g\" marks Kant's later additions. \"breaks off\" translates the German \"bricht ab\" (an editorial remark in the source); the parenthesis opened at \"(Dreyfache dimension\" is never closed in the German.\n- Verhältnis = relation (the Latinate *relation* / *relationes* is rendered \"relation\" as well); bestimmen/Bestimmung = determine/determination, and the Latinate determiniren/determination are also rendered \"determine\"/\"determination\" (marked here); Object/obiect/Gegenstand = \"object\" throughout.\n- Verkettung = \"concatenation\" and Verknüpfung = \"connection\"/\"connect\" (glossary: connexio); Zusammennehmung = \"taking-together\"; Handlung = \"action\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Empfindung = \"sensation\"; Warnehmung = \"perception\"; allgemeingültig = \"universal validity\".\n- \"exposition\"/\"exponirt\" (Erörterung, *expositio*) kept as the Latinate cognate; \"exponent\" and \"coniugation\" left as in the source; Latin inflected forms (*dati*, *momentis*, *actibus*) rendered as *datum*, *momenta*, *actus* in the English.\n- \"dem Begriffe a noch mehr enthalten ist\" lacks a preposition (\"in\" expected); kept as \"to the concept a still more is contained\", not emended.\n- Ambiguity kept: \"eine Vorstellung von der innern Handlung\" and \"das Bestimmbare\" are left as they stand; \"der Verhaltnis\" (feminine article with neuter noun in the German) is rendered \"of the relation\" without emendation.\n- Doubtful or fragmentary readings: \"nicht [der] vermöge desienigen: worin\", \"Daß nun die Natur ... stehen müsse\" (no main clause), \"wodurch die Erscheinung von a exponirt d.i.\" (breaks off), and \"was [nicht] an sich selbst gedacht wird\" (bracketed deletion; sense uncertain). Several deleted fragments (\"[derse]\", \"[wa]\") are left untranslated.\n- \"in der Zeit der Folge nach\" is rendered \"in time according to sequence\"; \"zulangen\" = \"suffice\"; \"gleichgültig\" = \"indifferent\"; \"Umris\" = \"outline\"; \"Urbild\" = \"archetype\"; \"Einzelnheit\" = \"singularity\".",[]],"reflm:4675":["Remarks of Kant on the letter of Bertram of 20 May 1775, folded into two octavo leaves (2 X 182). R I 21-26.\np. I (address page):\nThe same being can subsist together with opposed predicates one after another. [The predicates so] Something is posited outside us only insofar as its representation constitutes persistence and a particular point of relation.\nIf something follows upon my representation, then the object of it would not yet follow upon it, if its representation were not determined as a sequence by something, which can never happen otherwise than according to a universal law. Or there must be a universal law, that all sequence be determined by something preceding, otherwise I would posit no sequence of objects for the sequence of representations. For to posit objects for my representations, it always belongs to this that the representation be determined according to a universal law, for in the universally valid point the object precisely consists.\nIn just the same way I would not represent something as outside me [if] and thus make appearance into experience (objective), if the representations did not relate to something that is parallel to my I, through which I refer them from myself to another subject. In just the same way if manifold representations did not determine one another according to a universal law. The three relations in the mind therefore demand three analogies of the appearance, in order to transform the [functions of the mind] subjective functions of the mind into objective ones and [its] thereby to make them into concepts of the understanding, which give reality to the appearances.\nEverything that is simultaneous in reciprocal relations belongs to a whole: *contra vacuum* [separans] interrumpens (*vacuum* [ter] terminans); from this follows the continuity of the breaks off.\nAll of this is grounded on conditions of experience; consequently it is not necessary and is also not seen as such, |\nbut rather they are *analoga* of axioms, which occur *a priori*, but [only] only as anticipations of all laws of experience in general.\nEverything that happens is connected *a priori*; everything that is simultaneous is connected *comitative*; everything that exists is connected *inhaesive*.\nThe axioms have a primitive, the analogies a derivative, the petitions an adopted certainty. [De] The derivative certainty from the nature of our thinking (g in general)[as] not as appearances, but actions of the subject, which thinking, [if it] insofar as it is to give an object, must [be] in a substance, be determined through a ground, and be combined with the whole of the power of representation. It is therefore derived from the subjective real condition of thinking in general. Everything that belongs to an aggregate (g objectively) is in reciprocal determination to one another, for otherwise it is only a subjective (g ideal) whole.\np. II (containing signature and date):\n(g Intuition\nThinking.\n*a priori*\nSensibility\nUnderstanding\nReason\nThe understanding therefore combines both extremes by connecting the *data* *a posteriori* with conditions *a priori*, but yet only *in concreto*, consequently [in a] to an empirical cognition.)\nThe ideal or real subject\n—\n—\n—\n—\nSeries\n—\n—\n—\n—\nAggregate.\nThe former are only [actus] actions of the mind, the second is something [unte] in the objects in themselves in relation to thinking without distinction of the subject.\nBoth are distinguished through the necessity of the relations, which flows from universality; the subject, which always lies at the ground, the member of the series, from which something determinate always follows, the manifold, of which each is determined by the others and in turn determines them.\nThe aggregate considered objectively must have a common ground of unity, through which the manifold depends on one another. (g The sequence from this is: much that agrees with one another has a common ground.)\n[See image below: successio aggregatio sustentatio]\nThe continuity in space and time.\nOn the intellectuation of apprehension.\na and b can in a threefold way by means of the x in relation\nx\n/ \\\nbe: either a:b or a:x:b or a+b=x.\nThe inner necessity of the appearance, namely when the same is detached from everything subjective and [under a] is regarded as determinable through a universal rule (of the appearances), is the objective. The objective is the ground of the agreement of the appearances among one another.\n––––––––––\nIn all three unities necessity rules. Every aggregate is contingent; hence there must be something through which the *respectus* of it become necessary. Everything that happens is contingent, hence its origin must be necessary. Everything that breaks off.\n––––––––––\nThe objective is the ground of the agreement of the appearances. Hence threefold agreement: 1. in the common subject, 2. in the [a] common beginning, 3. in the common whole.\np. III (letter page). Across the letter:\nThe difference of all our cognitions[the] is according to matter (content, object) or form. As far as the latter is concerned, it is intuition or concept. The former is of the object insofar as it is given |\n, the latter: insofar as it is thought. The faculty of intuition is sensibility, that of thinking is understanding (of thinking *a priori*, without the object being given, reason). The understanding is therefore opposed to sensibility and to reason. The perfection of cognition according to intuition is aesthetic, according to concepts it is logical. The intuition is either of the object (*apprehensio*) or of ourselves; the latter (*apperceptio*) extends to all cognitions, also those of the understanding and of reason.\nTranscendental logic deals with cognitions of the understanding according to content, but undetermined with regard to the way in which objects are given.\nAbove the letter, parallel to it:\nThe condition of all apperception is the unity of the thinking subject. From this flows the connection (g of the manifold) according to a rule and in a whole, because [as well] the unity of the function must suffice for subordination as well as coordination.\np. IV:\nKey. Schaff.\nInkwell. Pen and knife. Paper. Writings. Books.\nSlippers. Boots. Fur coat. Cap. Night-trousers.\nNapkins. Tablecloth. Hand towel. Plates. Dish. Knife and fork. Salt cellar.\nBottle. Wine and beer glasses. Bottle of wine.\nTobacco. Pipes. Tea things. Tea. Sugar.\nBrush.\nOn concepts that nevertheless cannot be determined *a priori*, i.e. constructed.\n(g If x, which is the objective condition of a, is at the same time the subjective one of b, then there arises a synthetic proposition, which is true only restrictively. e.g. All existence belongs to a substance, everything that happens, [in a series] under a member of the series, everything that is simultaneous, in a whole ([dav] whose parts determine one another reciprocally.). x the time, in which (g insofar as in it) what happens is determined, is the subjective condition, to think it [ve] in the concept of the understanding only as [substance to think] sequence from a ground. The subjective condition signifies the condition of the specification of a concept of the understanding corresponding to this relation. Principles of this kind are not axioms. Actual anticipations of the appearance there are not. One finds them confirmed through experiences, because [the] laws of experience thereby become possible. Other appearances give no laws. They have no evidence, because not the appearances, but experiences become possible through them. Synthesis of thinking and of the appearance.)\nThe subjective conditions of the appearance, which can be cognized *a priori*, are space and time: intuitions.\nThe subjective condition of empirical cognition is the apprehension in time in general and thus according to conditions of inner sense in general.\nThe subjective condition of rational cognition (g is) the construction [in time] through the condition of apprehension in general.\n––––––––––\n[Rules of the critique]\n[Laws of intuition. Analogies of nature]\nThe universal relation of sensibility to the understanding and to reason is either that through which they are given *a priori* [or the], thus the (g sensible) condition of intuition, secondly the sensible condition of judgment in general about that which is given, finally the sensible condition of the concept *a priori*. The rules (g *a priori*), which enunciate these conditions, contain in general the relation of the subjective to the objective. Either of the subjective, through which the objective is given, or of that through which it is thought as given in general (as object) or is determined *a priori*.\nEverything that is given is thought under the universal conditions of apprehension. Thus the subjectively universal of apprehension is the condition of the objectively universal of intellection. Everything is thought *a priori* under the subjective condition of construction, although the latter is only problematic, i.e. the condition is not given, but yet necessary for construction. To determine *a priori* is to construct.",[]],"reflm:4676":["P. I–II:\n(g With all reality is the relation of substance to *accidens*, with that which happens: of ground to sequel etc etc.)\nThe proposition of identity and contradiction contains the comparison of two predicates a and b with x, but only [through] so, that [x by means] the |\nconcept a of x is compared with b (*substantive*), thus x becomes useless. It is a principle of form, not of content, thus merely logical. A principle of *analysis*, from which nothing is cognized objectively. [It can In cat] It can be cognized in categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive form. If I refer both predicates to x and thereby to one another, then it is synthetic: no x, which is learned, is without science, for there [signifies it] it requires the restriction of time, namely: simultaneously. The (g lack of) science contradicts, to be sure, learnedness, but not the human being x, who is learned, except insofar as he is learned. Thus the contradiction is directed either at the concept a, which I have of x, or at x, to which this concept does not necessarily attach, directed. The synthetic validity of b and *non b* with regard to x, which can be thought through the concept a or *non a*, is called exchange.\nBut if a cannot be separated from b in x, e.g. no x, that is a body, is indivisible, then it is to be seen that the x, which is thought through a, can never be thought through *non a*, that no [body] being, that has the nature of a body, can never become incorporeal, and that the a in itself with regard to x is no predicate, but is with it an exchange-concept and thus is valid *substantive*.\nDifference of the affirmative and negative propositions and of the principles of affirmation and negation, which are equal according to contents.\n––––––––––\nBut if a and b are not identical, whether they be used affirmatively or negatively, and x is not wholly (g determined) thought through the concept of a, then a and b are not in a logical but in a real relation (g something different) of *combination*, hence not of [identit] *involution*. Thus their relation is determined not through [them] their concepts in themselves, but by means of x, of which a contains the designation. How are such *syntheses* possible. x must be a *datum* of sensibility, [which then through] in which a *synthesis*, i.e. a relation of coordination, takes place; for [a discursive] this |\ncontains more than is thought through its concept a, and is the representation of a *in concreto*. Now there are three cases where a transcendental subject is sensible and [through Begr] gives at hand a relation of concepts: either that it is the [construction] intuition of a or the appearance of a or the appearance of a (g or the empirical cognition). In the first case [is] the relation of a:b follows from the construction of a=x. In the second [and third] from [the V Erf] [example of] [empirical object of a in d] the sensible condition of the intellection of a, in the third drawn from the observation. The first two *syntheses* are *a priori* (all three objective). Because in the second case a [the an Erfahrungsb] signifies a generally sensible condition of perception, but x the condition of the subject in general, in which the relation of all perceptions is determinable (for perceptions are not merely objective, but presuppose sensation, which has only subjective validity). So [the Ve] a will signify the general of perception, x the (g sensible) condition of the subject [therein] (*substratum*), in which this perception is to get its position. Consequently the condition of the *disposition*. b finally the general function of the mind, to determine for a its position in x, thus the *exponent* of the relation of the perceptions [in the mind toward one another], hence to determine their position according to a rule.\nP. III:\nIf something is apprehended, then it is taken up into the function of apperception. I am, I think, thoughts are in me. These are all relations, which, to be sure, do not give rules of appearance, but make it the case that all appearance be represented as contained under rules. The I makes up the *substratum* for a rule in general, and the apprehension relates every appearance to it.\nFor the origination of a rule three pieces are required: 1. x, as the *datum* for a rule (object of sensibility or rather sensible real representation). 2. a, the *aptitudo* for the rule or the condition through which it is related in general to a rule. 3. b, the *exponent* of the rule.\nIf now a norm for the rule of appearances in general or of experiences is to arise, e.g. Everything existing is in substance, then x is sensation in general as the specif: of reality. Because it is represented as reality, it becomes the matter of a rule or [it] becomes sensation capable of a rule, and a is only a function of the apprehension of the appearance as given in general. Because now everything must be given in time, it thus encompasses everything in itself, so b is an [e function] *actus* of apperception, namely [the] the consciousness of the subject, which apperceives, as of that which is given in the whole time, is necessarily combined with it, for otherwise the sensation would not be represented as belonging to me.\n––––––––––\nTranscendental *Thetic*: on the erection of the principles of pure reason. *Antithetic*: on the (g natural) use of these principles [in general]. For general logic also deals with the natural |\nuse [of the same] in the common understanding. The fundamental rules of the latter are abstracted from the common understanding, though not borrowed and derived from it. The general rules, however, or principles of thinking in general without determined objects or determination of cognition from the relation to the objects is at all times dialectical.\nP. IV:\nWe think everything to ourselves through predicates, thus there is at all times a relation to x. In judgments, however, there is a relation of a:b, which both relates to x. a and b in x, x by means of a:b, finally a+b=x.\n––––––––––\nThe (g absolute) predicate in general is reality and of what.\nDeterminate predicates (g relation predicates), which are real, go only to relations. Of these there are three, according to the three relations in judging.\nThe relation-predicates are transcendental, the relation of the predicates is logical.\nWhat does the relation predicate express in the mind for action and on what is its reference grounded, on the one hand to sensibility, on the other to the logical, so that through the first it gets reality, through the second the form of thinking.\nIs the x the form of (g inner) sensibility or the real of apprehension?\n===NOTES===\n- Satz = proposition (\"Satz der identitaet\"; \"setze\" in \"affirmativen und negativen setze\" is read as the plural of Satz, doubtful as written); Princip and Grundsatz are both rendered \"principle\"; Grundregeln = \"fundamental rules\".\n- wechsel / wechselbegrif rendered \"exchange\" / \"exchange-concept\" (one word for both); Einschränkung = \"restriction\", kept distinct from Schranke = limitation.\n- Stelle = \"position\" (not Ort = place); Beziehung / bezieht = \"reference\" / \"relates\", distinct from Verhaltnis = \"relation\"; bestimmen and determiniren are both \"determine\".\n- \"Erfahrungsb\", \"V Erf\", \"Ve\", \"identit\", \"Begr\", \"in d\", \"e function\" are broken-off words in deleted or abandoned text; left as they stand. \"specif:\" is an abbreviation left unresolved.\n- \"er\" (\"wird er die Materie\", \"wird [er] Empfindung ... fähig\"): \"it\" renders a masculine pronoun with no clear antecedent; \"Empfindung einer Regel fähig\" could also read \"capable of sensation of a rule\", and the English keeps only one reading.\n- \"der letzten\" (\"the latter\") has an unclear antecedent in the German; \"vor Handlung\" (\"for action\") leaves open \"for / before / as\"; \"als überhaupt gegeben\" is kept in the German order, \"as given in general\".\n- \"Urtheilen\" is \"judgments\" at P. IV (\"In Urtheilen\") but \"judging\" in \"im Urtheilen\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Erkenntnis = \"cognition\"; \"welches beydes sich ... bezieht\" keeps the singular verb.\n- Italics are used on Latin words only; \"combination\" is italicized as Latinate, the German Verbindung/combinatio not being distinguishable here; the sign (g ...) is carried over unchanged.",[]],"reflm:4677":["P. I:\nOnly by this, that [that] the relation, which is posited according to the conditions of intuition, is assumed as determinable according to a rule, [is from the] does the appearance refer to an *object*; otherwise it is only an inner *affection* of the mind.\n––––––––––\nEverything that is thought as an object of intuition stands under a rule of *construction*.\nEverything that is thought as an object of perception stands under a rule of *apperception*, self-perception.\n(g Experience in general. Either intuition or sensation.)\nThe appearance is made objective by this, that it is brought, as contained, under a title of self-perception. and thus the original relations of *apprehension* are the conditions [as well] of the perception of the (g real) relations in the appearance, and precisely thereby, that one says: an appearance belongs under it, it is determined from the universal and is represented objectively, i.e. thought. Thereby, that one represents it not as belonging [to] under the functions of self-sensation, but [as] by means of single perception, it is called mere sensation. We can just so *a priori* from the functions of perception [determine] these with regard to the objective, i.e. of the conditions [of the], which are independent of the single relations of the senses, determine as with regard to the relations of space and time. The mind must have a faculty to *apprehend*, and the functions of this are just as necessary to perception as the *receptivity* of appearances.\nIf we intuited intellectually, then no titles of *apprehension* would be needed in order to represent an object to oneself. It would then not appear at all. Now [is has] the appearance must [a ground] be subordinated to a function, whereby the mind disposes over it, and indeed to a universal condition of the same, because otherwise nothing universally valid would be encountered in it.\n––––––––––\nAll synthetic propositions have a condition of sensibility (an opening one) Either for the intuition [or and War] |\nconstruction exposition\nspecification\n(pure or empirical) or of thinking through the understanding or of insight through reason.\nThe x thus always contains the condition.\nEither an objective one of the appearance or a subjective one of the pure intuitions, both in judgments, where the predicate is sensible,\nor an objective one of the understanding with regard to the [perception] *intellection* or a subjective one of reason with regard to the *conception*, both with intellectual predicates.\n(g In an analytic proposition, then, the subject is always taken *substantively*. The concept of learnedness is contradicting the concept of the unlearned.)\nP. II:\nAll appearances refer to the concept of an object, which is valid for them all together, e.g. appearances of a quadrilateral; they stand thus under rules of estimation, by which this concept can be determined (optical illusion). The perceptions are not appearances alone, i.e. representations [of that] of the appearances, but of the existence of the same. E.g. That reality is there, that it is one after another, that it is (g with) other reality simultaneous. Perception is the *position* in inner sense in general and goes to sensation according to relations of *apperception* of self-consciousness, according to which we become conscious of our own existence. All perception stands just as well under a rule of estimation.\nThe *praesumtion* is no *anticipation*, because it does not determine, but only says that something is determinable according to a rule still to be found, according to a certain given exponent. It serves thus to attempt this deter|mination and to expound the appearance, and is the *principium* of the estimation of the same. E.g. What happens has its ground in some preceding thing.",[]],"reflm:4678":["P. I:\nThat in the soul there lies a *principium* of *disposition* as well as of *affection*. That appearances can have no other order and cannot belong otherwise to the unity of the power of representation than by being in accordance with the common *principium* of *disposition*. For all appearance with its thoroughgoing determination must after all have unity in the mind, consequently be subjected to such conditions whereby the unity of the representations is possible. Only that which is demanded for the unity of the representations belongs to the objective conditions. The unity of *apprehension* is necessarily combined with the unity of intuition, space and time, for without this the latter would give no real representation.\nThe *principia* of *exposition* must be determined on the one hand through the laws of *apprehension*, on the other through the unity of the faculty of understanding. They are the standard of observation and not borrowed from perceptions, but from the ground of the same in the whole. (g originally and abstracted from the same.)\nPure thinking (*a priori*), however, in relation to experiences, i.e. to objects of the senses, contains principles which contain the origin of all experiences, i.e. of that which is thoroughgoingly determined for the experiences.\nWe must expound concepts if we cannot construct them. Appearances we cannot construct, although intuitions. But we must have rules of their *exposition*. These rules are really rules of the appearance itself, but insofar as the inner of the same is to be discovered in the resolution of the same. The |\nrules thus of the resolution of appearances are properly the conditions of *apprehension*, insofar as it passes from one to another and *conjugates* them.\n[The perceptions stand under rules of the intuitions the conjugated under rules of the]\nThe principle: everything that is thought stands under a rule, for only by means of the rule is it an object of thinking.\n––––––––––\nThe *synthesis* (g *principium* of the) contains rules of thinking *a priori*, but insofar as it is determined to objects. Thus there is in it 1. pure thinking (g a) and the rule of the same, 2. the condition of the object, i.e. under which something is given (g x) (or brought) to be thought as object, 3. the determination of the thought from this relation (g b)/.\nThe *principium* of *analysis*: a rule of thinking in general. The *principia* of thinking, insofar as it is restricted by the condition of the subject or determined to the subject, are not principles, but *restrictions*. (1. On the possibility of empirical *synthesis* in general.) Cognition is determined *a priori* to an object, when it 1.\nconstruction\ngoes to the condition whereby object is given, and the cognition represents the same only through concepts of the appearance. 2. when it goes to [conditions of *apprehension* in general whereby] appearance, insofar as it contains the conditions to make a concept of it for oneself, |\nP. II: |\n3. when it goes to *apprehension* in general, insofar as it contains the condition of the unity of *perception* as well as of *intellection*, i.e. of the agreement of the appearances among one another and with the unity of the mind, consequently of *exposition*.\nThe [first go] second goes to the appearances, insofar as they can be brought under titles of thinking, e.g. what always is, is substance; the 1st to appearances among one another *a priori*, e.g. A triangle has three angles; the 3rd to that which makes up the thoroughgoing determination of the appearance.\nBesides these there are subjective *principia* of thinking and objective ones of thinking or determining according to rules *a priori*.\nOf the synthetic propositions: all objects of the senses are in space and time.\nAll objects of experience are under a rule of sensibility.\nSensation\nIntuition\nAppearance\nConcept\nThe determinability of the object according to the\nSensation: *perceptio positiva*,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nIntuition: *construction*,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nAppearance: *disposition*,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nConcept: *comprehension*.\nIn an analytic proposition the x drops out entirely, because a taken *substantively* already stands with b in a determinate identical relation.\nBut in synthetic propositions the x is that in which a and through the condition of a b is determined.\nIn the (g Through the) *construction* x of the concept a (triangle) the equality of 3 angles etc. is at the same time determined in the triangle. Through the *specification* x of the concept a the *relation* b is at the same time determined in this a.\nIf I determine the arising *specifice* in time, i.e. a reality in the series of time, then time is indeed the condition in which, but the rule the condition through which.\nIf x is the sensible condition under which a is specifically *determined*, then b is the universal function whereby it is determined therein.",[]],"reflm:4679":["P. I:\nWe are conscious of ourselves and of our own actions and of appearances, insofar as we become conscious of the apprehension of them, either by our coordinating them with one another or by apprehending one sensation through the other.\nWe would therefore not become conscious of appearances at all if we did not breaks off.\nAn object of the senses is only that which affects my senses, thus acts and is therefore substance. Hence the category of substance is principial. Every beginning of a state of the representation is always a transition from a previous one, for otherwise we would not perceive that the former had begun. Hence, since always the same subject holds of one object as well as of the other and also the boundary is common to both, the following one belongs to the previous one as to that which determines it. In the unity of the mind a whole is possible only thereby, that the mind alternately determines the other out of one partial representation and all together be comprehended in one action, which holds of all.\n––––––––––\nx:a = m:n\nIf a indicates the condition through which x is given, then the relation of a:b follows according to the proposition of contradiction and holds generally without limitation of the at the same time. E.g. in every reality (it is given only through the action of the subject) there is a relation of the substance to the accidens. x signifies here the subject. a the apprehension of the object. x:a is therefore the relation of the original action of the breaks off.\n––––––––––\nMere apprehension already declares that behind the appearance a substance, cause of the composition, must be; but the observation and judgment must show which is the substance etc. Where there is an action, there is substance, e.g. in the case of light, warmth; but whether light is substance does not flow from the apprehension, but from the exposition of the appearance. That something is represented as having happened is enough to regard it as effect; for in fact the apprehension of it is an effect that occurs in the mind etc.\nThe intellectual functions thus make the beginning in the apprehension, but the specification gives us the rule of application of this concept; hence determinate rules of synthesis can be given only through experience, but the universal norm of the same a priori.\nP. II:\nEmpirical intuition is appearance.\nAppearance, of which one is conscious, is perception.\nEvery perception must be brought under a title of the understanding, because otherwise it gives no concept at all and nothing is thought thereby. By means of these concepts we make use of appearances, or rather the concepts indicate the way in which we make use of appearances as the matter for thinking. 1. of intuition in general for magnitude, 2. of sensation, in order to determine the real relation in the appearance. We say: the stone weighs, the wood falls (g the body moves), i.e. it acts, thus it is substance. The field is prepared, the meadow dried out, the glass broken: these are effects, which relate to a cause. The wall is firm, the wax soft, the gold dense: these are connections in the composite. Without such concepts the appearances would altogether be separated and not belong to one another. If they have like relations to one another in space or time, these are nevertheless not determined from the objects of the appearances, but only placed next to one another.\nExperience is an understood perception. But we understand it when we represent it to ourselves under titles of the understanding. Experience is a specification of the concepts of the understanding through given appearances. Appearances are the matter or the substratum.\nExperiences are thus possible only thereby, that it is presupposed that all appearances belong under titles of the understanding, i.e. in all mere intuition there is magnitude, in all appearance substance and accidens. In the change of the same, cause and effect, in the whole of the same, interaction. Thus these propositions hold of all objects of experience. Precisely the same propositions also hold of the mind with regard to the generation of its own representations and are *momenta* of the genesis. Under the titles of apperception, however, all appearances must be brought, so that they as well according to intuition constructed breaks off.\nThe conditions, however, of the subsumption under these concepts are taken from the sensible relation, which stands in analogy with the action of the understanding and belongs to inner sense, of which the apperception the breaks off.\nWhence is that which acts regarded as if it were constant and as if only the actions, effects and compositions vary.",[]],"reflm:4680":["Everything that happens is, for the sake of the determination of its concept among the appearances, i.e. with regard to the possibility of experience, represented as contained under a rule, of which the relation is expressed through a concept of the understanding. In the appearance x, therefore, in which a is a concept, there must be contained, besides what is thought through a, conditions of its specification, which make a rule necessary, whose function is expressed by b. a cannot be specifically determined otherwise in time, when it happens, than by means of a rule. Thus no experience of a can take place without rule. Thus the proposition of sufficient ground is a *principium* of the rule of experience, namely to carry out such.\nThe proposition that everything that in time follows upon something follows upon something else in accordance with a rule, or that with regard to its sequence a rule takes place, does not lie in the specification of the concept a of happening or of contingency, for there only the appearance is meant. (However, happening is already an existence according to a rule of time.)\nThe ordering of appearances according to the relation of space and time requires a rule, just as appearance itself a form.",[]],"reflm:4681":["S. I:\nBefore an occurrence something must always precede (condition of perception).\nBefore an occurrence all sorts of things can precede, but there is one among these upon which it always follows.\nA reality always hangs (g on a point of time and on that which determines it) on something accompanying, [what] through which the point of time is determined for it (condition of perception).\nThere is all sorts of accompanying, but among this there is something that is always there.\n[An aggregate is many in reciprocal relation but among this must]\n(g With [the] regard to that which is simultaneous, there is always taking-together (condition of perception).)\nIt can (g however with) be taken together with all sorts of things; but [where the many is reciprocally determined this combination is objectively a] where something is to be considered as objectively combined together, there is a reciprocal determination of the manifold among one another.\nIf something were not always, thus something [subsisted] permanent, *stabile*, there would be no fixed point or determination of the point of time, thus no perception, i.e. *determinatio* of something in time.\nIf something did not constantly (g before an occurrence) precede, then among the many that precede there would be nothing [what] to which that which happens belongs in a series, it would have no determinate position in the series.\nThrough the rules of perception the [things] objects of the senses are determinable in time, in intuition they are merely given as appearances. According to those rules a quite different series is found than that in which the object was given.\nS. II:\n(g Nothing synthetic can be objectively valid except that which is the condition, except that through which it is given as object or through which something that has been given is thought as object. Object is only thought insofar as it stands under a rule of appearance, and the receptivity of the rule [makes that the Er] is what makes the appear|ance\nobjective; thus not the appearances stand under a rule, but the objects that lie at their ground. According to this rule they are exposited.)\nWithout [any] such rules of perception no experiences could be made, because these are the titles of appearances, just as the [sensible concepts of the Fo] sensible concepts are titles of intuitions.\nRule of the *praesumtionen* as estimation of appearances for judgments to be provisionally determined.\n(g One can indeed see much, but understand nothing of what appears, except when it is brought under concepts of the understanding and by means of them into relation to a rule; this is the assumption through the understanding.)\n(g The *synthesis* contains the relation of the appearances not in perception, but in the concept. That every relation in [concept must] perception nevertheless still presupposes a relation in the concept, indicates that the mind contains in itself the universal [source] and sufficient source of the *synthesis* and all appearances are exponible in it.)\n*principia* of perception.\n[Rules] Principles of observation or of the exposition of appearances in general.\nThey are *praesumtionen* of experience.\nAnalogies of the understanding.\nAxioms of intuitions, analogies of the understanding, *petitionen* of reason.\nWe perceive something only by being conscious of our *apprehension*, consequently of the existence in our inner sense, [consequently] |\nthus as belonging to one of the three relations in the mind. All observation requires a rule.\nThe intellectual of perception goes to the force of inner sense. [The principles of obser] The analogies of observation go to thoroughgoing perception or to the thoroughly determined perception.\nAll combinations are made by the mind, and the mind combines nothing objectively except what is necessarily determined from its *correlatum*; otherwise the representations are indeed placed together, but not connected, indeed in perception, but not in the concept.\n––––––––––\nOnly that which is capable of constant principles in the mind we call object. Thus estimation must precede objective judgments. For everything else, which does not accept such principles, is nothing for us and also cannot be perceived. Because perception requires a *coniugation* according to a universal [either] ground.\n===NOTES===\n- Warnehmung/wargenommen = perception/perceived (Wahrnehmung); Beobachtung = observation; Beurtheilung = estimation; Urtheil = judgment.\n- Grundsatz = principle (\"Grundsatze\"), rendered with *principia* where Kant writes the Latin; Satz = proposition. Both Latin and German forms stand for \"principle\".\n- Stelle = position (not in the glossary); Verknüpfung/verknüpft = connection/connected; Verbindung = combination; zusammengestellt = placed together; Zusammennehmung = taking-together.\n- Gemüth = mind; Kraft = force; Annahme = assumption; Dasein = existence; zureichend = sufficient.\n- Latin/Latinate forms left as Kant has them: *praesumtionen*, *petitionen*, *apprehension*, *coniugation*, *correlato*; *stabile* and *determinatio* are Kant's Latin.\n- \"woran begleitendem\" (third paragraph) is syntactically obscure; kept as \"on something accompanying\". \"[bestand]\" is a deleted word of doubtful reading (noun or verb form); rendered \"[subsisted]\".\n- \"Er|scheinung\" is broken across a line at | in the German; kept. Deletions in brackets and (g ...) insertions are carried over as in the edition.\n- \"exponibel\" = exponible; \"exponirt\" = exposited (cf. Erörterung/expositio).",[]],"reflm:4682":["S. I:\nThe concept: what happens there, is a determination of sensibility, but through the understanding, in that Something is posited in the sequence of time. Now this cannot happen otherwise than in relation to something preceding. Accordingly the rule, that what happens there is determined by something preceding, says nothing other than (g that all this is determinable in the order of time) that since the determination [of the re of et] of a position of existence in time must happen through the understanding, hence according to a rule.\nReality is that through which something is an object of perception. »With every reality there is a relation of the accident to the substance« wants |\nto say as much as: The determination of an existence in time in general can happen only through something that is in all time.\nThe analogies of appearance want to say as much: if I did not determine every relation of it through a universal condition of relation in time, I would assign to no appearance its position.\nThus the concepts substance, ground and whole serve only to assign to each [appearance] reality in the appearance its position, in that |\nS. II: |\neach represents a function or [power of] dimension of time, in which the object that is perceived is to be determined and out of the appearance experience become.\n===NOTES===\n- Stelle = position (not in the glossary); Daseyn = existence; Begebenheit not present; Zeitfolge = sequence of time (Folge = sequence).\n- Warnehmung/wargenommen = perception/perceived; Bestimmung = determination; Realität = reality; Obiekt = object.\n- The last clause (\"und aus der Erscheinung Erfahrung werden\") has no finite verb agreement made explicit; kept literally as \"experience become\", with \"soll\" governing both infinitives.\n- Syntax of the first paragraph (\"nichts anders aus, als ... daß da die Bestimmung ... geschehen müsse\") is obscure and kept as it stands; the (g ...) insertion interrupts it.\n- \"[of the re of et]\" and \"[power of]\" (potentz der) are deleted fragments, the first doubtful/truncated.\n- \"will so viel sagen\" at the | break is rendered \"wants to say as much\"; \"Accidentz/substantz\" are Latinate spellings of accident/substance.",[]],"reflm:4683":["S. I:\n1.\nA synthetic proposition that holds of all things in general is false, and that in general whose subject is a pure concept of the understanding. Unless it is not to hold objectively (g absolutely), but only under the subjective restriction of the use of reason.\nOnly the conditions of sensibility make the *synthesin* possible. 1. of the pure, 2. of the empirical intuition (outer and inner sense).\nFurther of the empirical or rational use of my understanding. For only in the sensible condition of a lies something in which someone, besides the concept of a, can cognize b as well.\nS. II:\n2.\nAll synthetic propositions have a homogeneity, although it seems that one concept is intellectual, the other empirical. In the exposition they are [empirical] homogeneous. One need only take, instead of the concept, its *specification* [and].\n*Concipere* means to make for oneself a concept of something a priori. The principles of conception [go to subj] are either of thinking in general or of absolute positing or of taking-together a priori. Of the first the sensible condition is the[reception] whole sensibility, of the second the whole thinking with regard to a *datum* in general, of the third the whole in itself or totality.\n––––––––––\nThe understanding can determine nothing in sensibility except through a universal act. E.g. Arising through a universal condition of sequence. Existence through a subject of all existence. Being-together through a universal unity.\n===NOTES===\n- Satz = proposition; Grundsatz = principle; Verstand = understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Anschauung = intuition; Erkenntnis/erkennen = cognition/cognize.\n- \"synthesin\" is the Greek accusative as written; left in italics. \"dati\" is Latin genitive, rendered *datum*; \"specification\" is Kant's Latinate form (*specificatio*-type), kept as \"specification\".\n- \"totalitaet\" (Latinate) and Allheit both would be \"totality\"; only \"totalitaet\" occurs here.\n- \"und der überhaupt, dessen subiect\" is elliptical; kept as \"and that in general whose subject\" (the antecedent is ambiguous between \"proposition\" and \"that which holds\").\n- \"Vom ersten ... vom zweyten ... vom dritten\" is elliptical; kept. \"die[Empfang] gantze\" has a deleted word \"[Empfang]\" (reception) with no space in the edition; kept.\n- \"[empirical]\", \"[des]\", \"[und]\", \"[gehen auf subj]\" are deletions/fragments carried over; \"subj\" is an abbreviated, truncated word.\n- Handlung = act; setzen = posit; Folge = sequence; Daseyn = existence; zusammenseyn = being-together.",[]],"reflm:4684":["S. I:\nHow can one know what more is contained in a thing in general, which is not given at all to the senses, beyond what one actually thinks through one's concept a. But because one time, in which something happens, is not to be distinguished from another, the [must] can sequence only be determined through a rule of time, and thus we can, in the sensible condition, still more than was thought in a, namely in this time as a *construction* (of the triangle) every time, where, if just the same member is, just the *correlatum* is, represent to ourselves. We thus represent to ourselves the object through an *analogon* of the construction, namely that it let itself be constructed before inner sense, namely that, just as something follows upon something other, always, when something happens, something other follow upon it, or that this representation be one of the general actions of the determination of appearances, which therefore give a rule |\njust as a triangle is constructed only according to a rule and serves all as a rule.\n––––––––––\nIn analytic judgments the predicate goes properly to the concept a, in synthetic ones to [the condition of] the object of the concept, because the predicate is not contained in the concept. The object, however, that corresponds to a concept has certain conditions of the execution of this concept, i.e. of the position *in concreto* (for every concept is a general action, which presupposes a *substratum*, in which the [object] representation of the object can be posited). Now the condition of all concepts is [proper] sensible; thus, if the concept is also sensible, but general, it must be considered in its *concretum*, e.g. triangle in its construction. If the concept signifies not pure intuition, but empirical, i.e. experience, then the x contains the condition of the relative position (a) in space and time, i.e. the condition of determining something general therein.\nOtherwise appearances are determined through time, in the *synthesis*, however, time through an appearance, e.g. of that which exists or happens or is together. These are the most general of appearances, of which [the real the] the reality is the matter.\nOf the intellectuation of appearance, e.g.: Something exists, something happens. This is indeed already an *intellectuale*, placed into the form of time. The something is reality (sensation), the happening is the existence as sequel. Through what, now, does the appearance become intellectual?\n––––––––––\nIn a synthetic judgment two pure concepts of reason can never stand in relation to one another, but a pure concept of the understanding with a concept under sensible condition, be it of appearance or of representation *a priori*. Cause of this.\nThe sum total of the laws of free actions, which in a natural way are determined through the communal choice, is right. Under choice I understand the will determining with force.\n––––––––––\nWhence do the analogies of the understanding have no evidence. They are nevertheless constitutive, only not directly objective.\nS. II:\nRational psychology (*psychologia rationalis*) as to the general predicates of the human soul:\n1. As to the subject. [Sim] Substance. Single in the human being. Simple. Immaterial.\n2. As to the forces. Representative force (*vis repraesentativa*), not single. Spontaneity, either practically or transcendentally absolute (freedom), either original or derivative.\n(g *Arbitrium* is either brute or free, and freedom either original or derivative; freedom is opposed either to brute necessity or to fatality, the former in the practical sense, the latter in the transcendental sense; in the transcendental sense, therefore, it is either original or derivative.)\n3. As to the state, either of human life: *Commercium* *) of body and soul, or of birth **) or of death.\n*(g either ideal or real)\n**(g either epigenesis or preformation)\nThe soul after death [is] either [contingently] lives from decree or from its nature, the latter life is immortality.\nThe argument of immortality is either natural or moral. The former either from rational principles (metaphysical) or empirical (physio|logical).\nThe metaphysical argument is concluded from the principle of life joined with inert matter devoid of life, that the principle of life through separation from matter is not deprived of life, but is liberated from the hindrances of life. And therefore animal life ceases after death and [or free] spiritual begins, as the soul is liberated.\nThe physiological argument is either strict or analogical (g this the best of all from human nature). The former cannot be given either for or against, since we have no experiences of the soul separated from the *commercium* of the body, because we think only in this *commercium*. Nor can one argue against from the dependence of the soul on the body in this life, because we do not see what the body does to the soul, [sim] when at the same time it is a hindrance to it, as, if someone is tied to a carriage, he cannot move himself unless he moves it. If the wheels are properly greased, the motion still proceeds freely, but they are not the causes of this motion, although, if they are braked by some cause internal to the carriage, they render the man immobile.\nThe moral argument is practically sufficient although not apodictic. It is teleological, drawn from the wisdom, goodness and justice of God, in one word from the laws of providence, and is most suited to common sense.\n(g animal life — spiritual)\nThe state after death is either of regeneration or of liberation, the former either to earthly life (metempsychosis) or heavenly (corpusc.).\nLife after death either [of the soul] of one dreaming or of one waking.\nThe former *Hypnopsychita* (g revival or) with personality or with the Lethean cup.",[]],"reflm:4685":["Innenseite I:\nThe real differences of the logical acts (*actuum*) are not with ....... │\nThe absolute of the actions of the understanding goes to the object, │ the relative to other representations, e.g. to the logical relation. │\nThe real differences of concepts are essential, the logical │ arbitrary, e.g. substance is no *accidens*, but subject and predicate │ are alternately etc. │\nInnenseite II:\nThe *qvidditas* is reality or existence through │ some predicate. And [its] the difference among the same | from others that have the same predicate is quality. │\nWilliam of Tyre's history of the sacred war | with the continuation of Herold. Basel. 1549.",[]],"reflm:4686":["S. I:\nAll appearances stand in community, i.e. there is nothing in the empty.\n––––––––––\nIn order to determine something, we must have something determining, therefore the first or complete determination *a priori* is impossible.\nThe dispute between the conditioned and the unconditioned.\nS. II:\nDifference of evidence.\nTopical. Place in the mind, place among one another.\nThe mind can become conscious of itself only through the appearances that correspond to its dynamical functions, and of the appearances only through [that which] its dynamical functions.\nAppearances, if they were seen through in their continuous interconnection, can only the dynamical functions breaks off, the rest is cut away.",[]],"reflm:4687":["Objects are given either in appearance or in the idea, and thought either in intuition or in the concept (*conceptus*). In the idea the object is given that is only possible thereby. This is perfection. This cannot be borrowed from things, but things are perfect insofar as they agree with the idea. The ideas are *repraesentationes archetypae* (archetypal representations), the sensible concepts *ectypae*, the concepts of the understanding are *reflectentes*.\nEither the conditions (*conditiones*), through which the objects are given, are those, that they are thought: [pr] *cognitio prototypa* (prototypal cognition); or they are thought only thereby, that they are given: *ectypae*.",[]],"reflm:4688":["The absolute is the relation to thoroughgoing determination. Agreement is absolute possibility; the position in every aim: actuality; actuality through possibility *in sensu absoluto* (in the absolute sense): absolute necessity.",[]],"reflm:4689":["From a particular *major propositio* (major proposition) one infers only to a possible, from a singular to the actual conclusion, which is contained in that, from a general to the necessity of the conclusion.\n(*Qvidditas*) *qvaeitas*, *qvalitas* (quality), *qvantitas* (quantity).",[]],"reflm:4690":["The inner necessity of a being is impossible to think. For in itself, from what pertains internally to a thing, the abolition of the same with all predicates is always possible.\nBut the [absolu] respective necessity can well be thought, and the necessity in every respect (*respectu*) as condition of the possibility of all things is absolute necessity.\nIt is the necessity of a general *substratum* of the manifoldness of concepts of things.",[]],"reflm:4691":["Beside, in and \nFirstly the alteration of the thing is the persistence of the thing. The thing is altered, in that its affections change.",[]],"reflm:4692":["Dependence proves inner contingency.",[]],"reflm:4693":["Alteration proves the contingency of the state;\nArising and passing away proves the contingency of the thing.\nBoth only the inner contingency, namely in itself; not the conditioned, (g that) under the circumstances and [in] the conditions also the opposite of what happens be possible.\nBecause we can never experience the contradictory opposite, the absolute contingency, i.e. in every *respectu*, also cannot be inferred from experience. *Omnimoda contingentia* (contingency in every way) (g *possibilitas*). Freedom is that whose sequels are absolutely contingent.\nThe possibility of the *contrarie oppositi* (contrarily opposed) does not prove contingency at another time. Rest of a moved body. Not-being and the opposite are not one and the same.\nThe *contradictorie oppositum* (contradictorily opposed), namely at the same time, (g as possible) is chance.\nThe *oppositum* at another time is *contrarie oppositum*.",[]],"reflm:4694":["*Omnitudo distributiva* (distributive allness) is *universalitas* (universality), the collective: *totalitas* (totality), absolute *totalitas*: *Vniversitas*.",[]],"reflm:4695":["The *compositum* (composite), in which there is *completudo* (completeness, allness of the parts), is a *totum* (whole), and this *completudo* is called *totalitas* (totality). *Simul sumtio* (taking together at once).",[]],"reflm:4696":["The bounds of [aggregation] composition: infinitely large, or no bounds (g immeasurable), of decomposition: infinitely small. Extensive, protensive, intensive magnitude.",[]],"reflm:4697":["A magnitude in which each unity is itself a magnitude is continuous; consequently one that contains no absolute unities in itself, hence in itself makes up no number [in itself], but multitude in general. Point, moment.\nOn the *maximum*. The greatest number. The infinite multitude. On the bounds and limitations. On the finite measure.\nThe infinite of increase is possible. But the infinite of taking together is not. *infinitudo progressiva, collectiva*.",[]],"reflm:4698":["At the top of the page:\nTo cognize and to determine the *quantitas phaenomenon* teaches |\nmathematics, the *quanta noumena* only philosophy. Totality, unrestrictedness. — Reality, degree etc etc.",[]],"reflm:4699":["Divisibility is no *accidens*; *accidens* is at all times positive, because it is an existence.\nBodies are *phaenomena substantiata*, not because they are composite, but because they are outer appearances, where the first subject is lacking.\nThinking beings I can represent to myself only as substances, because I lend them my I.",[]],"reflm:4700":["At the top of the page, \nIn a judgment the singular proposition expresses the unity, the particular the plurality and the universal the *omnitudinem*. A general proposition expresses the combination of the many that stands under the general concept of a subject, through the common predicate, or rather the combination in the *sphaera* of a concept; if the predicate is a *reciprocum* of the subject, then [is] the subject fills out the *sphaera* and designates a *totum*. The conditioned proposition expresses the relation of the ground to the sequel, which (g a) ground is either of subordinated sequels in a *sorites* or of coordinated |\nin the *copulativo*. Third, that the predicate is in the subject as an *accidens*, expresses the combination of the accidents in a substance.\nAll combination of the many is unity. Hence much is combined in a substance or through a ground or into a whole.\nThat from two things, from the one to the other can be inferred as its [sequel] predicate or as its sequel or from two things to the unity of a composite, experience must teach.\nWith regard to quantity just these relations hold as with regard to quality. Position, *defectus* and privation have the signs +a, 0, and –a.",[]],"reflm:4701":["The magnitude of substance is always cognized and measured through the persistence in its own state with all grounds of alteration. If an air has much elasticity, then the quantity of the substance is not to be measured from this elasticity, but from the *inertia* with which it resists the expansion of the same or also every force that [it] moves its mass.",[]],"reflm:4702":["The proposition: no substance perishes, means as much as: all alterations always relate to a subject, hence no alteration or change of the subjects can take place, because they would otherwise only be alterations. Hence these are a thread that is always inseparably fastened to certain points. The subjects are also called the standing, with regard to which one can cognize the rest as variable.",[]],"reflm:4703":["Opposite and \nNo substance can perish by itself. For to that an action is required, through which it is canceled. But it cannot cancel itself. No substance, however, can annihilate another either (if this one does not exist through that one). Because one can flow only into the state of the other and indeed only that which is common to its states.",[]],"reflm:4704":["The *principium generale commercii* is: all influence in the world is partly the effect of the acting one in the suffering one, partly counter-effect of the latter. This counter-effect's inner state and determination of the substance is the action, through which the *accidens* of the influence is actuated (apprehension). There is therefore [a] unity of the effecting forces of different substances in every *commercium*.",[]],"reflm:4705":["*compositum* (composite) either substantial or accidental.\nA *compositum* of substances and accidents is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4706":["The unity is either of composition or of decomposition; the former is the numeral unity, the latter the simple; both unity of the mathematical synthesis.",[]],"reflm:4707":["The question is whether a given magnitude is infinite. The condition is that it be given. Now through the at the same time the being given is indicated. On the other hand it is not given in apprehension or in determination.",[]],"reflm:4708":["(s Lex isonomiae.)\nA first beginning is impossible. For beginning can be thought only according to laws of sensibility, consequently only in a time occupied by objects of sensibility. Consequently a first beginning, before which no *phaenomenon* preceded, cannot be thought. Thus all generations are only alterations. But a first cause can well be thought, because this is merely something intellectual. That one cannot therefore serve for the explication of appearances: the cause of appearances must be in *commercio* with the world. From which it follows that nothing can arise without something, on the other hand, being canceled, so that the sum of reality remains. For otherwise time itself (the absolute) would stand in relation to the alterations and the cause of the alteration would be outside time. The *universum* itself cannot move, neither in empty space, nor much less in an entirely full space. For otherwise in the first case [the space] would be an appearance, to which the *correlatum* is no appearance, in the second a motion in no space at all would be. Hence every cause of motion is in community with the world, and none is possible, except that just as much is generated on the opposite side. A spirit that moved a matter absolutely, would effect this motion of the *universi* [determine]. He who |\nonly so far can move as he remains at rest in the sum, is in union with matter.",[]],"reflm:4709":["Beside and \nIn the appearance two things can be distinguished through the mere *relationes*, but not generally through the understanding. (s *diversitas phaenomenon*.)",[]],"reflm:4710":["If entirely similar things from different places were exchanged, then everything would be as before.\nAnalytic identity; synthetic of space and time.",[]],"reflm:4711":["The question is whether one can say: a thing is in two places at once, if [that] which is in one place is *interne* (g entirely) one and the same with that in the other.\nSpace also makes possible the real outer and, according to analogy, the inner conflict.",[]],"reflm:4712":["The question is whether 2 things can be different merely *numero* or whether they must be throughout specifically *plura* in order to be more than one thing.",[]],"reflm:4713":["93'. E II 860. 861. 894.\nEverything that is, is necessary, either absolutely or in a conditioned way*, thus *ut causatum alterius* (as the caused of another).\n*(s We therefore cognize also in all relations of the manifold in the composite a necessity, which must at all times be derived. Originally contingent nothing can be (g |\nM 93': |\nrather the reverse: every *causatum* is contingent in itself). |\nM 93: |\nIt is only asked: what is contingent? and on what does the *principium necessitatis* rest? On this, because otherwise there would be no determining grounds *a priori*.)\nThe *principium rationati* says only: everything that happens is necessary under a condition.\nEverything must be determined objectively *a priori*.\nEverything alterable has a beginning, thus arises and is contingent and is *causatum alterius*.",[]],"reflm:4714":["The *completudo logica* (logical completeness) is *universalitas*, the *realis* is *universitas*; just so *communitas* and *communio*.",[]],"reflm:4715":["We have three [prae] categories and their *praedicamenta* (predicaments): first of position (being and not being); 2. of *respectus*. 3. Of *completu|dinis*.\nThe first is whether something is or not; the second, what there is with respect to another or not; the third, how much of a thing is together etc. *Qvaeitas*, *qualitas*, *quantitas propositionis*.",[]],"reflm:4716":["The proof of Leibniz, who takes the world *intellectualiter*, from the force of representation of the monads, is not bad, except that it nevertheless proves more than it concludes; it leads to an idealism.",[]],"reflm:4717":["That in the series and the aggregate of sensibility there is no beginning and no totality, i.e. determinability with regard to the *omnitudinis*, comes from this, that the All here must be sufficient for the determination according to conditions of sensibility, consequently for the addition or the progression without bounds. But this is no judgment about the world in itself, in whose concept the All precedes, and from which each part alone is determinable. There are in this series not true things and true causes, but only appearances; and [a] the appearance in general must be without end, because in it alone the indeterminate action of sensible cognition can go on without end.",[]],"reflm:4718":["The outer senses give me nothing but impressions; only through the |\ninner intuition (which however goes only to myself) can I cognize the object that lies at the ground of the impressions. The question now arises: is that which we call body a particular kind of being, distinct from thinking ones, or is it only a particular appearance of their reciprocal presence. For the latter I have no ground; but I also cannot be refuted. This would be the mystical world.\n(s The physical or mystical idealist; the former denies objects of appearance altogether; the latter assumes such, but gives them other positive fanatical determinations.)",[]],"reflm:4719":["The transition from reality to negation is continuous, i.e. through infinite degrees of the smaller difference up to vanishing. Thus the decomposition of a body through a steady motion is also steady, and from the place in the body (outermost monad) it cannot occur immediately to a place outside the body, but through all degrees of the intervals between the two. Just so with every alteration.\nMonads can therefore be no objects of an empirical synthesis of outer appearance.\nOf the whole of all (g possible) decomposition and composition no experience can be given.",[]],"reflm:4720":["Opposite the section heading, as well as M § 406f.:\nSpace has in this something particular over the concept of time, that |\nfirst, the concept of time, hence the whole sensibility, can be thought on the determinations of the same; second, that a force that I posit anywhere in space, as the *principium* of appearances, does not remain merely a force in general, but through the relations of space to everything outer has determinate conditions and thus gives a determinate concept of the possibility of the object. Third, space is the ground of the possibility (g of the outer, hence) of the object, whereas time concerns only the state and in general goes to [everything] the mere existence [without d] as the ground of the relations of things in their existence and the measure of their duration. Hence time is no ground of the possibility of things, especially not of substances. Hence positive *principia intellectualia* (intellectual principles) are possible of physics, but not of [cosmology] psychology; everything in the latter is negative in relation to the pure doctrine of nature. 1. Lack of parts; 2. no such *commercium* (community) as matter has; 3. no such origin and perishing as body.",[]],"reflm:4721":["That a force is combined with motion is not *constat* (evident) from the motion, because in it no *accidens absolutum* (absolute accident) is to be encountered, but from the sensation that is to be encountered with motion, both when we move and when motion strikes us.",[]],"reflm:4722":["Nature simply or taken subjectively is that which corresponds to the necessary being.\nThe object of all empirical cognitions is nature.\nThe contingent all: nature.",[]],"reflm:4723":["Appearances are representations, insofar as we are affected. The representation of our (g free) self-activity is one in which we are not affected, consequently is not appearance, but [orig. free] apperception. Now the proposition of sufficient ground holds only as *principium* of the exposition of appearances, consequently not as exposition of the original intuitions.",[]],"reflm:4724":["We cannot prove freedom *a posteriori*, because the lack of perception of determining grounds gives no proof that there also are none of the kind. We also cannot cognize its possibility *a priori*, since the possibility of the original ground, which is not determined by another, cannot be comprehended at all. We can therefore prove it not at all theoretically, but as a necessary practical *hypothesis*.",[]],"reflm:4725":["The practical concept of freedom is that which suffices to do actions according to rules of reason, which thus gives to the imperatives of this one the power; the speculative or rationalizing concept of freedom is that which suffices to [Handl] explain free actions according to reason. The latter is impossible, because it is the original in the *derivativo* (derivative).",[]],"reflm:4726":["Over and \nOf which the ground (and its relation to the sequel) can be given through the senses |\nnot at all, i.e. incomprehensible. Now the ground of the higher desire is not sensible, so it cannot be gained insight into [be] be, because our reason has the use only *a posteriori* upon that which has been given to the senses.\nJust so can to the sense the frequent repetitions of the one in the *actualiter* (actually) infinite (namely the past according to time [and the] one another and the present at once), but well the *potentialiter* (potentially) infinite. Those are given entirely, but the generation of them is never entire.",[]],"reflm:4727":["Whether God is free, although he can do nothing evil?",[]],"reflm:4728":["Spirit is a pure intelligence. (pure is what is separated from everything foreign in kind.) Thus spirit is an intelligence, separated from all community with bodies. If in *psychologia rationalis* (rational psychology) I abstract from all *commercio* (commerce) with bodies, then out of the concept of soul that of spirit becomes, and *psychologia* becomes *pnevmatologia*. If I leave out the intelligence and take merely soul in community with body, there remain *animae brutorum* (souls of brutes).",[]],"reflm:4729":["Negations in general mean: some realities not. This posits however the opposite, namely all with a restriction. Thus to us |\nthe concept of all reality as a *substratum* of reason is necessary; but therefore we cannot regard a highest reality as necessary in itself. Further, according to the *principio exclusi medii* (principle of the excluded middle) every thing is considered in relation to everything possible, as in a division, hence in a whole of reality.\nThe existence of a thing can never be proved from mere concepts, because existence is not one of the predicates and because from concepts nothing further than the respective affirming or denying, but not the absolute position of the object with its predicates, can be inferred. The concept that lies at the ground is a necessary presupposition and therefore seems to be a concept of the necessary being.",[]],"reflm:4730":["At the very bottom of the page:\nIn the original being the possibilities of things lie not as parts, but as sequels. Because the original being could otherwise be composed through *limitata* (limited things). Boundary concept of magnitude: *infinitum*. Of the dynamic: *libertas* (freedom). Of existence: necessary.",[]],"reflm:4731":["The cognition of God from the first mover grounds breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4732":["Around M § 822:\nIn *theologia naturalis* (natural theology) we cognize the original being only in relation to the world, consequently not according to its absolute predicates (for we surely do not want to think the *ens extramundanum* (extramundane being) through *praedicata mundana* (mundane predicates)), but according to relational predicates with regard to the world; thus only the causality, hence the exponent of the relation to the world. e.g. As a (a work of art) to b (the understanding artist): so c (the world) to x (that which I |\ncall understanding in God). Whether I can infer so securely, and this concept is not sufficient for us, indeed also entirely correct?",[]],"reflm:4733":["The objections with regard to the existence of God and his properties [müssen] are all taken from the condition of sensibility, which one has held to be intellectual, and from the subjective conditions of comprehensibility, which one has held to be objective (existence has a magnitude, that we know from time; this magnitude is called duration; afterwards however we separate time from duration, and that is intellectual; from this arises eternity, in which concept no contradiction can be, except only with sensible conditions). One must not again be so zealous and anxious about this play of grounds and counter-grounds. Whoever is in a fortress will not have a battery fired at every bragging Pandour.\nThe infinite and sole space, the [Grund] condition of the possibility of all outer presence of things, as they appear, is indeed not a proof of the existence of a ground- and original being that comprehends everything and in which everything is sustained, from which also all unity and relation stem, because it is as it were possible through its place in the All; but it is yet a proof that the human mind can think no combination without a common ground and no determinations without in One that contains everything. Just so with time, in which all existence lies. This serves to assume the assumption of such a being as subjectively necessary, hence also as sufficient for *praxi* (practice).",[]],"reflm:4734":["If we find in a being the connection with the very same effects, then the inner constitution of the determinations, |\nwhich contain the causality, may be whatever it will. We cognize the cause nevertheless only from the causality and the effect. Thus, if that which is in God relates just so to the misery of human beings as mercy, then we can call God merciful, wrathful, jealous etc.: but only *per analogiam* (by analogy), since we must not compare the absolute with ourselves.",[]],"reflm:4735":["God judges not according to kindness (indulgence), but justice; gives laws according to brightness and governs according to kindness. Thus in the case of our faults we will have to [hope] trust not in the kindness of the judge, but of the ruler, both to help our impotence, and also to give us, in accordance with this worthiness, undeserved blessedness.\nHe cannot hope to get into heaven if he has [prayers] begged off and repented much, but only if he is a better human being. Unless, then, etc etc. The Old Testament was not so. Hence Christ prescribes actions and faith, not petitioning and observances.\nThat the divine law with regard to us is a holy law, we see from our conscience, which notwithstanding our frailty nevertheless does not call good the slightest transgressions, but condemns them all.",[]],"reflm:4736":["The polytheism of subordination is actually monotheism,\nthe polytheism of coordination Manichaeism.",[]],"reflm:4737":["On the natural cognition of God according to the *analogia per viam reductionis et eminentiae* (analogy by the way of reduction and eminence).\nThe absolute we do not cognize.",[]],"reflm:4738":["On M § 890:\nGod is the author through one will: *contra emanationem, Spinozam* (against emanation, Spinoza).\nGod is the author through free will: *contra fatalistas* (against the fatalists).",[]],"reflm:4739":["(g Freedom in God is subjective necessity, but not natural.)\nThe freedom of the divine will consists in the determination of all things according to his highest good pleasure, thus according to the necessary rules of the highest good pleasure, thus not [üb] in this, to dispose over these rules. His will is holy, his end is kind, and his judgment is just. [God k] God is the lawgiver, not that he gives the laws [freely] arbitrarily, but that through his will he subjects creatures to his laws.\nHe cannot dispense from laws. Yet he is kind, but only [with regard to] so, that he gives *complementa* to *fragilitati humanae* (human frailty), not indulgently.",[]],"reflm:4740":["God's counsels are wise, his will is holy, his end (g government) kind, [his judgment is] his court is just.",[]],"reflm:4741":["At the very top of the page:\nPhysicotheology. Main rule. One must appeal to God as an immediate cause in no individual case, but only generally in |\nregard to the [first] last substratum in the world (substance), unless where it is supposed to be a revelation. For our reason is not a faculty the use of which we can cut off the progress of at will. It is a law to itself. It is against reason [itself] to cut off further inquiry and wantonly [to the last point] to exempt oneself from all further effort, in that one presumes to judge what God has done immediately. For reason alone can decide what is fitting or not. There is an interval, indeterminate for us, between an occurrence or arrangement of nature and God, where we must expend our forces to explain everything according to laws of nature.",[]],"reflm:4742":["At the very bottom of the page:\nIn the appearances no absolute first is to be encountered. But in the synthesis of the understanding indeed. Thus there is indeed no first beginning, but indeed first cause, part, [etc], action etc etc. A first as *phaenomenon* would appear as boundary of nothing. The antinomy of reason is thus nothing other than the difference of the principles of reason, insofar as the *data* are given sensibly, i.e. dependent on objects, or intellectually, i.e. from the mind itself, which indeed in regard to (g individual) possible experiences, but not of the whole of them agree. Hence considered *a posteriori* all actions of human beings are empirically determined, *a priori* undetermined and free.",[]],"reflm:4743":["Cause and beginning, the former intellectual, the latter sensitive. The beginning is only in the world, but not of the world. Why did God not create the world earlier? God stands in no [abs] relation to absolute empty time.",[]],"reflm:4744":["The world is a proof of the divine omnipotence,\nof the divine wisdom,\nomniscience,\nof the divine kindness,\nof the divine justice.",[]],"reflm:4745":["Providence, which makes every end unanimous with the whole of all ends, is wisdom.",[]],"reflm:4746":["[It] *Creatio et annihilatio in cursu mundi* (creation and annihilation in the course of the world) is against the principles of the empirical use of reason; rather the divine influence is *conservatio*. Because with regard to him there is no time-sequence. *Conservatio non est influxus. Non concursus* (g except in *actionibus liberis*) *Est vel Providentia* (g in *creatione*) *vel gubernatio in conservatione Mundi. Prior, vel Providentia tantum generalis aut etiam specialis; haec in individui statum in genere vel singularem casum, et tum nominatur directio extraordinaria.*",[]],"reflm:4747":["One can ask where the order of nature, not of the whole world, but of a particular kind of things begins. e.g. the order of the arrangement of the earth, or the production of each animal. Finally one can ask: How does one represent to oneself at all the beginning of the order of nature?",[]],"reflm:4748":["*Concursus* is contradistinguished from *actioni solitariae*. Co-working or sole working. The (g forces of the) world are divided into nature and freedom; with regard to nature God is sole cause, with regard to freedom he concurs. *Causarum sibi subordinatorum nexus non est concursus.*",[]],"reflm:4749":["How the *intima praesentia* (innermost presence) brought Spinoza to regard the substances not as effects, but as actions of the Godhead.",[]],"reflm:4750":["Inner sense contains a feeling of the divine omnipresence; for merely by our being immediately conscious of the cause of us and of everything, we are conscious to ourselves of our connections with other things.\n(s We cannot become conscious as in God; rather, were we in him, God would be conscious of us as his determinations and we of ourselves not at all.\nOf the divine omnipresence we cannot become conscious (g through sense).)",[]],"reflm:4751":["If God concurs in the creature's action, then the creature also concurs in the actions of God; for the *concursus* is reciprocal, although not equal on both sides. It is a share on both sides in a third as effect.",[]],"reflm:4752":["Predestination is that subordination of the things of the world under the divine counsel, whereby all occurrences are regarded as ends and all causes as means. Then there is no order of nature.",[]],"reflm:4753":["Revelation as a dispensation of the extraordinary direction, according to which someone's insights improve religion; such writings are called imparted by God.\n(s To regard our own limitation of cognition as divine wisdom.)",[]],"reflm:4754":["Revelation is for each creature, according to its way of cognition, nature.\nThe revelation of God through reason must precede every other; for it gives the first correct concept, according to which I can test every other.\nRevelations of his existence or of his will.\n(s The pure concept of both God can reveal to us merely inwardly through our reason and not outwardly either through works or words.)",[]],"reflm:4755":["Benefit of not-knowing that a God is.",[]],"reflm:4756":["S. I:\nA. Space.\nDialectic of [appearances] sensibility.\n1. Is space something actual (g real). *Substantia*, *Accidens*, *relatio* (substance, accident, relation). (g *Idealitas spatii* (ideality of space):) Hobbes: *est phantasma rei existentis tanqvam externae* (it is a phantasm of an existing thing as external). Carthesius: *spatium habet pro abstracto extensionis Materiae* (he holds space to be an abstract of the extension of matter). *His accedit Leibnitz* (Leibnitz accedes to these). *Clark vero defendit realitatem spatii* (Clark, however, defends the reality of space). Newton: *est sensorium omnipraesentiae divinae* (it is the sensorium of divine omnipresence). Epicur asserted the subsisting, Wolf the inhering reality of space.\n2. Is there a *vacuum mundanum et extramundanum* (a vacuum within the world and outside the world). It would be appearance without an object that would determine the intuition and the positions of the same. In the latter case motion of the whole of the world. In the former outer intuition without objects. If *vacuum* signifies as much as: what is not filled with matter (impenetrably extended), then a *vacuum* will be possible.\n3. All parts of space are in turn spaces. The point is no part, but boundary. Continuity.\n4. All given magnitudes of space are parts of a greater one. *Infinitudo* (infinity).\n5. Unity, hence pure intuition, and not concept of the understanding.\nB. Time.\nAbsolute space and time or adhering space and time.\n1. Time is nothing real. Clark held it to be real as pure time, Leibnitz to be an empirical concept of succession.\n2. Is there an empty time before the world and in the world, i.e. are two different states separated by time that is not filled up by a continuous series of alterations. The moment of time can be filled, but in such a way that no series of time is designated.\n3. All parts of time are in turn times. The moment. Continuity.\n4. All given times are parts of a greater time. Infinity.\n5. Unity of time. All-concept.\nDialectic of the understanding.\nTranscendental doctrine of magnitude.\n(g Continuity of space and of time and of all magnitudes.)\nSpace and time do not consist of simple parts (g their parts are themselves magnitudes), i.e. absolute unities: continuity.\nThe magnitude of a thing in space and time is continuous.\nThe magnitude of a ground is called degree, intensive, extensive, protensive.\nDetermination of the magnitude through number and given unity (likewise their magnitude).\nInfinite is greater than all number. (g The totality (the All) is not to be understood in series, not to be comprehended in the aggregate.)\nInfinite of continuation or of taking together. (g infinitely small of composition or decomposition.) Where the former is the condition, the latter does not take place.\nInfinite space and elapsed infinite time are incomprehensible.\nIn the world the domain, the progression and the division go into the infinite.\nWhence is mathematics demonstrative? because it is [Vernunfterk] cognition in intuition *a priori*.\nWhich are the boundaries (g of [demo] mathematical cognition)? That which can be represented *a priori* in intuition, thus space and time and alteration in time.\nS. II:\nThe perfection of a thing (g *in sensu* [substantivo] *adiectivo* (in the adjectival sense)) is the totality of the manifold that [zu e] is requisite to make up a thing. A perfection (*in sensu substantivo, transscendentali* (in the substantive, transcendental sense)) is reality.\nTranscendental doctrine of appearances.\nReality and negation.\n[Giebts ein Vac]\n( g The sum total of appearances *In mundo non datur* (In the world there is not given)\n*saltus* (leap), — — — —\n*hiatus* (chasm), — — —\n*abyssus nihili* (abyss of nothing). )\n(g all parts of space and time are in turn times. From nothing everything passes over into something only in time.)\n(g There is nothing simple in appearance, consequently no immediate transition from a determinate state (not its boundary) into another.)\n(g there is no empty time between two states. The alteration is only the continuation of the progression.)\n(g All appearance consists again of appearances, no sensation is simple. [Alle] Appearance, however, does not consist of the nothing and the appearance, thus not of the empty and the full.)\nOn the ideality and reality of appearance in general.\nAll spaces and times are parts of a greater one.\nAll parts of space and time are themselves spaces.\n––––––––––\nIn the propositions on the infinite [und dem Unendlich] of expansion and division philosophy chicanes with mathematics. In those on the empty and the full chican breaks off.\nSpace and time belong only to appearances and thus to the world and not outside the world.\nA *hiatus*, chasm, is a lack of interconnection of appearances, where the transition of the same is missing. The appearances become possible through space; the empty of space is no chasm and belongs too to the determination of sensible intuition.\nThe proposition that all alteration is continuous cancels the ceasing of a substance.\nThe proposition that, if an alteration in the whole of the appearance ceased, it would never begin again; likewise, that each part of the appearances is implicated with the rest and through their alterations is altered along, hence each thing is constantly altered. (g Beginning belongs to appearance, origin to the idea of the understanding.)\n––––––––––\nTranscendental doctrine of experience.\n(g Antithesis: there is no [substantz sondern alles sind erscheinungen] at all.)\n1. Something as substance, that is matter, neither arises nor passes away, from nothing comes nothing, i.e. matter is eternal (g *ex nihilo nihil (in mundo) fit* (from nothing nothing (in the world) comes to be)), though dependent.\n2. Every state of the world is a sequel, for with the continuity of alteration everything is in beginning and ceasing, and both have a cause.\nAntithesis. For there would be no first beginning.\n3. All appearances together make up a world and [es ist nichts isolirt. 1stlich Es ist eine Welt d.i. ein Ganzes aus substantz] belong to actual objects (against idealism). God as cause does not belong to the world. For only through the agreement of the representations with objects do they agree together among themselves and get the unity which perceptions must have that are to be appearances.\nOn n: 2. What happens has a ground, i.e. is whereby determined according to a rule. Unity of the [simultaneitaet] succession. For |\nwithout this rule unity of experience would not be possible. From this it follows that in the world the serial sequence of appearances has no beginning at all. But whether the world itself has an origin, that belongs to intellectual concepts.",[]],"reflm:4757":["P. I:\nThe principles of the possibility of experiences (of the distributive unity) are at the same time principles of the possibility of the objects of ex|perience.\n(g appearance) (g of the experiences.)\n1. (g Unity) Of intuition, 2. of the giving or of the existence of the appearances.\nImmanent (g space and time are conditions of appearance) or transcendent (g they are not) principles.\nThe former of the [understanding] empirical, the latter of the pure use of reason. Agreement of reason with itself in the whole.\nThe former needs (g has) no first a priori, but a posteriori and from there *progressus* or *regressus* *in infinitum*.\nImmanent principles of the empirical use of the understanding:\n1. There is no boundary of the composition and decomposition of the appearances.\n2. There is no first ground or first beginning.\n3. Everything is mutable and variable, thus *empirice* contingent, because time in itself is necessary, but nothing necessary attaches to time.\nTranscendent principles of the pure use of the understanding:\n1. There is a first part, namely the simple as *principium* of composition, and they are together limitations of all appearance.\n2. There is an absolute spontaneity, transcendental freedom.\n3. There is something necessary in itself, namely the unity of the highest reality, in which all manifoldness of the possibilities can be determined through limitations |\n, as in space the shapes and (g whereby) also all existence, as in time every state.\nBecause space and time are only conditions of appearance, there must be a *principium* of the unity of pure reason, through which cognition would be determined regardless of appearance.\nGround of the antithetic or of the\napparent antinomy of pure reason.\nThe former [ge] are principles of the exposition of the appearances, the latter of the spontaneity of pure reason. Agreements with itself in the whole, thus also of morality.\nP. II:\nWe must have principles of the original unity or of the systematic unity of our cognitions, i.e. of the spontaneity [ihr] of the same, insofar as we want to act independently and to determine the appearances themselves practically or ourselves among the appearances *originarie*.\nThey are principles of the self-determination of reason [of the unity of the actions which]\nor of the unity of the whole of our determinations of reason.\nFor reason all possible cognitions make up a whole, thus the synthesis of absolute unity is the condition of reason.\n––––––––––\n(g The soul is in the appearances one substance with the body, it appears only as *entelechy* of the body. It is not in community but union with the body and in no relation of place to the body. For it is intelligible, but the body merely *phaenomenon*. But there is no relation of the *noumenon* to the *phaenomenon*.\n––––––––––\nDialectic.\nRules.\n1. What does not belong at all to appearances is not to be judged according to rules of appearance, e.g. God with space and time.\n2. What does not belong to outer appearance, e.g. spirit, not to subject to the conditions of this.\n3. What cannot be comprehended (g and what cannot be represented in intuition): the totality of the infinite or of infinite division, not to hold to be impossible. The infinity of the series, the [infinity of] finitude [without] of the derived without the *substratum originarium*.\nFurther, the principles of the absolute unity of reason not to be mixed with those of empirical unity.\na. Simplicity of the thinking subject.\nb. Freedom as [principium] condition of the [actions] rational actions.\nc. [Infinite] *Ens* [infinitum] *originarium* as *substratum* of all combination of its representations in a whole.\n[d Real unlimited totality of the same not with]\nd. Limitation of the world according to origin and content not to be mixed with bounding.\n––––––––––\nPrinciples of reason are those which the conditions of the [determinations] unity of our cognition, insofar as it is a priori [under] determinable, consequently only those which contain the completeness of speculative cognition a priori, which agrees with the ideas of the practical a priori.",[]],"reflm:4758":["P. I:\nThe principles [of the synthetic use] of the synthesis of pure reason [a priori] in general are all metaphysical.\nThose of the synthetic use of reason with regard to intuitions that can be given in experience are prin|ciples\nof the empirical or physical use. Those with regard to intuitions that cannot be given a posteriori, principles of the hyperphysical [use] or transcendent use. (The former are mathematical, the second dynamical.)\nThe principles of the hyperphysical use go to the collective universality of the synthesis, those of the physical to the distributive.\n1st principle. The principles of the possibility of experiences are also principles of the possibility of the objects of experience. Example.\n2nd principle: In everything that exceeds the boundary of experience, we can assume only principles of the [synthesis] absolute unity of the synthesis a priori (g i.e. of the unity of the use of reason a priori).\n––––––––––\n1. Mathematical principles a priori and their possibility. Evidence.\n2. Metaphysical principles of mathematical synthesis in general. Infinity of the synthesis of intuition, both according to composition (g progressive) and decomposition.\nNo other synthesis of appearances takes place than with appearances, consequently that which is empirically possible, hence not with the empty.\nA. Dynamical principles.\nMathematical and dynamical principles of the possibility of experiences*: the former of intuitions, the latter of the relation to apperception, i.e. to existence.\n*(g all appearances stand under rules of a dynamical unity, so that experiences come to be.)\nMathematics deals with nothing but what can be given a priori in intuition. Not with reality etc. Not with existence.\nObject and existence of the same.\nThe transcendent principles are principles of the subjective unity of cognition through reason, i.e. of the agreement of reason with itself.\nObjective principles are principles of a possible empirical use.\nP. II:\nEverything actual must be able to be cognized a priori (Possible) and must also be given or determined a priori (Necessary).\n––––––––––\nThere must be two kinds of principles of unity (g a priori). Unity of the intellection of the appearances a priori, insofar as we are determined through them, and unity of the spontaneity of the understanding, insofar as [it itself and] the appearances are determined through it.\nThe unity of the relation to the whole of cognition, hence principles of the totality of the synthesis and the *termino* a priori, the first, outermost (for what is given in intuition is the *terminus* a posteriori), hence the synthesis of composition and not merely of decomposition, are principles not of the empirical use of reason with regard to appearances, but of the architectonic and pure use. The simple (g of substance). The spontaneity of action, the [original] original being, the universal cause are the cardinal concepts, [which] on which the unity of the [whole in] use of reason in the whole rests.\nThe understanding itself (a being that has understanding) is simple. It is substance. It is transcendentally free. It is affected with sensibility (space), in community with others. All objects of the same make up one (composite), which is called world (unity of space). The totality of appearances is immeasurable, but limited and limits. The whole is contingent or dependent. Everything is grounded on an original understanding, which is the all-sufficient ground [of] the world.\nThe (g Necessary) unity of time and space is transformed into the necessary unity of an original being, the immeasurability of the former into the all-sufficiency of the other. The beginning (g of the world) in time into the origin of the same. The divisibility of the appearances into the simple. This breaks off.\nUnity of reason. Unity of the self-determination of reason with regard to the manifold of the unity of the rules or principles. Not of the exposition, i.e. of the analytic unity of the appearances, but of the determination (comprehension), i.e. of the synthetic, through which |\nthe manifold, given as in general (not merely to the senses), has unity in a necessary way.",[]],"reflm:4759":["S. I:\nOn the principles of empirical [*exposition*] *intellection*, in distinction from the principles of *comprehension*.\nHow one could deceive both the dogmatic enemy of religion and the dogmatic religion-brooder by an apparent *antithetic* and thus a disguised *skeptic*.\nHow one could refute this skepticism itself through the dogmatic principles [of the positive] with regard to the practical use of reason.\nHow one could derive from the principles of the unity of reason with regard to totality principles that conflict with the [conditions] principles of determination in appearances, which must always be partial and conditioned, but must be regarded as belonging to cognition as a whole.\nThere is a *synthesin prototypam* and *ectypam*: the former of self-determination *a [priori] termino a priori, non empirice dato*, from nothing, the latter *a termino a posteriori*, — the former *simpliciter*, the latter *secundum qvid*. I am, I act, I together with all the manifold, I insofar as I exclude the manifold. The principles of the [s] absolute synthesis are rational and conditions of practical cognition *a priori*.\n––––––––––\n1. Principles of intuition. [Von] That conditions of intuition are not of the things.\n2. Of the understanding, e.g. *principium rationis*.\n3. Of reason.\n––––––––––\nReason goes from the universal to the particular [or from the], the understanding from the particular to the universal. The latter universal is it only *secundum qvid* and belongs to the empirical or physical use of reason. The former is absolute and belongs to the free use or metaphysical. Likewise to the moral.\nS. II:\n(g *Exposition* and [*comprehension*] rationality.)\n(g On appearances.) (g Unity of experience and unity of reason.)\nPrinciples of the *exposition* of appearances presuppose [them] these all together as conditioned, hence nothing posited absolutely.\n1.\nno absolute totality (g *totalitas secundum qvid*) [of synthesis] of composition, hence the [*regressus* infin] *progressus* infinite.\n(g unconditioned bounding in *mundo phaenomeno*. *Inter phaenomena non datur universitas absoluta*.)\n2.\nno absolute [division of] totality of *decomposition*, hence no unconditionally simple.\n(g *non simplicitas absoluta*.)\n(g The infinite progress cannot be comprehended and the unconditioned cannot be made intuitive.)\n3.\nno absolute totality of the series of generation, no unconditioned spontaneity.\n(g *non causalitas absoluta*.)\n4.\nno unconditioned necessity. All things can be taken out of time and space.\n(g *non necessitas absoluta*.)\n(g World in the physical understanding\nSimple —\n—\n—\n*spontaneitas*\n—\n—\n*necessitas*\n—\n—)\nAll these propositions are certain as principles of the empirical use (g objective), but contrary to reason.\n(g On things in general.)\nPrinciples of rationality or *comprehension* (g of the same). From the universal to the particular: absolute synthesis.\n1. Unconditioned all of the (g dependent) whole. Origin of the world.\n(g *in mundo noumeno datur universitas*.)\n2. Unconditionally simple.\n(g *monas*.)\n3. Unconditioned spontaneity of action.\n(g *libertas transscendentalis*.)\n4. Unconditionally necessary existence.\n(g *necessitas absoluta originaria*.)\n(g World in the metaphysical understanding\nSimple —\n—\n—\n—)\nThese propositions are subjectively necessary as principles of the use of reason in the whole of cognition: unity of the whole of the manifold of the cognition of the understanding. They are practically necessary with regard to the breaks off.\nSubjective conflict of the partiality of sensibility and the totality of reason in determination of cognitions:\nof the conditions of the empirical use in *exposition* of appearances,\nof the conditions of the rational use in *comprehension* of appearances.\n[The empirical *canones*] The *canon* of the empirical use goes to universality (g of appearances), that of the rational to *universitatem* of things.\nWhat cannot be determined in appearance is not on that account impossible.\nThere must be principles of the self-determination of reason, which are distinguished from those wherein reason is determined through appearances and their conditions. These are principles of the unity of cognitions in the whole, hence not of partial but of total unity.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsatz = principle; Latin-form principien also = principles (one English word for two forms). Satz = proposition.\n- totalitaet = totality, rationalitaet = rationality, universalitaet = universality (Latin-form words, rendered by cognates); *universitas/universitatem* kept in Latin.\n- Begrentzung = bounding; Grenze = boundary; Anschauend gemacht (participle) = made intuitive (cf. anschaulich).\n- Verstand in 'Welt im physischen/Metaphysischen Verstande' rendered 'understanding' for consistency; the German may mean 'sense/meaning'.\n- 'bricht ab' is the editors' remark that the text breaks off; left in place. 'da' in 'denen, da die Vernunft…' rendered 'wherein' (could be 'whereas').\n- Brackets are deletions/insertions of the Academy apparatus; (g marks later additions; both carried over as they stand.)",[]],"reflm:4760":["S. I:\nThe ground of the antinomy of reason is the conflict: 1. All empirical synthesis is conditioned, the mathematical as well as the dynamical. A. All appearance has parts and is itself a part. B. Everything that happens is a sequel (g what is there, is conditioned) and is itself a ground. There is therefore no first and last. No simple, no bound of magnitude, no first ground, no necessary being. I.e. we cannot arrive at it in appearances (g and must not appeal to it). On the other hand [the] 2. the transc. synthesis (g through pure concepts of reason) is unconditioned, but also happens through mere intellectual concepts; so there is really no antinomy. The world is limited. Consists of the simple. There is freedom. There is a necessary being. [Ground of this synthesis] Ground of these principles. Unity of the entire use of reason, in that it has collective unity.\n1. Infinite (of addition (g *composition*) and of division (g *decomposition*)). (g Finite.) 2. Infinite of derivation. 3. Infinite of contingency.\n––––––––––\nThe principles of the possibility of experiences go breaks off.\n––––––––––\nThe synthesis of the parts and of the whole [are] is in empirical cognition always conditioned, thus also that of effects and causes (substantial) and of the contingent. For they go to the unity of appearances, where the manifold is given sooner than the unity. Determinable unity: where the unity rests on the rule of the *regressus*. On the other hand the unity of the pure use of reason (simple, free, necessary) is determining and a *progressus*, which begins from the condition *a priori*, e.g. from freedom in morals. These conditions do not belong in the field of appearances and make the synthesis *a priori* possible in general.\nWhat can never become an object of our senses is absolutely [impossible] impossible as appearance, consequently also no empty space. But that something can occur in appearance that is regarded as a sequel of empty space, is quite possible.\nS. II:\n(g Only the conditions of the empirical synthesis are objective.)\n[Principle of]\nSkeptical principle of the *comprehension* of appearances.\nThere is no absolutely first of the synthesis of appearances (because all together is conditioned).\n(g No absolute boundary)\n1. No first of the aggregate in space and time. (The most absolute in it is that whose boundary is determined by nothing. (g The boundary can be determined only among appearances.) The empirical synthesis is always conditioned.) I.e. The whole of appearances is *a priori* unbounded. The totality can therefore not be determined through successive addition.\n2. No absolutely first of composition (no absolute boundary of division) (nothing simple), no simple part [of that which in the Re] of the extended or of change.\n3. No absolute boundary of subordination [of sequels and grounds. No] of the sequence of actions and [sequels] effects. No first action. No transcendental freedom.\n4. No first cause (No primal being).\nFor all appearance is possible only in space and time. But time (g and space) is determinable only through appearance. But it is without a first.\nThese propositions are contradicted merely by the (g which however does not contradict the *exposition* of appearances) incomprehensibility of the propositions: There is a *regressus in infinitum* [of the] (from the empirical *terminus* on) of dimension, of division, of [sub Entst] generation and of dependence. With the experiences we always remain in the chain of appearances.\nBut insofar as the things lying at the ground of appearances are taken, which consequently are thought only through concepts of the understanding, the unity of their synthesis requires an absolutely first (g of the inner state of reason) (i.e. unconditioned) [of the synthesis of totality, of] of origin, of composition, of action, of existence in general. These are conditions of the (subjective) unity in the use of reason with regard to appearances, just as those principles of the manifold. In that sameness of the condition, in this unconditioned unity.\n===NOTES===\n- Folge = sequel; Reihenfolge = sequence; Folgen = sequels. Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; Grund = ground.\n- '(g da ist, ist bedingt)': 'da ist' could be 'what is there' or 'that is'; left as 'what is there'.\n- 'die synthesis a priori überhaupt möglich': überhaupt may attach to 'a priori' or to 'possible'; English left equally open.\n- 'darauf' rendered 'it'; antecedent not fixed by the German.\n- 'In ienem … in diesem' = 'In that … in this' (former/latter not specified); 'so wie iene principien' = 'just as those principles'.\n- Truncated or broken-off readings ('Re', 'sub Entst', 'gehen bricht ab') are the manuscript's and left as they stand; 'bricht ab' is the editors' remark.\n- Brackets are Academy-apparatus deletions/insertions; (g marks later additions; both carried over as they stand.)",[]],"reflm:4761":["P. I:\nProof of the future life from the analogy of nature with regard to the purposive determination of its predispositions.\n*evolution*.\nThe merely moral proof is valid only for some human beings and only for a certain point in time of the future existence, namely those have committed (g children, savages, the dull) — and only until they are punished |\n—. By contrast the (g physico-) teleological proof is valid for all for this reason, because in the whole of human nature the predispositions are and because their development and practical use prepares similar sequels to infinity.\nEstimation of our cognition. The proof has practical (g positive) sufficiency and the metaphysical negative with regard to the dogmatic presumption of the opponents.\nOn the *hypothesi metaphysica**\nOf the\npneumatic\nlife problematic.\n—\n—\n—\n*commercio*\n—\n— —\n—\nappearances (g apparitions)\nThe latter not to deny dogmatically, but principles of the reluctance of the understanding against each individual narrative. Maxim of reason. Unity. *miracula comparativa* are worse than *rigorosa*. *ortus phaenomenorum univocus* — *aeqvivocus*. No laws.\n* we do not know whence the whole appearance of the corporeal world and of our own comes, but one cannot regard it as necessary.\nThe practical sufficiency is that which is adequate to connect, through reason with moral laws, the incentive, namely the *effectus a lege determinatos*.*\n*(g From experience one cannot infer the existence of God in strict understanding.)\n(g Fatalism.\nCosmological proof from the relation of the contingent to a will.)\n––––––––––\n(g *Sum*)\nThe Wolffian proof infers from an existence in general, thus cosmologically, to the existence of an *entis realissimi*; it is properly an ontological proof that something necessary must be *realissimum*.\nThe divine will is intellectual, sufficient to itself and inde|pendent.\nWithout affect. Regret, anger (g *tamen iustitia punitiva*, not itself revenge). Compassion.\n*Voluntas antecedens in universale bonum, conseqvens (decretum) in* [*conseq*] *individuum possunt a se differre et absqve successione esse.*\n(g The *providentia generalis* is natural, the *specialis* supernatural; the latter goes to *individua*, but the extraordinary direction to single occurrences.)\n*Beatitudo divina.*\n*Caussalitas libera mundi qvoad substantiam est creatio.*\nIn the world there are beginnings, but not a beginning of the world. The world has always been. [God has created an eternal world, but not from E] Empty time before the world. The resolution is not in time.\nThe cause\nof the existence of the world is\ncreation,\n—\n—\nof the duration — — —\npreservation.\nNo world can [itself] something [what to it s] create. *Mundi partes omnes sunt res creatae.* Because otherwise they would depend externally on themselves.\nThe divine will on the particular in the world,\ninsofar as it belongs to creation, is\nprovidence,\nto preservation\n—\n—\ngovernment.\nP. II:\nNature (g simply) or the whole\nof appearances,\n— —\n[contingen] limited things,\n— —\ncontingent things.\n(g *Mundus. Essentia compositi et mutui commercii semper est derivativa. ens derivativum.*)\nRefers necessarily to God as original, not derived being. (everything composite [posits] is (g dependent) contingent, derived (g limited).)\n1. According to the mere concept.\n2. According to existence.\nAbsolute completeness [and], unity (g and necessity) of the original being. Being above nature and world.\nCannot be properly cognized through predicates of nature, but according to analogy.\n*Theologia vel rationalis vel revelata. Prior vel Transscendentalis vel naturalis. Posterior vel Metaphysica vel Physicotheologia vel Moralis theologia.*\n*Transscendentalis — Deista.*\n*Naturalis — Theista; revelationem negans: Naturalista.* [*Deum*] *Mundum deum vocans est Atheista* (g *explicite*). *Deista est Atheus cryptice talis.*\nThe concept of a thing, insofar as it is negatively determined, is always a derived concept, because it is congruent only with the part of some other concept different from it. Of different things one must necessarily be negatively determined and thus its concept be derived. There is thus only a single being whose concept is a *conceptus originarius*; the concepts of other things are only limitations of it. One always asks: whence then does this or that have precisely these limits.\nIn it they are all determined according to substance. From it all state stems originally. In it they are all *in commercio*.\n––––––––––\n( g (g *primus,*) *motor*. The highest being, the highest intelligence, the highest good. )\n(g Emanation or creation. Intell: analogy (g not anthropomorphism).\nUnderstanding. Feeling. Will.\nForesight. intimate presence.\nNot *mundi causa bruta* (*genitor*), but *libera* (*autor*).)\nThe transcendental concept of things, i.e. as beings in general, [has ih] has for the cognition of their difference a ground concept of an *entis realissimi*, in which they are all determinable.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Translation of 'Anlage' as 'predisposition' (not in glossary); 'Triebfeder' as 'incentive'; 'Rathschlus' as 'resolution'; 'Urwesen' and 'Ursprüngliches' both rendered with 'original'.\n- 'sufficientz' (German-spelled Latinate form) rendered 'sufficiency'; glossary 'zureichend' = 'sufficient' not used here.\n- 'Schranken' and 'Einschrankungen' are both rendered 'limits'/'limitations' (Schranke = limitation; Einschränkung = limitation); 'Vorsehung' = providence, 'Vorhersehung' = foresight.\n- 'die verbrochen (g Kinder, Wilde, Dumme) — haben': the object of 'verbrochen' is missing and the relative pronoun is absent; the text looks elliptical or corrupt and is rendered as it stands.\n- 'in striktem Verstande': 'Verstande' kept as 'understanding'; it may also be read idiomatically as 'in the strict sense'.\n- 'unseres eigenen' has no noun supplied; 'our own' left open. 'ihm' ('In ihm sind sie alle…') may be 'him' or 'it'; 'it' chosen following 'Wesen'.\n- '[zufall]' is a truncated deleted reading, rendered '[contingen]'; '[hat ih]', '[was zu ihr s]', '[Gott … von E]' are broken-off deleted readings, left fragmentary.\n- 'Schätzung' = 'estimation'; 'Gnugsam' = 'sufficient'; 'Erkentnis' = 'cognition'; 'Grundbegrif' = 'ground concept'. The 'S. I/II' of the locus notes rendered 'P. I/II'.",[]],"reflm:4762":["S. I:\n(g 1.) *Realitas* (reality) (s *partim reale, partim negativum. negatio supponit realitatem. Bonum metaphysicum*). *Negatio*. *Realitas cryptica* (g *apparens*).\n2. *Vnitas* [*vel*] *Mathematica (aggregandi) vel qvalitativa (concipiendi)* (g *connectendi*) (s *subordinandi*). e.g. The unity of the system. Of the speech. Of a machine. Common being. *Multitudo*. [*Def*] *Indefinitum. Numerus. Infinitum. Qvantum* (g *assignabile*). *Qvantitas. Maius. Minus. Aeqvale, Mensura. (Continuum, Discretum). Limes.* (g *terminus*.)[*Constructio*] *Constructio* (g of intuition or of magnitude in general). *Arithmetica. Geometria. Evidentia. (Mathesis. scientia rationalis. e conceptuum constructionibus, qvare pro obiecto habeat qvantitatem.)* [*Ens limitatum Illimitatum*]. *Perfectio formalis. Ens perfectissimum transsc: Ens limitatum, illimitatum* (s *maximum, omnisufficiens*), *infinitum, reale.* (s possibility, perfection, order, unity.) *Qvantum* (s *vel dabile vel*) *assignabile.* (s *Omni assignabile omni assignabili maius, minus.*)\n*Maius, Minus.* (s *in spatio non datur*) *Maximum, Minimum. Infinitum simultaneum, successivum addendi et comprehendendi.* (s *Infinite parvum (infinitum divisionis).*)\n*Unitas* as the way of determining something: the unity,\n— —determination itself: A unity.\nUnity, perfection, truth, possibility. *vel praedicabilis vel praedicamentalis. Vnitas vel determinandi vel determinata* [or the unity of determination and the determining unity]. (s *unitas determinans, unitas determinata*.)\nThere is no *qvantum assignabile infinitum et infinite parvum*.\nSomething can be greater and smaller if, besides the smaller, it contains still more, although of a different kind.\nIn space there is already a *simultaneitas*; it is thus an *infini|tum\ncomprehensionis*, but we cannot think it, because our concept is an *infinitum successionis*.\n*Conceptus maximi et infiniti non sunt eadem* (s *numerus maximus est impossibilis et multitudo maxima — infinita, non item numerus.*)\nIrrational is a [number] magnitude that is congruent with no number, but is either greater or smaller than *qvilibet dabilis*.\nIt is a natural use of our pure reason, but this is dialectical. To set this within limitations, there belongs discipline of pure reason and after this canon. Beforehand, however, in transcendental logic the analytic of the basic concepts and of the principles; of cognition *a priori* domain and limitations.\n*Compositum et simplex*.\nUnity of the taking-together and connection.\n*Phaenomena in spatio et tempore sunt qvanta continua.* The appearance in space is the extended, the appearance in time is alteration.\nS. II:\n*Qvod* (g *Positio qvae*) *non est qvantum, est simplex* (g *vnitas absoluta*.)\n*In qvo non est compositio, est simplex.*\n*Caussa ponens vel tollens.*\n–––––––––––\n(g Here only real relations are spoken of, hence of the relations in existence.)\n(g If I leave aside everything that is called inherence, there remains merely that whose existence is subsistence, i.e. *substantiale*.)\nCategories of relation (s *determinatio*): 1. *subiecti*, 2. *principii*, 3. *Compositi* (*realium* (g *non logicorum nec idealium*)). [a. subsistence and inherence. b. *Caussae et Caussati*. c. *compartium et totius*] (g Inclusion, sequel and accompaniment.) (s Threefold unity:) a. Relation of inherence (g subsistence), b. dependence (g causality), c. *commercii* (g *reciprocatio*).\n(g Whose appearances include one another (g whose representation at all times a part of the representation of another) (g e.g. of the rainbow and rain), follow one another and accompany one another.)\n*Praedicabilia* are. (s A. *Substantiale et accidentia (substratum).*) A and B. *Agere. Pati. Vis, facultas, receptivitas.* A, B, and C. *Compositum substantiale. Compartes. actio et reactio. Simplex substantia.*\n*Praedicabilia phaenomenorum. Substantia phaenomenon est perdurabile constans*, in whose [concept] representation that of the *accidentia* is always included. *accidens est variabile.*\n*Caussa phaenomenon, cui qvaedam alia secundum regulam succedunt.*\nCompositum*(g substantiale), [qvod] in qvo (qvatenus est compositum) status plurium qvovis tempore simul sunt.\n*(g Commercium (g interaction). Qvatenus [determ] non nisi simul determinabile est. Relation of the parts to one another, of the parts to the whole; whether they are simple, absolute unity.)\nIgitur per has categorias phaenomena (g modus compositionis) qvoad [existentiam constanti] existentiam, i.e. experientiae, perpetuis legibus subiiciuntur, qvemadmodum qvoad intuitum[legibus] regulis et qvoad conceptus conditores normae.\nOn alteration.\n––––––––––\nIn all categories the relation is heteronomic, in that of the *compositum* homonomic.\n(g The relation to perception (g given), to thinking and [sensing] determining *a priori*.\nAre not predicates.\nThing, non-thing. Being, attributes. *Ens necessarium.* | *Absolute hypotheticum.*)\n( g analytic principles of possibility synthetic — )\n––––––––––\nFalse definition of substance by Carthes.\n––––––––––\nOn causality. 1. Ground and sequel (real relation). 2. Ground of existence. Cause. 3. Substance as cause. *Causa afficientiae*.\nOf this substance the *respectus* to the *accidentia* as *principium* is force. The causality of force with regard to the *accidentia*: action. The dependence of the *accidentia* on the force of another: suffering. The possibility of acting: *facultas*, of suffering: *receptivitas*.\nThe *principia* of the exposition of the *phaenomena* are *principia* of *intellectio*, not of the *perspicientia* of the same: to seek causes, but not to determine. and in general to determine nothing but the intuition.\n––––––––––\nThe ground precedes in the position (the concept of the same), although it is simultaneous with the sequel. I first light the candle, thereafter it shines, although, when it burns, it shines simultaneously. The bordering of the ground on the sequel is simultaneous, just as the initial moment is simultaneous with the year, but yet precedes.",[]],"reflm:4763":["S. II:\nEverything transient is a contingent thing, but not the alterable.",[]],"reflm:4764":["S. II:\nThe multitude that is no part (infinite). The unity that is no multitude (simple). Ground that is no sequel.",[]],"reflm:4765":["S. II:\nOn the principles [*a pr*] of the pure understanding.\nAll appearances have a determinate magnitude (whose relation to another is assignable).\nThe infinite does not appear as such, likewise not the simple.\nFor appearances are enclosed between two bounds (points) and are thus themselves determinate magnitudes.\nRationalizing principle (from subjective grounds of reason.) [All possible magnitudes are] No object of intuition (g should mean: no appearance) is infinitely large and infinitely divisible, i.e. everything has bounds of extension and division.\nThe all in the case of the infinite and the parts of the finite (infinitely divisible) is for the human understanding, but not in itself, impossible.\n––––––––––\nAll appearances are real and *negatio*: Rationalizing: All reality must be sensation.\n––––––––––\nEverything that is there (g insofar as it is there), is substance and *accidentia*; insofar as it happens (g follows): a sequel from a ground; insofar as it is simultaneous: Composed in a whole and [is] reciprocally determined.\nRationalizing: [All substance is necessary] [Everything alterable is contingent] Everything persisting (g in the appearance) is substance. (determined in itself.) (*phaenomenon substantiatum*.)\nEverything that happens is determined in the series. There is no absolutely first beginning.",[]],"reflm:4766":["Unity (connection) (g agreement), truth (quality), completeness (quantity).",[]],"reflm:4767":["[Ein Die Merkmale] The manifold in a concept can be weighed either in relation to the thing itself or to things in general.\nMetaphysical unity is real (*substantia*, *ratio*, *totum*).\nMetaphysical truth: *respectus ad data. si conditiones datae sunt.*\nMetaphysical completeness: *omnitudo realitatis*.",[]],"reflm:4768":["Necessity is best divided into the conditioned and unconditioned. The former in turn into the internally or externally conditioned. For *hypotheticum* is opposed not to the *internum*, but to the *categoricum*.",[]],"reflm:4769":["The necessary without a ground is incomprehensible, but the contingent determination with a ground is so just as well; that is necessary in every respect, this just as contingent.",[]],"reflm:4770":["A concept cannot, with regard to that which pertains to it of necessity, be altered.\nIt is remarkable that the mark of all action is alteration, either *actu* or *potentia*. of the inner action: the inner, of the outer: motion. One sees that the position in time and the order thereof nonetheless constitutes the condition of all concepts of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4771":["What is absolutely necessary is therefore unalterable, because it is [in ei] not subordinated in a series, hence another state finds no ground.",[]],"reflm:4772":["At the top of the page:\nFirst analytic of the categories. without synthetic propositions. Distinction of the pure or to *phaenomena* applied concepts of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4773":["An object is possible only insofar as it contains reality; otherwise the *intuitus* is empty, i.e. without object. All objects, insofar as they are thought absolutely, i.e. through understanding, presuppose reality [and negations]. [What all] The sum total of all reality is only a single object; the manifoldness of objects is thus possible only through the all of reality and its limitation. An *ens originarium* thus lies at the ground of the possibility of the manifold.",[]],"reflm:4774":["At the bottom of the page:\nEvery reality borders on one side with nothing. For every sensation fills the [empty] time and is [out of] in the empty time as arisen from nothing through continuous position, hence to be regarded as continuous magnitude. Reason begins from the highest reality.",[]],"reflm:4775":["At the bottom of the page:\nTo the anticipation of the appearance there belongs also that the intensive magnitude of matter can decrease infinitely down to empty space.",[]],"reflm:4776":["What [can] not exist except as the *rationatum* (consequent) of another, is *dependens* (dependent); what does not exist except as the predicate of another, is *accidens* (accident). What exists, even if it is not the *rationatum* of another, is *independens* (independent); what exists, even if it is not the predicate of another, is *substantia* (substance).",[]],"reflm:4777":["By confusing these concepts with one another Descartes and Spinoza erred.",[]],"reflm:4778":["*Existentia* denotes: 1. an accident to be real; 2. not to belong to pos|sibility\nas a logical predicate, therefore not to be posited through identity nor understood through analysis.\nThat which does not exist unless another is posited, denotes:\n1. from the side of the predicate inherence (in logic it is only competence or agreement),\n2. from the side of the subject force.",[]],"reflm:4779":["At the bottom of the page:\nContinuous quantity (*qvantitas continua*) arises from nothing in a time through continuous position.",[]],"reflm:4780":["At the very bottom of the page,\nInfinity is the absolute impossibility of a complete synthesis (not of the completeness of the object) of the composition or decomposition of a given object. The [synthesis of the] appearance is infinite, and the division of the same goes into the infinite. This [synthesis concerns as well] infinity concerns as well the dynamical as the mathematical synthesis. In the intellectual, on the other hand, the synthesis is complete, [and the] but the condition of cognizing this completeness in concreto is sensible (a first or outermost). Reason therefore demands independence from the sensible, but the determination of its concept can only be sensible (antinomy). The omnisufficiency of reason regarded as determining is, with regard to us, an origin of practical laws of our reason, which necessarily presuppose completeness as hypothesis.",[]],"reflm:4781":["Cosmology is necessary for this reason, that, when we take together everything that is composed among one another, everything contingent and everything that is sequel, we may arrive at the absolute, and at what further has no *respectum dependentiae* (respect of dependence). Hence the consideration of the boundaries also occurs here internally (empty space, empty time). Ontology goes to the complete resolution, cosmology to the complete combination. Since now the world is the whole of actual things, which are related to another as their presupposition, one arrives at the concept of the original being, which contains everything completely and absolutely, is related to no thing a (g parte) priori, but a parte posteriori to all.",[]],"reflm:4782":["According to the laws of appearance a *regressus in infinitum* (regress into the infinite) is not impossible, but indeed in the *subordinatione intellectuali* (intellectual subordination); the former is subjectively impossible to think whole. The totality is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4783":["Free actions occur according to a rule* just as natural ones. But they are not to be determined a priori in accordance with it like these; both are therefore in conformity with reason, whereas blind fate and blind chance are *qvalitates occultae* (occult qualities) and contrary to reason.\n*\n(g There are rules contrary to which actions cannot occur, although they are not to be determined according to these.)",[]],"reflm:4784":["At the very top of the page and \nIf something occurs, it must have a cause. But the causality of the cause is [as wied] also something that occurs; it must therefore in turn have a cause, and so into the infinite. The same holds of the other synthesis.\nThe idea is a concept to which the empirical one can indeed, as to a *principium*, be in conformity, but approximating into the infinite, never adequate.",[]],"reflm:4785":["The egoist and the materialist are directly opposed to one another: the one denies the existence of anything in space and thus of things outside him, hence of bodies, but has merely a monadic existence; the materialist denies everything inner of substances. Egoism is more apparent.",[]],"reflm:4786":["All life is derived and conditioned. There is only one original and unconditioned life. All other life depends on the determining cause in time.\n*Primus motor* (s God as living being), i.e. that which gives the series of changeable states [first] the first beginning, is not a mere [blind] necessary nature, but freely acting intelligence.\nThe life of creatures is the series of alterations from inner *principio*. This must have a beginning. God commences all life.\n[Life in tr] Motion in transcendental understanding is life.",[]],"reflm:4787":["Opposite the heading »Voluntas Dei«:\n*contra fatalismum* (against fatalism).\nHis freedom can be comprehended most easily, but not the freedom of his creatures.",[]],"reflm:4788":["At the bottom of the page:\nWe can understand and have insight into divine freedom very well, but not the human. Were the human merely intellectual, we would be able to have insight into his choice through reason; were he a *brutum*: so too. But as a sensible and rational being not, because his action afterwards is a *phaenomenon*, but beforehand under practical laws *noumenon*.\n(s The will is the property of a being in that it is, through its understanding, cause of the objects — faculty of desire only through its representations.)",[]],"reflm:4789":["When did God create the world? — Not in time, hence not from eternity. — Yet we must distinguish the *actuatio mundi* (actuation of the world) in *initio* (in the beginning) |\nfrom the *actuatio durationis creatio*. The general is — it exists through God.",[]],"reflm:4790":["Were matter existing *originarie* (originally), then (s matter would preserve itself) the preservation would be directed only at form and God could not be regarded completely as the author of all form, because matter cannot be merely passive, hence a part of the guilt and of the merit is always to be attributed to it. Also not all form could be originally laid in it, it would have to be produced in it successively; therefore providence would not alone be enough, but also gubernation, i.e. extraordinary *concursus* (concurrence) to [Sch] providence.",[]],"reflm:4791":["The actions of freedom are (g externally) *phaenomena* (g internally *noumena*) and insofar among the *phaenomena* not sufficiently determined. On the whole all actions are good (in the intellectual world), although they are determined beforehand by God.",[]],"reflm:4792":["(g Preservation. Omnipresence.)\nCreation is the production of the world.\nProvidence is the arrangement [of the same of the grounds for the ends] (g in creation), whereby the states (g following) upon one another become consonant with the divine ends.\nProvident care is providence insofar as it goes to the welfare of the creature; providence also goes to punishments.\nGovernment: the preservation (g according to general laws) of nature, in accordance with the rule of providence.\nSteering (g guidance), *direction*, is the [Ang] action of making the occurrences of the world appropriate to his particular ends.\n*Praedestinatio* (predetermination): the preordaining of the determining grounds for individual ends, especially free actions.\nCooperation: the immediate supplementing of the natural faculties for the fulfillment of individual ends.\nAs creation is related to preservation in *nexu causali* (causal nexus): so providence to government in *nexu finali* (final nexus).",[]],"reflm:4793":["Providence is the [Besti] arrangement of the order of nature.\nGovernment is the cause of the course of nature,\n*direction*: of individual occurrences,\n(g Freedom)\nNot the cause of evil.",[]],"reflm:4794":["The ground determines the sequel. The sequel bears witness to a ground, namely leads to seeking some ground.",[]],"reflm:4795":["Logical predicates are\naffirmations or negations,\nreal\n—\ndeterminations —\n—",[]],"reflm:4796":["Logical position: affirmation,\ntranscendental position: reality.\nThe opposition of realities to one another is real.\nThe opposition of realities to negations is logical.",[]],"reflm:4797":["*Essentia* (essence) is the internal *principium* (principle) of all determinations (*determinationum*) adhering to the concept (*conceptui*) of a being (*entis*).",[]],"reflm:4798":["Through experience we indeed cognize, empirically, how the object is given to us; but not the *principium* of these determinations through reason.",[]],"reflm:4799":["The first complete ground of the logical predicates or of the determinations.\nThe essence of things: substance, or of the determination of the same.",[]],"reflm:4800":["The complete ground-concept of a thing is the essence.\nGround-concept is the completion of the subordination of the inner determinations.\nComplete is the completion of the coordination of the inner determinations.\nWe therefore seek the essence, because we like to complete everything and because thereby everything gets unity, when from one ground and in one whole, in one subject the manifold is cognized.",[]],"reflm:4801":["Above, beside and \nPossibility is agreement with the conditions of thinking in general, impossible: what conflicts with them. What agrees with the analytic conditions of thinking is logically possible; what with the synthetic, is really possible. Logical possibility without real is the empty concept without content, i.e. relation to object.",[]],"reflm:4802":["Concept,\nJudgment (truth) and\nInference.\nPossibility,\nActuality,\nNecessity.\nUnderstanding,\nSense,\nReason.",[]],"reflm:4803":["Understanding,\nIntuition,\nReason\nThought,\ngiven*,\ngiven thereby that it is thought.\n*(g whose cognition depends on the object. The object of empirical cognition.)",[]],"reflm:4804":["Unity, truth and perfection can be taken *formaliter* (formally) or *materialiter* (materially). In the first case it is only *singularis*, in that the manifold fits only into one form, in the second *pluralis* possible. A *constitutivum* of the multitude is taken *materialiter*, e.g. a part of something insofar is perfect; but if it is the form, it is no part.",[]],"reflm:4805":["Essential unity (transcendental) (g In concepts, not in the relation of concepts) is: 1. unity of the *principium* of the manifold (the essence is the *principium*); 2. unity of the agreement among one another: *essentiae et attributorum* (of essence and attributes); 3. unity of the connection and composition into one being.",[]],"reflm:4806":["Thoroughgoing interconnection of that which belongs to the essence.\n*unitas*\n*Consensus*\n*unicitas*.\nUnity\nUnanimity\nOneness\ntranscendental unity: derivation (g of the manifold) from one concept. Essence.\ntranscendental truth: derivation of the manifold from one another. attributes.\ntranscendental perfection: derivation of the concept from the manifold.\n(g The unity (g etc etc.) is taken here adjectivally, i.e. *formaliter*, not as the concept of an object, but as the agreement with the possibility of a concept of the understanding in general. This threefold way of interconnection and of formal unity does not belong among the categories, but (s to) the unity of the understanding through the same.)",[]],"reflm:4807":["Unity is the form of the understanding.\nCombination of the manifold with One. Unity: understanding.\nCombination of the manifold with one another. Truth: power of judgment.\nCombination of the manifold into a whole. Perfection: reason.\nKant subsequently turned the thought of this Rfl. somewhat |\ndifferently in the four following lines, which date from approximately the same time, and connected the addition with the words with One (and thereby with the whole Rfl.) by a sign:\nthrough derivation (g determination) (g of the manifold through one) from One,\nthrough derivation from one another (in One),\nthrough derivation of the One from the manifold.",[]],"reflm:4808":["The rule is not the *proposition* itself, but is only expressed through it.",[]],"reflm:4809":["The rule must be distinguished from the formula of the same. The former is only a general ground of the use of a faculty, whereby the manifold of the same becomes unanimous. Through a rule the manifold in the cognition is brought under unity, is thereby comprehended, and has something lasting.",[]],"reflm:4810":["There are rules of the generation of representations through general action or of the putting-together of the same.",[]],"reflm:4811":["The formative power requires a form, the reflecting power a rule, the former for intuiting, the latter for thinking.\nThe rule is the validity of a concept, of bringing the manifold to unity in a general way. The understanding gives rules, reason laws.",[]],"reflm:4812":["Rules *a priori* are laws; *a posteriori* they are never without exception.",[]],"reflm:4813":["The unconditioned is either unconditioned necessity or contingency: fate and chance with regard to occurrences and to the series without a first among things.",[]],"reflm:4814":["Above and \nThe determinations of a changeable thing [are] can (g hypothetically) be necessary (g and also are.). [They are not] (A state is not the contradictory opposite.) Another state is not the *oppositum* of the thing.\nBut the changeable thing itself cannot exist (g in itself) necessarily, because its existence would otherwise be determinable only from concepts and thus only in a single way.",[]],"reflm:4815":["Nothing changes by itself alone; pleasure does not change into displeasure.",[]],"reflm:4816":["Changeability of the concepts or of the things with regard to the same;\nChangeability of the things in existence.\nBesides contingency something more belongs to changeability. The succession of the states is [not] change. Continuance of the thing. Arising and perishing is no change.",[]],"reflm:4817":["*Realitas phaenomenon* or *noumenon*. The former is what corresponds in the appearance to the perception (g sensation) or to the lack of perception. *Noumenon* is what in the object in itself is *position*.\nEmpty sensible intuition would be *phaenomenon absque realitate*.",[]],"reflm:4818":["We go through the categories analytically.",[]],"reflm:4819":["Difference of *quantitas* and *qualitas* in general.\nThe word determination (g predicate) is logical. Likewise the concept of the thing (object), of possible and impossible, merely logical. general negation [or affirmation] *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4820":["Extensive magnitude in that which is simultaneous; protensive in that which is successive; intensive breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4821":["In the case of the *continuum* every part is itself magnitude. No simple part.",[]],"reflm:4822":["Above and \nAll magnitude is either an aggregated (g *aggregatum*) (*discretum*) or a continuous magnitude (*continuum*). The latter is the magnitude of every unity, and without it there can also be no aggregated magnitudes, because in all magnitudes the whole and the part must always be homogeneous. The whole must always have the term of the parts. e.g. a *quantum* of money, but not a magnitude of ducats*.\n*(s A *quantum* that is thought before the composition (g as such) is *continuum*; that which is thought only through the composition, where the parts therefore precede, is *discretum*.)",[]],"reflm:4823":["The subject of inherence and the ground of inherence.",[]],"reflm:4824":["We cognize the substantial only through the persistent amid all manner of determinations.\nEverything in substances that we cognize is force. *vis primitiva*.",[]],"reflm:4825":["The duty of the philosopher is to treat all force as derivative, yet not to deny that there are primitive ones.",[]],"reflm:4826":["Beside and \nCombination with the category of magnitude.\nSubstance is either absolute unity or in itself multiplicity: *compositum substantiale*.",[]],"reflm:4827":["The reciprocally (g determining) relation of simultaneity is the composition; they have a point of time in common, consequently [also a gr] in the understanding, insofar as they are composed, also a ground in common; hence simultaneity is the condition of community.",[]],"reflm:4828":["*Plura coordinata, h.e. se invicem determinantia in uno, faciunt compositum.*",[]],"reflm:4829":["The relation of the manifold in a *compositum* is *actio atque reactio sive actio reciproca et passio reciproca*.",[]],"reflm:4830":["At the lower margin.\nFrom the *phaenomena* one can well arrive at the *substantia comparativa* and *phaenomena substantiata*, but not at the substances themselves. That many monads lie at the ground of bodies cannot be concluded at all.",[]],"reflm:4831":["Beside and \nWhether there is a first subject for the senses, a first ground and first part for the senses. The *phaenomena* do not therefore have a first. The universe consists of simple parts, but not therefore just the bodies.",[]],"reflm:4832":["Physical division is the dislocation of the manifold: displacement. Space is not physically divisible.",[]],"reflm:4833":["*infinitudo progressiva est possibilis, collectiva non item.* Totality.",[]],"reflm:4834":["In and \nWe find no difficulty in the infinity of the form of all magnitudes, namely space and time.",[]],"reflm:4835":["Below and \nThe infinite whole is distinguished from the infinite series.",[]],"reflm:4836":["At the very top of the page.\nA *quantum* is *infinitum* in which an infinite *progressus* is possible. — Thus [infinite] multitude can be considered as infinite. *infinitum actuale* would be that where the *progressus* would be total.",[]],"reflm:4837":["Among all sensations there are only two that are of one kind in everyone and [therefore each also to all the rest] understandable to everyone: pleasure and displeasure in the inner ones, and motion or moving force and rest in the outer ones. Both also serve for metaphysics, the latter for that of nature, the former for that of morals, as the only empirical thing that can be laid at the ground and to which the rest of all [laws must be reduc] *phaenomena* or principles must be able to be related.",[]],"reflm:4838":["Rest is natural to every body, motion is made. Everything, |\ninsofar as it is natural, is determined according to rules. All things have a nature, and things of the world belong to an all-encompassing nature.",[]],"reflm:4839":["The first inner *principium* of everything (g according to a rule) that belongs to the possibility of something is the essence; of what belongs to the actuality, is the nature.",[]],"reflm:4840":["157 E II 569.\nM 157':\nThe inner (s general) *principium* of the *accidentia** (g determinations) according to general laws is nature. (s Nature and freedom.)\nM 157:\n*(s The first inner principle [of one] of everything that belongs to the existence of a thing. The principle of that which belongs to existence is called law. Thus existence under laws.\nTo existence belong inner and outer determinations. The principles are substance, cause and *commercium*.\nNature taken substantively (g materially).)",[]],"reflm:4841":["Transcendental theology serves as the norm of the natural; for because in the latter the concepts are empirical, but the object is not an object of experience, we will have to distinguish, |\nwhich empirical concepts and with what restriction or expansion they suit the original being, highest being. First: these concepts cannot be abstracted from outer experiences, because these do not give what the things are, but how they appear. thus only from inner ones. These predicates will be attributed to God *per reductionem*, *per eminentiam*, and the remaining ones *per analogiam*. Transcendental theology is thus the norm of the natural, indeed also its origin.",[]],"reflm:4842":["One disputes whether the ancient peoples had just as correct concepts of morality of the deity and the future life. These concepts are natural and correct everywhere, although not refined. Only their religions had two faults: first, that they [the moralit] set the moral law and their worthiness so leniently that [they] could reconcile the religion with its weaknesses; second: that they used something other than morality, namely natural or prescribed adorations, as means of religion. With regard to the first the Greeks and Romans above all, with regard to the second Christians and others have erred. Thus theology is indeed easy, but the concept of religion hard. The heathens adored subordinate deities to whom the government of the world was entrusted; the Christians the supreme deity. That was a theological, but not at all a moral difference, except only contingently or indirectly.\nSocrates and Christ are distinguished from one another heaven-wide in morals (g According to human or divine demand). The first demanded it heavenly (valid for heaven), the other earthly (for human beings); yet in Socrates the universal principle is good; only the determination of the particular actions that are contained under the moral |\nprinciple was dependent on each one's private judgment and not precise or pure.\nThe ancients had no concept of holiness, i.e. the moral purity of the heart; rather their [virtue] morality was virtue, i.e. strength, hence rough.\nChrist wanted to improve not theology but morality with regard to it, whereby then theology itself also is purified.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriffe = concepts; Gottheit = deity; Heiligkeit = holiness; reinigkeit/gereinigt = purity/purified (rein = pure).\n- »von moralitaet der Gottheit und dem künftigen Leben«: no comma in the German, so the series could be 'morality of the deity' or 'morality, the deity, and the future life'; left ambiguous.\n- »daß [sie] die religion mit ihren schwächen konnte zusammen reimen«: the subject of 'konnte' and the antecedent of 'ihren' are unclear (religion's or their weaknesses); 'its' kept, ambiguity noted.\n- »sie« in 'foderte sie' is ambiguous (the moral / morality); rendered 'it'. »in Ansehung ihrer« could refer to theology or morality; rendered 'with regard to it'.\n- Anbetung = adoration; Gesetz = law; Grundsatz = principle; Bestimmung = determination; praecis rendered 'precise'.\n- Deleted/inserted readings in [ ] and (g ...) are the Academy apparatus, carried over as they stand.",[]],"reflm:4844":["Moral theology. 1. (g practical) proof of the existence of God, 2. Properties (g so much as belong to religion (g divine morality)).\nThe All is good (g *universitas*) — best world, but not on that account everything good.\nEvil is also positive from freedom, but together with the punishments it is in turn a part of the good.\n===NOTES===\n- Beweis = proof; Daseyn = existence; Eigenschaften = properties (not in the glossary; plain cognate chosen).\n- »so viel zur Religion gehören« is kept literal; the number of the verb does not clearly match 'so viel'.\n- *universitas* is Kant's own Latin.",[]],"reflm:4845":["The good is also from us; but, because it rests on agreement with the order of nature, so it is also from God. Chiefly because the true ethical good rests merely on belief in God.\nNot all utilities are at the same time ends, except those that follow according to the order of nature and not freedom.\nUnconditioned decree with regard to morality.\nFate (*praedestination*) with regard to natural occurrences.\nEvil is not from God, because it rests in freedom, consequently not the divine determination. (g It is contrary to the order of nature, consequently to God.)\nOnly good conduct can entitle to the hope that God will supplement extraordinarily.\n===NOTES===\n- sittlich = ethical, moralisch = moral; Glaube = belief; Zweck = end; Begebenheit = occurrence; Bestimmung = determination; Freyheit = freedom.\n- »und nicht freyheit erfolgen«: whether 'according to' governs 'freedom' as well is left open, as in the German.\n- »nicht der Göttlichen Bestimung beruht«: the preposition governing the genitive/dative is missing; left as it stands.\n- Rathschlus rendered 'decree'; Verhängnis rendered 'fate'; the German word »praedestination« is kept in italics.",[]],"reflm:4846":["God is not at all the (g determining) *auctor* of our actions, but indeed the occasioning one of the good ones.\n===NOTES===\n- *auctor* is Kant's Latin; 'der Guten' may be 'of the good [actions]' or 'of the good [ones]'; left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:4847":["Tetens: *Philosophische Versuche über die menschliche Natur und ihre Entwickelung* vol. I (Leipzig 1777) p. 19\nRight margin, addition to »Search« line 2 from top:\nTucker.\n===NOTES===\n- Editorial bibliographic and locus notes of the Academy Edition; the title is kept in German.\n- Z 2 v.o. = line 2 from the top; Bd. = vol.; S. = p.",[]],"reflm:4848":["4847. p. 131.\nRight margin next to the words »man diess letzte« to the end of the paragraph »genennet werden soll«:\nthe omission of the determinations of an action can make a general representation before all comparing.\n===NOTES===\n- Bestimmung = determination; Vorstellung = representation; allgemein = general.\n- »vor allem Vergleichen« is ambiguous: 'before all comparing' or 'above all in comparing'; the first is rendered, the second is possible.\n- The quoted words are the Academy Edition's references to Tetens and are left in German.",[]],"reflm:4849":["p. I [also: XXIII 471,3-473,7]:\nThe end of metaphysics: [the restrin] 1. to settle the origin of synthetic cognition *a priori*. 2. to have insight into the restricting conditions of the empirical use of our reason. 3. To show the independence of our reason from these conditions, hence the possibility of the absolute use. 4. Thereby |\nto extend our use of reason beyond the boundaries of the world of sense, albeit only negatively, i.e. to remove the hindrance that reason itself (from principles of its empirical use [makes]) makes. 5. To show the condition of the absolute unity of the same [vornem] so that it could be a [princ] complete *principium* of practical unity, i.e. agreement to the sum of all ends.\n(g The same principles of expansion are in turn negative with regard to the empirical use, where nothing but nature is valid.)\nThe dogmatic use of our reason beyond the boundary of (g possible) experiences cannot be objectively determining, and no new *synthesis* takes place, but is only an agreement of the theoretical with the practical unity, since the practical use beyond the boundaries of the pragmatic, hence also beyond the present world, according to the analogy of the empirical use, but [with] in relation to conditions of a complete unity [is] led and thereby our business of reason *a parte priori* and *posteriori* is completed.\n(g Liberation of the unity of reason from the restrictions of its empirical use makes the transcendental possible.)\nBecause the expansion of reason here is merely negative, nevertheless the absolute unity of the cognition of objects in general and of all its ends [must] (free from all restriction of sensibility) is required for the absolute spontaneity of reason, so the expansion is practically necessary.\nReason is the faculty of the absolute unity of our cognitions.\np. II:\nThe principles of the completion of our cognition, i.e. of the (g of the absolute unity of the use of reason) absolute whole of the same are *synthesis* of reason.\nThey contain conditions of wisdom, i.e. of agreement to the sum of all our ends.\nWe complete only through the independent, thus not through sensibility.\nThe determination of all objects through mere reason is thus the completion of our cognition of understanding (s *in progressu* of my existence).\n1. With regard to the self-cognition of reason. Completion *in progressu*.\na. I belong in a universe.\nb. am simple.\nc. Free. intelligence.\nd. My existence is externally not dependent (g on the body) nor contingent.\n(s among empirical principles belongs this: The way of the existence of all things of the world is contingent, only the *ens originarium* exists in all understanding in a necessary way.)\nHere I consider myself not as soul, but intelligence. The *synthesis* is here merely negative, namely to separate the conditions of sensibility from me as intelligence.\nAnd the ground of this synthesis is the freedom of reason from the restricting conditions of sensibility, which is a negative *principium* of morality, thus of wisdom.\n2. Completion *in regressu* from the conditioned to the unconditioned.\nIt is an *ens originarium*,\na. which is all-sufficient and sole,\nb. Simple,\nc. free cause (intelligence),\nd. Necessary according to its nature.\nThese are the conditions of the complete unity of all objects and hence cognitions. This unity, however, is the condition of the agreement of everything practical.\nThese cognitions are not dogmatic, but only a liberation of the (g absolute) unity of the use of reason in the theoretical and practical from the conditions of the empirical use, in order [according to their analogy] to establish principles of the pure practical use.\n––––––––––\nReason is free from the conditions of sensibility and must be so in the practical. The continuation of the function of reason |\nup to complete unity over the restricting conditions of sensibility.\n––––––––––\n(s The concepts of the unity of reason, e.g. of the absolute whole — ground, cannot according to conditions of empirical cognition be represented *in concreto*. But they also do not go to the world of sense, for that is no object of pure reason, but to the world of understanding, which lies at the ground of that.)\n===NOTES===\n- Erweiterung = expansion, ausdehnen = extend (Ausdehnung = extension in the glossary); einschränken and restringiren/restriction both appear as 'restrict(ion)': one English word for two German words.\n- Gegenstand = object, obiekt/obiectiv = object/objectively (Latinate member marked); Erkentnis = cognition; Vermögen = faculty; Zweck = end; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Verstandeswelt = world of understanding; Weltall = universe (lit. world-all).\n- Fragments [restrin], [vornem], [princ] are broken-off deleted readings, left as in the manuscript; 'es ist ein ens originarium' could be 'it is' or 'there is'; 'welche ... ist' (freedom or conditions) and 'der selben' (reason's unity or the use) are ambiguous and left so.\n- »den transscendentalen« is masculine with no noun supplied (presumably Gebrauch); not supplied. The syntax of »da der praktische Gebrauch ... geführt [wird]« is kept, with the verb in brackets at the end.\n- Zusammenstimmung = agreement; summe = sum (cf. Inbegriff = sum total).",[]],"reflm:4850":["p. I [also: XXIII 488,19-25]:\nThat which makes space into an object of outer senses is matter. That which makes time into an object of inner sense is the sensation and perception combined with consciousness. Just as the moving forces of matter striving toward the center point of a world-body, just so they act toward the periphery striving up from it to the circumference, in that they do not cancel one another but mutually penetrate.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand = object; Empfindung = sensation; Warnehmung = perception; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Kräfte = forces.\n- Umfang is 'domain' in the glossary; rendered 'periphery' here because the geometrical sense demands it, and Umkreis = circumference.\n- The first clause after 'Sowie' has no finite verb in the German; this is preserved.\n- Weltkörper = world-body; Mittelpunkt = center point.",[]],"reflm:4851":["22. p. I, II:\np. I:\n( g Whether the concepts are merely *educta* or *producta*.* *praeformation* and *epigenesis* )\n*(g *producta* either through physical (g empirical) influence or through [gel] consciousness of the formal constitution of our sensibility and understanding on the occasion of experience, hence still *producta* *a priori*, not *a posteriori*.)\nThe doctrine of *ideis connatis* leads to enthusiasm. *acqvisitae* are *acqvisitae* *a priori* or *a posteriori*, the former are not always intellectual. Thus the division of cognition into *sensitive* and intellectual is not the first, but rather into that of *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The former either sensible or intellectual.\n(g The study of the object is dogmatic or skeptical, thus that of the subject either physiological or critical. Critique separates 1. the |\npure from the empirical faculty of cognition, 2. sensibility from the understanding.)\nPlato took all cognition *a priori* to be intellectual. Leibniz too, and thus they did not know the sensible of space and time. Leibniz also explains it as intellectual, but confused.\n––––––––––\nSynthetic cognition *a priori* is possible only under the principle that all relation of representations to an object and determination of the concept of the same is nothing other than the representation of the necessary combination of the same in one consciousness. But representations cannot be combined in one consciousness if they [themselves] are not considered as belonging to a *datum* (as object).\np. II:\n(g Metaphysics of nature and morals.)\n[See image below table for correct format]\n*Metaphysica*\n(g *Generalis*)\n(g *Specialis*)\ngeneral:\nparticular:\n[on] reason and its concepts\non from reason\nthemselves constitute the object.\ndifferent\n(g Transcendental philosophy)\nObjects applied.\nCritique of\nOntology\n(g in the world.)\n(g world itself and\npure\nOf experience\noutside it)\nReason\n(g *physiologia rationalis*\n)\nIdeas\nimmanent\n(g transcendent)\n*Physica rationalis*\n*Psychologia rationalis*\n*Cosmologia*\n*Theologia*\n(g Method: dogmatic or critical. (s Physiological: Locke.))\n(g for the latter either: how we arrive at the principles and concepts, or: what they contain, and: how they are possible.)\n(g cognition is called transcendental with regard to its origin, transcendent with regard to the object, which can be encountered in no experience.)\n(g From empirical principles one cannot arrive at transcendent ones, and yet these are the [in front] proper end of metaphysics.)\nWhy not empirical psychology?\nThe *conceptus intellectuales* are *acqvisiti* (g but not from the senses). For [the] we acquire the forms of the bodies that we make from clay, thus the bricks, although we take the clay from the earth.\nParts: 1. Ontology, cosmology and theology *rationalis*; these both: 1. *transscendentalis*, 2. *naturalis*. *Cosmologia naturalis* has as object objects of the senses, outer or inner. and the principles empirical or rational.",[]],"reflm:4852":["Philosophy empirical or rational. The former physics, the latter *metaphysica*. The latter either *metaphysica generalis* (transcendental philosophy) or *specialis*.",[]],"reflm:4853":["Metaphysics does not deal with objects, but cognitions.",[]],"reflm:4854":["*primum principuum conceptuum est ens, iudiciorum est principium contradictionis.*",[]],"reflm:4855":["All philosophy is either *rationalis* (not logic) or *empirica*. The former: whose *principia* are not from experience. The *rationalis* is either *pura* or *applicata*. The former transcendental, the latter *metaphysica*; or: the former *metaphysica generalis*, the latter *specialis*. The *metaphysica specialis* has as ground either sensation or feeling. The former the ground (*materia*) of appearance, the latter of desires. The former metaphysics of nature, the latter of morals. The former either *physiologia rationalis* or *theologia naturalis*, *physica generalis* or *cosmologia specialis*, *psychologia rationalis* or *physica rationalis*.",[]],"reflm:4856":["In every relation in which we want to make something understandable, both members must be known are. Now the object is [known the] unknown, but sensation known. Thus it cannot be made understandable. By contrast, sensation and the feeling of pleasure are both in ourselves, thus it is determined through itself and understandable.",[]],"reflm:4857":["Pleasure and displeasure alone constitute the absolute, because they are life itself.",[]],"reflm:4858":["Transcendental philosophy requires first of all critique (g to distinguish it from the empirical). 2. Discipline. 3. Canon. 4. Architectonic.",[]],"reflm:4859":["Origin of transcendental concepts 1. *per intuitionem mysticam*. 2. (g *influxum*) *sensitivum*.3. *per praeformationem*. 4. *per epigenesin intellectualem*. (g *intellectualia* intuitive or discursive.)\nThe end of metaphysics is God and a future world.\nEpicurus nothing *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4860":["The imputations of Epicurus, which represent his doctrines as absurd, deserve no belief. The useful aim of philosophical history consists in the prefiguration of good models and the presentation of instructive transgressions, likewise in the cognition of the natural progress of reason from ignorance (not gross error) to cognition. If someone tells me very absurd opinions of a person whom I have already recognized as acute through a single sample, I do not believe him. his expression has perhaps been incautious or not understood, just as of one in whom I have perceived an unsuspicious illusion of probity I do not believe the slander. What use is it to me to know it? It is ugly to play the accuser.",[]],"reflm:4861":["Logic considered as propaedeutic is good for beginners, but as architectonic only for masters.",[]],"reflm:4862":["Plato's doctrine of ideas was supposed to serve to prevent us from seeking from empirical principles that which can have its sources and archetype in mere reason, namely true perfection. But from things, [which] thereof what has served merely as a means, to seek out the idea of the constitution (not of the ends), is giddy, fanatical.",[]],"reflm:4863":["Organic or spiritual intuiting, the former through the body.\nThat our soul without body as spirit should intuit other things, i.e., externally, is an overstepping of the limitations of the *datum*. For we cognize the soul only as the object of inner sense and the body as the means of the outer. Our intuiting is physical and not mystical; the physical is not pneumatic, but organic.",[]],"reflm:4864":["It is necessary and certain that every true paradox finds resistance at the beginning, in order to be sifted and examined and thereafter |\nto be all the more durable. just as certain foods must be repugnant at the beginning, in order thereafter to please all the longer.",[]],"reflm:4865":["Metaphysics is no *organon*, but *canon* of reason, a ground not of doctrine, but of discipline, not of dogmatic, but of critical cognition, not to increase cognitions, but to keep off errors, not of the object, but of the rules of the subject, not the mother of religion, but its bulwark, not of objective, but of subjective use. The important basic truths of morals and religion are grounded on the natural use of reason, which is a use according to the analogy of the empirical use and reaches to the boundary of the world *a priori* and *a posteriori*, insofar as it is the boundary, and thus to the bordering, but not beyond the same. This natural use is not free from aberrations of speculation, it brings forth a belief and no knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4866":["M IX:\nLocke a physiologist of reason, origin of concepts. He committed the error that he took the occasion to arrive at these concepts, namely experience, for the source. nevertheless he made use of it beyond the boundary of experience.\nWolf was an artist of reason, he made use of it and did not inquire at all after the sources. dogmatic, not critical.\nLambert analyzed reason, but the critique is still lacking. Crusius (everything that I can think is possible) assumed innate principles (though not Platonic ideas); but since it perhaps |\nM X: |\nonly prin|ciples\nof the empirical use of the understanding could be, he was not sure whether he could also use them beyond the boundary of experience.",[]],"reflm:4867":["The use of the understanding is either mystical or logical; the latter metaphysical or physiological. Aristotle and Epicurus.",[]],"reflm:4868":["Plato presented mystical *intellectualia*, Aristotle logical *intellectualia*; the latter erred in that he said they were also situated in the senses. For the concept of cause never lay in sensible intuition.",[]],"reflm:4869":["Chief rule: that all principles whatsoever are only of empirical use, hence that reason has entirely and solely rules of use with regard to experience.",[]],"reflm:4870":["Whether the use of reason with regard to the boundary concepts of the world is transcendent? Whence then come the transcendent *qvaestiones*?",[]],"reflm:4871":["Despair is either pusillanimous or foolhardy; in the former an appeal is made to healthy understanding, either to take metaphysics from it or to throw it away thereby; in the second |\nan appeal to mystical intuition and immediate endowment of the understanding. In skepticism there is no state of rest.",[]],"reflm:4872":["M XII:\nAll analytic judgments have as ground the proposition of identity and contradiction. The synthetic ones have as many *axiomata* as there are *conceptus synthetici* given *per intellectus naturam*. These *conceptus synthetici* are the grounds through which the understanding can attain concepts of experience of things [If], and thus the *principia synthetica* are valid only with regard to the laws of experience, hence *a posteriori*, and are practically true. But *in abstracto* they are valid only subjectively.\nM XI:\n(g judgments of experience)",[]],"reflm:4873":["At the inner margin, across:\nTranscendental philosophy considers not the objects, but the human mind according to the sources from which cognition *a priori* descends in it, and the boundaries. Hence pure mathematics is no part of transcendental philosophy, but the sources are, from which it arises in the mind.",[]],"reflm:4874":["In the method of philosophy (empirical) 2 pieces as boundaries. |\nUniversal laws and rules and the opposite of the same, namely differences in the individual. Hovers between senses and reason, *a posteriori* and *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4875":["One cannot demonstrate the existence of God to anyone who is indifferent with regard to morality, and one may not demonstrate it to one who is decided for the moral laws.",[]],"reflm:4876":["Misological maxims give a logical anarchy.",[]],"reflm:4877":["A science of the understanding that goes to objects in general can speak only of the rules of the use of the same, either of use in general or of the use of the pure understanding. In the latter case it is transcendental logic. As many as there are logical moments, so many are there transcendental elements (categories).",[]],"reflm:4878":["If this writing needs favor in order to get through, then let it always be rejected.",[]],"reflm:4879":["Maxim: that there be great manifoldness in the application, i.e., the closer determination, but yet unity of the rule.",[]],"reflm:4880":["The steps in metaphysics have so far been in vain. One has invented nothing in it. Nevertheless one cannot give it up. Subject instead of object.\nOn the incompetence of the understanding with regard to objects that are beyond the senses.\nOur pure reason is in itself and in a natural way dialectical.\nPhilosophy of pure reason. 1. subjective, 2. objective part; the former: transcendental philosophy, consideration of pure reason itself; the latter: cognition of objects. The last: metaphysics of nature and of morals.",[]],"reflm:4881":["Instinct drives the philosopher to systematic treatises, and that has its great benefit in the expansion of cognition. Talent confines him to individual pieces, and that would have great benefit in correctness, if only there were a means to unite and preserve such. The capacities of philosophers are different in this. One can consider everything in the large and in general or extract rules, the other better notices the parts and differences. All want to make systems.",[]],"reflm:4882":["The appearance of conjugation is a subsumption of a given representation under the general capacity to dispose the sensations. The function of this capacity is the concept of the understanding, and the conditions of the same make the rules, namely of the transition from one representation to another. Thus nothing can be perceived except under the presupposition that it stands under a rule.\nThe concept of the ground is no appearance, but a function of the mind, under which everything must be able to be subsumed, consequently under rules. The observation is only [a], that a case is given. Cognition is the subsumption under the function and its rules.\nIf space were nothing subjective: how then should I arrive at cognitions of it *a priori*? how should this cognition also fit objects.\nJust so, if ground were nothing subjective: how should I come upon it *a priori*?\nHuman reason progresses with pleasure in the expansion of its cognitions beyond the world of the senses and the moral one, but upwards it becomes dizzy.",[]],"reflm:4883":["*theologia naturalis*. After the analogy of the empirical use of reason.",[]],"reflm:4884":["The correctness of moral propositions can be cognized independently of theology and all sciences.",[]],"reflm:4885":["Metaphysics leads to certainty not in the dogmas of reason, but in the maxims of the same.",[]],"reflm:4886":["*dilemma practicum*. The denier of God acts imprudently if he ventures it on the existence of God, or like a villain.",[]],"reflm:4887":["Bulwark of religion, critique and (g discipline.) canon of cognition and *organon* of wisdom. Bulwark of religion.",[]],"reflm:4888":["If one asks, not whether yet another proof of the existence of God could be invented in metaphysics, but whether it could not be discovered of beings that are entirely different from the world, [the] their nature, so etc etc. Otherwise, if one wants nothing but a proof for the already presupposed existence of God, that is easily found. Proofs for moral propositions.",[]],"reflm:4889":["Metaphysics is the cognition of nature *a priori*, whose object is at least given through the senses; transcendental philosophy is the pure cognition *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4890":["Cognition *a priori* is opposed to the empirical; philosophy about the same is transcendental philosophy. Everything that we cognize *a priori* with regard to the objects of experience lies therein.",[]],"reflm:4891":["The more recent philosophers have thought that, because they can make quite good use of their powers without knowing them and their laws, whence they come, e.g. mathematics, it will also be so with regard to the first principles of reason, with regard to quality. (One born blind, who has seen a yellow piece of money, can represent a hundred to himself, but not yet a white one.)",[]],"reflm:4892":["The foremost thing is that, before one ventures a doctrine of pure reason, a critique of it must first have been instituted. But critiques require knowledge of the sources, and reason must know itself. To this investigation one is driven only after long errors.",[]],"reflm:4893":["Locke an influxionist, at the same time a physiologist of the understanding. Lambert an analyst and architectonic. Wolff a mere dogmatist and mathematical head. Crusius a *praestabilist* of reason. (g denied the subordination of all principles under the *principium contradictionis* and yet wanted to give an origin, can give no mark which then are innate principles and which are substituted.) Empiricists of pure reason. Healthy understanding. Misologists. Our end of metaphysics. (*In sensibus nihil esse veri.*) Worth.\n1. Difference of the *phaenomena* and *noumena*.*\n2. Origin of these latter. Innate, mystical; or acquired, logical. Plato, Leibniz. Aristotle, Locke. Formulae abstracted from the senses or not.\n*(g difference of the objects or of the cognition of them.)",[]],"reflm:4894":["Plato the intellectual philosopher. Mystical.\nEpicurus the empirical philosopher.\nAristotle the [int] breaks off.\nLocke had the advantage that, since he did not cognize the *intellectualia* as innate, he sought the origin.\nInnate intuitions or logical principles, Crusius.\nThe *intellectualia* of Plato were innate, because they are *intuitus*; those of Aristotle were acquired as *conceptus*. They are not, however, borrowed from the senses, otherwise Locke could not use them beyond the boundary of appearances. Crusius has *conceptus connatos* or *axiomata connata per praestabilitam cognitionem*.",[]],"reflm:4895":["*Metaphysica est philosophia intellectus puri et transscendentalis est Critica intellectus puri. Metaphysica propria. organon.* (Metaphysics is the philosophy of the pure understanding, and the transcendental is the critique of the pure understanding. Metaphysics proper. *Organon*.)",[]],"reflm:4896":["transcendental\nAnalytic is where the understanding weighs itself.\n—\n—\n—\ndialectic, where it speaks *a priori* of objects. Illusion.\n(g Logic of truth and of illusion.)",[]],"reflm:4897":["There is no transcendental doctrine; hence the *organon* of pure reason is a science that shows the use of pure reason with regard to the empirical in general; thus all philosophy of pure reason is either the critique or the *organon* of the same. The first is transcendental philosophy, the second metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:4898":["At the inner margin, across:\nWe now no longer need to feign in metaphysics (g still conceal something), we can present the objections of reason against theology |\nboldly and without fear, indeed [their] strengthen them, in that we make between them and those dogmatic speculations no partial distinction. For when we afterward investigate them down to their sources, we discover the misunderstanding and unite reason with religion again.",[]],"reflm:4899":["At the inner margin, across:\nIt seems, to be sure, that nothing is more contrary to taste than metaphysics; but the ornaments that gleam on beauty lay first in dark crypts, at least they go only through the dark workshop of the artist.",[]],"reflm:4900":["I do not occupy myself with the evolution of concepts like Tetens (all actions through which concepts are generated), not with the analysis like Lambert, but merely with the objective validity of the same. I stand in no competition with these men.",[]],"reflm:4901":["Tetens investigates the concepts of pure reason merely subjectively (human nature), I objectively. That analysis is empirical, this one transcendental.",[]],"reflm:4902":["All cognitions are philosophical, i.e., belong to philosophy as materials, which cognitions are from concepts. Philo|sophy\nitself, however, as the legislation of human reason, consists of metaphysics and morals. The rest belongs to erudition.",[]],"reflm:4903":["Pure mathematical theorems are methods of determining magnitudes. Here is the method breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4904":["Transcendental freedom is the necessary hypothesis of all rules*, hence of all use of the understanding. One ought to think thus and so etc etc. consequently this action must be free, i.e., not already determined (subjectively) of itself, but have only an objective ground of determination.\n*(g It is the [condition of the] property of beings in whom the consciousness of a rule is the ground of their actions.)",[]],"reflm:4905":["Necessity, freedom and infinity are the three stones of stumbling of reason, against which there are three rationalizing principles, on which the skeptical use of the same is grounded.\n[The question of] substance, action and composition;\nmodality, relation and quantity.\n===NOTES===\n- vernünftelnd = rationalizing (vernünfteln = rationalize); Grundsatz = principle; Gebrauch = use.\n- Anstoß rendered 'stumbling'; 'derselben' left ambiguous ('the same'); 'wieder' read as 'wider' (against).",[]],"reflm:4906":["All categories concern objects of the (s inner) intuition, (s of the activity. Repetition) of the understanding, of reason. The first is merely consciousness.\n===NOTES===\n- Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding; Bewußtsein = consciousness.\n- '(s' and '(g' are the Academy's sigla for later additions, carried over unchanged; the nesting of the parentheses is as in the source and the syntax is unclear.",[]],"reflm:4907":["There is no science of pure intuition except mathematics and no use of reason that would be apodictic and at the same time evident, except mathematics with regard to objects and morals with regard to actions. All other research is investigation of nature.\n===NOTES===\n- Wissenschaft = science; Gegenstände = objects; Naturforschung rendered 'investigation of nature'.",[]],"reflm:4908":["Whether sensible representations according to Leibniz are merely confused representations of the understanding. The understanding does not intuit.\n===NOTES===\n- sinnlich = sensible; Vorstellung = representation; 'schaut an' = 'intuits' (cf. Anschauung = intuition).",[]],"reflm:4909":["Whether through the representation of space we stand in relation with all creatures |\nin general. This is permissible, if space is indeed something subjective, which however is connected with all our forces and through which all creatures are combined with one another. but then certain creatures as spirits will be represented with regard to space only mediately.\n===NOTES===\n- 'geht an' rendered 'is permissible'; Kräfte = forces (Kraft = force; power); verbunden = combined (Verbindung = combination).\n- Verhältnis = relation; geister = spirits (lower case as in source).",[]],"reflm:4910":["If the doctrines of the existence of God (g of freedom) and of another world rest on secure principles of the practical use of our reason (*dilemmatis practici Maior*), then metaphysics may always drop out as a dogmatic *principium* of the same; it still serves critically to keep off the pseudo-metaphysics, and indeed both sensibility from pure concepts of the understanding, and also dialectic from pure principles.\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn = existence; Grundsätze = principles; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding.\n- *Maior* is Latin in the source (the major [premise] of the practical dilemma); 'derselben' ('of the same') ambiguous.",[]],"reflm:4911":["It is a great advantage if one can make the science technical, i.e. bring under functions of the imagination and can divide; e.g. table of the categories. The technical is merely mechanical∗ or architectonic. The former *a posteriori*, the latter *a priori*∗∗. Art and science.\n∗(s divide according to the measure of the eye)\n∗∗(s according to a principle)\n===NOTES===\n- Footnote asterisks of the source are rendered ∗ / ∗∗ to avoid confusion with italics markup.\n- Einbildungskraft = imagination; Eintheilen = divide (Einteilung = division); Wissenschaft = science.",[]],"reflm:4912":["Metaphysics is not for children and youths, but for men. It is a kind of revision of reason. One must already know it in order to be able to estimate its validity. The theologian needs |\nno metaphysics; the simpler, the more [attached] to the empirical principles: the more useful. The concept of the divine perfections he can cognize without metaphysics. Only at the conclusion of his studies does he need it. Logic and morals make the beginning. The practical method of teaching morals also comes last. The logician, metaphysician and moralist at once thereby first of all gets true importance. — — surely no examination is possible in it.\n(g one will not take this judgment ill of a man who, while he is salaried to teach metaphysics publicly, wishes to convince himself that he is nevertheless [for anything] good for something.)\n===NOTES===\n- Brackets in the source: '[irgend wozu]' is the Academy's deleted reading, rendered '[for anything]'. The bracketed '[attached]' is NOT in the German, which reads 'je mehr an den empirischen Grundsätzen' (the phrase is elliptical; I should have left it unbracketed and elliptical: 'the more at the empirical principles').\n- Vollkommenheiten = perfections; erkennen = cognize; Begriff = concept; Gültigkeit = validity; Urtheil = judgment; überzeugen = convince.",[]],"reflm:4913":["Academy of the sciences: revision college. Encourages through honors all writings that it improves. In it, reports are read out.\n===NOTES===\n- Wissenschaften = sciences; Nachrichten rendered 'reports'; the sentence is fragmentary as in the source.",[]],"reflm:4914":[", across the inner margin.\nM XVII:\nThere are pragmatic laws of reason, in that namely one should follow no other rule than that according to which it is possible to use reason. These are rules of the power of judgment, to make use of speculative reason; |\nM XVIII: |\ne.g. the sight of miracles goes beyond all reason in its force of proof, but not the narration. Hence it is a rule for reason, i.e. its use, not to ascribe belief to narrations of miracles, because even if something true has been introduced through miracles, it should afterwards not be believed on account of this narration, but from itself, because otherwise new narrations can overturn it again.\n===NOTES===\n- Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Beweiskraft = force of proof; Gebrauch = use; Gesetze = laws.\n- 'der Vernunft zu gebrauchen' has a genitive after gebrauchen; rendered 'to use reason'. 'M XVII/XVIII/XIX/XX' are the Academy's page locations in Baumgarten's Metaphysica.",[]],"reflm:4915":["*a priori secundum quid* (in a certain respect) or\n— —\n*simpliciter* (simply).\n===NOTES===\n- The English in parentheses is added beside the Latin as the job requires; the source has Latin only.",[]],"reflm:4916":["Through mere pure concepts of the understanding no object at all can be cognized determinately. Meanwhile these are nevertheless the ways of cognizing the object, not as it appears, but is.\n===NOTES===\n- Arten = ways (Art = way; species; kind; manner); erscheint = appears (Erscheinung = appearance); bestimmt erkannt rendered 'cognized determinately'.\n- 'ist' has no expressed subject beyond the preceding 'es' ('as it … is').",[]],"reflm:4917":["It is not enough to know what the representations contain in themselves, nor to what occasions and conditions they owe their origin, but in which faculties and capacities they have their seat.\n===NOTES===\n- Vermögen = faculties; Fähigkeiten = capacities; Bedingungen = conditions; Veranlaßungen = occasions.",[]],"reflm:4918":["Difference between Copernicus and Tycho.\n===NOTES===\n- None beyond the fragmentary nature of the note.",[]],"reflm:4919":["The method decides everything, not how one has thought, but ought to think. philosophizing know or (g a) philosophy know. Philosophy must principally consist in the sources of cognition *a priori*.\n===NOTES===\n- 'philosophiren wissen oder philosophie wissen' is elliptical and kept so ('know' could be read as infinitive 'to know [how to] philosophize' or as 'knowledge').\n- Erkenntnis = cognition; Wissen = knowledge (not used here as noun).",[]],"reflm:4920":["Mathematics proceeds only with concepts that it can make intuitive *a priori*. Philosophy can only subsume under pure concepts and never make them intuitive.\n===NOTES===\n- 'anschauend' is rendered 'intuitive' (glossary: anschaulich = intuitive); the German participle may be read as 'intuiting'.\n- rein = pure; Begriff = concept.",[]],"reflm:4921":["Idea is at all times a concept of reason, according to which the manifold can be ordered. Thus a *systema naturae* is either arranged according to an idea (which however is only didactic) or according to a *dessin*.\n===NOTES===\n- Idee = idea; Vernunftbegrif = concept of reason; Manigfaltige = manifold.\n- *dessin* is a French loan word in the source.",[]],"reflm:4922":["That the radius can be carried around 6 times in the circle cannot be derived from the concept of the circle.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriff = concept; 'herumtragen lasse' rendered 'can be carried around'.",[]],"reflm:4923":["That the pure principles be of empirical use with regard to experience and not of transcendent with regard to ideal objects. They are valid in relation to the way in which bounds for our practical cognition are to be determined by the speculative.\n===NOTES===\n- 'von transscendentem' is elliptical (no noun expressed); first sentence has no main verb ('seyn' in subjunctive).\n- 'von der speculativen' could be 'by' or 'from' the speculative; left as 'by'. Grenzen = bounds; gelten/gültig = valid; Erfahrung = experience.",[]],"reflm:4924":["Dogmatic transcendent doctrine is rationalizing, namely reason overstepping its |\nlimitations. The disciplinarian of the rationalizers is the teacher of morals or even the mocker.\n===NOTES===\n- Vernünfteley = rationalizing (vernünfteln); Vernünftler = rationalizer; Schranken = limitations; Lehre = doctrine; Sittenlehrer rendered 'teacher of morals'.",[]],"reflm:4925":["Philosophy is the legislation (nomothetic) of human reason. The art of reason is the doctrine of the skill of reason according to rules (not laws).\nThe philosopher is in cognition just such an ideal as the sage in the use of his free will. He is the model of all use of reason.\n===NOTES===\n- Vernunftkunst = art of reason; Geschiklichkeit = skill; Gesetzgebung = legislation; Erkentnisse = cognition (dative); Gebrauch = use.\n- 'nomothetic' is a Greek-derived term left as in source.",[]],"reflm:4926":["We can appeal to common human understanding when we want to determine the rule and the incentive of our conduct, thus that which really concerns us. But if we want to ascend further than our duty concerns [so] and to overreach ourselves in theoretical assertions, then we appeal in vain to common human understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- gemeiner Menschenverstand = common human understanding; Triebfeder rendered 'incentive' (not in glossary); Behauptungen = assertions; versteigen rendered 'overreach ourselves'.\n- '[so]' is a bracketed deletion in the source, carried over.",[]],"reflm:4927":["Philosophy is really nothing other than a practical knowledge of human beings; everything else is knowledge of nature and an art of reason; but the magisterial dignity over human reason and all forces, insofar as they are subject to it, belongs to philosophy. O! it is to be regretted that we let this meaning dwindle. Without such a distinguishing term this knowledge is not set apart from others, and there is no actual doctrine of philosophy.\n===NOTES===\n- Kenntnis / Kentnis = knowledge (the glossary also allows 'acquaintance'); Bedeutung = meaning; Benennung = term; Lehre = doctrine; Kräfte = forces; Vernunftkunst = art of reason.\n- 'obrigkeitliche Würde' rendered 'magisterial dignity'.",[]],"reflm:4928":["Across the inner margin: M XIX:\nAll metaphysical axioms that are not apprehendent (exemplar) |\nare called petitions and are hypotheses of pure reason, which in themselves have no ground. For all hypotheses are physiological, namely the datum of their possibility is given, but only the assumption of it *in casu* is postulated. Nevertheless I can make transcendent hypotheses, in order to set against equally dizzying |\nM XX: |\nsomething, so that *petitiones* hold the balance to the petitions. e.g. Perhaps [is] are the pure spiritual natures the only true thing in the world. This all belongs to the negative means of securing practical propositions of reason against the usurpations of dogmatic speculation; they are retortions or refutations by analogy.\nM XIX, between the lines of the preface:\nsynthetic propositions without proof are either axioms or petitions or hypotheses. In the last the possibility is known beforehand, only the positing arbitrary; in the second the possibility is assumed.\n===NOTES===\n- 'ebenso schwindelnden … etwas': the noun governed by 'schwindelnden' is missing (the page break intervenes); kept as is.\n- Möglichkeit = possibility; Beweis = proof; Grund = ground; Mittel = means; Sätze = propositions; synthetisch = synthetic. 'apprehendent' kept as in the source.\n- '[ist]' in 'Vielleicht [ist] sind' is a deleted reading, carried over. *petitiones*, *in casu* are Latin in the source.",[]],"reflm:4929":["We cannot comprehend a first beginning from freedom at all, but just as little a first beginning without freedom, i.e. through chance or necessity of nature (g blind happenstance and blind fate).\nDialectic is artificial or natural; the thetic of the latter [is] contains the true subjective principles and consequences.\n===NOTES===\n- begreifen = comprehend; Freyheit = freedom; Zufall = chance; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Folgerungen = consequences; Grundsätze = principles.\n- '[ist]' is a deleted reading in the source, carried over.",[]],"reflm:4930":["Illusion from lack of judgment is the semblance (the first illusion): *apparentia*. The illusion that conflicts with judgment and |\ndoes not yield to it is the natural illusion: *species*. Of such a kind is transcendental illusion. One will never eradicate it.\n===NOTES===\n- Schein = illusion; Anschein rendered 'semblance' (a different German word, so marked); Urtheil = judgment; Art = kind.",[]],"reflm:4931":["Against transcendent assertions stand not refutations but counter-assertions.\nApparency is opposed to probability and remains even with the certainty of the opposite, is from the *vitio subreptionis*.\n===NOTES===\n- Scheinbarkeit rendered 'apparency' (cf. Schein = illusion); Warscheinlichkeit = probability; Gewisheit = certainty; Behauptungen = assertions.\n- *vitio subreptionis* is ablative in the source (fault of subreption), kept as written; the sentence's construction ('is from') is kept.",[]],"reflm:4932":["All synthetic principles are either anticipations of appearance or of experience, if they are objective; but if they are not that, they are subjective.\nPresumptions of reason are those where I assume that wherein my reason can be exercised fully, e.g. unity of forces and causes.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsätze = principles; Erscheinung = appearance; Erfahrung = experience; Einheit = unity; Kräfte = forces.\n- 'jenes' rendered 'that'; its referent (appearance or experience) is ambiguous in the German as well.",[]],"reflm:4933":["The soul is unity as object of inner sense, but from this I cannot infer that it is persistent as object of outer intuition.\nConversely, to infer from the synthetic but apodictic propositions of space that it is nothing objective, consequently an object of observation.\nThe empirical anticipations are not apodictic, consequently not axioms. The expounding and adjective predicates.\n===NOTES===\n- obiect / Gegenstand both = 'object' (Latinate 'obiect' in source, 'Gegenstand' in the second paragraph).\n- beharrlich = persistent; Anschauung = intuition; Beobachtung = observation; schließen = infer; Sätze = propositions.\n- The second paragraph and the last sentence are fragmentary and kept so; 'exponirende' = expounding (cf. Erörterung = exposition).",[]],"reflm:4934":["The analytic contains that which to all transcendental cognition |\nis common, i.e. the mere concepts prior to every judgment, thus the elements thereof. Consequently no falsity can yet be in them; it is therefore the logic of truth.\nJust as the logical analytic considers only cognition in general and thus gives the guideline of truth.\nThe transcendental concepts are of no hyperphysical use at all except in *subsidium* of the moral ones.\nEpicurus erred in this, that he made the maxims of reason into axioms, thus the subjective rules into objective ones. E.g. One must seek nothing but mechanical explanations, cite no grounds except those which [themselves] can be exhibited through the course of nature.\nThe maxims of reason are founded on the best use of it.\nEpicurus was a philosopher of method. He established more the necessary maxims of reason than axioms and theories of it. He wanted to set it within its proper bounds, but was not dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:4935":["The entire error hitherto seems to me to have been this, that one has wanted to proceed in metaphysics from the parts to the whole, indeed even in that one has mixed in heterogeneous parts. Only, it is here possible merely to begin, in a wholly unmixed cognition, from the whole, and a single one must carry it out completely.",[]],"reflm:4936":["The contradictions and the strife of the systems are still the only thing that in recent times has kept the human understanding free from complete decay in matters of metaphysics. Although they are all dogmatic in the highest degree, they nevertheless completely take the place of the skeptics for one who regards this game as a whole. For this reason one may thank a Crusius just as well as a Wolf that through the new ways that they struck out they at least |\nprevented, so that the understanding not let its rights lapse in a stupid repose, and the germ of a secure knowledge was still always preserved. Analyst and architectonic philosopher. In such a way the course of nature leads its beautiful but for the most part enigmatic order finally even through destructions to perfection. Even a *system de la Nature* is advantageous for philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4937":["Transverse at the inner margins. M XXII:\nIt is of the greatest importance to make a science of reason technical. The logicians have tried it in vain with their syllogistic as a factory [verg]. Only with regard to magnitudes have the inventors of the algorithm succeeded. Should it not be so too in the critique of pure reason, not for the expansion but the purification of cognitions? |\nM XXI: |\nThrough the technical method one can, in the designation, give each concept its function or rather express the *functiones* themselves in themselves and against one another. (Algebra expresses them only against one another, perhaps also so in the transcendental algorithm. The mistakes can be prevented only thereby, and the overlooking.)",[]],"reflm:4938":["The use of reason in geometry is intuitive with regard to the object, discursive with regard to the form; the form of algebra is not discursive but technical.\nThe thetic is the dogmatic part, not objective, but the dogmatic grounds of the method. Distinction of dogmatic illusion, i.e. principle of truth.\nThe mathematicians have believed that, if they worked on the objects of pure reason, they would accomplish something superior. But it is to be regretted that, if they take this cognition objectively, they work an ungrateful field. But in the critique of reason they can create the greatest benefit. Holland.",[]],"reflm:4939":["Critical assertions are something quite different from dogmatic ones; these are objective, those subjective.",[]],"reflm:4940":["The philosophy of ignorance is very useful, but also difficult, because it must go all the way to the sources of cognition.",[]],"reflm:4941":["He who says that there is a God says more than he knows, and he who says the opposite, likewise. No one knows that there is one, but we believe it.",[]],"reflm:4942":["Because metaphysics contains *quaestiones* that are immediately pressing and hang together with the highest ends, so breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4943":["If someone speaks through synthetic propositions a priori, he speaks only of his own thoughts, e.g. that there is a necessary being etc:, namely that without this presupposition he does not have a complete concept of the existence of things. Or he speaks of conditions of the cognition of experience a priori, and then it is objective. e.g. every *compositum reale* has at its ground a force of combination of all parts. Meanwhile these propositions are actually analytic.",[]],"reflm:4944":["One cannot say that transcendent propositions are unsettled. For without the condition to determine them they are not merely indeterminate, but have no meaning at all, e.g. absolutely necessary. Just as, if the pole constitutes the zenith, it is not merely uncertain which quarter of the heavens we have there, but all quarters of the heavens, through which alone we can after all determine our course at sea, cease.",[]],"reflm:4945":["M XXII:\nThere can be no *quaestiones* of transcendental-philosophy whose answer would be unknown to us. For if the predicate is not determined through the subject, then it signifies that the question in itself is nothing, because the predicate in this case has no meaning at all, is neither affirmative nor its *oppositum contrarium*. Just as if I ask, [which which] where I nevertheless, if I am under the pole, am to seek east. Our cosmological concepts have a meaning only |\nM XXIII: |\nin the world, thus none with regard to the boundary or the whole of the world. The possibility of the sensible rests on the condition of that which is given; now neither the boundary nor the whole can be given to the senses.\nWhether the world is 100 million sun-thicknesses large, I do not know, because experience has not taught it. Whether it is infinite, I do not know, because I cannot know it through any experience.",[]],"reflm:4946":["If I assume that a concept a priori has an object, then I must also be able to cognize everything about it a priori for which the concept contains the condition. There is thus nothing uncertain and unsettled there, and nevertheless reason contains nothing other than conditions of empirical use, and thus all attempts of it that are transcendent are impossible and futile. The transcendent concepts are not concepts of objects. They are ideas, *ideae*.",[]],"reflm:4947":["Transverse at the outer margin:\nIn metaphysics one must be subtle. For all this cognition is a priori and without derivation from its first sources insecure.",[]],"reflm:4948":["Transverse at the inner margin:\nOf the influence of probity on insight and of the good heart on the spreading of cognition.",[]],"reflm:4949":["If we regard nature as the continent of our cognitions and our reason consists in the determination of the bounds of it, then we cannot cognize these otherwise than insofar as we also take along with it that which makes the bounds, the ocean that bounds them, which however we cognize only according to the shore, namely God and the other world, which are necessarily considered as bounds of nature, although distinguished from it and unknown to us.",[]],"reflm:4950":["One can gather together the whole of metaphysics in very few sheets, insofar as it is expanding. The analysis or the elucidating can still be continued henceforth.",[]],"reflm:4951":["M XXIII:\nIn transcendental cognitions (g or discursive cognitions) of pure reason no more than one proof is possible. Because everything must flow from the proposed concept. E.g. the existence of a necessary being from the concept of necessity.\nNot all concepts let themselves be constructed. Only those of magnitude, because they make up the synthesis of intuition. But the dynamic one, whereby |\nM XXIV: |\nsomething that does not lie in the intuition is found from what is given in accordance with the law of transcendental association, requires empirical condition and cannot be given a priori.",[]],"reflm:4952":["The sophistical dialectic is an art of illusion. The philosophical one a science of the resolution of illusion and has a paedeutic part, which contains the criterion of truth, and a skeptical one, which indicates the sources of illusion and secures the truth against it.",[]],"reflm:4953":["The belief that there is no God, no other world, is impossible, but the opinion indeed is not (s or doubt and uncertainty).\nAgainst all philosophy of pure reason is misology and empiricism.\nThe empiricism of pure reason. Healthy understanding.\nThe doctrinal concept is either the realism or the formalism of pure reason. The latter permits only principles of the form of the use of our reason *a priori* with regard to experiences. Hence it permits neither to affirm nor to deny dogmatically beyond the boundary of experience. If religion and morality have other sources of cognition than merely speculative ones, then it shows the practical principles of reason as necessarily combined with theoretical *postulata* (postulates). But a *postulatum theoreticum* (theoretical postulate) is a necessary *hypothesis* of the agreement of theoretical and practical cognition. Then metaphysics withstands all objections of pure reason and shows how they are dialectical, i.e. the dialectic of the same.\nIf [they] the *dogmata cardinalia* (cardinal dogmas) are secured *in respectu practico* (in practical respect), though not *speculativo* (in speculative), then metaphysics illustrates and defends.",[]],"reflm:4954":["I have read excellent and acute thoughts, but they do not bring science a single step further and expire with novelty. The cause is this, because there is actually as yet no science and the laying of the foundation has not yet occurred. Just as notions relate to insights, so acute thoughts to the doctrinal edifice.",[]],"reflm:4955":["Sensibility in human beings is not so very much distinguished from reason that both should not go only to one and the same objects, at least such as are represented in one and the same way, notwithstanding that the one is valid for the objects with regard to all possible positions of sensibility, the other not.",[]],"reflm:4956":["Two contradictory propositions whose hypothesis is false can both be false; e.g. that God moves in space in eternity, also does not move in eternity.",[]],"reflm:4957":["The citing of books is not necessary in a system of transcendental philosophy in the outline, as little as in a geometry. Unanimous judgments of others furnish a ground of proof only where it is not a matter of the rule but of its application, i.e. the power of judgment, and where not everything must be derived from one idea, but rather the concept from a multitude of compared observations.",[]],"reflm:4958":["We can make metaphysical hypotheses if we can test them on the appearances. e.g. of the difference of the matters, which is original; but not transcendental hypotheses.\nThe unity of the system is in all hypotheses a touchstone, but in the dogmatic one a *principium* (principle) of correctness.\nThe boundary of appearances belongs with [the] to the appearance, but the thing that makes the boundary is outside the same. Consequently we have cause to infer a being as the cause of the world and a future world, but no means to determine it.",[]],"reflm:4959":["M XXV:\nNow it is ridiculous to ask: what do you have as opinion about the community of the soul with the body, the nature of a spirit, the creation in time. I opine nothing at all about this. But: what origin these |\nthoughts have in human understanding, in that it |\nM XXVI: |\ngoes beyond its boundary. Whence this question may be necessary and with regard to the object can be answered only subjectively. That I know, and there I am above all opinion.",[]],"reflm:4960":["The opponents of religion are provoked to contest truths that they would have let count as propositions of belief, if they had not been challenged by the brazen presumption of those who pretend more than they know.",[]],"reflm:4961":["Freedom brings it about that we regard much as possible that is not actual; e.g. that the river takes another course, because we ourselves can direct it.",[]],"reflm:4962":["In transcendental judgments it is not at all permitted to opine, as little as in geometry.",[]],"reflm:4963":["On healthy understanding as *qualitas occulta* (hidden quality). How far its authority reaches; usurped authority. Lazy philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4964":["Through this my treatise the worth of my previous metaphysical writings is completely annihilated. I will seek only still to save the correctness of the idea.",[]],"reflm:4965":["To cognize something *a priori* means: to have insight (the principles may even be empirical). To determine something *a priori* (i.e. to give and construct) means: to comprehend. To comprehend absolutely is without all empirical conditions.",[]],"reflm:4966":["All difficulty of metaphysics concerns only the reconciling of the empirical principles with ideas. The possibility of the latter is not to be denied, but they cannot become empirically intelligible; the idea is no *conceptus dabilis* (givable concept) at all, no empirically possible concept.",[]],"reflm:4967":["To the intellectual of magnitude belong totality, the limitations or finitude. Such a concept of magnitude is no mere comparison- and relation-concept; it can be understood absolutely of a thing.\nThe whole, the part.\nBoundary-concepts: The first, the last. Everything.",[]],"reflm:4968":["If the doctrine of space and time were to stop at this, that they are merely affections of the mind, not objective conditions, it would be a subtle but hardly significant consideration. But that one must not on this account extend these concepts beyond the boundaries of sensibility is important. That we actually intuit everything in time proves that this is a condition of all our intuitions of the senses, but not on that account that this also has any meaning without this condition.",[]],"reflm:4969":["All new theories that make a great alteration have had to be introduced by someone else. The inventor has never been able to bring them into currency. It was the confirmation through foreign reason that occasioned a good judgment in favor of the innovation.",[]],"reflm:4970":["M XXVII:\nThe philosopher treats that which in all human cognitions is the self-subsistent and lies at the ground of everything; hence the philosophical also pleases immediately among all doctrines.\nPhilosophy is the science of the suitability of all cognitions with the vocation of the human being. To this belongs first philodoxy as the culture and instruction of all talents, i.e. the suitability of cognitions with all sorts of ends. Second, the suitability of the same with the expanded use of reason. Third, the founding of the supreme maxims of the speculative as well as practical use of reason; here great scholarship, indeed even the art of reason of famous men drops out and is without philosophy. Metaphysics and morals, both architectonic, are the two door-hinges of philosophy. The philosopher is no misologist, but one learned in the laws of human reason, and the foremost laws are those that restrict the presumptions of reason to the end of humanity. First extensive historical and rational cognition, just as for a lawgiver first multiplicity of citizens and of the arts. One must, in order to prescribe laws to reason, know it. But only in the maxims of reason does philosophy lie; the other cognitions are philosophical, i.e. belonging to that.\nM XXVIII:\nHistory and description must first of all be treated suitably to reason (after previously they were merely adequate to the desire for knowledge). Afterward their interconnection according to maxims of reason; for their system according to the legislative idea of reason.",[]],"reflm:4971":["It has happened to various ones that the maxims of reason have been held by others to be axioms of it, and the rules of method to be principles of theory (g doctrine), likewise the restriction of judgments has been held to be restriction of objects. Just as Epicurus's ever cheerful heart, which can never be attained otherwise than through agreement with the universal and essential order of things and thus with everything that one calls ethicality, was held, through an unfortunate ambiguity of expression, to be for the sensual pleasure of the senses.",[]],"reflm:4972":["In the world of sense we follow the principles of empirical cognition, in the world of understanding the principles of pure cognition of the understanding. These last, however, are those that have no relation to exposition of the appearances, [but] through which we are affected, but merely to that which is given through the understanding (pure use of freedom), or have morality. Here now is the necessary presupposition that a God is. A belief.",[]],"reflm:4973":["Metaphysics is something that cannot be learned at all.\nIn the hypothetical and disjunctive inferences of reason the subsumption is an alteration of the problematic into the assertoric. How is this transition in ontology?",[]],"reflm:4974":["The understanding is the faculty of bringing everything that may be given through the senses under universal rules for all forces of representation of the human mind.\n1. The conditions of sensibility, under which the concept of the understanding alone is possible (*in concreto*).\n2. Of the actions *a priori*, to determine the function peculiar to each representation.",[]],"reflm:4975":["The faculty of rules is: 1. either to find rules for given representations (g bring them to rules) or also for possible ones. 2. to bring representations under a given rule.",[]],"reflm:4976":["The transcendental antithesis takes place everywhere where I want to think something without the conditions through which alone it can be given. E.g. There is a first in the series of the contingent.\nAll analytic propositions of pure reason are correct *in thesi*, the synthetic ones only *in hypothesi*; the hypothesis, however, is that they are supposed to go to experience as concepts of it or to the condition of sensibility, and yet without the same, or to the completion and the boundaries. If the hypothesis is lacking, then the propositions are arbitrary. If the new condition altogether contradicts it, then they are false. The general hypothesis goes to everything that can be given to us, consequently is an object of the senses. What in respect of magnitude (g everything possible) *excedes* the measure of the senses or in respect of quality as mind or in respect of generality as substance, highest being, goes beyond the limitations of synthesis through the understanding. They are therefore not axioms but anticipations, if they are presented without restriction.\n===NOTES===\n- Antithesis = *antithesis*; Bedingung = condition; Zufälligen = contingent; Sätze = propositions; Hypothesis/hypothesis = hypothesis (Kant's own spellings).\n- Grentzen = boundaries; Schranken = limitations (kept distinct); Gegenstand = object; Geist = mind; Wesen = being.\n- Ambiguity kept: 'ohne dieselbe' (feminine singular) could refer to experience or to the condition of sensibility; 'ihr' in 'ihr gar wiederspricht' = the hypothesis.\n- 'excedirt' rendered with the Latinate 'excedes' (Latin *excedere*); 'axiomen' and 'anticipationen' = axioms, anticipations.\n- '(g' is the Academy's/Kant's sign and is left as it stands.",[]],"reflm:4977":["The understanding cognizes the objects as they are. This proposition can have twofold meanings:\n1. as they would appear if the senses were completely sharp.\n2. What constitutes the origin of the appearances: real functions. The being and non-being (g of something) of a thing in general.\n3. (The things according to their absolute quality, which correspond to these appearances.\nThis something *in concreto*. The latter is mystical and chimerical. Spiritual intuiting.)\nOur understanding cognizes [the] even the first sensitive grounds of the appearances *a priori* or rather the elements of the experiences and |\ntheir fundamental laws; that is also the cause why it can reduce the appearances.\n===NOTES===\n- 'zweyerley Bedeutungen' (twofold) is followed by three numbered items; left as in the text.\n- Erkennen = cognize; Gegenstände = objects; Erscheinungen = appearances; Verstand = understanding; Bedeutung = meaning; Ursache = cause; Gründe = grounds.\n- 'Geistiges anschauen': Geist = mind/spirit; 'Spiritual' chosen, 'geistig' could also be 'of the mind'; 'anschauen' (verb) rendered 'intuiting' (cf. Anschauung = intuition).\n- 'sensitive' is the Latinate German form 'sensitive' kept as the cognate; 'complet' = completely.\n- '[die]' is an editorial bracket, kept in place; the sentence 'erkennet [die] so gar die erste...' is syntactically awkward and kept so.",[]],"reflm:4978":["Of the mathematical concept of magnitude, the only one that without any *data* is capable of a cognition *a priori* into the infinite (likewise geometry), because it is pure intuition of its arbitrary actions and no quality mixes itself in, [but] also through no logical analysis is proceeded in it, no synthetic principles of reason are encountered in it. On the other hand the anticipations of empirical cognition admit a great distinction of the subjective and objective conditions or rather of the relation to experience, to understand them, from that to reason, to cognize them *a priori*. With quantity the same is given to me arbitrarily according to its conditions, with the concepts of synthesis through experience. Those of the thesis are accompanying concepts [of proper cognition] of positing, either absolutely or *a priori*.\n===NOTES===\n- The first sentence has no finite main verb that clearly governs 'Von dem mathematischen Begriffe...'; left as incomplete/awkward as in the German.\n- 'Erkenntnis' = cognition; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'verstehen' = understand; 'Grundsätze' = principles; 'Größe' = magnitude; 'Setzens' = positing.\n- 'dieselbe' (feminine) in 'bey der quantitaet wird mir dieselbe...' is rendered 'the same'; its antecedent is not fixed.\n- 'Die der thesis' rendered 'Those of the thesis' (antecedent plural, unspecified); 'schlechthin' = absolutely.\n- '[sondern]' and '[der eigentlichen erkenntnis]' are the Academy's brackets, kept in place.",[]],"reflm:4979":["M XXX:\nThe proposition: no magnitude is mathematically infinite, is a synthetic proposition; thus it has only a subjective correctness. On the other hand: no number is infinite, has an objective one.\nOn the other hand [is d] the proposition:* no magnitude lets itself be divided or diminished into the infinite (the angle of contact really divides every rectilinear one into the infinite, but yet gives thereby no infinitely small part. For that is not alleged), has not even subjective correctness. For where [the infinite] the part is possible only through the limitations of the infinite, there it is not to be assumed as consisting of elements. One must not appeal to the divisibility of the unity.\nM XXIX:\n*(g meanwhile the possibility of such a magnitude is not to be comprehended through reason. For that would have to arise through generation of it from its parts. The proposition: the substantial is *stabile* and *accidentia fluunt*, has objective correctness with regard to experience. For in this stability I cognize only the subsistence. Likewise: every *compositum substantiale* requires a communally inflowing force. Also: everything contingent has a ground; for the contingency I cognize only by this, that it is not at all times, and the ground is the accompanying, by which I can recognize it again.)\n===NOTES===\n- 'Satz' = proposition; 'Zahl' = number; 'Größe' = magnitude; 'Einheit' = unity; 'Theil' = part; 'Kraft' = force; 'Grund' = ground; 'begreifen' = comprehend.\n- 'nicht angeblich' is doubtful: 'angeblich' normally means 'alleged'; 'angebbar' (assignable) may be intended. Rendered literally.\n- 'wo [das unendliche] der Theil nur durch die Schranken des unendlichen Möglich ist': the bracketed words are the Academy's; 'Möglich' (capitalized in the source) taken adverbially with 'ist'.\n- 'Gemeinschaftlich einfließende Kraft': 'gemeinschaftlich' rendered 'communally' (cf. Gemeinschaft = community); could also modify 'Kraft' as 'common'.\n- 'stabilitaet', 'subsistentz', 'zufälligkeit' rendered as the English cognates; 'stabile', 'accidentia fluunt', 'compositum substantiale' are Kant's Latin. The asterisks (*) before 'keine große' and '(g' are the manuscript's reference marks and are kept; the italic-looking asterisks around Latin are markup.",[]],"reflm:4980":["M XXX:\nWhether there is a single simple action?\n===NOTES===\n- 'einfach' = simple; 'Handlung' = action.\n- 'M XXX' is the Academy's locus note, kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:4981":["Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nThat which we have as aim by the restriction of the [sensible] axioms of sensibility to the mere use in this world, |\ndoes not go to this, that we make place for the cognitions through the pure understanding, but of these we also show that, although the *principia* here may be otherwise, yet the objects too can be only of the senses, but undetermined with regard to the way of the same.\n===NOTES===\n- 'zur Absicht haben' = 'have as aim' (Absicht = aim); 'Gebrauch' = use; 'Art' = way; 'bestimmen' = determine.\n- 'Platz vor' (for 'für') rendered 'place for'; 'obzwar die principia hier anders seyn' kept as a subjunctive ('may be otherwise').\n- 'Reinen Verstande' = pure understanding; 'obiecte' = objects (Latinate spelling, one English word with Gegenstand).\n- '[sinnlichen]' is the Academy's bracket (deletion), kept. 'Am Innenrand quer' rendered 'Along the inner margin, crosswise'.",[]],"reflm:4982":["M XXIX. Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nThe foremost of all sciences of reason are metaphysics and morals, but the former for the sake of the latter. The utility of the same is dialectical; in that reason sets something against the dogmatic objections of reason, the grounds of morals become without hindrance.\nM XXXIII. On the lower margin:\nE.g. One makes the empirical propositions into dogmatic ones: that the soul does not think without concurrence of the body, into the insight that it cannot think; to this one answers from the very same tone that perhaps the body is only the aid against the hindrance of the same. Against this again objections and answers are made; meanwhile reason betakes itself to its natural vocation. The answers must be considered only as retorsions, but not as dogmatic propositions.\nThe political constitution permits doctrines of coercion, because it is the same with what heart they are accepted, but the moral does not, because sincerity is here essential.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Vernunftwissenschaften' = sciences of reason (Wissenschaft = science); 'Nutze' not in the glossary, rendered 'utility'; 'Bestimmung' = vocation; 'Lehre' = doctrine.\n- 'derselben' (after 'Der Nutze') is ambiguous between metaphysics, morals, or both; left as 'the same'. Likewise 'desselben' in 'die Hindernis desselben' (soul or body).\n- 'wieder' (twice) is read as 'wider' (against) in 'Hülfsmittel wieder die Hindernis' and as 'again' in 'wieder Einwürfe'; a doubtful reading of the orthography.\n- 'Setze' read as 'Sätze' (propositions); 'retorsionen' rendered 'retorsions' (Latin *retorsio*).\n- 'das moralische' rendered 'the moral' with no noun supplied; the sentence 'werden die Gründe der moral ohne Hindernis' kept as awkward as in the German.",[]],"reflm:4983":["In metaphysics the pure *notiones*, in morals the pure idea.\nThe idea *in concreto* is the archetype in rational understanding. The rule *in concreto* is the example. The analogue of the archetype is the symbol (*typus*), that of the example is the likeness. The [acceptance for] imitation of the archetype is the copy, the imitation of the rule is the attempt. The *apparentia* of the [archetype] copy is* etc etc. The [eige] harmonious sequel of the archetype is the counter-image, e.g. the human being as very image of God, the subject obedient to the sovereign (his corresponding counterpart). Prototype, *prognosticon*.\n(\n* g The reproduction or depiction, counterfeit, shadow-image. The idea of nature and of morality; the former is genetic, the latter exemplary. The *principia* for us are rules. Image ([Erkentn] intuition of the object), archetype, copy, counter-image, symbol, prototype, shadow-image. The counter-image or counterpart corresponds to a main image. )\n===NOTES===\n- Urbild = archetype; Nachbild = copy; Gegenbild = counter-image; Gegenstück = counterpart; Sinnbild = symbol; Vorbild = prototype; Ebenbild = 'very image'; Abbild = reproduction; Abbildung = depiction; Afterbild = counterfeit; Schattenbild = shadow-image; Hauptbild = main image. These are not glossary terms; each German word has one English word here.\n- 'Gleichnis' rendered 'likeness'; 'Beyspiel' = example; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'Folge' = sequel.\n- 'apparentz' rendered with Latin *apparentia*; 'notiones', 'typus', 'prognosticon', 'principia' are Kant's Latin.\n- The sentence 'Die apparentz des [Urbildes] Nachbildes ist* etc etc.' is broken off in the source; the asterisk is the manuscript's reference mark.\n- Academy brackets '[Annehmung zum]', '[eige]', '[Erkentn]' are kept; '[eige]' is a broken-off word.",[]],"reflm:4984":["In metaphysics to think and to think about it is a difference; the first all do. Some, instead of thinking about the same, rave about it.\nMathematics, physics and metaphysics, although they are rational, contain no *dogmata*. Physics is not apodictic, because the first causes are given only contingently. Mathematics is not from concepts. Metaphysics has no objects that are given *a priori*.\n===NOTES===\n- 'ist ein Unterschied' kept literally ('is a difference'); 'das erste' = 'the first' (not specified further).\n- 'über sie/dieselbe' = 'about it/the same'; 'schwärmen' rendered 'rave'.\n- 'zufallig' = contingently (Zufall-/zufällig = contingent); 'Begriffe' = concepts; 'Ursachen' = causes.\n- 'dogmata' is Kant's Latin (italicized); 'obiecte' = objects.",[]],"reflm:4985":["The transcendental propositions of pure understanding are thetic when they are restricted to the condition of appearance, i.e. of the [*datorum*] conditions of sensibility, under which the object is given. |\nBut if they become transcendent, then they are antithetic and give fine material to the skeptical method. The transcendental antithetic is from the nature of reason itself and no contingent or arbitrary one.\n===NOTES===\n- Satz = proposition; Bedingung = condition; Erscheinung = appearance; Stoff = material; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.\n- '[datorum]' is the Academy's bracket, Latin genitive plural, kept in place and in italics.\n- 'thetisch/antithetisch' rendered 'thetic/antithetic'; 'Antithetic' (noun) kept as written; 'sceptischen' rendered with American spelling 'skeptical'.\n- 'keine zufällige oder willkührliche' has no noun expressed; 'one' supplied only as the English pronoun.",[]],"reflm:4986":["The *organon* of pure reason is a guide to determine the boundaries and rules of the same. Instead of doctrine (objective) it contains discipline and, for *criterio* (criterion) and norm, the canon.\n===NOTES===\n- 'doctrin' and 'Lehre' (cf. 4988, 4989) are both rendered 'doctrine' (one English word for two German words).\n- 'criterio' is Kant's Latin ablative; the English is added beside it.\n- 'Grentzen' = boundaries; 'derselben' (after 'Regeln') is ambiguous and left as 'the same'.\n- 'Anleitung' rendered 'guide' (also 'guidance'); 'disciplin' = discipline; 'canon' = canon.",[]],"reflm:4987":["All philosophy has for object reason: the maxims, the boundaries and the end. The remainder is art of reason.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Zweck' = end; 'Grenzen' = boundaries; 'obiect' = object; 'Maximen' = maxims.\n- 'Vernunftkunst' rendered 'art of reason'.\n- 'philosophie' (not Weltweisheit) = philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4988":["After the logic of pure reason, which is merely critical, follows the *organon*, which is didactic, and through which not merely the estimation is [ge] rectified, but also the procedure is guided. It contains discipline and canon. Not doctrine, because it contains merely negative doctrines.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Beurtheilung' = estimation (glossary); 'Lehren' and 'doctrin' both = doctrine (one English word for two German words).\n- '[ge]' is the Academy's bracket (a broken-off word), kept; 'berichtigt' = rectified.\n- 'Verfahren' = procedure; 'Critisch' = critical; 'didactisch' = didactic.\n- 'welches' (twice) have 'organon' as probable antecedent; left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:4989":["[The] *organon* is the practical logic.\nDiscipline is negative and manly doctrine. (Against error.)\nDoctrine is positive and here children's doctrine.\nThe method of my presentation has a disadvantageous shape. It looks scholastic, consequently brooding, dry, indeed restricted and far different from the tone of genius.\n===NOTES===\n- 'doctrin' and 'Lehre' both = doctrine (see 4986, 4988); 'Kinderlehre' = 'children's doctrine'.\n- 'Vortrag' rendered 'presentation'; 'grüblerisch' = 'brooding' (doubtful as to nuance: 'ruminative' also possible).\n- '[Das]' is the Academy's bracket, kept. 'genie' rendered 'genius'.\n- 'eingeschränkt' = restricted (cf. Einschränkung); 'männliche' = manly.",[]],"reflm:4990":["Reality is the *transcendentaliter addendum*, negation: *subtrahendum*. It serves for transcendental magnitude, E.g. all matter that contains extension, all limitations: extension (space), time intension.\nReality is the object of sensation *in abstracto*,\nnegation\nthe lack of the same.\n===NOTES===\n- Realität = reality; Größe = magnitude; Materie = matter; Ausdehnung/extension = extension (one English word for German 'extension' and Latinate 'extension' in the text); Raum = space; Zeit = time; Schranken = limitations; Empfindung = sensation.\n- 'intension' is Kant's own word, kept as the English cognate; 'transcendentaliter addendum' and 'subtrahendum' are Kant's Latin.\n- The line division of 'negation' and 'der Mangel derselben' is kept as in the source; no verb is supplied.",[]],"reflm:4991":["It matters little whether some propositions of pure philosophy about the object are true or false; it is more important whether they are thought in the proper method and have their proper place in the whole of cognition, as on the general map.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Es ist wenig daran gelegen' rendered 'It matters little' (idiomatic; literally 'little is lain on it').\n- 'Satz' = proposition; 'Erkenntnis' = cognition; 'obiect' = object; 'Methode' = method.\n- 'Generalcarte' rendered 'general map' ('chart' also possible); 'seyn' is subjunctive ('be').",[]],"reflm:4992":["M XXXI:\nOne must, in the estimation of the writings of others, choose the method of participation in the general cause of human reason. From the attempt to search out that which concerns the whole, one finds it worth the examination to offer a helping hand to the author, or rather |\nto the common good, and to treat the errors as side matters. It is sad for reason as a whole to destroy everything.\nSince it would have been vain to credit myself with such extraordinary luck or exceptional acuteness that I would avoid all pitfalls by the same method and [unt] alone hit upon the right path on such confused ground, I have expected a more fortunate outcome than all my predecessors have had merely from the frame of mind into which I put myself and constantly maintained; likewise from the length of time through which I kept the mind open to every new teaching, which pieces I doubt that anyone before me has ever observed. I have [on the ground of] in the territory of the philosophy of pure reason not yet settled anywhere, I have written no large books in it and have not put my vanity under the necessity of defending them and of remaining with one and the same opinion.\nM XXXII:\nI have even never looked again at the small attempts that I scattered about of it, so as not to seem entirely idle, in order not to remain fastened to one and the same spot, and the critique of them [did not strike me any more] from others, whose mildness nonetheless did not take my courage breaks off?",[]],"reflm:4993":["In mathematics one can indeed make the division into pure and applied mathematics, for the objects of experience give no *principia mathematica*, but these are only applied to them. But philosophy one must divide into pure and empirical, because in the latter there lie even certain *principia philosophica* that are undetermined in the former. The universal of the experiences of inner and outer sense, through which they are all possible, is the boundary of pure reason *a posteriori* and thus in the |\napplication. There are namely possibilities that are given *a posteriori*, but serve *a priori* as grounds, to which the remainder let themselves be reduced.\nThe qualities [have] according to their relations must often be given anew through the senses, but the quantity *qua talis* and its relations are at all times, even in applied mathematics, given *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4994":["Many acute and good thoughts have been recorded, but to mere loss; for they have no place in any system, because one has not yet found the outline for it.",[]],"reflm:4995":["The concept of God: is it a dogmatic concept or a concept of belief?",[]],"reflm:4996":["The cognition of God is important only with regard to the practical, but this must be sufficiently certain for itself. For otherwise it would not be obligatory for itself and would not become so through the cognition of God. Thus the belief in God must arise from morality, which thereby supports itself. It is good that we do not know, but believe, that there is a God. Method of teaching such. For mere speculation it is an opinion, which oversteps the limitations.",[]],"reflm:4997":["To proceed according to a guiding thread requires only diligence and attentiveness. But to find the guiding thread itself and the torn-off pieces of it, |\nthe inspiration is demanded, which is just the same in thinking as the stroke of luck is in occurrences.",[]],"reflm:4998":["Along the inner margin, crosswise. M XXXI:\nIn experience there is appearance and real concept. The experience of our alterations contains the mere form of appearance of time and the concept of being. [The The latter] Both together make up something that gives a correct ground in the field of experiences. The former, however, consequently |\nM XXXII: |\nthe concept of existence affected through it, goes no further and has no inner reality, but only the worth of a conditioned form of our representations, i.e. it is no thing or its affection in itself, but only the sensible representation of it.",[]],"reflm:4999":["Along the outer margin, crosswise.\nAppearance and illusion are two different things. The former comes down to how the object is given, the latter to how it is thought. The *apparentia* in an appearance rests on the judgment about the affection of the sense, and, if this is correct, it nonetheless remains *phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:5000":["In pure philosophy, metaphysics of nature and of morals, only one proof can be given, because it must be drawn from a single concept.",[]],"reflm:5001":["A necessary hypothesis of the optional or the indispensable use (practical hypothesis) of the understanding and of reason.\nA necessary theoretical hypothesis with regard to the whole of all experiences (existence of God).",[]],"reflm:5002":["In transcendental cognitions only a single proof is possible, namely from the concept of the subject.",[]],"reflm:5003":["The transcendental analytic is in this still the logic of truth, because it does not judge objectively; the dialectic, however, judges objectively. In it everything must be taken from the subject and yet judged objectively. Hence the subjective laws here easily count for objective ones, namely those that are grounded on the *conditiones* of comprehensibility, for such as concern the apprehension of the object, and these again for universal ones, although they are nonetheless restricted through human sensibility.",[]],"reflm:5004":["Transcendental philosophy in its part on the propositions of the same is dialectical, because, since without critique they carry with them no other touchstone of truth, they must be considered as problematic, consequently also with retention of their opposites, and can become dogmatic only in accordance with agreement with the canon.",[]],"reflm:5005":["The synthetic propositions make up the content of our cognition: what we know; the analytic only the materials for this cognition. |\nWith regard to the former (g *in concreto*) I know no more than anyone; but I know what the understanding can know thereof. I know the rules of the understanding with regard to them.",[]],"reflm:5006":["On the basic maxim of reason, to be difficult with regard to everything that diminishes the use of it according to universal laws. For reason consists in cognizing something from the universal. Consequently: what limits the use according to universal laws, likewise the use *a priori*, is contrary to it. [All] Something for innate breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5007":["Philosophy is the lore of laws of human reason. The artist of reason needs rules, the teacher of reason laws. *leguleius* (pettifogger).\nIt is *nomothetica* (lawgiving) of human reason.\nThe science of the rules of human reason is logic.",[]],"reflm:5008":["The [pra] The theoretical conditions of everything practical are freedom, origin, and future. or the inner and the outer principles of all our ends together. These are also the *crux philosophorum* (cross of philosophers).",[]],"reflm:5009":["Nothing more wretched can be found than to make into rules the emergency aids of our presumptuous reason, where it is not still more wretched to regard these presuppositions as divine revelations.",[]],"reflm:5010":["We in fact make use of the skeptical method in many cases, in order to try whether a proposition also has universality; for if something false follows from it, then it proves too much.",[]],"reflm:5011":["It is primarily necessary to show whence in mathematics so much, in the philosophy of pure reason nothing at all synthetic can be cognized *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5012":["Transcendental cognition is the only natural dialectic.",[]],"reflm:5013":["At the beginning of the transcendental science.\nMy aim is to investigate how much reason can cognize *a priori* and how far its dependence on the teaching of the senses extends. [It] Which are thus the boundaries beyond which it cannot go without the assistance of the senses. This object is important and great, for it shows the human being his vocation with reason. In order to attain this final end, I find it necessary to isolate reason, but also sensibility, and first to consider everything that can be cognized *a priori*, whether it also belongs to the territory of reason. This separated consideration, this pure philosophy is of great utility.",[]],"reflm:5014":["The cause why among the three parts of metaphysics: *Physica |\nrationalis*, *Psychologia Rationalis*, *Theologia Naturalis* the latter has the word nature last, is this, because in those the object is given through the senses and cognized through reason, in this the object is given through reason and cognized according to the analogy of the senses.",[]],"reflm:5015":["A good while was required before the concepts had so arranged themselves in me that I saw them make up a whole and distinctly draw the boundary of the science that I had in view. Before the disputation I already had the idea of the influence of the subjective conditions of the cognitions on the objective, [but] afterwards that of the difference between the sensitive and the intellectual. But the latter was in me merely negative.\nOne will not be allowed to believe that everything hitherto was written and thought at pure loss. [It changes] The dogmatic attempts can always continue, but a critique of them must follow upon them, and they can only be used in order to judge about the illusion |\nthat befalls human reason when it mixes up the subjective with the objective and sensibility with reason.\nTwo *metaphysici*, of whom one proves the thesis, the other the antithesis, take, in the eyes of a third observer, the place of a skeptical examination. One must do both oneself.\nI do believe that this doctrine will be the only one which, when the minds have cooled off from the [heat] dogmatic heat, will alone remain and then must always continue; but I doubt greatly that I will be the one who brings about this change. The human mind is of such a kind that besides the grounds that are supposed to enlighten it, time is also required for it, in order to give them force and progress. And when prejudices are contested, it is no wonder that one at first still contests these efforts through the very same prejudices. For it is necessary first to abolish the impressions and the old habit. [Man w] I could cite various cases where not the originator of the improvement, but later on those who, after long contradictions, found it anew again, to bring such onto the track, have set it in motion.\nI can still picture to myself the reproach that various things are not clarified that should still have been said. [Es] That is just as much as if one made the reproach to someone that, since he only wanted to write a small book, he did not write a large book. What is lacking in a writing makes no fault (missed aim), but the deficiency does that is met with in what one gives out as complete. Moderation and power of judgment are required not to say everything good that one knows, and not to overload one's work with all one's ideas (*Einfälle*), so that the main aim does not suffer thereby. In the *analysi* I have said some not insignificant things etc etc.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriffe = concepts; Grentze = boundary; Wissenschaft = science; deutlich = distinct; Idee = idea; Erkenntnisse = cognitions; Bedingungen = conditions.\n- Gemüther / Gemüth = mind (not Geist); Kraft = force; Fortgang = progress; Eindrüke = impressions; urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Absicht = aim; erläutert = clarified.\n- Syntax of the sentence beginning »Ich könnte verschiedene Fälle anführen« is obscure (»solche auf die Bahn zu bringen« has no clear attachment); kept as in German.\n- »Einfälle« is left in German in parentheses because no glossary equivalent exists; »analysi« is Latin (ablative/dative) in the source.",[]],"reflm:5016":["In the inner margin, transverse:\nMetaphysical possibilities, e.g. that the mind of man is prepared through birth, as a school, for a greater development of ideas, count not as opinions but as answers to the objections that such cases could not be thought at all.\n===NOTES===\n- »Am Innenrand quer« is the editors' locus note. The source spelling »Moglichkeiten« is kept as is.\n- Geist = mind (also the glossary's »spirit«); Gemüt elsewhere is also »mind«, so one English word stands for two German words.\n- Meinungen = opinions; Einwürfe = objections.",[]],"reflm:5017":["In the inner margin, transverse:\nI must confess: I have a certain superstition with regard to various expressions that have occurred to great heads. I do not seek the meaning behind them; but when a concept arises in my thinking [so] and the word strikes me, it seems I feel the inspiration, or also the whole sensation, that he had who had the expression with the same concept, with whom I sympathize.\n===NOTES===\n- Bedeutung = meaning; Ausdruck = expression; Begriff = concept; Empfindung = sensation.\n- »Köpfen« rendered literally as »heads«; »Nachdenken« as »thinking«.\n- The final relative clause (»mit dem ich sympathisiere«) can attach to »derjenige« or to »Begriffe«; the English keeps the same ambiguity. The bracketed »[so]« is a deleted word.",[]],"reflm:5018":["Logic has no empirical *principia*. It is a science of the objective rules of reason, namely those of correct cognition. Consequently the subjective *principia* are merely practical with regard to the human understanding and belong to the *Organon*. After the *psychologia empirica*.\n===NOTES===\n- Wissenschaft = science; Vernunft = reason; Erkentnis = cognition; Verstand = understanding.\n- »Nach der psychologia empirica« is left as a bare fragment; whether »nach« means »after« in order or »according to« is not settled by the text.",[]],"reflm:5019":["I have cited no one by whose reading I have learned something. I have found it good to leave out everything [di] foreign and to follow my own idea. I have not fought against systems etc etc. I have not cited myself, but overturned everything. I do not approve\n|\nthe rule, if one has previously proved something in the use of pure reason, afterwards no longer to call this into doubt like a firm principle.\n===NOTES===\n- »wieder systeme« read as »wider« (against) rather than »wieder« (again); the spelling is doubtful, and »again« would be the other reading.\n- »angeführt« = cited; it could also be read as »led«, but »cited« is kept in both places.\n- Gebrauch = use; Vernunft = reason; rein = pure; Grundsatz = principle; bewiesen = proved. »[di]« is a deleted fragment.",[]],"reflm:5020":["In many places my presentation would have become far more distinct if it had not had to be so distinct.\n===NOTES===\n- deutlich = distinct, in both occurrences; the repetition is kept.",[]],"reflm:5021":["According to Priestley and Locke all cognitions must be empirical, and not a single synthetic one can have true necessity. This conflicts, however, with the use, which is without exception universal.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkenntnisse = cognitions; synthetisch = synthetic; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Gebrauch = use.\n- The relative clause »der ... allgemein ist« refers to »Gebrauch«; the noun is left without a qualifier, as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5022":["Transcendental philosophy is the grave of all superstition. Maxims of reason, conditions of intuition and understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- Philosophie = philosophy; Vernunft = reason; Bedingungen = conditions; Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding.\n- »Anschauung und Verstandes« can be read as »of intuition and of understanding« or as »of (intuition and understanding)«; the German is a bare juxtaposition.",[]],"reflm:5023":["M XXXIII:\nAll remaining philosophical sciences must gain very much if this derivation of our force of reason (which is indeed without benefit) is stopped up; |\nM XXXIV: |\nthat is the preeminent thing about mathematics, that it represents everything as it can be given in experience, but *philosophia transscendentalis* does not.\n===NOTES===\n- Wissenschaften = sciences; Vernunftkraft = force of reason (Kraft = force); Erfahrung = experience; vorstellt = represents.\n- »Nutzen« is rendered »benefit«; it is not Gebrauch (use).\n- »M XXXIII« and »M XXXIV« are the editors' page references, kept as printed. The antecedent of »nicht« (»does not«) is left elliptical as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5024":["(on the dedication.) You have honored me with your letters. The effort, [to you] at [your] your desire, to give a concept of the method of pure philosophy has [my answers from the still obscure in] occasioned a series of considerations to develop the concept lying still obscure in me, and, as the prospects expanded with the progress, exposed the answers to an incessant postponement. This writing can serve in place of [the er] an answer, as far as the speculative part is concerned. Since it is to be ascribed to your summonses and hints, I wished that it belonged wholly to you through the effort of taking it into your treatment. Academies of sciences should take into consideration more the state of the sciences of your age in general than individual elaborations. Yours is the only — — breaks off.\nIt is hardly necessary to mention that this [writing of the] dedication borrows a recommendation from your name and is not there to the end, to the same the breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- »Zuschriften« rendered »letters«, »Zuschrift« in the second paragraph »dedication«; the same German word may be meant in both places.\n- Begriff = concept; Betrachtung = consideration; Fortschritt = progress; Zustand = state; Wissenschaften = sciences; Schrift = writing.\n- »bricht ab« is the editors' remark; the passage is cut off in both paragraphs, and nothing is supplied. The final clause (»demselben die«) is a fragment.\n- Bracketed words are deletions and false starts in the manuscript; the sequence »[Ihnen] auf [Ihr] Ihr Verlangen« is kept as printed.",[]],"reflm:5025":["My method is not very suited to holding the reader to it and pleasing him.\nOne must begin one's estimation from the whole and direct it to the idea of the work together with its ground. The rest belongs to the execution, in which much may have been missed and may become better.\n===NOTES===\n- Beurteilung = estimation (glossary: judgment); Idee = idea; Grund = ground.\n- »an sich zu halten« is kept literally; »sich« could be the reader or the method, and the English is likewise loose.\n- »kan gefehlt seyn und besser werden« is kept as a compressed construction.",[]],"reflm:5026":["On what the plausibility of a metaphysical hypothesis (Swedenborg) rests. On a supposed *intuitus intellectualis* after the analogy of the sensible.\n===NOTES===\n- »Scheinbarkeit« is not in the glossary; »plausibility« is chosen, from Schein (illusion) with the suffix; »apparentness« would be the other reading.\n- *intuitus intellectualis* is Latin in the source. »des sinnlichen« = of the sensible; sinnlich = sensible.",[]],"reflm:5027":["Dogmatic metaphysics is a *Magia iudiciaria*∗, *visionaria*. It is not the *organon* but the *catarcticon* of transcendent reason. Worms of the brain. Palliative means. Relapse. Thorough cure through purging.\n∗(g *Contemplativa*; difference from *magia empirica*.)\n===NOTES===\n- ∗ stands for the footnote asterisk of the source, to avoid confusion with the italics markup. The »g« in the note is the editors' siglum and is kept as printed.\n- »visionaria« and »Contemplativa« are Latin in the source and are left untranslated.\n- Vernunft = reason; Mittel = means; »Würmer des Gehirns« kept literally.",[]],"reflm:5028":["Our method also greatly promotes sincerity. Otherwise one had to |\nconceal the difficulties against important propositions of belief of philosophy and cite merely the advantageous. A paradoxical author could easily be refuted, for he was no longer permitted to answer. He who adhered to the common opinion kept the field alone. Now to every thought the free gate is opened through the skeptical method, and even its objection, with the *retorsion*, serves to determine the limitations of the understanding better.\nThat is also a *fallacia ignorationis elenchi*, to begin from the harmfulness of a proposition when one is to investigate its correctness.\nWhen shall we at last lay aside this great remainder of a barbarous constitution, of imperiously prescribing the way to reason, which is after all the only thing that can guide us.\nHe is always right in such disputes who has the last word. But he always has the last word who asserts the orthodox proposition. For either the [bookseller] publisher is already in fiscal investigation etc etc.\n===NOTES===\n- Glaubenssätze = propositions of belief (Satz = proposition); Meinung = opinion; Schranken = limitations; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; behaupten = assert.\n- *retorsion*, *fallacia ignorationis elenchi* are Latin in the source. »Verfassung« = constitution.\n- The final sentence breaks off with »etc etc« in the source; »[Buchhandler]« is a deleted word.",[]],"reflm:5029":["The intellectual in the objects of the senses (or of experience) is not that they are given in another way than through senses, but that through which they are thought *a priori*, and how one would think everything through concepts, it might be given as it will. The ancients seemed to want to withdraw from reflecting cognition and believed the understanding capable of its own intuitions.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstände = objects; Sinne = senses; Erfahrung = experience; Begriffe = concepts; Erkenntnis = cognition; Verstand = understanding; anschauungen = intuitions; fähig = capable (Fähigkeit = capacity).\n- »und wie man alles durch Begriffe denken würde, es möchte gegeben seyn, wie es wolle« is kept as a loose construction; the clause can attach to »das« (»that ... and how«) or stand alone.\n- »reflectirenden« kept as »reflecting« (the participle of the Latin-derived verb).",[]],"reflm:5030":["That substance endures through all alterations of nature, we infer from this: we cognize in this endurance only substance. |\nThus the change of substance is for our [conditions] concepts of experience a *contradictio in adiecto*.\n===NOTES===\n- »fortdaure« / »Fortdauer« rendered endure / endurance; this is not beharren / Beharrlichkeit (persist / persistence), which the glossary reserves.\n- schließen = infer; erkennen = cognize; Veränderung = alteration; Wechsel = change; Erfahrungsbegriffe = concepts of experience.\n- The first sentence is syntactically awkward in the German (»schließen wir daraus: wir erkennen ...«); the colon is kept. »[Bedingungen]« is a deleted word.",[]],"reflm:5031":["I have chosen the school method and preferred it to [the free swing] the free movement of the mind and of wit, although, since I wanted every thoughtful head to take part in this investigation, I found that the dryness of this method [the] would deter readers of the kind who precisely seek the combination with the practical. I would, even if I had been in the greatest possession of wit and of the charms of the writer, have excluded them from this, for it matters much to me to leave no suspicion that I wanted to win over and persuade the reader, but so that I would have to expect either no assent at all from [him] him or merely through the strength of the insights.\nThe method too arose for me only through attempts.\n===NOTES===\n- Geist = mind (also Gemüt elsewhere = mind); Verbindung = combination; überreden = persuade (cf. Überredung); Einsichten = insights.\n- »Witz« = wit; »Kopf« = head; »einnehmen« = win over.\n- The sentence beginning »damit ich ... zu erwarten hätte« is syntactically loose (»entweder ... gar keinen beytritt ... oder blos durch die stärke der Einsichten«); kept as in German, and the final alternative is left incomplete.\n- Bracketed words (»[die]«, »[von ihm]«, »[Ein]«, »[dem freyen Schwunge]«) are deletions; the placement of »[the]« follows the manuscript order.",[]],"reflm:5032":["All synthetic propositions that express the condition without which it is in general impossible to cognize an object are objective. Those without which it is impossible to cognize it a priori [or] completely are subjective.\nAll our cognition (g a priori) always has *correlata*; if one of them is lacking, we cannot cognize the other completely. All our cognition of magnitude is determined only possible through limitations, thus we cannot cognize magnitude in general absolutely completely, because here we are not to posit any limitations.",[]],"reflm:5033":["In arbitrary concepts one can very easily become conscious of what one thinks in them. Hence the evidence of mathematics.",[]],"reflm:5034":["The antithesis of possibility (g priority) and actuality. No thing is possible without something actual. There is no actuality without possibility presupposed. The analysis of the transcendental concepts is the whole ontological doctrine, but the synthesis contains nothing but antithesis. There is no proposition without antithesis, and why? how do they conflict with one another?",[]],"reflm:5035":["Along the inner margin, crosswise. M XXXV:\nWolf did great things in philosophy; but he only went straight ahead and extended cognition, without sifting, altering and reshaping it through a particular critique. His works are therefore very useful as a magazine of reason, but not as an architectonic |\nM XXXVI:\nof it. Perhaps this, although in Wolf himself it was not exactly to be approved, is nevertheless in the order of nature, that first, without a correct method, the items of information, at least the attempts of the understanding, are multiplied and afterward brought under rules. Children.",[]],"reflm:5036":["I could not put these considerations into this state without at the same time paying attention to the remaining influences of pure philosophy, which I have at the same time completed. For I am not of the opinion of an excellent man who recommends [ein], once one has become convinced of something, afterward no longer to doubt it. In pure philosophy that does not work. The understanding itself also already has a natural aversion to it. One must precisely weigh the propositions in all sorts of applications and even borrow a particular proof from these, attempt to assume the opposite and take postponement so long until the truth is evident from all sides.\nI have always had before my eyes that I had only to work on transcendental philosophy, that the boundary of each science must be exactly observed and that the mixing serves only to make dazzling illusions. but precisely thereby I have lost much that could have served to recommend the writing.",[]],"reflm:5037":["If I only achieve [ver] so much that I convince that the working out of this science must be suspended until this point has been settled, then this writing has achieved its purpose.\nAt first I saw this doctrinal concept as in a twilight. I tried quite seriously to prove propositions and their opposite, not in order to erect a doctrine of doubt, but because I suspected an illusion of the understanding, to discover wherein it lodged. The year 69 gave me great light.",[]],"reflm:5038":["In *Metaphysicis* *fictio* does not count as *probatoria*, but as *supple|toria*\nor *defensoria*, *replicatoria*. e.g. Of the possibility of a pure spirit, of the world of spirits etc. they are then, as something that one can think, opposed to the objections from what one cannot think, assumed only problematically in themselves.",[]],"reflm:5039":["Division. The [law] nomothetic (legislation) of pure reason: 1. negative part, discipline; 2. positive part, canon. Lastly architectonic.\n1. transcendental philosophy. 2. Metaphysic. *Organon*.",[]],"reflm:5040":["Even if, like Hume, I had all embellishment in my power, I would nevertheless have misgivings about making use of it. It is true that some readers are frightened off by dryness. But is it not necessary to frighten off some, with whom the matter comes into bad hands?",[]],"reflm:5041":["All further explanation by the understanding of space, time and apperception is impossible.",[]],"reflm:5042":["M XXXV:\nA concept of reason (world, freedom, necessity) never indicates an object, namely of intuition; consequently the question: whether the world has a beginning |\nM XXXVI: |\ncan say only so much as: whether I can expound a pure concept of reason empirically. Here there is really no |\nobjective question. Thus it is also not problematic, but one can at any time answer it with: No, namely such an object cannot be represented empirically. In metaphysics there is no uncertainty.",[]],"reflm:5043":["The propositions that are seen into immediately after the principle of contradiction are not seen into from it. For this proposition, according to its entire validity, can nevertheless only be cognized convincingly *in concreto*.",[]],"reflm:5044":["What was at first held to be a doctrine of pure reason is now its discipline, i.e. its training and *animadversion*. The discipline is a restriction of the powers of the mind or inclinations within their fitting limitations. The discipline is negative. Not dogmatic. The spirit must not only be instructed: *institution*, but disciplined, i.e. its bad manners weaned from it.",[]],"reflm:5045":["That two *opposita* can be false at the same time, but not true at the same time, because [they] in the one the subject is taken sensibly and the predicate intellectual, in the second conversely. e.g. There is a first beginning of the world and there is none. In the first, beginning signifies the supreme ground, which is constant; in the second the first member of the series. If now the first is taken sensitively, then it is false; the second too.",[]],"reflm:5046":["One will henceforth expect no synthetic objective cognitions of pure philosophy. But it is nevertheless good always to continue in it problematically, in order to discover [the subject] the cause of the origin of the axioms and the subreptions, which make subjective laws into objective ones.\nThe foremost investigation is this: how do we come to cognitions in general and primarily to those a priori? What is the ground of the correctness of the same and of their reliability?",[]],"reflm:5047":["Mathematics can probably have no utility in the invention of objective philosophical propositions, because it cannot judge about the certainty of the *data*. But if these are once settled, then a mathematical head, just as an *arithmeticam vniversalem*, so can invent a transcendental *analysin*.",[]],"reflm:5048":["Mathematic is never empirical, but either *pura* or *applicata*. Philosophy, however, can be *rationalis* or *empirica*. The former is either *pura* or *applicata*. Of magnitudes I can assume no *principia* other than *a priori*. There are no opinions in it.",[]],"reflm:5049":["[The inner sense] Consciousness is the intuiting of oneself. It [would] be not consciousness, [thus] if it were sensation. In it lies all cognition, whatever it may be. If I abstract from all sensations, then I presuppose consciousness. It is the [transcendental] logical personality, not the practical one: this is the fac|ulty\nof freedom, by virtue of which one, without being externally determined, can be cause on its own. The moral personality is the capacity of the motives of mere reason, by virtue of which a being is capable of laws and thus also of imputations.",[]],"reflm:5050":["There are errors that one cannot refute. One must lead the perverse head into [sciences] cognitions that enlighten it; then the error disappears of itself. In vain will one take from anyone the blind opinion of sympathy. Teach him physics!",[]],"reflm:5051":["Along the inner margin, crosswise. M XXXVII:\nAll concepts in general, from wherever they may take their material, are reflected representations, i.e. representations brought into the logical relation of multi-validity. But there are concepts whose |\nM XXXVIII: |\nentire sense is nothing other than one or another reflection to which occurring representations can be subjected; they can be called concepts of reflection (*conceptus reflectentes*), and, because every way of reflection occurs in judgment, they will comprehend absolutely in themselves, as grounds of the possibility of judging, the mere act of the understanding that is applied in judgment to the relation.",[]],"reflm:5052":["One can, if one wants, derive everything from fundamental forces, only |\nnot the criterion of truth from a sense of the true and false that is made especially for the distinguishing of it. For then all touchstone of our judgments is lost.",[]],"reflm:5053":["Discipline. How negative cognitions keep errors away.",[]],"reflm:5054":["Dialectic is that which contains the arguments *pro et contra*.",[]],"reflm:5055":["4 titles of the concepts of the understanding, under each 3 categories. And to these various *praedicabilia*. The *praedicabilia* that are mixed, e.g. in the verbs of action and suffering, time and number.",[]],"reflm:5056":["Our faculty of fiction has its boundaries; another way of intuition than ours cannot be thought, although also not negated.",[]],"reflm:5057":["The rules of the [use] formal use of the logical rules, without weighing the particular kind of cognition, are dialectical, namely of the play of representations according to logical laws, which is properly only an illusion of truth; for it is not precisely true because the cognition has this form.\nHere there occur only instructions of the power of judgment, and there are in this properly not rules but knacks.",[]],"reflm:5058":["In all [general] judgments illusion rests on the confusion of the subjective with the objective. Chiefly in principles of reason, where *a priori* subjective grounds can also be objective ones.\nIn transcendental science everything must be taken from the subject, only some of it refers from it to objects; hence the dialectic in it is something that belongs to the nature of the understanding and of which a science is possible.",[]],"reflm:5059":["The subreptions of the power of judgment. 1. That we cognize everything only through predicates. [so that] and thus have predicates with an undetermined subject. That we hold that which is always the condition of the remaining predicates for the first subject. 2. That we hold the conditions of distinction for necessary differences of things. 3. That we predicate of a subject that is thought internally (thinking being) conditions of outer presence, and of the outer, e.g. body, conditions of the inner. Representation.",[]],"reflm:5060":["Illusion and truth both belong to the understanding. We have a concept of things as they are, i.e. as they are represented according to a judgment about |\nthem, what agrees with the appearances according to general laws; what agrees only with the particular conditions of the appearance is an illusion.",[]],"reflm:5061":["With regard to rules in general the following errors are committed. 1. General rules without particular determination. e.g. [Ub] to prevent indistinctness. 2. Many particular determinations without general principles. The rules drawn from reason about a common object all have a *principium*, e.g. laws.\nAll consideration of the method is the most important thing of a science. The method is either constitutive and scientific or dialectical and a mere manner. Idea.",[]],"reflm:5062":["Other sciences can grow gradually through united effort and addition. The philosophy of pure reason must be sketched out all at once, because what matters here is first to determine the nature of cognition itself and the general laws and conditions and not to try out one's power of judgment on good luck.",[]],"reflm:5063":["Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nThe transcendental dialectic is the critique of illusion, as the analytic is the doctrine of truth. With the ancients it was for the most part |\na technique of illusion or sophistical art. The critique of illusion comes after philosophy. The critique of taste is no doctrine; taste [leid] gives rules and indeed suffers restrictions, but not prescriptions; one should not have given it the name of a science, chiefly not that which is borrowed from an old term that has an entirely wide sense.",[]],"reflm:5064":["The analysis of the concepts that we already have by no means suffices for a cognition of things *in concreto*; we must, through a *synthesis*, in that we consider the concept in many cases *in concreto*, gather up much that essentially belongs to the concept but does not lie in it. Otherwise such analyses are empty (Baumgarten: *perfectio phaenomenon*). Just as if Philolaus said the earth moves, and Copernicus proved it.",[]],"reflm:5065":["I have a very great opinion of the genius of the ancients. We act in a corps, which not only makes genius (as also bravery and skill at arms) dispensable, but also hinders it.",[]],"reflm:5066":["I am very well content if one finds the best of these doctrines propounded among the ancients. That a judgment of the understanding |\nhad already lain before the sound concept of others is to be presumed and serves to unite the understanding of many. But I understood the passage *de iudiciis coexistentiae* in Locke only afterwards.",[]],"reflm:5067":["Those who, after the example of the Newtonians, assumed certain fundamental actions of the cognition of truth did not consider that indeed such a force, which is the first for itself, must be assumed with its peculiar effects, like gravity. But that a force originally to cognize the true, [because this] i.e. to cognize objects according to their true constitution, cannot be assumed without showing how either the objects of [the E] this fundamental force or at least their representation is given thereby.",[]],"reflm:5068":["Belief in God and another world is a sequel from the necessary maxims of theoretical as well as practical reason.",[]],"reflm:5069":["The skeptical method gives a proof *a posteriori* for the discipline of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:5070":["Transcendental philosophy is very necessary, for in an empirical one we can be taught about our errors by experience.\nIt is strange that here a critique, hence first attempts at cognitions, must occur before a *canon* can be established; for a doctrine it will indeed never become.",[]],"reflm:5071":["If something intellectual is considered under a sensible condition, then with *praedicata sensitiva* both *opposita* are false; e.g. *Quod non est in loco, nec est mobile nec immobile*. Yet these predicates are not properly opposed contradictorily, but *contrarie realiter opposita*. If God is not in places, then he is neither in one nor in many places.",[]],"reflm:5072":["In all systems of reason something has always remained over, and it has successively enlarged itself. Mathematics retains its acquisition, and its stock grows, in that daily something new is added. In natural science, from Aristotle up to now, something has always remained over even of false systems, after it was sifted; but metaphysics annihilates itself entirely, in that it makes room for another. The ground has not been investigated. What one took for the ground were the first stones one laid and which slowly sank in a swampy ground. This compels one to draw the methods under suspicion and to investigate the sources in the subject.",[]],"reflm:5073":["The critique of pure reason is a preservative against a sickness of reason, which has its germ in our nature [namely]. It is the opposite of the inclination that fetters us to our fatherland |\n(homesickness). A longing to lose ourselves outside our circle and to move into other worlds.",[]],"reflm:5074":["On discipline. One cannot put intellectual concepts in the place of appearances, e.g. in space, of which those would be only the confused representations, e.g. *ens obscure repraesentativum*.\nWhether nature is an *automaton*, or whether extra-worldly influences also take place in it. Providence.\nPreface. To isolate sensibility and understanding.\nTranscendental ideas are concepts from principles of speculation, not of the intellection of appearances, thus concerning the absolute totality in the synthesis of appearances.",[]],"reflm:5075":["In transcendental science it is no longer a matter of going forward, but back.",[]],"reflm:5076":["Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nThe boundary line of sensibility. Reason cannot restrict it through objections. It, reason, not through conditions or slip something under it. Meanwhile it has a genuine territory with regard to its principles.",[]],"reflm:5077":["Speculative maxims of reason go to the appearances in the world and nature, likewise to the boundaries of the world and original being, in order to obtain expansion and unity; they make use of the analogies of experience.",[]],"reflm:5078":["There is a remarkable difference between the mathematical general formulas and the philosophical ones. The former one cognizes with respect to their correctness only if one is conscious of their truth *in concreto*. Hence they are never empty. In transcendental philosophy nothing is objectively uncertain, because everything merely on the mere relation to object breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5079":["One will still be able to pick out much belonging to analysis and critique from old and new. But then what befell the scholastics will happen: they will be laid aside forever. The matter of metaphysics can no longer be maintained.",[]],"reflm:5080":["To the maxims of reason belongs also: that nature everywhere makes a system, although our cognition is seldom one.",[]],"reflm:5081":["Baumgarten: The man was sharp-sighted (in the small), but not |\nfar-sighted (in the large)*. Instead of his *aesthetic* the word is better suited: critique of the beautiful.\n*(g A Cyclops of a metaphysician, who lacks the one eye, namely critique.)\n(g a good analyst, but not an architectonic philosopher; his outlines of sciences.)",[]],"reflm:5082":["The metaphysical hindrance of all morality is the denial of freedom, and of all theology the denial of necessity.",[]],"reflm:5083":["Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nTranscendental philosophy proves that with our cognition [the] we can never go out beyond the world of sense. If they seem to be more general, they are merely subjective. That even mathematics is the only science that independently of experience, thus entirely *a priori*, can determine something, That the qualities, however, must be given empirically.",[]],"reflm:5084":["Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nIf one leafs back and forth, nothing can seem more pedantic, although it is aimed quite properly at the abolition of everything pedantic in things that concern the nature of the soul, the future and the origin of all things. Only human understanding errs here through subtlety and must be refuted thereby.",[]],"reflm:5085":["Across the inner margin: M XXXXIII:\nThat [crit] transcendental philosophy as Critique not, so |\nlike the *catharcticon* of the Pyrrhonian, itself also purge away, but must necessarily be treated with diligence, just as one must always root out weeds, since the human understanding contains the seed for them, or one cannot, like the pox |\nM XXXXIV: |\nat once entirely eradicate the vices.\n===NOTES===\n- Critick = Critique; Verstand = understanding; Philosophie = philosophy.\n- 'abführe' rendered 'purge away'; the clause is elliptical, and I kept its subject and mood unresolved.\n- The attachment of 'wie die Poken' is ambiguous in the German (to 'Laster' or to the manner of eradicating), and I left it so; the sentence is broken off by the page-break signs.\n- Catharcticon is Greek-derived, in italics.",[]],"reflm:5086":["In the world of understanding the *substratum* is: intelligence, the action and cause: freedom, the community: happiness from freedom, the original being: an intelligence through idea, the form: morality, the *nexus*: a *nexus* of ends. This world of understanding already now lies at the ground of the world of sense and is the true self-subsisting.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstandeswelt = world of understanding; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Gemeinschaft = community; Urwesen = original being; Zweke = ends (Zweck).\n- 'das wahre selbststandige' is a substantivized adjective; I kept it as 'the true self-subsisting'.\n- Grund = ground; Glückseligkeit = happiness.",[]],"reflm:5087":["M XXXXII:\n* There are only four sources of cognition: 1. senses, 2. understanding, 3. divine information, 4. mystical illumination and intuition. The latter we are not authorized to assume, because it is presumption. They are also not to be convinced. Senses and function of the understanding make up all cognition. For those who partake of the inner light this is not written.\nM XXXXI:\n*(g If they could only adduce something that someone had discovered from natural science through this intuition, without going the way of abstraction; and if they cannot, how they can then hope to go still beyond it.)\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis = cognition; Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding; Nachricht = information.\n- 'Sie sind auch nicht zu überzeugen': 'sie' may refer to the 'latter' sources or to the persons; I left it ambiguous.\n- 'über dieselbe' (feminine) could refer to 'Naturwissenschaft' or 'abstraction'; I left it ambiguous as 'it'.\n- '(g' is the Academy's sign for a later addition, carried over.",[]],"reflm:5088":["The unity of understanding of the appearance lies in the analogies of experience. The unity of reason of the principles of these different causes of the appearance: where does it lie? and how is it objective? likewise how does it lead to the existence of God and of another world? likewise of the unity of the ends?\n===NOTES===\n- Verstandeseinheit = unity of understanding; Vernunfteinheit = unity of reason; Erscheinung = appearance; Daseyn = existence; Zweke = ends.\n- The last sentence is elliptical ('imgleichen der Einheit der Zweke?'), and I kept it so.",[]],"reflm:5089":["M XXXXI:\nDiscipline. We cannot judge synthetically through mere concepts of the understanding. They must always contain [data of the] conditions of appearances and serve only for their *exposition*. Mere concepts of the understanding can no more than intuition of the understanding relate to an object. This is always determined through sensibility, for [we ca] our general concept is only a sign for the concrete. Intellectual cosmology is just as much as mystical an *ens rationis*. The usual scholastic and doctrinal method of metaphysics makes stupid, in that it effects a mechanical thoroughness. It narrows the understanding and makes it incapable of accepting teaching; it is not philosophy. Critique, on the contrary, expands the concepts and |\nM XXXXII: |\nmakes reason free. The school philosophers do it like freebooters, who, as soon as they land on an unoccupied coast, entrench themselves at once.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstandesbegriff = concept of the understanding; Verstandesanschauung = intuition of the understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Belehrung = teaching; Gründlichkeit = thoroughness.\n- 'exposition' stands in the German (Erörterung = exposition); *ens rationis* = Gedankending.\n- '[wir ko]' is a deleted, broken-off word ('können'?); the reading is doubtful.\n- 'macht dumm' is left without an object, as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5090":["We have something in the head, and have grasped it, only then when we can bring it forth from ourselves, at least in ourselves. One has the words in the head only when one can pronounce them in oneself. (To this belongs a consciousness of one's organs.) We have grasped what a line is when we can draw it in thought; and the con|nection of things in the world we can cognize only when we can bring it forth through a general action, consequently from a *principium* of inner potency. Substance, ground, composition.\n===NOTES===\n- erkennen = cognize; Verknüpfung = connection; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Grund = ground; Zusammensetzung = composition.\n- 'potentz' rendered 'potency'.\n- 'fassen' rendered 'grasp'; 'brings forth' = hervorbringen.\n- The final three nouns stand as a bare list in the German.",[]],"reflm:5091":["The cognition of pure reason (speculative) can never be applied further than to the field of experiences and has beyond its boundary no meaning at all. Mathematics itself contains nothing that cannot be demonstrated in experiences.\nNecessity in the empirical sense and freedom signify [things] that which on the one hand is always according to a rule, on the other what is not determined through sensibility, but subjectively through the *synthesin* of the understanding.\nSulzer in his small writings hopes in vain for demonstration.\nOne can here survey what and how much can be invented.\n===NOTES===\n- Grenze = boundary; Bedeutung = meaning; bedeuten = signify; Erfahrung = experience; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.\n- '[Dinge]' is a deleted word, carried over.\n- 'Synthesin' is Greek accusative, in italics.\n- 'erfunden' rendered 'invented'.\n- Sultzer = Sulzer; 'hoft vergeblich demonstration' kept elliptical.",[]],"reflm:5092":["To the dialectic of pure reason belongs also the rationalizing against space and time as *realia* or *idealia*, likewise against infinite division.\n===NOTES===\n- Vernünfteln (noun) = rationalizing; Raum = space; Zeit = time; Theilung = division.",[]],"reflm:5093":["Principles of pure reason are: that an absolute completeness of the presuppositions of synthesis be assumed. Hence the *principium necessitatis, contingentiae, compositionis et decompositionis*. They contain the condition of the absolute [unity] collective unity (systematic) [for] of cognition in general. In reason there is a *nexus* of *prosyllogisms* and *episyllogisms*. The completeness of this synthesis rests on the completeness of the principles as well as of the application.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsatz = principle; Principien = principles (same English word for both German words).\n- Einheit = unity; Erkenntnisses = cognition; Vollstandigkeit = completeness.\n- Brackets mark deletions in the manuscript: '[Einheit]' and '[vor]'; the reading 'absoluten[Einheit] collektiven Einheit' is awkward and kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:5094":["The concept of a substance already carries the concept of freedom with it; for, if I could not act myself independently of external determination, then my action would be only the action of another, consequently I would properly be another's action, thus I not substance.\n===NOTES===\n- Begrif = concept; Substantz = substance; Handlung = action; Bestimmung = determination.\n- The last clause ('also ich nicht substantz') is elliptical and kept so.",[]],"reflm:5095":["It is impossible that the appearances should give something unbounded (g and absolute), whether of expansion or of division or of causality or of the condition of existence in general. Hence these negative propositions are objective and dogmatic; on the other hand reason demands the absolute and complete of the synthesis (*principia a priori*). These are objectively practical, just as those are objectively speculative.\n===NOTES===\n- unbegrentztes = unbounded (Grenze = bound); Ausbreitung = expansion (not Ausdehnung); Daseyn = existence; Bedingung = condition.\n- 'Sätze' = propositions.\n- '(g' is the Academy's sign for a later addition.\n- 'Die sind' / 'iene': the referents are left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5096":["The efforts of the metaphysicians have not all been in vain. Only they have no stability without a system of critique. They have had this in thought.\n===NOTES===\n- Bestand = stability (not Beharrlichkeit = persistence).\n- Critic = critique; 'in Gedanken gehabt' rendered 'had in thought'.\n- 'Sie' in the last sentence could refer to the efforts or to the metaphysicians; left ambiguous.",[]],"reflm:5097":["One must make everything difficult at first for the presumptuous thinkers. They should not boldly appeal to their healthy reason. They do not travel in a cultivated land.\n===NOTES===\n- Gesunde Vernunft = healthy reason; Vernunft = reason.\n- 'Vorwitzig' rendered 'presumptuous'.",[]],"reflm:5098":["Do I travel on a plane or on a spheroid. The curvature I must measure.\n===NOTES===\n- Ebene = plane; *sphaeroid* is Latinate and given in English as 'spheroid'; Curvatur = curvature.\n- The first sentence has no question mark in the German.",[]],"reflm:5099":["*Materia et forma* (matter and form).\n*Logica et realis* (logical and real).\n*Conceptus*\n*praedicati* (of the concept, of the predicate).\n* *Consensus et oppositio* (agreement and opposition).\n[*Logica est*] ([it is logical])\n*realitas* (reality)\n*negatio* (negation)\n*Absolutum et relativum* (absolute and relative).\n*Logica Identitas et diversitas realis* (logical identity and diversity; real).\n*(g *vel inter se*\n*vel respectu*\n*tertii; posterior est* [*vel*]\n*identitas et diversitas*.) (either among themselves or with respect to a third; the latter is [either] identity and diversity.)\n===NOTES===\n- Latin throughout; Latin terms kept in italics with the English beside them.\n- 'Conceptus / praedicati' is broken across lines in the manuscript; I did not supply a connection between them.\n- 'Logica Identitas et diversitas realis' is ambiguous in whether 'realis' qualifies only 'diversitas' or both nouns.\n- Brackets are deletions/insertions in the Academy text; '(g' marks a later addition.",[]],"reflm:5100":["Across the inner margin:\n1. Morals: how I become worthy to be happy, without aim at the means of becoming partaker of happiness. 2. Worldly prudence: how I become happy without regard to my worthiness to be so. 3. Wisdom: how I become happy by becoming worthy to be so. Philosophy is the *organon* of wisdom and must deal with the vocation of my nature, the boundaries and the ends of my faculties. The 1st has nothing but *principia a priori*, the second *a posteriori*, the third from both.\n===NOTES===\n- Absicht = aim; Mittel = means; Bestimmung = vocation (glossary also: determination); Grentzen = boundaries; Zweke = ends; Vermögen = faculties.\n- Glückseeligkeit = happiness; Weltklugheit = worldly prudence; Weisheit = wisdom.\n- 'Moral' is a bare noun in the German.",[]],"reflm:5101":["Locke thinks to get through with this, that he says: we have just as little distinct concept of body as of spirit.\n===NOTES===\n- deutlich = distinct; Begrif = concept; Geist = spirit (glossary: mind; spirit; 'spirit' kept consistently, cf. 'Geister' in reflm:5104).\n- 'damit durch zu kommen' kept literal.",[]],"reflm:5102":["Those who limit themselves to what is generally accepted and [everyone] as it were current through contract, and therefore seems generally understandable, have an easy game to show themselves in an advantageous way with their shallowness; they spread easily, because they feel no difficulties and feel themselves bound nowhere; they seem to extend cognition and acquire repute, because [they] to them, like brazen persons, everything that they do with confidence also befits reliably.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis = cognition; gangbar = current; Seichtigkeit = shallowness; dreust = brazen.\n- '[jedermann]' and '[sie]' are deletions in the manuscript; the last clause is syntactically awkward ('[sie] ihnen ... ansteht') and kept so.\n- 'ansehen' (noun) rendered 'repute'; 'zuverlaßig' = reliably (adverb or adjective, ambiguous).",[]],"reflm:5103":["The intelligible world has laws according to which I fit for every world, not merely for this one or the world of sense, into whose arrangement of my own and outer nature or society I also come. It has its own *principium constitutivum*: God, and *regulativum*: moral laws. It fits with the rules of prudence, when these are extended; is not an object of intuition; of it no physiology is possible; it is an object of belief as to substance, but of the reflecting understanding as to the general laws.\nWe know God also only through moral grounds and according to properties combined with morality. To make out the connection of the other world with this one, the transition is grounded on analogical inferences. The *principia* however of inference through analogy and the *argumenta practica* κατ' ανθρωπον (according to man), to which the first also relate, make up the *transitus* (transition) (*argumentum ad modulum humanitatis*, argument to the measure of humanity) *secundum assumta humanae naturae, non hominis singularis* (according to what is assumed of human nature, not of the singular human being).\n===NOTES===\n- Verstandeswelt-type terms: intelligible Welt = intelligible world; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; Glaube = belief; Gemeinschaft not present; kennen = know; Verknüpfung = connection; Schluß = inference; Übergang = transition.\n- 'in welche einrichtung ... ich auch komme' is syntactically loose; the relative clause is kept as it stands.\n- 'des reflectirenden Verstandes' follows 'Gegenstand' in the genitive: 'object of the reflecting understanding'.\n- Greek κατ' ανθρωπον kept; gloss added beside it.",[]],"reflm:5104":["The maxims of reason consist in this, that one assume constitutive unity in the whole of appearances (if one begins *a priori*) and regulative unity in the parts (if one proceeds analytically from the parts to the whole). That thus no causes be assumed except those whose law can be found through observation (although the ground of this itself is not specifiable). That thus no spirits, no blind caprice etc etc. But in the actions *a priori* freedom. That nothing be derived immediately from God, because one cannot observe his rules of action, although *a priori* God is regarded as the highest *principium* of unity according to rules, even in the practical. *In summa*: that the understanding obtain its unity according to totality (constitutive) and the unity of the rules. It thus does not limit itself, but it demands nonetheless a first as *principium* of its synthesis.\n===NOTES===\n- Erscheinung = appearance; Beobachtung = observation; Grund = ground; Geister = spirits; Handlung = action; Verstand = understanding; totalitaet = totality (Latinate form, cf. Allheit).\n- 'angeblich' (as written; cf. 'angebbar') is a doubtful reading; rendered 'specifiable'.\n- Willkühr = caprice; the sentence 'Daß also keine Geister, keine blinde Willkühr etc etc.' is elliptical and kept so.\n- 'schränkt ... ein' = limit (Schranke = limitation).",[]],"reflm:5105":["Dogmatic principles are (g determining) cognitions of reason through concepts. Thus the mathematical ones are not dogmatic, nor are the principles of the empirical use of reason from concepts, for these do not determine, still less the principles of pure reason, but only the moral.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsatz = principle; Principien = principles (one English word for both); Vernunfterkentnis = cognition of reason; Gebrauch = use; bestimmen = determine.\n- '(g' is the Academy's sign for a later addition.\n- 'nur die moralische' is elliptical: the noun it modifies is not stated; I kept it so.",[]],"reflm:5106":["Anyone who wants to judge the products of pure reason literarily, i.e., through wide reading, undertakes futile labor. He can thereby provide himself with objects of judgment. Only, because he has not subjected his own understanding to a critique, his critique is nevertheless always idiotic and not philosophical. It makes use of such principles, the examination of which is actually the end. *Philodox* instead of *philosopher*.",[]],"reflm:5107":["The word »lediglich, blos, allein, nur« against the words »überhaupt, schlechthin, schlechterdings«. The former are words not of limitations, but of the *actus* of restriction. The words »an, durch, zu« are the [relations] functions of the categories.",[]],"reflm:5108":["The German language is the only one among the learned living ones that has a purity that is peculiar to it. All foreign words are in it forever recognizable, whereas English and French can be filled with such without it being noticeable that they had accrued to them elsewhere. For this reason it is worth the trouble to pay attention to this and rather to make use of the foreign words in parentheses. This attentiveness gradually makes the language rich and at the same time very significant and determinate.\nLimitations must be posited for the new composition of words.\nThe general of a language and the *idiotism*.",[]],"reflm:5109":["M XXXXVII:\nThe original as magnitude is unrestricted. As thing in general is simple. Original action (g origin itself) is free. Original existence is absolutely necessary. Everything externally derived is restricted; as something that is given in the appearance (g *Mundus sensibilis*), composite; as sprung forth, conditioned and dependent; as existing, conditionally necessary. The soul is no appearance. In it lies relatively the All of reality of all pos|sible\nappearances. It is respectively with regard to sensations simple (I); respectively with regard to actions [or] free; with regard to all existence of the appearances a necessary *substratum*, which is subordinated to no appearance.\nWe have two principles (g *a priori*) of the empirical use of the understanding: principles of the exposition of the appearances, i.e., of the determination of the concepts from the same; 2. of the architectonic use of reason in relation to the practical. The latter are merely intellectual.\nM XXXXVIII:\nIt is a necessary hypothesis of the theoretical and practical use of reason in the whole of our cognition, consequently in relation to all ends and an intelligible world, to assume that an intelligible world lies at the ground of the sensible, of which the soul as intelligence is the subjective archetype, an original intelligence however the cause; i.e., as the *noumenon* in us is related to the appearances, so is the supreme intelligence related with regard to the *mundus intelligibilis*; for the soul actually contains within itself the condition of all possible appearances, and in it all could, if only at the beginning the data were given, be determined *a priori*.\n(g Reason, *mundus intelligibilis*. Not new synthesis, but completed through boundaries *a priori*.)\nAmong the appearances there is no end. The boundary of the same and thus the completion of the synthesis is in *mundo intelligibili* according to the analogy of our soul and intelligence.\nThe principles: Everything in the world consists of what remains and what is alterable, which inheres in the former. Everything alterable, every state has a cause. And: everything that flows in is in community. This cognition of the *mundi phaenomeni* is never completed. But in the *mundo intelligibili* everything must be completed; consequently it refers |\nto the *ens originarium*, where all of this unconditionally takes place. No decomposition is completed.\nM XXXXVII:\nThe [determinability] unity of the (g absolute) whole of our cognition, determinable through reason, is the cause of the synthesis extended beyond the world of sense, i.e., the synthesis of the same with the intellectual. From ideas. The idea of intelligence, independent of appearances, and of the *mundus intelligibilis*, which remains left over, and of the transcendental *principium* [of] the unity of everything.",[]],"reflm:5110":["Reason is determining with regard to freedom and ends in general, and freedom determines appearances. With regard to them there must be a first and only ground of everything.",[]],"reflm:5111":["1, No absolute completion of the use of reason in sensibility.\n2, Liberation of reason from the restricting conditions of the same. Antinomy of reason: There is a completion of the *compositio*, *decompositio*, of generation and of dependence, and there is also none. The completion is only in the *mundo intelligibili* and its *principio* of unity: the *ente originario*.",[]],"reflm:5112":["The mathematician, the beautiful mind, the natural philosopher: what do they accomplish when they carry on wanton mockery over metaphysics. In their interior lies the call that summons them at all times [themselves] to make an attempt in its field. They cannot, if as human beings they do not seek their final ends in the satisfaction of the aims of this life, avoid asking: whence am I, whence is the whole. The astronomer is summoned to these questions still more. He cannot refrain from seeking something that satisfies him in this. With the first judgment that he makes about this, he is in the territory of metaphysics. Will he now here, without any guidance, rely merely on the persuasions that can grow up [in] him, although he has no map of the field that he wants to roam. Into this darkness the critique of reason sets up the torch, yet does not illuminate the regions unknown to us beyond the world of sense, but the dark space of our own understanding.\nMetaphysics is, as it were, the police of our reason with regard to the public security of morals and religion.",[]],"reflm:5113":["On the inner margin, crosswise:\nThe concept of completion either through magnitude (All) or through the boundary or through purity and separation from all attaching condition (e.g., possibility, necessity) or also the absolute, which hangs on no [possibility] condition.",[]],"reflm:5114":["Dialectic:\nNot only that illusion often deceives; but it is also necessary to explain how it happens that I am deceived, e.g., at the rising of the moon.",[]],"reflm:5115":["It is a strange fate of human understanding, when it, be it through a natural propensity or also through [the importance and] the true interest that drives it, sees itself entangled in a science and as it were condemned to it, which after centuries-long efforts with united force of the most acute heads still cannot be brought a step further. If one wants to give up the effort (g indignantly), then [we would partly through a particular propensity] partly the natural movement of our mind draws us back to it, partly we run up everywhere against questions with regard to our most important concern, with regard to which we can be satisfied in no other way than through some insight in this field. Of this kind I know, fortunately for human beings, only a single science, namely metaphysics, a theoretical philosophy of pure, i.e., of all sources of experience free reason, it is the stone of Sisyphus, at which one rolls restlessly and without ever bringing it to its permanent place. If I say that it has not been brought a single step further, then by this I do not understand the analysis of the concepts of reason; for that is nothing other than the greater enlightenment of what we already know, and in this various people have done much in the exact determination of the meanings of words. But that is not what is sought; rather cognitions of objects that can be taught us through no senses, that thus do not dwell in us, but are to be sought and created: these are what, with regard to which all labor up to now has been futile. One can easily convince oneself of this if one even only considers that even a single cognition that is a determinate contribution to science must by everyone as such be ac|cepted,\ne.g. air in bodies, whereas these only through persuasion come into vogue in certain places.",[]],"reflm:5116":["M LI:\nI have not always judged of this science thus. At first I learned from it what most recommended itself to me. In some pieces I believed I could contribute something of my own to the common treasure, in others I found something to improve, yet always with the intention of acquiring dogmatic insights thereby. For the doubt so boldly uttered seemed to me so much ignorance with the tone of reason, that I gave it no hearing. When one reflects with real earnestness to find the truth, one finally no longer spares one's own products, although it may seem at the same time that they promise us a merit toward the science. One subjects what one has learned or thought oneself entirely to critique. It lasted long that I found in such a way the whole dogmatic theory dialectical. But I sought something certain, if not with regard to the object, then with regard to the nature and the boundaries of this kind of cognition. I found gradually that many of the propositions that we regard as objective are in fact subjective, i.e. contain the *conditiones* under which alone we have insight into or comprehend the object. Yet through this I became indeed cautious, but not instructed. For since there really are cognitions a priori that are not merely analytic but expand our cognition, there was lacking to me a critique, brought under rules, of |\npure reason, above all however a canon of it; for I still always believed I would find the method of expanding dogmatic cognition through pure reason. For this |\nM LII: |\nI now needed the insight how a cognition a priori is possible at all.",[]],"reflm:5117":["All maxims of pure reason are principles of the unity of reason, in its empirical as well as transcendent use. What disturbs this unity of its use is objectionable.",[]],"reflm:5118":["The world can have a beginning only for sensible intuition. For the beginning is a boundary of the appearance *ab antecedenti* (from what precedes). But such an intuition itself presupposes a sensible subject, which itself belongs to the world and thus can never intuit a first beginning. It thus has no beginning. But this boundlessness does not belong to the magnitude of the things that appear, but to their appearance. To the pure understanding it has an origin.",[]],"reflm:5119":["M IL:\nMetaphysics, insofar as it wants to go further than the pure principles of the understanding with regard to experiences, has throughout no other than a negative use, both with regard to the nature of the corporeal and the thinking, and in relation to what is beyond or\n|\nabove nature. The latter cognitions must be cognized according to an analogy of the empirical principles continued beyond the boundary [fortg] of the world, hence as maxims of the general unity of reason. Then the metaphysical concepts serve [den] polemically against dogmatic doubt, in order to set limitations to its objections.\nPhysical utility of metaphysics is also to hold off the dogmatic synthesis a priori, which can hinder the *continuitaet* (continuity) of cognition according to laws of experience, and serves insofar to the expansion of it. 1. that one not come upon last parts; 2. that the different density not |\nM LII: |\npresuppose empty spaces; 3. that there be no material immediate influence without contact; 4. that the world have boundaries. The first and second assertion are principles of reason.\nUtility in (g rational) psychology: 1. that not all substances are material; 2. that experience is not the boundary of all cognition and this world not the world in general.\nIn theology: 1. that the world does not comprehend all things; 2. that not everything is contingent etc etc.\nThe utility is thus throughout negative. 1. To remove dogmatic negations, which restrict the empirical extension of cognition. 2. To restrict dogmatic assertions, which want to extend reason uselessly beyond the practical use.",[]],"reflm:5120":["Relation of appearances to the unity of the world. Unity of the system, i.e. of the All.\nThe unity of the world is the unity of nature in the whole. The latter is the unity of nature and has principles of the understanding. The other has merely principles of reason, according to which nature in the whole is related to something outside nature as a ground of the possibility of it. For the connection of nature is contingent, because it rests on many beings. The ground of it must be unity of the cause. Unity of nature is thus |\nderived from the original unity of the primal being. The absolute unity is unity of reason. The absolute All is opposed to the hypothetical. The absolute unity of the synthesis rests on the conditions of absolute generality.",[]],"reflm:5121":["The only insoluble metaphysical difficulty is this, to combine the supreme condition of everything practical with the condition of speculative unity: that is freedom with nature or the causality of the understanding with regard to appearances. For freedom is the possibility of actions from causes of the understanding. The spontaneity of the understanding in the series of appearances is the riddle. Thereafter absolute necessity is the second riddle, which nature does not give up, but the pure understanding. This is the original condition of the possibility of nature. In the first an appearance is not necessary, but contingent under the conditions of the appearance. In the second something is necessary without any condition: thus the first absolutely contingent and the first necessary.",[]],"reflm:5122":["Whether one can say through mere transcendental concepts: everything that exists is in the substance; [has] the contingent has a ground; and: what is found outside one another is in a relation of a possible community. The definitions of these concepts must then turn out differently.",[]],"reflm:5123":["If the actuality of something is to be proved, whose concept cannot be other than transcendent: e.g. (g the *realissimum*) the simple, freedom, absolute necessity, then the proof can |\nnever be conducted from experience, but merely from transcendental grounds.",[]],"reflm:5124":["The proposition that synthetic propositions a priori take place only as principles of the possibility and exposition of experiences is independent of the explanation of the categories. For where else should they come from?\nAnalytic judgments, because they do not expand, are therefore not to be despised.",[]],"reflm:5125":["My author Baumgarten is an excellent man in judgments of clarification, but when he passes over to judgments of expansion, which indeed are chiefly demanded in metaphysics, he is without foundation.",[]],"reflm:5126":["An *organon* is either according to form or according to content. The *organon* according to mere form (of a cognition of reason) is the doctrine of method. Thus [we have] is the transcendental *organon* only the doctrine of method.",[]],"reflm:5127":["1st part of transcendental philosophy is that of immanent |\ncognition a priori: analytic; — the second of the transcendent: dialectic. Both parts of the critique.",[]],"reflm:5128":["Since we have cognitions a priori, which however go only to experiences, we go with them to things in general, because those are not borrowed from experiences.",[]],"reflm:5129":["A science of things in general really abstracts from all difference and determinations of things as objects and deals thus merely with pure reason: transcendental philosophy.",[]],"reflm:5130":["On the lower margin:\nTranscendental philosophy has 2 parts: critique of pure reason and ontology.",[]],"reflm:5131":["At the very top of the page:\nOntology is the science of the first cognitions of the pure understanding: 1. of concepts, analytic. 2. of judgments.",[]],"reflm:5132":["On the difference of concepts, intuitions and sensations. On empirical and pure intuitions and such concepts.",[]],"reflm:5133":["Transcendental philosophy (s which presents the elements of our cognition a priori) is a science of the possibility of a synthetic cognition a priori.",[]],"reflm:5134":["We can indeed hold that to be impossible which conflicts with itself; but not that which conflicts with the conditions under which alone we can cognize the object. That is not therefore possible which does not contradict itself, but breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5135":["Something in the logical understanding is an object; in the transcendental it is something or nothing, i.e. possible or impossible.",[]],"reflm:5136":["In and \n*subiecti contradictorii nullum est praedicatum* (of a contradictory subject there is no predicate).",[]],"reflm:5137":["Above and \n*Principium contradictionis* (principle of contradiction) is the negative general, but not sole principle.",[]],"reflm:5138":["*Principium contradictionis*: the restricting, not constitutive principle of all cognition, *conditio sine qva non* (indispensable condition).",[]],"reflm:5139":["The *principium contradictionis* is the restricting, not constitutive principle.",[]],"reflm:5140":["Above and \nObject in general is possible or impossible.",[]],"reflm:5141":["Impossible is: 1. that whose thought contradicts itself;\n—\n— :2. to which no object belongs.",[]],"reflm:5142":["Being and non-being is the first; logical: copula, synthetic, analytic; real: possibility, actuality, necessity.",[]],"reflm:5143":["Object either possible or impossible.",[]],"reflm:5144":["Logical possibility, actuality, necessity held to be real (of things).",[]],"reflm:5145":["To the first four words of M § 13:\nAlthough the thought does not itself contra- breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5146":["Under and \nA thing can be undetermined in relation to a concept; posited in itself it is thoroughly determined; posited in itself signifies: relation to the whole possible concept.",[]],"reflm:5147":["Because the matter of possibility must be given to us, and we cannot think it beforehand, all thinking concerns the form. An object of thinking in general for the human understanding is *formaliter*: what does not contradict itself, and *realiter*: what is given.",[]],"reflm:5148":["Three principles. 1. Of contradiction, 2. of ground, 3. of determination.",[]],"reflm:5149":["The relation in a judgment is either analytic or synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5150":["The possibility of things according to mere categories cannot be seen into.",[]],"reflm:5151":["The supreme principles are so only according to the form, because they go to |\nsomething in general. not: from which, but: according to which it is to be judged.",[]],"reflm:5152":["Through the fact that something is actual, it can become an object of intuiting. But if one abstracts from all actuality, it still always remains an object of cognition. This is possibility.",[]],"reflm:5153":["Next to and \n3 principles: 1. of possibility, 2) of truth, 3. of the necessity of judgments; the latter always says *aut aut*, in order to prove apodictically and apagogically.",[]],"reflm:5154":["The *principium determinationis*. Is distinguished from the *principium omnimodae determinationis*.",[]],"reflm:5155":["The possibility of the concept (logical) is that the concept subsists in itself; that of the thing (real), that an object corresponds to the concept. Thus the reference to the general *conditiones*, through which something in general is given.",[]],"reflm:5156":["Above and \n*Nihil*. 1, *negativum*, 2, *privativum*, 3. *conceptus inanis*, 4. *intuitus mere formalis*.",[]],"reflm:5157":["In the left margin \nUnfolding and derivation, contradiction and opposition.",[]],"reflm:5158":["In the right margin \nComparison, combination, connection.",[]],"reflm:5159":["Comparison, interconnection, combination.",[]],"reflm:5160":["In the remarks on M § 14:\nIf I [the ground] posit something at will and something else is also posited, then the former is the ground.",[]],"reflm:5161":["Which of the two, a or b, which have the relation of ground to sequel, is *hypothesis*? Relation of connection:\n*Categoria*\n*subiecti*,\n— —\n*principii*,\n— —\n*compositi*.",[]],"reflm:5162":["*absolutum* is that which is considered in every reference; because we cannot [kön] do this *in concreto* (i.e. *in casu vel qvaestione data*), we consider it *interne*, and what holds positively there holds also in every reference, but not the negative.\nLater (φ) is written into the Rfl. with other ink:\nThe absolute the *restrictionen*",[]],"reflm:5163":["What agrees with the conditions of a concept in general is possible. What exists itself gives a concept. Mere possibility: what agrees with the conditions of a concept *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5164":["5. E II 1369.\nM 5':\nBetween two *logice oppositis* there is no third, but between *realiter oppositis* (gladly, unwillingly) the indifferent is =0*. Motion and rest are not *contradictorie opposita*. For both do not agree with God and the whole of the world. Such *opposita* are both restricted through a condition, as here of existence in space; if the condition drops away, then there is no contradiction. E.g. The actions of human beings are as appearances |\nM 5: |\nnot free, but as *intellectualia* free. The one proposition has here a condition, the other an opposite one; in this case the *oppositum praedicati* can also be said of the other and thus two apparent *opposita* can be |\ntrue at the same time. There is a necessary being *in serie ascendente*, but not *descendente*. In the one case it is a hypothesis of reason (g given presupposition of the members); in the other no such hypothesis is possible, for the former is conditioned, the latter categorical.\nM 5': |\n*(s The first can be seen into, the second not.)",[]],"reflm:5165":["In and \nThe synthetic conditions (g principles) of possibility are only objective if they contain the conditions of the possibility of experience, not of comprehension.\nA possibility is that which data are for thinking; no possibility, that for which there are no data for thinking.",[]],"reflm:5166":["In and \nThe logical concept of possibility: *principium contradictionis* (g analysis), or the real concept: *principia* of synthesis.",[]],"reflm:5167":["5. E II 505.\nSubjectively representations A and B can follow one another thus or also conversely. In order that the sequel hold objectively, i.e. serve for experience, it must be determined, so that I cannot reverse.\nWhy is the *principium rationis sufficientis* next to the *principium contradictionis* the only law?\nThe principle of |\nM 5: |\ndetermination, derivation and division.",[]],"reflm:5168":["In the case of *ratione* and *rationato* the sequel must necessarily be thought not objectively but subjectively, namely: that if I posit the ground, then the *rationatum* is posited.",[]],"reflm:5169":["The connection of the ground with the sequel is synthetic or analytic.",[]],"reflm:5170":["A cognition must 1. be possible as cognition; 2. true, i.e. grounded.",[]],"reflm:5171":["Every judgment has its ground, but not every thing is sequel.",[]],"reflm:5172":["The expression *synthesis* is more general than *nexus*.",[]],"reflm:5173":["The ground: *qvo posito*, if I posit it arbitrarily, something else is posited.",[]],"reflm:5174":["Possible, impossible* and empty.\n*(g impossible is a=–a; for a–a=0 is possible.)",[]],"reflm:5175":["We can much not [for] possible also not give out as impossible, and no rational human being must nevertheless believe it. Ghosts.",[]],"reflm:5176":["Besides the *principium identitatis et contradictionis* there must be other *principia* of *nexus* and of opposition. For through those only the *nexus* and *oppositio logica* can be seen into, but not the *realis*. Which, now, are these *principia synthetica*?",[]],"reflm:5177":["5. E II 831.\nLogically impossible is that of which the thought itself is impossible; metaphysically impossible would be that of which the thought is indeed possible, but the thing impossible. But we cannot assume and reject that. Physically possible is what is possible according to the laws of nature and conditions. Morally.\nM 5:\nThe metaphysical possibility of a thing in itself, insofar as it also cannot be an object of experience.",[]],"reflm:5178":["In and \nThe possibility of a concept from the proposition of *contradictio*, not the possibility of the thing.",[]],"reflm:5179":["In and \nIn every reference (absolutely) it is possible to be upright, but not always to be happy: thus to make oneself worthy of happiness, but not partaker of it.",[]],"reflm:5180":["Under and \nThe possibility of things only through intuition or as principle of experience.",[]],"reflm:5181":["What is possible only under a certain condition (g presupposition), its possibility is restricted through the *condition*; what is possible in a certain case, its possibility is extended, until that which is possible in every aim is possible simply, i.e. without *condition*.\nThe conditional possibility belongs to the *causata*, the absolute possibility to the first cause. Possibility *in abstracto* is merely the non-contradicting; this possibility signifies only the admissibility of the idea. Possibility *in concreto* alone is objective, i.e. that something be possible in the *omnimoda determinatione interna*. What is possible *omni respectu* is necessary, because it is then possible *absqve hypothesi* |\nand at the same time the ground of everything or the All itself, for it consents with everything.",[]],"reflm:5182":["*vid.* § 14. Combined are a and b, whose position makes up one; [or] whose combination is necessary according to a general rule, i.e. of whose one the other can be inferred, are connected; whose combination is contingent, i.e. etc etc., are composed. In all combination there is unity; only [with the] with that of composition is it arbitrary. The necessary transition from a to b is either that from the inner possibility of b, which presupposes a (*accidens*), or of the actuality (*rationatum*), or of the relation, state (*totum et pars*) (g what is reciprocally in combination according to the determinations). The reference to another makes up the concept of an *accidens*, e.g. of thought, of motion. The former is a *principium essendi*, the second *fiendi*. In the former lets from the given concept on breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5183":["Above and to the right of the heading »Connexum«, but yet probably \nThe ground as ground consists in causality; as thing, however, it can also be posited without sequel.",[]],"reflm:5184":["The analytic criterion of possibility is that there be no contradiction, and is not to be seen into. The synthetic criterion of possibility cannot be seen into from concepts. The synthetic conditions of the possibility of experience are at the same time the conditions |\nof the possibility of the objects of experience. This, however, is not the possibility of things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:5185":["*Possibilitas interna et externa* for itself or in relation is something other than conditioned and unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5186":["The conditioned possibility (s the possibility also in *nexu* is greater) is smaller than the absolute, for this is not restricted; the material possibility *non solum in thesi, sed hypothesi* is greater.",[]],"reflm:5187":["The inner or relative possibility; this is the absolute (in every relation) or restrictive.",[]],"reflm:5188":["*nexus* of subordination: through ground. All connection has a ground.\n*nexus* of coordination: through common ground.",[]],"reflm:5189":["7. E II 1072.\nThe principium rationis as anticipation, hence with the conditions of sensibility, is objective. (g for in time there is always something preceding.*)\nThe *principium rationis* as a *postulatum* of *perspicientz* is subjective.\nThe *principium terminationis* as a demand of *comprehension* is subjective.\nIf both are made objective, they conflict with one another; but subjective, not.\nM 7:\n*(g What is given as contingent (arising, *transitorium*) only [through] by means of sensation and not *a priori* from [itself] ourselves, has a ground. (s But contingent means precisely that which does not exist for itself, but only as *rationatum*.) Free actions are given *a priori*. Occurrences, however, or what takes place, only through experience and *a posteriori*. The former is the beginning (*terminus*), to be cognized according to a general law; the second cannot be cognized according to general rules without an *antecedens* and requires for this a ground.)",[]],"reflm:5190":["The *principium rationis* (principle of reason) in the world (g is cosmological, not transcendental) is a *principium cognoscendi* (principle of cognizing), not of acting.",[]],"reflm:5191":["This proposition signifies: nothing happens except according to a general rule of the imagination. For that it be represented in the object, i.e. [of] the [represent] determination of us in the object, comes precisely from this.",[]],"reflm:5192":["Beside and \nThe true proposition is: *Omne contingens est rationatum* (everything contingent is a *rationatum*). Only |\nthat whose opposite is possible in itself has a ground, and where the impossibility of the opposite is concealed, there the thing cannot be taken to be true until that has become manifest.\nBut the existence of all things seems to be contingent in itself; at least we do not understand the absolute necessity in existence and our insight goes only to the *respective position*. In this, however, that which is posited *respective* is always posited by something else, although the *nexus* between ground and sequel breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5193":["Everything in the world has a ground, means just as much as: it can be cognized *a priori* (s as necessary, either absolutely or conditioned) and stands under a rule of order. *1st synthetic proposition *a priori*. Everything has a ground of cognition.\nEverything must be able to be cognized *a priori*, i.e. from the action of the mind, under which a connection is brought.\n(g Conversely I cannot infer with certainty, as in no affirmative judgment.)\n*(s All *coniugation* presupposes the unity of the action in the mind, under which the appearance can be brought, thus insofar as the position of the one is connected with that of the other according to a rule.)",[]],"reflm:5194":["Everything that happens, i.e. on which something follows, must always follow thereupon, |\ni.e. stands with regard to what precedes under a rule. The appearances have a rule. 1. That always something precedes. 2. That thereby the *conseqventz* is determined (always follows thereupon).",[]],"reflm:5195":["That through which something else is posited determined is ground; through the sequel a ground is posited undetermined.",[]],"reflm:5196":["In the last third of M § 20:\nEverything is necessary, absolutely or conditioned.\nEverything must flow from the general as something particular; everything can be cognized *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5197":["This *principium* is a maxim of the use of reason.",[]],"reflm:5198":["Everything whose inner possibility consists in relation has a ground. This is an identical proposition. All composition. all succession. What happens. everything purposive.",[]],"reflm:5199":["Synthetic propositions. Everything is in a substance. 2. Everything contingent has a ground. 3. everything united has a communal |\nground. Precisely these propositions applied to sensibility, e.g. What happens has ground.",[]],"reflm:5200":["In and \nThe concept of the ground gives a rule in general. The whole of the grounds, either in an aggregate or in a series, is complete.",[]],"reflm:5201":["The non-determining ground is the ground of possibility, e.g. if someone has put into the lottery.",[]],"reflm:5202":["7'. E II 1085. 1084.\n*Principium rationis* is the principle of the determination of things in the sequence in time; for that cannot be through time, but this must be determined through the rule of the existence of appearances in the understanding. *Principium* of the possibility of experience.\nM 7':\nThus it is not possible to determine for things their place in time without presupposition of this principle, through which the course of appearances is first made uniform. Succession in the concept of the sequel concerns only the sequel [of that] in positing.",[]],"reflm:5203":["With everything passive or what is given, apprehension must not only be encountered, but also be compelled, in order to represent it as given |\ni.e. the individual apprehension must be determined through the general. The general is the relation to the rest and to the whole of the state. Through its being distinguished from the arbitrary, it is considered as given, and only through its being subsumed under the categories, as something. It must therefore be represented according to a rule, so that appearance may become experience and so that it the mind as one of its actions of self-consciousness may comprehend, in which, as in space and time, all data are encountered. The unity of the mind is the condition of thinking and the subordination of each particular under the general the condition of the possibility of associating, through one action, a given representation with others. Even if the rule does not fall into the senses, one must nevertheless represent the object to oneself as in accordance with a rule, in order to conceive it as that which represents something, i.e. has among the remaining determinations a certain place and function. The beginning cannot be determined according to a subjective rule, consequently not a free action, which has its beginning in each point.\n(g Everything that happens, but not as a first beginning (freedom), has its determining ground.)",[]],"reflm:5204":["Contingency can be represented empirically or intellectually. In the latter case it is a concept of reason, i.e. *a priori*. That now everything that is *a priori* undetermined in itself and yet is determined must be through something else, is identical.",[]],"reflm:5205":["The proposition: everything contingent has its ground, is entirely correct, only that contingency can be cognized only through that which happens; consequently the proposition is not valid beyond the bounds of possible experience.",[]],"reflm:5206":["*Ratio est, quo posito ponitur aliud* (A ground is that, which being posited, something else is posited).",[]],"reflm:5207":["The question whether the *principium rationis* is subjective or objective, and in what understanding it is objective, is grounded on the exposition of the question: by what do I cognize empirically that something is a ground and something a sequel. Without doubt only thereby, that A can never be without B, but B well without A. That now everything is as it were in a series, and indeed where each is connected according to a general rule of order with an *antecedens*, that is the ground — —",[]],"reflm:5208":["The things that are given to us *a posteriori* must just as well have a relation to the understanding, i.e. a way of appearance, through which it is possible to get a concept of them, as a relation of sensibility, i.e. a way of impression, through which it is possible to get appearance. Hence everything that can only become known to us *a posteriori* (through senses) will stand under the general conditions of a concept, i.e. be in accordance with the rules through which it is possible to get concepts of things and to connect everything with the concepts of things and to subordinate it to them. Accordingly everything will appear so that there must be a possibility of cognizing it *a priori*. Free actions are already given *a priori*, namely our own.",[]],"reflm:5209":["Since we do not have insight into the possibility of a real ground: how then do we want to say *a priori* that there must absolutely be such, except where something already refers, according to the rule of identity, to a presupposition. Does this *principium* not hold as anticipation, because without rule we would also have no experiences, but this rule consists in the order of time and of space according to general laws?",[]],"reflm:5210":["The ground is that through which, when I posit it, something else is posited according to a rule, [not] not according to an empirical rule, which has no true generality, but according to a true rule.",[]],"reflm:5211":["Over and \nThat everything that appears must appear in relation to the whole, is to be seen from space and time. That it stands under a rule, however, is to be seen from this, because otherwise it would not appear in this whole according to the unity of the relation to the same.\nIn the world something is, according to time and circumstances, always a sequel in appearance. The question is whether it is also a real sequel, i.e. connected according to a general rule.",[]],"reflm:5212":["The proposition: everything can be cognized through reason. Every assertion must be able to take place *a priori*. Because *a posteriori* only appearances occur, but the things are independent of their appearances. |\nAll cognition *a priori*, however, goes from the general to the particular, from the necessary in itself to the contingent.",[]],"reflm:5213":["Beside and \nSomething can indeed appear, but never appear completely, without standing under a rule a priori, i.e. in relation (*coniugation*) with the other, which is determinable a priori. For since one appearance is connected with the other, because the unity of action connects them, they therefore all with one another, [with] the whole state is connected with a subjective ground, unity in the manifold must flow from this, without which no real appearance can take place.\nFree actions as appearances are determined through grounds, but as (g *physice*, but not *practice**) automatic actions they are original, and the subject determines itself according to grounds. The subject is determined, but not passive, and the necessity of its action is itself necessarily unknown to it.\n*(g what is practically determinable must be physically undetermined.)",[]],"reflm:5214":["All determination in time can happen only according to a principle a priori, i.e. so that according to a rule each follows upon something preceding.",[]],"reflm:5215":["That which is the condition under which we would posit something according to a rule is the cause. If in winter I let a stork fly, it does not become warm. So it is not the cause.",[]],"reflm:5216":["The empirical laws a priori contain the conditions of *apprehension* and *conception* (together of *intellection*). We can combine nothing in the appearances and thus give them a real form, except by combining them with one another, through one another and together with one another, and the appearance determining the mind to this activity. Thus something as object of experience is possible only insofar as it appears in conformity with the laws of apprehension; i.e. if its appearance were whole, it would have to be interconnected according to the laws of apprehension. Just as nothing can appear except in the general sum total of space and time, so nothing can become experience, except insofar as it is combined with another according to the general law of the activities of the mind. Thus nothing happens contingently, i.e. without being subject to a general rule with regard to that with which it is combined (whether this may be with appearing or not). For we can encounter the ground of a particular connection only in an object, insofar as it contains that which can be subsumed under a general rule for connecting. Ground, namely, and sequel are not mere apprehensions, but inferences or general actions of transition.\nEverything that is given to us through experience must be possible to cognize a priori, i.e. its possibility must be able to be cognized from the laws of sensibility or of the understanding, [which] in relation to which experiences alone take place. That it can be cognized a priori signifies: that it has an object and is not merely a subjective modification.",[]],"reflm:5217":["Every object of the senses is in a series, in which it is *determinirt* a priori: the principle of empirical cognition with regard to what happens; everything that is there has a ground, is a *principium* of cognition a priori or of reason. Everything, |\nthat has a ground, has a first ground: *principium* of *comprehension*.",[]],"reflm:5218":["A complete ground is *determinirend*.",[]],"reflm:5219":["Everything in itself contingent happens through a ground; for, were it in itself necessary, it would happen in itself according to a rule and would be the *principium* of it; since it is not that, it is a *principiatum* of a rule.",[]],"reflm:5220":["The proposition of sufficient ground is the *principium* of order in the course of nature, that of freedom from the beginning in the series of things. Because there must be a beginning in the series of sequels, there must be freedom, and even in the course first beginnings are possible. If there is nothing but nature, the series of the [subordi] connected is continuous. But if there is freedom, it is interrupted.",[]],"reflm:5221":["Everything that happens, happens according to a rule, [lies] is determined in the general, can be cognized a priori. Through this we distinguish the objective from the subjective play (fiction), truth from illusion. The appearance has an object if it is a predicate of a substance, i.e. is one of the ways [of] cognizing that which persists there; thus the appearances belong to the representation of something persistent only through this, insofar as they are connected with one another and have unity through something commonly valid. Something can indeed appear to us without the ground of it appearing to us; but we cannot cog|nize it,\nwithout the cognition presupposing a ground, because otherwise it would be no cognition, i.e. objective representation.\nThis is therefore a condition of the cognition of objects, hence of the objects themselves, for mere appearance does not yet give an object. [Ob] It is indeed no condition of apprehension, for that goes immediately to appearance, without knowing its ground. But the appearance belongs to a whole of time, and in this they can be connected only if they flow from the general. Through time things are not connected, but in time through the general of their determinations.\nWe [can] must, if we were to apprehend the appearance completely, be able to make for ourselves a concept of generation. But this is possible only if the contingent, that which happens, [taken] in the whole or taken in its whole determination (g relation) is necessary. If I did not represent to myself that the occurrence with regard to the whole were necessary or a side of that which is constant, I would hold my representation to be no cognition and thus also not to be something that pertains to an object.",[]],"reflm:5222":["The *qvo posito ponitur aliud* signifies the position through the understanding and thus that which I myself do, hence not *qvod datur ut phaenomenon*.\nSecondly: [according to] what, if I posit it at all, posits something else according to a general rule. It is therefore not the *nexus phaenomenorum*. For these give no general rule. *Posito rationato* |\n*secundum regulam communem non ponitur ratio*, because the affirmative proposition cannot be converted.\n*Nexus* of *insition*, of *subordination*, of *coordination*.",[]],"reflm:5223":["Every concept is considered as if it stood in a determinate relation to all possible predicates, and the object therefore lies in a determinate *positu* toward all possibility and is thoroughly determined.\n*Determinabile* is the transcendental matter.",[]],"reflm:5224":["All concepts a priori, arbitrary, [and] transcendental and moral concepts permit a logical essence, [but the] which is at the same time real essence, because, without the concept preceding, we would have none at all *in concreto*; but the concepts a posteriori, which are of empirical origin, permit no cognition of the real essence.",[]],"reflm:5225":["Inner determinations are *affectiones*, outer *relationes*. The *relationes* of the first [ground] cause are no *affectiones*. The *relationes* of all created beings are *affectiones*; their relation cannot change (*variation*) without their inner alteration (*mutation*); that is the property of a thing that belongs in general as a part to the whole and where this *partialitet* is a condition of its existence. Can one not say of this being that all its *affectiones* are at the same time *relationes* and that an inner alteration cannot be without an outer?",[]],"reflm:5226":["13. E II 993.\nA law goes to the intention, which may be good. The rule to the exercise and procedure with an arbitrary intention. Hence practical geometry has only rules, not laws, likewise prudence. Yet physics has laws, because it does not contain what is to be done, but what happens, and thus the agreement with that which is in conformity with a ground not arbitrary, but actually given. Laws have a general validity, rules can have a merely common validity. The arithmetical rules are of the first kind; the rules of thrift, of ha|bits,\nM 13: |\nof foods, of politeness are only *in subsidium* of the practical understanding *in concreto* and are necessary, because we place no particular trust in its correct use or in our own inclination, to guide the former well on the whole, to bring the latter under constraint. They are the go-cart of weak heads, of the childish.",[]],"reflm:5227":["Plumb line, guiding thread. Through the norm everything down to the individual actions is determined, e.g. spherical trigonometry. One need only go along the thread. e.g. mechanical construction.",[]],"reflm:5228":["Possibility, actuality and necessity are indeed logical, but no [real] metaphysical predicates, i.e. determinations. Through them we do not cognize the (g something in the) things, but the relation of their concepts to the faculty of the mind to posit and to cancel. 1. The |\nrelation to the faculty (possibility), second to the activity, third to the activity, the opposite of which is not in our faculty.\nThetic proposition: modal or pure.",[]],"reflm:5229":["If every possible were *qvodammodo indeterminatum*, then some determinations would not be possible.\nHence, because every determination (g also) must belong to mere possibility, but existence does not belong to it, it is no determination.",[]],"reflm:5230":["everything that exists is thoroughly determined, for it is posited objectively, i.e., absolutely; but it is for that reason not determined through its concept. Conversely: what is thoroughly determined through its concept is for that reason not existing, precisely because it is only posited respectively to the concept.",[]],"reflm:5231":["Synthetic judgment. 1. relative to the concept; 2. also beyond the concept, in itself; 3. thoroughly determined through the concept.\nAll existential propositions synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5232":["On things, matters and beings. The thing is [indeterminate with regard to the] without personality, but merely insofar as it is thought; matter, insofar as it is given.",[]],"reflm:5233":["The thing seems to be the object in distinction from its determinations.\nThat whose determinations contradict one another or are empty concepts is a non-thing.",[]],"reflm:5234":["Skill has rules, prudence maxims, morality laws. The lack of skill (g compliant unskillfulness) is replaced by a norm or guideline (pattern), [the mere animal capacity] (g refractory) needs discipline. [(Examples]",[]],"reflm:5235":["Prudence can be subjected [according to] rules, which however can never be so determined for all cases that a practical understanding would not be required in particular to cognize whether a case is under this rule or not. For the rules say only what is good to do taken on average. Thus no rule is without exception. But laws are at all times without exception, though not without restriction, if they contain merely *motiva obligandi* (motives of obliging) and not *obligantia* (what obliges).",[]],"reflm:5236":["Morality has laws, practical instruction rules or norms, guideline, only not to err in the exercise. One sets rules for oneself if one does not trust oneself to conduct oneself according to prudence in individual cases.",[]],"reflm:5237":["The rule goes only to the way in which what one wills shall happen |\n; the law contains the *imperativus* (imperative) of what shall happen (of the end); the maxim is the subjective law, i.e., what one has set oneself to do universally.\nBecause the rule is the application of a law *in concreto*, for which healthy understanding is required, whose place cannot be replaced by a general prescription, the rules, because they are empirically abstracted from most cases and therefore have no true universality, also do not hold punctually, and so there is no rule without exception.\nLaws are to be without exceptions, likewise axioms; but rules are never without exception, for they are the breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5238":["Laws [are have a merely] are objective prescriptions, rules prescriptions from a subjective ground. Rules are at all times practical, but serve to apply a law well *in concreto*. Of this, in turn, no laws can be given, but healthy understanding is required and, in default of it, rules, which never guide securely, but only fittingly in most cases; whoever therefore holds to his rule should know how to make the exceptions according to healthy understanding. They are the go-cart of the stupid. When rules of politeness are given to someone, rules of life. What cannot be cognized a priori.",[]],"reflm:5239":["Rules that one makes for oneself in eating, in play, in intercourse are often raised to laws when one does not trust oneself to much good on account of the exceptions, chiefly on account of the affect.",[]],"reflm:5240":["The law (g the command, *praeceptum*, if it is the will of another), the statute (g *statutum*), the guideline (norm), the guiding thread (g formula, model, pattern, archetype. Idea).\nLaws of religion contain the canon and the statutes. The former are *principia normalia*, the latter observances (*formalia*). The canon is *principium constitutivum*, the statutes *regulativa* and arbitrary, can therefore not burden the conscience.",[]],"reflm:5241":["Rules are prescriptions of optional use; thus all arithmetical, geometrical etc etc. rules are problematic. Laws are rules of necessary use. Thus they enunciate a necessity. All transcendental rules are laws.",[]],"reflm:5242":["Nature has laws, skill rules.",[]],"reflm:5243":["In relation to the data, e.g. That also for a thing possible in itself the conditions of existence be given, consequently [the pos] it be synthetically possible. e.g. A spirit.",[]],"reflm:5244":["Order is a condition of distinctness, this of the cognition of truth, but order is not truth.",[]],"reflm:5245":["Perfection is a mathematical concept, expresses the totality, *completudinum*, and is only one (the *maximum* is the proper measure for objects of the understanding; for there it must not be arbitrary, but given: perfectly evil, hell). The [form] quality in what is perfect is the *consensus* to one. But that is only the form of perfection. This pleases especially, whatever the material or end may be; just as talents without merits, because this *consensus* can nevertheless be directed to the good or imitated. The children of the world are more prudent etc etc. Finally a perfection presupposes the end, consequently the first grounds of satisfaction and dissatisfaction, and can only be borrowed from feeling.",[]],"reflm:5246":["Good is what reason cognizes as object of the will. The agreement with an end of reason.",[]],"reflm:5247":["Next to and \nThe idea is unique (*individuum*), self-subsistent and eternal. That is the divine of our soul, that it is capable of ideas. The senses give only copies or even *apparentzen*.",[]],"reflm:5248":["Idea is the representation of the whole, insofar as it necessarily precedes the determination of the parts. It can never be represented empirically, because in experience one goes from the parts to the whole through suc|cessive\nsynthesis. It is the archetype of things, because certain objects are possible only through an idea. Transcendental ideas are those where the (g absolute) whole [the] in general determines the parts in aggregate or series.",[]],"reflm:5249":["The inner necessity of a thing cannot be thought at all; consequently all such necessity must be respective, either in every respect or only in some, i.e., absolutely necessary. The inwardly breaks off.\nAbsolutely possible is not every thing.",[]],"reflm:5250":["Over and \nInwardly necessary, i.e., without any relation, nothing can be thought except merely a judgment in which a relation lies, which however itself stands in no relation at all to other judgments. But absolutely necessary, i.e., in every relation, a thing can be if it contains the condition of all possibility. 1. as a *datum originarium* the primitive and universally valid existence (matter of possibility); 2. as a |\nsingle thing, because the possibility of many rests on the difference of the negations. These however are limitations. But the limitations ultimately presuppose the unlimited and the particularity the *omnitudinem*.",[]],"reflm:5251":["Inner contingency is not yet the respective, still much less the absolute contingency. Only freedom is an absolutely contingent action *respectu stimulorum*. [It is free]\n*omnimode* contingent (throughout): of this we cannot see into the existence.",[]],"reflm:5252":["The relation of every possible thing to the All, i.e., the thoroughgoing determination, through which it as it were borders on everything possible and receives its determination merely through this bordering, proves that we represent it as contained in an All. Explanation of this appearance.",[]],"reflm:5253":["At the lower margin:\nThe absolute necessity we can indeed think, so that we understand it; but to see into, to think a priori is not so easy.\nThe concept of the understanding of the absolute necessity of a proposition is problematic, the concept of reason is [dogmatic or] definitive. The definitive concept is either subjective and a concept of method or objective and dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:5254":["At the inner margin, crosswise\nWhat is inwardly possible is not yet possible in every relation. |\nBut what is possible in every relation can be so only as a ground, and consequently it is a universally valid and necessary being.",[]],"reflm:5255":["*Praedicatum est vel constitutivum vel modale, prius determinatio.* (The predicate is either constitutive or modal, the former a determination.)\nExistence is no constitutive predicate (*determinatio*), thus it also cannot be found *per analysin* from the concept of a thing as belonging to its content. Thus it cannot be proved objectively from concepts, but can well [as] in relation to everything else as derived concepts be regarded like a necessary *substratum*.",[]],"reflm:5256":["A thing cannot be inwardly necessary where it is not necessary in every regard; for that reason, since its necessity is restricted by no condition, it is considered regarded as inner.",[]],"reflm:5257":["The first hypothesis of everything is necessary in every regard, but the inner necessity cannot be seen into.",[]],"reflm:5258":["The simply necessary can only be represented as necessary condition of the cognition of things in general and indeed of all things. It is then thought as condition of the possibility of the matters, and indeed on account of the derivation of all possible relations from |\nthe relation of each given one to the All of possibility it must consist in the all-sufficiency of the condition.",[]],"reflm:5259":["The absolutely necessary is the substratum of possibility in general, the *ens realissimum*, namely insofar as all possibility consists in the limitation of it.",[]],"reflm:5260":["The first hypothesis of possibility is necessary, that of all possibility is absolutely necessary. In every respect necessary as hypothesis is only a single one, because everything is only once.\n===NOTES===\n- *Nothwendig* = necessary; *Möglichkeit* = possibility.\n- *In aller Absicht*: *Absicht* (glossary: aim; intention) is rendered 'respect' because the phrase is an idiom (= *in aller Hinsicht*). The reading is doubtful.",[]],"reflm:5261":["Absolute necessity is when the opposite conflicts with the [condition] synthetic condition of possibility; *nihil vel logice tale vel transscendentaliter*.\n(s Absolute necessity, since it cannot be cognized according to analytic principles of the understanding, will be from synthetic ones.)\n===NOTES===\n- *Gegentheil* = opposite; *wiederstreitet* = conflicts with; *erkant* = cognized (*erkennen*); *Verstand* = understanding.\n- The Latin is left untranslated in the text. Its sense is roughly 'nothing [is] such either logically or transcendentally' and is left to the reader.\n- *(s* is the Academy Edition's sign, carried over as it stands.\n- *[Bedingung]* is the bracketed (deleted) reading, translated inside the brackets.",[]],"reflm:5262":["At the upper margin and at the outer margin\nIt is indeed something in every respect (*absolute et sine conditione restrictiva*) necessary, if it is necessary in itself (*interne*); but we have no concept of the inner necessity of a thing, |\nbecause positing and canceling in itself are equally possible; therefore absolute necessity as a necessity related to everything in general must be grounded on a presupposition.\nEither something is necessary under [all condi] a condition (*restrictive*) or in some application (*application*) *extensive*. Under all conditions nothing is necessary; for, if it already has a condition, then it is not necessary under the opposite.\nAbsolute necessity is the necessity of a presupposition (*hypothesis originaria*) with regard to everything thinkable. Namely the *data* for all *determination*, the all-sum-total of reality. This relates to the whole of sensibility [as well] and of the senses as well in accordance with form as with matter. Whoever would have insight into himself entirely with regard to all his sensitivity and form, so would [would] we be able to determine beforehand everything that can only be an object for us.\nThe *hypothesis originaria* is not the necessity under a *hypothesis*, but the necessity as hypothesis.\n===NOTES===\n- *setzen* = posit; *aufheben* = cancel; *Einsicht/einsehen* = insight / have insight into; *Inbegriff* = sum total (*Allinnbegrif* = all-sum-total).\n- *Empfindsamkeit* = sensitivity (distinct from *Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility and *Empfindung* = sensation).\n- *Wer ... einsehen würde, so würden wir*: the German anacoluthon is kept; the doubled *würden [würde]* is the editors' bracket.\n- *so wohl ... so wohl* kept as 'as well ... as well'; *obiect* = object.\n- The bracketed fragments [all condi] and [as well] are deletions, translated inside the brackets.",[]],"reflm:5263":["[What contradicts itself is not in conformity with the conditions of understanding.]\nHow the conflict of the subjective conditions or their presupposition may imitate and substitute the truth of the objective ones. e.g. A mathematically infinite is possible, because it does not conflict with the rules of insight; it is impossible, because it conflicts with the conditions of *comprehension*. Everything has its ground, because without this the being of the thing cannot be seen into; Something has further no ground above itself, because it would otherwise conflict with the conditions of comprehending. There is no freedom (transcendental). There is |\none. There is an absolutely necessary being, because without this no comprehension. There is no etc etc. being, because the same conflicts with insight. If, however, these concepts are taken only according to the conditions of *apprehension*, then they are restricted to the objects of the senses and also have only a restricted meaning. e.g. Everything that happens has in a certain order a first ground, or rather the *axiomata* are not transcendent, but hold only as *anticipationes*. So that they hold only of the *data* of the senses, insofar as they can be understood. e.g. What happens has a ground, because without a rule according to which it is given according to laws of sensibility, one would not think an object through the representation.\nThe *principium contradictionis* contains the *conditiones* of thinking in general. The *anticipationes*, which affirm the *conditiones* of apprehension of the concepts of the understanding (e.g. In every substance there is *aliqvid perdurabile*, or a substance always lasts), contain the conditions (g *postulata*) of understanding and are therefore with regard to the sensible *conditiones* always true.\n(To understand something under its determinations (representations) i.e.: to think or posit an object that corresponds to them.)\nApprehension without the *conditiones* under which something can only be given is a function, but yet no concept, or rather *potential*. To understand something is: to cognize, when it is previously given; but to see into: to cognize *a priori*; to understand logically is: to cognize something distinctly.\n===NOTES===\n- *Verstehen* = understand; *Verstand* = understanding; *einsehen/Einsicht* = see into / insight; *begreifen/Begreifung* = comprehend / comprehension; *erkennen* = cognize; *deutlich* = distinct.\n- *Gegenstand* and *Obiect* both = object.\n- *aliqvid perdurabile* is left untranslated (roughly 'something lasting').\n- The first sentence (*Wie der Wiederstreit ... unterschiebe*) is a fragment with no finite verb; *nachahme und unterschiebe* are subjunctive forms, rendered 'may imitate and substitute'. The reading is doubtful.\n- *(g* is the editors' sign; *potential* is Kant's Latinate word, kept italic.\n- The opening line in brackets is a deleted passage.",[]],"reflm:5264":["If I cancel the light, then I retain space with the negative predicate; if I cancel cognition, then I retain the subject, if I cancel a part from the whole or a sequel from a cause, then I retain the universe or the cause. If I cancel everything, however, and retain nothing, then this is an empty thought, because no object is left over of which something might be predicated. Accordingly the |\n[Emptiness] transcendental emptiness is nothing at all and also cannot be thought.\n===NOTES===\n- *aufheben* = cancel; *Folge* = sequel; *Erkentnis* = cognition; *Gegenstand* = object.\n- *Leere* in brackets is a deletion, translated inside the brackets; the sentence breaks across the | line break.",[]],"reflm:5265":["Another All of things is absolutely impossible, another world is indeed possible in itself, but hypothetically impossible.\n===NOTES===\n- *All* = All (kept capitalized as in the German); *an sich selbst* = in itself; *Welt* = world.",[]],"reflm:5266":["At the very top on the upper margin and crosswise on the inner margin:\nOnly the contradictory opposite of the determinations (namely *omni respectu*) proves contingency; now, however, the *opposita determinatio* at another time is not the *contradictorie oppositum* and thus does not prove contingency. The change of determinations proves still much less the change of things, thus also not the contingency of the subject. There is no transition from the *principiis* of appearance to the concepts of reason, thus also not from alteration to contingency.\n===NOTES===\n- *Wechsel* = change; *Veränderung* = alteration (two German words, two English words).\n- *Sachen* and *Dinge* would both be 'things'; here *Sachen* is rendered 'things'.\n- *Zufälligkeit* = contingency; *Bestimmung* = determination; *Erscheinung* = appearance.",[]],"reflm:5267":["The successive opposite is the counterplay.\n===NOTES===\n- *Das succedirende Gegentheil* = 'the successive opposite'; *Wiederspiel* is rendered literally as 'counterplay'.",[]],"reflm:5268":["The alterable is to this extent not determined through concepts. Thus logically contingent, not with regard to existence.\n===NOTES===\n- *Veränderliche* = alterable (cf. *Veränderung* = alteration); *zufällig* = contingent; *Daseyn* = existence.",[]],"reflm:5269":["Above, beside and\nIf I think a thing *partim reale, partim negativum*, then I think it first of all [as real] thoroughly as real and separate off some reality from it; for *negare est realitatem tollere*. Consequently, if I think it in its thoroughgoing determination, I must lay the concept of the *realissimum* at the ground.\nThat *ens realissimum* is logically possible is no doubt; whether it is *realiter* possible, 1. so that all reality exists in it as subject, 2. or through it as ground, or 3. of it as part of a whole? Of this it can only be cognized that the latter cannot be *ens realissimum*.\n===NOTES===\n- *durchgängig* = thoroughgoing; *Grund* = ground; *Theil* = part; *Gantzes* = whole.\n- *von ihm als Theil*: the preposition *von* is ambiguous ('of' / 'from'); 'of' kept.\n- The Latin is left untranslated in the text (roughly 'partly real, partly negative'; 'to negate is to remove reality'; 'really').\n- *[als real]* is a deletion, translated inside the brackets.",[]],"reflm:5270":["42'. E II 1623.\nThe principle of thoroughgoing determination: *qvodlibet existens est omnimode determinatum, i.e. ens qvodlibet per se non nisi ut omnimode determinatum dari potest, sed per conceptum de ipso multimode potest esse indeterminatum*.\nThat proposition has the sum total of all possible predicates with their *oppositis* and, since to existence belongs merely reality, the sum total of all realities with their *oppositis* in thought; and, since every thing that does not contain all reality always presupposes an other that contains it: so it also has the concept of a thing that contains all reality, as of an *entis logice originarii*, before its eyes, whose reality or the sequels of the same give all things through limitation and in relation to which [ein D] every thing can alone be determined in distinction from others.\nIf I represent to myself the understanding that thinks reality as light and, insofar as it cancels it, as darkness: then one can think the thoroughgoing determination either as a carrying-in of light here and there into the darkness or the darkness as mere limitation of the general light, and thus things differ only merely through the shadows, reality lies at the ground and indeed only a single general one. In the opposite case all things differ only through their light, as if they were originally raised out of the darkness. I can, however, well think a negation if I have reality, but not if no reality is given. Thus reality is *logice* the former, and from this it is concluded that it is also *metaphysice* and objectively the first. |\nM 42': |\nBecause objects of the senses are not given through the understanding (g and in general not *a priori*), so here the negation is the first and the darkness, out of which the light of experience works out shapes. Thus in accordance with appearances [is] are manifolds original, and the unity arises when one abstracts from the manifoldness.\n===NOTES===\n- *Satz* = proposition (*Jener Satz*); *Folgen* = sequels; *Dunkelheit* and *Finsternis* both = darkness; *Gegenstand* and *obiectiv* = object / objectively.\n- [ein D] is an abandoned fragment (the start of *Ding*?); left in German. Reading doubtful.\n- *Sind Manigfaltige Ursprünglich*: *Manigfaltige* taken as a plural noun; 'manifolds'. The reading is doubtful.\n- The Latin is left untranslated in the text; the principle says, roughly, that whatever exists is determined in every way. *E II 1623*, *M 42'* and *(g* are the editors' marks.\n- *Einheit* = unity; *Erfahrung* = experience; *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Realitaet* = reality; *limitation* is Kant's Latinate word.",[]],"reflm:5271":["In and\nThe *principium omnimodae determinationis* is: Every thing stands, with regard to its entire possibility, under the concept of the *ens realissimum*.\n===NOTES===\n- *Begriff* = concept; *Moglichkeit* = possibility. The heading is the editors' locus note.",[]],"reflm:5272":["One cannot say: every thing is either thoroughgoingly determined or not, but: it is such at all times, but only not [through the] always through the concept of it. except the *ens realissimum*.\n===NOTES===\n- *durchgängig determinirt* = thoroughgoingly determined; *Begrif* = concept.\n- *[durch de]* is a broken-off deletion, rendered '[through the]'. The final sentence fragment is kept as it stands, with its lower-case *ausser*.",[]],"reflm:5273":["The metaphysical doctrine of the *genus summum*, *species* etc etc. and of thoroughgoing determination is properly the doctrine of the *ens realissimum*, as the sum total of all determination of things, the *substratum* of the All to some and one.\n===NOTES===\n- *Lehre* = doctrine; *Inbegriff* = sum total; *Bestimung* = determination.\n- *vom All zu einigem und einem*: the phrase is obscure and has been kept literal.",[]],"reflm:5274":["The *principium determinationis* says that every thing with regard to one of the *praedicatis contradictorie oppositis* is determined at all times.\nThe *principium omnimodae determinationis* says that it is determined with regard to all together.\nThe principle is. All negations in things are limitations. Consequently the *ens a priori omnimode determinatum* is the *ens realissimum* and lies at the ground of our concepts of the real possibility of things just as much as the *principium contradictionis* of the logical. That is the material principle, and all negations are only the formal of things considered transcendentally.\n===NOTES===\n- *Grundsatz* and *princip* both = principle.\n- *Das Formale* = 'the formal'; *materiale* = 'material'; *limitationen* = limitations.\n- *Der Grundsatz ist.* is kept with its full stop as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5275":["Magnitude is a relation of comparison [from], the three remaining of connection; the logical relation is also [the] either of comparison: identity and diversity, or of connection: universal and particular.\n===NOTES===\n- Die Große = \"magnitude\" (Größe); Verhältnis = \"relation\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Vergleichung = \"comparison\".\n- [aus] translated as \"[from]\"; [der] as \"[the]\". Both are editorial deletions or insertions, kept in place.\n- The syntax of \"das logische Verhältnis ist auch [der] Entweder …\" is awkward and is kept so.",[]],"reflm:5276":["*Qvantum et mensura* (quantum and measure) (*Multitudo, unitas*) (multitude, unity).\n*Compositum et simplex* (composite and simple).\n*Totum et pars* (whole and part).\n*Substantia et accidens* (substance and accident).\n===NOTES===\n- All Latin in the original; English glosses added beside it. The spelling *Qvantum* is Kant's.",[]],"reflm:5277":["Mathematics is the science of the construction of concepts; thus it goes only to intuition as such and has only empirical use.\nPhilosophy goes only to concepts of being in general, hence to that which corresponds to sensation, and can therefore not make its concepts intuitive. For just that reason it also goes to objects independent of the conditions of intuition.\n===NOTES===\n- Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Gebrauch = \"use\"; Empfindung = \"sensation\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\" (Gegenstand).\n- Seyn überhaupt = \"being in general\"; Seyn is not Wesen, so \"being\" here is a different German word from the glossary's Wesen.\n- \"anschauend machen\" = \"make intuitive\".",[]],"reflm:5278":["In and \nEverything real exists either merely as determination of another (the way to be of another) or also merely as subject. That existence is inherence — In our state — this, subsistence.\n===NOTES===\n- The heading \"In und \" breaks off after \"und\"; the text is evidently cut off or has a gap, left as is.\n- existirt/existentz = \"exist/existence\" (Existenz); Dasein is not used here. Zustand = \"state\"; Art zu seyn = \"way to be\".\n- inhaerentz and subsistentz are given as \"inherence\" and \"subsistence\" (Latinate forms in German spelling).",[]],"reflm:5279":["In and \nNexus of sustentation.\nNexus of subordination: of the series.\nNexus of coordination: aggregates.\n===NOTES===\n- Heading \"In und \" breaks off, as in the preceding passage.\n- Nexus, sustentation, subordination, coordination are Latinate words in Kant's German text, so they are left in the same form.",[]],"reflm:5280":["Dependence and adherence, [necessity] necessity and independence.\nThe *existentia rationati* (existence of the grounded) is *dependentia*, of the *praedicatum*: *inhaerentia*.\n*Qvod non existit nisi pars alterius, est limitatum* (*ens mundanum*); *qvod existit, etiamsi non sit pars alterius, est ens realissimum, mundus* (*omnitudo, vniversitas*). (What does not exist except as part of another is limited (worldly being); what exists even if it is not part of another is the most real being, world (all-ness, universe).)\n===NOTES===\n- Abhängigkeit = \"dependence\"; Anhängigkeit = \"adherence\" (the two German words differ; the English cannot show the sound-play). Selbständigkeit = \"independence\".\n- \"praedicati\" in the Latin is read as the genitive attached to \"inhaerentia\" in a parallel construction; the elliptical syntax is kept.\n- [Nothwendigkeit] is a deleted word; kept as \"[necessity]\".",[]],"reflm:5281":["Above and \nThe first of things is the absolutely necessary.\nThe first of states is the absolutely contingent.\nNecessity and freedom incomprehensible, because the former breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- \"Über und \" is a locus heading that breaks off after \"und\"; \"Über\" rendered \"Above\" (the sense of the placement is not settled).\n- schlechterdings = \"absolutely\"; zufällig = \"contingent\"; unbegreiflich = \"incomprehensible\" (begreifen).\n- \"ienes\" = \"the former\" is ambiguous in the German between the two named items only through word order; \"former\" is the literal reading.",[]],"reflm:5282":["In and \n1. *accidens inhaeret subiecto* (the accident inheres in the subject). 2. subject is *causa* (cause). 3. *accidentia faciunt totum* (accidents make the whole).\n===NOTES===\n- Heading \"In und \" breaks off.\n- Latin kept; English glosses added in parentheses.",[]],"reflm:5283":["Predicates are either determinations or *accidentia*. The |\nmagnitude is no *accidens*. Of intellectual relations, e.g. *substantia*, ground, whole.\nWhat is in space or time is its *accidens*. — *antecedens*, *consequens*, *Spatium*.\n===NOTES===\n- Praedicate = \"predicates\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; Grund = \"ground\"; Raum/Zeit = \"space/time\".\n- The sign | falls inside the sentence (page or line break) and is kept; \"Die | Größe\" is split across it.\n- \"dessen\" in \"dessen accidens\" is ambiguous in its referent; kept as \"its\".",[]],"reflm:5284":["Categories of the real subject, ground and whole, in distinction from the logical ones. They are real relations.\n===NOTES===\n- Verhältnisse = \"relations\"; Realverhältnisse = \"real relations\"; Grund = \"ground\".\n- \"zum unterschiede der logischen\" is elliptical (\"categories\" is understood); kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:5285":["Because our understanding cannot think otherwise than through judgments, we also can have no concepts of things except through predicates, [which] which be combined with something constant as characteristics of the subject. Hence the concept substance [otherwise] and accident otherwise has no significance.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstand = \"understanding\"; Urtheile = \"judgments\"; Begriffe = \"concepts\"; Bedeutung = \"significance\"; Verbunden = \"combined\" (Verbindung).\n- \"Kennzeichen\" is not in the glossary; rendered \"characteristics\". \"bestandig\" = \"constant\" (not \"persistent\", which is for beharren).\n- \"welche welche\" is a repetition after the deletion, kept. \"seyn\" is an infinitive in the German (\"be combined\"), which makes the clause obscure; kept.\n- Syntax of the last sentence is unclear; \"sonst\" appears twice (once deleted).",[]],"reflm:5286":["The existence of something in a *substratum*.\nThe existence of something through a *principium* in a series.\nThe existence of something in a *compositum*. There is not inherence there, but still reciprocal connection.\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn = \"existence\" (Dasein); Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; wechselseitige = \"reciprocal\".\n- *substratum*, *principium*, *compositum* are Latin in the text.",[]],"reflm:5287":["With substance combined, time gives persistence or alteration in acting or suffering. If the substance is altered, that signifies its inner state.\nThat inheres which pertains to another as a predicate and has the ground of existence in it.\nSubstances are things that do not inhere; *accidentia* however are again determinations in which nothing inheres. They all inhere in the same subject. Unity.\n===NOTES===\n- Beharrlichkeit = \"persistence\"; Veränderung = \"alteration\" (verändert = \"altered\"), distinct from wechseln = \"change\"; Bedeuten = \"signify\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\".\n- \"Mit der substantz verbunden die Zeit\" has no finite verb before \"Giebt\"; the order is kept, so the construction is ambiguous. \"in ihm\" could refer to \"another\" or to the predicate; kept as \"it\".\n- \"inhäriren\" rendered \"inhere\"; \"Daseyn\" = \"existence\".",[]],"reflm:5288":["*entelechia*, the first proper *substratum* or the ground of all appearances to be cognized through the pure understanding. The rest is *substantia phaenomenon*.\n===NOTES===\n- Erscheinungen = \"appearances\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; reinen Verstand = \"pure understanding\"; Grund = \"ground\".\n- \"eigenthümliche\" rendered \"proper\".",[]],"reflm:5289":["At the bottom of the page, \n1. *Substratum* (g inherence). 2. *Principium Caussalitatis* (g consequence). a. *ratio realis. sive synthetica*. *Caussalitas substantiae qvoad accidentia est Vis. primitiva, derivativa. Actio, passio, Influxus*. 3. *Commercium. Reciproca actio*. *Triplex Vnitas, cuius functiones sunt a priori, sed non nisi a posteriori dari possunt (construi)*. *Substantia, Ratio, Compositum. Phaenomena*. Time is constant, while [determinations] appearances change.\n(Latin: principle of causality; real ground or synthetic; the causality of substance with regard to accidents is force: primitive, derivative; action, passion, influx; commerce, reciprocal action; threefold unity, whose functions are *a priori* but can be given (constructed) only *a posteriori*; substance, ground, composite; phenomena.)\n===NOTES===\n- \"Zuunterst auf der Seite\" = \"At the bottom of the page\" (Seite could also be \"side\"). The comma at the end is as in the German.\n- \"g\" is the Academy's sign, kept as in the source. Erscheinungen = \"appearances\"; Zeit = \"time\"; bestandig = \"constant\".\n- The Latin *Ratio* is left in Latin (translatable as \"ground\"); *Phaenomena* kept.\n- [Bestimungen] is a deleted word, kept in brackets.",[]],"reflm:5290":["Above and \nThe relation of a substance to the accident is merely *actio*. *Vis*. That of substances among one another can be *actio* and *passio*; if it is *mutua*, then it is *commercium*.\nSince we know a thing only through its predicates, we cannot know the subject by itself alone.\n===NOTES===\n- Heading \"Über und \" breaks off after \"und\".\n- Kennen = \"know\"; Ding = \"thing\"; Prädicate = \"predicates\".\n- \"Die der Substanzen\" = \"That of substances\", referring back to \"relation\"; the ellipsis is kept.",[]],"reflm:5291":["Above and \nThe substance is only alterable and does not change. It is altered in that the state changes; i.e. it persists.\n===NOTES===\n- Heading \"Über und \" breaks off.\n- veränderlich/verändert = \"alterable/altered\"; wechselt = \"change(s)\"; beharrt = \"persists\"; Zustand = \"state\".\n- \"Sie wird verändert\" is passive; kept.",[]],"reflm:5292":["2. Substance as cause, 3. As part of a whole in community.\nThe substantial is the thing in itself and unknown.\n===NOTES===\n- The numbering begins at 2; item 1 is not in the passage (fragment).\n- Ursach = \"cause\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Ding an sich selbst = \"thing in itself\"; unbekant = \"unknown\" (kennen).",[]],"reflm:5293":["The real relations are opposed to the ideal ones; these are only relations of representations, not of objects. The ideal ones are aesthetic or logical.\n===NOTES===\n- Realverhältniße = \"real relations\"; Vorstellungen = \"representations\"; obiecte = \"objects\" (Object).\n- \"ideal\" and \"real\" are given as in the German; \"die idealen\" is a noun-adjective with \"relations\" understood.",[]],"reflm:5294":["Bodies are *substantiae comparativae*, *substrata phaenomenorum*. It is laughable to want to think the soul as bodily; for we have the concept of substance only from the soul, and the one of body we form after it. The transcendental concepts must not overstep the boundaries of the *intellectualia* and make the *sensitivum* in the same understanding into the intellectual. e.g. substance as *noumenon* or *phaenomenon*; consequently not the proposition: bodies are divisible.\nNecessity cannot occur in experience, likewise substance; hence the intellectual concept is not valid in its entire purity of the sensible.\n===NOTES===\n- Grentzen = \"boundaries\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; gilt = \"is valid\" (gültig); sinnlichen = \"sensible\".\n- \"in demselben Verstande\" rendered \"in the same understanding\"; Verstand could also be read as \"sense\" (meaning) in this idiom.\n- \"von dem sinnlichen\" is ambiguous (\"of the sensible\" / \"from the sensible\"); kept as \"of\". \"der Satz\" is in the nominative though the construction is elliptical.",[]],"reflm:5295":["At the bottom of the page:\nWhat absolutely (in every aim) (g in itself, not logically) is subject, the last subject, which does not further presuppose another subject as predicate, is substance. That predicate for which not |\nfurther another universal concept can be given as subject, is that through which the substance is represented immediately as a something to which this predicate adheres.\nThe mind intuits the substance. [The sustentation] Of outer things only the actions, from which substance is inferred, because they are predicates.\n===NOTES===\n- Absicht = \"aim\"; schlechthin = \"absolutely\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; schaut an = \"intuits\" (Anschauung); geschlossen = \"inferred\" (Schluß).\n- \"was nicht weiter als praedicat ein ander subiect voraussetzt\": \"further\" may go with \"presuppose\" or with \"as predicate\"; ambiguity kept.\n- The sign | falls between \"nicht\" and \"weiter\"; \"g\" is the Academy sign.",[]],"reflm:5296":["At the top of the page:\nThe sustentation seems to designate a relation of one thing to the other, and inherence is nothing further than the representation of a real predicate, i.e. of an action of the substance, among which the ground-action designates the substance.\n===NOTES===\n- Zuoberst = \"at the top\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Grundhandlung = \"ground-action\" (Grund = \"ground\").\n- \"unter denen\" is plural and has no clear plural antecedent (\"actions\" is singular); kept as \"among which\".",[]],"reflm:5297":["Between substance and accident the logical relation is synthetic. The subject is itself predicate (g for one can think everything only through predicates, except I), but it is called a subject only because it is no further predicate: 1, because no subject is thought for it; 2, because it is the presupposition and *substratum* of the others. This latter can only be inferred from the continuance, while the other changes. Thus it belongs to the essence of a substance that [it] it be *perdurabel*. If one assumes that the substance ceased, then this ceasing proves that it is no substance, and since for this appearance no *substratum* is thought, there are predicates without subjects, thus no judgments and no thoughts.\n===NOTES===\n- synthetisch = \"synthetic\"; Wesen = \"essence\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Urtheile = \"judgments\"; Fortdauer = \"continuance\"; Voraussetzung = \"presupposition\".\n- \"Ich\" = \"I\". \"das andre wechselt\" may refer to the other substrata or to the predicates; ambiguity kept.\n- *perdurabel* is a Latinate word in the German text; kept in italics. \"[es] sie\" are deleted and inserted pronouns, kept.",[]],"reflm:5298":["All concepts are predicates, and these are substance or accident |\nor relation. Space and time are neither of the two, thus not at all predicates of objects in themselves. Space and time are intuitions *a priori*. For from their concepts the propositions [not synthet] (synthetic) cannot be derived, which we have of them *a priori*. How are intuitions *a priori* possible? Not otherwise than that the form of intuiting something through senses, without matter, i.e. a given object of the senses, can be represented for itself. Thus space and time are forms of sensible intuition. Thus we can cognize much *a priori* of space and time and of the objects in them, i.e. as objects of the senses, which therefore just does not hold of the same objects as things in themselves.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriffe = \"concepts\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Sinne = \"senses\"; Gegenstände and obiecte both = \"objects\" (Gegenstand / Object); Dingen an sich selbst = \"things in themselves\"; sinnlich = \"sensible\".\n- \"Sätze … herleiten\": the propositions are the object of \"derived\"; \"nicht synthet\" is a deletion, \"(synthetische)\" an original parenthesis.\n- \"Nicht anders, als daß …\" is elliptical; kept. \"gilt\" = \"holds\" (gültig); \"eben nicht\" = \"just does not\" (could attach to \"gilt\" or the whole clause).",[]],"reflm:5299":["A whole is either of derivation or of composition. The latter is that in which one in a certain state is not without another in a similar one also being. Thus many make up a real unity. e.g. If a part of a body cannot be moved without the other being moved along. Everything here, with substances, depends on the reciprocal influences of the alterations.\nComposition is the contingent unity of the many. Hence not every whole is composed. e.g. space, because here unity precedes plurality or plurality presupposes unity in order to be thought in it. This unity consists in connection either with a third or among one another. This composition is either ideal or real; the first through taking together, the second through connection.\nA whole is thus either of continuation or of composition; the former is *qvantum continuum*, the second *discretum*. The former has contingent plurality in One, the second contingent unity in many.\nSomething through which many is posited is therefore no whole, but that which consists of many (each of which without the thing itself, and the thing itself only through each of them, subsists). God is thus no whole.\n===NOTES===\n- Ganzes = \"whole\"; Zusammensetzung = \"composition\"; Ableitung = \"derivation\"; Fortsetzung = \"continuation\"; Einheit = \"unity\"; Vielheit = \"plurality\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Zustand = \"state\"; Theil = \"part\"; gesetzt = \"posited\" (setzen).\n- \"einem … ein anderes\": \"one\" and \"another\" are neuter and have no stated noun; kept.\n- \"Zusammennehmung\" = \"taking together\" (a verbal noun).\n- The parenthesis \"deren jedes ohne das Ding selber …\" is obscure in word order; the German order is followed (the verb \"besteht\" is last).\n- \"Etwas, wodurch vieles Gesetzt wird\" – \"Gesetzt\" capitalized in the German; read as \"posited\".",[]],"reflm:5300":["In and \nThe simple of separation, what is not composed of several of different species, e.g. water.\n===NOTES===\n- Heading \"In und \" breaks off.\n- Scheidung = \"separation\"; Einfache = \"simple\"; species is Latin in the German text, kept as \"species\" (Art would be \"kind\").\n- \"Das Einfache der Scheidung\" is a genitive construction that could be read as \"of separation\" in the sense of what is attained by separation; kept literal.",[]],"reflm:5301":["*Coniunctio est vel compositionis merae et est idealis, vel inhaerentiae et est realis.* (Conjunction is either of mere composition and is ideal, or of inherence and is real.)\n===NOTES===\n- All Latin; English gloss added in parentheses. *merae* is the genitive of *mera*, agreeing with *compositionis*.",[]],"reflm:5302":["Something is simple either according to quantity or also to quality: *compositum similare*; the first is, *qvod non constat partibus ullis* (which does not consist of any parts); the second, *qvod non constat heterogeneis* (which does not consist of heterogeneous things).\n===NOTES===\n- qvantitaet / qvalitaet = \"quantity / quality\"; Einfach = \"simple\".\n- \"compositum similare\" is placed after the colon and may belong to the second member (quality) or be an example for both; ambiguity kept.",[]],"reflm:5303":["On the beginning of § 224 »Ens compositum est«:\n*Complexus* (g *plurium*) *se invicem realiter determinantium*. *unum plurium aggregatorum est complexus*. [*Complexus aggregatorum*] *Si unitas* [*aggrega*] *complexus consistit in identitate complexus, est* [*totum*] *qvantum*. *qvantum est compositum ideale*.\n(A complex (g of several) of those really determining one another. One of several aggregated is a complex. [A complex of aggregates] If the unity of the complex consists in identity of the complex, it is [a whole] a quantum. A quantum is an ideal composite.)\n===NOTES===\n- \"Zum Anfang von § 224\" rendered \"On the beginning of § 224\"; the lemma is kept in the original Latin.\n- The Latin of the second sentence is elliptical (\"unum plurium aggregatorum\") and its construction is doubtful; the parenthetical gloss is one possible reading.\n- \"g\" is the Academy sign; bracketed Latin words are deletions, \"[aggrega]\" a broken-off word.",[]],"reflm:5304":["*Complexus plurium, quatenus per solam compositionem est cogitabilis, dicitur compositum, et hoc praecedit simplex, si dicatur in se non nisi per compositionem possibile.*\n(A complex of several, insofar as it is thinkable only through composition alone, is called composite, and this precedes the simple, if it is said to be possible in itself only through composition.)\n===NOTES===\n- All Latin; English gloss in parentheses. \"hoc praecedit simplex\" may be read with *simplex* as object (\"precedes the simple\"), as translated; *si dicatur* has no stated subject (*hoc* or *simplex*).",[]],"reflm:5305":["Ideal *compositum* is that whose parts, without belonging to the whole, are impossible for themselves.\n===NOTES===\n- Theile = \"parts\"; Ganze = \"whole\"; unmoglich = \"impossible\" (möglich = \"possible\").\n- \"für sich\" = \"for themselves\" (could also be \"by themselves\"); kept literal. \"Compositum ideale\" is Latin in the German; given as *compositum* with \"Ideal\" in English.",[]],"reflm:5306":["The division of the inseparable is the division and is *compositum ideale* (ideal composite).\n===NOTES===\n- Theilung and Eintheilung are both rendered 'division' (Einteilung / Teilung = division).",[]],"reflm:5307":["That composition which is to contain the ground of the possibility of the relation is ideal.\n===NOTES===\n- Relation is the Latinate word in the German, kept as 'relation'.",[]],"reflm:5308":["A whole is either quantitative or qualitative.* The latter is a *compositum* or *quantum discretum*. *Partes qualitativae* are called members. They are distinguished from the whole in accordance with quality. E.g. rainbow and bow simply.\n*(g The relation of a whole of substances is *commercium*.)\n===NOTES===\n- Glieder = 'members'; Ganzes = 'whole'; schlechthin = 'simply'.\n- The Latin is spelled 'qv-' in the manuscript; normalized here.\n- '(g' is the Academy's marker, kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:5309":["Magnitude*, whole, composite: how are they distinguished?\n*(g What contains the same many times.)\nThe *correlatum* of magnitude is unity.\nThe *oppositum* of magnitude is the simple, absolute unity.\nThe *correlatum* of ground is the sequel, the *oppositum* is *sterile*.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; Einheit = unity; einfach = simple; Folge = sequel.\n- *sterile* is Kant's Latin and stands as written.",[]],"reflm:5310":["Every whole is divisible or has parts that can be distinguished, but is not on that account composite; for sometimes the parts are possible only through division.\n===NOTES===\n- Theilbar = 'divisible'; Zusammengesetzt = 'composite'; Theilung = 'division'.",[]],"reflm:5311":["Every body can be considered as composite only after the division. If one wants to cancel all composition, all division must first be thought as completed.\n===NOTES===\n- Eintheilung = 'division'; Zusammensetzung = 'composition'.\n- 'allererst' is rendered 'only' in 'only after'.",[]],"reflm:5312":["In and \nA *phaenomenon* that is a *substratum* of other *phaenomena* is therefore not substance except only comparatively. In the appearances we cannot cognize anything as substance (this is only a concept of apperception), but rather something appears only as the *substratum* of the appearance, to which everything in the appearance is ascribed.\n===NOTES===\n- 'In and' is the incomplete heading as printed.\n- Erscheinung = appearance; erkennen = cognize; beylegen = 'ascribe'.\n- 'darum nicht substantz als nur comparative' is elliptical and kept so.",[]],"reflm:5313":["That space and time are intuitions without things signifies that they must be not objective representations, but subjective ones.\n===NOTES===\n- Anschauung = intuition; bedeuten = signify; Vorstellung = representation.\n- 'Das' in the German is probably 'Daß', and 'seyn' appears both as indicative and as subjunctive; the English leaves this as is.",[]],"reflm:5314":["The possibility of a *compositum noumenon* cannot be simply assumed; for we would have to think up community for this.\n===NOTES===\n- Gemeinschaft = community; schlechthin = 'simply'.\n- 'compositi noumeni' is in the genitive and is rendered with 'of a'.",[]],"reflm:5315":["Space is the form of all relations, which before all intuition of outer things precedes.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Vor ... vorhergeht' is redundant in the German and kept so.\n- Verhältnisse = 'relations'.",[]],"reflm:5316":["That of which the composition must be thought sooner than the parts is ideal.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Wovon' is kept as the German relative; the construction is awkward in English and left so.",[]],"reflm:5317":["Time is the form of consciousness, i.e. the condition under which alone we become conscious of things.\n===NOTES===\n- Bewustseyn = consciousness; Bedingung = condition; 'nur' = 'alone'.",[]],"reflm:5318":["Space is a mere possibility, but in actual things something actual.\n===NOTES===\n- Wirklich = 'actual'; the sentence is elliptical, with no verb in the second clause.",[]],"reflm:5319":["Because inner sense alone is infallible and change is perceived through it, time seems to be something absolute; but it is nonetheless for that reason only the form of inner appearance, and we really have the representation of time, although what lies behind this appearance remains unknown.\n===NOTES===\n- untrüglich = 'infallible'; Veränderung = 'change'; wargenommen = perceived (Wahrnehmung = perception).\n- 'darum doch nur' is rendered 'nonetheless for that reason only'.",[]],"reflm:5320":["Time is actual as form of inner sensibility; thereby it is opposed to the *fictum*. For there are actually changes in us. Bodies are things, even if they were not there. But space is at all times only form.\n===NOTES===\n- Sachen and Dinge are both 'things'; here the German has Sachen.\n- Veränderungen = 'changes'. 'ficto' is in the dative.",[]],"reflm:5321":["Of time we have the running (present), the elapsed, flowed-away (past) and the coming (future) time. Here time flows, the things are standing. But: a thing endures, it has lived through etc etc., presupposes time as at rest. Eternity. Existence either in general or of a thing. A thing covers much time and has much before it. It cannot make fast its existence in time, it goes through it in flight, or time flees.\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn = existence; verlebt is 'has lived through' and is broken off by 'etc etc.', so the syntax is unclear.\n- verlaufene, verflossene = 'elapsed', 'flowed-away'; bevestigen = 'make fast'.",[]],"reflm:5322":["Space goes only to objects of the outer senses, but not to that which we posit only through the properties that are borrowed from the inner senses.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstände = 'objects'; setzen = 'posit'; Eigenschaften = 'properties'.\n- 'geht auf' is kept literally as 'goes to'.",[]],"reflm:5323":["Space and time are not things, nor their predicates, thus nothing objective.\n===NOTES===\n- Dinge = 'things'; obiectiv = 'objective'.",[]],"reflm:5324":["In and \nSpace does not go to Something regardless of all determinations of sense.\nSpace does not go to objects of inner sense, that is: it is no intellectual predicate.\n===NOTES===\n- 'In and' is the incomplete heading as printed.\n- 'unangesehn aller Bestimmungen' is rendered 'regardless of all determinations'; Bestimmung = determination.\n- Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'.",[]],"reflm:5325":["Time the form of inner sense. Space the form of outer. Consequently, what we know only as an object of inner sense is not subject to the condition of the outer appearance.\n===NOTES===\n- kennen = 'know'; obiect = 'object'.\n- 'der condition der äußern Erscheinung' is the dative object of 'unterworfen'.",[]],"reflm:5326":["Whence the propositions of space, time and number can be seen a priori before all experience.\n===NOTES===\n- eingesehen = 'seen'; it is not in the glossary and I have not added 'insight'.\n- Zahl = number; Sätze = propositions.",[]],"reflm:5327":["If the concept of space, as Leibniz opines, were taken from the things, then the propositions about it, as propositions of experience, would have no apodictic certainty.\n===NOTES===\n- Erfahrungssatze = 'propositions of experience'; Gewisheit = 'certainty'.",[]],"reflm:5328":["Physical division is separation of the substance, the mathematical the distinction of the parts.\n===NOTES===\n- Theilung = 'division'; Trennung = 'separation'; Unterscheidung = 'distinction'; Theile = 'parts'.",[]],"reflm:5329":["If space were something objective and necessary, whence would we cognize that. From experience we can derive no judgment that would at the same time be cognized as absolutely necessary, and a priori it is not cognized and not cognizable. The representation would have to be created-in, but then it would also not be necessary. It must therefore be intuited in God.\n===NOTES===\n- anerschaffen = 'created-in', chosen to avoid the interpretive 'innate'.\n- Urtheil = 'judgment'; angeschaut = 'intuited'; erkant = 'cognized'.\n- 'er' in 'a priori ist er' refers to the masculine Raum.",[]],"reflm:5330":["The first subject. The first ground. The first part.\nThe subject that holds everything in itself. The ground that grasps everything under itself. The whole that comprehends everything.\nThe *totalitas absoluta* of reality. Of the series. Of the coordination.\n===NOTES===\n- begreift = 'comprehends'; Grund = 'ground'; Theil = 'part'.\n- *totalitas* is Kant's Latin for Allheit (totality).",[]],"reflm:5331":["The magnitude of unity is degree. The diversity of the magnitude of one and the same object, e.g. light of the very same paper, warmth of the same stove. There it seems not to be the magnitude of the aggregate. Objects have not degrees, but mere determinations of the same reality. Relations have no degrees; but causality, unity has degrees.\nExtension also has a degree, because according to the meaning of the word it arises from nothing and spreads itself out. Space has extensive magnitude.\n===NOTES===\n- Verschiedenheit = 'diversity'; Gegenstände and obiects = 'objects'; Grad = 'degree'; Wortbedeutung = 'meaning of the word'.\n- 'die causalitaet, die Einheit hat grade' is ambiguous: 'unity' may be in apposition to 'causality' or a second subject. I kept the German's lack of a conjunction.\n- Verhältnisse = 'relations'.",[]],"reflm:5332":["The infinite, the greatest, the all-sufficient thing.\n===NOTES===\n- Großeste = 'greatest'; allgnugsame = 'all-sufficient'; the line is a list of terms with no verb.",[]],"reflm:5333":["In and \nThe plurality that is given at the same time, insofar as it is unlimited, cannot at all be cognized determinately; for in limitation the determination consists. The one that is not given at the same time cannot be cognized through limitation, but progression.\n===NOTES===\n- Vielheit = 'plurality', so as not to collide with Menge ('multiplicity'); Beschränkung = 'limitation' (Schranke is also 'limitation').\n- Fortgang = 'progression'; bestimt = 'determinately'.\n- 'Die' is feminine and refers back to Vielheit; the English keeps this by 'The one'.",[]],"reflm:5334":["In and \nNo *quantum* can be given to us as *infinitum*, for it is not given in itself, but only through the *progressus*, which is never given as *infinitus*. But a *progressus in infinitum* can be given; *indefinitum*: that whose boundary we leave undetermined.\n===NOTES===\n- an sich selbst = 'in itself'; Grenze = 'boundary'; unbestimmt = 'undetermined'.\n- Latin technical terms are kept in italics throughout.",[]],"reflm:5335":["Real infinity (*infinitudo realis*) is omnitude (*omnitudo*): that which is not limited (*limitatum*). What lacks ends (*termini*) differs from what lacks limits (*limites*).\n===NOTES===\n- The whole passage is Latin. 'Ends' for *termini* and 'limits' for *limites* are my glosses; the manuscript has the ablatives *terminis*, *limitibus*.\n- The first sentence's *qvod non est limitatum* is rendered as the appositive 'that which is not limited'.",[]],"reflm:5336":["The concept of the *maximum* is relative; that of the *omnitudo* indeed absolute, but undetermined. That of the *infinitum* subjective.\n===NOTES===\n- *maximi*, *omnitudo*, *infiniti* are genitives, so the English supplies 'of' with 'that'.\n- 'subjectiv' is read as the predicate adjective.",[]],"reflm:5337":["The limited (*limitatum*) of sensibility: *non Infinitum* (not infinite).\nThe limited (*limitatum*) of understanding: *non Omnitudo* (not omnitude).\n===NOTES===\n- Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'; Verstand = 'understanding'.",[]],"reflm:5338":["Infinite signifies more than not finite or bounded, but also, taken in the whole, great beyond all measure. Hence I can say: the world is not finite, also not infinite, for it is a *phaenomenon* and no thing. As *phaenomenon* it can have no determinate boundary and can never be taken whole, but one can only progress in it.\n===NOTES===\n- Unendlich = 'infinite'; begrentzt = 'bounded'; Grentze = 'boundary'; Welt = 'world'; Sache = 'thing'.\n- 'im Gantzen genommen' is kept as 'taken in the whole'.",[]],"reflm:5339":["At the very top of the page:\nThe determination of boundaries occurs in the infinite (space and time),\nThe determination of limitations in the infinite thing.\nThe former in an infinite appearance, the latter in an infinite reality.\n===NOTES===\n- Grenzen = 'boundaries'; Schranken = 'limitations'; Bestimmung = 'determination'.\n- 'Zuoberst auf der Seite' is the editor's locus note.",[]],"reflm:5340":["At the very top of the page:\nThe infinity of the world in accordance with magnitude (time and space) makes no difficulty, but rather the prorogation, set out into the infinite, of the *terminus* of the chain of grounds, [because] since the interest of reason requires a complete comprehension.\nBut if one also concedes infinity as possible, there still remains the question whether empty time and space cannot bound the world, i.e. whether an empty time and space be the condition of the possibility of things.\n===NOTES===\n- ausgesetzte is doubtful; 'set out' is literal, and other readings ('postponed') are possible. 'weil da' is kept, with the editor's bracket, as 'because since'.\n- Begreifung = 'comprehension'; Vernunft = 'reason'; Kette = 'chain'.\n- 'Zuoberst auf der Seite' is the editor's locus note.",[]],"reflm:5341":["The possibility of thinking to oneself an empty space and empty time is nothing other than the diminution of reality in both (in the first, of impenetrable extension), which, since it can be diminished into the infinite, passes over into nothing. If I lift out a part of it, which is penetrated by another, a comparatively empty space remains. Space penetrates everything, i.e. something extended is the basis of the possibility of outer references in accordance with relations of space. A completely empty space is a mere idea of decomposition down to nothing, but not of complete cancellation. In space I must be able to distinguish where I am. There must therefore be correlates. The priority of space before the appearances. If an empty space and time (g outside the world) were possible, then an empty space and time in the place of the world would also be possible. I would then have time only in myself and space in the representation of my body; consequently time and space would really not be empty. Entirely empty is without any intuiting being, consequently nothing at all.\n===NOTES===\n- Beziehungen = 'references', Verhältnisse = 'relations'; the distinction is kept for the two words. Stelle is rendered 'place' (as Ort is).\n- Aufhebung = 'cancellation'; Ausdehnung = 'extension'; anschauend wesen = 'intuiting being'.\n- 'Ganz leer ist ohne ...' is syntactically elliptical and is kept so.",[]],"reflm:5342":["Inner diversity is no condition of the concept [of the] distinguished things, but rather the diversity of places. It is also no condition of the appearance; therefore the proposition is false, but for |\nreason true; i.e. I can through reason concede distinguished things there only where the inner determinations are diverse. I.e. a priori I [no] the difference cannot cognize.\n===NOTES===\n- Verschiedenheit = 'diversity'; Unterschied / unterschieden = 'difference' / 'distinguished'.\n- The brackets [des] and [keinen] are the Academy's; the last sentence has a clash between [keinen] and nicht, left unresolved. The | marks a page or line break, and the English keeps the break after it.\n- Örter = 'places'; Satz = 'proposition'.",[]],"reflm:5343":["Opposite the heading »Simultanea«:\nEverything that is, is somewhere and sometime.\n===NOTES===\n- 'irgend wann' = 'sometime'; the Latin lemma is kept in its quotation marks.",[]],"reflm:5344":["That everything in space is mere appearance, namely nothing but relation —, that everything in time (also the inner sense) is appearance, mere representations of outer things and *relationes* [to us] of their representations to one another, us and our feeling.\n===NOTES===\n- 'dem innern Sinn' is in the dative and its connection is uncertain: 'also the inner sense' leaves open 'in' or 'to'.\n- The passage is a dependent clause with no main clause; 'relation' and 'relationes' stand for Latinate forms of Verhältnis.\n- Gefühl = 'feeling'; [zu uns] is in the Academy's brackets.",[]],"reflm:5345":["The *subiect* itself we know only through its relation to the change of place; accordingly we do not know that which is present in the place, and the action that it exerts in different places. At the very least we do not know all this rationally; but the question is whether our thoughts do not still contain the inner of the world.",[]],"reflm:5346":["Matter is the *subiect* to which all outer appearances are related. (The *phaenomenon* to which they relate everything is impenetrability.) That to which everything is related as *subiect* must remain; for without this there is no unity of the accidents. All matter as *substratum* is therefore *constans, stabilis, perdurabilis, permanens*, without which |\nthe *fluxum* and *transitorium* of the states could not be perceived. This proposition is grounded on the possibility of experience as unity of appearances.",[]],"reflm:5347":["Time is a series in which the order of the synthesis is determined, the first, the middle and the last. Three points as boundaries.",[]],"reflm:5348":["Why do we not say (g also) of things in different times (g as of those in different spaces) that they are for that reason different things: because without the sameness of the things in different times these different times themselves could not be cognized as such. The persistence of things lies at the ground of the sequence of time. But that is always possible only in space. This persists; but I can perceive it itself only through things in it (which therefore also persist and in which I cognize it (g as) persistent).",[]],"reflm:5349":["Opposite and \nAll arising and passing away (g of a state as appearance, i.e. everything that happens) happens in time, because between the 0 and the reality there is an infinite series of transition.",[]],"reflm:5350":["A worth: for which another can be given as *aeqvivalent*. Virtue has no price. Dignity is the inner worth, which therefore has no price.",[]],"reflm:5351":["Objective causes are only in the free will.\nHow these can at the same time be subjective, i.e. *causae efficientes* (efficient causes), is not to be explained. *Hypothesis* of freedom.",[]],"reflm:5352":["An action that has its inner worth has no *causa impulsiva* (impelling cause).",[]],"reflm:5353":["Of that which is possible only through a cognition, the original cognition is idea. That thing according to the idea is called archetype; can it not be brought forth: ideal.",[]],"reflm:5354":["The idea.* The archetype, copy, imitation, *Apparenz*.\n*(g A made representation. It is single, because it goes to the completeness, which is only one, but not to the concept of something, insofar as it is given, but can only be thought.)",[]],"reflm:5355":["Outside ontology there is only cosmology and theology.",[]],"reflm:5356":["Metaphysics proper consists of *cosmologia rationali* and *theologia naturali*. The former has two parts: nature and freedom, and their laws *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5357":["At the very bottom of the page, \nThere is only one time and one space, hence only one world. Grounded on the presupposition that everything (at least the finite) is in time and in space, and is an example of a *petitionis sensitivae*.",[]],"reflm:5358":["We do not sense outer substances (only outer effects on us), but we only think them in addition. But only in the relation to the affections of our mind; thus not what they are in themselves, but the *perdurable* in the appearance.",[]],"reflm:5359":["At the very top of the page. \nIf someone wanted to have it that the world should have taken its beginning earlier, he would still have to show under which conditions this would take place |\nand by what one would know it *in concreto*. If some members before Adam were human beings, one would only say: Adam is later, or those are earlier in regard to him. Thus all [all] priority is merely *relatio*. The world can therefore not arise earlier, but only one part earlier than the other. If matter were still to be added to the boundaries of the world, it would be posited not to space, but to the old whole.",[]],"reflm:5360":["Since empty space and empty time are no object of empirical cognitions, neither of experience immediately, nor through any inference, but the infinity of the world is likewise beyond experience, just as those are outside it: so the totality (*absoluta*) of the [world] appearances, i.e. the idea of the world, is a problematic concept. The intellectual possibility of one of these conditions of appearance does not fit here; for they are synthetic propositions, which take place only as conditions of possible experience.",[]],"reflm:5361":["We prove here only that an intellectual series has a first. (g The cause of the series is not in the series, hence no first. Hence the series can be without a first and yet have a cause that is not again in a series.)\nOf the series in appearance we do not speak.",[]],"reflm:5362":["Beside and \nThat no single supposed part of the *universum* lies infinitely far removed from the other, but that in the *universum* a progress according to all kind of magnitudes into the infinite is possible.\nWhere reason is cause, there a first is also to be found: freedom and highest being. But where it only cognizes the connection, there the synthesis is infinite.",[]],"reflm:5363":["Under and \nFreedom (g in the world) and, of a free highest cause, absolute necessity outside the world are dynamical *principia* of reason.",[]],"reflm:5364":["Under and \nEvery series of the contingent rests on a necessary. But this does not signify: it is a first of the series.",[]],"reflm:5365":["The beginning belongs to the series, but not the cause of it. A series of contingent things can be without beginning, i.e. in that [at all] no empty time goes before, but [before each series] all appearances in a series are determined *a parte priori* and *posteriori*, but can nevertheless have a cause.",[]],"reflm:5366":["There is no blind dependence (g blind chain) (series of the dependent without an independent), no blind transition, no blind [generation] arising, no blind necessity.\nBlind is what is not [posited] assumed in conformity with the laws of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:5367":["In the series of appearances there is no first cause; for that is valid only of things in themselves, but there the series of the cause is not subordinated according to time.",[]],"reflm:5368":["To and \nThis will say only: we can think no (g absolute) totality according to empirical synthesis, but must nevertheless think one according to pure intellectual, because everything contingent must have a complete cause, indeed not in itself, yet outside itself. We have no image, but yet a concept. For of the infinite no image is possible, and of the finite in infinite time and space no *substratum* of this image.\nWe cannot think determinably, in empty space and empty time, the place of the beginning of the world. How something could begin absolutely in time, still less how time itself could begin.",[]],"reflm:5369":["In and \nBetween nature and chance there is a third, namely freedom.\nAll appearances are in nature, but the cause of the appearances is not contained in the appearance, hence also not nature. Our understanding is such a cause of the actions of the power of choice, which indeed as appearances are nature, but as a whole of the appearances stand under freedom.",[]],"reflm:5370":["Chance is opposed to the purposive (g to that which human reason can take into account in the case of ends); the fortuitous to that which happens according to constant and known laws.\nWe do not see that without a cause working according to ends nothing purposive can arise and that natural necessity could not just as well bring forth something purposive as absolute necessity: a rational highest being; but because experience teaches that there are laws of blind nature, so [untersch] we see from this the difference of the choosing causes from the physical ones.",[]],"reflm:5371":["That chance [wech] must alternate the occurrence with its opposite. That indeed each time the same occurrence that has happened before is possible, and so all following times, but that the same occurrence is not always possible one after another, if it is to stem from chance. Hence dice, if always the same side comes up, are not held to be right. We also find it probable that, if several times something that is subject to chance has succeeded in a similar way, it is now a greater probability that it will fail, than before. Whence does it come that, when we have had good weather for a long time, we find rain more probable than after long rain. Yet one believes that some human beings are especially lucky, some days of play, certain cards. (g Whoever has often lost in the lottery, e.g. the first class, believes he has more hopes.)\nChance has laws, e.g. shipwrecks.\nSomething is called chance for this reason, because its opposite also happens in just the same (generically identical) circumstances. Hence the opposite in chance must be, *per hypothesin*, all the more necessary, the more often something has happened.",[]],"reflm:5372":["Insofar as an occurrence does not happen under a particular rule of its causes, it is chance. But there is no rule in the causality of the causes if there are many of them that are not determined particularly to this occurrence. E.g. rain, frost, diseases are chances in a military campaign. Nature, chance and aim.\nIn human generation many variations happen through chance. Just so in the luck of the human being. A chance (luck) that is attached to a human being is a non-thing. Even under a divine providence the luck of human beings is left very strongly to chance, i.e. to the influence of many causes that are not attuned to the person and his merit and that the human being cannot bring under a rule.\n===NOTES===\n- Begebenheit = occurrence; Zufall = chance (not in the glossary, used consistently); Glück = luck; Absicht = aim; Ursache = cause.\n- 'caussalitaet' is the manuscript spelling; Unding = non-thing.\n- 'nicht auf diese Begebenheit besonders bestimt': the adverb 'besonders' can attach to 'bestimt' or to 'Begebenheit'; kept as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5373":["(s In the world there is no absolute necessity; but this can be outside it.)\nFate (destiné) and chance (luck and misfortune) are *qualitates occultae* (occult qualities). We need reason for explaining as well as for acting; blind happenstance and fate are opposed to both.\n===NOTES===\n- '(s' is carried over as it stands (editorial sign for a layer of writing).\n- Schicksal = fate; Zufall = chance; Ohngefehr = happenstance; erklären = explain.\n- 'beyden' (both) could refer to explaining and acting; kept.",[]],"reflm:5374":["Principles of the (g speculative) reason about everything that appears.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsatz = principle; Vernunft = reason; '(g' carried over unchanged.\n- 'von allem, was erscheint' can be read 'about' or 'concerning'; 'von' is kept as 'about'.",[]],"reflm:5375":["Everything stands [either] under a rule, either of necessity or of freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- '[either]' is the Academy's bracket, kept in place; the doubled 'entweder' is kept.\n- Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Freyheit = freedom.",[]],"reflm:5376":["Between two states there is a time. In this, if the states are different, the thing is neither in the one nor in the other. Thus either there is between them an empty time (*saltus*) or a time filled with smaller intermediate degrees of the states. Just so there is no transition [out of] among the appearances in space through the empty. Because this would be an appearance without perception.\n===NOTES===\n- Zustand = state; Grad = degree; Übergang = transition; Erscheinung = appearance; Warnehmung = perception.\n- '[aus] unter': the bracketed 'aus' is carried over (rendered '[out of]'); the reading of the bracket is the editors'.\n- *saltus* (leap) is Kant's Latin.\n- 'Weil dieses Erscheinung ohne warnehmung seyn würde': 'dieses' (neuter) with 'Erscheinung' (feminine) is as in the source, and I read it as 'this' and added 'an' for English.",[]],"reflm:5377":["*Mundus non*\\* *est in abysso* (The world is not in an abyss): no beginning, end and boundary of the world.\n*in mundo non datur hiatus* (in the world no gap is given) (g *saltus*. *vacuum*. we fill all space through a kind of sensations.)\n*Nihil accidit per casum. Nihil per fatum.* (Nothing happens through chance. Nothing through fate.)\n\\*(g There is no absolute time or space. Pure intuition signifies here not something that is intuited, but the pure formal condition that goes before the appearance. Absolute time is empty intuition.)\n===NOTES===\n- Asterisk after 'non' and the footnote asterisk are carried over as in the source; English glosses of the Latin in parentheses are mine.\n- Grenze = boundary; Anschauung = intuition; bedeutet = signifies; Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance.\n- 'vor der Erscheinung vorhergeht' = 'goes before the appearance'.",[]],"reflm:5378":["The world is no *interruptum* (interrupted thing), but *continuum*. The removal of matter from space is nothing but the infinite diminution.\n===NOTES===\n- Aufhebung = removal (could also be 'sublation'/'cancellation'); Verminderung = diminution; Materie = matter; Raum = space.",[]],"reflm:5379":["All alteration or transition happens in time through a continuous approach.\n===NOTES===\n- Veränderung = alteration; Übergang = transition; Annäherung = approach; 'continuirlich' = continuous.",[]],"reflm:5380":["The physical continuity takes place: if the substances must ascend from the lowest classes to the generation of the greatest; |\nif a mineral must first be a plant, then an animal, then it goes through all the middle degrees. If animals are properly plants that have gone through a certain degree. Through the joining together no true continuity arises, but through generation from the small degree to the greatest.\n===NOTES===\n- 'continuitaet' = continuity; Gewechse (Gewächse) = plants; Erzeugung = generation; Zusammenfügung = joining together; Grad = degree.\n- '|' carried over at its position.\n- The two 'wenn' clauses, including the second ('Wenn Thiere ...'), are fragmentary in the German and left so.",[]],"reflm:5381":["Whether certain oppositions allow a continuity (A grain and a heap). Where a continuous transition is possible, there the difference is only in magnitude. With that which is no vice, and vice, the difference is in quality. Whether understanding and erudition are distinguished only according to magnitude. The transition from one to the other is not a *continuum*.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; Verstand = understanding; Gelehrsamkeit = erudition; laster = vice; qvalitaet = quality.\n- 'Der übergang von einem zum andern': the referents are not stated in the German and are not supplied.",[]],"reflm:5382":["The proposition of continuity wants only to say: all *diversa* (different things) are *remota* (remote), i.e. they are in connection only *per intermedia* (through intermediates), between which the difference is still smaller. I.e. no difference [verk] is the smallest, because no transition is elementary and is the smallest, thus always has a magnitude. To transition belongs a time, hence an approach to a new state. The smallest difference would be called a *differentiale*; but since there is no smallest, it is called *fluxion*.\nSince there is a determinate number of the creatures between two genera, there is no continuity of forms. But there could be one *in potentia* (in potentiality), if matter organized itself from the mineral to the human being; for these forces lie in a unity, and the differences have a magnitude, between which infinitely smaller ones take place.\n===NOTES===\n- Satz = proposition; Verknüpfung = connection; Gattung = genus; Zahl = number; Einheit = unity; Kräfte = forces; Zustand = state.\n- '[verk]' is a bracketed (deleted or incomplete) fragment, kept as is.\n- 'weil kein Uebergang elementar ist und der kleineste ist': the subject of 'ist' is ambiguous (transition or difference); kept ambiguous.\n- 'Weil der Geschopfe zwischen zwey Gattungen eine bestimmte Zahl ist' is syntactically irregular; rendered freely enough to be read in English.",[]],"reflm:5383":["Were there no continuity in the alteration, then for a time the thing would be unaltered, i.e. at rest.\n===NOTES===\n- 'continuität' = continuity; Veränderung = alteration.\n- 'eine Zeit' could be 'for a time' or 'a time (long)'; kept as 'for a time'.",[]],"reflm:5384":["The immediate combination of the boundaries of one and the same series.\nLaw of the continuum. Quality of magnitudes.\n===NOTES===\n- Verbindung = combination; Grenze = boundary; Gesetz = law; Größe = magnitude; 'continui' is Latin genitive, rendered 'of the continuum'.\n- Both lines are fragments with no verb.",[]],"reflm:5385":["*Lex continui* (law of the continuous) in nature, the continuity of the *species* or affinity, mediate or immediate, is a mere play of wit. The complete manifoldness gives at the same time unity of progression. For then between two different things A and E still others B and C, whose differences are smaller, must be encountered, if all manifoldness is to take place. For similarities are among all appearances, because otherwise no *commercium* would take place.\n===NOTES===\n- Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness (cf. das Mannigfaltige = manifold); Fortgang = progression; Einheit = unity; Ähnlichkeit = similarity; Witzspiel = play of wit.\n- 'Denn Ahnlichkeiten sind unter allen Erscheinungen': 'unter' = 'among'; 'commercium' kept in Latin (cf. Gemeinschaft = community).",[]],"reflm:5386":["121. E II 1463. 1745.\n M 121':\n*Non datur simplex phaenomenon; erit enim pars definita omnis phaenomeni, ergo occuparet partem spatii atqve temporis, qvae iterum est spatium atqve tempus.* (There is no simple phenomenon; for it will be a defined part of every phenomenon, therefore it would occupy a part of space and of time, which in turn is space and time.) |\nM 121: |\n*igitur percurrendo phaenomena a simplici ad simplex non datur transitus nisi per infinita intermedia.* (therefore in running through phenomena from simple to simple there is no transition except through infinitely many intermediates.)\nTwo states as series do follow one another; but not two simple determinations of space and time.\n===NOTES===\n- The locus line, '|' signs and spacing are carried over; the English in parentheses after the Latin is mine.\n- Zustand = state; einfach = simple; Bestimmung = determination; Raum = space; Zeit = time; Reihe = series.\n- 'wohl' in 'folgen wohl auf einander' is rendered 'do'.",[]],"reflm:5387":["All motion in a triangle is interrupted, i.e. the body rests at its apex, and no two different states follow one another immediately, although two [verschi] alterations can follow one another (series).\n===NOTES===\n- Bewegung = motion; Zustand = state; Veränderung = alteration; Reihen = series.\n- '[verschi]' is a broken-off bracketed word, left as is.\n- 'in der Spitze desselben': 'Spitze' = apex, rendered 'its apex'.",[]],"reflm:5388":["All differences of given representations have a magnitude, e.g. of speed, of extension, of the series, and can be determined only through that. But in the object the [groß] difference is not discrete — breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- Vorstellung = representation; Größe = magnitude; Ausdehnung = extension; bestimmen = determine; Obiect (Object) = object.\n- '[groß]' bracketed fragment kept; 'bricht ab' is the editors' or Kant's note that the text breaks off, kept.\n- 'dadurch' (through that) has no clear antecedent; kept.",[]],"reflm:5389":["Continuity. That all magnitude that begins from 0 arises in a time and that every difference has a magnitude, hence none is the smallest. The first is clear from this, because with 0 and a given magnitude, likewise the arising, there is a series, of which 0 and the magnitude are extremes, likewise a degree of progression.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; Grad = degree; Reihe = series; Unterschied = difference; 'progression' = Progression (Latin-derived, cf. Fortgang).\n- 'Das erste' refers to the first of the two 'daß' clauses.\n- 'bey 0 ... imgleichen dem entstehen' is irregular; kept close to the German.",[]],"reflm:5390":["All appearances stand as representations in time and are determined in time. As a part of a whole appearance it cannot be determined in a moment, but in a part of time (be apprehended genetically). A part of time lies between |\ntwo boundaries and thus two moments, is thus itself [bestimbar in der] a time, hence every part of the appearance exponible in time; thus like time itself it does not consist of simple parts.\n===NOTES===\n- Erscheinung = appearance; Vorstellung = representation; Augenblick = moment; Grenze = boundary; einfach = simple; 'exponibel' = exponible (cf. Erörterung = exposition).\n- 'sie' (it) in the second sentence follows the singular 'Erscheinung' in the first, but could be read as 'it' = the part; left ambiguous.\n- '[bestimbar in der]' is bracketed (deleted) text; '|' at the line break.",[]],"reflm:5391":["Two concepts opposed in themselves cannot be distinguished (g merely) according to magnitude. Vice and virtue.\n===NOTES===\n- an sich = in itself (here 'in themselves'); Begriff = concept; Größe = magnitude; '(g' carried over.\n- 'an sich opponirte' could attach to 'opposed' or to the whole phrase; kept as in German.",[]],"reflm:5392":["In and \nEvery alteration in the world is only a continuation of a series already present, and just as much ceases as begins. Therefore everything in the world does alter; but the all of the series does not alter, because things do alter in time, but not time itself.\n===NOTES===\n- Veränderung = alteration; sich verändern = to alter; Reihe = series; Welt = world; Zeit = time.\n- 'In und' at the opening is a fragment and kept as is.\n- 'das All der Reihe' = 'the all of the series' (cf. Allheit = totality).",[]],"reflm:5393":["What appears externally can be united with a *principium repraesentativum* (representative principle), but not as external appearance.\n===NOTES===\n- Erscheinung = appearance; vereinigt = united; 'äußerlich' / 'äußere' = external; the English in parentheses after the Latin is mine.",[]],"reflm:5394":["Idealism is mystical; egoism Spinozistic, if its defense is dogmatic. If it is skeptical, i.e. merely problematic, then egoism is a [pro] docimastic of reason.\n===NOTES===\n- Vernunft = reason; Vertheidigung = defense; 'docimastic' is a transliterated Greek technical word (a testing), kept.\n- '[pro]' bracketed fragment kept; 'sie' = 'its/it' refers to the defense, with 'egoism' (egoismus) as the subject of 'Ist sie'; ambiguity noted.",[]],"reflm:5395":["The idealist asserts: the bodies [werden] are only an illusion; the realist: they are an appearance, to which nevertheless a particular kind of substances really corresponds. The illusion is that behind which nothing true is.\n===NOTES===\n- Schein = illusion; Erscheinung = appearance; behaupten = assert; besonder = particular; Art = kind.\n- '[werden]' bracketed (deleted); 'wirklich' rendered 'really'.\n- 'Der Schein ist, hinter dem nichts wahres ist' is elliptical; 'that' is added for English.",[]],"reflm:5396":["*Compositum substantiale intellectualiter spectatum est qvantum discretum, qvod constat simplicibus; idem ut phaenomenon est qvantum continuum.* (The substantial composite, regarded intellectually, is a discrete quantum, which consists of simples; the same as phenomenon is a continuous quantum.)\nThe *mundus sensibilis* (sensible world) can be weighed by the understanding, but only according to principles of appearances. *Mundus intelligibilis* (intelligible world) is *pnevmaticus*. If I think the *mundum sensibilem* according to concepts of the *intelligibilis*, then it is *mundus mysticus*. *Mundus sensibilis* has substances, forces and effects, but not the absolute of them [and real], but their appearances, place and alteration of the same.\n===NOTES===\n- Verstand = understanding; Grundsatz = principle; Begriff = concept; Erscheinung = appearance; Kraft = force; Wirkung = effect; Ort = place; Veränderung = alteration.\n- 'erwogen' = weighed (considered); '[und reale]' bracketed text kept.\n- The English glosses of the Latin are mine.",[]],"reflm:5397":["*Mundus intelligibilis est monadatum, non secundum formam intuitus externi, sed interni repraesentabile.* (The intelligible world is of monads, representable not according to the form of external intuition, but of internal.)\n===NOTES===\n- Entire passage Latin; the English in parentheses is mine.\n- 'monadatum' is genitive plural of a coined form, kept; 'intuitus' = intuition (Anschauung).\n- 'monadatum' could be read as dependent on 'mundus' (a world of monads); 'repraesentabile' is neuter and is left unresolved.",[]],"reflm:5398":["With regard to the existence of the things of the world — Either idealist or dualist. The idealist either egoist or pluralist.\nWith regard to the grounds of explanation of the form[der] Either materialist or pneumatist.\n===NOTES===\n- Dasein = existence; Ding = thing; Welt = world; Erklärungsgrund = ground of explanation (Grund = ground); Form = form.\n- 'Form[der]' has a bracketed fragment kept as is; 'Pnevmatist' = pneumatist.\n- Both sentences have no verb.",[]],"reflm:5399":["Imagination presupposes a sense, whose form it can reproduce. Were there no outer sense, we would also not be able to imagine things outside us as such, hence according to three dimensions of space.\nWere the cause of the intuition of space in us, we would be able to become conscious of it as a representation of inner sense, and there we would have to ascribe space and figure to our representations of things just as to the things themselves.\nDreams can represent to us things as outer that are precisely then not there; but [it] we would not even be able to dream anything as outer, if these forms were not given to us through outer things. That one must believe the actuality of outer things, if we cannot prove them, would not be needed; for that has no relation to any interest of reason.\n===NOTES===\n- Einbildungskraft = imagination; Sinn = sense; Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Beweis (beweisen) = proof (prove); Vernunft = reason; glauben = believe.\n- '[sie]' is bracketed (deleted) text, kept; 'wovon jene' ('whose ... it'): 'jene' = the imagination.\n- 'nicht nöthig' = 'not needed' (nöthig, distinct from notwendig = necessary).\n- 'sie' in 'wenn wir sie nicht beweisen können' could refer to the outer things or to their actuality; kept ambiguous as 'them'.",[]],"reflm:5400":["The question whether something is outside me is just as much as [whether] if I asked whether I represented to myself an actual space. For this is something outside me. But this does not signify that something exists in itself, but rather that such *phaenomena* correspond to objects. For in the case of the *phaenomenon* there is never any talk of absolute existence. Dreams are in accordance with the analogy of waking. Apart from the representations of waking agreeing with other human beings I have no other marks of the object outside me; thus a *phaenomenon* in space outside me is what can be cognized according to rules of the understanding. How can one then ask: whether there actually are outer *phaenomena*? We are indeed not immediately conscious of them, that they are outer, i.e. not mere imaginations and dreams, but yet that they are the originals of all possible imaginations, thus themselves not imaginations.",[]],"reflm:5401":["In bodies we find nothing but impenetrability and divisibility, i.e. the possibility of parts being without combination with the others. From this it does not follow that [something there] [the] some of these parts are the last, i.e. indivisible, except only by means of the concept substance. But this concept with regard to [the] appearance is merely the persistent with regard to the alterations, i.e. the divisions, and does not signify the last subject of all relations. Hence the body is substance. But *in sensu intellectuali* (in the intellectual sense) a *corpus mysticum* (mystical body) is no substance, but an aggregate of them. The last subject does not appear outwardly. The *substratum* of outer appearance is merely what acts persistently; combination or separation is indifferent with regard to it. It inheres further in no other and is no *accidens* of another (g e.g. matter), which could vary. The *substantia noumenon* is not merely what persists in the case of the appearances, but is the last subject in every respect, hence cannot itself consist in relations. But this concept, that matter is in every respect the last subject, does not appear; rather appearance cannot give substances at all, but only its *phaenomena*, which are all *continua*.",[]],"reflm:5402":["At the very top of the page:\nThe distinction between substance as outer *phaenomenon* and the inner representation is this, that the former is the subject of inherence, without determining the unity, but the latter determines the unity through the I.",[]],"reflm:5403":["The *phaenomenon substantiatum* does not consist of simple *phaenomena*, rather all *phaenomena* are *quanta continua*.",[]],"reflm:5404":["Next to, under and \nOne cannot argue about appearances further than the conditions of appearance reach. Hence not from grounds of the intellectual concepts [which] about space and time, that they are no *substantiae*, *accidentia*, *relationes*. For from this it follows that it is only the form of appearance. Likewise from impenetrability and extension not further than to the divisibility that space permits, of which it is the appearance, not from the concept substance, if it has been abstracted from inner sense.",[]],"reflm:5405":["*Essentia* (essence) is the internal (g logical) *principium* (principle) of conceivability (*conceptibilitas*), *Natura* (nature) (g causal *principium*) of the causality of all determinations.",[]],"reflm:5406":["The first [pri] inner (g determining) *principium* (principle) of what belongs to the concept of a thing is essence.\nThe first [pri] inner (g determining) *principium*, however, of what (g to) existence belongs, is called nature*(s *materialiter* (materially): the sum total of appearances, *formaliter* (formally): the *nexus* of determinations).\n*(s Nature in opposition 1) to essence, 2) to art, 3) to freedom. In the last sense the essence is thought as such a one, which gives itself laws.)",[]],"reflm:5407":["Nature (of a thing) taken adjectivally in relation to |\nnature (according to totality), substantively against the hyperphysical. | Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:5408":["The inner *principium* (principle) of the concept of an object is essence. The inner *principium* of the actuality of everything that pertains to an object is nature.",[]],"reflm:5409":["All appearances lie in one nature.\nNature is the inner *principium causale* (causal principle) according to constant laws: *Leges stabiles* (stable laws). Substances have nature.\n*physice necessarium vel practice* (necessary physically or practically).",[]],"reflm:5410":["Nature takes its first rules from the understanding. *Commercium* (community). All effect of substances outwardly is reciprocal, indeed all motion of bodies is reciprocal.",[]],"reflm:5411":["Nature in relation to chance or fate. Opposite: freedom.\nNature in relation to the supernatural. Opposite: absolute necessity and contingent nature of the things in the world.\nNature of the world in general.",[]],"reflm:5412":["The mathematical antinomies go to the essence of appearances, the dynamical to nature. The first to appearances determined merely through space and time, the second through real grounds.",[]],"reflm:5413":["That which in the appearance of a rational being only relatively begins and in time presupposes something else, which determines its existence according to a rule, has in this, as intelligence, its ground in that which does not begin and in time is subordinated to no preceding state. Therein consists the freedom of a rational being as cause through its reason. For that is a faculty to determine itself *a priori*. For were the determining grounds empirical and given *a posteriori* subjectively, then the judgment of reason could not be regarded as *a priori*, hence as absolutely necessary.\nIn order to judge objectively universally and indeed apodictically, reason must be free from subjectively determining grounds; for if these determined, the judgment would be contingent only as it is, namely according to the subjective causes of it. Thus reason is conscious of its freedom in objectively necessary judgments *a priori*, namely that only the relation to the object is the ground of it.",[]],"reflm:5414":["Empirically one can indeed bring out rules, but not laws; like Kepler in comparison with Newton; for to the latter necessity belongs, consequently that they be cognized *a priori*. Yet one always assumes of rules of nature that they are necessary, for that is why it is nature, and that they can be seen *a priori*; hence one calls them laws *anticipando* (by anticipation). The understanding is the ground of empirical laws; consequently of an empirical necessity, where the ground of lawfulness can indeed be seen *a priori*, e.g. the law of causality, but not the ground of the determinate law. All metaphysical principles of nature are only grounds of lawfulness.",[]],"reflm:5415":["The harmony of the substances of the world is either *generaliter* or *singulariter stabilita* (established generally or singularly); the first is necessary, when many substances make up a whole, in which [if a part] the state of a part always also belongs to the state of the whole.",[]],"reflm:5416":["The *harmonia generaliter stabilita* (harmony established in general) is never *praestabilita* (pre-established); for this is always *singulariter stabilita*. therefore: *omnis harmonia est vel interne vel externe stabilita* (every harmony is established either internally or externally); the latter *vel singulariter vel generaliter* (either singularly or generally), the first *vel occasionaliter vel praestabilita* (either occasionally or pre-established).",[]],"reflm:5417":["The ground of the universal connection of substances is also the ground of space.",[]],"reflm:5418":["We cannot use the *sensitiva* (sensible things) for the explanation of the *intellectualia* (intellectual things), hence not space for the explanation of the *commercium* (community). Still less tautologically forces.",[]],"reflm:5419":["*Principium commercii debet esse omnibus commune. Ergo causa communis.* (The principle of community ought to be common to all. Therefore a common cause.)",[]],"reflm:5420":["The harmony of substances is either[real or ideal. The first is influence, the other *harmonia externe stabilita* (harmony established externally)] founded on a *commercium* (community) of the|same\nand is called influence: *harmonia naturalis* (natural harmony), or [without] on an outer determination and is called [*externe stabil*] *artificialis* (artificial); this is either *per* (by) breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5421":["Under and \n(s *influxus precarius, postulatus* (precarious, postulated influx))\n*Influxus hyperphysicus est contradictorius; nempe est harmonia [appar] speciem influxus prae se ferens.* (Hyperphysical influx is contradictory; namely it is a harmony [appar] bearing the semblance of influx.)",[]],"reflm:5422":["*Influxus physicus est vel naturaliter originarius vel derivativus; posterior est rationalis.* (Physical influx is either naturally original or derivative; the latter is rational.)",[]],"reflm:5423":["*Harmonia vel absqve commercio vel ex commercio.* (Harmony either without community or from community.)",[]],"reflm:5424":["[*influxus*] *Commercium est vel originarium vel derivativum, et in hoc influxus vel [avtomaticus] physicus vel hyperphysicus. hic vel avtomaticus vel occasionalisticus.* (Community is either original or derivative, and in the latter influx is either [automatic] physical or hyperphysical. This is either automatic or occasionalistic.)",[]],"reflm:5425":["*Nexus substantiarum est vel physicus vel hyperphysicus.* (The nexus of substances is either physical or hyperphysical.)",[]],"reflm:5426":["If it cannot be inferred from the determinations of the one substance to the other, there is no *nexus realis* (real nexus), but *idealis* (ideal).",[]],"reflm:5427":["*influxus vel realis (physicus) vel idealis. hic est harmonia absqve influxu.* (Influx is either real (physical) or ideal. This is harmony without influx.)",[]],"reflm:5428":["*Commercium est vel originarium vel derivativum, prior influxus physicus crassior. Derivativum e causa communi est vel physicum vel hyperphysicum, et hoc vel praestabilitae vel occasionalis harmoniae.* (Community is either original or derivative, the former a cruder physical influx. The derivative from a common cause is either physical or hyperphysical, and the latter either of pre-established or of occasional harmony.) The last is a *commercium* or harmony without nature.",[]],"reflm:5429":["Community (*commercium*) (the ground of it, *communitas*, consists in this, that what befalls one for a change has an effect on all) is twofold: either [according to laws of the] of the real effects or of the ideal ones, which are merely the *phaenomenon* of the effects. The influences of bodies are merely the *phaenomena* of the effects. For matter effects only relations of space; but these are not something actual or an *accidens* and thus no true *relation* from one *substantz* to the other, in order to change it internally. The outer *relation* without inner, however, is nothing true, but a mere *apparentz*. The community of bodies among one another can be understood according to *legibus phaenomenorum*; the community of spirits only according to *legibus intellectualibus*, the application of which, however, demands experience; that of bodies and spirits cannot be seen into at all, because the latter can be cognized only through inner sense and thus their changes *realiter*, the effects, however, of the first and of their forces go only to *phaenomena* of relations in general without their (g inner) determinations.",[]],"reflm:5430":["Nature and essence.\nNature and art.\nNature and freedom.\nThe nature of a thing (*formaliter*). Nature simply (*materialiter*): the world. Sum total of the objects of the senses according to rules.",[]],"reflm:5431":["The inner *principium caussale* of everything that constitutes the [state a] actuality of a thing is nature of the thing. Of the world.\nThe inner *principium possibilitatum* — *essentia*.",[]],"reflm:5432":["The nature of a thing is the complete real ground of that which flows necessarily from the constitution of a thing according to universal laws. Thus the natural is 1. to be contradistinguished from the artificial or arbitrary, added, e.g. senses and erudition. 2. from the essence, which contains that which relates necessarily to the concept of the thing, thus not always the whole nature [das]; 3. from that which arises externally: *accessorie*. Contrary to nature, insofar as something resists the laws of the nature of a thing and the possibility of concluding something from it (g according to) universal rules. (g 4. from freedom.)\nThe universal real ground [wod] of the determinations inhering in the thing is nature; thus that through which, according to a universal law, that which belongs to the predicates of its existence is determined.",[]],"reflm:5433":["The word nature is taken either substantively or adjectively. Substantively it is the aggregate of all objects of the senses, adjectively it is the (g inner) principle of the efficacy of a being.",[]],"reflm:5434":["Whether we have an experience that we are free?\n(s No! For otherwise we would have to be able to experience of all human beings [sage] that they can resist the greatest *stimulo*. Against this the moral law says: they ought to resist, consequently they must be able to.)",[]],"reflm:5435":["The will is a faculty to act according to the representation of a rule as a law. Faculty [according to] of ends. *stimuli* are pleasure that precedes the law. *independentia a stimulis* is where the law precedes pleasure. (g *arbitrium purum*.) (s Freedom is *caussalitaet* of pure reason in the determination of the power of choice.)",[]],"reflm:5436":["Freedom is the faculty of being determined only through reason, and not merely mediately, but immediately, thus not through matter, but form of the law. thus moral.",[]],"reflm:5437":["Either the object of the action or the action itself must interest. The good is either the useful or the good in itself.",[]],"reflm:5438":["The faculty of producing the motives of willing simply by oneself is freedom. This *actus* does not itself rest on willing, but is the spontaneity of the causality of willing. Over this we make ourselves reproaches or approval.",[]],"reflm:5439":["Next to and \nOuter freedom, also inner.\nThe faculty of the power of choice to act according to laws given by itself.",[]],"reflm:5440":["Freedom, insofar as it is a concept of reason, is inexplicable (g also not objectively); insofar as it is a concept of the activity and causality of reason itself, it indeed also cannot be explained as a first principle, but is a self-consciousness *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5441":["All actions of ours and of other beings are [nothw] necessitated, only the understanding (g and the will, insofar as it can be determined through understanding.) is free and a pure self-activity, which is determined through nothing other than itself. Without this original and |\nunchangeable spontaneity we would cognize nothing *a priori*; for we would be determined to everything, and our thoughts themselves would stand under empirical laws. The faculty to think and to act *a priori* is the only condition of the possibility of the origin of all other appearances. The ought would also have no meaning at all.\nFreedom and absolute necessity are the only pure concepts of reason, which are objective, although inexplicable. For through reason one understands the self-activity of going from the universal to the particular, and of doing this *a priori*, hence with a necessity simply. The absolute necessity: with regard to the determinable, and freedom: of the determining.",[]],"reflm:5442":["Logical freedom with regard to everything that are contingent predicates.\nAll contingency in the object is objective freedom (to think the opposite); if the thought suffices for the deed: also subjective freedom.\nTranscendental freedom is the complete contingency of the actions. There is [transcendental] logical freedom in actions of reason, but not transcendental, namely objective.",[]],"reflm:5443":["The talents give human beings importance, the merits dignity, all morally good qualities a worth.",[]],"reflm:5444":["Simply (unconditioned in every aim) nothing is good except the existence of freely acting beings, and in these not understanding, happiness etc etc., but the disposition to use well everything that is mediately good |\nThus the practical disposition of good aims. This is thus the good-naturedness of the will.",[]],"reflm:5445":["The moral laws do not arise from reason, but are that which contains the conditions through which alone it is possible that free actions can be determined and cognized according to the rules of reason. This happens, however, if we make the universally valid end the ground of the actions. So that the particular ends agree with those, which one can regard as if all things were possible through them.\nThe morally good demands complete unity of the ground of the action for reason, consequently that it be taken from the *idea archetypa*, which is the end of the whole world.\nWhat is in conformity with the conditions under which [something] everything happens according to rules *a priori* pleases necessarily. For it brings forth the agreement with the entire nature. and thus a consciousness of the agreement of the actions with themselves and with all others. Only from the whole and the supreme ground can that be derived which according to universal laws breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5446":["Moral laws are the [of the necessity of the actions from the], which contain the conditions through which free actions become in accord with the universally valid end, thus the private will with the original and supreme will. Either with the universal end of nature or of freely acting beings. Thus the will is [as] considered according to the unity of the ground, insofar namely as all will lies in one will: that which is the cause of nature and of every other.",[]],"reflm:5447":["The constitution of the morally good will is good conduct (of the human being), is virtue, if it happens from inclination, and fear of God, if it happens from obedience toward God.",[]],"reflm:5448":["The moral motives are supposed to have not merely *vim obiective necessitantem* for the [practical] conviction of the understanding, but *vim subiective necessitantem*, i.e. they are supposed to be *elateres*. The subjective condition under which they can be so [is] is called feeling. If it were an actual feeling (*proprie*), then the *necessitation* would be pathological; the *causae impulsivae* would be not *motiva*, but *stimuli*; not the *bonitaet*, but the *iucundum* would move us. Thus the *sensus moralis* is so called only *per analogiam* and is supposed to be called not sense, but disposition, according to which the moral motives in the subject necessitate just as *stimuli* do. It is thus *in sensu proprio* a non-thing, a mere *analogon sensus* and serves only to express a faculty (not receptivity), for which we have no name.",[]],"reflm:5449":["Over and next to the conclusion of M § 730 »servitutem — servile«:\n*Arbitrium servum est, in qvo imperium in semet ipsum cessat.*",[]],"reflm:5450":["Nature and freedom are two principles of the determination of our concepts. For explanation we can avail ourselves of [through] freedom only *secundum qvid*; for mere nature gives the last determining grounds. For this alone contains principles of actual behavior. If I am to give a ground why someone behaves thus or thus, the appeal to his free choice is a circle.",[]],"reflm:5451":["The soul thought according to intellectual determinations:\n1. according to the subject,\n2. the forces,\n3. the state.",[]],"reflm:5452":["The soul as *substantz*.\n*intelligentz* (not pure).\n*simplicitas*. *Vnitas*.\n*libertas*. *Commercium cum corpore*.\n*Sedes*\n      as spirit.\n*immaterialitas* (s *personalitas*)\n       *ortus*, *mors* (s *conscientia*)",[]],"reflm:5453":["All inner experience [has] is a judgment, in which the predicate is empirical and the subject is I. Independent of this experience thus remains merely the I left over for *psychologia rationalis*; for the I is the *substratum* of all empirical judgments.",[]],"reflm:5454":["Metaphysical predicates: 1. The soul is substance; 2. a substance distinct from matter, no object of outer senses; 3. Simple, hence immaterial; 4. but whether it persists, does not follow.",[]],"reflm:5455":["The *psychologia empirica* contains no empirical conditions of the propositions about the soul, but only the empirical concept [through which] the soul itself with everything that belongs to it, in order to distinguish it from everything else, thus does not belong to *philosophia pura*, but also not to *physiologia*, but to metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:5456":["In and \nThinking and willing cannot be an outer appearance at all. Now I know myself as soul only according to thinking, feeling and willing. Thus we cannot represent the soul at all as an object of outer senses. Everything of which we are conscious in it, [is] thus indicates an immaterial being. We would have to perceive outer things and ourselves through one and the same sense.",[]],"reflm:5457":["In and \nOn the possibility of the *commercium* of that which is only an object of inner sense with that which is only of outer sense. We know in matter only the outer, in the soul only the inner, immediately. The *commercium* (g among) the objects of outer senses we do not know originally and *a priori*, just so not the *commercium* of the inner forces of the soul. But the first *data* of outer cognition already contain concepts of *commercium*, and those of the inner also. The *phaenomena* are thereby compared according to identity. We can thus neither see *a priori* the natural possibility nor impossibility; consequently all *systemata* about it are in vain, and it is a proposition of experience that inner determinations be grounds of the outer and conversely, and this is an original force.\nOn the seat of the soul: whether it is determined as material substance in regard to space [outwardly] in the body, or only through the *commercium* with the body in regard to the rest of the world. It has its seat, i.e. the first and immediate connection with the nervous system.\nEverything of which a general rule can be cognized through experience is natural. Accordingly the *commercium* is natural. Immateriality is a correct problematic concept, which cannot be refuted at all.",[]],"reflm:5458":["The concept of the human soul is entirely independent of the concept of materiality. One also cannot understand determinations of inner sense at all as outer *phaenomena* and conversely. The proof that the soul is immaterial [is] would be this: 1. it is not matter (*materialismus in sensu crassiori*), hence simple; 2. it is no simple part of matter, for matter does not consist of simple parts. An animal is animate matter. All animate matter is at the same time ensouled. For the *principium* of life is absolute *internum*, i.e. thinking and willing, i.e. determinations of itself according to choice.",[]],"reflm:5459":["*sedes animae*. What is merely an object of inner sense can be no object of outer. Accordingly the substance of the soul cannot be exposed to the conditions of outer intuition, but merely the subject of its influence, and the acting force of the soul (outwardly) is a *phaenomenon*. There, however, everything that is an instrument of sensation is immediately the subject of its presence. It can act only by exciting the interaction of the bodily parts, the attraction and repulsion of the same.",[]],"reflm:5460":["In and \nThe soul does not move other things thereby, that it mov|es itself.\nIt is no object of the *phaenomena*. It [moves] brings forth motions in others, which do not [or] have their determining ground in matter, whereby however the motion of the same in sum is not altered.",[]],"reflm:5461":["I as the correlate of all outer intuition am human. The outer intuition, to which all the rest in me are related, is my body. Thus I must, as a subject of outer intuitions, have a body. The conditions of outer intuition and of inner determine each other reciprocally (*commercium* of soul and body). The actuality [of a b]ody in regard to myself is the interconnection of outer appearances among one another, and in regard to others the agreement in the relations [with] of the outer appearances of that through which others designate their intuition, with the designations of my understanding, consequently still the agreement of all outer appearances among one another. The actuality of bodies is not the actuality of things, but of appearances, which [themselves] are related to x, y, etc. etc. Something that is represented only as object of appearance and yet is supposed to be no such, is nothing for us. Idealism denies more than one knows; realism assumes more than what is in question.",[]],"reflm:5462":["In the case of *epigenesis* we must assume that the soul in general belongs to the intelligible world; that it has no place in space, that, if an organized body has arisen through generation, it has the |\ncondition in itself of being straightaway ensouled through the intelligible animating principle; and that in the body itself the soul is present not locally, but virtually.",[]],"reflm:5463":["With empirical proofs we do not get out of the world of sense.",[]],"reflm:5464":["The empirical proofs hold neither for nor against, because we can conduct no experience other than in *commercio* with the body. We are harnessed to the body like to a cart. Now its wheels must be in good condition, otherwise we cannot move it and on that account not ourselves either. Here it is argued only κατ ανθρωπον. Illusion opposed to illusion.",[]],"reflm:5465":["1. A proof *a priori* from the concept* (I think) of a living being in general. [2. From the cognition of another being *a priori* (of the necessary, God): *theologia moralis*] 2. The proofs *a posteriori*** neither for nor against the immortality of the soul are possible from *psychologia empirica*, because we can make no experience other than in *commercio* with the body. Cosmologically a proof *a posteriori* can be conducted.\n*(s of the (g immaterial) soul; 2, from the divine attributes)\n**(s either from the nature of the soul or the analogy)",[]],"reflm:5466":["M 314:\n* an argument is either natural or moral**; the former either rational or empirical (s the latter either strictly moral or theological).\n*(s Through the moral, immortality is not proved, nor strictly the universality and eternity.)\n(s The empirical is physical or teleological.)\nM 314':\n**(s The physiological is either a strict or an analogical; (g an argument,) what is in necessary combination, constrains; what consists in mere comparison, touches. Strict argument is genetic, the analogical is elective.)",[]],"reflm:5467":["The destruction (*interitus*) of the body removes animal life, not spiritual.",[]],"reflm:5468":["1. That the continuation of the life of the soul does not depend on the *commercium* with the body, because all matter is lifeless.",[]],"reflm:5469":["Whether animal life is all life or the condition of the beginning of the spiritual?",[]],"reflm:5470":["The bodily means of thinking can fall away when the hindrances have fallen away.",[]],"reflm:5471":["Only the objections that the materialist makes against the immortality of the soul can be refuted metaphysically. The proof itself is only moral.",[]],"reflm:5472":["The proof of immortality is twofold. 1. from grounds of nature; 2. from moral grounds.\nI. a. from the nature of the soul known through experience, b. the nature of a thinking being cognized *a priori*, c. the analogy of nature in general. (g II.) The moral proof: a. theological from the contingent will of God (g [as originator of moral: laws]) (g from moral grounds of the divine will.); b. absolutely moral from the necessary will of God, in that morality is regarded as necessary in itself and determines the belief in God and at the same time the concept of his will.",[]],"reflm:5473":["We have to prove: 1. the persistence (g of the substance) of the souls: incorruptibility, for which simplicity is not enough;\n2. the continuation of the [person not merely] intelligence, not merely according to the faculty, but also according to the *actus*. Sleep of the soul;\n3. the continuation of the person and of the consciousness of the identity of oneself. Not metempsychosis.",[]],"reflm:5474":["1. That the soul will be after death,\n2. that it will live as intelligence,\n3. that it will be conscious of the identity of its person.\n*immortalitas*. 1. from empirical psychology,\n2. from the concept of immateriality,\n3. the analogy of nature.",[]],"reflm:5475":["The moral proof does not say that the soul will live in the future, but that the upright person cannot avoid assuming this and regarding it at least as possible.",[]],"reflm:5476":["If to the Pythagorean principle I had to assume the existence of God, then it would not be hypothesis, but postulate. Now one must assume the existence of God for the occurrence of good actions as incentive, and they are yet as necessary as geometrical propositions.",[]],"reflm:5477":["The moral *postulata* are evident, their opposite can be brought *ad absurdum morale*. Their ground is that good conduct is the only condition under which one is worthy to be happy. The ground of their *necessitation* does not lie in their offering means to the desire for happiness, but in their prescribing to it the *conditionem sine qua non*, by which it is limited. But our longing to be happy is just as necessary and at the same time fair. Now mere worthiness cannot move without hope that it will also make us partakers of the end; thus it is a necessary moral *hypothesis* to assume another world. Whoever does not assume it falls into an *absurdum practicum*.\nThe *dilemma practicum* shows that prudence and honesty can be together in the hope of another world.\nIt is a principle of reason that we should not long to be happy where we are not worthy of it. There is also another, that it not be demanded to conduct oneself worthily if we cannot hope to become so.",[]],"reflm:5478":["The objections against the immortality of the soul are: 1. From experience during life: decline of the mind; 2. From the contingency of the generations and waste of the germs. Preexistence. Birth is appearance in the world of sense.\nSeparation of the soul and of the body. Place.",[]],"reflm:5479":["Community with the other world is either mystical or physical. The mystical cannot be admitted in this world, because thereby laws of experience would be interrupted.",[]],"reflm:5480":["Apparition of spirits. Premonition. Dream interpretation. Second sight. Sympathy of the soul.\nHappiness in this world consists in progressing, hence it can never be fully attained; every moment drives us out of the present state.\nMoral perfection too. Thus the future, on which progress it may be. Hence not improvement in dying. Not eternity |\nof rejection or beatification, but unforeseeableness of progress.\nM 322':\nThat without which reason could not have any known law is contrary to its maxim, e.g. appearances, spirit.",[]],"reflm:5481":["In and \nWe can extend our concepts of beings *a priori* by leaving out something that is empirical, but not by adding something non-empirical. The concept of unreasoning souls or of beings not united with animality.",[]],"reflm:5482":["Possibility can be considered *materialiter* or *formaliter*. Likewise necessity. Formal necessity is at all times only the necessity of judgments. Something is *materialiter* necessary, and indeed absolutely unconditioned without a restricting *respectum*. The proposition: there is a necessary thing, is inferred from this, that the *illimitatum* must be presupposed before all negation.",[]],"reflm:5483":["If the proof [in s] to assume the necessity in the existence of a supreme being according to laws of the understanding and maxims of the will is also not apodictic, it is practically necessitating (*necessitirend*).",[]],"reflm:5484":["Proof *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The latter is physicotheological or moral. The first transcendental or cosmological. The proof *a posteriori* cannot become a demonstrative one, because the ground for believing in a God amounts only to a necessary *hypothesis*.",[]],"reflm:5485":["He who contests the proofs of the existence of God or even sets up proofs of the opposite, in order to show that the illusion is on the side of the opponent too, conflicts only with the knowledge of the divine existence, but not with the belief, which is the *principium* from principles.",[]],"reflm:5486":["*Ens summum* is partly *originarium*, partly *ens entium*.\nWhether an apodictic or practical proof of God is possible.\nThe proof is sufficient either logically or morally.\nA propaedeutic or introductory (Wolf), but no apodictic proof.\nBoundaries of the world of sense *a priori* and *a posteriori*.",[]],"reflm:5487":["Deism, atheism.\nWithout a determining concept of necessity nothing follows but deism.",[]],"reflm:5488":["The proof of the existence of God is either transcendental or [met] natural (physiological); the latter is either metaphysical: from the existence of a world in general, or physicotheological: from the present world.",[]],"reflm:5489":["*Conceptus terminator*. The object of all empirical cognition is nature. The *principium* and the end of nature.",[]],"reflm:5490":["The being above nature we can cognize not through properties of nature, but only according to an analogy of them. Divine existence relates to all possible duration as time to eternity.",[]],"reflm:5491":["The natural concept.\nSome reality and some not: is a division and presupposes the all of reality (s Necessary presupposition in thinking in general). The proof 1. from mere concepts, 2. from experience in general (something exists) or from determinate experience: physicotheological.",[]],"reflm:5492":["The necessary subjective condition of the (g inner) possibility of things is itself a (g in itself) necessary presupposition of the understanding. The being of all beings, the highest, the all-sufficient.\nThe necessary presupposition for the concept of the inner possibility of things is a subjectively necessary condition of the under|standing.\n(s That an absolutely necessary being must exist means: Our concept of possibility goes before actuality only with appearances.)",[]],"reflm:5493":["The proposition of the ancients: from nothing comes nothing, or: possibility presupposes something actual, relates to this, that our understanding can only order, but not bring forth data, but must find such ready at hand; thus all possibility is conditioned.",[]],"reflm:5494":["We arrive at the concept of God through pure reason in two ways: 1. in that we are led to the concept of an absolutely necessary one. 2. in that we are led through concepts of possibilities to the *summam realitatem*. From the latter as a mere predicate one cannot infer to the former. But both can be united.",[]],"reflm:5495":["M 332.\nHuman beings would probably like to have a theoretical certainty of the existence of God, independent of all grounds of conduct, so that it would remain free to them to direct themselves according to the cognitions, to proceed either in accordance with the moral laws or the laws of inclination, depending on whether they cognized that a God is or not. But the moral laws are of such a kind that with them there is no freedom to choose; and [sie] no moral purity is possible if before the resolutions of the same cognitions precede, which make them unnecessary and reduce everything to prudence. Hence only so much light is given to us as can subsist together with the originality of the moral motives and their driving power. If we were indiffer|ent with regard to them, the grounds of proof (logical) would not alone convince us. Free belief in God is a merit, apodictic certainty and forced belief from fear none.\nM 332':\nHe who believes himself objectively free is a free spirit and a practical atheist. He who holds him merely for the author of general laws of nature without particular governance is a naturalist and [auch] without religion, as that one is without morality.",[]],"reflm:5496":["The author of nature (original being) is no *principium* of the governance of the world according to the conditions of freedom.",[]],"reflm:5497":["The concept of God (g as supreme cause, necessary being) is a *conceptus terminator*. It belongs to the world and yet is no part of the world; according to the first we can cognize in it everything that relates to the world, for which we need to cognize it as cause of the same; but we do not cognize what is in it. For with regard to that it also has no meaning.",[]],"reflm:5498":["The cognition of the divine existence from practically sufficient principles is the belief in the same.",[]],"reflm:5499":["The necessity of a being. 1. As (g original being) independent, 2. As first cause, 3. as author, a. of the contingent in general or b. of the order.",[]],"reflm:5500":["331'. E II 1646.\nM 331:\n* Absolute necessity is not inner (g whichever concept (g of the thing) I may assume, its opposite is |\ninnerly possible). It is not outer as hypothesis (g of everything) of existence. Consequently as hypothesis of all possibility. Now the substratum of all possibility *a priori* is the highest reality. Thus the opposite of the same is not among the possibilities. For all negations are limitations; consequently in all possibility the unlimited is presupposed. This is laid at the ground in every aim, hence absolutely necessary: positive concept of necessity. (g Cartesius's proof. Wolf.)\nM 331':\n*(g The necessity of space and time cannot be proved, but only as a necessary condition of the possibility of things according to form, thus also of the *ens realissimum* as a condition of the possibilities of the same according to matter; meanwhile both grounds are only subjective.)",[]],"reflm:5501":["Proof from thinking in general, and not from the determinate concept of a thing.\nThe proof of a being that comprehends everything cannot be cognized *a posteriori*, nor otherwise *a priori* than from the conditions of our reason, to determine the object of cognition in the all.\nThe concept of absolute necessity also cannot be acquired otherwise. It rests on this, that everything that is to be thought is given in sensation and *negationes* are limitations.",[]],"reflm:5502":["331'. E II 1628II.\nM 331:\nWe can think non-being determinately only insofar as we think the thing itself, and the non-being of the determinations if these |\ndeterminations are given to us. Thus [when] I cannot think determinately that nothing at all exists, because I would have to say: there does not exist a body, spirit etc. etc. But this would not be thought if nothing at all were given.\nIn the concept of the *ens realissimum* alone are the concepts of all things generally (*omnimode*) determinable; thus it is the *substratum* of the thinkability of all things. Absolute necessity is distinguished from the conditioned in that the former is a necessary *hypothesis* for a determinate thinkable, the latter for everything.\nM 331':\nwe can indeed have a concept of a (form) shape without anything being there, but not of a reality; for this presupposes sensation, hence empirical intuition. Thus at the ground of all possible real concepts lies a *substratum originarium* as *realissimum*. Our thinking is only a reflecting. The material condition of all thinking (g of an object) is thus that something be given as [object] in intuition. The first formal and real ground of thinkability is necessary. Just as I represent to myself various possibilities, so I must assume a *substratum*. Every thing I can cancel, but not the condition of its possibility.",[]],"reflm:5503":["That is unconditionally necessary which is necessary without restrictive condition, consequently in every respect.",[]],"reflm:5504":["The possibility of a determinate limited thing does indeed precede actuality; but with regard to all things in general an actuality precedes all possibility. This is absolute reality.",[]],"reflm:5505":["The perfect precedes the imperfect in the idea *a priori*, and the latter is determinable only in the former. (g — thoroughgoing determination.)\n(g We would not have a concept of the imperfect at all if we did not think the perfect.)\nThe cosmological proof is from the existence of the contingent, primarily from the *primo motore*. The [cause] causality of the cause of the contingent is freedom. But the absolute necessity of such a being cannot be seen, i.e. the necessity in every respect.\n*physicotheologie*: author according to the analogy.\nMoral necessity to assume a God. Possibility is already enough — atheism.",[]],"reflm:5506":["The Cartesian proof is this, that a concept can be so arranged that actuality comes in among its predicates, although the concept of the thing in itself distinguishes nothing on account of existence or non-being. That therefore the non-being of this thing contradicts itself. But the cancellation of a thing with all predicates is always possible; consequently it shows the falsity of the presuppositions, that it is feasible to make such a concept as could not be canceled at all.",[]],"reflm:5507":["Around M § 815:\nThis all comes down to the faulty concept: to take existence from a thing; it should read: to take a thing itself. Existence constitutes no *constitutivum* of a concept; internally, therefore, no concept contains it, and thus absolute necessity |\nis no inner but respective, but in every relation, namely of the general *substratum* to everything possible.",[]],"reflm:5508":["Even if the existence of God does not flow from the conditions on which we ground the concept of possibility, enough nonetheless follows from them for it to be granted by one who can judge about this *a priori*. The subjective conditions of thinking thus serve very much to convince *cat anthropon*, not apodictically.",[]],"reflm:5509":["The supreme *principium* 1. of the possibilities of things; 2. of nature; 3. of [Mo] morality.",[]],"reflm:5510":["At the top of the page:\nThe *summum bonum reale* is the generally valid cause.\nThe *summum bonum formale* (g *finale*) (ideal) is the free power of choice, which is generally valid for every power of choice; hence this is also the form of the causality of the former.",[]],"reflm:5511":["It belongs to moral theology that in the most perfect world moral evil must be possible, because freedom and incentive to evil must be in it. Whether it is possible that God does not prevent it is only to be seen by him who cognizes the relation of the parts to the whole.",[]],"reflm:5512":["M 335:\nOf the *ens originarium* (s *omnisufficiens*) (s necessity, all-sufficiency, unity. *Noumenon*) 1. *Necessitas*. 2. *substantialitas*.* 3. im|*mutabilitas*.\n4. *impassibilitas*. (g b. *omnisufficientia*. *infinitudo*.) 5. *Vnicitas*. 6. *simplicitas*. 7. *immaterialitas* (s *supramundanum* (*contra spinozistas*)). 8. *ens extramundanum*: *extra spatium et tempus* (s Not in *commercio*, but indeed in *nexu* with the world.) 9. *aeternitas*. 10. *omnipotentia*. **11. *omnipraesentia*. (s 12. *immensitas*. *omnitudo*. 13. *Analogia* as *ens extramundanum* *contra anthropomorphistas*.) (s Is not an *ens homogeneum* with the world. Is not a member in the sequence of degrees of things.)\nM 335':\n*(g Unchangeability rests not on necessity, but on this, that the *realissimum* is not determinable *ad oppositum realitatis*.)\nM 335:\n**(s If something exists as conditioned; the possibility of creation in time or from eternity.)",[]],"reflm:5513":["M 335':\nThree ways of perfection: 1. *transscendentalis* of causality; 2. *naturalis* as author; 3. of the ends (s *summum bonum*). *Ens optimum*. As supreme |\nM 335: |\n*principium morale* (s holy being).",[]],"reflm:5514":["*Theologia rationalis* *contra* (g *pharisaeismum*) *clericismum* (*plebeii* (g *laici*)).\n*Theologiae communitas* *contra* *sophistas* (*idiota*).\n(g *Theologia arrogans* (s or *popularis*).)",[]],"reflm:5515":["Speculative theology is only negative, in order to keep off the errors. Practical theology is positive.",[]],"reflm:5516":["Moral theology alone requires a determinate concept of a highest being; natural theology does not, but makes existence intuitive.",[]],"reflm:5517":["Next to and \nCosmological proof: *Ens limitatum* can be determined in a twofold way, NB. *respectu* to its concept, but not to existence or non-being. *Ens realissimum* only *vnico modo*, but not with regard to existence.",[]],"reflm:5518":["Around and also perhaps \nAll possibilities presuppose the concept of an *ens realissimum*. and this concept presupposes [presuppose a] existence, because realities, without their having been given, cannot be thought in sensation either, but what has been given in sensation exists.\nAbsolute necessity rests on the presupposed condition of all possibility, not on the identity of a concept with itself, from which no existence follows.",[]],"reflm:5519":["*Necessarium absolutum*. Since difference can be thought only through limitations, [so lies] so can the foundation of the |\npossibilities not be in many beings, because these themselves always presuppose something else, but one must contain everything, and the multiplicity [of the] or manifoldness rests on the limitations of this unity.\nFor in our subject there is unity of all concepts, because [be] possibility is only [with] the agreement with the entire inner *data*.",[]],"reflm:5520":["M 336'.\nA well-natured soul is easily* to be convinced to belief (g in God and a future), but a wicked one cannot be helped. The hardness of his heart makes him keen merely on speculation, and he at most fears a God, but does not believe him, i.e. does not assume him.\nM 336:\n*(g From the Italian proverb: Go about with your friend as if he could one day be your enemy. God, protect me from my friend; from my enemy I will well guard myself.)",[]],"reflm:5521":["The specific mark of God is not magnitude, but the necessity of his nature; but this is at the same time inseparably combined with the concept of the all as condition of cognition through reason.\nOne can proceed from a smaller to a greater being by degrees, but not from the contingent to the necessary.",[]],"reflm:5522":["The ground of the transcendental proof lies in this. We can think [no first] possibilities only derivative, not originary; |\nconsequently the All given to us is the *substratum* of possibility, where through limitation and through altered relations all our thinking *a priori* rests on it. The subjective ground of all possibility is thus this sum total (subjective) of reality, which makes up unity in us; for through this alone can the thoroughgoing relation [what] and agreement, which is the form of possibility, be understood.",[]],"reflm:5523":["At the top of the page and \n[Not Ma] In itself nothing at all is necessary, i.e. according to the laws of our understanding; for that non-being of a thing has in itself nothing conflicting. But from the fact that something is not cognized as necessary in itself, I cannot conclude that it is contingent, i.e. that it could happen that it is or also that it is not, i.e. that its non-existence is possible as something that follows upon existence, or existence which follows upon non-existence. Although the existence of all things can be canceled in itself, namely the thought, the thing is not therefore to be canceled. The proof of the necessary, therefore, from this, that if something exists it must be necessary or contingent, is not valid. It is neither of the two. Its existence has no connection at all with the thought of the thing.",[]],"reflm:5524":["On M § 826?\nTranscendental theology contains nothing but *praedicata logica Entis realissimi*; the *determinationes* as *praedicata realia synthetica* belong to theism.",[]],"reflm:5525":["1. The inner logical necessity proves no necessary existence: modalities of the determinations are not themselves determinations. |\n(g Absolute necessity from respective predicates to possibility.)\n2. The conditioned is the only possible under given conditions. The all of the conditions of the existing is the only thing possible absolutely, i.e., in every respect; that which exists at all is, in thoroughgoing respect, the only possible. The highest condition must therefore be the *principium* of all possibility, hence the *ens realissimum*.\nThe transcendental proof is conducted from the concept of the *entis realissimi* not as *conceptus determinati*, but of *principii determinantis summi* (*qvoad existentiam omnem*). Necessary existence is not inner, but respective, as necessary hypothesis of all possibility.",[]],"reflm:5526":["Around and \nThe first subjective condition of thinking, i.e., of the representation of the objects according to possibility, is that all matter of the representation be given in sensation and objectively in perception, second the form of the combination of the manifold. Hence that which contains the matter and data of everything possible is presupposed as an object of perception, i.e., the matter of everything possible exists as necessary presupposition. The manifoldness of the objects with regard to this all of reality rests on the form of the limitation of this all. Hence at the ground lies the absolute unity of the all, in which everything is possible through limitation. Something must therefore exist as a *substratum* of possibility.",[]],"reflm:5527":["Above, beside and \n*Ens necessarium* is a subjectively necessary *hypothesis* of the thinking of all possibility. All possibility of a limited thing is derived in the idea and presupposes a greater, which is limited. All possibility therefore has as condition the *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5528":["Beside and quite possibly also \nTranscendental *theology* has the advantage that in it alone the necessity of a highest being can be cognized. Hence it alone can also determine it thoroughly as unity etc etc.\nIt alone purifies *theologia naturalis* of the influence of empirical predicates, anthropomorphism. The disadvantage is that the ground of proof is only subjective; for existence cannot be cognized completely a priori. But as presupposition it can nevertheless be necessary in the practical use of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:5529":["*Anthropopathôs*.\nThe deist wants to prevent anthropomorphism.\nWe can represent God to ourselves only according to the analogy with human beings, but not according to similarity with them. Anthropomorphism is often more harmful than atheism.\nSpeculative anthropomorphism.\nThe practical.",[]],"reflm:5530":["(g Presupposed existence of a world.)\nThe Wolffian cosmological proof of the existence of God is really transcendental. (g Something exists. I am.) There must be a necessary being, one or several.[Es existirt] I am not the necessary being. The necessary being must be unlimited. For the concept of the limited permits many, thus the possibility of the opposite; but the concept of the unlimited does not permit the opposite of a single determination.",[]],"reflm:5531":["*Theologia naturalis*. The original being as a cause of nature, thus in relation to the same, since the causality is expressed through determinations of nature. The chief concept of causality is freedom as the causality with regard to the contingent, consequently intelligence.",[]],"reflm:5532":["*Anthropomorphism crassior, subtilior* (coarser, subtler). Prevention of the same *via reductionis*; the purity of the concept of God.",[]],"reflm:5533":["The archetype is nowhere in the world, but solely in the deity. (g It is an idea, i.e., a cognition that contains the ground of the possibility of the objects and of all estimation of them and cannot be abstracted from them.) There is no creature that is equal to the idea of morality; at least moral perfection can be assessed only according to the preceding idea of it. We have an empirical idea of the beauty of a human being and of a horse; otherwise we would not be able to say what each lacks. This idea is single (ideal in the head); everything is determined in it, if it is to have the greatest absolute perfection. That which is there according to the idea is the after-image (imitation), and the sensible representation of it apparentz. We must study the archetype of the things that are possible only from the understanding from ideas and not from experience. The idea of morals and religion.",[]],"reflm:5534":["353. E II 1713.\n1. To determine God through psychological predicates, but from ontological grounds of the concept of a *realissimi*;\n2. from physicoteleological grounds.\nin all theology, if we determine God even through psychological or moral predicates, we must nevertheless, in order to infer them in God, derive them from the concept of the |\nM 353: |\n*realissimi*. This: whether as *complexus* or *ratio*.",[]],"reflm:5535":["Whether God through his understanding (g not reason) also contains the ground of beings and of their possibility.\nThe difficulty is not how God cognizes the future, but how he cognizes at all the actual, when it is present to us. Only through the consciousness of his own will, that it be there; but thereby free actions seem to fall away.\nFreedom is absolute spontaneity of actions. The question is whether that of which God is the cause could have absolute spontaneity. If the force that he imparted is capable of determining itself to acting from objective grounds, consequently reason is an efficient cause, then he has imparted absolute spontaneity; the possibility is not to be seen. It amounts to this, how God could make a substance.",[]],"reflm:5536":["Whether God cognizes the things and their actions by producing them?",[]],"reflm:5537":["*cognitio qviescens et operativa*.\nThe former: where God cognizes himself and everything possible.\nThe second: where he [sei] cognizes the products of his will.",[]],"reflm:5538":["The possibility of the foreseeing of free actions has its difficulties only from the possibility of freedom itself.",[]],"reflm:5539":["The difficulty of the divine foreseeing of our free actions lies not in this, that they are future, for before God nothing is present, past or future, but in this, that God at any rate also cognizes present free actions, and thus how even the *scientia visionis* of them is possible.",[]],"reflm:5540":["The effect of God on the human being as [freyes] free being is the effect of independent reason on our limited nature. For God is only idea of reason.",[]],"reflm:5541":["(g In nature there is nothing evil, but ill in the parts. In freedom alone is evil. The whole of nature is yet good, and the evil concerns only the part. as every part can have collision.)\nImperfection is from ill (which is positive) and this from evil, which is an absolute ill and not merely respective, |\nto be distinguished. All ills come from the lack of unity in the world-whole. Hence conflicts arise. With perfect unity not only according to substance but also according to forces everything must agree with the nature of the being and thus be good. In the human being evil rests on the animal, what [dem rationalen] on impressions and inclination, and its conflict against the rational, what is directed at universally valid ends. If we considered the human being before the development of his reason as a species of animals, we would find the origin of evil.",[]],"reflm:5542":["The new discoveries in astronomy not only extend but also change somewhat physicotheology. For if the human race is the whole genus of rational beings and the only one, one cannot well comprehend how it agrees with the wisdom and goodness of God, since one can after all think up something more perfect. But if there are millions of other worlds, then this is one level of the rational creatures, which together with their deficiencies could not be lacking.",[]],"reflm:5543":["Every series has its beginning; but that the whole series has a beginning presupposes that it can be considered whole.",[]],"reflm:5544":["A first beginning cannot be thought according to laws of sensibility. A sequence without beginning not according to laws of reason.\n*conceptus hybridus*, which makes up the boundary concept.",[]],"reflm:5545":["We must philosophize about nature as if the world had no beginning, and about God as if it had no succession.\nTo regard God as the highest point of the series is a confusion of kinds.",[]],"reflm:5546":["Beside and on the last three sentences of M § 926, esp. the word »creare« (1909-12):\n*architectus et creator, moderator* (s 1. *Auctor mundi*, not *causa physica*). *actuator substantiae mundi est creator, formae est architectus*.",[]],"reflm:5547":["*Theologia applicata*:\nCreation,\nGovernment,\nAiming at ends (determination, completion).",[]],"reflm:5548":["Above, in and \nThe bordering of the world on an empty time and empty space is impossible. Outside the world there is no possible appearance. Now however the world with all its predicates is only the object of appearance.",[]],"reflm:5549":["In and \nWhy is the empty time before the world or the empty space outside the world not filled or are both full or is there no time and space at all outside the world. But if I nevertheless remain in the world, is the time of the measuring of it bounded or unbounded.",[]],"reflm:5550":["Whether God comprehends all [create] in his creation of the world that is possible in itself? and thus also every possible free action? and whether it is then free. How it is possible to create a freely acting being. What chooses from its own liking according to a universal law is free. The motive causes do not determine it; it determines itself. But the grounds of the opposite also become, according to universal laws, only motives to dissuade from the actions. The connection of the *causarum moventium* in freely acting beings therefore consists only in this, that their actions are to be determined according to universal (g objective) laws of choice.\nThe choice according to a universal law of choice is the ground of the appropriation of that which has been liked; but the desire that was determined not from an inner *principio*, but singly as impression, could not be appropriated.\nThe choice according to naturally universal laws would be physically determined, but according to logically universal grounds, according to a [reflected] universal principle, in that I have the faculty of comparing something with all grounds of my liking, is choice at liking, and I am then free from singular determination; all motives can be compared with these universal rules of liking and have their force, whether I am subjectively determined to these actions or not.",[]],"reflm:5551":["The honor of God properly names no end, but says only that the world is constituted such that it brings the greatest honor to a divine author, i.e. one can cognize his perfection from it and also have the sources to acquire it therein, as is possible when created; but in what this perfection consists is not indicated thereby, and it the highest moral and physical finite good. To be happy and worthy of happiness.",[]],"reflm:5552":["S. I:\nConcepts of reflection (their amphiboly)\n[which lead to paralogisms].\nParalogism is a syllogism that is false according to form, although it is correct according to matter (the premises). — It arises when [a Be] the middle concept is taken in both premises in different meaning — namely when the logical relation (g in thinking) in one of the premises is taken in the other for a real one (of the objects of intuition):\n1. Identity and difference. 2. Agreement and conflict. 3. The inner and outer. 4. The determinable (matter) and determination (form).\nDifferent relation to the faculty of cognition: to sensibility or the understanding for difference of things and identity [of the] before the former also such before the latter are breaks off.\n1. Things identical according to quality and quantity are not different (many) things, but one and the same.\nBefore the understanding, to be sure, two drops of water, one egg like the other, but not in intuition in space as *phaenomena*.\n2. What is not logically opposed to itself is also not opposed to itself in space and time (really) a –a.\n3. [Sub] Outer things (g substances) must have inner determinations, but the determinations of matter consist in sheer outer relations; hence I cannot infer monads, which have representations, because these are the only inner.\n4. Matter (the constituents of a thing) precedes form — but in intuition the form, which is given for itself alone, precedes matter.\nSomething and Nothing\n1.\nConcept without object;\nthis is nothing. *ens rationis*.\nThought-entity.\n2.\nEmpty object of a concept.\n*nihil privativum*. Shadow.\n3.\nEmpty intuition\nwithout object.\n*ens imaginarium*.\nSpace.\n4.\nEmpty object without concept.\n*nihil negativum*.\nn. 1. and n. 2\nthought-entity is distinguished from\nnon-thing.\naxiom, anticipation, analogy, postulate.\nThe synthetic propositions *a priori* are\nprinciples of possible experience, thus go\nonly to objects of the senses.\nConclusion of the ontology.\nThe understanding prescribes the law to nature; but none reaching further than that of the form of appearances, which grounds the possibility |\nof experience in general. For nature as object of empirical cognition must conform to this, because otherwise it would not be nature for us, since it would be impossible to find in it an interconnection that would conform to our faculty of bringing the manifold of appearances into an interconnected consciousness, it would consequently not be cognizable.\n(g Empirical intuition and concepts make up experience.)\nWe can have synthetic cognition *a priori* of objects of experience, namely when they [upon] contain principles of the possibility of experience in general.\nS. II:\n( g 1. That the possibility of synthetic judgment *a priori* depends solely on the ideality of space and time. (g That if we were to cognize things in themselves, we would perceive them, thus would not cognize them *a priori* as so necessary.) (g because only from the fact that our faculty of intuition has this form can we know *a priori* how the objects will be intuited by us — these forms are the merely subjective of the faculty of representation — and this is objective with regard to things as appearances.) )\n3.\nThat we must place under all our pure concepts of the understanding a schema, a [relation] way of putting together the manifold in space and time. — That this schema is merely in the sensible representation of the subject, that we thus [dad] 1. cognize only objects of the senses, consequently do not reach out to the supersensible. (Geometry). 2. The concepts, however, can be extended to all objects of thinking in general. But they give no extension of theoretical cognition. In practical respect, however, where freedom |\nis the condition of their use, [thus] practical-dogmatic cognitions can nevertheless [propositions] take place — God, freedom, and immortality (spiritual nature).\nIn nature, however, i.e. in space and time, nothing unconditioned can be encountered, and yet reason demands the same as the totality of conditions, because it wants to make the object itself. — Hence in cosmology, where nature is considered as the whole of all objects of the senses, antinomy is encountered; — in theology, where we have to weigh an object only in practical dogmatic aim, the relation of the supersensible object, lying beyond nature, [only] to the things of the world can be cognized only according to the analogy with an intelligence of nature, and also only insofar as it is thought in moral respect for human beings.\nThreefold intellectual (g intelligible) (*noumenon*) contains the unconditioned, and of freedom and its laws one can have cognition and thereby prove the objective reality of humanity as *noumenon* in the midst of the mechanism of the same as *phaenomenon*. — God as unconditionally necessary substance. Freedom as unconditioned causality and immortality as personality (spirit) independent of the *commercio* with the body (as condition).\nThe categories applied to the intelligible can nevertheless ground practical-dogmatic cognitions, if namely they are directed to freedom and determine the subject of the same only in relation to it; for then we cognize God only according to the analogy of the subsistence [of the same] of a thing amid all change of the accidents in time (g duration), freedom according to the analogy of the causality in the [sequence] connection of force with the effects in the temporal sequence, immortality according to the analogy of the connection of many at all times, consequently [with the] the simultaneity of the breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5553":["P. I:\nJust as the senses relate to the understanding, so the understanding to reason. The appearances of the former receive in the second |\nunity of understanding through concepts, and concepts in the third faculty unity of reason through ideas (through prosyllogisms a higher subject is always found, until finally no other can be found any more, of which the previous one would be predicate; just so with conditioned inferences, but there the prosyllogism proves the minor).\nThe cognitions a priori, which go to the objects themselves, are exhausted in the transcendental analytic and are the categories of the understanding. Thus reason cannot go to the objects (appearances) themselves, but to the concepts of the understanding of them. How indeed should a concept of reason (if there are any such) be objective, since it [the condition] expresses not the unity of (g possible) experience (as that through which all objects are given to us), but that of the cognitions of understanding. Meanwhile, however, cognitions of reason that contain entirely a priori and not empirical unity would have to be borrowed from the logical form of reason, except that they would have to concern a synthetic unity, to which the concepts of the understanding would be subjected. and thereby go mediately to a particular [unity] determination of the unity of appearances. Because reason seeks to bring cognitions of understanding (judgments) under higher (universal) conditions, [so can] so [far as] long as such a cognition can still be regarded as conditioned: so one can say that it is a *principium* of the use of the understanding, [in the series of] to find the unconditioned for the conditioned. [This unconditioned now (g which contains the rule) in all concepts of relation of the understanding For] The concepts of relation, however, are nothing other than the [connec] unity of the conditioned and its condition, [as] and reason raises this relation only up to the condition that is itself unconditioned. — Thus all cognitions of reason will be parallel to the three kinds of syllogisms. and more of them will not be possible. The principle of pure reason: all conditioned cognition stands not only under conditions, but finally under such as are themselves unconditioned, may be a mere |\npetition or a postulate (which we do not yet want to decide): so it is still at least the ground of all application of reason, which we at least approach. Every syllogism is nothing other than [the cognition] a judgment by means of the subsumption of its condition under a universal rule, which is therefore the condition of the condition of the proposition of the conclusion. The conditioned syllogism is called so improperly, for there are more conditions of judgments than merely the ground with regard to the sequel (*conditio consequentiae*). There is also a condition of inherence etc etc.\nThe proposition: if the conditioned is given, then the whole series of all conditions is also given, through which the conditioned is determined, is, [in da] if I abstract from the objects or take them merely intellectually, correct.\n––––––––––\nThe unconditioned can never be given, but must always be in thought. Hence ideas. The absolute totality of the conditions is the only unconditioned. Unity of the principle for reason. approximation. Rules of the synthesis of the subordination of the empirical concepts.\nP. II:\nSystem of the transcendental ideas. 1. The titles are 3 according to the three kinds of syllogisms; of the dialectical inferences there are 4 according to the 4 categories. In the ideal of pure reason, because there [ein Ha] all categories are together in one idea, we have no need to distinguish them; for it is the *principium* of all possibility, through which then the categories themselves are determined.\nOn the threefold kinds of transcendental illusion. There are two ideas and one ideal.† The paralogism of pure reason is properly a transcendental subreption, in that our judgment about objects [vor] and the unity of consciousness in the same is taken for a perception of the unity of the subject.\n†(g The first illusion is that in which the unity of apperception, which is subjective, is taken for the unity of the subject as a thing. The second: in which the subjective determination of sensibility and its condition is taken for an object. The third: in which the universality of thinking through reason is taken for a thought of an all of the possibilities of things.)\nThere is no deduction of the transcendental ideas except a negative one.\nOn the conclusion of the dialectic: that all dialectical questions be completely answered.\nAll concepts of synthesis require a third: either possible experience or the idea. With regard to the transcendental idea they cannot have objective validity, but can still be deduced as necessary problems or questions.\n––––––––––\nIn the understanding the concepts direct themselves according to possible experience, before reason, however, possible experience according to concepts, just as all virtue in practice must direct itself according to concepts and is possible only thereby. although it never attains the concept. Possible experience in the regress to the conditions directs itself according to the concepts of reason or transcendental ideas. Therein consists precisely the business of reason, to procure for the use of the understanding in the greatest manifoldness unconditioned unity. That concept of reason which combines the greatest particular unity with this universal one agrees with possible experience and is so far a correct rule. But the concept cannot be objectively valid which does not (g con-)sist in (g the) relation to possible experience.\nThe ideal must be thoroughly determined, for the idea is the |\nstandard. It is the unmutilated being of a thing and wherein, as members of the disjunction, all possibilities lie.\n––––––––––\nAll three transcendental ideas cohere through a syllogism. Namely: All objects of the senses are ultimately grounded on a (g existing) noumenon.\n––––––––––\nReason serves to give to the understanding [Ausbreitung in der] necessity and to the sphere of its use domain and unity.\nThe way of regarding the particular as a determination of the universal (of the true universal, which is not drawn as induction from the particular), is a unity a priori, which is entirely different from the unity of experience.\nP. III:\n(g Lucian's Writings. First Part. Zürich, at Gesner. 1769.)\nThe system of the dialectical syllogisms rests on their unity among one another in one syllogism, in which 1. subjective unity of all representations, i.e. unity of the subject, second [collective] synthetic unity of the object or of the appearance, [dritt] this either of the appearances: collective, or of the thoughts of objects in general: distributive (disjunctive).\nReason [seeks] cognizes first the universal in abstracto, then it seeks for the particular the universal, in order to infer from this in turn to the particular.\nFirst section: on the [transsc:] ideas in general.\n––––––––––\n1. Remark. In the syllogisms, if the major is a proposition of experience [begrif] (All human beings are mortal), then that is nonetheless a universal concept. But since it is so only from induction, it is nonetheless no cognition from concepts. Thus such are nonetheless finally demanded by reason, so that true universality may arise; for from |\nthis alone does reason want to hold something for determined in itself and necessary.\n2. The analytic inferences are indeed from concepts; but the major is a universal identical judgment, which expresses not unity of the diverse, but of the harmonious and identical.\n––––––––––\nThe unconditioned subjective conditions of thinking.\nThe unconditioned (g objective) condition of appearances.\nThe unconditioned objective condition of all objects in general.\n––––––––––\nUnder the idea I understand a concept that is sufficiently grounded in reason, but to which no [corres] object can be given in any possible experience.\nTo what then does the idea go, and why is it grounded in reason, in order to relate itself to objects. The idea of the soul is grounded on this, that the understanding must relate all [Er] thoughts and inner perceptions to the I and assume this as the one persisting subject, so that the most complete unity of [Erk] self-cognition may come out.\nThe idea of the unconditioned for all conditions of appearance is grounded in reason as a prescription, to seek the completeness of all cognition of understanding in subordination.\nThe idea of the unconditioned unity of all objects of thinking in an *ens entium* is necessary, in order to seek the affinity among everything possible and thereby both thoroughgoing connection and unity of the principle.\nIn right, mere concepts of reason are necessary, in morals, however, ideas. To the former a congruent object can be given in experience, to the latter not, because they contain the highest unity of reason of freedom agreeing with itself and with all ends of its determination.\nLegislation has need of ideas, [ob sie] and these can never be carried out completely. They are not on that account, however, void and superfluous.\nTranscendental philosophy requires ideas just as necessarily as morals.\nPlato's hyperbolic elevation of the ideas as archetypes in the highest intelligence, when they are personified, is not to be censured; they are the standard of the things, which limit one another and individually do not fulfill their end, so that no experience is congruent with it.\nP. IV:\nWhether the inference from the human soul through the concepts of the world to God and from this so back to the intelligible life of the soul does not proceed?\nWhence, however, comes the dialectical illusion in the transcendental ideas. That which makes all illusion: namely the confusion of the subjective conditions of our thinking with the objective. This we cannot avoid, because we must think an object unconditioned and have no other way of thinking it than only that which the particular constitution of our subject brings with it. We can nonetheless unravel the rationalizing inferences either in form or in the minor or major, because the synthesis goes further than the data and the kind of inference permit. The supposition of pure reason is, if one presupposes what one is to prove, and proves it through the consequences. Namely the thoroughly determined a priori is presupposed, in order thereby to represent to oneself the thoroughgoing determination of everything possible.\nIf I assume: I am no appearance of inner sense, but a thing in itself and noumenon, then my inhering *accidentia* must also be noumena. *atqvi*: I represent to myself the series of the conditions in the appearance; therefore they must be given in me in themselves. This conclusion, made into the minor in the episyllogism, reads: If all appearances are given in One in themselves, then the idea [von der durchga], which determines the things in themselves, is given in some intelligence.\n(g One says that something is a mere idea, when one cannot even approach it.)\n1. The unconditioned of inherence (g or of the aggregate). 2. That of the [conseqventz] dependence or of the series. 3. That of the concurrence of all possibilities to one and of one to all.\n(g Just as the syllogisms determine a judgment in the universal through subsumption, so reason determines the objects in the totality of the conditions.)\nIn transcendental psychology the sameness, simplicity and modality of passive existence flows from the one concept of substance.\nThe absolute is as much as the unconditioned, this as the completed, which is thought negatively without restricting condition.\nWe can think the universal only through the separation of all restricting conditions. The abstraction from the determinations of the self makes the I seem unconditioned.\n(g How can morals rest on ideas, since one nevertheless demands that the actions are to be in conformity with them. One can only call them concepts of reason. [The sage is an idea]. In their whole purity they are ideas. Justice.)\n––––––––––\nWe can call reason the faculty of ideas‡. There are ideas of sensibility, also those of pure reason. These are either practical or speculative; the latter are transcendental ideas. These are necessary concepts of reason, to which no object can be given in the senses. As (g pure) concepts, however, they must nonetheless be taken from the categories; as concepts of reason they must, however, be merely inferred; as necessary concepts of reason they must contain the necessary conditions of the whole use of the understanding, i.e. of the use in its totality, and as transcendent concepts this totality must go so far that it surpasses all sensible intuition; for otherwise they would be immanent. For this the absolute is required etc etc. How does the transcendental syllogism read?\n‡(g Reason is a faculty, to determine a to the whole domain of its condition. If I say: Caius is mortal, so I consider first mortality in the [ihrem] whole domain [so fe] of the concept, under which Caius is contained, i.e. human beings, and subsume the former [weiter] in this domain, in order to determine it therein.\n1. The unconditioned unity (g of the synthesis) of the subject. 2. The unconditioned unity of the synthesis of the conditions in the appearance. 3. The unconditioned unity of the synthesis of thinking in general. All through the four categories, so far as they indicate unconditioned unity.\nThe transcendental idea can have nothing but the powers of cognition as object or representations in general in relation to them. Thus 1. the apperception, second the apprehension of the appearance, 3. the concept of the understanding in general. The first is concept of reason of the subject, the second of the object, so far as it can be given, the third of the object of thinking in general.\nThat pure reason has no objective content of its dialectical inferences.)\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand and Object (obiect) are both rendered 'object'. Schluß = 'inference'; Vernunftschluß = 'syllogism'; Erkentnis/Erkenntnis = 'cognition'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Vermögen = 'faculty'; Kräfte (Erkenntniskräfte) = 'powers'.\n- Sache (an sich selbst) = 'thing', Ding also 'thing'. Ausbreitung, Geschäfte, Richtmaas ('standard'), Vorschrift ('prescription') are non-glossary choices. Warnehmung = 'perception' (Wahrnehmung).\n- Footnote asterisks of the original are rendered † and ‡ so as not to collide with italics markup; the footnote at P. III–IV ('Caius') is open-ended and its closing parenthesis falls at the end.\n- Doubtful/corrupt: 'ein dem Ganzen umfange seiner Bedingung zu bestimmen' (final footnote) is ungrammatical as written; rendered literally ('a to the whole domain'). 'Das die reine Vernunft' stands for 'Daß'. 'selbst unbedingt sein' (plural 'sein' for 'seien') rendered 'are'.\n- Ambiguity kept: 'Der Grundsatz ... mag eine bloße Petition oder ein Postulat seyn' (the clause is left unattached to a main verb, as in the German); 'welche ... Regel' ('which' could refer to rule or subsumption).\n- Brackets, (g ...) marks, | and truncated fragments (e.g. [Ausbreitung in der], [von der durchga], [corres]) are carried over as printed, untranslated where the fragment is incomplete. Latin (prosyllogism, *principium*, *conditio consequentiae*, *ens entium*, *atqvi*, *accidentia*, in abstracto, a priori, noumenon) is left in Latin.",[]],"reflm:5554":["S. I:\nThe concepts of reflection can be taken logically, hence merely [intellectual] analytically, or transcendentally, hence synthetically. Sameness and difference intellectual is analytic, but determined sensibly synthetic, and difference of place makes, regardless of the identity of the things in themselves, numerical difference. Agreement and conflict logical rests on the principle of contradiction; in empirical use: two realities that together yield a reality or negation. The inner and outer logical is what is a predicate of the thing itself or of another, empirical: what is in another place of space. The determinable and the determinate: the substantial and the substance.\nOf these the most famous proposition of subreption is: *principium indiscernibilium*.\nOur three higher powers go to unity, truth and perfection through understanding, power of judgment and reason. The latter in the transcendental understanding brings forth all sorts of confusion.\nOne can regard the whole monadology as the *systema*\n... of Leibniz from the concepts of reflection. The inner and outer intellectual taken gives *monas*, because *compositum* is a *totum relationum*, and *repraesentativa*, because the *repraesentationes* are inner, place and extension however outer. Space is nothing but the *phaenomenon* of the outer. But what then do the monads represent, if they merely represent themselves to one another, each thus the representations of the others? (One also cannot with Maupertuis derive the outer of motion from the inner.) Leibniz. *harmonia praestabilita*. Because the monads do not stand in *commercio* by virtue of coexistence in space.\nS. II:\nNoumenon signifies (g properly) everywhere one and the same, namely the transcendental object of sensible intuition (g This however is no real object or given thing, but a concept, in relation to which appearances have unity.), for something must after all correspond to this, even though we know nothing other than the appearance of it. We cannot say, however, that the (g pure) categories have objects; rather they merely determine the transcendental object in relation to our sensibility through the synthesis of the manifold of intuition. Thus [is] no noumenon corresponds to them.\nThe transcendental object, which corresponds to the appearances, (g or also every object) can be called noumenon only insofar as it can be represented through the concept of understanding. Now this is impossible through the |\ncategory, since the conditions of intuition are lacking, thus we have no concepts of noumena.\nWe can use these concepts according to an analogy with the sensible [think]; but, because they have objective validity only in relation to the synthetic unity of apprehension in time, they therefore relate in themselves to no object at all and under the sensible determination only to *phaenomena*.",[]],"reflm:5555":["χ−θ? (ψ?)\nRemark of Kant on the back of the undated letter from Glave (X 243 [2 X 260]) in vol. II of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant p. 273:\nJust as a pure concept of understanding arises only through the form of judgments, in that I make them synthetic (g and thereby think an object), so a pure concept of reason arises through the form of a syllogism. This however is the subsumption under the universality [of a] of the condition of a judgment; thus the concept is a representation of the totality of conditions, according to one or another relation of a judgment, to cognize an object. The logical condition of the judgment is the relation to the subject etc.; the concept of a thing through this logical function is the category. The [totality] universality of the [condition] relation is the logical form of the syllogism; the concept of a thing through the [subsu] representation of the totality of the condition [of the judgment] of the (g application of) categories is the concept of reason.\nThe concept of the totality of the synthesis according to the categories of relation is the pure concept of reason.\nWithout the concept of reason we would indeed have experiences, but the collective unity of experience would be lacking, as that in which after all all [part] empirical cognition must be determinable.\nThe totality of the synthesis in a subject, 2. in a series, thirdly in a system. *Suppositum* is the presupposition of that which is to be found, and the conditions of it, in order to find the synthesis |\nof the cognitions, whereby we are to approach it. Idea is such a *suppositum*, which indeed cannot be given in itself, but can nevertheless bring formal unity of reason into our cognition.",[]],"reflm:5556":["Analytic possibility rests on the principle of contradiction and is the possibility of the concept; synthetic possibility, which must be added, i.e. that an object can correspond to the concept, on this, that something be in accordance with the conditions of an object of experience (g in general). (g Only in experience can an object be given.)\nThe actual is opposed to the impossible and at the same time to the merely possible.",[]],"reflm:5557":["Possibility, actuality and necessity are not determinations, but modality of the position of the thing with its predicates.",[]],"reflm:5558":["The propositions of modality, e.g. A is possible (*realiter*) (g Actual etc etc.) are synthetic propositions. but not to the concept of the thing, but to thinking in general something is added.",[]],"reflm:5559":["That possibility (g of a thing) from which all possibility must be regarded as derived, is presupposed absolutely, consequently regarded as *positio absoluta* and as an existing thing, and there holds the |\ninference from the *esse* of a thing to the *posse* of all others, hence also the *posse* in general presupposes an *esse*. From the possibility of things in general one can infer to a determinate thing as ground.",[]],"reflm:5560":["Unity of the hypothesis consists (g 1.) in this, that, if I assume something as ground, the manifold can be derived from it. Unity.\n2. That I can assume this [unity] ground or concept and it therefore has reality. Truth.\n3. That the given sequels all together agree with the one ground, consequently no *hypothesis subsidiaria* is necessary. Perfection.\nHypothesis of a spiritual nature of the soul:\n1. That the *phaenomena* of thinking can be explained from it and the independence from the body.\n2. That something of this sort also has reality, consequently synthetic possibility; for the analytic is clear. Since that, however, would have to be cognized from the analogy of experience, which however it lacks, truth drops out; *ens rationis ratiocinantis*.\n3. That all *phaenomena* together can be derived from it, as many as we know of them, and thus agree with this ground as the sole one. But this fails; for the dependence of the soul on mechanism requires yet another hypothesis, namely the influence of matter on a spirit. Likewise the concept of God.\nIn the distinction of the idea (g *ratiocinatae*) from a chimera truth is sooner than [perfection] order, in the difference of experience from dream order is sooner than truth and a criterion of the same, order of nature.\nFormal or material unity, truth and perfection. The unity of a thing or unities.",[]],"reflm:5561":["These concepts are not the categories [but] of things, but logical criteria of agreement with the laws of understanding.\n1. Unity of the concept, that it be not [still on] another than this one. or unity of the meaning of the word — Clarity.\n2. Truth of the elementary propositions (multiplicity of them) — Distinctness.\n3. Completeness of the marks in a definition — Thoroughness and precision.",[]],"reflm:5562":["The transcendental concepts go to the relation of a multitude of representations to an object. 1. Unity of the consciousness of something; 2. combination of the representations among one another in one consciousness; 3. the representation of the object arising from it.\nThe three concepts of the possibility of a thing [as] in general: unity, truth and perfection relate to the three formal principles of all judgments: that of contradiction, of sufficient ground and of the determinability of the same with regard to all possible predicates (*judicia categorica, hypothetica et disjunctiva*).",[]],"reflm:5563":["[Logical] Criterion of empirical truth. Order of nature or. the order in itself, i.e. combination according to rules, proves the relation to an object and not merely arbitrariness.",[]],"reflm:5564":["Whether it is possible that nothing at all exist?",[]],"reflm:5565":["Analytic possibility does not yet prove the synthetic. The non-being of a thing is without contradiction. From this I cannot yet infer that it is *realiter* possible that it not be.\nPossibility and necessity of propositions or of things.",[]],"reflm:5566":["The necessary is (g so far) determinable in only a single way (*oppositum est impossibile*); the absolutely necessary is only a single one; otherwise, if there were more, one could cease, without all existence thereby being abolished.",[]],"reflm:5567":["That which [not] without real condition is necessary can nevertheless be necessary as condition, which is nevertheless no absolute, but restrictive necessity.\nAbsolute dominion can nevertheless be conditioned, but not restricted.",[]],"reflm:5568":["Necessary is that whose existence can be cognized *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5569":["Necessity is either logical or real. Analytic and synthetic. Synthetic necessity [from] either from concepts or intuitions or the relation of concepts in general |\nto intuitions. Necessary existence is either derived or original. *Necessitas originaria vel derivativa*. Necessity as condition is properly hypothetical, but yet not conditioned; it is necessity as presupposition.\nThe necessity of a thing in itself is that of existence, not of the relation of a predicate to the subject or of the conditioned to the condition. Existence does not belong necessarily to a thing necessary in itself. For then it lies in the concept; rather it is the condition for positing an existence in general.",[]],"reflm:5570":["Hypothetical necessity is to be distinguished from the necessity of a hypothesis, which is opposed to postulated necessity, which is absolute. The former is the necessity of a premise, the latter of a principle of cognition. (g The absolute necessity of a thing is a necessary hypothesis of reason.)\nLogical necessity: that whose opposite is logically impossible from concepts, i.e. contradicts itself. Real necessity: that whose opposite is a nonthing, i.e. whose not-being cannot synthetically subsist together with a concept. The former the necessity of judgments, the latter of things.",[]],"reflm:5571":["A thing in general as limited is to that extent in itself undetermined with regard to many predicates; but as unlimited it is thoroughly determined.\nBut as an individual (this thing) it is also thoroughly determined, only not from the concept of a thing in general.",[]],"reflm:5572":["Bottom margin\nReal necessity is the necessity of the existence of things, logical necessity the necessity of the connection of cognitions, e.g. in judging, likewise the necessity of a hypothesis for the contingent.",[]],"reflm:5573":["At the top of the page:\nOur concept of possibility is properly derived; namely it presupposes something given, which (g agrees) with the conditions of the understanding as to form. Only a single thing is originally possible, namely the *realissimum*, and this is presupposed.",[]],"reflm:5574":["Above and \nWhether something can be determined in another way or not, we see either from the *principium contradictionis*; and then, if it [cannot in another] can be determined, i.e. does not contradict itself, real contingency does not follow;\nor from alterations: but there also not.",[]],"reflm:5575":["*Nulla mutatio est absolute necessaria*, it always has another cause. Hence no *absolute necessarium* can be *mutabile*.",[]],"reflm:5576":["How is alteration in general possible. This cannot be explained, because it is determination in time and presupposes forces, but these a posteriori.\n[All] Alteration is not an arising or passing away of things but of their determinations, while the thing remains. E.g. motion is alteration of the relation of the body, not of the body itself. Inner motion is alteration of the body.",[]],"reflm:5577":["Mere form without reality (g thought as thing in itself) is *ens imaginarium*. Space.\nMere negation without reality is *non ens*.",[]],"reflm:5578":["Realities in appearance (experience) can indeed conflict with one another, but not in *noumenis*, because in these the *oppositum* of reality must be thought a priori, consequently cannot be otherwise than logical, i.e. negation.",[]],"reflm:5579":["Opposite and \nBecause an *ens partim reale, partim negativum* is always thought as a defect (*quoad entitatem* in general), but this presupposes the concept of the *completum*, the concept of the *realissimum* lies at the ground of all things. The *realissimum* is also represented through its concept as *singulare*, and all its determinations indeed only generically and not specifically breaks off?",[]],"reflm:5580":["To each reality a there corresponds its opposite =0 and its counterplay (*contrarium*) –a, i.e. a ground that, connected with a, becomes =0. Pleasure, pain, indifference; truth, error, ignorance. But light, darkness and deprivation of light through dark bodies, i.e. shadow. Virtue, vice, *adiaphoron*. In general: cause, inefficacy (inertia) and hindrance. Benefit, harm, being indifferent.",[]],"reflm:5581":["The quantitative [disposition] decomposition, division of the homogeneous, is to be distinguished from the qualitative (g decomposition) of the heterogeneous or from mixture (of what is different as to matter). Both go to infinity. Water, for example, consists of [infinite] matters, which again consist of others to infinity. No chemical elements.",[]],"reflm:5582":["(g There is no *quantum discretum*, but *quantitatem discretam*. Number.)\nThe *quanta* are different with regard to quantity, the same with regard to quality, namely of decomposition without absolutely first parts. Yet the *quantum* of absolute position or reality (g sensation) is distinguished therein from other *quanta*, in that the former vanishes into nothing, but the latter leave a positive boundary, although no part remaining. Boundaries of space (threefold) and of time (one). The generation of reality has a moment, that of the *quanti extensivi* an (g *quasi*) element: differential. (The former is to be regarded like the line that describes a surface in a time [with accelera].) As a different moment |\ngenerates different degrees of velocity, so difference of impression a different degree of sensation. Intellectual, however, a reality is not [as] to be regarded as a multitude of different ones, is* however in continuous interconnection with nothing; division of the parts.\n*(g We have 3 *quanta*. Space, time and sensation (motion, reality). The first has a positive of the boundary, which is *quantum*; the second, which is no *quantum*, the third nothing positive at all and no boundary, but limitations. Reality taken transcendentally is not of different kind.)",[]],"reflm:5583":["Mathematics as synthetic cognition a priori grounds its possibility on this, that it can construct its concepts; for it has to do only with space and time, [which] in which objects of intuition can be given a priori. But these are *quanta*, therefore it is a science of *quanta*. But it also considers quantity by means of number, by means of multitude, which can be constructed in time through counting. (g This science yet cannot go further than to the world of sense, because only for this can intuition a priori be given.)",[]],"reflm:5584":["Possibility of the construction of concepts, since therefore intuition is given through synthesis a priori.",[]],"reflm:5585":["*Principium* of the possibility of mathematics as a synthetic cognition a priori. It is the synthesis in intuition a priori, i.e. space and time. Pure mathematics.\n*Principium* of the mathematical cognition of appearances: Every appearance has as intuition its extensive magnitude and as sensation its degree. For (as concerns the latter) every sensation arises from not-being, because it is a modification. Thus through alteration. But all alteration passes from 0 to a through infinitely [small] many small steps.",[]],"reflm:5586":["The magnitude of that which is represented as multitude or as unity.",[]],"reflm:5587":["Some concepts cannot be constructed, such as the concept of reality, of cause etc. etc. The magnitude of something, insofar as it is represented as multitude, is extensive; as unity intensive. Space and time are extensive. Velocity intensive. Magnitude of the ground.",[]],"reflm:5588":["48'. E II 615.\nM 48:\nThe magnitude of a thing, insofar as it is not intuited as composite*, whose arising and passing away, however, can be regarded as composite (in time), is a degree.\nM 48':\n*(g because its parts are not outside one another in space.\nIntellectual magnitude is that of the ground.)",[]],"reflm:5589":["1. Possibility of (g pure) mathematics.\n2. [Application] Possibility of applied mathematics. For all things (g as appearances) have a magnitude: extensive and intensive. (g Through this mathematics gets objective reality. It does not concern *entia rationis*.)\n3. All things as objects of the pure understanding also have a magnitude, namely metaphysical, but no transcendental magnitude, because there they are compared as things in general with all others, but here as concept (g of the thing) with the essence of the thing itself.\nThe possibility of natural science rests on the dynamical principles, namely those that concern the existence of things in combination. The former concerned only the (g empirical) intuition and the possibility [according to] of empirical concepts of the objects, but not insofar as they belong to a nature.",[]],"reflm:5590":["All properties of things have a degree, but the thing itself (substance) does not.\n(g All intensive magnitude must yet in the end be brought to extensive.)\n(g Inner) possibility [in itself] has no degree, but the possibility in connection does. The possibility in all connection (absolute) has the highest degree; [ab] on the other hand the conditioned possibility, which takes place only in some connection, has a degree that is smaller by as much as there are many conditions that belong to it. E.g. many throws belong to hitting a terne.\nConnection is not always condition. Contentment is possible in solitude, in business, in want, in sickness. But these are not its restrictive conditions.\nConnection takes place either with its condition, which, when it is complete, is called complete possibility; or with other grounds, i.e. circumstances.",[]],"reflm:5591":["If a magnitude is given as a thing in itself, then the whole precedes the *composition*, and there I cannot, because this |\ncomposition can never be completed [k] and thus the *qvantitas* of it can never be wholly cognized, conclude that such an [qua] infinite *qvantum* is impossible. It is only impossible for us, according to our way of measuring magnitudes, to cognize it entirely, because it is immeasurable. From this it does not follow that another understanding could not cognize the *qvantum* as such a whole without measuring.\nJust so with the division.",[]],"reflm:5592":["In appearances alone can a first in time only be sought, and in them there is nevertheless no first. But a first of the ground in general must be sought in the intellectual.",[]],"reflm:5593":["Mathematics has the particular feature that it (g only) goes to such objects as can be represented *coram intuitu*. and thus it always has empirical confirmation.\nIt is possible (g as) a synthetic cognition *a priori*, because there are two *intuitus a priori*: space and time, in which a synthesis of composition is possible *a priori*. These two objects are *quanta* and indeed *originaria*, and the mere synthesis of them is the *qvantitaet*. All concepts of *quanta* can be constructed in them, i.e. given *a priori* in intuition, likewise all concepts of *qvantitaet*, i.e. of number, which requires time as well as space. The *universale* is here given in the *singulari* in intuition [weighed] and in the *singulari* the universal of the synthesis is considered. In the case of *qvalitaeten* this does not work. Through discursive cognitions no mathematics can arise. Mathematical cognitions as cognitions of reason *a priori* are apodictic and as *intuitus* demonstrative, both together evident.\nMathematics is distinguished from philosophy not through the |\nobject (both consider magnitude), but through the *modum cognoscendi*. This, however, determines the difference of the objects. (dynamical *principium*.)\n(s Philosophy deals with magnitudes as far as one can get on through mere concepts, and mathematics with qualities as far as mere intuition reaches. E.g. the former with the question of the simple parts, the latter with the cause of the gravitation of the moon.)",[]],"reflm:5594":["93. E II 892.\nWe have of the *ens contingens* no other concept than that it is one whose not-being can be thought. But then everything is logically contingent. Really [*realiter*] contingent would be that whose not-being is in itself impossible, although it can be thought; but of this we have no concept, it is problematic. Substances we represent to ourselves as *entia a se* together with their states. Ends are *entia ab alio*.\nWe have to do only with appearances and not with *entibus per se*. Thus it is only according to the contingency in the appearance that the question is, |\nM 93: |\ni.e. that of what happens.",[]],"reflm:5595":["(s The *principium nexus finalis* is called: everything is good;\nThe *principium nexus effectivi*: everything has its natural ground.\nThe supreme cause of the *causarum finalium* is God;\nThe supreme cause of the *causarum efficientium*: nature.)\nAn effect whose concept can be through reason cause (*causa efficiens*) [of] the same, is the end, and the *nexus* of such effects or [of] effecting causes is *nexus finalis*.\nThe end is objectively, the effect (g of an action) subjectively the cause of the same.",[]],"reflm:5596":["Principles of the understanding are principles of the exposition of experience. Principles of reason are those according to which experience itself is given (through the understanding) *a priori* (freedom and necessary being). Rationalizing principles: where the subjective conditions of reason are taken for objective. Principles of reason are transcendental maxims (g of speculation). Rationalizing ones are transcendental paralogisms.\ntranscendental ideas of the reason determining itself *a priori*.\nThe sophistical principle of reason is: what does not stand (g wholly) under the conditions of empirical determination is false. Thus all synthesis of magnitudes that is without end is impossible; all dynamical synthesis that is not mediately determined (freedom and necessity), impossible. On the other hand, the possibility of the idea of reason is nevertheless also not to be seen into.",[]],"reflm:5597":["The concepts of the world, of the simple, of freedom and of the supreme cause are nothing but concepts of reason, because they cannot be shown *in concreto* in experience. Their principles are therefore not principles of the empirical*, but of the transcendent use of rea|son;\nnevertheless they lie in the progress of reason to the completion of the synthesis.\ndistributive or collective unity of experiences in general. All experience is not the All of experience, and the whole of possible experience is no object of experience. But hereby there nevertheless occurs that, [through which] what the condition of the possibility of a whole is.\n*(g not to expound experiences, but [a pri] to determine them in an idea through restriction of the synthetic whole. 1. Progress to the synthetic unity of the parts. Beginning of the synthetic whole.)",[]],"reflm:5598":["*Principium commercii*.\nAll combination of things, insofar as they are simultaneous, is a *commercium*. For insofar as they are simultaneous, i.e. I can go over from A to B just as well as from B to A and each can be *conseqvens* in the synthesis of the other, they determine one another reciprocally, i.e. are in *commercio*. On the other hand, if [only] I could determine B only through A and not A through B, then B follows A.\nEvery thing has, with regard to the coexisting, a relation of community. In the temporal sequence nothing can be determined reciprocally in the same meaning.",[]],"reflm:5599":["Uppermost on the page. For the conclusion of the Ontologiε?\nThe principles of the possibility of experience are independent of experience. The connection of the principles *a priori* (necessary) with experience is either this, that they depend on experiences, or these on them.",[]],"reflm:5600":["Next to and \nWe call nature the object of possible experience. Thus all our cognition *a priori* goes only to nature.\nPrinciples of the understanding are rules *a priori*, which contain the conditions of the synthetic unity of possible experience.",[]],"reflm:5601":["We have up to here spoken of nothing but concepts whose objects can be given to us in the senses, except in the concepts of the *ens realissimum* (most real being) and of the simple (*monas*).\nNow we come to concepts of the unconditioned totality of the synthesis (concept of the world).\nThe [whole] composite of substances as absolute whole (*est vel mundus sensibilis vel intelligibilis*, it is either a sensible or an intelligible world).",[]],"reflm:5602":["The use of the concepts of the understanding was immanent, that of ideas as concepts of objects is transcendent; but as regulative principles of the completion and at the same time of the determination of the limitations of our cognition they are critically immanent.",[]],"reflm:5603":["We have in the ontology spoken of concepts of the understanding whose use is possible in experience, because they themselves make experience possible.",[]],"reflm:5604":["Cosmological ideas rest on the thought of the absolute totality of the conditions in a whole, which is just thereby unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5605":["Opposite and \nThe doctrine of monads must not pass over into physics.\nAre bodies then substances or [not] accidents in themselves? They are neither the one nor the other; for they are not things at all or objective reality, but are appearances, of which the doctrine of monads does not hold. Something substantial may lie at their ground.",[]],"reflm:5606":["Atomistics, although it is very arbitrary, is nevertheless more suited to philosophy than monadistics. The former gives mechanical explanations from figure and laws of motion, the latter metaphysical ones from merely intellectual concepts, which cannot be *principia* of the *phaenomena*.\nThe *principia physica* are either mechanical or dynamical.",[]],"reflm:5607":["All possible objects of experience have their nature, partly their particular one, partly the common one with other things. Nature taken substantively signifies: the sum total of all objects of experience. [The] Nature rests on forces (fundamental forces) and is in general the lawfulness of appearances.\nNature is to chance — —\nto freedom — —\nto fate }\n}\n}\nopposed.\nThings are not appearances in themselves, but merely because there are beings that have senses; just so they belong to a nature, |\nbecause we have understanding. For the word nature also does not signify something in things in themselves, but only the [unity] order of their appearances through the unity of the concepts of the understanding or the unity of consciousness in which they can be combined.\nWe do not have understanding because there is a nature; for we could never know its rules (g laws) from experience; [sie best] their necessity consists precisely in this, that they are cognized *a priori*.\nPrecisely for this reason we can have cognitions *a priori* of appearances as well as of nature, in which they are connected, because the form of our sensibility lies at the ground of the former and the form of our understanding of the second as principle of possibility.\nTo say: »we can determine *a priori* the constitution of things« and yet at the same time: »these things have such constitution independently of our faculty to determine it« is a contradiction; for where do we then get our cognition from?",[]],"reflm:5608":["Things are represented as appearances, because there are beings that have senses. The same beings, however, also have understanding, under whose laws the appearances stand, insofar as their possible consciousness must necessarily agree with a generally valid consciousness, i.e. they have a nature.\nAppearances stand to one another in the relation of the manifold [der] in a pure sensible intuition, and their consciousness in the relation to a common apperception, both *a priori* and necessarily.\n1. Nature taken generally and *formaliter*:\nNature of a thing; [der] corporeal and thinking nature.\n2. *Materialiter*: as sum total of appearances in opposition to the intelligible world.\nNature is opposed to blind chance (contingency) and to blind necessity (fate).\nTo the latter, if I do not assume a cause outside the world, miracles belong.\n(*non datur abyssus, saltus, casus, hiatus.*)\nNatural necessity opposed to the supernatural.\nNature is not opposed to freedom, but distinguished from it.\nFreedom is the independence of causality from the conditions of space and time, thus [von] the causality of the thing as thing in itself. Natural mechanism and freedom do not conflict with each other, because causality is not taken in one sense.\n*Casus* is absolute contingency. *Fatum* the unconditioned necessity in the world.\n(g All laws cognized through experience belong to heteronomy, but those through which experience in general is possible, to autonomy.)\nM 143':\nThe infinity or finitude of the composition or division must be assumed on both sides in the world of sense. Yet these ideas of absolute totality belong to the concept of things in themselves; therefore both are false. By contrast the concept of natural necessity and that of freedom: of these the first belongs to the world of sense, the second to the intelligible, and the idea of totality belongs to the things themselves, which the idea of freedom, if it were counted as belonging to the world of sense, might contradict; but now, as belonging to the intelligible world, both can be true.\nThe causality of a being, to think itself, with regard to appearances, as independent of determining grounds of the world of sense, is no contradiction, if [man] the being is only given under a concept of a thing in itself. Now a rational being as intelligence is given as such; consequently freedom can be thought in it. By contrast no causality can be thought of the intelligible of bodies, for their appearances betray no intelligence; |\nthus no freedom can be thought of their *substrato intelligibili* either, and we know it through not a single predicate.\nWe need freedom only for the objectively practical from grounds *a priori*.\nThe faculty of beginning an occurrence absolutely (without the causality itself beginning).\nIn bodies we do indeed have a representation of the intelligible, but do not know the same as cause. In intelligences the intelligible is presupposed according to causality; here the question is how something can be a cause.\nAntinomy.",[]],"reflm:5609":["*Maxima realitas phaenomenon* (greatest reality of appearances) is *mundus perfectissimus* (most perfect world): *compositum realissimum* (most real composite).\nThe principles of the possibility of experience hold only in the world and do not go to the whole of the world. Hence this can be thought neither as finite nor as infinite.",[]],"reflm:5610":["Opposite and \n*In Mundo non datur abyssus*, i.e. there is nothing unconditioned in the world of sense, for this belongs to the intelligible; the senses, however, [verlie], when they assume space and time for determinations of things, [go] here into the infinitely empty or full.\n*In mundo non datur saltus*, i.e. all appearances are generated through all things' degrees from 0 to something.\nWere time and space conditions of the existence of things in themselves, one could not say in the mathematical antinomy: both opposites are false, for one would have to be true; nor [both can] |\nin the dynamical: both can be true, for one would have to be false. Now the contradiction is encountered only because space and time as things in themselves are nonetheless supposed to contain an unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5611":["Reason is not in the chain of appearances and is, with regard to all of them, free with regard to its own causality (the actions of reason itself are also not appearances, but only its effects are). Were everything determined through reason, everything would be necessary, but also good. Were it determined through sensibility, nothing would be evil or good, nothing practical at all. Now the actions are for the most part occasioned through sensibility, but not entirely determined; for reason must give a *complement* of sufficiency. Reason gradually draws sensibility in the *habitus*, excites incentives and forms thereby a character, which however is itself to be attributed to freedom and is itself not sufficiently grounded in the appearances. Therefore all actions hang together well with laws of sensibility, but not with omissions of the understanding, whose opposite is always possible according to all grounds of sensibility. If one now asks whether the understanding itself, in itself as well as with regard to what it does or omits, is not determined, we must say: not a single possible experience, because it would still always be only appearance, can give this. The understanding is itself no object of sensible intuition. Beyond experience, however, determinability through other causes does not hold, because beyond this boundary the understanding can only be represented as cause (supreme understanding) and the concept of cause serves only for the principle of the synthesis of appearances, but not of the actions of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:5612":["277. E II 1552.\nM 277':\nWe explain committed free actions according to laws of the nature of the human being, but we do not thereby cognize them as determined; otherwise we would not regard them as contingent and demand that they should and must have happened otherwise. In free actions reason flows in not merely as a comprehending, but as an effecting and driving *principium*. How it does not merely rationalize and judge, but takes the place of a natural cause, we do not understand, much less how it is itself determined through impulses to act or omit. (How the representation of the good in general, which abstracts from my state, is nevertheless effective on my state and how this reflection, which itself contains no affection, can be contained in the series of natural appearance. For the good is a relation of pure reason to object. We must therefore regard future actions as undetermined through everything that belongs to *phaenomenis*. Reason makes use of the constitution of nature according to its laws as incentives (honor, peace of mind), but is not thereby determined.\nThe resolution of this is. The interconnection of reason with the *phaenomenis*, with which it is [not at all] supposed to stand in *commercio*, can not at all |\nM 277: |\nbe understood (g they are *heterogenea*). The true activity of reason and its *effect* belongs to the *mundus intelligibilis*. Hence we also do not know in what measure we are to impute. Nevertheless we know this much of the influencing might of reason, that it is determined and *necessitated* through no *phaenomena*, but is free, and judge the action merely according to rational laws (in the case of imputation). The actions here in the world are mere schemata of the intelligible; meanwhile these appearances (this word already signifies schema) nonetheless hang together according to empirical laws, if one regards reason itself according to its utterances as a *phaenomenon* (of the character). But what the cause of that is, we do not find in *phaenomenis*. Insofar as one cognizes one's own character only from the *phaenomenis*, one imputes these to oneself, although they are determined in themselves through |\nexternal causes. If one knew it in itself, then all good and evil would be attributable to no external causes, but only to the subject alone together with the good and disadvantageous sequels. In the intelligible world nothing happens and changes, and there the rule of the causal combination drops out.",[]],"reflm:5613":["Every action as appearance has its determining ground in another positive or negative action of mine, this in turn in another, and so on to infinity. There is thus no complete ground at all among the appearances, thus always only a necessity conditioned under my own liking (to apply or to avert this or that attention). This condition, however, because it always remains [so] and because it is the condition of one's own self-activity, is never an externally conditioned necessity. The same action, however, as *noumenon* [determined through my good or evil will] is not under the rule of that which (g in me) happens and must be represented through [the Zurei] another determining ground in connection, except in the case of a good will; there the objective necessity (which is a freedom from physical causes) is at the same time a subjective one. On the other hand, with an evil will, because it is still a will and not nature, all its actions are objectively impossible and subjectively contingent. For this contingency is the condition under which an objective law can be thought, in regard to which an action can be represented as evil. An [evil] action evil in itself, which one ought to omit, is evil precisely because we do it without an objectively sufficient ground; and the will is evil because it is subjectively not determined through precisely the same rule.\nFreedom is the determinability of the power through mere reason. Reason, however, is not a cognition that contains the way in which one is affected by objects; consequently the use of reason is itself freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- Handlung = action; Erscheinung = appearance; Grund = ground; zureichend = sufficient; Gegenstand = object; Erkenntnis = cognition; Kraft = power (here glossed 'power' rather than 'force').\n- '[Zurei]' is a deleted, broken-off word in the German, left untranslated; 'noumenon' is Latin/Greek, italicized.\n- 'Nothwendigkeit' = necessity; 'Zufalligkeit' = contingency; 'bestimbarkeit' = determinability (Kant's spelling).\n- The syntax of 'nicht unter der Regel desienigen, was ... geschieht und durch ... einen andern bestimmenden Grund in Verknüpfung muß vorgestellt werden' is difficult and has been kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:5614":["As appearance not only an action but also its |\nopposite could have its sufficient ground in the *regressus* ([within] between two states), to infinity (through decomposition). But this series of grounds can never be complete and cannot be composed *a priori* from the smallest elements of the ground onward.\n===NOTES===\n- 'regressus' is Kant's Latin; 'decomposition' is Kant's own foreign word, in roman type in the German.\n- 'a priori ... von den kleinsten Elementen des Grundes an' : the phrase 'a priori' could attach to 'composed' or to 'from the smallest elements ... onward'; the English leaves this open.\n- 'Zureichenden Grund' = sufficient ground; Zustand = state.",[]],"reflm:5615":["The pleasure in the action either precedes the law or follows from it. In the latter case it is respect.\n===NOTES===\n- Lust = pleasure; Achtung = respect; Gesetz = law.",[]],"reflm:5616":["279'. E II 1551. 1513.\nM 278':\nEverything that happens is sufficiently determined, but not from appearances, but according to laws of the appearance. For in beings acting freely there is a constant influence of intellectual grounds, since the opposite is possible as appearance. But the action or its opposite will be grounded among the appearances in such a way that only the moment of determination is intellectual. This, however, cannot be used in the empirical explanation, because it is not perceived. For from the intellectual to the determinate action there is an infinite intermediate series of incentives, whose interconnection with the given state can be cognized only according to general laws of possibility. For example: |\nM 279': |\nSomeone entices me to drink, this enticement misleads me [But before this] and can thus be explained according to laws of the senses. The misleading would also be necessary if I were merely animal. Meanwhile it is possible that intellectual choice intervenes, which is exempted from the law of dependence on the senses; this determines only another course of sensibility. This can also be [explained and] connected with the first given state according to laws of nature, but only through an infinite intermediate series of appearances. Thus vice as well as virtue happens according to laws of nature and must be explained accordingly. (Honor, health, reward.) Even the morally good actions from the above incentives, education and temperament. The explanation also has its ground; only the first direction |\nof these causes, the moment of determining them, [is unknown and] is not encountered among the appearances, but also cannot be missed among them, because we cannot observe the appearances up to the moment of their beginning.\nThe law of cause and effect (*caussalitatis*) rests on the condition of the possibility of a unity of experience. This unity cannot fully take place with free beings, except when they are fully intellectual.\nThe higher choice is the faculty of making use of the incentives or sensible stimuli according to their laws, yet always in conformity with the representation of the understanding (in relation to the last and general ends of sensibility). A posteriori, therefore, we will have cause to find the ground of the action, namely the explanatory, but not determining ground of it, in sensibility; but *a priori*, when the action is represented as future (*antecedenter*), we will feel ourselves undetermined toward it and capable of making a first beginning of the series of appearances. If there is free choice, the appearances of rational beings make no *continuum*, except with firm principles of the understanding. Only the actions governed partly by understanding, partly by senses can therefore be explained according to no rule of the one or the other. Before the action we place ourselves merely in the standpoint of the understanding. Since the understanding now is not actually affected, but can affect sensibility: so its action is not predetermined, but determining *spontaneo*; and the opposite of that which happens without understanding could always have happened. Thus the action is contingent only in a conditioned way (under conditions of the understanding), insofar as it is unreasonable.\n===NOTES===\n- Willkühr = choice; Triebfeder = incentive; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Erklärung = explanation; Erklärungsgrund / Bestimmungsgrund = explanatory ground / determining ground; Wesen = being.\n- 'verleitet' / 'Verleitung' = 'misleads' / 'misleading'; 'Reitz' = enticement.\n- 'cannot be missed' renders 'kan ... nicht vermisset werden' (vermissen: to miss, find lacking).\n- 'A posteriori' (roman) is left unitalicized as in the German's Latin-in-roman only where the German prints it so; 'a priori', 'caussalitatis', 'antecedenter', 'continuum', 'spontaneo' are Latin, italicized. 'spontaneo bestimmend' is kept as written.\n- 'E II 1551. 1513.', 'M 278'', 'M 279'' and '|' are editorial apparatus, carried over unchanged.\n- 'Es können also blos die ... regirte Handlungen nach keiner regel ... erklärt werden' is kept in its awkward word order.",[]],"reflm:5617":["Among the appearances everything must be determined, but either according to [laws] pathological or moral laws; if the first, then the opposite is nevertheless possible according to laws of reason, thus the human being free; if the latter, then the subject is also free. The agreement with laws of reason does not belong among appearances, and the transition from |\nsensibility to reason in *regressu* also cannot be observed; but, as much as is observed, everything happens by means of sensible incentives, through which the action is determined.\n===NOTES===\n- Triebfeder = incentive; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Vernunft = reason; 'regressu' is Latin (ablative), italicized.\n- 'sondern, so viel beobachtet wird' kept literal ('but, as much as is observed').",[]],"reflm:5618":["Pure freedom acts according to laws of inwardly determining grounds, but they do not fall in the senses. Animal choice proceeds according to sensibly determinable laws. The mixed human choice (*libertas hybrida*) also acts according to laws, but whose grounds do not [occur] entirely occur in the appearance; hence with the same appearances the same human being can act differently. Here one must first await a character, and then one has a law for explaining the appearances, but never for determining them.\n===NOTES===\n- Willkühr / Wilkühr = choice; rein = pure; Erscheinung = appearance; Charakter = character.\n- '[vorkom] ganzlich vorkommen': the deleted fragment '[vorkom]' is rendered '[occur]'; the reading of the whole clause is as it stands.\n- 'libertas hybrida' is Kant's Latin.",[]],"reflm:5619":["The difficulty regarding freedom is: how a (g absolutely) first action is possible that is not determined through a preceding one. For the latter is required for the unity of the appearances, insofar as it is to give a rule of experience. But if we do not count the actions of reason among the appearances (principle of reason) and the determination through it to action by means of incentives according to laws of sensibility (association, habit): then everything *quoad sensum* is necessary and can be explained according to laws of the appearance. It cannot, however, be predetermined, because reason is a *principium* that does not appear, thus is not given among the appearances. Hence the causes and their relation to action according to laws of sensibility can well be cognized *a posteriori*, but not the determination of them to the *actus*. This interconnection of actions according to laws of the appearance without determinacy through it is a necessary presupposition of practical rules of reason, which in themselves are the cause of a regularity of the appearances, because |\nthey pass over to actions only by means of sensibility. In the appearances there is no *hiatus* for the understanding, but these also cannot be determined *a priori*, i.e. from the absolutely first onward.\nOverall the difficulty here concerns not the lack of the sufficient ground, but only of it among the appearances. If in the higher powers, their omissions or perfections, the action is determined: then it is (g then) not [more] the [Rede] question of the ground of that which happens, but [it shows] what is there at all times, namely the rational will, from which the opposite of the evil was always possible.\nReason determines itself with regard to its concepts, sensibility is determined by the object. Hence the former is also not grounded on conditions of apprehension and apperception, but determines the synthesis *a priori*.\nOne cannot say that the opposite of all our actions must be subjectively possible, in order that one be free (good actions), but only of those arising from sensibility. But even in this case they are determined under sensibility, although taken generally still undetermined. Sensibility and reason do not determine one another, but each works according to its laws; but they direct one another (harmony).\nThe causality of reason is freedom. The determining causality of sensibility: animality.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Zusammenhang' = interconnection; 'Einheit' = unity; 'Triebfeder' = incentive; 'Kräfte' = powers; 'Begriff' = concept; 'Gegenstand' = object; 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection.\n- 'hiatus für den Verstand' (German 'vor den Verstand'): 'vor' with the accusative is rendered 'for'; 'before' is also conceivable.\n- 'Es ist ... nicht [mehr] die [Rede] frage': deleted words '[mehr]' and '[Rede]' are left in brackets untranslated beyond 'more' / the German word, as the sentence is syntactically broken here.\n- 'a priori ... vom absolut ersten an': 'd.i.' = 'i.e.'. 'quoad sensum', 'principium', 'actus', 'hiatus', 'apprehension', 'apperception', 'synthesis' retained as in the source; Latin italicized.\n- 'caussalitaet' = causality (Kant's spelling); 'Thierheit' = animality.",[]],"reflm:5620":["Just as reason judges not through the senses, but yet in relation to them, according to the universal conditions of a cognition in general: so also this same reason not through feeling, but yet in relation to it, according to the conditions of the [Möglich] universal validity of the judgment about satisfaction and dissatisfaction. It is the appetition made universal, as that is the apprehension made universal. No feeling distinguishes right from wrong, but reason sketches the conditions under which alone a rule takes place for judging about this. The satisfaction in regularity is actu|ally\na satisfaction in the ground of constancy and security with regard to everything agreeable that is intended through the use of freedom. It is also a pleasure over the use of reason and the happiness wrested from blind choice.",[]],"reflm:5621":["We must indeed (by virtue of time) transpose the soul into the world of sense, but we cannot transpose it into the corporeal world, but only this into its representation. Hence the soul as such has no place in the human body, and its place is the place of the body in the world. Hence no *sedes animae in communi sensorio* (seat of the soul in the common sensorium). No departing from the body. Virtual.",[]],"reflm:5622":["On existence after death. 1. *Spes vitae futurae* (hope of a future life). 2. *Immortalitas* (immortality): a. not universal, not necessarily eternal; b. /either from empirical or rational grounds. The latter: 1. from the principle of the immateriality of that which lives, and of the body as hindrance of life. For then it has lived before the human being. 2. From simplicity — 3. From the analogy of nature.\n*Status post mortem* (state after death).\n*Personalitas* (personality). Sleep of the soul. Existence as pure intelligence or vehicle.",[]],"reflm:5623":["The anticritique is either skeptical or dogmatic. The first re|jects\nall [philosophische] expansion of cognition through pure concepts and at the same time the investigation of this reason itself. The second asserts the former and rejects merely the latter. The first is *misology*, the second *ignava ratio* (lazy reason).",[]],"reflm:5624":["On theology: every presupposition that can be proved neither *a priori* nor *a posteriori*, but happens only for the benefit of our insight of reason, is hypothesis, if the [Fol der] propositions for whose sake the presupposition happens are contingent: e.g. contingent perfection in the world; but it is postulate if they are necessary *a priori*: e.g. moral propositions and their moving force. Thus the presupposition of the existence of God for the benefit of morals is a postulate. A hypothesis does not exclude that there should not also be other grounds of explanation. e.g. that also another than the most perfect being could be cause of the perfection perceived by us. A postulate determines the ground as the only possible one. Likewise if I found order and perfection everywhere in the essence of things, the existence of God would be a postulate and the original being as all-sufficient ground of the possibility of things would have to be presupposed in all teleological considerations.",[]],"reflm:5625":["(s No empirical theology.) *Theologia est vel rationalis vel revelata. Prior vel transscendentalis vel naturalis vel moralis. Naturalis vel cosmotheologia vel Physicotheologia.* (Theology is either rational or revealed. The former either transcendental or natural or moral. The natural either cosmotheology or physicotheology.)",[]],"reflm:5626":["*Entis originarii possibilitas originaria* (original possibility of the original being). *Ens realissimum* (the most real being).",[]],"reflm:5627":["We can think of [verschiedenen] things that belong under a certain universal concept different ones, without contradicting ourselves; but of God only a single one can be thought that does not contradict itself (*conceptus singularis*, not *communis*). All transcendental theology rests on determining this concept; but from the fact that all concepts except a single one (g [von]) cannot belong to a deity without contradiction, it cannot be concluded that the object of it is itself necessary.",[]],"reflm:5628":["The cosmological proof (g If something exists, then something must also be necessary) fails in this, that, because according to the (g universal) concept of an *entis limitati* many different kinds of things, but according to that of a *realissimi* only one thing, and consequently not the opposite of a given thing, can be thought, the former could be only contingent, but the latter alone only necessary; but a thing that is determined differently from a given one is indeed determined through opposed predicates, but is not the opposite of the former, precisely (g just) because it is another thing. The opposite is the *determinatio opposita* (opposite determination) of the same thing.\n(g The sequel is false: limited things cannot be necessary, because the concept of a thing can be determined *ad oppositum* in them.)\nIt would sound better if I said: all *limitata* have *possibilitatem logice derivativam* (a logically derivative possibility), but the *ens necessarium* should be *originarium*, so it cannot be a *limitatum*.\n(s On analogy, on the use of empirical predicates *per reductionem et eminentiam*. *Anthropopathos* *Theosophia*.)",[]],"reflm:5629":["Over, beside and \nUnbelieving is he who admits no other assumption of reason [warheiten] propositions than those that have logical certainty.\nPractically unbelieving is he who does not want to be upright except insofar as he knows for certain that it also has its reward (not from self-interest), thus not upon or in belief; or also he who attributes so much force to the moral disposition as to abstract from all sequels and to content himself merely with acting rightly.\nThe former is the unbelief of distrust.\nThe latter is the unbelief of self-conceit.",[]],"reflm:5630":["Were we to have complete insight into the nature of things, nature and freedom, determination of nature and determination of ends would be entirely one and the same. So it is with God; hence all ends in the world at the same time follow from the essence of things and in an original being are one and the same with its nature.",[]],"reflm:5631":["On divine blessedness. Self-sufficiency.\nDifference from happiness, which rests on outer things. Analogue of self-contentment, to need nothing outer.\nWe have weighed the attributes of God [als] [nach] physicotheologically. Now the existence of such a being is still to be proved according to physical principles.\nInference of physicotheology.\nThis consists (after the concept of God has been determined physiologically) in this, that from experience of the constitution of this world the existence of God be proved. Thus not the cosmological proof from the existence of any world, but from the constitution of this world. Here there are the following difficulties.\n1. [Wir] Only so much as we know of this world-perfection can we prove of God. Thus the concept of God is [imgleichen] wholly inconstant and alterable. Since one regarded the earth merely as the stage of the deity, many gods had to arise, who worked against one another, and even the supreme deity had to |\nhave human faults. Now that we regard it only as an infinitely small part of the whole, we can at least ascribe to God more greatness [als] and goodness than shines forth in his work. — One must have infinite cognition in order to cognize something as a work that proves an Infinite.\n2. [Wenn wir auch die Un] We can indeed, if we otherwise already cognize the existence of the *ens realissimum*, explain the imperfections in the world as mere illusion, but not infer from this imperfect to the highest perfection.\n3. Thus physicotheology is a ladder without highest rungs, it presupposes a pure theology of reason.",[]],"reflm:5632":["M 394e:\nProvidence (*Vorsorge*, provision): founding of the laws: concerns the arrangement of the world. (s[im Weltan] Wisdom laid in the beginning for the future.)\nGovernment (*gubernation*): preservation of the whole (g [im] course of the world) in accordance with laws.\nGuidance (s dispensation) (*directio*): the determination of each part to the agreement (g of individual occurrences) with the laws. (s is government with regard to an individual occurrence.)\n(s Destiny, (*praedestinatio*, fate): is a predetermination of an occurrence, in which the free will has no share.)\nProvidence is either the general one in accordance with general laws of nature or the particular (specific) one, which is related to individual beings.\n*(s Particular providence is distinguished from particular direction. The former [nach d] by means of the order of nature. The possibility (g of a particular provision) in accordance with an order of nature |\nis incomprehensible to us, because we cognize nature only as general lawfulness and not as a whole.)\nAll things (g and occurrences) stand under a general and particular providence; but not everything that happens happens through a particular providence, for which quite particular ordering, directed at individual cases, would have to be sought**. For not everything that agrees with God's end is his end, and does not always contain the motive of his ordering. *providentia legislatoria* or *generalis* is to be distinguished from *providentia decretoria* or *specialis*. The *actus* of a *providentia specialis* [are] do not belong to ordinary, but to extraordinary providence, and are distinguished from *directio specialis* only in that the former are thought as already included in the creation. The divine resolution is an *actus* and goes to the whole, and from the idea of it all particular arrangements must be derived: it is not composed together out of parts, for then an aggregate of ends and no system would arise.\nM 394:\nThe *providentia specialis* is at all times a miracle (*miraculum praestabilitum*) and can be admitted only as such. This is what is particular to God's *providentia generalis*, that here the welfare of the individual is not sacrificed to the art in the whole. No individual occurrence can come about that does not also belong to the best of the whole; all occurrences happen as if they were *praestabilirt* by divine particular providence. The cause of this is that the very possibility of things rests on the dependence of their essences on the highest wisdom, and the parts in the whole are always immediately determined through wisdom; but according to our way of explanation we cannot express this so, because thereby the derivation from general laws of nature is hindered and *ignava ratio* is introduced.\nM 394e:\n**(s The (g wisdom in) supplementing of the lack of purposive|ness\nof the freedom of the world-beings through divine [wisdom] influence is the *concursus*.)",[]],"reflm:5633":["On the use of the cognition of God granted to human reason. 1. Not to pursue and investigate the concept of it with a speculative aim, but only in order [so wohl] to regard it as a necessary boundary concept of all natural philosophy and the *non plus ultra* of human reason. Thus not to want to investigate it in itself. Also not to base explanations of nature on it as cause, and thus to set aside the maxims of the use of reason that alone is proper to us. 2. To regard it as a concept belonging to morals, but not in order to make them possible, but in order to give [sie] it [pra] the force of the incentive. 3. To use it, through combination with morals, for a religion, in which, however, morals and not theology prescribes the rule, so that we do not, by thinking anthropomorphistically, thereby [unsern] ourselves invert the moral principles. With regard to religion, to follow the [maxims] concepts of reason in accordance with (g general) laws of nature, by considering these themselves as a divine arrangement. To derive divine punishments and rewards from the general and at the same time moral interconnection of nature and freedom. [Das kün] To regard the fates of this life as the progression of nature toward better ends. The future life as an effect in accordance with laws of nature, the future welfare or misery as something that we naturally expect from ourselves and our conduct, in a word: to seek God in the order of nature and so to revere him. For how he is in himself we cannot grasp; and if we depart from the order of nature, we invert our vocation.",[]],"reflm:5634":["The cognition of God is not to serve to change our use of reason in accordance with the order of nature and of morals, but |\nto give completeness to it, so that we connect the former with ends and the latter with physical laws of the course of nature.",[]],"reflm:5635":["*Revelatio est vel symbolica vel* [intellectualis] *intuitiva.*\n[Die] Revelation is either to be considered with regard to the one to whom it has happened, or to those who are to believe the report of it.\nRevelation can also have as its sole aim to set in motion a doctrine that needs no revelation in order to maintain itself once it is there, because it has its proof in general human reason. Then revelation is only of local and temporal necessity.\nRevelation of such things as the human being does not cognize through reason as duty, consequently not as duty necessary in itself, [kan nicht] is in itself contingent.\nAll (g merely) revealed theology (g which cannot be cognized through reason) and religion [wir] gives, with time, a merely learned religion. A merely natural religion can also have been set in motion through revelation; with this it is indeed not necessary, but still useful, that the learned one too go in pair with it.",[]],"reflm:5636":["P. I:\n*Quaestio facti* is in what way one first put oneself in possession of a concept;\n*quaestio iuris*, with what right one possesses it and uses it.\nThe universality and necessity in the use of the pure concepts of the understanding betray their origin, and that it must be either entirely inadmissible and false or not empirical.\nIn pure sensibility, pure imagination and pure apperception lies the ground of the possibility of all empirical cognition *a priori* and of the synthesis in accordance with concepts, which has objective reality. For it goes only to appearances (which are in themselves contingent and without [Verb] unity), so that one properly cognizes oneself only as the thinking subject, but everything else as in this One. Self-cognition.\n––––––––––\nAll representations, may they come from wherever they will, are nevertheless in the end, as [modif] representations, modifications of inner sense, and from this point of view their unity must be regarded. The |\nreceptivity of them corresponds to a spontaneity of the synthesis. Either of the apprehension as sensations or of the reproduction as imaginings or of the recognition as concepts.\n(g transcendental principles of mathematics (not mathematical principles), namely that all intuitions and sensations are magnitudes and that the mathematical propositions about magnitudes have reality, although only as of appearances.)\nP. II:\nNo appearance can ever prove an empty space nor an empty time. Because appearances are nothing in themselves, namely not objects subsisting for themselves, the empty space is a perception of an extension without matter of the appearance.\nEvery magnitude has a quality, i.e. continuity. Every quality has a magnitude, i.e. intensity (degree). The boundaries of the extensive magnitude are not at the same time limitations of the intensive, but these can, notwithstanding this, grow into the infinite. The [Grenzen] limitations of the intensive magnitude, e.g. weight, are therefore not yet the boundaries of the extensive (or if the latter are equal, the former are also equal), but this can grow into the infinite. Against the atoms and the void.\n––––––––––\nSince the objects of our senses are not things in themselves, but only appearances, i.e. representations, whose objective reality consists only in the constancy and unity of the interconnection of their manifold, the objects do not give the concepts, but the concepts make it so that we have objects of cognition in them; since they too, as representations, are modifications of inner sense, their possibility rests on the synthesis of appearances in time.\n––––––––––\nIt is a very important question whether the categories are of merely empirical or also of transc: use. On schematism.",[]],"reflm:5637":["P. I:\n(g Key — through the nature of synthetic judgments a priori.)\nWere no space given a priori in our subject as the form of its (g sensible) intuition and objects outside us given merely in this form, then no synthetic propositions that at the same time hold of actual outer objects would be possible a priori. For if we were to take the representations of the objects from how they are given in themselves, then everything would rest merely on experience and no synthesis a priori at the same time with the necessity of judgments would hold, at least not objectively. Were time not given as the form of inner sense subjectively and thus a priori (g and no understanding to compare them), then apperception would not cognize the relation in the existence of the manifold a priori, for in itself time is no object of perception; it would also indeed determine the succession and coexistence of the representations, but not the place of the objects in time, and thus could not make any experience, if it did not have rules of [time determined] in the time determinable in the object; but these it cannot take from the object.\nI ask everyone whence he wants to take the mathematical and necessary (g synthetic) propositions about things in space, if space were not already in us a priori the condition of the possibility of the em|pirical\nrepresentation of the objects, through which these can be given to us. We determine a priori the manifold of appearance as to existence through the categories. I ask: where is this synthesis to be taken from, if time, the condition of the possibility of all perception, did not lie a priori at the basis and thus the rule of the determination of existence in this time and through it among one another even before all perception did not flow from the subjective constitution of our [Anscha] sensibility, on which all objective rests.\n[Nevertheless limits] From this it follows, however, that our synthetic propositions a priori can hold only of appearances, but not of things in themselves. That those are given only through the synthesis and as far as it reaches, that thus the concepts do not reach from them as things in themselves beyond all possible synthesis, whereby then the misunderstandings arise, of proceeding from the relative totality, which alone occurs in all empirical synthesis, to the absolute.\nReason, which will not let this restriction please it, supposes that our experiences and also our cognition a priori go immediately to objects and not first to the subjective conditions of sensibility and of apperception and by means of these to unknown objects, which are represented through those alone. It therefore takes various paths. 1. The empirical path and universality through induction. 2. The fanatical one of intuition through the understanding. 3. That of predetermination through innate concepts. 4. The *qvalitas occulta* (occult quality) of healthy understanding, which gives no account at all. |\nP. III: |\nIf one grants these, they abolish all critique of pure reason and open a wide field to all fictions. Hence it belongs to the discipline of pure reason to investigate them and, as found appropriate, to stop up such paths.\nP. IV:\nOn 66. [Against this cheerful One can] Healthy, to be equally active into late old age and useful to the world through insights, is a good fortune, which is to be begrudged to no one. If only that very same stalwart |\nmathematical man would renounce all judgment about this (g his) sphere or [could renounce. Perhaps sees itself Once but one must nevertheless in this and] (g with that *de gente hircosa centurionum*) say (g could): *qvod sapio, satis est mihi*. Yet since these problems of human reason are essential and can never be turned away, at least one will have to fast over them to the point of being sick, so that after this everyone can be healthy and yet [his of the researching] reason be satisfied.\n––––––––––\nNot only does reason overlook the ideality of the objects of the senses, but it also bristles against it as against everything that restricts its sphere of action. Hence it is necessary to investigate the paths that it takes. The first is empiricism. But since not only does the mathematical cognition a priori refute the falsity of this supposed origin of our cognitions, but also the concepts that occur in experience contain a necessity (cause) that experience could not teach, so falls, after Locke, who thereby earned almost too much honor, after Leibniz had already refuted him, away. Thus there remain the epigenesis, the mystical intuition, the involution. Finally there still comes the *qvalitas occulta* of common reason.\nThat our sensibility is nothing other than a confused intellectual representation is false. Incorrect concept of the sensible.\n––––––––––\nAll our cognition is grounded on the subjective, insofar as it represents an object through the synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition.\n––––––––––\nNo *dogmata*.\n––––––––––\n(g On the canon: the end of the whole of metaphysics is God and the future and the end of these our conduct, not whether we should arrange it in conformity with morals, but whether it is without sequels.)\n[The Be] Transcendental propositions are either those where indeed an object but no concept, but only the problem of it, is (g paralogism). or those where indeed a concept but no object (reality of it) is. Both come from this, that reason reaches farther than understanding.\n(g We have no permission to appeal to God. contingency of mathematics and possibility of explaining all order from laws of nature. God. we also cannot prove synthetically the necessary generation through understanding.)\nDiscipline of the opponent. He also speaks more synthetically than he can assert.\nWhether there are pure hypotheses of reason. Permission for it.\nLastly on the speculative interest of reason. Even if one has realized those ideas of reason, one must nevertheless explain nature as if there were no beginning, no simple, no freedom, no absolute contingency and as if no cause located outside the world were there. For nature is our problem, the text of our interpretations. Who knows what Epicurus thought of it. and what his gibberish explanations of nature are supposed to be anyway.\nP. II:\nThere is in all our thoughts not the slightest trace of (g intuition) of other objects than those of the senses and no thoughts that went to something other than the exposition of appearances. An intellectual intuition of the objects outside us, which do not exist through us, also seems to be impossible.\n(g If one assumes intellectual intuitions, this yields no cognition of the understanding through concepts and thus no thinking and also no cognition to be communicated.)\n––––––––––\nSuppose: we had everything a posteriori through experience and immediate perception of the objects, thus also space and time, then we would know nothing other than contingent truths of them. A priori we can cognize nothing of them synthetically, unless these intuitions are given to us a priori, consequently not through objects, but through the subject, but the relation to the objects, because these are given through those subjective conditions as appearances.\nEmpty *spatium* of 1 cm.\nReason now abhors principles that are not its own work. It is its maxim to assume everything as explicable. Consequently no sensible primitive intuition.\nAt the very bottom of p. II:\nThe logical system of cognitions of the understanding is now either the empirical or the transcendental. The former Aristotle and Locke, the second either of epigenesis or of involution, acquired or innate. The so-called healthy understanding is *asylum ignorantiae*.\nP. III. At the very top under Obiecte als (27421):\nThe cognitions need not be originally inspired; we see it in the synthetic cognitions a priori of mathematics.\n––––––––––\nThat one must assume no *mundus intelligibilis*.\nAbout 6 cm lower:\nA (g sense) world in general is the object of an intelligence. But it would not be so, were it not the work of an intelligence. For without rules of order there is no nature. These are however contingent, consequently an arrangement.\nAt the bottom of the page under 27228:\nEvery explanation of something purposive according to universal laws of nature would otherwise have to grieve. E.g. the flattening of the earth. It for|bids itself of its own accord, to be sure, to explain the *organisatio* mechanically; but if it were feasible, one could always set about it. Who then makes the existence of a most perfect being comprehensible. Contingency grows indeed with perfection.",[]],"reflm:5638":["P. I:\nOur concept of the world is, for the empirical synthesis, neither too large nor too small, if thereby only the totality of appearances [auf kein] limited by no restricting condition is understood, hence a problematic concept, which serves merely as a rule.\nIf one says: the conditions are [to a given] thereby also given, in that the conditioned is given, that means: that the rule is given according to which [they] each of them can be found; but thereby the totality of the series is not given: the collective all. The synthesis (g of the *regressus*) is unrestricted (*indefinita*), this seems as much as: the series is infinite (*infinita*); the *synthesis inversa*[des] *in consequentia* has a beginning, this seems: the series has a beginning. We have an outermost of the senses (relative) and also a first of our use of reason; from this we begin.",[]],"reflm:5639":["P. I:\nThe proposition: the concept of an absolute totality of the series of conditions must be either too large or too small, signifies: that no such concept at all is possible. For absolute time would have to be determined either by this, that [some] the synthesis would be congruent with a part [ode] of it or with the whole time. But we have only a concept of the magnitude of time by means of appearances. Our world-concepts are transcendent, and it is said through such a principle that they can all together be immanent and thereby alone adequate to the ob|ject.\nBut that no totality can be in the (g empirical) synthesis of appearances signifies that it [through] with regard to the empirical is *indefinita*, but is not given as infinite, because it is given only through the synthesis, which is always finite.\nIt is a remarkable rule or maxim of reason, which belongs to its discipline, that one must not prove apagogically any [proposition de] transcendental proposition of reason from concepts, since thereby it is often only demonstrated that our concept is faulty on both sides. E.g. that there can be no absolute freedom of religion, and on the other side: that there must be an absolutely complete one. One has a faulty concept of religion or of freedom. [and through the] But [through] such an antinomy [is a] serves nevertheless as a skeptical method for testing the correctness of our concepts and presuppositions. One shows the obstacles and [Sch] contradictions on both sides and is thereby held back from judging dogmatically on the one or the other, thus is impelled merely to criticize one's judgment.\n[The unrestricted synthesis is not] The infinity of the synthesis in a series is not the infinity of the manifold of the members considered as given, for this [member] manifoldness is given only through the synthesis. It is [in the pr] as in the *progressu* merely potential.\n––––––––––\nBecause the series of conditions cannot be given; but the concept can, one must rather say: the series is too large for the concept, than: the concept too small for the series, for the series is fitted to the concept and not the reverse.\nP. II:\nWhether, if I say: the world [must] is too large for our thoughts, it signifies just as much as: our thoughts are too small for the world. That which is given is the world. and not the thoughts. Where, then, does the fault lie: with the world or with thinking. The fault lies with thinking, because we think further than what is empirically given; for a world is not empirically given, but everything given and what we can think belongs — in the world.\nIt should say: the thought of the world must for the neither too large nor too small, hence be precisely adequate to the world as a sum total of all [given] appearances. But the world is a mere synthesis of appearances, in which the ground of the synthesis can always be determined only internally and not outside the appearances. The synthesis according to empirical laws and as *indefinita* is adequate to the world.\n––––––––––\nBecause the concept of appearances is given not before the synthesis, but only through it, the synthesis in itself is, with regard to the appearances, indeterminate, consequently [infinite although] it goes to infinity, although the appearance is not on that account given as infinite. It is therefore always finite, and all given world is finite, to be reckoned from the point *a priori*. On the other hand it is *potentialiter* [of the appearance] infinite according to illusion, namely if one considers the synthesis as given through the object. In such a way the thought or the concept according to which we are to think the world is neither too large nor too small for it, but is entirely adequate to this problematic concept or to the problem that lies in the concept, i.e. to the possibility of all empirical cognition in the field of appearances.\n(g In the senses there is no completed synthesis and nothing complete and unconditioned.)\nThe world must be neither too large nor too small for our thoughts, means as much as: one must think it so that its concept agrees with the conditions of the thoroughgoing empirical synthesis and its rule. Or conversely: the concept of the world must itself arise from this. Now this is a synthesis proceeding without end from the conditioned to conditions and a *progressio indefinita*, [through] in which time itself is determined through the synthesis of appearances and thus [neither] time neither determines the appearance nor limits itself with regard to the appearances. For then the world is an idea, [which only] whose object is given only through this synthesis and the rule of it, but never as an absolute whole for itself and all possible synthesis in a collective unity.",[]],"reflm:5640":["[Also: XXIII 483,12-485,6]\nA reason that was determined sensibly would not be reason.\nOf the inherited and yet imputed wickedness.\nIn short, the key is this: freedom signifies the [possibility] relation of an action as appearance [partly] (g on the one side) to causes in the appearance [partly] and on the other side to an intelligible faculty of it, whereby they themselves are the cause of the appearances and with regard to which the empirical conditions are not determining.",[]],"reflm:5641":["On the back of C. Spener's letter to Kant of 28 Apr. 1781 (X 248 [2 X 265]):\nQvant:\nQval. Relat:\nModal",[]],"reflm:5642":["p. I. At the upper margin:\nMy apparent idealism is the restriction of sensible intuition to mere experience and prevention, that we not stray with them beyond the boundary of the same to things in themselves.* It is merely the prevention of the transcendental *vitii subreptionis*, where one makes one's representations into things. I once called this doctrine transcendental idealism, because one has no name for it.\n*(g It would not have been worthwhile, for the sake of the objects of experience, to set up one's principles so high. One may have the cogni|tion *a priori* from wherever one wants, so they are confirmed through experience and so far reliable in use. Nevertheless it would also there be agreeable for the scientific's sake. But where the use goes further than experience, where there is danger of fiction, where powerful and apparent conflict is found, there it is necessary etc etc.)\np. II:\nFor judging, concepts are required, and for the concepts [judgments] intuitions. The concepts, insofar as they go to intuitions *a priori*, cannot arise through the single empirical consciousness of the manifold, otherwise they would not be concepts of the connection of intuition; but they [nu] are possible only through [the relation of the] the combination of the same in one apperception by means of the unity of the synthesis of the same. And in this consist the concepts *a priori*.\n––––––––––\nDialectic. Up to here we have had to do only with appearances, in whose exposition, their principles and use there is nothing but truth, and there was no idealism. For truth consists merely in the interconnection of representations thoroughgoingly according to laws of the understanding. In this consists all difference from dream. Not in this, that the images separated from the mind exist thus for themselves. But now there arises first an illusion, and indeed a natural and unavoidable illusion, since our judgments say something about objects that is not contained in our concepts of them, i.e. of possible experience, and there our theory is the refutation of idealism.\nFirst, the illusion consists in this: that in the field of experience, according to mere laws of experience, we represent to ourselves a *progressus* (progress), which is not an empirical *progressus* but a mere idea, which can be no experience. We remain in the world of sense [but] and are guided by nothing but the principles of the [law] understanding, which we use in ex|perience, but we make our possible *progressus* into an object in itself, in that we regard the possibility of experience as something actual in the object of experience.\nHere antinomy shows itself. All ideas that constitute the ground of this dialectic are contained herein: psychological as well as theological, but only insofar as they belong in the series of possible experience, which is supposed to bound itself. Here the ideas are only to complete the *progressus* and are cosmological. But there shows itself yet another illusion, where they do not belong to the series and are supposed, for themselves [such a one], to add something to experience, and there the ideas are partly psychological, partly theological. The hypothetical syllogisms are the guidance: for the first the categorical and the disjunctive for the last. The first lay as ground the subjective connection of all representations in a subject, the last the objective in an idea. Of the first subject there is then no concept, of the second object there is only an ideal.\nIf we demand for truth still something more than the thoroughgoing interconnection of intuitions according to laws of the understanding, in what would we then posit it, if this were not at the same time the representation of a determinate object. If it is supposed besides this still to be in agreement with something else, which does not lie in our representations, [s] how would we compare it with that. All objects (g are determined only through the representations in me; what they may otherwise be in themselves is unknown to me) are at the same time in us; an object outside us is transcendent, i.e. entirely unknown to us and unusable for a criterion of truth. dialectic.\nIdealism assumes no other than thinking beings. That we never do, but only that we do not regard our representations as their properties. |\nContinuation at the upper margin: |\nIn the senses there is indeed neither truth nor error, because they do not judge at all, [sonde] hence all appearances are insofar without possible error and no illusion.\nThe idealist concedes that actually extension and bodies outside us could be, but not actually be, thus merely a dream in us. |\nWe assert that these can be nothing but representations and only in us, but their objects may nevertheless be outside us, but we know nothing of what they may be in themselves.\nBetween 2807-14:\nSynthetic unity of apperception *a priori* is the synthesis of the manifold according to a rule *a priori*. The logical function is the action of uniting precisely the same consciousness with many representations, i.e. of thinking a rule in general. The unity of intuition *a priori* is possible only through the combination of the manifold in one apperception, which must therefore also take place *a priori*, consequently also the unity of the synthesis of all empirical intuitions, because they are to be encountered in space and time.\nWhat would one now demand that I should assert, so that I would not be an idealist?\nIdealism is a metaphysical whim [merely to], which goes further than is necessary to awaken to thinking. It goes with the philosophers as with the theologians. Syncretists. Semipelagians.\n===NOTES===\n- \"p.\" renders the Academy's \"S.\" (Seite). Gegenstand and Object/obiect are both \"object\"; Ding and Sache/sachen are both \"thing\"; Grundsatz and Principien are both \"principles\"; Beschaffenheit does not occur, Eigenschaften is \"properties\".\n- Erkenntnis/Erkentnis = cognition; Erfahrung = experience; Anschauung = intuition; Verbindung = combination; Verknüpfung = connection; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Vorstellung = representation; Erscheinung = appearance; Wesen = being; Ausdehnung = extension; Progressus is Kant's Latin, left in italics; \"Exposition\" is Kant's own word (cf. Erörterung, expositio).\n- Schein = illusion; scheinbar = \"apparent\" (in both \"scheinbarer Idealism\" and \"scheinbarer Wiederstreit\"); Wiederstreit = conflict; Wiederlegung = refutation.\n- \"da\" (man seine Vorstellungen zu sachen macht; unsere Urtheile etwas sagen; und da war kein Idealism) can be local, temporal or causal: rendered \"where\", \"since\", \"there\"; the sense is not settled in the German.\n- Doubtful or broken-off readings left as written: [urtheile] (noun or verb), [nu] (nun/nur?), [s], [sonde] (presumably sondern, broken off); \"um des scientifischen Willen\" is obscure (rendered \"for the scientific's sake\"); \"Daß thun wir niemals\" has \"Daß\" where \"Das\" is expected.\n- Ambiguities kept: \"welche\" (Verbindung or Apperception); \"ausser dem\" (besides this / outside this); \"die\" in \"die sich selbst begrenzen soll\" (Reihe or Erfahrung); \"zum Denken aufzuweken\" (awaken thinking / awaken to thinking); \"wirklich\" in \"daß wirklich Ausdehnung ... sein könten, aber nicht wirklich seyn\" is left in the German position.\n- The sentence \"Die Hypothetische Vernunftschlüsse sind die Leitung: zum ersten ... zum letzten\" is obscure in the German (plural subject, singular predicate) and is left so; \"legen zum Grunde\" = \"lay as ground\"; \"bestimmten\" = \"determinate\"; \"wissen\" = \"know\"; \"Grille\" = \"whim\".\n- \"(g\" and \"|\" are the Academy's apparatus and are carried over unchanged; the English hyphenations \"cogni|tion\" and \"ex|perience\" mark the position of the line break in Er|kentnis and Er|fahrung.",[]],"reflm:5643":["S. I:\nIn all synthetic cognition *a priori* there must be thought, first, intuition *a priori*, second, concept of the synthesis of the manifold *a priori*. On this are grounded the principles of synthetic cognition in general. For these contain nothing but the conditions under which alone certain intuitions are brought under concepts of the synthesis of them. The latter are called categories.\n(g We can sense something without thinking, [we] it is questionable whether we cannot also think without sensing. (*a priori*.) But we can also intuit without sensing. If we are now to think without sensing, then this must relate to that intuition.)\nIntuition is immediate relation of the power of representation to a single object. Concept the representation of the same through a mark, |\nthat is common to it with others. Intuition belongs to the senses, concept for the understanding.\nConcepts of the synthesis of the manifold of possible intuitions are nothing other than the combination that the representations have in a consciousness (g can, insofar as they are necessary with regard to an intuition), but only thought synthetically, i.e. that to one [each] (g to the concept of the object) something other added would bring forth the consciousness of the representation of an object.\nIn the representation of an object, which is to contain the manifold of the intuition of the same, the synthetic unity of the latter is necessary. The representation of this necessary unity, under which all manifold of intuition must stand, is, if it is to become cognition of an object of intuition, the principle of synthetic cognitions in general, and it itself must take place *a priori*.\nS. II:\nSomething that is determined with regard to the functions of the [magnitude] judgments, is the object, and this determination is determination of the object and so in the rest. The categories are therefore concepts for determination of the objects of our cognition in general, insofar as the intuition has been given for it. Therefore principles, to make experience out of appearance, which is purely objective, i.e. universally valid empirical cognition, since therefore the synthesis must be determined *a priori*, because otherwise it would not be necessary and universal. For we know an object only as a something in general, to which the given intuitions are only predicates. How these can now be the predicates of a third cannot be cognized through their comparison, but through the way in which in[de] a consciousness the consciousness of the manifold in general could be regarded as necessarily combined. [The concept] In the representation of an object the manifold is united. All intuitions are only representations; the object, [that] to which they are related, lies in the understanding.\nA synthesis can never as necessary and thus *a priori* be cog|nized\nfrom the representations that are to be combined synthetically, but from the relationship of the same to a third concept, in which [the connec] and in relation to which this combination is necessary. This third concept is that of an object in general, which is thought precisely through this synthetic necessary unity and which is determined with regard to the logical functions of such unity. For thereby first of all the manifold of representations becomes objective, i.e. cognition, and appearance empirical cognition.\nThat something is objectively determined I cannot cognize *a posteriori* without determining it objectively according to a rule *a priori*; for everything that is objectively determined must let itself be determined *a priori* from the concept of the object, to be sure not according to the matter, yet according to the form of the connection. Through precisely the same representations through which the concept of an object is determined, is a concept, which conversely determines those representations objectively.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis/Erkenntnis = cognition throughout; erkennen = cognize; kennen = know. Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Verbindung = combination; Verknüpfung = connection; Bestimmung = determination.\n- obiect/Obiect/Gegenstand: all rendered \"object\" (Latinate obiect, Obiect vs. Gegenstand not marked in the English); Grundsatz and the Latinate Principien both = \"principle\".\n- empfinden (verb) rendered \"sense\"/\"sensing\"; the glossary has only Empfindung = sensation.\n- Beziehung = \"relation\" (beziehen = \"relate\"); Verhaltniß = \"relationship\"; allgemein = \"universal(ly)\".\n- determinirt (Latinate) and bestimmt both = \"determined\".\n- Literal/unresolved: \"in[de]\" is a deletion fragment left as is; \"welche\" (\"which is purely objective\") may refer to Erfahrung or Erscheinung; the final sentence has \"wird ein Begrif, der...\" with no completed predicate (\"is a concept, which...\"), left as it stands.\n- \"er|kannt\\nwerden\": the line break falls before \"werden\"; the English auxiliary precedes, so the | and line break are placed within \"cog|nized\".\n- Sigla (g, (s, [ ], | are carried over unchanged; \"Functionen der [Größe] Urtheile\" retains the bracketed deletion.",[]],"reflm:5644":["19. S. I, II.\nS. I:\n(s Nature does not suffice us, neither with regard to the effecting causes nor ends.)\n(s It is something different: science of pure reason, and: science of the principles of pure reason in general. Possibility of such a one is Transcendental Philosophy.)\n(s Metaphysic. Either [*pura* or] *vniversalis* — *Ontologia*, or *specialis* and *applicata*: 1. cosmology, which contains objects of experience —, 2. Theology.\n*Metaphysica pura*. *Ontologia* is the system of pure principles *a priori*. Logic is a science of the principles of all reason, itself to be sure pure, but does not deal with the principles.)\nA science is not sufficiently determined by this, that it contains the first principles of human cognition, but: that this cognition be of a particular kind, *a priori*. [Logic also] Cognition from principles *a priori* is cognition of pure reason and either pure mathematics or pure philosophy. Metaphysic: the system of pure |\nphilosophy (g of speculative or practical philosophy (of nature or of morals). All cognition of reason from concepts has its metaphysic.) Mathematic is cognition of reason through construction of concepts in intuition, because it goes only to objects of the senses. Philosophy, cognition of reason from concepts, goes therefore also to things that are not objects of the senses. (g Even possibility of mathematics.)\nThings can only be given through the senses, but we can nevertheless cognize much of them *a priori*. Things that are not given to the senses, if they are to be cognized *a priori*, (s that we become aware of them only on the occasion of experience proves nothing.) constitute the *metaphysicam puram*.\n(s The proper metaphysic has 2 parts: the 1st goes to things as objects of experience: psychology and general natural science, the second to objects as ideas: world and God, which nevertheless necessarily belong to reason. Therefore 1. all elements and principles of pure thinking with abstraction from all objects (g ontology) — 2. Application to objects: a. of the senses, b. of pure reason.)\nTherefore *metaphysica* is either *pura* or *applicata*.\n(g philosophical is a cognition of reason from concepts,\nmetaphysical: pure cognition of reason from concepts.)\nBefore metaphysic precedes transcendental philosophy, which, just like logic, does not deal with objects, but with the possibility, the [domain] sum total and the boundaries of all cognition of pure reason (also of pure mathematics). It is the logic of pure cognition of reason.\nBefore transcendental philosophy, critique of reason in general.\nS. II:\n(g As long as I only analyze concepts that have use in experience, or present principles of the same, it still goes; but if I depart from it and go further, there the difficulty begins. I will first present everything critically, then own propositions.)\nThus first metaphysic itself is the object, and there that which investigates its possibility is critique.\n(s Ontology is the sum total of all concepts and principles of pure thinking.)\nSecond, in the (g proper) metaphysic the system of all pure concepts *a priori*, which we relate to objects\\*: transcendental philosophy\\*\\* (s pure reason entire), ontology (s *conceptus dabiles*), cosmology and theology (s *ideales*). (s To explain hyperphysically. Hyperphysical. metaphysical. physical.)\n\\*(g if concepts are *a priori*, consequently not given through objects, then they can only be taken from the understanding itself in relation to objects in general. 1. To things in general: Ontology; 2. Connection in a whole; 3. Dependence [this] on a ground.)\n\\*\\*(g what logic is with regard to all cognition, that is transcendental philosophy with regard to pure cognition *a priori*.)\nThird, these principles applied to objects of outer and inner experience in general. *Physica rationalis*, *Psychologia rationalis*.\nNot *psychologia empirica*.\n(g 1st part occupies itself with things;\n2nd part with ideas (in itself).)\n(g All philosophical cognition has its metaphysic, which determines the principles *a priori*.)\nWe have many cognitions *a priori*, of which we are in need with regard to experience. e.g. Everything has cause. But with regard to these we needed no metaphysic.\nThe objects of experience do not do fully enough for reason itself according to these principles. Hence reason goes beyond experience, and with regard to these metaphysic is necessary: God and another world. In metaphysic there is a part that merely analyzes the use of reason with regard to experience. This is\n|\nvery useful for culture, but dispensable for science. Hence yet another part: synthetic, through which concepts are made transcendent.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis/Erkenntnis = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Vernunfterkentnis (compound) = \"cognition of reason\", distinct from \"cognition of pure reason\" (Erkentnis der reinen Vernunft). Principien and Grundsatz(e) both = \"principle(s)\"; Satz/Sätze = \"proposition(s)\".\n- Obiect/obiecte and Gegenstand/Gegenstande both = \"object(s)\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Moglichkeit = \"possibility\"; Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Umfang = \"domain\" (in a deletion); Grenzen = \"boundaries\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Sinne = \"senses\".\n- wirkenden Ursachen rendered \"effecting causes\" (wirken; cf. Wirkung = effect). \"inne werden\" = \"become aware of\"; \"gnug/gnüge thun\" = \"suffice\"/\"do enough\"; \"Art\" = \"kind\".\n- Asterisks \\* and \\*\\* are Kant's footnote marks, escaped so as not to be read as italics. \"Hyperphysisch erklären\" is a fragment: \"hyperphysisch\" could be adverb or adjective, left as \"To explain hyperphysically\" / \"Hyperphysical\".\n- Ambiguity kept: \"deren\" in \"in Ansehung deren\" may refer to experience or to the objects/cognitions; \"brauchten\" (past indicative or subjunctive) rendered \"needed\"; \"[dieses]\" in a deletion rendered \"[this]\".\n- Latin in roman type in the source (*metaphysicam puram*, accusative, as written; *vniversalis* as spelled) is italicized; *an sich* = \"in itself\".\n- Sigla (s, (g, [ ], | and the page-break line are carried over unchanged; \"Transscendental/transscendent\" = \"transcendental/transcendent\".\n- The opening \"19. S. I, II.\" is the editors' locus line, kept as is.",[]],"reflm:5645":["36. Sheet 1. 2.\nSheet 1. p. I:\nIn order to find out the need of our age with regard to the danger of getting through happily between the two cliffs of dogmatism and skepticism and [thereby] at the same time to determine both concepts appropriately to this need, we must first of all establish the character of the same with regard to the way of thinking that makes that caution necessary.\nExtensive knowledge and possession of a great multitude of sciences does not yet constitute the character of the way of thinking [and because]; for this depends on the quality and specific constitution of the power of judgment (g and on the principles that determine), what use one intends to make of those. Whether our age has come far in knowledge [is there] and whether its cognition is to be called great, can be judged only comparatively; our descendants will perhaps find it still small. But a [skill] faculty can indeed already now have attained its maturity, so that the later world has no need to add anything further (because here it depends not on the quantity but on the quality [of the manifold] in the use of our cognitive powers), and this is the faculty of the power of judgment (*iudicium discretiuum*).\nOur age is the age of critique, i.e., of a (g sharp) judgment of the foundation of all assertions, to which the |\nexperience of long times, perhaps also the cautious investigation of nature set going by the famous Bacon of Verulam through observation and experiment, has brought us not only in the assertions of natural science but by analogy also in all the rest, of which the ancients knew nothing and so were accustomed to wavering opinions. In this a future age can hardly surpass us, even if we often from negligence do not [all] (g as we should) make use of these principles of critique. Certainly no past (g age) surpasses us in this, and this can therefore be called the (g scientific) character of ours.\nIn all science, if we abstract from the multitude of items of knowledge, [is] the essential aim is that it distinguish itself from mere opinion, hence certainty. The method that one uses in it is merely the means to attain this. Certainty is the unchangeability of the taking to be true. But the taking to be true is unchangeable either objectively: when we cognize that no preponderant ground to the contrary is possible in itself, or subjectively: when we are convinced that neither we nor any human being could ever get hold of greater grounds to the contrary.\nThe (g with consciousness) unchangeable taking to be true is knowledge, the subjectively unchangeable taking to be true belief. The [indeed] taking to be true with the consciousness of its changeability is opining.\nExample in history. The word believing can signify either [with regard to] the source of our cognition or [the way and] the degree of taking it to be true. In the former meaning no cognition [through] of history can arise otherwise than through a trust in testimonies of others, i.e., through our believing others. In the second meaning an acquaintance with history can certainly be a knowledge and |\np. II: |\nit does not need, according to the degree of taking to be true, from one's own experience, to which they do not dispute the name of knowing, [to be distinguished] to be distinguished by the term of belief. Thus one knows that a Louis XIV lived, just as surely as if one had seen him oneself, and so a good part of history is true science; the taking to be true is in it objectively unchangeable. It is impossible [that ever] to concede sufficient grounds to the contrary of the|same.\nOn the other hand I hold the history of the 7 first kings of Rome to be true, although only from insufficient, yet such grounds as are of the kind on which all history rests, and [of] besides I am convinced that no one will ever prove the contrary, because all documents for it are lacking, i.e., I believe it. But that perhaps even before the founding of Rome there was a cultivated people and a flourishing city at the same spot, from which [the] perhaps the Cloaca Maxima still stems, is an opinion that can be heard; |\nbut it is not impossible that one might still find a proof to the contrary, if perhaps this subterranean work were to be cleared out and inscriptions of its builder (perhaps Servius Tullius) were to be found; I therefore do not recognize in my taking to be true the unchangeability.\nAll knowledge is either empirical [ode], i.e., derived from experience, or rational: arising from reason, hence possible *a priori* and self-standing. To the former are counted one's own experience and history (i.e., reliable report, hence a knowledge, of the experience of others). The second certainty is independent of all experience.\nAll empirical certainty is combined with the consciousness of the contingency of the truth; for experience teaches indeed that something is constituted thus or otherwise [se] or has happened, but never that it could not at all be otherwise constituted or have happened.\nCertainty of reason is inseparably combined with consciousness of the necessity of that which is held to be true. All cognition which [with the] according to its essential constitution is combined with the consciousness [of its] of its necessity, is apodictic. Therefore [is] must [all] every proposition of reason be apodictically certain, and in reason [propositions] assertions (*assertionibus*) neither opining nor believing takes place.\nBut all apodictic propositions are twofold: cognized either from mere concepts or (g only) through the construction of concepts. The former are called *dogmata*, the second *mathemata*. If it [could be proved], as Leibniz held, could be proved from the mere concept of a body as a composite substance that it consists of simple parts, then this would be a *dogma*; but if through the geometrical presentation of a space which a body occupies, and the just as well geometrical division of this space the divisibility of the same to infinity is proved, then this proposition is a *mathema*. Philosophy alone |\np. III: |\ncan therefore con|tain *dogmata*,\nbecause it distinguishes itself precisely in this from mathematics, which, just as (g well) as that, [a] is a cognition of reason, that it judges merely from concepts, but this not otherwise than through construction of concepts.\nPhilosophy is therefore indeed capable of apodictic certainty, but not of the intuitive [son] by means of intuition *a priori*, as mathematics can procure such, but only of the discursive from concepts. Hence that contains indeed principles, this alone axioms, that proofs (*probationes*), this alone demonstrations; that [delivers] procures conviction, this at the same time evidentness (*evidentiam*).\nAddition in the margin: |\n(g Critique. Art of distinguishing opining from knowledge.)\n[All cognition, even if it were not even cognition of reason but only historical, nevertheless requires certain principles which contain in themselves the criteria of truth. Thus there are certain principles according to which one assesses the probability of a history, not merely such as [flow] from the nature of the things narrated and are therefore taken from reason, but also those that determine the way in which experience of old time could be brought to us (g with reliability).]\nTo the certainty of reason one can [oppose] set to the side the historical certainty, by which one understands the empirical certainty that does not rest on our own experience (hence on reports of the experience of others). Just so there also takes place a belief of reason, which is analogical to historical belief, although it rests on other grounds. In the certainty (g of) of both kinds objective unchangeability of the judgment is thought, in [second] belief of both kinds only subjective unchangeability. That [it in] the planets contain rational inhabitants can be believed according to reason; for as many grounds of proof of it as one can reasonably expect given our distance from them, [make] furnish (g so) great an analogy between them and the earth as is necessary for this consequence, and one is moreover certain that no one will ever know anything more about them in order to prove the contrary.\nThe belief of reason is called moral certainty, if it [according to] to the |\nobligation, to lay it [in] his actions [as principle] as ground, is sufficient. For because the moral laws, which contain the grounds of obligation, (g also rest on reason and therefore) are objectively unchangeable, so insofar [belief of reason reason] certainty can take place. But this cannot be called certainty of reason (*certitudo logica*), because it is not [merely] from theoretical [principles of reason follows but as] principles, but merely for the sake of practical maxims must necessarily [as true] be assumed, and [may], because these maxims rest on moral laws, which have objective unchangeability, so one may not call such a belief of reason moral belief, but one can call it moral certainty. Thus the propositions: There is a God, there is a future life, would furnish for speculative cognition only a belief of reason and yet [as] count as morally certain [gewis[ange]]; for because only through them are all [well-founded hindrances] (g objections against) the obligation according to moral laws removed (namely those that are taken from the consideration that observation or transgression of the latter according to the mere course of nature could have no effect proportioned to them), [but this objective certainty] so is [just so objectively] their assumption [just s] for the sake of this their necessity [and objective unchangeability] likewise [an unchangeable] practically necessary, and it is impossible that grounds to the contrary could be, because otherwise grounds to the contrary of morality could also take place and this would therefore itself be doubtful and uncertain.\np. IV:\nBy *dogmata* one understands apodictic propositions from concepts. Hence mathematics contains indeed apodictic propositions, but no *dogmata*, because they are possible not from concepts but only through their construction. The propositions that between two points only one straight line is possible or in every triangle the sum of all angles is equal to two right ones, of which the first is an axiom, the second a demonstrated theorem, are *mathemata*, i.e., cognitions, [die] whose (g insight and) certainty is intuitive (g but thereby nevertheless apodictic); but that [everything\n|\n(g what) exists contingently is a proposition must have a cause is indeed also an apodictic but only discursive proposition from concepts hence] every statement must be truthful and that lying is reprehensible, is an apodictic, but discursive proposition from concepts and can be called a *dogma*.\nAll [sciences] apodictic certainty is [therefore] contained only in two sciences, which encompass the cognition of reason *a priori*: in mathematics or philosophy. *Dogmata* can be encountered only in the latter. But philosophy is either theoretical, where reason has to do merely with itself, or practical: where it has to do with the laws of the will. The first part, insofar as it contains apodictic, i.e., pure cognitions of reason, is called metaphysics, the second, which contains just such practical laws, pure morals.\n––––––––––\nSummary Concept.\n[Dogmatism is the way of thinking]\nBetween dogmatism and skepticism the middle and only lawful way of thinking is criticism.\nThis is the maxim never to assume anything as true otherwise than after complete examination of the principles.\nAll principles are [either] (g now on one side) taken *a posteriori*, i.e., empirically, and these (g in turn either) one's own experience or testimonies (g of) the experience of others, hence experience (*in sensu stricto*) or history.\nAll principles are on the other side taken *a priori* and from reason, but these (g either) from reason insofar as it judges merely according to concepts, hence philosophical principles, or insofar as it judges merely in accordance with the construction of concepts, i.e., their presentation *a priori* in intuition.\nOwn experience and mathematics [not] need this not at all, that only seldom a critique, so that illusion not be taken for perception. But so much the more do history of distant ages among the empirical and speculative philosophy among the cognitions of reason need critique, i.e., examination of the principles.\np. V:\nDogmatism is the way of thinking, without [examin] critique (i.e., examination |\nof the principles) to adhere to assertions. The most natural propensity of the human being with regard to cognitions is toward dogmatism: 1. because of laziness, because going back to principles is harder than the progression to the application of principles once assumed and brought into circulation. 2. Because through critique cognition is not extended but only secured. 3. From fear of uncovering the wretchedness of our knowledge to our own and others' eyes.\nSkepticism is a chosen principle of doing damage to dogmatism, yet not [by means of crit] with the aim of introducing [the truth] true conviction [zu] against it, but only to overthrow the persuasion of others. [For this] The inclination to this is not natural but artificial and can arise only from displeasure at the usurpation of dogmatism. The occasion for it is given to it [in the historical] (g firstly in) empirical cognition by the polymathy [of the] of all, chiefly of ancient history and the system-building of the same, 2ndly in cognition of reason [Meta] speculative philosophy, namely metaphysics and general morals with their [questions] assertions about the highest good. It is therefore indeed a malice, in that it (g seeks) nothing but to do harm, namely seeks to rob human reason of all hope in the most important questions of reason: but not unwitted malice, for dogmatism as (g an assent) usurping self-conceit can at least not complain about the resistance of the misologist as about injustice. Yet it is (g to say the least) folly; for it transforms all taking to be true into illusion, which it sets against truth, must therefore nevertheless concede criteria of the difference of the same, and yet it denies these entirely.\nDanger is the possibility of a greater evil. There is therefore danger in dogmatism as well as in skepticism, in the former of the snatching up of a jumble of errors among a small number of truths and of the contempt for the latter themselves on account of their relationship with those; in the latter, of the denial of our duty everywhere to avail ourselves of our reason, and of a laziness of the former glossed over by an apparent pretext.\nThis danger can only through the greatest critical diligence, on the empirical side the sources (g of history) and the derivation |\nof the same to us and on the side of reason the nature and the faculty of human reason so [far] as well in its speculative use in metaphysics as in the practical in morals to track down and its boundaries, likewise its domain and the necessary principles of the latter convincingly to establish, be removed.\n===NOTES===\n- Fürwahrhalten / Vorwarhalten = 'taking to be true' throughout; Wissen = 'knowledge', Kenntnisse/Kunde = 'items of knowledge' / 'acquaintance', Erkenntnis = 'cognition'. 'Knowledge' therefore stands for Wissen and for Kenntnis (and 'knowing' for wissen).\n- Glaube(n) = 'belief' / 'believing'; Meinung / Meynen = 'opinion' / 'opining'; Überzeugung = 'conviction'; Überredung = 'persuasion'; Gewißheit = 'certainty'; Unveränderlichkeit = 'unchangeability'.\n- 'Sufficient' renders both hinreichend and zureichend (unzureichend = 'insufficient'); 'effect' renders Effect (Wirkung would also be 'effect'); 'observation' renders Beobachtung both of nature and of laws (where 'observance' is also conceivable).\n- Preserved ambiguities: 'dem Grade ... der eigenen Erfahrung, der sie ... nicht streiten' (p. II; the subject 'sie' and the construction are left as obscure as in the German); 'der Faulheit der ersteren' (last-but-one paragraph) kept as 'of the former'; selbständig = 'self-standing'; Augenscheinlichkeit = 'evidentness' (Latin *evidentia* given).\n- Doubtful or truncated readings carried over as in the text: '[ode]', '[se]', '[zu]', '[Meta]', '[Prüfun]', '[son]', 'gewis[ange]', '[sey da]' (here '[is there]'); 'ungewitzte' (Bosheit) is of uncertain sense, rendered 'unwitted'. The garbled bracketed passage on p. IV (the principle of causality) is rendered word for word.\n- Other terms: Stelle = 'spot'; Vielwisserey = 'polymathy' (literally 'much-knowing'); Erfahrenheit = 'experience'; scheinbar = 'apparent'; sich bedienen = 'avail oneself'; Darstellung = 'presentation'; Augenschein/Anschauung = 'intuition', intuitiv = 'intuitive' (anschaulich would be the same word); 'Dogma/Dogmata', 'Mathema/Mathemata' italicized as Latinized forms.\n- Editorial notes (Zusatz am Rand; the sheet and page notes) are translated; the signs [ ], (g ...), | and || are kept in place, with their contents translated; 'g' marks the later insertion in the Academy apparatus.",[]],"reflm:5646":["P. I:\nThe I.\nAccording to the appearances of inner (g as well as outer) sense one can never regard oneself as the identical self, even according to the sensible character. Merely with regard to morality, which [Int] is the pure consciousness of ourselves independent of determination in space and time, is always the same subject of free actions under the same laws [so long as] in everything of which we are conscious of ourselves, and there the whole of our actions is regarded like unity, and we cannot, because we once improve ourselves, believe that we therefore already had another personality and could not be punished on account of the previous one, as [almost] almost all human beings believe. Admittedly this cannot happen by human beings (s as judges). Just so one reproaches oneself for the evil of one's childhood years (Rousseau: Bandgeschichte) or also for what we have done in drink. Only the improvement is nevertheless an experience that in us the character is not so entirely evil.\n===NOTES===\n- \"S. I\" rendered \"P. I\" (Seite, page of the manuscript sheet); \"(g\" and \"(s\" are Academy sigla, carried over unchanged.\n- Bracketed items are carried over in place; [Int] is a broken-off word fragment, left as is; [so long as] = [so lange], [almost] = [fast] (deleted, then repeated).\n- Bestimmung = determination; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Erfahrung = experience; Gesetz = law; Einheit = unity; Besserung = improvement; Personlichkeit = personality.\n- \"Blos in Ansehung der Moralitaet ... ist immer dasselbe Subiect\": the syntax is left as in the German (the main verb \"ist\" follows a long relative clause); \"wie Einheit\" kept as \"like unity\". \"Bandgeschichte\" left in German (reading as printed).",[]],"reflm:5647":["P. IV:\nReason has no other than analytic use. The synthetic, through which it makes principles, is dialectical. Hence the |\nprinciples of the understanding are either immanent or transcendent. The objective principles of reason are dialectical, the subjective dogmatic.\nThe principles are either anticipating or ...., the last are dialectical. The anticipating principles either of transcendental apprehension or association, both of recognition; that breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- Grundsatz = principle (\"Verstandesgrundsatzen\" is the manuscript's form); Gebrauch = use; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason.\n- \"....\" is in the source: a gap in the text, not filled.\n- \"die letzte\" (plural verb \"sind\") rendered \"the last\"; \"die bricht ab\" rendered \"that breaks off\", the antecedent of \"die\" being unspecified in the German (possibly an editorial/authorial remark that the note breaks off).\n- \"Either ... or\" in the third sentence is left elliptical as in the German (\"entweder der ... oder ...\"). Recognition = Recognition (Latinate German word, same English for it).",[]],"reflm:5648":["Remark of Kant on the empty back of the letter of G. Hufeland of 11 October 1785 (X 388f. [2 X 412]) in vol. I of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant p. 206:\nOne must have thoughts to give up.\nOne cannot get away from cognitions that concern the first beginning.\nThe decrying of hypotheses.\n===NOTES===\n- \"gedanken Preis zu geben haben\" (lower-case \"gedanken\" in the source): rendered \"have thoughts to give up\" (preisgeben); the construction is left as brief and obscure as in the German.\n- Erkenntnis = cognition; \"abkomen\" = \"get away from\".\n- \"Die Verschreyung der Hypothesen\": \"decrying\" for Verschreyung; the genitive could be objective (hypotheses are decried), left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5649":["P. I:\nTiedemann.\nMetaphysics can take three paths: 1. That general cognitions (g of nature) are possible merely through experience (also their concepts). 2. That general cognitions are possible also merely from concepts a priori and through reason. 3. That [Erfa] general cognitions do indeed take place a priori and before all experience, but only insofar as they are grounds of the possibility of experience. The first path is empirical, |\nthe second dogmatic, the third critical, because the critique (g and resolution) of experiences alone can direct it. For the last path it is required that to the (g empirical) intuition [in de] pure sensible intuition a priori and to the empirical concept pure concepts a priori, which relate merely to the unity (g of consciousness) of these intuitions, lie at the ground.\nThat, however, besides sensibility and understanding (both a priori) the faculty of reason still contains principles of the use of both, and indeed to restrict the concepts of the understanding to experience and those of sensibility also to the same, so that their conditions not be extended to beings in themselves, God and spirit. At the same time thus to give reason freedom to think something still beyond experience, which, however, is indeed necessary for the completion of our use of the understanding, but can never be thought through theoretical concepts otherwise than negatively, through moral ones alone positively, and contains the totality of conditions for everything.\nP. II:\nHow much one wishes that, when one wants to elevate oneself to extra-sensible objects, one were rid of the restricting concepts of space and time, likewise, when one wants to arrive at an absolute whole of all conditions of the understanding, that one were rid of the conditions continuing always in the understanding [rid], which make totality impossible, although they belong to every experience, in a word: that one were rid of that which properly belongs only to the possibility of experience and goes no further.\nThe pure concepts of reason God (g freedom) and other world are properly of moral origin.\n––––––––––\nWith space and time one can take only two paths: 1. that they are [mere] concepts, 2. mere intuitions. In the first case they are a. empirical or b. concepts a priori. In the second they are [ans] 1. intuitions of things in themselves through observation and yet neces|sary,\n2. [anschau] formal intuition a priori, i.e. consciousness of the way in which [we] objects are represented to ourselves to the senses.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkenntnis = cognition; Begriff = concept; Anschauung = intuition; Vernunft = reason; Verstand = understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Beobachtung = observation; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Einheit = unity; Bedingung = condition.\n- Gegenstand/Gegenstände = object(s); \"aussersinlichen\" = \"extra-sensible\" (sinnlich = sensible); Wesen an sich selbst = \"beings in themselves\"; Totalität (Latinate) rendered \"totality\", the same English as Allheit.\n- Bracketed fragments [Erfa], [in de], [ans], [anschau] are broken-off words, left as is; [los] = [rid], [bloße] = [mere], [wir] = [we] are deletions; the German \"los sey\" in the first S. II sentence is rendered \"were rid of\".\n- The sentence beginning \"Daß aber ausser...\" has no main clause in the German; left as a fragment. \"Zum Grunde liegen\" (\"lie at the ground\") is placed at the end as in the German, so the datives (\"to the intuition ... to the empirical concept\") are left unresolved.\n- \"uns Gegenstande den Sinnen vorgestellt werden\": \"uns\" with \"Gegenstande\" (accusative/nominative unclear) preserved as \"objects are represented to ourselves to the senses\"; doubtful construction, left as is.\n- \"Zu\" in \"zum Grunde\"/ \"S.\" rendered \"P.\" (Seite); \"neces|sary\" retains the | page/line break.",[]],"reflm:5650":["P. I:\n(g If the concept of space and time were not borrowed from the form of our sensibility, one would have to have taken the synthetic propositions of the same from the things themselves, thus space and time would have for themselves objective reality either as substances or accidents or relations; [these] both however go before the things, thus they would have to be mere concepts, but from these synthetic propositions cannot be taken.)\nMetaphysics.\nSubstance is the last subject of reality. Its relation to the [this] existence of this is called force, and this alone is that by which the existence of the substance is designated and in which its existence also itself consists. Because now every force has a degree, so for much smaller degrees of the same kind there can be many subjects, and a whole be composed from them, if they stand externally in community, or they can also be [in] so combined that they make up only one force, in which no external community of different subjects is encountered, i.e. unity of the subject [with] of much [degrees] great degree of force is just as much as plurality of the subjects of smaller degrees, indeed |\none can be transformed into the other. For if from a drop of water a drop of quicksilver were to come (as far as the weight is concerned), the multitude of the parts would not be increased, thus not of the subjects, and the same could nevertheless arise, if 14 drops of water were swallowed up into one. Thus the simple subject is not indivisible on that account, for it is not divisible as composed of many subjects, but as [a single] (g unity of the) subject, but of a magnitude of force, changeable into many subjects of smaller. The soul is simple means as much as: it does not consist of many subjects in space. This also cannot be, for we cognize it through no forces that can appear in space; but from this it does not follow that the absolute unity of the subject should not be transformed into a plurality of the same without alteration of the degree of reality in the whole.\nThe constitution of something as absolute subject, which does not inhere in others, signifies a force, [whose magnitude] which does not consist of a plurality of forces mutually determining one another, but [unity and] consists in degree.\nThat a being can exist as unity of the substance and nevertheless be dissolved into a plurality of the same, involves no contradiction. For it is not necessary[necessity] that plurality of the subjects must already have been beforehand and consequently that the substance must be thought as composed already before the dissolution, i.e. as containing that plurality of the subjects as parts, which are possible in it through the dissolution. For if this dissolution is merely the effect of the positing-outside-one-another of that which previously inhered in one, then the inherence is transformed into a subsistence and the accident into an absolute subject. This always happens in the dissolution of the degree. Here we never have the concept of plurality of the subject, because it is considered internally and not its [in] external relation (extensively) according to magnitude. Nevertheless the intensive magnitude can always be regarded as potential plurality of the subjects, which can be separated; for instead of all forces ceasing according to degree (whereby then also the |\nwhole subject would cease), so [all] these degrees (g of all forces) and with them also the concept of a subject in which they inhere (for this is not at all distinguished in the one from that in the other), can exist separated from one another. The resisting force of a body could decrease, in that degrees of it were lost entirely or also in that that which would be lacking to it, (g without a part that is outside the other being taken away, but each one, though with diminished degree, remaining.) existed as particular subject.\nP. II:\nThat we can [through that] conclude from immediate perception and indeed validly universally for all thinking beings that they must be simple [nature] beings, would be impossible, for perception, without a contradiction in the opposite [could] being encountered, can furnish no necessity: if here the thinking subject were not merely considered as object of its own inner sense, since only the subject of all one's own thoughts can be its object, consequently, since it must be distinguished from all objects and their manifoldness, can be represented through nothing but the unity of *apperception*, but on that account also represents nothing at all but merely a something without further predicates (as subject). [If] Thus thinking can take place only in the unity of *apperception*. Outside me I can perceive no thought, consequently also not a thinking subject as such. A thinking being can thus [out of] through the united representation of different (g outside one another) thinking subjects represent its thoughts to itself as having arisen.\nOn existence in subjects in general.\nWe can think only through judgments, because we have concepts only through the (g necessary) unity of the consciousness of the manifold of representations. In judging, everything that we think of the object is predicate and the object that we think is in a further *respectu* |\nitself predicate. In the existing we must nevertheless think subjects in themselves, which are not predicates, but our concept of their reality is none other than that of reality and their inherence, consequently force and that of certain degrees. Only the subject remains merely a something. But every degree can be represented as composed of smaller ones and each as belonging to one subject, then it is a *compositum*, or all to one subject, then it is *simplex*. Whether now that which has existed as unity of the subject, i.e. a great degree of force, can exist as plurality of the subjects and what it depends on, that something which existed (g as) unity of the subject should exist as plurality, cannot be explained at all nor refuted.\nOn Metaphysics.\nI have proved that human reason in its speculative use [not get further] can extend itself to no other objects than to objects of a possible experience and of [them] these also nothing more than can be given in any possible experience, that consequently, because metaphysics places its importance not in those cognitions that [through] find themselves on the way of experience [confirm] or at least let themselves be confirmed through it, but rather in that which goes beyond the boundary of all possible experience, all dogmatic utility of the same [entirely] falls away, indeed that its existence even (g as useless) would have to fall away, if [the] cognitions that we actually have *a priori* and without the aid of experience did not let us believe that its use, since those are independent of experience, could well reach further than experience, and from there again important cognitions through false but deceptive judgments [Einwü] attacks and difficulties could arise. Now it depended on how cognitions *a priori*, consequently without being [gebor] derived from experience, could nevertheless go everywhere to objects of experience, indeed even to nothing else. This I accomplished in such a way that I showed intuitions *a priori* and also concepts *a priori*, of which the first presented nothing but the form of appearances, these the form of concepts of things in general, which appear, whose use, although they are representations *a priori* |\nare, (g itself) extends merely to experience. [That] Here everything that is to be accomplished was comprised in one problem: how are synthetic cognitions *a priori* possible?",[]],"reflm:5651":["p. II:\nA root is a quotient that is at the same time the *divisor* of a given magnitude. In every division two magnitudes must be given: the *dividendus* and the *divisor*, and the quotient, i.e. the number, is to be found, that becomes from the unity just as the *dividendus* from the *divisor*. Thus the relation of the *dividendus* to the *divisor* is given, and (g now) the relation of the *divisor* to unity is to be found, which (g is equal to the previous one and what) the quotient expresses. But is extraction of the root: only the *dividendus* is given* as (g the) 2nd power of an unknown magnitude x, which is to be found, namely a:x\n= x:1, thus here –9:x\n= x:1. so that x2 = –9. Now x may be +x or –x, so x2 is always =9, [and] but not =–9.\n1:3 = –3:–9 | √–9 = (√9) × (–√9) [not (√9)× √–9] = 3 × -3.\n*(g the *divisor*, which is at the same time quotient, is to be found, thus the middle geometrical proportional number between the given one and unity. The unity must here at every time be positive, otherwise –9 cannot [with it only] the product breaks off.)\n[The Gro] The [un]conditioned connection (g in an unconditioned totality) is expressed through the sign –, that breaks off.\nIf for a *substantia phaenomenon* (substance as appearance) the substantial, for a *causa phaenomenon* (cause as appearance) the (g sensibly) unconditioned cause, for a *commercium* (community) the unconditioned unity of different substances in their inter|action\nis to be found, or the ground of the same: then this unconditioned is -a. But this multiplied with a gives [du] in the whole –, i.e. that the whole lies outside sensibility and for such a magnitude no root can be found. [but] But indeed the root as category only designated with + and – at the same time, which in the first case is a[s] *phaenomenon* (g but not whole) and in the second a [phaen] *noumenon*, which would indeed be positive, but cannot be given, hence it signifies only the root with the preceding sign –, so far as it multiplied with itself without sign (as category) [ab] something: the concept [ein abso] of a whole with the sign – of an absolute whole, which is yet impossible, signifies. The regulative principle sticks here in the taking away of all boundaries from the sensible, i.e. the progress into the infinite and the assumption of a supersensible √–a, which however cannot be given.\nThe categories of quantity come near to addition, those of quality to subtraction, those of relation to multiplication, those of modality to division, and, if all these are to be regarded as sensibly unconditioned compositions, then the latter are impossible roots.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; Zahl = number; Einheit = unity; Verhältnis = relation (both for the arithmetical relation and the category); Verknüpfung = connection; Unbedingte/unbedingt = unconditioned; Totalität = totality (same English word as Allheit).\n- Original has Dividendus/divisor/Qvotient/Product in Latinate forms; the Latin ones are italicized, the German-spelled ones are not. Categorie = category.\n- The Academy signs (g ...), [ ... ] and | are carried over as they stand; (g = later insertion). Fragments such as [Die Gro], [du], [ab], [ein abso], [phaen] are broken-off readings and are not completed.\n- 'die bricht ab' and 'das Product bricht ab': the German is a bare statement of breaking off; 'die' could be relative or demonstrative; left as is. The sentence 'Allein ist Ausziehung der Wurzel: ...' is kept in its incomplete construction.\n- 'ein[s]' rendered 'a[s]'; the bracketed letter is the Academy deletion.\n- Wechselwirkung = interaction; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Grenze = boundary; Fortschritt rendered 'progress'; Wegnehmung = 'taking away'; Annehmung = 'assumption'; übersinnlich = supersensible.",[]],"reflm:5652":["p. I:\nThe similarity of the categories with the *species Arithmetices* (species of arithmetic).\n1. Those of magnitude through addition, 2. of quality through subtraction of the parts from the whole (that is given), 3. of causality cognized through multiplication, for there a state of the world becomes out of a preceding one just as this one out of the preceding. They are actually so generated, and in the series of causes is true multiplication of appearances. 4. Of modality through division, since the cause is to be found |\nwhich arises out of the first in such a way as the given effects each from its causes.\nIn all this it is to be noted that the categories are here applied only to appearances. Hence, because quality in the appearance is the object of sensation (the empirical in space), it is given as unity and only divisibility can be thought in it. — On empty space and time in the case of quality and the proof that it cannot be experienced, but also never proved and, since its opposite also cannot be proved, one is compelled always to assume as small degrees of filling as is needed for explanation of the appearances.\nThe ideal of the universal original being is to be compared with the irrational magnitude. For to seek the square root means: to seek that number for a given one, out of which this becomes just as that unknown one out of unity. Thus to seek that cause which is necessary through itself. It is an unnameable root, for [es] it can from no alleged unity [in ihr selbst] become so, as the world becomes out of it, i.e. the necessity of the effect from a cause can be completely given, but the necessity of the cause, that is not effect, requires a generating concept, which is not encountered in our whole reason, but yet is not contradictory.\n––––––––––\nIn is the intuition as magnitude, A the reality, –A the *remotion* (removal) of the same. A–A the objective negation =0. [A] d × 0 (*h.e. determinatio, qvae semet ipsam tollit*, that is, a determination that cancels itself) *est impossibile* (is impossible). *Substantia determinat temporis quantitatem* (substance determines the quantity of time) [*Mutatio et*], *Causalitas successionem et positum in tempore subordinatorum* (causality, the succession and the position in time of things subordinated), *commercium simultaneitatem* (community, simultaneity). *Substantia est =ant, accidens at. Causa adt, causatum dta.*\n––––––––––\nThe general refutation of the empirical origin of time and space is that both give synthetic propositions *a priori*, and from this follows also the ideality of the same as intuitions.\n===NOTES===\n- Wirklich rendered 'actually'; Wirklichkeit would be 'actuality'/'reality'. Realität = reality; Erscheinung = appearance; Anschauung = intuition; Empfindung = sensation; Zustand = state; Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; Grund not present here.\n- 'erkannt' = 'cognized' (erkennen); 'Erfahren werden' = 'be experienced' (Erfahrung = experience); 'Beweis' = proof; 'Erklärung' = explanation.\n- 'Urwesen' = 'original being' (Wesen = being); 'Irrationalgröße' = 'irrational magnitude'; 'wiedersprechend' = 'contradictory' (Widerspruch = contradiction).\n- 'In is the intuition as magnitude': the German reads 'In ist die Anschauung als Große'; a sign or word before 'ist' appears to be missing or unreadable; not supplied. Likewise 'ant', 'at', 'adt', 'dta', '[A] d' are left as printed and may be abbreviations or damaged readings.\n- *remotion* is printed in the German text in Latinate form; the English gloss is added by me. English glosses of the Latin are given in parentheses at first use only.\n- '[es]', '[in ihr selbst]', '[Mutatio et]', | are Academy apparatus (deletions, line/page breaks) carried over unchanged.",[]],"reflm:5653":["S. I:\nAgainst (g material) idealism.\nIt is grounded on this, that we are immediately conscious of our own existence, but of outer things only through an inference from the immediate consciousness of mere representations of [something] things outside us to the existence of the same, which inference, however, is not evident in its consequence, as the property of our imagination [known to us] proves, which is a faculty of representing to ourselves intuitively objects even without their presence.\nAgainst this argument it is sufficient to adduce only this, that the transcendental consciousness of ourselves, which accompanies the spontaneity of all our actions of the understanding, but which consists in the mere I without the determination of my existence in time, is indeed immediate, but the empirical consciousness of myself, [i.e. d] which constitutes inner sense (as that the form of the intellectuality of my subject), by no means takes place immediately, and that the consciousness of other things outside me [and] (which must also be presupposed as intellectual and insofar is not a representation of them in space, but can be called intellectual intuition, through which we have no cognition of things) and the determination of their existence in space must be at the same time with the determination of my existence in time, I thus of my own empirically determined existence no more than of that of the things (which I do not know as to what they are in themselves) outside me.\nFor in space alone do we posit the persistent, in time is incessant change. Now, however, the determination of the existence of a thing in time, i.e. in such a change, is impossible without combining with its intuition also the persistent. This must therefore be intuited outside us as object of outer sense [given]. But since I [insofar as I] am at the same time determining my existence and thus insofar am not empirically conscious of myself, so I also cannot insofar be [conscious] of the persistent outside me empirically, i.e. be conscious of it as given in space, but only of my determination of the representation of the same, insofar as I am merely affected by them according to the form of space, [which] in that I draw the same and thereby at the same time become conscious of my own existence in time.\nThe intuition of a thing as outside me presupposes the consciousness of a determinability of my subject, in which I am not myself determining, which thus does not belong to spontaneity, because the determining is not in me. And in fact I cannot think any space as in me*. Thus the possibility of representing [a] things in space [as] in intuition is [merely] grounded on the consciousness of a determination through other things, which signifies nothing further than the original passivity of me, in which I am not at all active. That the dream produces [just such] deception of existences outside me proves nothing against it; for outer perceptions must in every case have preceded. To get originally a representation of something as outside me, without in fact being passive, is impossible. —\n*(s and through space [is] the representation of an object as outside me (in intuition) first gets reality. Conversely I would also get through space the concept of the existence of something outside me, if the concept of a relation, which belongs to *commercio*, and indeed as given in perception, did not lie at the ground. This concept, however, is that of the mere passivity in a sta|te\nof representations. That this is not inferred, because we do not perceive the cause of the existence of a representation in us, but [in de] an immediate perception is, must be proved. — If we were affected merely by ourselves, without however noticing this spontaneity, then only the form of time would be encountered in our intuition: and we would not be able to represent to ourselves any space (an existence outside us). The empirical consciousness as determination of my existence in time would thus go around in a circle and presuppose itself — but above all be impossible, in that even the representation of the persistent would be lacking, in which is no continuous synthesis as in time.)\nS. II:\nOn the margin:\n(g That this is the only possible ground of proof.)\nAbove and below 30830-3098:\n(g That we must always carry out determination of space and of time at the same time, but just as little may thereby determine our existence in space as the existence of the things of space in time.\nPersistence inheres inwardly in the representation of space, as Newton says. The persistence of the form in our mind is not the same (for the form of time is just as persistent), but as representation of something [persistent] outside us, which we place beneath all determination of time and therefore (g as) persistent represent, and thus also cannot regard it as spontaneity of self-determination. — The proposition is: [our] the empirical consciousness of our existence in time is necessarily combined with the empirical consciousness of a relation of something outside us, and the one is just as little a deception from a false inference, also (g quite) just as little an inference, as the other.)\nThe representation of space lies at the ground of the determination of time because of persistence (likewise only in it can one through a line that I draw, in that I am conscious of my synthesis merely in the subject, |\nget a representation of time as magnitude). Now the persistent cannot [a determination] be merely thought in the determination of time and belong to the spontaneity of self-determination, for then it would not lie at the ground of the determination of time. Consequently it must be represented in relation to the mere receptivity of the mind, i.e. in relation to something affecting, which is distinguished from me, and this representation cannot be inferred, but must be original.\n––––––––––\nNot everything that is in time is at the same time in space, e.g. my representations: but everything that is in space is in time. In time I represent only namely myself as well for myself alone as also in community, and indeed not through inferences, but immediately, i.e. a *correlatum* to my state, without however cognizing the same, and the sensible, but real representation of this outer relation is space, this representation itself, however, thus also everything that is represented in space, is in time.\nThat, when I make myself the object, space is not in me, but (g nevertheless) in the formal subjective condition of the empirical consciousness of myself, i.e. in time, proves that something outside me, i.e. what I must represent in another way than myself, is combined with the empirical consciousness of myself and this (g is) at the same time a consciousness of an outer relation, without which I could not empirically determine my own existence.\nIt comes down to this, that I could become conscious of myself in an outer relation through a particular sense, which however is required for the (g time-)determination of inner sense. Space proves a representation that is not related to the subject (g as object); for otherwise it would be the representation of time. That it is now not related to that, but immediately to something distinct from the subject as existing, that (g is) the consciousness [of the same] of the object as thing outside me. Thus that we have an outer sense and that even imagination can impress images on us only in relation to the same, that is the proof of dualism.\nAll objects of the senses are in time; but not everything that is in time (i.e. all objects) is in space. If now, however, all representations of things outside us were only objects of inner sense and representations of ourselves, then [I would have to all things] the [representations] objects of inner sense would be at the same time all objects, [also] and space would itself be time.\nThe proof of dualism is grounded on this, that the determination of our existence in time [unver] by means of the representation of space contradicts itself, if one did not consider this as the consciousness of an entirely other relation than that of the representations in us to the subject, namely as the perception of the relation of our subject to other things and space as the mere form of this intuition. For if the space-[representation]perception were grounded merely on ourselves without an object outside us, it would at least be possible to become conscious of [the ground of] these representations as [inne] containing merely a relation to the subject. Since, however, in the latter way only the intuition of time ever comes out, the object that we represent to ourselves as spatial must rest on a representation of something other than our subject. That we can, however, be conscious of an outer relation, without being able ever to cognize the object itself, but only the form of this relation of ourselves to the presence of the same [to], makes no difficulty. Nor that in dreams and lively fantasies it is possible, without actuality of the object Yet the subjective of this intuition makes no objection. For [the rela] without an outer sense, [which we however] whose representations we only repeat and compose in another way (as also happens with inner sense in fantasizing), we would not be able to have any dreams at all.\nOn the left half of the page, crosswise:\nOn idealism.\nOn the margin (Phase θ3-4):\nPreface.\nIf we, (g in order) to explain the purposiveness of the things of the world, cite a cause that is the cause according to the analogy of an understanding, then that is a tautological explanation, for end signifies that which has such a form as if the representation of the thing were at the same time the cause of the same. If, however, we apply just this causality (g to) the world, insofar as it is a moral whole, and to the reality of its laws through their result, namely the highest good, then it sounds different. For there it is not tautologically explained *idem per idem*, but an effect is brought out that would not have followed from the world alone.\nNow it still depends on whether one can say God is the cause of substance without having at the same time determined all its actions. We indeed do not have the slightest concept of the possibility of such a causality nor an example of the reality of the same. But if this is assumed, it would still concern only the intelligible, with regard to which the concept of freedom is already of itself necessarily combined with the concept of a substance; because substance must be the last subject of its actions and cannot itself be the manner of acting of another. How the substance of the world may be constituted in itself we do not know. But (g it in) its empirically cognizable character, which concerns merely appearances, is no thing nor object of creation; but in it everything is always determined inwardly in the world of sense, which does no detriment to the freedom of the intelligible. If one now said: in the intelligible character everything is good (because time vanishes there), only the causality through |\nfreedom is not to be comprehended at all. But the possibility of being cause of a substance, even less. The difficulties drawn from this against freedom are therefore null, because one can combine no concept with the hypothesis.",[]],"reflm:5654":["1788). L Bl. D. 7. p. I, II. R I 200-202.\np. I:\nAgainst idealism.\nIf there were no outer objects of our senses, hence no sense at all, but only imagination, it would still at least become possible to become conscious of this its action as a spontaneity; but then this representation would belong only to inner sense and contain nothing persistent that could lie at the ground of the determination of our existence in empirical consciousness. The mind must therefore (g itself) be immediately conscious of a representation of outer sense as such, i.e. not through an inference from the representation as effect to something outer as cause, which, because it is valid only [Hyp] as hypothesis, contains no certainty.\nBut how [is] can the consciousness of a representation of the senses [yet not] (g as) merely [as] passive determination take place and become conscious of the object of it as outer, but at the same time also of it or its appearance as persistent?\nHere it is now to be noted that every object signifies something distinguished from the representation, which however is only in the understanding, hence inner sense itself, which makes us ourselves into the object of our representations, (g itself to) something different from our self (as [Gegen] transcendental object of *apperception*) relates. If we therefore did not relate the representations to something different from ourselves, they would never yield cognition of objects; for, as concerns inner sense, it consists only in the relation of the representations, whether they may signify something or nothing, to the subject.\n––––––––––\nThe above proof wants to say this much: if there were not an outer |\nsense, i.e. a faculty to become immediately (without syllogism) conscious of something [outside] as outside us and, in contrast, of ourselves in relation, then not even the representation of outer things as such in us as belonging to intuition would be possible, i.e. not even space itself. For inner sense can contain nothing but [the sequel] the time-relation of our representations.\n––––––––––\nOne can indeed posit time in oneself, but not oneself in time and determine oneself therein, and yet empirical self-consciousness consists in this. In order, therefore, to determine one's existence in time, one must intuit oneself in outer relation with something else, [in] which for precisely that reason must be considered as persistent.\nBecause time cannot be perceived outwardly in things, [so] in that it is only a determination of inner sense, we can determine ourselves in time [represent] only insofar as we stand in relation with things outside us and consider ourselves therein, and being outside us brings along an existence, [which kei] insofar as it is not subject to any change, i.e. being persistent.\nThe existence of a thing in time cannot be determined through the relation of its representation [to] in the imagination to other representations of the same, but as a representation of sense to that which [in A] is persistent in the objects of the same.\np. II:\nI [still work until] climb even through heavy subtleties to the summit of the principles, not so much as if healthy understanding could arrive at it without this detour, but in order entirely to take the force from all sophistical subtleties that rise up against it.",[]],"reflm:5655":["S. I:\nTrichotomy. Every relation of representations through concepts has a threefold dimension: 1. the relation of a representation to consciousness, 2. of one (g other) representation to consciousness, 3. the connection of both together in one consciousness. Only thereby does the connection of representations among one another first become possible (*connexa vni tertio sunt connexa inter se*).\n––––––––––\nI cannot say (g through inner sense): [that I am in space but] space (g or relation of space) is in me, but time or the relation of time is in me. On the other hand time is in me, and I am in time. That [I as] the thinking being [through] in the representation of inner sense is merely appearance to itself, signifies nothing further than if I say: I, in whom the relation of time alone is to be encountered, am in time. The *continens* is at once *contentum*.\nThe proposition: I am (g of myself) [a] (g as) object [of] of a sense (g conscious), means just as much as: I cognize myself in the appearance before, namely, how my existence is given to me for thinking. It also means as much as: I am in time, but the relations of time are merely in me (it is not possible to represent them (g as) outside me, like those of space, although the representation of the latter too is in me).\nIt is not possible that I [merely] as merely in me intuitive the relation of time [on the other hand myself] and myself (g itself) nevertheless also in this time as object of intuition [think] represent, [without myself] if this consciousness concerned me as thing in itself.\nI intuit myself in time, but not in space; but time is (g a relation) in me, but space is [something] a relation outside me.\nThat I am in time, which yet is a mere relation in me, consequently the *continens* a *contentum* and I in myself, that already indicates that [that I when I posit myself in time as] I think myself in twofold meaning [posit].\nS. II:\n† Likewise, when I pay attention to the representations of inner sense, |\nI dissolve everything into nothing but relations of time, and the absolute for the understanding is lacking. — [For if we except the feeling of pleasure which however is no cognition then is] Everything (g is) in us representation, [in (the] posited in relations of time, and if we ask what then it represents, so [have we] it is either [outer things] the outer [in space], of which we have just seen that it relates to nothing but relations of space, to which the thing in itself is uncognizable for us, or the inner relation of these representations in time to one another, where the pure synthesis, which the concepts of the understanding express, is again nothing other than connection of these representations [is nu] with regard to the unity of time, where the feeling of pleasure and the (g with it connected) faculty of desire furnishes only the mere relation of those representations to the subject without cognition or to the object through the determination of the causality of the subject, hence also no cognition of the thing in itself, and of this nothing remains but the idea of something [which] what (g my) self-consciousness independent of all these conditions of time [my] indicates as an object [best its nature determined and ken], but gives nothing to hand [as a G] what it would be cognizable in itself and without relation to the causality of myself in the world of sense.\nThat which [in empirical consciousness] speaks decisively for the (g empirical) consciousness of myself as an appearance, not as thing itself, is the mere manner of determination of my existence in this consciousness. Time is as a sum total of relations in me [and nonetheless am I] (not of the relations outside me) [and yet I say I am in time], i.e. I must presuppose my existence (g time is determination of my existence) in order to be able to think time (g as determination [of the same] of this my and all things outside me existence). Nevertheless I also say: I am in time, i.e. (g I am determination of time) I must presuppose time in order (g it through) my existence (empirically) [cognize] to be able to determine. Were now my existence here in the same meaning (g to) be understood, there would be in this |\na contradiction. Thus my existence, which I presuppose, must be taken in another meaning than just the same, when I consider it only as determination of time. Now, however, it is before all determination merely the existence of a thing, nevertheless, although not determined in time, yet as existence (in itself) thoroughly determined, although to me as such uncognized; thus it is, so far as time must be presupposed in order to determine me and my existence, mere appearance. Nothing is thereby taken from the cognition from experience of myself, only this is not extended to the whole [Erk], although to the whole only possible cognition, and so the supersensible is left over, but at the same time every attempt to determine it theoretically is declared extravagant.\nThe first signifies: all things outside me are appearances, for the [subjective] condition to determine their existence is in me. — The second: I myself am appearance, and time, which is merely in me, can serve only myself as condition, so far as I distinguish my pure I from it.",[]],"reflm:5656":["1788).\nRemark of Kant on the address page of the letter of Ch. Fr. Heilsberg of 23 Nov. 1788 (2 X 554) in the III Dorpat volume of letters, leaf 11':\nThat an object corresponds to the categories (g of magnitude) can be shown only in a sensible intuition. If one departs from this even in a single case, the whole beautiful [Abg] precision of the system, in that one also has insight into the ground whence all cognition *a priori* comes, is lost.\nConcepts of number are those which first determine the concept of time as a *quanti*. They do not presuppose the concept of time, but only the sensible form of it and first determine the concept of time as a *quanti*. Thereby, however, neither is an object in time (of the kind that space is, whose parts are all at the same time), nor [E] my own existence in time determined, but merely the syn|thetic\nunity of the manifold, whereby a magnitude is possible, is given, thus for the concept of magnitude, without touching thereby any quality of it (because they can all only be given in time), reality is procured.\nImputation is the estimation of an action (the free one according to laws) with regard to its origin from freedom. This origin, however, can only [through] be thought, so far as it is under moral laws, for that is the causality from freedom.",[]],"reflm:5657":["S. I:\nNB. On transcendent ideas. I cannot even place the categories under the concepts of the original being and of the supersensible in general. But when I call it cause, I thereby understand only a being from whose concept I could derive [the Or] my knowledge of the order of the world (the physical and the moral) just as I derive this from things in the world that I know through their effects. Thereby I properly attribute nothing to this being, but only think in addition an unknown to me principle for my theoretical, chiefly practical use of reason as of a being in the world, in which I am to consider and act, and indeed in accordance with a determination not merely restricted to the sensible. The taking to be true of my way of thinking in accordance with this principle, namely that it also agrees with the world in a wider [interconnection] domain and duration than I may have insight into, is the belief in such a being. The theoretical determination of it consists of nothing but words, which outside the relation of such a concept as principle of the practical (where it has an absolutely necessary interest) have no meaning.",[]],"reflm:5658":["Meiners: *Historia doctrinae de vero deo omnium rerum auctove atque rectore* (Lemgo. 1780) Part I p. 63\nMargin at top above the beginning of Section II, which deals with the Phoenicians:\nIf the concept of God precedes morals, it is conceived very immorally.\nThe Trinity proves an ancient moral concept.",[]],"reflm:5659":["In the same place Kant has, at I 131, in the sentence: Indi »tam bonos quam malos genios vel Deos vel Debos vel etiam Dewtas romano verbo nominare solent ... loca porro taetra et formidolosa, in quibus scelesti quondam et nefarii homines ob impia facta omni tormentorum genere cruciantur, Gehennae nomine indigitant« underlined in lead the words spaced out by me and set two exclamation marks in the margin beside the last word, beside the words »romano verbo«:\nCattle!",[]],"reflm:5660":["In the same place II 263 in the margin on the right next to the lines dealing with the Ionian natural philosophers, lines 7-8 from the top:\n*Thales Physicus, Pherecydes Theologus.*",[]],"reflm:5661":["-Sch. XI 1 pp. 261-3. Hb. IV pp. 499f. Ki. L pp. 193f.\nAnswer to the question, is it an experience that we think?\nAn empirical representation of which I am conscious is perception; that which I think in addition to the representation of the imagination by means of the apprehension and taking together (*comprehensio aesthetica*) of the manifold of perception is the empirical cognition of the object, and the judgment that expresses an empirical cognition is experience.\nIf I think a square a priori, I cannot say, |\nthis thought is experience; but this can well be said if I apprehend a figure already drawn in perception, and think the taking together of its manifold by means of the imagination under the concept of a square. In experience and through it I am taught by means of the senses; but if I think an object of the senses merely arbitrarily, then I am not taught by it, and in my representation I depend in nothing on the object, but am wholly the author of it.\nBut the consciousness of having such a thought is also not an experience; precisely because the thought is no experience, but consciousness in itself is nothing empirical. Nevertheless this thought produces an object of experience, or a determination of the mind that can be observed, insofar, namely, as it is affected by the faculty of thinking; I can therefore say, I have experienced what belongs to it in order to grasp in thought a figure of four equal sides and right angles in such a way that I can demonstrate its properties. This is the empirical consciousness of the determination of my state in time through thinking; thinking itself, even though it also occurs in time, takes no account of time at all when the properties of a figure are to be thought. But experience is impossible without combining a determination of time with it, because in it I am passive and feel myself affected in accordance with the formal condition of inner sense.\nThe consciousness, when I carry out an experience, is representation of my existence, insofar as it is empirically determined, i.e. in time. Now if this consciousness were in turn itself empirical, then the same determination of time would in turn have to be represented as contained under the conditions of the determination of time of my state. Thus yet another time would have to be thought, under which (not in which) the time that constitutes the formal condition of my inner experience would be contained. Thus there would be a time in which and together with which a given time elapsed, which is absurd. But the consciousness of carrying out an experience, or also of thinking in general, is a transcendental consciousness, not experience.\nRemarks on this essay.\nThe action of the imagination of giving an intuition to a concept is *exhibitio*. The action of the imagination of making a concept out of an empirical intuition is *comprehensio*.\nApprehension of the imagination, *apprehensio aesthetica*. Taking together of the same, *comprehensio aesthetica* (aesthetic comprehending), I take the manifold together into a whole representation and thus it receives a certain form.",[]],"reflm:5662":["-Sch. XI 1 pp. 263-5. Hb. IV pp. 500-2. Ki. L pp. 194-6.\nOn miracles.\nNo motion can be produced in the world either by a miracle or by a spiritual being without effecting just as much motion in the opposite direction, consequently according to laws of effect and counter-effect of matter, for otherwise a motion of the universe in empty space would arise.\nBut also no alteration in the world (thus no beginning of that motion) can arise without being determined by causes in the world according to laws of nature in general, thus not through freedom or genuine miracles; for since time does not determine the order of occurrences, but conversely the occurrences, i.e. the appearances according to the law of nature (of causality), determine time, an occurrence that happened or was determined in time independently of this would presuppose a change in empty time, consequently the world itself would be determined as to its state in absolute time.\nRemarks.\n1. One can divide miracles into outer and inner, i.e. into alterations of the appearance for outer and for inner sense. The former happen in space, the latter in time. If miracles in space were possible, then it would be possible for appearances to happen in which effect and counter-effect are not equally great. For all alterations in space are motion. But a motion that is to be produced by a miracle, the cause of which is not to be sought in the appearances. But the law of effect and |\ncounter-effect rests on this, that cause and effect belong to the world of sense (to the appearances), i.e. are represented in relative space; since this now does not hold of the cause in the case of miracles in space, they will also not stand under the law of effect and counter-effect. If now a motion is effected by a miracle, then, since it does not stand under the law of effect and counter-effect, the *centrum gravitatis* of the world will be altered through it, i.e. in other words, the world would move in empty space; but a motion in empty space is a contradiction, for it would be the relation of a thing to a nothing, since empty space is a mere idea.\nIn a similar way it is proved that there can be no miracles in regard to the appearances in time. For an appearance in time is a miracle if its cause cannot be given in time, does not stand under its conditions. But since only by the fact that both cause and effect belong to the appearances can the latter be determined in relative time, this will not be able to happen with an effect that is produced by a miracle, because its cause does not belong to the appearances. Thus a supernatural occurrence will be determined not in relative but in absolute (empty) time. But a determination in empty time is a contradiction, because for every relation two correlata must be given.\n2. A miracle is an occurrence whose ground is not to be found in nature. It is either a *miraculum rigorosum* (strict miracle), which has its ground in a thing outside the world (thus not in nature); or a *miraculum comparativum* (comparative miracle), which has its ground indeed in a nature, but in one whose laws we do not know; of the latter kind are the things that are ascribed to spirits. A *miraculum rigorosum* is either *materiale*, where the force that produced the miracle is also outside the world, or *formale*, where the force is indeed in the world, but its determination is found outside the world, e.g. if one held the drying up of the Red Sea at the passage of the children of Israel to be a miracle, then it is a *miraculum materiale* if one gives it out as an immediate effect of the Deity; whereas |\na *miraculum formale* if one lets it dry up through a wind, which however was sent by the Deity.\nFurther, the *miraculum* is either *occasionale* or *praestabilitum*. In the first case one assumes that the Deity stepped in immediately; but in the other one lets the occurrence be produced through a series of causes and effects, all of which are there for the sake of this one occurrence. —",[]],"reflm:5663":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 271f. Hb. IV S. 507. Ki. L S. 202.\n    On the formal and material meaning of some words.\nThere are several words that, used in the *singulari*, have a different sense than when one uses them in the *plurali*; they are then to be taken in the *singulari* in formal, in the *plurali* in material meaning: these are unity, perfection, truth, possibility*. Unity used in the *singulari* is qualitative, used in the *plurali* quantitative. Qualitative unity is to be considered like the ground of the whole, quantitative like a part of the whole. Thus one cannot say, e.g., that warmth consists of lukewarmnesses, one therefore does not determine its magnitude according to the parts that it contains, but according to the effects that it produces, e.g. that it extends bodies, and one can therefore not ascribe to it a proper magnitude, but a degree; the unity that is found with it is therefore qualitative unity. — The unities of which discrete magnitudes (numbers) consist are quantitative unities.\nPerfection (used *formaliter*) of a thing is the agreement of its realities to an idea; perfections (used *materialiter*) are these realities**.\nTruth in the *singulari* (used *formaliter* and *qualitative*) is the agreement of our cognition of an object with it; truths in the *plurali* (used *materialiter* and *quantitative*) are true propositions.\nPossibility of an object (used *formaliter* and *qualitative*); possibilities (used *materialiter* and *quantitative*), objects, insofar as they are possible.\n*(g One sees that this is grounded on the titles of the categories: quantity, quality, relation and modality.)\n**(g Thus one speaks of the perfection of a clock, insofar as there is found in it what one can expect of a good clock. Perfections of a clock are properties of it that agree with the concept of a good clock. — But one must also still distinguish quantitative and qualitative perfection from perfection.)",[]],"reflm:5664":["Science is a whole 1. as aggregate; 2. as series. Science of the first principles.\n( s subord: *utrinqve* terminated  coordinated *in infinitum* )",[]],"reflm:5665":["Logic deals with thinking without object. Physics with the cognition of [Geg] things from experience. Metaphysics with their cognition before all experience. The origin is twofold: 1. how we have arrived at it .. psychology; 2. how the cognitions *a priori* are possible. transcendental philosophy.",[]],"reflm:5666":["The preceding goes before either in the way: principles *a priori*, or in origin, i.e. historically.",[]],"reflm:5667":["Metaphysics is the system [of the principles] of all cognition *a priori* (g from concepts) in general.\nThe science of the possibility, domain etc. etc. of cognition *a priori* is transcendental philosophy. Sum total of metaphysics. To draw transcendental philosophy and boundaries from the available pure reason: is critique of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:5668":["M I:\nSome cognitions are *secundum quid a priori*, others *simpliciter*, where there is nothing empirical.\nM II:\nThe distinguishing mark of cognition *a priori* is necessity.",[]],"reflm:5669":["In cognition one can look to connection with grounds and sequels and to generation.",[]],"reflm:5670":["Every cognition that is respectively *a priori* is called principle.",[]],"reflm:5671":["All our cognitions are: 1. *a priori* or *posteriori* connected with others; or 2. simply *a priori*, which cannot be drawn from experience (properly not from the senses), or simply *a posteriori*.",[]],"reflm:5672":["All cognition has its origin either *a posteriori*: from experience, or *a priori*. Metaphysics is a science of the cognitions *a priori*.\nThe *principia* of philosophy are either *a posteriori*: [physiol:] physical, or *priori*: metaphysical.",[]],"reflm:5673":["Our cognition consists before the understanding in an interconnection of grounds and sequels, of the derived and the original.\nWe distinguish cognition:\n1. according to connection,\n2. according to its way, origin.\nTwofold connection: subordination and coordination; in the latter there are no boundaries.",[]],"reflm:5674":["Metaphysics is science of the principles of all cognition *a priori* and of all cognition that follows from these principles. Mathematics contains such *principia*, but is not science of the possibility of these principles.",[]],"reflm:5675":["Metaphysics cannot be the foundation of religion, but well its bulwark, and indeed as such it is indispensable. For the opponent possesses a dialectical metaphysics, to which we must oppose the critical one; and this opponent lies in every natural human reason.",[]],"reflm:5676":["Out of faint-heartedness one becomes an empiricist, the other a mystic; the first asserts that raw understanding better than cultivated judgments.",[]],"reflm:5677":["One can either attain to science in metaphysics, or not: skeptical. Reason can be studied through entirely.",[]],"reflm:5678":["The two cardinal propositions: God and a future world.",[]],"reflm:5679":["Metaphysics deals either with objects of pure reason or with objects of experience through pure reason, not according to empirical, but rational principles.",[]],"reflm:5680":["1. Of principles of pure reason: transcendental philosophy 2. *Metaphysica applicata* to objects: nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:5681":["In all philosophy what is properly philosophical is the metaphysics of the science. All sciences in which reason is used have their metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:5682":["Instead of the distinction of synthesis and analysis: *synthesis regressiva* and *progressiva*.",[]],"reflm:5683":["The mere possibility of the proposition that a God is, is sufficient for religion, and this possibility is apodictically certain.",[]],"reflm:5684":["Whence is the supreme analytic principle expressed negatively?\nThe *principium summum* is expressed negatively, in order to designate necessity. Principle of negative judgments.",[]],"reflm:5685":["In and \nThe principle of the determination of the undetermined: Every thing is in itself thoroughgoingly determined, but not always through its concept; the latter is: *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5686":["*Principium contradictionis* is the principle of all logical possibility, i.e. of concepts, insofar as such can be cognized *a priori*, but not of things.",[]],"reflm:5687":["The possibility of analytic combination can be seen *a priori*, but not of the synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5688":["The possibility of a concept rests on this, that it does not contradict itself; the possibility of a thing, on this, that the concept has objective reality, that an example of it can be given, i.e. an object corresponds to it, e.g. *ens absolute necessarium*.",[]],"reflm:5689":["Dogmatic metaphysics is that which proceeds without critical investigation of the main question: how is synthetic cognition *a priori* possible? So it is also with history without critique.",[]],"reflm:5690":["Everything impossible *per se* is also [hypothetically] absolutely impossible; not everything possible *in se* is absolutely possible, but only *sub hypothesi aliqua* (under some hypothesis).",[]],"reflm:5691":["Possibility is either inner or outer. The latter is either restrictive outer possibility under certain conditions or absolute in every aim.",[]],"reflm:5692":["»Only under a condition« or: »also possible under it«, is opposed to the inner: either under a restrictive condition |\n(g as sequel) *hypothetice* or [*in nexu*] under an extensive one as ground. Finally absolutely possible without any restriction and the greatest extensive possibility.",[]],"reflm:5693":["Hypothetical possibility can be considered restrictively and also extensively. The latter, if it is complete, is absolute possibility. For in the more numerous aim and outer relation something is possible, the more it approaches absolute possibility.",[]],"reflm:5694":["What contradicts itself is impossible in every aim, i.e. in itself nothing, because it is internally impossible. but this does not hold conversely; what, considered in every aim, is not possible, is in itself impossible.\nDownright impossible is something other than in every aim. The former refers to restrictive, the latter to extensive conditions.",[]],"reflm:5695":["* *In thesi* (s according to the concept: logical) possible, but not *in hypothesi*. (s no real possibility.)\n*(s »*in hypothesi*« is extensive, »*sub hypothesi*« restrictive.)",[]],"reflm:5696":["In general *in thesi* possible or generally *in hypothesi*; this is wider.",[]],"reflm:5697":["At the very top of the page:\nIt is remarkable that the categories of quantity and quality have no *opposita* and *correlata*, as those of relation and modality do. (although those of quality contain an *oppositum* as gradation, namely negation.) The ground of this seems to be this, that the first contain merely the apprehension of intuition and produce it synthetically, but the second are relation-concepts either of the objects among one another or to the faculty of cognition.",[]],"reflm:5698":["Above and \nHypothetically possible is something either *sub conditione restrictiva* or *constitutiva*. The first is the condition of agreement, which must not be conflicted with; the second the condition of derivation. What is impossible under no *conditione restrictiva* is absolutely possible, i.e. possible under every *hypothesi*.",[]],"reflm:5699":["The *principium rationis* holds only of experience; for we can have no concept of a real ground except only through experience, since namely, when something is posited, something else follows according to a rule (consequently is determined). Thus we can also say nothing, from mere concepts, about the relation of things to real grounds.",[]],"reflm:5700":["Every thing (s existing) is in itself thoroughgoingly determined, but through its concept undetermined with regard to many possible predicates. Only a single thing is thoroughgoingly determined through its concept: *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5701":["The sequel does not determine the ground, but well the ground the sequel.\nAll predicates are determinations of an object in general.\n*Praedicatum logicum* (logical predicate) can be analytic;\n*determinatio est praedicatum syntheticum* (determination is a synthetic predicate).",[]],"reflm:5702":["My concept determines a thing or is determined in a certain way; synthetically no thing is determined through its concept, thus only in relation to another cognition, e.g. intuition or experience.",[]],"reflm:5703":["All determination is *synthesis*.",[]],"reflm:5704":["*Quo posito ponitur aliud, illo hoc non ideo determinatur. Si illo determinatur, illud est ratio.* (That upon whose positing another is posited does not for that reason determine this. If this is determined by it, that is the ground.)\nTo determine logically means either to affirm or to deny a predicate of a thing (copula in a judgment), regardless of the content; to determine metaphysically means to ascribe to a concept a predicate that in itself contains a being or excludes such a one. |\nWhat in itself excludes a being is negation, and only in the opposite can the real be encountered.",[]],"reflm:5705":["The first inner ground of that which belongs to the possibility of a thing is essence; of that which belongs to actuality: nature.",[]],"reflm:5706":["The first logical ground of all [determinations that the] predicate of a thing is the logical essence,\nthe first (g inner) real ground of everything that belongs to the existence of the thing, the nature.\nSubsequently (in phase ψ3) deletions were made and words added, so that the first sentence now has the following form: |\nThe first inner ground of everything that necessarily belongs to the concept of a thing is the logical essence. |\nIn addition, above the first two words of the Reflexion was added: |\nlogical, identity, |\nand at the end: |\nThe analytically first ground is the logical, the synthetically first ground the real essence. Nature. On the complaint that the essence of things remains hidden from us.",[]],"reflm:5707":["The subordination is analytic or synthetic, the latter carried up to the first inner real ground: essence.",[]],"reflm:5708":["On the rule in general.\nIt is the objective unity of the consciousness of the manifold of representations (consequently that which is valid universally). The rule is either empirical, if the condition of unity lies in mere perceptions. It can therefore be objective in no other way than in relation to possible experience as cognition of the objects of perception. The possibility of experience is therefore the ground of the [necessity] objective |\nvalidity of the rules of perception, and this possibility of experience is grounded on the necessary unity of the consciousness of the representations, insofar as cognition (of the objects) is to come from it. All representations must be represented in relation to a consciousness and thus as universally subjected to the [Bed] unity of consciousness (of this relation to consciousness we are not always conscious, and then the representation is obscure, but nevertheless always compared with this consciousness).",[]],"reflm:5709":["On the existence\nof outer things.\nIdealism is the opinion that we experience immediately only our own existence, but that of outer things only infer (which inference from effect to cause is in fact uncertain). But we can experience our own existence only insofar as we determine it in time, to which belongs the persistent, which representation has no object in us. [If it] On the mere imagining of a persistent outside us [Gr] this representation also cannot be grounded, for an imagining is impossible to which no corresponding object can be given. It is that which gives the object in intuition, and our representation, insofar as it belongs merely to the consciousness of ourselves, has no such object.",[]],"reflm:5710":["Everything that exists is thoroughgoingly determined; but this thoroughgoing determination does not constitute the concept of existence, but that a thing is posited absolutely and not merely in relation to its concept.\nI cognize existence through experience, but not the thoroughgoing determination; this happens through reason.",[]],"reflm:5711":["The proposition: »a thing A is actual« is synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5712":["*Possibile* etc etc. are no synthetic predicates.\nSomething is either merely thought (*a priori*) or given (also to be represented *a posteriori*) or given through this, that something is only thought in general (or that it itself is only thought): necessary (hypothetical or absolute).",[]],"reflm:5713":["Everything that exists is thoroughgoingly determined, but not conversely.",[]],"reflm:5714":["Being is then taken only problematically when it is to consider judgments in relation alone; but assertorically every time when it is considered in itself. But if the problematic *position* is at the same time analytic, it is not distinguished from the absolute and is apodictic.",[]],"reflm:5715":["God contains attributes, from each of which all the remaining can be inferred. Such a thing cannot belong under a species, but is possible only as a single one: *conceptus singularis*.",[]],"reflm:5716":["Beside and \nIn existence there is more than in possibility, but not in the existing thing.\nexistence is not determination of the thing, but of the understanding through the object.",[]],"reflm:5717":["That between possibility and actuality in things in themselves there is a difference, in that namely something is possible (g *omni respectu* and in |\nits thoroughgoing determination) although it is not actual, we cannot have insight into, because we would have to cognize the thoroughgoing determination of actual things.\nThat whose opposite in its thoroughgoing determination would be impossible would be absolutely necessary, i.e. necessary absolutely, not respectively to a concept. In such a way perhaps everything would be absolutely necessary, although considered merely respectively contingent (in a certain *respectu*).",[]],"reflm:5718":["The cause of our distinction of the possible from the actual is this: because we do not cognize the things through intuitions of the understanding, thus consider them first respectively to our faculty of thinking, afterwards to intuition.",[]],"reflm:5719":["Something is actual because it is possible, because, if it is possible only as *rationatum*, if the ground is actual, it must also be actual. But nothing is actual because it is merely possible, although everything that is possible may be actual. For possibility is a mere relation to our understanding, actuality either a connection with our fundamental experience space and time, which contain an original actuality, or a connection with an infinite understanding; here, however, an existence is always presupposed. But that we can cancel every being (of the real) proves not the possibility of non-being, but the dependence of our concept of being on the connection with experience.",[]],"reflm:5720":["The possibility of the thing is distinguished from the possibility of existence, and the possibility of the [not] opposite of the thing from the possibility of non-being; e.g. *possibilitas oppositi* in the finite, which therefore does not prove contingency.",[]],"reflm:5721":["How can the possibility of a thing (not merely of the concept) be cognized *a priori*. A triangle is possible from three sides, of which two together are greater than the third. A circle is possible. I can give them in pure intuition. What can be given in experience in general, thus is in accordance with the categories, is objectively possible; but the opposite is not therefore impossible.",[]],"reflm:5722":["We can assume no object as possible except that which we can exhibit in intuition, consequently set forth its actuality; otherwise, if the representation does not contradict itself, the thought is indeed possible, but not the thing.\nThe logical (g inner) possibility (g according to the proposition of contradiction) without real (g to which no intuition corresponds): empty concept.\nImpossibility we can cognize only according to the proposition of contradiction, when the discussion is of objects to which no intuition can be given.",[]],"reflm:5723":["The difference between possibility and actuality we posit in the connection with space and time, which we regard as in themselves necessary, hence as the foundations of all actuality. If we now consider the things merely according to the form of space and time, but not connected with it, they are merely possible. This difference must therefore fall away when the discussion is of the thing in itself. The second distinction of both concepts is merely logical: namely the undetermined is merely possible; in thoroughgoing determination only that is possible which is actual. For the former contains a mere relation of the object to the understanding, the latter with my existence. What is possible in thoroughgoing determination is, if it is possible as ground in every aim (consequently as *independens*), necessary. If it is possible only as sequel, then, if the possibility is thoroughgoing, there must be a ground for it. The concept of thoroughgoing determination is a relation to omniscience.",[]],"reflm:5724":["*non ens* [is ent]: non-thing;\nimpossible, empty\nconcept. or *ens rationis*: thought-entity*.\n[",[]],"reflm:5725":["an impossible, an empty, a negative concept; a mere form of representing things, which is not itself a thing: *ens imaginarium*; that whose concept itself is impossible is logically impossible, A non A =0: no concept. [If] The object that is impossible, although its concept is possible, is A × 0: *nihil privativum*, *non ens*.",[]],"reflm:5726":["17. E II 639. 597.\nSpace and time.\nOn the beginning, according to the distinction of analytic and synthetic cognition and the principle of contradiction. Every object of thinking is either something or nothing. Either insofar as no thought corresponds to the object, i.e., the thinking itself is nothing, i.e., contradicts itself, or to the thought (which does not contradict itself) no object ((g none) in intuition) corresponds. Thinking, considered subjectively as representation, before it is analyzed, always has an object; but if the thinking contradicts itself, then [it] the thought and for that reason the object is nothing, and both are struck out. Where the thought remains, there the object is problematic after the *analysis*.\nSomething can now either [posited] be merely posited or be repeatedly (*iterative*) posited, in order to bring about the representation of the object; in the latter case it is plurality, in the [second] first [unity] One. All plurality is therefore of the same kind, and the repeated positing is addition. [This] The object whose representation arises through the plurality of given ones is a *quantum*; and the representation of it as of an object that contains much in itself is that of magnitude. In all magnitude there is composition. That [what] out of which something is composed is called unity and is comparatively (i.e., out of the like unity) not composed, hence simple. Thus unities are relatively simple, but in themselves they can again be composed, i.e., be magnitudes.\nAn object of a certain kind is uniform in the same kind, e.g., space. As plurality, however, the unity can nevertheless be of a different kind from the *quantum*, and the unity can be a *quantum* that is in turn composed of unities of an entirely different kind. Accordingly not every plurality is a magnitude, |\nM 17: |\nbut that which is of the same kind with the object in which the magnitude is considered. What multitude, what counting, what number, what infinite magnitude may be: given (is contradictory) or only infinite progress, through which the magnitude is determined only negatively. Space and time are *quanta a priori*. *Quantum continuum et discretum*. Measure. The measure of magnitude in itself is totality, of the comparative ones the given unity (in intuition). We know only comparative magnitudes. Boundaries: space and time alone have them; magnitudes of pure thinking: limitations. [That which] In thinking there are limitations, in intuition boundaries.\nWhen the following category is at an end, it must be considered in combination with the preceding one. Right after the categories the concepts of comparison. Identity and diversity; similarity, equality, con|gruence.\n*oppositum et contrarium*. and thereafter the categories themselves compared. On division in general.\n===NOTES===\n- *obiect* (Object) is rendered \"object\" throughout; Gegenstand does not occur in the passage.\n- Vielheit = plurality; Menge = multitude; Größe = magnitude; Einheit = unity; Allheit = totality; Zählen = counting; Maaß = measure.\n- Grenzen = boundaries; schranken = limitations (glossary Grenze, Schranke).\n- Gleichartig / ungleichartig = \"of the same kind\" / \"of a different kind\"; gleichformig = \"uniform\"; Art = kind.\n- Eintheilung = division; Verbindung = combination; Anschauung = intuition; Fortschritt = progress (German; Latin *progressus* not given).\n- \"(g\" is the Academy apparatus sign for a later addition, carried over unchanged; \"identitaet und diversitaet\" and \"con|gruentz\" are German-spelled Latinate forms, rendered without italics.\n- Wording \"gegebene (ist contradictorisch)\": the noun to which \"gegebene\" refers is not stated (\"given [ones]\"); left elliptical.",[]],"reflm:5727":["(g The) magnitude [is] of a thing is [that] the unity which can be generated through the mere repetition of one and the same. The repeated positing of just the same is the multitude; what therefore has a magnitude (*quantum*) contains a multitude [whose E]. The magnitude is the united multitude or unity that is one and the same with a given multitude. The determinate concept [of one] of the magnitude of a thing rests on the unity's being given. All continuous magnitudes can, according to their magnitude, be cognized only relatively. The divided magnitude can be cognized absolutely, namely through the absolute unity and the number.\nWe can construct the concept of magnitude, i.e., exhibit it a priori in intuition. But we can make for ourselves a concept of a magnitude *in concreto* only if we bring about the [concept] empirical concept according to the rule of construction, e.g., by our measuring and counting and thus becoming conscious that we could also have thought and determined such a magnitude a priori.\nDynamical magnitude (ground), mathematical (magnitude).\n===NOTES===\n- Menge = multitude; Größe = magnitude; Einheit = unity; Zahl = number; Begriff = concept; erkennen = cognize; Anschauung = intuition.\n- \"[deren E]\" is a deleted, broken-off reading; the deleted \"E\" is left as it stands and not completed.\n- \"Zusammenhang\" is not used; \"Vereinigte\" = \"united\"; \"construiren\" = \"construct\"; \"darstellen\" = \"exhibit\".\n- \"(g\" is the Academy apparatus sign for a later addition.\n- \"Die Größe [ist] eines Dinges ist [das] die Einheit\": the syntax of the revision is kept as it stands.",[]],"reflm:5728":["All magnitude is composed, because it is divisible, and not conversely; the [compositions] parts are not prior to the continuous magnitude.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; zusammengesetzt = composed; theilbar = divisible; Theile = parts.\n- \"[Zusammensetzungen]\" is a deletion in the manuscript, carried over in brackets.",[]],"reflm:5729":["How great something is can be cognized only relatively. Absolute unity there is not outside *ens realissimum*; thus unity is not always that which, repeated several times, makes up the magnitude; for *ens realissimum* is unity, but cannot be repeatedly posited.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; Einheit = unity; erkennen = cognize; wiederholentlich gesetzt = repeatedly posited (setzen = posit).\n- \"etliche Mal\" = \"several times\"; \"ausmacht\" = \"makes up\".",[]],"reflm:5730":["The inner ground of distinction of things, insofar as they are [not] considered as |\nquanta, is the quantity; insofar as they are not considered according to their quantity (intuited or judged), is the quality. The figure is quality. Through this even equal spaces can be distinguished.\n===NOTES===\n- The sentence appears corrupt or garbled as transcribed (\"ist die quantitaet, so fern sie nicht ... ist die Qvalität\"); translated as it stands without emendation.\n- \"[nicht]\" is a deletion/insertion of the Academy apparatus; carried over.\n- quantitaet = quantity, Qvalität = quality; angeschauet = intuited; beurtheilt = judged; Raum = space; Figur = figure.",[]],"reflm:5731":["That something is a *quantum* can be cognized from the thing itself; but the quantity not otherwise than through something else assumed. E.g. One cannot cognize the magnitude of the earth through German miles if one has of these nothing other than the concept of the 15th part of a degree, but has not measured the degree through rods (thus *alio assumto*).\n===NOTES===\n- erkennen = cognize; Größe = magnitude; Begriff = concept; quantitaet = quantity.\n- \"durch etwas anderes angenommene\": the noun is not repeated (\"something else assumed\"); kept elliptical.\n- Ruthen = rods; Meilen = miles; Grad = degree.",[]],"reflm:5732":["The measure of the *quantum* must always itself be a *quantum* of the same kind. For first it must be homogeneous, because *aliquoties sumtum* it is to bring forth that. Second also itself *quantum*, because as *simplex* it would be heterogeneous from the *compositum*. A *quantum* that as such consisted of simple parts would be *continuum* ...\n===NOTES===\n- Art = kind (\"von derselben art\"); homogen / heterogen = homogeneous / heterogeneous.\n- The passage breaks off after \"continuum\" with an ellipsis in the source; nothing is supplied.\n- einfach (in Latin, *simplex*) = simple; Theile = parts.",[]],"reflm:5733":["The unity of a thing (qualitative) and of a unity (quantitative, mathematical).\n===NOTES===\n- Einheit = unity; qvalitativ / qvantitativ = qualitative / quantitative.\n- The genitives \"eines Dinges\" and \"einer Einheit\" are kept as they stand; the sentence has no verb.",[]],"reflm:5734":["Over, beside and \nUnity, truth and completeness (transcendental perfec|tion)\nare the *requisita* of every cognition [which] respectively with regard to understanding, power of judgment and reason (for the latter apodictic certainty is required, i.e., complete truth).\nEverything derived from One.\nEverything combined in One.\nThe one derived from everything.\nUnity of the subject, of the ground and of the whole.\nPossibility\nActuality\nNecessity\nThere are three transcendental criteria of the possibility of things in general. Threefold formal unity.\n===NOTES===\n- \"Über, neben und\" is a locus note, left as literal (\"Over, beside and\") with its line break; the target of the heading is not stated.\n- Einheit = unity; Warheit = truth; Vollstandigkeit = completeness; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Erkentnis = cognition; Verstand = understanding; Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Vernunft = reason; Gewisheit = certainty.\n- Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; criterien = criteria (Kriterium); verbunden = combined (Verbindung = combination); abgeleitet = derived.\n- \"respective auf\" = \"respectively with regard to\" (the exact sense of \"auf\" is left open).",[]],"reflm:5735":["In and \nUnity of the rule (universality); truth in the subsumption under rules, for these alone make something objectively valid, i.e., true; finally perfection, interconnection of all predicates in one principle. The essence is the unity, the essential pieces the truth, all attributes together the perfection.\n===NOTES===\n- \"In und\" is a locus note, kept literal with its line break.\n- Wesen = essence (glossary also \"being\"); Wesentlichen Stücke = essential pieces; Zusammenhang = interconnection.\n- Einheit = unity; Warheit = truth; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Allgemeinheit = universality; gültig = valid; Princip = principle.\n- \"die\" in \"denn die machen\" = \"these\" (the Regeln); kept as a pronoun in the German order.",[]],"reflm:5736":["Unity of the object and unity of the manifold in the object. The former quantitative, the latter qualitative. 1. Unity out of many, 2. unity of the many among one another, 3. many out of One.\n===NOTES===\n- Manigfaltige = manifold; Einheit = unity; obiect = object; qvantitativ / qvalitativ = quantitative / qualitative.\n- \"Vielen\" and \"Vieles\" are rendered \"many\" (neuter); \"Eines\" = \"One\" (capitalized in the source).",[]],"reflm:5737":["Formal unity is philosophical, material mathematical.\nUnity of a thing\nOne unity\nMany agrees with One, many agrees among one another, One with many.\n===NOTES===\n- Einheit = unity; Formale / materiale = formal / material; philosophisch / Mathematisch = philosophical / mathematical.\n- \"stimmt zu\" = \"agrees with\"; the last clause has no verb in the German and none is supplied.",[]],"reflm:5738":["Determination of a thing with regard to its essence (g as thing) is transcendental.\n===NOTES===\n- Bestimmung = determination; Wesen = essence (glossary also \"being\"); Ding = thing.\n- \"(g\" is the Academy apparatus sign for a later addition.",[]],"reflm:5739":["*consensus unius ad varia, variorum inter se, variorum ad unum.*\nThe three criteria of the inner possibility of a* thing.\n*(g thought-entity, e.g. hypothesis: *unum*, possibility; *verum*, actuality; *bonum*, necessity.)\n===NOTES===\n- Gedankendinges = thought-entity (glossary *ens rationis*); Ding = thing; criterien = criteria; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.\n- The asterisks after \"eines\" and before \"(g\" are Kant's own footnote marks, kept in place; they are not emphasis.\n- The footnote text begins with \"(g\", the apparatus sign for a later addition.",[]],"reflm:5740":["In the relation of a representation to object in general unity of the object must be thought through a concept. Plurality of the predicates that flow from the concept. Totality of the determinations (g as ground,) to the whole concept. Totality.\n===NOTES===\n- Beziehung = relation; Vorstellung = representation; obiect = object (no article in the German, kept: \"to object in general\").\n- Einheit = unity; Vielheit = plurality; Allheit and totalitaet both rendered \"totality\" (Allheit / the Latinate *totalitaet*).\n- Bestimmungen = determinations; Begriff = concept; \"(g\" is the Academy sign for a later addition.\n- The syntax of \"zum Gantzen Begrif\" (\"to the whole concept\") is left as it stands.",[]],"reflm:5741":["Transcendental properties of things are those which are essentially combined with the concept of a thing in general.\n===NOTES===\n- Eigenschaften = properties (glossary Beschaffenheit is also \"property\"; the German word differs); Dinge = things; Begriff = concept.\n- verbunden = combined (Verbindung = combination); wesentlich = essentially.",[]],"reflm:5742":["In hypotheses unity of agreement: possibility, of connection: actuality, of derivation: necessity.\nThe three requisites of a definition besides the formal one, namely of distinctness, because it is logical.\n===NOTES===\n- Einheit = unity; Zusammenstimmung = agreement; Verknüpfung = connection; Ableitung = derivation; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.\n- Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Definition kept as \"definition\" (Erklärung is not used).\n- The final clause (\"außer dem formalen, nämlich der Deutlichkeit, weil sie logisch ist\") is ambiguous as to what \"sie\" refers to (the distinctness or the definition); the English \"it\" preserves this.",[]],"reflm:5743":["For the cognition of a thing is required. 1. Unity of the concept (object), that the concept not represent [several] or other than the one object. *Conceptus vagus*. Distinction from all the rest. 2. [Unity] Plurality as determination of this unity connected in one consciousness, or truth. 3. Totality as plurality [determ] determined through the unity, or perfection.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis = cognition; Einheit = unity; Vielheit = plurality; Allheit = totality; Warheit = truth; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Bestimmung = determination.\n- \"nicht [mehrere] noch andere als\": the deletion \"[mehrere]\" and the following \"noch andere\" are rendered as literally as possible.\n- \"bestim\" and \"[bestim]\" are broken-off words; the second is shown as \"[determ]\" to mark the deleted fragment.\n- verknüpft = connected (Verknüpfung = connection).",[]],"reflm:5744":["1. The object as determinable. 2. The determination of the same with re|gard\nto the distinction from others. 3. The thoroughgoing determination through one concept.\n===NOTES===\n- obiect = object; bestimbar = determinable; Bestimung = determination; Begrif = concept.\n- Ansehung = regard (\"in Ansehung\" = \"with regard to\"); Unterschied = distinction (\"difference\" is also possible).\n- durchgängige = thoroughgoing; the page-line break falls within \"An|sehung\\ndes\", shown here at the corresponding place in \"re|gard\".",[]],"reflm:5745":["1. Unity of the object, [thereto] thereupon to which the manifold is related, i.e., concept. Unity of consciousness.\n2. Agreement of the manifold with the object according to rules, i.e., truth.\n3. Interconnection of all rules from one concept, i.e., from principles, i.e., perfection.\nThis is nothing but distinction of an *ens rationis* (thought-entity) from an *ens reale*.\n===NOTES===\n- Einheit = unity; Mannigfaltigen = manifold; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Zusammenstimmung = agreement; Warheit = truth; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Vollkommenheit = perfection.\n- \"[dazu] darauf\": deletion and replacement, both adverbs of reference (\"thereto\", \"thereupon\"); the English is awkward as the German.\n- *ens rationis* = Gedankending (glossary); *ente reali* is kept in the ablative as written.\n- Principien = principles; Begriff = concept.",[]],"reflm:5746":["1. The determinable something is One with regard to all sorts of *praedicatorum oppositorum*.\n2. The determination of the same and its truth.\n3. The possible thoroughgoing determination with regard to that which belongs to its essence, i.e., perfection.\n===NOTES===\n- Etwas = something; Eines = One; Bestimmung = determination; Warheit = truth; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Wesen = essence (glossary also \"being\").\n- durchgangige = thoroughgoing; \"allerley\" = \"all sorts of\"; *praedicatorum oppositorum* kept in Latin genitive plural.",[]],"reflm:5747":["I assume, e.g., through hypothesis [the] an evil *principium*: 1. it lacks unity, for there can be no completely evil. etc etc.\n===NOTES===\n- Hypothese = hypothesis; Principium (Latin form in German text) = *principium*; Einheit = unity.\n- \"kein vollstandig böses\": the noun is not stated (\"completely evil [one]\"); left elliptical.\n- \"[die]\" is a deletion carried over in brackets.",[]],"reflm:5748":["Of concepts that can be used either merely as predicate (g adjective), or also as subject. *Formaliter* or *materialiter*. In the latter case they permit a *pluralem*. E.g. unity, [perfection] truth, perfection, possibility, reality.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriffe = concepts; praedicat / subiect = predicate / subject; Einheit = unity; Warheit = truth; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Möglichkeit = possibility; realitaet = reality (glossary Realität).\n- \"(g\" is the Academy sign for a later addition; \"[Vollkommenheit]\" is a deletion, carried over.\n- *pluralem* is the Latin accusative as written; *formaliter* / *materialiter* left in Latin.",[]],"reflm:5749":["1. Unity of the determinable object.\n2, Plurality of the determinations and their agreement among themselves according to laws of the understanding: formal truth, logic.\n3. Totality of the determinations in the [Be] complete concept of an object.\nThrough this we distinguish the thing from the non-thing.\nThese propositions are [subjective] merely logical and not metaphysical, i.e., objective as determination of things.\n===NOTES===\n- Einheit = unity; Vielheit = plurality; Allheit = totality; Bestimungen = determinations; Gesetzen = laws; Verstand = understanding; Warheit = truth; Begriff = concept.\n- Sätze = propositions; Undinge = \"non-thing\" (a literal compound of Un- and Ding).\n- \"[Be]\" is a broken-off deleted fragment, left as it stands; \"2,\" keeps the comma in the source in place of a period.",[]],"reflm:5750":["[The unity] The relation of the many among one another, insofar as they are contained [together] in one, is combination. The combination according to a rule: order.\nIn the mind all order is in time [either] and indeed one after another. What can be intuited reciprocally one after another is simultaneous. Harmony and melody.\nRule is: the universality of the condition in the determination of the manifold.\nOr it is the unity of the condition under which something is determined universally valid. or the determination of a concept, insofar as it is at the same time universally valid.",[]],"reflm:5751":["Unity of the condition under which something is posited universally is rule.",[]],"reflm:5752":["The concept of perfection belongs to the concept of totality or of *totalitas*, what is accomplished (g completed) (*perfectum*) in contra|distinction to what is begun (*affectum opus*). The perfection of a thing does not yet make up a perfection that arises thereby that I posit the thing, out, hence not [in regard] in regard to things in general. All completion contains a relation to satisfaction 1. respectively to a problematic end, and there the satisfaction rests on the unity of the manifold and on the subjective ground of comprehensibility; or 2. to a categorical end, and there it is called a perfection. Perfections in the metaphysical sense are realities [da]. The good is the object of choice; hence a thing is not good because it is perfect in its kind; but the requisites belonging to its perfection are respectively good on it. Perfections in the human being are means to [se] all sorts of possible ends of his existence in general, thus talents, not well-being and health.",[]],"reflm:5753":["Perfection can mean either (g in the theoretical sense) mere completeness, and there it is, as the perfection proper to the essence of each thing in relation to the thing itself, transcendental; as completeness of the entity or reality [unter] of all things in general it is metaphysical perfection. The first is the perfection of a thing, the second a perfection of which there are many.\nOr it signifies in the practical sense the good, and there either that which is good in itself (good will), or that which is good as means to all ends, the fitness for all ends, e.g. understanding.",[]],"reflm:5754":["On Caput II. In the categories of modality being is considered, and indeed the possibility not so much of the thing as of its existence, whereby it is considered not merely in itself, but also in relation to all requisite [things] conditions. If it is connected with them, then it is actual. If these conditions [not merely contingent] are already given in the concept of it, then it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:5755":["[The necessity] Existence that can be cognized fully a priori is [that of] absolutely necessary; that only under a condition [is], consequently *secundum qvid a priori*, is hypothetically necessary. The concept of an *absolute necessarii* is problematic, i.e. the possibility [de] of such an object cannot be comprehended. The human understanding cannot cognize any existence a priori from the mere concept of a thing. It completes the hypothetical necessity.",[]],"reflm:5756":["The opposite is either logically impossible or really (*realiter*). The latter either absolutely without condition, neither logical nor real, and is a mere idea; or under the condition of a possible experience, e.g. freedom [it its opposite] is really (*realiter*) impossible in experience.",[]],"reflm:5757":["Agreement with the conditions of an experience in general: possibility.\nConnection of a thing with experience in general: actuality.\nThis connection, insofar as it can be cognized a priori, i.e. independently of experience, is necessity.",[]],"reflm:5758":["From mere concepts the existence of a thing cannot be cognized |\n, consequently not *simpliciter* a priori. But also not [through] under presupposition of pure intuitions. Since beyond concepts and intuitions a priori there is nothing further but experience, so for [Noth] necessity experience must go before the necessary, i.e. go before the condition of actuality.",[]],"reflm:5759":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined. The absolutely necessary thing is supposed to be regarded through its concept as existing, consequently also as thoroughly determined. *Deus est conceptus singularis*; that each of his predicates belongs to him necessarily and he is determinable in *vnico modo* does not prove the necessity of his existence.",[]],"reflm:5760":["I can say: *ens necessarium* we can think only as *realissimum*, because this concept alone is thoroughly determining. But not conversely: an *ens realissimum* I must think as *ens necessarium*, for there I would go beyond the concept, in that I would posit the object outside it. *omnimoda determinatio* is not *definitio existentiae*.",[]],"reflm:5761":["E II 332:\n*Necessarium ens est, cuius nonexistentia est impossibilis. Absolute tale non involvit contradictionem, sed transscendit conceptum humanum.* For not-being involves in itself no contradiction for the reason that being as synthetic predicate is not identical with the concept of the thing. For it must namely in a contradiction precisely the same thing that was affirmed in a concept, thus lay in it, also be denied. If space and time were objects outside thoughts or their |\nproperties, they would be *entia necessaria* or properties of the same. For their concept is already a being, which contains the ground of the possibility of things.",[]],"reflm:5762":["On the relation of the real ground to the sequel and that this cannot be cognized and distinguished otherwise [than] *in concreto*, which is the ground and which the sequel, than that, if [A] I posit A, B constantly follows upon it, and not conversely.",[]],"reflm:5763":["Possible is what is determined in regard to time in general, thus in regard to the concept of time, in which therefore the conditions of determination in time lie; e.g. the real possibility of attractive force. (g it is grounded in general.) This possibility we cognize only through experience. Secondly [also possible] actual is what is determined in relative time, thus is at some time, i.e. is determined through other things given in time and in regard to them. Finally a necessary being is what is determined in regard to absolute time, on whose presupposition therefore all relative determination of time rests.",[]],"reflm:5764":["29'. E II 1092.\n M 29:\nThe difference between that something is thought or given is this, that it is an object of indeterminate or of thoroughly determinate thinking. In itself there is here no difference between things, but only in relation to our cognition a priori. What can be thought a priori only as thoroughly determinate is necessary; what is thought only as indeterminate is to that extent contingent. Although our concept is never thoroughly determinate, in that it does not contain everything, the understanding nevertheless |\nrepresents the object as thoroughly determinate in itself, |\nM 29': |\nand thinking and apperceiving are one and the same, consequently possibility and existence. It also cannot be distinguished in things themselves, but only in their relation to the understanding, at the basis of which apperception lies. Consequently I myself am determinate only in a single way in relation to everything.",[]],"reflm:5765":["The conditionally necessary, as hypothesis, is (g it) therefore not in itself.",[]],"reflm:5766":["In and \nWe have no example of hypothetically necessary things except only in regard to the form of them; e.g. [water] formation of ice, of salts from mixture.",[]],"reflm:5767":["A judgment about the existence of an object is at all times synthetic. Necessary judgments do not therefore represent the object as necessary.",[]],"reflm:5768":["There is indeed a difference between absolute, i.e. inner, and hypothetical necessity, if the latter is thought only under a possible hypothesis. But if it is carried out to its highest actual ground, then, as far as the degree of necessity is concerned, the difference vanishes. For the necessary sequels of a necessary cause are inseparable from its absolute necessity. But an absolutely necessary being can have only a single complete effect of its existence; and if all possible existence is grounded on |\nthe same, then there can also be only a single complete possibility, namely the entire actual.",[]],"reflm:5769":["Possibility seems to be distinguished from actuality only in regard to the appearance of things. The opposite of the predicates of a thing may well be possible, but not the opposite of its existence in the thoroughgoing determination.",[]],"reflm:5770":["Contingency and necessity are not differences in the existence of things, nor of the determination of things in themselves, but merely of their concepts, thus at all times relative to this concept, thus never in itself. The possibility of alteration can be explained as belonging to the thing itself.",[]],"reflm:5771":["Everything necessary must be cognized a priori as existing, thus from the mere possibility and the concept. But to cognize these hypothetically means to consider this concept of a merely possible as an actual, adhering according to its condition. If therefore the thing is posited that contains the condition, then from the mere possibility of the same the conditioned follows. E.g. »Glass must break by the fall« a priori from brittleness in general. This brittleness adheres to glass, and thus the breaking also exists as necessary. To infer actuality from the mere possibility.\n===NOTES===\n- erkennen = cognize; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = actuality; existirend/existirt = existing/exists (Existenz-stem), distinct from Dasein.\n- \"diese erkennen\": \"diese\" (plural) has no clear antecedent; left as \"these\".\n- \"von einem blos Moglichen als einem wirklichen\": the attachment of the participle \"anhängend\" is ambiguous; kept as \"as an actual, adhering according to its condition\".\n- Begriff = concept; Bedingung = condition; Bedingte = conditioned; schließen = infer.",[]],"reflm:5772":["The possibility of things is distinguished from the possibility, actuality or necessity of their existence. The former consists merely in this, that their concept has nothing in itself conflicting; it is, so to speak, the agreement of their archetype with the understanding that thinks it. The |\npossibility of existence, by contrast, signifies the agreement of the *position* of such an object outside the understanding. If now the thinking itself is not the cause of it, then the material and the combining ground of the same must be outside the same. Existence does not belong at all to the idea of a thing, and the possibility of it, if it is complete, cannot be distinguished from actuality and necessity.\n===NOTES===\n- Dasein = existence (here Dasein throughout; Existenz would also be \"existence\" under the glossary).\n- Gegenstand = object; Stoff = material; Verbindende Grund = combining ground (Verbindung = combination); bedeuten = signify.\n- \"position\" is Latin in roman type in the original; italicized. Urbild = archetype.\n- \"desselben\" / \"demselben\" (\"of the same\") are left as ambiguous as the German; \"Jene\" = \"the former\".\n- The sign | falls mid-phrase (\"Die | Möglichkeit\") and is kept in place.",[]],"reflm:5773":["On and \nContingency, i.e. possibility of the opposite, cannot be cognized through alteration, but only from this, that all alteration is a *caussatum*, and thus in itself, and without a cause being there for it, is contingent. A priori it cannot be cognized in anything.\n===NOTES===\n- The locus note \"Über und\" is as printed, with the numbers apparently absent; rendered \"On and\".\n- Zufälligkeit = contingency; zufällig = contingent; Veränderung = alteration (not in the glossary); Ursache = cause.\n- *caussatum* is Kant's spelling of causatum.\n- \"sie\" in the last sentence could refer to contingency or to alteration; left ambiguous (the grammatical antecedent is feminine singular, Zufälligkeit or Veränderung).",[]],"reflm:5774":["The necessary can, with regard to the predicate, in respect of which it is determined, be determined in only a single way. Among this, however, existence does not belong.\n===NOTES===\n- bestimmt = determined; determinirt = determined (two German words, one English word; Latinate member determinirt).\n- \"in absicht worauf\" rendered \"in respect of which\" (Absicht elsewhere = aim).\n- \"Darunter\" (\"among this\") has no clear antecedent; left as is.\n- Daseyn = existence.",[]],"reflm:5775":["Absolutely necessary is either a thing or a concept of a thing. The concept of the *realissimum* is in every aim in relation to concepts of things in their thoroughgoing determination necessary. Its abolition leaves a complete void for the understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- \"in aller Absicht auf\": Absicht = aim (glossary); the construction is obscure and has been kept literal.\n- durchgängige Bestimmung = thoroughgoing determination; Verstand = understanding.\n- Aufhebung = abolition; \"ein volliges leere\" is the German's own awkward neuter noun-adjective, rendered \"a complete void\".\n- *realissimum* is Latin.",[]],"reflm:5776":["*vnico modo determinabile* means to the author a thing that is thoroughgoingly determined through its concept; of this kind is only the *ens realissimum*; for the *mere negativum* is no thing, and the *partim reale partim negativum* is thoroughgoingly determined through no concept. Now Leibniz had attempted, following Descartes's precedent [from] to prove that a thing which is thoroughgoingly determined through its concept also necessarily exists; but the inference is found invalid. Thereupon he or Wolf attempts to reverse the proposition and to say: Every necessary thing is thoroughgoingly determined through its concept (consequently what is not thoroughgoingly determined through its concept is no necessary being. Since now some necessary being must be, there must also exist a being thoroughgoingly determined through its concept, i.e. a (g such as) *ens realissimum*), hence an *ens realissimum*. But then the [opposed] (g *per accidens*) converted proposition would also have to be true, which however, because only a single thing can be *ens realissimum*; and indeed it would have to be, precisely for this reason, because [it] the concept of the same is a |\n*conceptus non communis*, but *singularis* [because zw] and the combination of convertibility [thereby] precisely for that reason must constitute a *conversionem simpliciter talem* and indeed from mere concepts, just so from the concept of the *realissimum* the necessity would follow as from this that concept, which is yet false.\n===NOTES===\n- The sentence beginning \"Aber alsdenn\" is syntactically broken in the German (\"welches doch falsch ist\" at the end; \"Satz ... wahr seyn müssen, der aber, weil ...\" never completes); kept as it stands. The last clause's \"aus dieser jener Begrif\" is rendered \"from this that concept\".\n- Bracketed words are the Academy Edition's deletions/insertions, translated in place; \"[from]\" renders \"[aus]\"; \"[because zw]\" renders \"[weil zw]\" (zw is a truncated word, not completed). The marks (g and the closing parenthesis are the editors' apparatus.\n- Schluß = inference; Satz = proposition; durchgängig bestimmt = thoroughgoingly determined; Wesen = being; Ding = thing; Verbindung = combination.\n- \"Wolf\" as in the German; \"convertibilitaet\" rendered \"convertibility\".\n- The sign | falls before \"conceptus\" and is kept.",[]],"reflm:5777":["Through no single concept of a limited thing is this thoroughgoingly determined; for a priori no thing can be thoroughgoingly determined except only through the idea of the *maximum*.\n===NOTES===\n- beschränkt = limited (cf. Schranke = limitation); durchgängig bestimmt = thoroughgoingly determined.\n- \"dieses\" (\"this\") refers to the thing or the concept; left ambiguous as in the German (grammatically the thing).\n- *maximi* is genitive in the original (\"idee des maximi\"); rendered *maximum* in English.",[]],"reflm:5778":["The necessary is determined in only a single way. The concept of a thing which according to this concept is determinable [on] in only a single way, is *conceptus entis realissimi*, and hence it is inferred: only *ens realissimum* can be *absolute necessarium*; i.e., however, only *necessitas logica*. The thing itself to which this concept pertains can be determined otherwise than according to this concept.\n===NOTES===\n- \"[auf]\" is a deletion/insertion in the apparatus, rendered \"[on]\".\n- bestimmt / bestimmbar / determinirt = determined / determinable / determined.\n- \"laut diesem Begriffe\" rendered \"according to this concept\" (same English as \"nach\").\n- \"kan ... seyn\" \"d.i.\" kept as \"i.e.\"; Latin terms in italics as printed (*absolute necessarium*).",[]],"reflm:5779":["Only the *omnitudo* is absolute unity and *individuum rationale*.\nThat the necessary must be determinable in only a single way is *conditio sine qua non*, but for that reason not yet the sufficient ground of its necessity, as little as that it contains no contradiction.\nAnd then all this is only logical, because the possibility is grounded merely on |\nthe lack of contradiction and not on the possible unity of all this reality in one thing.\n===NOTES===\n- zureichender Grund = sufficient ground; Widerspruch = contradiction (Kant's spelling Wiederspruch); Einheit = unity; Realität = reality.\n- \"wie, daß es keinen wiederspruch enthält\" kept elliptical: \"as little as that it contains no contradiction\".\n- The sign | falls mid-phrase (\"auf | dem Mangel\") and is kept.",[]],"reflm:5780":["Even if the concept of a thing can be determined in only a single way, these predicates indeed, but not existence, pertain to it necessarily; and an existence could be necessary, although not from the concept that we have, or any other; for from the concept of no thing can an existence be inferred.\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn = existence; schließen = infer; Begriff = concept.\n- \"ihm\" and \"diese Prädicate\" kept as in the German; \"nothwendig\" is adverbial and could attach to \"zukommen\" only (\"pertain necessarily\"), kept in that position.\n- \"aus dem Begriffe, den wir haben, oder jedem andern\" kept literal.",[]],"reflm:5781":["That a necessary being cannot be altered follows, to be sure, when time is a mere form of sensibility and [be] determines the existence of no things in itself, but never when it belongs to determinations of the thing in itself. For then, when it is actually altered, this is not the contradictory opposite of its determinations in existence.\n===NOTES===\n- Wesen = being; Form der Sinnlichkeit = form of sensibility; an sich = in itself; Bestimmung = determination.\n- \"und [seyn] keiner Dinge Daseyn an sich bestimmt\": the bracketed [seyn] is an editorial deletion/insertion (\"[be]\"); the subject of \"bestimmt\" is not repeated and may be time or sensibility; left as ambiguous as the German.\n- veränderen = alter; wirklich = actually; \"dieses\" has no explicit antecedent.\n- *contradictorische* rendered \"contradictory\".",[]],"reflm:5782":["In and \nThe necessary must be determined in a single way, because it is to be cognized a priori as existing. Now the *ens realissimum* is thoroughgoingly determined through its concept, thus only this can be an *ens necessarium*. If we want to assume one, therefore, we must think it so; but from the concept of the highest reality existence cannot be cognized a priori, and yet even then everything is only relative to the human understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- The locus note \"In und\" is as printed, with numbers apparently absent; rendered \"In and\".\n- erkennen = cognize; Verstand = understanding; Realität = reality; Daseyn = existence (existirend = existing).\n- \"selbst denn\" is obscure; rendered \"even then\". \"alles\" (\"everything\") left without an antecedent.\n- \"eines\" (\"one\") has no expressed noun; the German likely has ens necessarium in view; left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:5783":["The *ens necessarium* is that whose opposite is absolutely impossible. The human understanding, however, cannot have insight into this impossibility, |\nexcept by this, that non-being contradicts its concept. Now the non-being of a thing never contradicts the concept of the thing in itself; thus the concept of the *ens necessarium* is unattainable for human reason, but yet to be assumed necessarily, because otherwise the series of the conditionedly necessary is never ended.\nThat it is determinable in only a single way follows from this, because its existence is to follow from the mere concept. Now everything that exists is thoroughgoingly determined, and existence can therefore only [be inferred there] be inferred from a concept that thoroughgoingly determines [or]; otherwise it does not follow at all from the concept, but from another assumed existence.\nThere is a difference: to assume the necessary being for the sake of another, and: to cognize a being through its concept as necessary.\nWe cannot say that a being exists contingently because it is altered, but: that we then could not cognize its existence from its mere concept; for then it would have to be determinable in only a single way. But we cannot cognize the existence of any thing from its mere concept, although, if we could cognize it, the thoroughgoing determination would at the same time be contained therein.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* alone suits the concept of the *ens necessarium*.\n===NOTES===\n- Existenz = existence and Daseyn = existence (one English word for two German words); Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; einsehen = have insight into.\n- \"aus einem Begriffe, der durchgängig bestimmt\": the verb has no auxiliary; it can be read as active (\"that thoroughgoingly determines\") or as a participle (\"that is thoroughgoingly determined\"); the active reading is kept.\n- Bracketed words are editorial deletions/insertions: [da geschlossen werden] = \"[be inferred there]\", [oder] = \"[or]\".\n- \"Reihe des Bedingt nothwendigen\" kept literal; \"seinem Begriff\" (\"its concept\") antecedent ambiguous between the being and the thing.\n- The sign | falls after \"einsehen,\" and is kept.",[]],"reflm:5784":["* I posit something either as object of a partial or of the thoroughgoing determination. In the first case I posit through the concept of a thing the general and thus many sorts. In the second case the individual [and of that] what cannot be many sorts. The *substratum* of the thoroughgoing determination is what contains the matter for all determinations of a thing in general; hence I cannot say: if I assume such a one, but reason presupposes the like with all possibility. With regard to thoroughgoing determination, therefore, possibility and actuality are one and the same, and its thoroughgoing determination is not thought as something general, but at the same time as individual.\n*(s We cannot prove that, if a concept is of such a kind that it at the same time includes in itself the thoroughgoing determination of the object, this necessarily exists, but only: that, if we could derive existence from mere concepts, this would at the same time have to contain the thoroughgoing determination. That there could be no more than one *necessarium* cannot be seen at all from concepts.)\n===NOTES===\n- The initial * and the sign *(s ... ) are the Academy's apparatus (an asterisk-marked note and a later layer), carried over as they stand.\n- obiect = object (Object/Gegenstand would also be \"object\"); Materie = matter; Vernunft = reason; Bestimmung = determination; Beweis/beweisen = proof/prove.\n- \"vielerley\" rendered \"many sorts\"; \"[und dessen]\" = \"[and of that]\".\n- \"sondern die Vernunft setzt dergleichen ... voraus\": rendered literally, \"sondern\" = \"but\"; \"bey aller Möglichkeit\" kept as \"with all possibility\".\n- \"einzelne\" = individual; \"necessarium\" is Latin in the original.",[]],"reflm:5785":["Our concept of absolute necessity does not signify the inner necessity from the concept of a thing, but the externally, in relation to everything possible, necessary presupposition and condition of their possibility (*ens realissimum*), just as time is regarded as absolutely necessary, because everything must be in it.\n===NOTES===\n- bedeuten = signify; Begriff = concept; Bedingung = condition; Möglichkeit = possibility; Zeit = time.\n- \"ihrer Moglichkeit\": \"ihrer\" may be singular (feminine) or plural; rendered \"their\", leaving its antecedent (everything possible) unresolved.\n- \"im Verheltnis\" is Kant's slip for Verhältnis; rendered \"in relation\".",[]],"reflm:5786":["The proposition: the necessary being is determinable only in a single way, signifies: all determinations belong to it necessarily. This does not, however, prevent there being still other and differently determined necessary beings. But if the proposition is to mean: all determinations must be given through a single concept, then this indicates only that we could otherwise have no concept of a necessary being a priori according to which the thoroughgoing determination would at the same time be given; so, to be sure, only the concept of the *realissimum* is suitable for it, if we already know that a being is necessary; but from that one cannot infer even the possibility, let alone the necessity, of such a being, but only that we could determine it thoroughly in this way alone [the] from a mere concept, as *ens singulare*.",[]],"reflm:5787":["If something through concepts [as ei] as a necessary being [is to] can be cognized, then this concept can be none other than that through which the thing is at the same time thoroughly determined, hence is determinable in only a single way; for otherwise it would not be thought as a (necessarily) |\nexisting, but only as a merely possible being. But the former is impossible [therefore also].",[]],"reflm:5788":["The existence of the *contradictorie oppositorum*, which is not contradictory. When a predicate conflicts not with the thing but with another predicate.\nChange concerns merely existence.",[]],"reflm:5789":["That the concept of time is presupposed in the concept of the connection of the *contradictorie oppositorum*; *contradictorie opposita* in different times do not contradict one another.",[]],"reflm:5790":["Unchangeable is said of something either insofar as it does not exist in time at all (God), or insofar as it is not considered at all according to its existence (the essences of things). That is the nominal unchangeability and must not be used at all. The real unchangeability is merely in appearances. In them, however, merely relative, not absolute.",[]],"reflm:5791":["Change is the connection of determinations with their opposite in the existence of a thing *post se invicem*. The thing remains, but the determinations arise and pass away.",[]],"reflm:5792":["The possibility of change, i.e. [how] a thing (g as) to think itself as determined in its existence in a twofold opposite way, is not possible a priori, from mere concepts, but only in time, and in this too the cause of it only in experience.",[]],"reflm:5793":["What is changeable does not therefore exist contingently, and the |\nnecessary being is not therefore precisely unchangeable. However, if we take the necessity comprehensible and graspable by us, namely existence from concepts, then, because in the concept of a thing in itself time drops out, everything [what the] opposite of the predicates also with regard to the thing (its concept) must be contradictory; for in the existence of a changeable thing predicates are opposed only to predicates and can be one after another without contradiction, because none of these predicates is determined through the concept of the thing.\nIf I want to think for myself the concept of an unchangeable being, then I must be able to specify a concept that would at the same time contain the existence of the thing, hence the thoroughgoing determination through a concept and not merely that, but also the objective validity of it.",[]],"reflm:5794":["All changeability proves the contingency of the thing according to mere concepts of the understanding (g as thing in itself), because through the concept it is not determined as to existence, hence thoroughly; but not the contingency as appearance.",[]],"reflm:5795":["The logical possibility of the opposite does not prove the real one in the connection.",[]],"reflm:5796":["In and \nEverything changeable is through its concept undetermined with regard to the *praedicata opposita*, thus logically contingent.",[]],"reflm:5797":["In and \nContingency is inferred from change only because the thing is then in time. This, however, can also be empty. everything in time is contingent.",[]],"reflm:5798":["The changeable is hypothetically necessary, but the change with every ground necessary. Everything changeable is contingent, i.e. not absolutely necessary. For it is in time, hence appearance; therefore it is nothing absolutely necessary; absolutely necessary [can] is that whose opposite cannot take place at all, not even one after another.",[]],"reflm:5799":["Change itself can be absolutely necessary, and the changeable is contingent.",[]],"reflm:5800":["Lower margin, \nThe possibility of change, that a state contain the ground of its opposite in the sequel, cannot be comprehended; but for the comprehension of the sequence of time in experience it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:5801":["The following determination is in itself contingent, but not the sequel and the series.",[]],"reflm:5802":["The possibility of change cannot be cognized a priori.",[]],"reflm:5803":["In and \nContingent is that whose opposite is possible in its place.",[]],"reflm:5804":["In and \nChange of the relation is not always change of the thing; e.g. when the fire makes hard or soft.",[]],"reflm:5805":["Above and \nChange is the connection of contradictorily opposed determinations in the existence of a thing (which nevertheless do not contradict the con|cept\nof the thing, but only *praedicatum praedicato*, not *subiecto oppositum*).\nWhat makes possible what is impossible according to the mere concept of a thing? Time. (g *determinationes oppositae* can merely succeed one another.) Thus time does not belong to the concepts of things in themselves, but to the way in which we intuit them.",[]],"reflm:5806":["The change of that which belongs to the essence of a thing is merely a change in determination of concepts, [not] thus logical, and not change of things and their existence.",[]],"reflm:5807":["At the inner margin \n*contrarie* opposed determinations [have] are *realitas ut ratio opposita realitati*.",[]],"reflm:5808":["From change we can well infer that a state is [necessary] contingent, but not that a thing is contingent. The first signifies that the state has a cause; but that does not hold of the thing, which contains the cause of it.",[]],"reflm:5809":["Change first makes possible the concept of contingency in existence; hence to be sure everything contingent is changeable, and everything that is not changeable, necessary. Change makes necessary only the inference to a cause.",[]],"reflm:5810":["The logical unchangeability with regard to the concept is commonly taken for the real unchangeability with regard to existence, which many infer from it as persistence of things with regard to certain properties.",[]],"reflm:5811":["In themselves things can determine themselves *ad oppositum* neither for themselves (g alone) nor in community; for *nulli subiecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*, and *ratio*[cont] &lt;8A *est etiam ratio* >8 *Non A*. thus time, in which change alone can be thought, is no [representation of] determination of things in themselves; hence all changes are merely determinations of appearances, and time itself, in which existence is determined changeably, is not something that adheres to the existence of things in themselves, but merely to our way of sensible representation (not of the understanding). Unchangeable, therefore, is not merely the (g in itself) necessary being, but also everything intellectual. [In] On the objects of the senses, however, every [change necessary] determination that constitutes the change is necessary, but only ever hypothetically to infinity. The principle of contradiction, insofar as it is restricted to what is in time, does not go to concepts of things, but [on] of mere appearances; and it does not contradict itself that both predicates *opposita* (s only not at the same time) belong to those as sequels in appearance and neither to the same as things in themselves.\nI as object of sensible intuition am changeable and cannot bring the concept of myself to transcendental predicates of things in themselves, e.g. the simple or the composite etc etc., but can only speak of my intuition. I, however, as subject of thinking and merely as object of reason, can again cognize no predicates through which such an object would be given to me in concreto, no extension, persistence in time, but also not their opposite. Thus all psychology as doctrine goes only to the human being as thinking being, not to the mere I in general; but psychology as critique prevents what holds of me insofar as I am human from being taken for the only thing that holds for me as a thinking being in general.",[]],"reflm:5812":["Beside and \nThe concept of the *ens realissimum* is the ontological place for all |\npossible things, insofar as they distinguish themselves from one another as things in general. Their difference then consists merely in the limitation of the concept of the *omnitudo realitatis*. Instead of thinking each thing as determinable according to the *principium exclusi medii* with regard to the real of all predicates, or rather for the sake of this determinability, in order to represent them analytically, one takes a thoroughly determined one as the ground of all.",[]],"reflm:5813":["A merely possible thing cannot to that extent be changed, because change is the existence of the *oppositorum* in time.\nThe only contingent (of nature) is that which is considered as actual merely through freedom.",[]],"reflm:5814":["*Realitas phaenomenon* is what corresponds to sensation. Full and empty space.\nWe do not always need true reality, in which no negation (*non esse*) at all is thought; rather initially we also have to do with *realitatibus phaenomenis*.",[]],"reflm:5815":["On M § 135:\nNegation is not lack of things, but of the determination of things. — *Realitas, negatio, limitatio*; every *negatio* is either merely *limitatio*, i.e. *oppositum* of quantity, or *negatio repugnantiae* and an *oppositum* of quality. What holds of a *quantum* also holds of the *limes quanti*; for the quality remains.",[]],"reflm:5816":["Beside and \nThe negation [as] is either according to quantity or quality. In the first case it is to be regarded as a vanishing *quantum* and |\nnothing but mere limitation and not contradictorily opposed to reality, [for] so that one of the two would take place, but *disparatum* or *defectum*; in the second case it is *negatio oppositionis*, as when I say: a spirit is not composite, consequently simple. On the other hand I cannot infer: a spirit [is not in] does not take up a space, consequently it is present in a point. For here the same quality of presence is assumed, and the point is a vanishing space. God does not move, consequently he rests.",[]],"reflm:5817":["39'. E II 1387.\nM 39:\nIn the phenomena negation is nothing other than restriction; in the pure understanding it is remotion, thus the qualitative *oppositum*. Limitation is either merely of determination or of counter-effect. The former is logical sublation, the latter real. Motion is transformed into rest either merely as *defectus* through lack of determining grounds or through reaction.\nM 39':\nIn the mathematical antinomy one proposition is the quantitative opposite of the other, but they have the same condition and are therefore both false. In the dynamical propositions one is the qualitative opposite of the other, but the condition is different; hence both can be true.",[]],"reflm:5818":["At the very top of the page. \nThe counterplay is what can be the positive ground of the opposite of a reality.",[]],"reflm:5819":["The *contrarium* of a reality (respectively such) is the *ratio* of removing the reality, e.g. descent with respect to ascent. Reality absolutely such not [has contr] is contrarily opposed to a reality likewise absolute.",[]],"reflm:5820":["Negation is either of a deficient ground or of a removing one (really oppo|sed).\nWe do not know whether the negations arise from lack or real conflict.",[]],"reflm:5821":["Limitation has straight to the 0, thus reality too.",[]],"reflm:5822":["In and \nThe real is also opposed to the merely formal in intuition. Hence empty space, where no being of something is represented.",[]],"reflm:5823":["In and \nOnly reality in appearance can be opposed to a reality and negation agree together to a reality.",[]],"reflm:5824":["Beside and \nNegatio is not specifically distinguished from reality. It is the means between two opposed realities [and the Ub], which is common to both. Between *contradictorie oppositis* there is no means, but indeed between *realiter oppositis*.",[]],"reflm:5825":["Below and \nThe possibility of change rests on the contrariety of certain *realitatum phaenomenorum*.",[]],"reflm:5826":["41. E II 676. 677. 1640.\nM 41':\nOne can consider negation either as opposed to reality or as opposed to affirmation; in the latter case it is the logical *oppositum*, in the former [the *evanescens*] limitation. [Extension in] »A spirit is not extended« does not signify so much as: »its extension is vanishing«, but: »it cannot have it at all«. By contrast the point is not extended as a vanishing space. Spirits therefore cannot be regarded as points.\nIf I say the non-being of a predicate, then thereunder |\nnot straightaway the mere vanishing of the same must be thought, and I may not reckon the subject in the same kind of things, but often I must count it to another kind.\nAbsolute reality is what pertains, with regard to a thing in general, to being. Comparative, what pertains to the being of a certain kind of [E] perceptions; thus pain is comparative reality, but for that reason not absolute. *Negatio absoluta* is the mere restriction of the existence of an object as thing in general. *Realitas evanescens est defectus in sensu absoluto.* [A th] The existence in which all possible existence is contained is without limitations. But it cannot be composed of much restricted existence. Thus existence without limitations is that of an *ens realissimum*, at the same time considered as ground. Of this the possible existence of every other thing is considered as derived, because as thing in general with regard to its negations it can be represented merely as (g a) restricted *ens realissimum* or its restricted product [am]. Possibility we can no longer distinguish from actuality, as soon as we have arrived at the first substratum of the determination of all concepts. |\nM 41: |\nIf non-being is considered merely as limitation, then all realities are assumed as of one kind.",[]],"reflm:5827":["To a thing belongs neither a predicate nor [the] its non-being, insofar as this can be regarded merely as a diminution of the predicate. For that is not the contradictory opposite.",[]],"reflm:5828":["Over and \nNon-being (g of B) as a contradiction of the predicate B with the concept A cannot at all subsist together with the concept A and is its contradictory opposite. But the non-being which [not to the concept] contains no contradiction with the concept is the privative opposite or *defectus*. E.g. »The soul is not extended, because it is not at all determined according to space or because [its] exten|sion [again] contradicts its concept«, would also contain the proposition: »the soul is not present as a point in space«. For the point is after all nothing but vanishing extension, and the concept of it does not contradict the [extension] concept of extension, but is only a lack of the extended.",[]],"reflm:5829":["Over and \n*opposita logice* under an inadmissible condition, e.g. of extension, are both false. E.g. the soul is either extended in space or unextended in the same, i.e. a point. *opposita* under two different conditions are not contradictorily opposed; the opposition is inadmissible, and both propositions can be true. E.g. the will as appearance is under natural necessity, and as intellectual it is free. Both conditions are to be thought in all beings, but only in the will do we notice the last.",[]],"reflm:5830":["If I say: God is not changeable, because nothing breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5831":["The *oppositum* can be qualitative or quantitative *oppositum*. The first is the contradiction, the second *quantum* = 0 or restriction; hence in a formula rest can be a motion = 0, the [extension in space] simplicity an extension = 0, the unchanged duration a change = 0.",[]],"reflm:5832":["A barrel of apples is indeed *compositum*, but not *quantum*. [In the latter can the] The latter is [its u] a *compositum* whose unity, assumed at will, brings out the *compositum* through frequent repetition.",[]],"reflm:5833":["Space is *quantum*, matter *compositum*, body *totum*. *Mundus* however *totum absolute tale*.",[]],"reflm:5834":["A *compositum* is (g one) the same with several things conjoined. If [pl] the conjunction of several has been made several times, the positing of the same is a *quantum*.\n*Compositum*, insofar as it is not a part, is a *totum*.",[]],"reflm:5835":["A *quantum* is one the same with many (s homogeneous) (taken together). This as *totum* contains *omnitudo* (allness).",[]],"reflm:5836":["A *compositum* is what is represented through the conjunction of several. If these several are homogeneous, it is a *quantum*; if the *omnitudo* of several, it is a *totum*; thus *pars* belongs to the *compositum*, *quantum* and *totum*.",[]],"reflm:5837":["Related concepts, which represent several posited in one: *compositum*, *quantum*, *totum*; each has *partes*. In the first I begin with it as unity, in the second I determine another unity through its repetition.",[]],"reflm:5838":["Totality (*totalitas*) is the greatest, not infinity. The [abs] unity (as homogeneous part of a magnitude) is to that extent the smallest and the absolute unity simply the smallest. The simple has no magnitude at all, is also no homogeneous part of a *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:5839":["Not every *quantum* can be regarded as *totum*.",[]],"reflm:5840":["The unity of a multitude is *omnitudo*. Totality (*totalitas*) is synthetic *omnitudo* (collective).\nIt is the category of totality in the synthesis of magnitude or of aggregation.\nOf the absolute all.",[]],"reflm:5841":["If I do not look to the homogeneity of the many, then *simplex*, *compositum*, *totum* are in opposition with the *quantum*. *Unum*, *pluralitas* and *omnitudo*.",[]],"reflm:5842":["In and  M 45:\nIf several are homogeneous, it is a *quantum*\\*; a real *totum* (in which there is no ideal nexus, but a real one) is a *compositum*. One arisen through repetition of the same is a *quantum*, through addition or conjunction of several is a *compositum*.\nM 45':\n\\*(g Every quantum is thought as compositum, not conversely; for the parts can be heterogeneous.)",[]],"reflm:5843":["A thing can be seen as *compositum* (in a series), but without totality (of the aggregate). Thus the concept of the *compositum* is not yet that of a *totum*; in the case of the *compositum* homogeneity is not required, but indeed in the case of a *quantum*. The *totum* is always considered as a *quantum* according to a certain concept.\nTotality belongs to the concept of a *compositum* as homogeneous, i.e. as *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:5844":["A *qvantum*, through whose concept of quantity the multitude of the |\nparts is determined, is *discretum*; through whose concept of quantity the multitude of the parts is undetermined of itself, is *continuum*.",[]],"reflm:5845":["[Spatium] A *qvantum*, all of whose possible parts are *qvanta*, is a *continuum*. Therefore that which does not consist of simples. Therefore that in which how many parts there are is undetermined in itself. Therefore any *phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:5846":["Opposite and \nThe *qvantum* in which all quantity alone can be determined is, with regard to the multitude of the parts, undetermined and *continuum*. Space and time.",[]],"reflm:5847":["Every *qvantum* is a *compositum*, [which] whose parts are all of the same kind as it; consequently it is *continuum* and does not consist of simple parts. consequently not indeed every *compositum*, but yet every *qvantum* is a *continuum*.",[]],"reflm:5848":["Every *qvantum* has a quality (*continuum*) and every *qvale* a quantity (degree).",[]],"reflm:5849":["Every *quantum* (g *continuum*) as such is that through which a multitude [of] homogeneous parts is posited; consequently it necessarily precedes the composition. The composition that precedes the multitude gives *quantum discretum*.",[]],"reflm:5850":["Since the measure of magnitude is itself magnitude, all magnitude can be determined only relatively and, in the case of *phaenomena*, is also only a [representation] concept of the way in which representations can be composed through common unity. There is no concept of abso|lute\nmagnitude, except where the all is already determined through the [itself] concept of the thing; only metaphysical perfection is absolute magnitude.",[]],"reflm:5851":["extensive magnitude, the multitude of the homogeneous in an object or of that which is posited through the object, thus the magnitude of a whole and that of a ground; that something is ground is quality.\nUnity, possibility, truth has *in plurali* extensive magnitude, *in singulari* intensive.",[]],"reflm:5852":["The magnitude of a *quantum* as multitude (given) is extensive. The magnitude of unity is intensive, i.e. the degree. (s magnitude of the aggregate, magnitude of the ground.)",[]],"reflm:5853":["All objects as objects of intuition have extensive magnitude, as grounds of sensation intensive.",[]],"reflm:5854":["Threefold way in which things determine existence. Thus (g real) relation first of the subject, 2ndly of the cause, 3. of the union in a whole of substances.\nThe category of relation (of the unity of consciousness) is the foremost among all. For unity properly concerns only the relation; thus this makes up the content of judgments in general and alone allows itself to be thought as determined a priori.\nOn the section on *substantia* (g the relation of the realities). Category is the concept through which an object in general is regarded as determined in re|gard\nto a logical function of judgments in general (i.e. of the objective unity [of the] in the consciousness of the manifold), i.e. that I must think the manifold of its intuition through one of these moments of the understanding.\nThere are therefore [only] three logical functions [of] under a certain title, hence also three categories: Because [the] two of them show the unity [of consciousness [of] two *oppositorum*] of consciousness in two *opposita*, but the third connects consciousness on both sides once again. More ways of the unity of consciousness cannot be thought. For let a be a consciousness which connects a manifold, b another, which [dasse] connects in the opposite way: then c is the connection of a and b.\n*Judicia infinita*. »Anima est non mortalis« does not merely signify that A belongs under the sphere non B, but under the sphere C outside B, which restricts and bounds B, thus signifies limitation of the proposition: »a est b«.",[]],"reflm:5855":["The *per se esse* of a substance is not an *a se esse*.",[]],"reflm:5856":["In and \nThat whose existence is never a determination of another thing: substance. Whose concept is determination [of] other things, is logical subject or predicate.",[]],"reflm:5857":["Reality, whose *esse* is *inesse*, is [subst] accident; whose *esse* is *subsistere*: substance.",[]],"reflm:5858":["In and \nEverything can be thought as subject or predicate, but not everything can exist as subject.",[]],"reflm:5859":["Relation and modality belong to the consideration of the nature of beings, quantity and quality to the doctrine of beings.",[]],"reflm:5860":["In substance there is. 1. The relation of inherence (accident); 2. Of causality (force); 3. Of *commercium* (influence).",[]],"reflm:5861":["The sensible concept of sustentation (the bearer) is a misunderstanding. Accidents are only the way of existing of the substance in accordance with the positive.",[]],"reflm:5862":["We cannot think up any fundamental force. or cognize its possibility a priori, e.g. impenetrability.",[]],"reflm:5863":["*De commercio*.\nIn the concept of space it lies that a substance of the world cannot flow into another without suffering from it.",[]],"reflm:5864":["Logic, which presents objective rules of the use of the faculty of cognition, and ethics, which does this in regard to the faculty of desire (the ought), presupposes for both only faculties |\nof the mind. Psychology, which explains what happens, does not prescribe what ought to happen, occupies itself with forces of the mind.",[]],"reflm:5865":["*Unum plurium coniunctorum* is, for the sake of the combination, *compositum* and, insofar as it is no part of a greater *compositum*, *totum*. Thus we can think something as *compositum* without therefore regarding it as *totum*, e.g. if we assume no boundaries or determinate magnitude of it at all.",[]],"reflm:5866":["*Complexus*, taking together, is not *compositum obiective tale*.",[]],"reflm:5867":["To divide up is not to divide.",[]],"reflm:5868":["A *compositum*, among all *coniuncta*, is only that to be called which is thought through the combination of the manifold, of which one is possible without the other.\nOne of many mutually determining one another is a *compositum*.\nOne of many mutually influencing one another is a *compositum substantiale*.",[]],"reflm:5869":["*compositum ideale* is any *qvantum* whose parts do not precede the possibility of the *compositum* (s but whose parts already presuppose the whole as given), e.g. space.\n*reale*, whose parts are in *commercium*. Therefore the relation of cause to caused is not composition.\nThe *compositum* of parts that can also exist outside the combination with others is real.\nWe cannot call the accidents parts. For their *nexus* is inherence in a third, not *aggregatum* to one another.",[]],"reflm:5870":["On the possibility of a change.\nA thing arises and passes away, a state begins and ceases. A thing whose state begins and ceases is changed; and what is changed, that remains.",[]],"reflm:5871":["We can notice the changing only in that which persists. If everything flows, then even the flowing cannot be perceived. Experience therefore of arising and passing away is possible only through that which persists. Thus [Subst] there is something in nature that remains (neither arises nor passes away), and this is substance. only the accidents change. *principium* of the possibility of experiences. Place designates the substance. In different places there are different substances; [die accide] what adheres to the persistent in a place and distinguishes itself from the persistent is accident.",[]],"reflm:5872":["Nothing in the world arises from nothing.\nNothing in the world is annihilated.\nNo experience is possible concerning this.\n(s Propositions of the persistence of substance.)",[]],"reflm:5873":["We can cognize things only through predicates, thus arising and passing away only as predicates; now one can have no concept of something as predicate without subject, thus the subject must remain in all that passes away; otherwise we cannot cognize the passing away itself through experience.",[]],"reflm:5874":["The parts of a real *compositum* are in *commercium*.\nThreefold relation: of the accident to the substance, of the effect to the cause and of the parts in a composite among one another.",[]],"reflm:5875":["Above, next to and \nThe only thing that is cognized a posteriori in appearances is the matter or the real of them, namely what corresponds to sensation. The formal of appearances, space and time, is cognized a priori and is pure intuition. To experience belongs, besides the [appearance] intuition, also the real in general and unity of the manifold of intuition.",[]],"reflm:5876":["69. E II 414. 355.\n M 69':\nOn space and time.\nIt is so far from being the case that the sensible intuitions of space and time should be confused representations, that they rather provide the most distinct cognitions of all, namely the mathematical ones. And [they] that they are the forms of sensible intuition, that makes it comprehensible how mathematical cognitions of [appearances a priori] things a priori are possible; which 1. would not take place if the objects of the senses were things in themselves, 2. Also not, if appearances were nothing other than indistinct intuitions of things; for then our cognition of appearances would always be taken only *a posteriori*, because the form of them would be not in our senses, but in the things.\nM 69:\nThe mathematical properties of matter, e.g. the infinite divisibility, prove that space and time do not belong to the properties of things, but of the representations of things in sensible intuition; for since the essential of these representations is composition, if I cancel that, nothing (hence also nothing simple) remains.\nSpace is no concept *a priori*, but intuition, which precedes the concept. For whence should synthetic judgments *a priori* come? and what sort of object should then be represented thereby, since space contains no object yet?\nSpace is itself a *synthesis a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5877":["A *compositum* (composite) is either formal, which contains nothing but the form of composition, e.g. of sensible intuition (*intuitus sensitivi*). [this] or real, and this either a real *phaenomenon* or a real *noumenon*.",[]],"reflm:5878":["Between M § 230 and 231:\n*Phaenomena*, insofar as they are outside one another at the same time, are in *commercio* (community).",[]],"reflm:5879":["The (concept of) space is itself only a form of composition; thus, if this is cancelled, then everything is cancelled and nothing remains.",[]],"reflm:5880":["In and \nThat of which, if the thought is cancelled (*compositio*), the object itself is cancelled, is merely appearance.",[]],"reflm:5881":["In and \n*Continuum* — *discretum per se*.",[]],"reflm:5882":["A *compositum* is such either originarily or derivatively. The former, of which, all composition having been removed, nothing remains, whose division precedes composition; that, however, whose composition precedes all division, is derivative. That whose every division is quantitative, i.e. into homogeneous parts, |\nis a *continuum*; that whose division is heterogeneous into simple parts (not *quanta*), is qualitative.",[]],"reflm:5883":["Space, time and sensation.",[]],"reflm:5884":["*respectus* of coordination in that which is at the same time, of subordination: what is one after another, both in time. In the first the *respectus* of extraposition.",[]],"reflm:5885":["Space and time are *composita idealia*, neither of substances nor of accidents, but of relations, which precede things.",[]],"reflm:5886":["In and \nSpace contains the form of all coordination in intuition, time of all subordination.",[]],"reflm:5887":["The magnitude of the multitude is extensive.",[]],"reflm:5888":["Everything that takes up a part of time or of space has extensive magnitude, extension and alteration. Every reality has its degree between a and 0 infinitely small breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5889":["All *division* is either quantitative or qualitative. The latter is separation; both are either *divisio immediata*: breaking up, or *mediata*: decomposition, *decompositio* into mediate parts. All division is either [logical] ideal or real. The ideal logical or mathematical. The former either of the *sphaera* or of the content. The real of a composite substance.",[]],"reflm:5890":["A given *quantum* (g in space and time), which nevertheless is supposed to be *illimitatum*, is impossible. Space is infinite *progressive*, but not *collective*.",[]],"reflm:5891":["The *quantitas* of an *aggregatum* is extensive, of *ratio* intensive: *gradus* (degree).",[]],"reflm:5892":["Totality as a collective concept cannot be thought of space and time. For there the plurality is only successive addition and the *completudo* of it never possible. On the other hand, well, of a thing that is thought through pure concepts of reason: *illimitatum*.",[]],"reflm:5893":["Next to and \nThe greatest and unrestricted is unique (s not (it does not grasp everything within itself)). The absolute totality is that which in a certain aim is unrestricted.\nThe progression in the construction of a magnitude is either finite or infinite. Both concern not the magnitude of the thing, but of the measurement, and hold only of appearances. The infinite is never given, but only the condition of the possibility of the *progressus in infinitum* or *indefinitum*.\n(s *infinitudo* is not the idea of *omnitudo*, also not of the *maximum*, also not of totality.)\nThe infinity of the possible or actual addition.",[]],"reflm:5894":["Negation of lack =0 can be regarded as an infinitely small degree, but not negation of the contradiction of concepts (opposition of quality).",[]],"reflm:5895":["In and \nThe infinite in a thing in itself is not impossible, but well the infinite of a given appearance, because, if here the representation is not given entirely, the thing is not given either. For the object is here only representation.",[]],"reflm:5896":["The totality of that which can be thought only through a *progressus* into the infinite is impossible. What is thought merely through a concept of the understanding as *quantum* can also be represented as given infinite; for it is given before the *progressus*.",[]],"reflm:5897":["What is given only through composition is [therefore] always finite, although the composition goes into the infinite.",[]],"reflm:5898":["Space and time are both nothing but compositions of sensible impressions. This composition goes into the infinite, but is never infinite. The magnitude of space presupposes the magnitude of time.\nInfinity of division. In infinite time infinitely small.",[]],"reflm:5899":["*indefinitum* is a general concept, which with regard to magnitude is undetermined. A *quantum singulare* is not *indefinitum*.",[]],"reflm:5900":["*Quantum infinitum* in itself may be possible, but not as *phaenomenon*, because here the *progressus in infinitum* is thought as completely given. Thus no infinite whole and no infinite multitude of parts of a [whole] given whole.",[]],"reflm:5901":["If, because the line ab can be divided into the infinite, it also actually consists of infinitely many parts next to one another, then the line af must also actually consist of infinitely many parts, because infinitely many parts can be carried on it, and if the first does not follow, the second not follow. Because the line ab can also be traversed in a finite time, and to each little part of time a speed can correspond, with which the space ae or ef would be traversed, so, if that infinite multitude of parts were actual, the traversing of an infinite space in a finite time would also have to be possible.\nIf all parts of the possible division (g at the same time) were actual, then there would be an absolute magnitude of space, which would be determinable from the multitude of all parts. But since to an infinite multitude belongs an infinite time, to cognize it entirely, an infinite time could be given entirely.",[]],"reflm:5902":["A thing in itself does not depend on our representations, can therefore be much greater than our representations reach. But appearances are themselves only representations, and the magnitude of them, i.e. the idea of their generation through the *progressus*, cannot be greater than this *progressus*; and since this is never given as infinite, but is only possible into the infinite, the magnitude of the world as appearance is also not infinite, but the *progressus* in it goes into the infinite.",[]],"reflm:5903":["If space and time were properties of things in themselves, then from their being mathematically infinite, i.e. the *progressus* in them, insofar as they are given as infinite wholes, being greater than all number, it would not follow that they are impossible, but incomprehensible for us. But now space and time are not things in themselves, and their magnitude not in itself, but given only through the *progressus*. Since now a *progressus in infinitum*, which would have been given entirely, is a contradiction |\nso a *infinitum mathematicum datum* is impossible, but a *quantum in infinitum dabile* possible. From this, however, it also does not follow that space and time have boundaries in themselves, for that is also impossible, but only that they are not at all things in themselves, but always have boundaries only where our thoughts and representations stop.",[]],"reflm:5904":["Just so nature is not the order of things in themselves, but that which the understanding posits for the appearances; of things in themselves I can say that they are not subject to the order of things, and of our understanding that it, in accordance with the order of nature, but not determined through the same, makes the appearances possible.",[]],"reflm:5905":["In the *Noumeno* I can think quantity only through the relation to the *illimitato*, i.e. to that which contains *omnitudinem*, as the measure, and indeed through mere negations. The *ens realissimum* is a determinate *quantum*, but not the *infinitum*, because the *quantum* in itself is not determined, how great it is, through the impossibility of measuring it out entirely. The *maximum* conversely is for that reason not also *infinitum*. But it is still a *totum absolute tale*. In *phaenomenis* there is no absolute totality.\nThe *infinitum reale* (of things (g of existence) in general), in which everything is, is Spinoza's concept. There is the limitation, where in space the bounding. There being precedes, afterwards the determination of the same.",[]],"reflm:5906":["*Argumentum* On the objective reality of time.\nIt was objected that the unknown something x, which at one |\ntime produces the appearance of the egg, produces in me at another time that of the chick; thus something in the object must have altered, because it could not contain the ground of two opposed determinations at once. I answer: It is the same object which produces the ground of the appearance of two opposed states as successively existing, and thus the appearance of an alteration. This is no harder to explain than how [appearance] alteration is possible, i.e. since a thing or a multitude of things is supposed to contain the ground of two opposites.",[]],"reflm:5907":["Leibniz's proposition is that *numerica diversitas* must also be *specifica diversitas* and conversely *specifica identitas totalis* must also be *numerica*. This last proposition holds of an object of pure reason, e.g. *ens realissimum* is *specifice totaliter idem* with another *realissimo*. Were it in space and time, *numerica identitas* would not follow from this, namely that it be a single being.\nAppearances are different things merely through difference of places, for the difference of places precedes *a priori* and [lets] makes, regardless of the quality of things, numerical difference.\nAgainst this the (g specific) difference of things in things in themselves or the difference of qualities always makes different things. But with *phaenomenis* it can be one and the same thing and must also be, which is determined otherwise in different times; thus here it says: *numero idem potest esse specifice diversum*, if one understands by that every determination through different quality.",[]],"reflm:5908":["The difference of places makes difference of things, because places in space, with all inner identity, are nevertheless different as found outside one another.",[]],"reflm:5909":["The parts of time do not determine one another reciprocally, but |\nthe preceding is only determining. Hence it has only one dimension.\nEverything that mathematically determines one another in space and time does not for that reason determine one another dynamically.",[]],"reflm:5910":["The proposition of contingency wants to say that everything is necessary either in itself or as *causatum alterius* (for the *causatum* is also necessary). This proposition, however, holds only [so] of this: that I can cognize *a priori* the existence of a thing [never otherwise], if I can think its non-being, only by means of a cause. e.g. a motion that is not known through any experience. — But the actual contingent is cognized not so much [in de] in the alteration, but in the form of purposiveness. This in matter (because it has no life) must have a cause that is different from it.",[]],"reflm:5911":["One knows the things in the world only through the relation that they have to a possible occurrence. Thus as causes and according to their forces.",[]],"reflm:5912":["Our concept of the contingent thing is the concept of a thing that is possible only as *causatum alterius*, not: whose non-being is impossible, i.e. unthinkable. Thus the proposition: *quodlibet contingens est causatum alterius* is tautological; e.g. We call the existence of organized beings contingent. We can namely cognize real possibility only by the object's being given in some way. If we want to cognize it *a priori*, then it must be cognized not through intuition (empirical), but as existence through something else, which *a priori* determines, i.e. cause.",[]],"reflm:5913":["If something is in itself contingent, then it is so far represented merely through understanding and must have a cause. But if something is given merely in a contingent way (in the appearance as something that alters), then the thing itself is not for that reason contingent.\n(s To infer from one's concept of a thing to the necessity or contingency of its existence means: to leap out of oneself.)",[]],"reflm:5914":["93. E II 893.\n*Principium contingentiae* signifies that actually nothing exists as absolutely contingent, i.e. that its existence must be cognized as objectively determined if it is subjectively determined in perception. — (g Everything is necessary, either absolutely or hypothetically; |\nM 93: |\njust so nothing is absolutely necessary either, but only in relation to the possibility of the objects of experience.)\nM 93':\n(g *Contingens* is either in the logical understanding that whose non-being can be thought, or: whose non-being is possible; the latter we cannot cognize. it requires |\nM 93:|\nacquaintance with the necessary.)",[]],"reflm:5915":["That something exists cannot be cognized through reason if it is contingent. [If it] Through experience it also cannot be cognized as existing (objectively), except insofar as according to empirical laws the perception of it is necessary. In a word: the objectively valid and the necessarily valid are one and the same. What I am to say of the object must be necessary. For if it is contingent, then it holds only in the subject, but not of the object.",[]],"reflm:5916":["Laws pertain to existing things (*leges pertinent ad existentia*), likewise mutability (*item mutabilitas*).",[]],"reflm:5917":["Every thing weighed according to its existence in itself (not as appearance) is an object of the understanding and not of mere experience, must thus be determined according to principles of the understanding as absolutely or hypothetically necessary. that whose existence is cognized only through the understanding is cognized *a priori*, thus as necessary.",[]],"reflm:5918":["In and \nThe contingent as such cannot be cognized *a priori*. But every object must be able to be cognized *a priori* as such.\nWhat is given in experience is precisely thereby thought under laws, hence as necessary.",[]],"reflm:5919":["The existence of a thing can be cognized only relative to a concept that I have of it, never absolutely as contingent or necessary.",[]],"reflm:5920":["Everything that is, is necessary, either in itself or through cause.",[]],"reflm:5921":["In and \nThat something is contingent with regard to a certain concept I can indeed cognize *a priori*, but not that it is contingent *in singulari* and *individuo*.",[]],"reflm:5922":["We can cognize the contingency neither of things nor of the state, not *a priori*, not *a posteriori* either. For the non-being that follows upon existence does not prove that it could have taken place in the place of existence. But the principle of contingency holds only of the empirically contingent. What is constant is empirically necessary;\n|\nwhat arises or passes away, empirically contingent. Thus every state has its cause, but not the substance, and this proposition holds only in the series of appearances, but not of these as things in themselves in relation to something outside the series.",[]],"reflm:5923":["95'. E II 983.\nDeduction of the pure cognitions *a priori*.\nIn experience alone can our concepts be given completely *in concreto*, and hence their objective reality be completely exhibited. Concepts whose nature it is contrary to be [so] exhibited in experience are mere ideas. Hence, in reference to possible experience, the objective reality of all concepts [to such], i.e., their significance, will have to be sought. Others, namely those that are mere ideas, can indeed be assumed as hypotheses, but not count as provable.\nNow, when it is a matter of the possibility of pure cognition *a priori*, we can transform the question into this one: whether experience also contains (g sheer) cognition that is given only *a posteriori*, or whether something is encountered in it that is not empirical and yet contains the ground of the possibility of experience.\nFirst, representation of the senses [immediately] belongs to all experience. Second, consciousness; this, if it is combined immediately with that, is called empirical consciousness, and the representation [which] (g of the senses), combined with empirical consciousness, is called perception. Were experience nothing further than an accumulation of perceptions, then [except] in it nothing would be encountered that was not of empirical origin.\nBut the consciousness of the perceptions refers all representation only to ourselves as modifications of our state; they are then separated among themselves, and above all they are not cognitions of any thing and refer to no object. They are therefore not yet experience, which must indeed contain empirical representation, but at the same time as cognition of the objects of the senses.\nM 95':\nIf we question logic about what can be called cognition in general, then a concept is [that which] a representation (g or sum total of such) that has been referred to an object and designates it; and in that we connect (separate) one concept with another in a judgment, we think something of the object that has been designated through a given concept, i.e., we cognize it, in that we judge about it. All cognition, hence also that of experience, accordingly consists of judgments; and even concepts are representations that are prepared for possible judgments, in that they represent something in general that has been given as cognizable through a predicate.\nTherefore [are] experience is possible only through judgments, in which perceptions indeed constitute the empirical materials, but the reference of these to an object, and cognition of it through perceptions, cannot depend on empirical consciousness alone.\nBut the form of every judgment consists in the objective unity of the consciousness of the given concepts, i.e., in the consciousness that these must belong to one another, and thereby designate an object, in whose (g complete) representation they are always encountered together.\nBut this necessity of the connection is not a rep|resentation\nof empirical origin [and nevertheless], but presupposes a rule that [is] must be given *a priori*, i.e., unity of consciousness that takes place *a priori*. This unity of consciousness is contained in the moments of the understanding in judging, and only that is object with reference to which unity of consciousness [a prio] of the manifold representations is thought *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5924":["The universal proposition is: Synthetic cognitions *a priori* from mere concepts are impossible, but they are possible 1. from the construction of concepts, 2. from rules that contain the possibility of experience and through which perceptions become objective cognitions. For in experience pure cognitions *a priori* alone get their significance and use.",[]],"reflm:5925":["NB. Synthetic (g pure) cognitions from concepts that cannot serve for the determination of the objects of experience in general, of which sort the transcendental ideas are, have the significance that they determine the boundary of all cognition of experience, i.e., show that this is never sufficient to itself and complete; consequently in every aim something that lies at the ground of experience must be assumed; but that we can cognize this only so far as it lies at the ground of experience, and are entitled to think it hypothetically only so far as practical propositions that go beyond grounds of experience require it.\nThe ground on account of which we can cognize something *a priori* is that objects of [experience] perception are appearances; the cause why we cannot cognize them completely is that we would then not take them as objects of experience, since this is never com|plete\nand, if we take them so, we always contradict ourselves.",[]],"reflm:5926":["Space and time are themselves nothing other than forms of the composition of the objects of sensation; hence also, if one cancels all composition there, nothing remains over. Now the unity of consciousness in this composition, so far as that is considered (g as) universal, is the concept of the understanding, and that unity belongs to experience as objective cognition, so concepts of the understanding are also required *a priori* for the possibility of experience. Something must therefore precede* even experience itself, through which it itself becomes possible; but in it all cognition *a priori* alone must have its reality.\n*(g For the logical form of the understanding in judging must after all precede, and the appearances [must, as determined] (as mere representations) must be regarded as determined in regard to each of these forms, otherwise no experience can arise from them. We can also put the word experience in place of the word objects of the senses. For we do not cognize the things in themselves; we can know nothing of them except all experience possible for us of them, and indeed so far as this is determined *a priori* from the form of sensibility and that of the understanding.)",[]],"reflm:5927":["It is absolutely impossible to cognize anything synthetically *a priori* of things in themselves, but only of appearances, because synthetic judgments require intuitions, either pure or empirical, but synthetic judgments *a priori* require pure ones. This [are] is however possible only as a form of our sensibility [because we] and holds only of appearances, not of things in themselves.\nCategory is the representation of the relation of the manifold of intuition to a universal consciousness (to the universality of consciousness, which is properly objective). The relation of the representations to the universality of consciousness, consequently the (g transformation of the empirical and particular) unity of [particular consciousness empirical] consciousness, which is merely subjective, into a consciousness that |\nis universal and objective, belongs to logic. This unity of consciousness, so far as it is universal and can be represented *a priori*, is the pure concept of the understanding. This can therefore be nothing other than the universal of the unity of consciousness, which constitutes the objective validity of a judgment.\nThe manifold, so far as it is represented as necessarily belonging to one consciousness (or also to the unity of consciousness in general), [as belonging] is thought through the concept of an object: the object is always a something in general. The determination of it rests merely on the unity of the manifold of its intuition, and indeed on the universally valid unity of the consciousness of it.\nTwo items of cognition take place *a priori*. 1. Intuitions, 2. unity of consciousness of the manifold of intuitions (even of the empirical ones). This unity of consciousness constitutes the form of experience as objective empirical cognition.",[]],"reflm:5928":["Synthetic propositions through concepts are always *a priori* and impossible; but through the construction of concepts (in sensible formal intuition in general) or the combination of universality with the empirical synthesis in general they are not only possible, but also necessary. For experience is nothing other than synthetic [cognition] connection of perceptions in one consciousness (as necessary, hence containing universality).",[]],"reflm:5929":["If I think something through the understanding as (g in itself) contingent, then I cannot think precisely the same thing through the understanding as existing without a cause. (but why must I think it as existing through the understanding, why not rather through experience?) (g what designates contingency?)\nIf I want to think a thing through the understanding, then something must be thought thereby as last subject, to which everything else belongs as predicate |\n(but what designates for me the last subject?). If I represent a thing as [multiplicity] sum total of the many homogeneous, then I must think it as magnitude; but what is given to me as magnitude prior to the multiplicity that is thought in it? Every thing has reality; but how is its difference from 0 possible to think?\nAll this indicates that our understanding indeed has laws for itself to think something, but that we can give these thoughts no application and meaning except through sensible intuition, which we bring under the condition of the unity of consciousness of the manifold, and that ultimately only in experience does the reality of the concepts of experience lie, and indeed of experience in general as such, which without those concepts would be merely a sum total of perceptions, whereas those without these would be a mere manifold mode of the thoroughgoing consciousness of itself in the manifold consciousness of the representations.",[]],"reflm:5930":["The objective unity of the consciousness of the manifold of representations is the connection of the same either with one and the same concept, e.g. All human beings [or] (in a word: a universally valid combination (g of the concepts) in one consciousness), and then the unity is called logical; or this logical unity of consciousness is regarded as determined in the concept of a thing and constitutes its concept: that is the synthetic or transcendental unity of consciousness. There the unity is represented [distributively], which concerns merely the relation of the [representations] concepts; here that which itself constitutes a concept of the thing through the unification of its manifold in one consciousness. e.g. [All] Many [human beings] things are outside one another, and on the other hand: space is One, which comprehends much outside one another. The former is the quantity [of one] of the concept of a judgment, the latter the concept [of] of a thing as *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:5931":["Category is the (g necessary) unity of consciousness (g in the composition) of the manifold of representations (g intuition), insofar as it makes possible the concept of an object in general (in |\ncontrast to the [sub] merely subjective unity of the consciousness of the perceptions). This unity in the categories must be necessary. e.g. Logically a concept can be subject or predicate. An object, however, considered transcendentally, presupposes something that is necessarily merely subject and the other merely predicate. — Ground — Logically something can be reciprocally ground and sequel; but really everything, if it stands in community of time, must stand in community. In a magnitude there is necessarily unity of the many.\nTo experience belong: 1. Intuition (the manifold); 2. Connection of the manifold in one (g empirical) consciousness; 3. Unity of the connection of the manifold, which is universally valid. Intuition is given *a priori* (the *progressus* in it also finite, also the unity of the connection).",[]],"reflm:5932":["Through the category I represent to myself an object (g in general) as determined with regard to the logical functions of judgments: of the subject (not predicate), of the consequence as ground, of the multiplicity in its representation. But why must I represent every object as determined with regard to not merely one, but all logical functions in judgments? Because thereby alone objective unity of consciousness, i.e. universally valid connection of the perceptions, and hence experience as the only reality of cognitions, is possible.\nThis unity of consciousness (g of the connection of our representations) is just as much given *a priori* in us as the foundation of all concepts, as form of appearance is the foundation of the intuitions. But both hold only of human cognition and also have objective meaning only with regard to it, indeed the |\ncategory can have no meaning *a priori* if there were no intuitions *a priori*.\nThus category is the concept of an object in general, insofar as it is determined *a priori* in itself with regard to a logical function of judgments (so that one must think the combination of the manifold in its representation [through no other] through this function).\nAll objects that we are to think must be determined with regard to all logical functions of the understanding; for thereby alone can we think, and through the fact that something [is represented as determined with regard to these functions] determines thinking in general (how it is to be thought), it is an object, i.e. something to which a particular thought, which is different from others, corresponds.\nAll principles [of the] of the pure understanding pertain to sensibility and indicate the conditions under which the representation of sensibility belongs under a category. They thus determine the rule of judgments in general with regard to appearances and are principles of possible experience; for without the categories our representations could not relate to objects, because they alone determine thinking in general [to] in relation to something in general.",[]],"reflm:5933":["The unity of the consciousness [of the representations in] of the manifold in the representation of an object in general is the judgment. The representation of an object in general, insofar as it is [determined] (of the logical unity) determined with regard to this objective unity of consciousness, is category.\nThe unity of consciousness is either empirical: in the perception of the manifold, combined by imagination. Or it is logical: the unity in the representation of the object. The former is contingent and merely subjective, the second necessary and objective. The former is required for concepts, the second for judgments and for their possibility in general. The schematism indicates the conditions under which |\nan appearance is determined with regard to the logical function and thus stands under a category; the transcendental principles indicate the categories under which the schemata of sensibility stand.",[]],"reflm:5934":["100. E II 984. 408.\n M 100':\nExperience is cognition (g *a posteriori*, i.e.) of that which is [in] object of sensation (*a posteriori*). Sensations give no cognition at all; thus something must be added over them (g *a priori*), [so f] if experience is to become possible. Over the representation *a posteriori* only that *a priori* from concepts can be added, and this can only be the connection (*synthesis*) [se *a priori*], insofar as it is determined *a priori* (for the mere comparison of the sensations gives nothing but sensation and no object).\nThe universal (g formal) principle of possible experience is therefore: *all perceptions [stand] are determined *a priori* with regard to their connection in one consciousness (for consciousness is unity, in which alone the connection of all perceptions is possible, and, if it is to be cognition of the object, must be determined *a priori*). The objective unity in the consciousness of different representations is the form of judgment. Thus all perceptions, insofar as they are to constitute experience, stand under the formal conditions of judgments in general, and the [de] determination of the same through this function is the concept of the understanding. [Experience thus] All experiences as possible perceptions stand *a priori* under concepts of the understanding, through which alone they can become empirical cognition, i.e. representation of the objects (*a posteriori*).\nM 100:\n*(g All appearances are determinable *a priori* with regard to their connection [through the with regard to] in accordance with the unity of consciousness in all judgments in general, i.e. they stand under categories.\nSpace and time are the forms of combination in intuition and serve to apply the categories *in concreto*.)",[]],"reflm:5935":["All synthetic cognitions from mere concepts go only to things as appearances, never to things in themselves, and to those merely insofar as they are intuitions (mathematics), or as belonging to a possible experience.\nWe can represent nothing to ourselves *a priori* except that of which we ourselves contain the grounds in our power of representation, either in sensibility or in the understanding: in the latter either, where the understanding merely determines the unity of consciousness *a priori* (theoretical), or where reason, merely in accordance with the understanding, directs the actions to objects (practical).",[]],"reflm:5936":["Ontology is science of the things in general, i.e. of the possibility of our cognition of things *a priori*, i.e. independently of experience. It can now teach us nothing of things in themselves, but only of the conditions *a priori* under which we can cognize things [that us] in experience in general. i.e. principles of the possibility of experience.",[]],"reflm:5937":["Apart from ontology there also belong to metaphysics the cosmological antinomy (among sheer ideas of pure reason), the psychological paralogisms, and the theological ideal. The last two are transcendent, but without contradiction. The cosmological ideas, however, conflict with one another. The psychological ones take the intellectual as given empirically, the theological the intellectual as given *a priori*. Existence from mere concepts.",[]],"reflm:5938":["The transcendental ideas serve to restrict the principles of experience, so that they are not extended to things in themselves, and to show that what is no object at all of possible experience is for that reason no non-thing, and that [es außer der] experience is not sufficient for itself and for reason, but always turns away further and thus from itself.",[]],"reflm:5939":["Ideas. Through the cosmological ideas we want to cognize the absolute whole of the series of conditions in appearances. Through the psychological ones the absolute constitution of an object of experience (g not in relation to senses) as a thing in itself. Through the theological one the existence of things from mere concepts (g without any experience), i.e. we [wollen ein] make for ourselves an idea that at the same time carries existence *a priori* inseparably with it.\nTo want to cognize the soul according to what it is in itself, from inner sense, is in vain.",[]],"reflm:5940":["The world is a *totum finitorum*, because one part is the *complementum* of the other to the whole, hence none is infinite; second: *contingentium*, because one part depends on the other.",[]],"reflm:5941":["*Mundus intelligibilis* is the *totum absolutum substantiarum per se*; *mundus [intelli] sensibilis* is the *totum phaenomenorum*.",[]],"reflm:5942":["In and \n*totum absolutum* (*qvod non est pars*) *substantiarum* is *mundus in sensu transscendentali*. Sense-world and understanding-world.",[]],"reflm:5943":["*Mundus noumenon* (*intelligibilis*) is the idea of a whole of substances that is no further part. *Mundus phaenomenon* is the application of this idea to appearances.\nOf the *mundus noumenon* one can think more worlds [zu] outside one another; in the *phaenomenon* there is only a single one, through space and time. The *commercium* of the substances of the first is possible only through *harmoniam praestabilitam*, 1. because many substances can be in community only through a common cause; 2. because this harmony would not be a nature, were it not already laid into the creation of the things of the world and thus preestablished.\nIn the *mundo sensibili* *influxus physicus* holds. In the *mundo noumeno* all substances are intellectual, at least we cannot think them through other predicates than those of representations. In the *sensibili* they are extended. Motion. The influence of the first and of the second is really nothing, for the alterations of the second are only *phaenomena*. Bodies are not substances and motion not an *accidens* of them, but only *phaenomena* of the intelligible.\n(g intelligible or intellectual.)",[]],"reflm:5944":["Over, beside and \nOne cannot say *corpus* [ph] *noumenon*, but *substantia composita noumenon* or *phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:5945":["Over and \n*Harmonia absqve commercio* is that which does not occur according to a general rule of nature and thus without the cause lying in the substances themselves.",[]],"reflm:5946":["Beside and \nWhether the state of the world does not always remain the same, while that of things changes.",[]],"reflm:5947":["The question of the *unicitas* of the world [ist] concerns either the copulative plurality or the disjunctive.",[]],"reflm:5948":["*Contingentia mundi*. Everything contingent once was not (for that is the mark by which contingency is cognized), thus it is a sequel of something preceding, thus it has a cause. Only that is taken to be contingent which is in a series that presupposes another.",[]],"reflm:5949":["The 4th antinomy. We need an absolute necessity, but we can reach none in the *regressus* of the world. For causality must always begin, and such a cause is contingent. This antinomy is unavoidable if we regard time as a determination of things in themselves and appearances as things. If this is not so, then in the time-series there is nothing necessary; but that which contains the ground of appearances is not in the time-series and can thus have causality without beginning and be necessary.",[]],"reflm:5950":["Whoever asserts the *regressus in infinitum* does not thereby yet assert an *infinitam seriem rationatorum datam*, if they are *phaenomena*; for these are given only in the *regressus*.",[]],"reflm:5951":["Because the *regressus* goes *in infinitum*, the absolute totality of the world of sense is not to be thought.",[]],"reflm:5952":["In and \nThe magnitude of the world-whole as of an aggregate or series (this in time or the series of causes). The magnitude of the series of appearances is not different from the magnitude of the *progressus in infinitum*.",[]],"reflm:5953":["Opposite and \n*Regressus in infinitum absqve caussa prima* is impossible among *noumena*, but necessary among *phaenomena*, which are not things but themselves subsist only in the *regressus*.",[]],"reflm:5954":["In *mundo* [non] (*vniverso*) *non datur* (s *infinitum*) *abyssus* either of things, or also of empty time and space. One of the two is necessary if *phaenomena* are to be represented in their absolute totality, i.e. as given in themselves.\nHere there is no completion, neither in the infinite nor in the boundary; but all sensibility has a cause outside itself, for it is not a thing in itself.",[]],"reflm:5955":["The proposition: In the world everything is nature, i.e. *non datur hiatus*, where something happens against nature and is torn off from its chain. We can explain occurrences only according to determinate laws of nature.",[]],"reflm:5956":["In and \n*Casus* in the theoretical or teleological sense. If space and time adhere to things in themselves, then the causality |\nof the same and of their cause must also be determined in time, hence naturally necessary, thus nothing unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5957":["*non datur hiatus*, because otherwise we would have to perceive time and space in themselves. *vacuum metaphysicum non datur*.",[]],"reflm:5958":["No absolutely empty space (for then there would be no outer relations). No empty time; for nothing can begin absolutely. No leap, no chance, no fate.",[]],"reflm:5959":["123. E II 1393. 1438.\nM 123':\n*Abyssus*, *Saltus*, *Casus*, *Fatum* (s *Hiatus*: *vacuum intermedium*; *circumfusum*) are all concepts of the unconditioned, which can be thought in a *mundo* [phaenomeno] *noumeno* or at least in connection with it, but which do not fit the *mundus phaenomenon*.\nThe absolute totality of composition must be thought in the *mundo noumeno*; in the *phaenomeno* the world-whole (g as) according to space and time (g given) is *abyssus*, and the like cannot be given. In the *noumeno* the absolute totality of division, in the *phaenomeno* that would give *saltus*. In the first, where the condition of time does not adhere to existence and there is no alteration that needs a cause, freedom can be thought; in the second it would be *Casus*. |\nIn the *noumenon* a being necessary for itself can be thought as cause of the world. In this one I can, according to its laws, think no other relation [ders] of the cause of the world to the world than in time, and there the causality is always contingent, hence the *causa* itself.\n(g In the world of sense all (g synthesis) unity is only that of the *regressus*. Without boundaries nothing is given therein. So also [nicht] |\nM 123: |\nin space and time, which are unbounded, nothing with an absolute totality of synthesis, nothing unconditioned is given.)",[]],"reflm:5960":["The totality of composition in the (g world of sense) world and of division are both false and must be so, because it is taken in space and time.\nBut the totality of cause and effect*, likewise of necessity, can both be true, because the one can be considered in the intelligible world and the other entirely outside all world in the intelligible, hence outside all dependence, i.e. contingency.\n*(g for these concepts do not necessarily belong to objects of the senses, if one namely wants thereby to cognize nothing, but only to have the possibility of a thought of them.)",[]],"reflm:5961":["123'. E II 1401.\nM 123:\nThe whole dialectic comes down to this. One wants at all costs to have the (g sense-)world held to be a thing in itself, although it can be thought only in space and time. Now as a thing in itself there must in|deed\nbe absolute totality of conditions in it. But this is possible in space and time neither [in] the composition nor decomposition nor according to origination. — Only, if everything is merely *phaenomena*, i.e. representations, which can at any time posit something only under conditions of time, then |\nM 123': |\nneither the world nor anything in the world is given in itself, but everything only as far as the *progressus* reaches, and one contradicts oneself if one thinks a *totum* [abso], which is supposed to be given in itself, and yet posits it in space and time.",[]],"reflm:5962":["132'. 133'. 133. E II 376. 1386.\nM 131':\nIf space and time were considered as conditions of the existence of the world in itself and *mundus Noumenon* not distinguished from the *phaenomenon*: then the causality of God with regard to the world would also be determined in time, consequently [the W] God would belong along with the world, [its] his causality would belong through time in [the] a series of causes and effects. God therefore, like everything that belongs together with the world to a whole, would be contingent.\nThe possibility of counting precisely the same subject, which belongs to the world of sense, with regard to the same [connection through] categories (of cause and of existence in general) among intelligible beings, thus either in the intelligible world or outside all world, is really the possibility of using the categories not merely with regard to objects of the senses but for things in general, but only as for something that we otherwise do not cognize except that it is not appearance; which is entirely well possible, because it signifies only the possibility of thinking, not of determining what is thought [to] through predicates of the world of sense.\n1. The same being, which as member of the world of sense has causality, which is always [according to] under rules of time-determinations conditioned, can as unconditioned with regard to time in respect of the same *caussatum* in the world of sense be free cause of the same, i.e. it |\ndoes not stand under the condition of time-determination and yet belongs to [the same] a world with the intelligible substratum of the *mundus noumenon*.\n2. Everything that [with the] belongs in time belongs to one (g and the same) world, even if it is assumed as the cause of the things in it, and exists contingently, because time does not necessarily require the existence of things and in general substances that would be necessary belong to no whole at all and to no world. But it is possible [it] to think its existence outside of time, thus also without counting it to a world, consequently without robbing it of necessity; and the category of existence is at least a concept that still always remains over, notwithstanding that it cannot be cognized determinately, e.g. as duration without time. — In a word: if space and time are regarded as properties of things, then it is not possible to get out of the contradictions.\nM 132':\nThe cause why the first two antinomies are both false is that I had to lay at the ground of both of them a contradictory concept, namely of a whole in space and time, which yet was supposed to be an absolute whole, consequently a thing in itself. For the conflict concerns the composition and division of an intuition, which [always] necessarily must happen in space and time, because [this the] these themselves make the concept of it as magnitude possible. By contrast causality and modality of existence — or better — Those antinomies concern the [possibility] inner possibility of appearances themselves (as *quanta*); the other, however, the possibility of the same through something else, thus the dependence or independence of their state or of their existence itself (i.e. the conditioned or unconditioned possibility [of their] of existence). In order to answer the first questions I had to seek the conditions [by] |\namong the appearances alone, which however here are never complete; in order to answer the second I could seek the unconditioned (because it can be something other as *correlatum*) outside the appearance in the non-sensible, and there both propositions could be true. — Thereby the ideality of space and time is proved indirectly, because contradictions with itself follow from the opposite. But I have also proved them directly, namely from this, that synthetic cognitions *a priori* are, that these however are impossible without intuition *a priori* (pure); that finally pure intuition, where the form of it is not given in the subject before the object, is impossible, consequently that we can anticipate only appearances, consequently all objects of the senses are mere appearances.\nFurthermore the cognition of God remains entrusted to the same grounds of sound understanding as before; only the enthusiastic daring to decide about it through speculation from ignorance of the faculty of pure reason or even a presumption [of] after the analogy of the principles of experience to infer so high up, is taken away and the determination entirely entrusted to morals.\nM 133:\nThe opposite of the proposition: »the world is infinite according to space and time« is: »it is not infinite (given)«; and there of both propositions the latter is true. But if one asks: »what is it then, if it is not infinite in space or time?«, one can answer in two ways; either: »it is finite in space and time«, and there one runs into sheer absurdities, or: »as a given whole it is not at all in space and in time«; for in both an absolute whole cannot be given, because it is a whole of phenomena, i.e. of representations of the senses, which are given only in [experience] perception, not in themselves (although the ground of possible perceptions, the thing in itself, is given without perception), and which, since the *progressus* of perceptions never has a [through] bound possible in perception, permit a *progressus* into |\nthe infinite, which therefore never makes up an absolute whole. Just this holds of division. These two propositions can both be false, because the one contains more than is required for contradiction. [They are a] That is the logical resolution of the antinomy. But they are also both false, because they contain with an impossible condition, namely: that the world be given whole in space and time (just (g as) a *compositum* whole (g i.e. as world) given), and nevertheless be given in space and time. For the first proposition is grounded on the presupposition that a whole of appearances is given in itself and outside the representations, which is contradictory. And that is the transcendental resolution of the antinomy.\nSecondly. The opposite of the proposition: »all occurrences (g things) in the (g sense-)world stand under the mechanism of natural causality«, is: »they do not stand under it«. Here both propositions cannot be false; but they could both be true, because the second contains less in itself than is required for contradictory opposition. For there it would have to be added that as things of the world of sense they do not stand under the thought mechanism. But because it can still take place that the things of the world of sense [also as], which are the causes of the occurrences in it, considered also as [things of an] intelligible beings could be causes (for that can be thought without contradiction and without the concept: cause being cognition), they could so far be excepted from the mechanism of natural necessity, without yet contradicting that as things of the world of sense they belong to it.\nThe other proposition: that all things are contingent, because [it] they are determined in space and time as dependent on one another and variable according to their existence, can be granted, and the opposition does not conflict |\nwith it: that there could be some necessary being; for this is then outside space and time, consequently of all dependence on the world, rather to posit everything as dependent on it.\nM 133:\nLeibniz's pre-established harmony is perhaps only the idea of an intelligible world without space and time, in which the divine universal presence is the principle of the real *nexus* as of an intelligent cause, whereby the relations in which finite beings intuit themselves (as the form of appearance), [to agreement] with the rules agreeing already in the creation are pre-established to thoroughgoing harmony with the intelligible world, in which alone immediate truth is.",[]],"reflm:5963":["God is either in space at rest or moved or both at once (like bodies on the earth) or neither of the two. Now the last is true; therefore all the others, consequently the disjunctive proposition made of the first two, are false. The truth of the last is really the explanation of the falsity of the first two, for God is not at all in space.\nMatter (g in itself) consists either of infinitely many parts or of a determinate multitude of parts. Neither of the two is true, for it presupposes the totality of the division of space and this the absolute reality of it.",[]],"reflm:5964":["125. E II 1382.\nOn M 125':\nWe can consider a disjunctive proposition, as such, beforehand problematically, namely as if its disjunction were not yet settled; and then it reads: every thing is either A or B, or: both A and B, or: neither A nor B.[Alles] Something in the world is either nature or chance, or both nature and chance, or neither |\nnature nor chance. If both can be true, the opposition is not correct; if both can be false, the division is not complete.* The world either has a beginning (in time) or is from eternity. [Beydes kan (g zusammen) nicht wahr seyn, also ist die opposition richtig; aber die division ist incomplet, weil die Bedingung eines] Because a condition attaches here to the world, namely time, which contradicts completeness, the division is not complete, and it is neither eternal, nor begun in time, because it is not total; for as appearance no totality takes place. But if I say: the human soul is either in its actions under the mechanism of nature (g\n|\nM 125: |\nif I add merely, then this is an exponible proposition) or it is free or both together or neither of the two, then the soul is taken in different *respectu*, partly as *phaenomenon* partly as *noumenon*, since I am conscious of its absolute self not merely as appearance *a priori*. (I am: a proposition that is not empirical). and there both propositions can be true, the opposition is not correct, and totality does not take place in it as *phaenomenon*, but indeed as *noumenon* with regard to the *phaenomenon*. The flower is either red or blue or neither of the two: yellowish, or both altogether: violet.\nM 125:\n*(g The opposition is correct, but the division is not complete. The actions of the human being are not merely nature, but by no means on that account chance; rather they are free, i.e. are also to be regarded as if they did not stand at all in the series of determining grounds of the appearance, but are determined *a priori*.\nFreedom is a faculty of determining oneself *a priori* to act, not through empirical causes. Nature and chance both place the action under conditions *a posteriori*, namely the [determining] in time every occurrence according to its *positus* determining members or alterations of the world.)",[]],"reflm:5965":["In the division into nature and freedom the ground of the |\naction is taken either in the series of appearances [or], and so is everywhere nature; or outside the series, and indeed [from the series itself] either from each member of the series particularly, and then there is no nature, but pure chance; or from each member together with the series, and then nature and freedom are possible together and indeed in the same subject, taken partly as *phaenomenon*, partly as *noumenon*. For the soul can posit objects to itself *a priori*, of which reason itself can also be cause *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5966":["The correctness of the disjunctive proposition is settled by this, that both copulative propositions of the disjunction: the affirming and the negating are canceled.",[]],"reflm:5967":["The vocation of our reason with regard to the transcendental (g cosmological) ideas is 1. [zu] to regard everything in the world of sense as empirically conditioned, hence always to go on in the series of conditions and indeed in the world always onward (not to leap to an unconditioned outside it) and thereby to procure no ease for its reason. Rule of immanent use.\n2. To procure the conviction that all objects of experience are nothing but appearances and that things in themselves must lie at their ground, hence to prevent the transcendent use of the empirical. Through the latter all antinomy is removed, which rests merely on this, that appearances are considered as things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:5968":["That in the world of sense in every kind of connection nowhere an |\nabsolute first, also no infinity can be represented as entirely given, consequently no absolute totality, proves that the absolute must be thought outside it and that it itself subsists only in relation to our senses.\nIf the first can be taken according to mere conditions of sensibility, which happens with the composition and division of appearances in space and time, then the completeness of the regress is impossible and there is always a conflict between the assertion of completeness through an infinite series and that through a bounded series.\nBut if the first is not necessarily a determination in space and time (dynamical idea), [so ist] but can belong also to the intelligible world, which as substratum lies at the ground of the sensible, then a first, hence totality in the same world is possible as *noumenon* and on the other hand infinity without totality as *phaenomenon* is possible at the same time.",[]],"reflm:5969":["The infinity of the regress and the totality [mithin die absolute] cannot be united. The bounding in the regress of appearances cannot be united with space and time, i.e. sensibility.",[]],"reflm:5970":["115. E II 1397.\nOn M 115':\n*In mundo non datur abyssus, saltus, casus, fatum* (in the world there is not given abyss, leap, chance, fate). For the totality of the connection of the conditioned with the condition, i.e. the absolute whole of the series of connection, is either that of the mathematical: of the composition of the appearance, or of the dynamical connection: of the derivation of existence. Both 1. of the way in which the series (g total) is given; 2. how an entirely given series is resolved; mathematical: of *composition* or *decomposition*; dynamical: the first arising or unconditioned existence in general.\n*non datur abyssus*: namely the totality through* an infinite, filled space and time as given, or through a finite series, which has been given absolutely-whole in one infinite empty space and time.\n*(g the world is given neither as infinite nor as finite in an infinite space and the time.)\n*non datur saltus*. The leap in metaphysical sense is the transition from a *quantum* in the appearance to the simple =0, as a part as well in the concept of appearance and its possibility as in the arising or passing away of the same, i.e. of alteration. Everything in the appearance is a *quantum*, as well insofar as it contains time or space (*extensive tale*), or fills them, i.e. is contained in time or space (*intensive tale*: reality in sensation).\n*non datur casus*. Occurrence without determining cause (in the world). The totality through determining causes in the series of appearances and in general in time is impossible. The arising of itself (*casus*) is also impossible; but the action of precisely the same acting *subiecte* as things in themselves, insofar as they determine the appearances, but (in that they themselves do not belong in the series of the *phaenomena*) are not determined through them, is freedom. Thus |\nM 115: |\nconnection of the occurrences according to laws of nature is possible through freedom, e.g. actions from intellectual principles, not sensible impulses.\n*Non datur fatum*, i.e. absolute necessity in the appearance and the arising of the same, but indeed of the intellectual cause, which is no part of the world of sense, also no substratum.\n(g *Saltus** is the immediate connection of the *oppositorum* in the same subject one after another. All appearances are *quanta*, and so also all parts of the same; for were something existing simple, it could only be generated or pass away *per saltum*. But the possibility of generating in time makes the possibility of the appearances.)\n*(g is an immediate *progressus* from some determination to its opposite, therefore from mere negation to a *quantum* or vice versa.)",[]],"reflm:5971":["The immediate connection of the opposed in the same |\nsubject is not possible, except insofar as I think the 0 as the infinitely small of the quality of A. e.g. The arising of pain from indifference is only possible if this is regarded as an infinitely small pain and thus increased.",[]],"reflm:5972":["Freedom is the causality without external condition. In the *mundo phaenomeno* it does not take place. But it is possible that natural necessity be in this, and in the *noumenon* freedom. Just so absolute necessity in existence, not in *phaenomena*, which exist in space and time in a certain position, which is always contingent.",[]],"reflm:5973":["116. E II 1394.\nM 116':\n*Non datur saltus.* Every difference (in the appearance) is a *quantum*. The *quantum*, however, must be possible through *repetitam positionem eiusdem*; thus 0 must be regarded as homogeneous with A, but only as vanishing or infinitely small; thus there is no *progressus* in the determination of a thing to another state except through increase of the same quality from the infinitely small on.\nAll appearance is *quantum*, and indeed *continuum*. Thus also the appearance of opposed determinations of the same thing, i.e. the alteration, is a *continuum*.\n*Non datur casus.* No occurrence happens of itself, but is always determined through natural causes. In this way, however, no absolute totality is to be expected. But if I say: a being is *a priori* and thus of itself cause of an occurrence according to the order of nature, i.e. the way in which [it] it combines such with other occurrences happens according to an order of nature, then it is freedom in one relation, namely to the subject as thing in itself, and natural necessity in another relation, as to a subject as a part in the series of appearances. — *Non datur hiatus.*\n*Non datur fatum.* All necessity is natural necessity of occurrences, i.e. always determined through other grounds in the same series. In this way, however, there is no totality in the series. But if |\nI relate the series entirely to something outside the same*, [whose] that is no *phaenomenon*, then absolute necessity of the entire series can be, but not in it, [but] in that I go from the parts to the whole, but rather in that everything in the series is thought as depending on that which [is given] outside it.\n(s *Lex continui.*)\n*(s The connection of the conditioned with the condition is always series; in the dynamical field, however, totality is possible through something outside the series, i.e. what is not *phaenomenon*.\nM 116: |\n*Non datur hiatus.* There is no empty space or empty time in the world; for both is no object of possible experience. To conclude, through an inference from the difference of specific gravity, indeed also of the figure of a cavity, the possibility of the enclosed empty space, nevertheless presupposes that it is by itself an object of perception. But if it cut the world through into two parts, then a *saltus* from the one to the other would nevertheless be necessary in order to connect them, and which could be inferred from nothing.)\n===NOTES===\n- Erscheinung = appearance; Begebenheit = occurrence; Bestimmung/bestimmen = determination/determine; Verbindet = combines; Verknüpfung = connection; Gegenstand/obiect = object (here the German is Latinate *obiect* in 'obiect der Warnehmung'; Gegenstand in 'kein Gegenstand möglicher Erfahrung').\n- Veränderung = alteration (not in glossary); Reihe = series; Theil = part; Gantze = whole; Warnehmung = perception.\n- '[sie] es': the bracketed word is carried over as '[it]' followed by the unbracketed 'it'; the German sentence is left as it stands, with its awkward clause order ('happens according to an order of nature' follows the 'i.e.' clause).\n- 'einer Beziehung … zu dem Subiect' kept as 'relation … to the subject'; 'Dinge an sich selbst' = 'thing in itself' (singular as in the German).\n- 'ausser derselben*': asterisk marks the footnote that begins '*(s Die Verknüpfung…'; 'er' in 'Wenn er aber die Welt…' and 'der' in 'und der aus Nichts…' left as 'it' and 'which' without resolution.\n- 'Schlus' = inference; 'folgern' = conclude (glossary); 'specifischen Schweere' rendered 'specific gravity'.",[]],"reflm:5974":["It seems that no absolute totality is to be expected, and it also is not except in the appearance.\n===NOTES===\n- 'sie ists auch nicht als in der Erscheinung': the subject of 'ists' and the elided verb are left as ambiguous as in the German (it can be read as 'it is not to be expected' or 'it is not [there]' except in the appearance).\n- Erscheinung = appearance; totalitaet = totality (Allheit is a different German word).",[]],"reflm:5975":["*Non datur casus.* In the world everything happens according to the mechanism of nature, namely as sequel from that which itself happens, insofar as the world is a *phaenomenon*; except insofar as in the subject the cause of this mechanism itself is, i.e. insofar as it can be considered as *noumenon*, that [not] determines itself of itself, independently of *phaenomenis*, i.e. a reason as principle [of one] of spontaneity. There everything indeed also happens according to the mechanism of nature in the world of sense; this combination itself, however, is grounded on the [subject] ground of appearances in general.\nThe necessity of occurrences in nature is not the necessity of things themselves, [these as] i.e. of the existence of nature. |\nThis, if it is ascribed to the appearances, is *fatum*. Consequently in the existence of nature itself there is no inner necessity, [hence] because it is no absolute whole, hence entirely outside it.\nThe faculty of representing things in themselves and through this representation being cause of the appearances is a faculty [to act] according to the mechanism of nature and yet to act of itself, i.e. to effect occurrences without itself being dependent on and determined by an occurrence, i.e. freedom.\nThe faculty of giving oneself laws originally is freedom. (g The faculty of determining, *independenter* of the mechanism of nature, this mechanism itself, is freedom.)\nThe things of the world of sense can be considered in a twofold way: 1. as appearances, and there everything happens according to the mechanism in general, they however are the subjects in which something happens; 2. as subjects [whose repre-] which represent appearances to themselves; and there [themselves] they indeed represent to themselves what happens, but nothing happens in them, but rather in them is the ground of the representations, that something happen.\nIf among the things of the world we also encounter ones active according to reason, then these themselves are [not app-] insofar not appearances; for reason as cause is no object of appearance, also not determined thereby, consequently insofar free from the mechanism of nature; but yet, as concerns the appearance of its effects, efficacious according to the mechanism of nature.\n===NOTES===\n- Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Erscheinung = appearance; Begebenheit = occurrence; Folge = sequel; Verbindung = combination; Vermögen = faculty; Vorstellung = representation; Wirkung = effect; Grund = ground.\n- 'Vernunft' = reason throughout; 'Gründe' = grounds.\n- Bracketed fragments ('[nicht Er]', '[deren Vorste]') are broken-off words in the edition; rendered '[not app-]', '[whose repre-]' and not completed.\n- 'das [nicht] sich von selbst … bestimt': 'that' could refer to 'noumenon' or to the subject; left unresolved. The sentence 'd.i. eine Vernunft als princip [einer] der spontaneitaet' is left elliptical.\n- 'diese … ist fatum' / 'Diese': the antecedent ('necessity') is not supplied; 'ihr' in 'ausser ihr' left as 'it'.\n- 'gesetze' (lower case in the source) = laws; 'independenter' kept in Latin form.",[]],"reflm:5976":["The proposition: everything happens either according to the mechanism of nature or through blind chance, permits a third, namely: through freedom, namely a [suf-] sufficient ground in the world, but not as *phaenomenon*, but *noumenon*.\n===NOTES===\n- Zufall = chance (cf. *casus* in neighboring Reflexionen); zureichend = sufficient (glossary).\n- '[Zu]' is a broken-off word, rendered '[suf-]'.\n- 'Satz' = proposition; 'Freyheit' = freedom.",[]],"reflm:5977":["I cannot explain freedom: that it has in common with other fundamental|forces\nin common. But I also cannot prove it empirically; for it is a mere idea of something that does not belong in experience at all.\n===NOTES===\n- Correction of my rendering: the German reads 'das hat sie mit andern Grund|kräften gemein', i.e. 'that it has in common with other fundamental forces'; the sentence above should be read with the '|' (line break in the manuscript) falling inside 'Grund|kräfte' = 'fundamental|forces', and 'in common' a single occurrence.\n- erklären = explain (Erklärung = explanation; definition); beweisen = prove; Idee = idea; Erfahrung = experience.\n- 'Grundkräfte' = fundamental forces (Kraft = force).",[]],"reflm:5978":["The sensible is grounded in the intellectual, but not in the same way as it is grounded in other conditions of sensibility, thus not according to the mechanism of nature. I.e. Freedom from mechanism with [Ub]conformity of the same in one subject can subsist in different meaning.\nI call mechanism of nature where the causality (g of the cause) of an occurrence is itself again occurrence; and so it stands with everything that happens, insofar as the cause is appearance; insofar as the cause, however, is thing in itself, the causality is not itself occurrence, for it does not arise in time. The objective grounds of reason and of these faculties, [according to] to determine through them, remain, and herein nothing happens, and nothing alters.\n*in mundo non datur nec casus nec fatum.* For it is a connection of the sensible with the intelligible: either as substratum of the world of sense or as cause of the appearances [but] that is distinguished from the substratum.\nIn the first [understanding] case it is reason itself as being in the world, insofar as it can act from objective grounds; in the second a being that can be cognized only through reason, which contains the ground of the *substratorum* of the world of sense. In neither of the two cases does the cause make up a part of the series and is thus not subjected to the mechanism of nature, but determines the mechanism itself.\n===NOTES===\n- Caussalitaet = causality; Ursache = cause; Wesen = being; erkennen = cognize; Bedingung = condition; Theil = part; Reihe = series.\n- '[Ub]conformitaet': '[Ub]' is a broken-off beginning, left as in the source; the intended word is not supplied.\n- 'sensibelen' / 'intelligibelen' rendered 'sensible' / 'intelligible'; 'sinnlich' elsewhere would also be 'sensible'.\n- 'dieser Vermögen, [nach] durch sie zu bestimmen': the syntax is obscure and kept so ('of these faculties … to determine through them').\n- '[Verstande] Falle': '[understanding]' is a deleted word before 'case' (Fall).\n- 'Substrato' / 'substratorum' kept in Latin forms of the source.",[]],"reflm:5979":["The world is 1. according to space and time (g no absolute totality.) neither infinite nor finite (but a *progressus*, nothing further, because we can speak only of possible experience.) *non datur* |\n*abyssus.* (g *Mundus intelligibilis* is totality without relation to time and number and *progressus*.)\n2. According to division neither consisting of infinitely many nor of a finite number of parts (because we also speak only of possible experience in the *decomposition*). Hence no monads. But *mundus intelligibilis* consists [of] not of *substantiis phaenomenis*, thus of monads. These have in their external relation nothing of space, thus only inner forces, i.e. *vires repraesentativas*.\n*non datur saltus.* There is nothing simple in time and space, no simple alterations.\n3. In the world as world of sense everything is nature and therefore an infinite *regressus* in the series of causes without *causa simpliciter tali*; for nothing can begin absolutely and empty time, which determines nothing, precede.\nBut at the same time as intelligible world (because here I think not the connection of space and time, but of causes and effects, which are mere categories) freedom can be thought, namely, without being determined by *phaenomena*, to determine them. But freedom only where *Caussalitas intellectualis* is, i.e. in intelligences, which are causes through reason.\n4. In the world of sense everything contingent, because everything is *rationatum*.\n===NOTES===\n- Totalitaet = totality (Allheit is the glossary form); Theilung = division; Zahl = number; Einfaches = simple; Verknüpfung = connection; zufällig = contingent.\n- Veränderung = alteration; Reihe = series; Ursache = cause; Wirkung = effect; Sinnenwelt = world of sense.\n- 'Zeit' and 'Raum' are given in the order of the German ('Raum und der Zeit' in 1.; 'Zeit und Raum' under *non datur saltus*).\n- '[aus]' is a deleted word before 'nicht'; the sentence 'Aber mundus intelligibilis besteht [aus] nicht aus substantiis phaenomenis, also aus monaden' is left as it stands.\n- 'sie zu bestimmen' (3.): 'sie' = *phaenomena*, left as 'them'; the infinitive phrase is elliptical in the German and kept so.\n- 'categorien' = categories; 'Caussalitas' kept in the source's spelling.",[]],"reflm:5980":["The egoist or pluralist with regard to the unity or plurality of substances. The pluralist is either idealist or materialist or dualist.\n===NOTES===\n- Einheit = unity; Vielheit = plurality (not in glossary); 'substantzen' = substances.\n- 'Ansehung' rendered 'regard'; the passage is a fragment without a verb in its first sentence, kept so.",[]],"reflm:5981":["Properly one should not say: God has created the appearances, but: things that we do not know, [in accordance with] which, however, a sensibility in us has been arranged correspondingly. We can represent the things in themselves to ourselves only as thinking beings; for otherwise we have no determinations that would be distinguished from the appearance. Thus |\nhe has made the actions of thinking of these beings harmonious and interconnected outside itself through an arrangement of sensibility. *Harmonia praestabilita* (pre-established harmony). It is general and arrangement of nature. The bodies do not flow into spirits and conversely, but the former, as appearances of also spiritual beings, are only proportional to the latter's inner determinations. *Composita* consist of monads, but bodies do not; they are *phaenomena substantiata*. *Causae occasionales* would indicate two kinds of substances, of which one would be only a *phaenomenon substantiatum*, or continuous harmony of the appearances with things in themselves established. If I speak of appearances as substances [so must I] (while I nevertheless judge: they are only appearances), so I must think among them all a pre-established harmony [set] and yet can thereby think only the *nexum realem* among the *substantiis noumenis*.",[]],"reflm:5982":["In the *mundo phaenomeno* nothing but relations are given to us in outer objects.",[]],"reflm:5983":["The best is always the [greatest] object that is the greatest objective ground of the world; the most perfect is the [greatest] object of the greatest unity of cognition.",[]],"reflm:5984":["146'. E II 1080. 1197.\nHow is alteration possible (hence causality)? How is *commercium* possible? Among *phaenomena* well.\nM 146':\n(g The *commercium* of the soul with matter as *phaenomenon* cannot be thought at all; for that would have to be in space. But the soul is no object of outer intuition, thus it must think itself as well as matter as *noumenon*. Consequently also not its existence in time. But if it is to determine this [think], it must have something outside itself and in community with it, but not given in space, but whose intelligible representation it must (g as) determine in space, in order to determine its own existence in time.\nWe must determine something as in space outside us, in order to determine our own existence in time. That outside us is therefore, before this determination, represented as *noumenon*.)",[]],"reflm:5985":["The *commercium* of substances as *phaenomena* in space makes no difficulty — the other is transcendent.",[]],"reflm:5986":["*Nexus substantiarum* (connection of substances) is either *realis* or *idealis*; the former is *commercium*, the latter *harmonia absqve commercio*.",[]],"reflm:5987":["That occasionalism and pre-establishism be an idealism.",[]],"reflm:5988":["Those are not three systems to explain the *commercium*, but the harmony of the *substantiarum* either *per commercium* or *absqve commercio*. The former is the *influxus physicus*. In the world of sense, by virtue of space a condition of the *commercium* is already present, and the outer causality (of influence) is not harder to comprehend than the inner causality of the *actionum immanentium*. Causality cannot be comprehended at all. But if we assume substances as *noumena* (without |\nspace and time), they are all isolated; consequently instead of space a third substance must be thought, in which they can all stand with one another in *commercio* *per influxum physicum*.",[]],"reflm:5989":["The existence of things in appearance is determined according to general laws. The sum total of all appearances is called nature in general.",[]],"reflm:5990":["World already signifies as much as nature taken *substantive*. Without an inner principle of its alterations [would] according to laws it would not be a world as a being different from God.",[]],"reflm:5991":["Over, beside and \nThe all of nature logically [collective] is to be distinguished from the whole of natural things, i.e., the totality of composition; the former is thought in distinction from the intellectual, the latter in distinction from a (g mere) part of the sensible.\nThe question is: whether God has wanted to make an arrangement of nature or an arrangement of art.",[]],"reflm:5992":["Over and \nThe all of nature is not the whole. The first concept is negative, namely: each one without exception; the second positive. and the concept of a *quanti* as *completi*.",[]],"reflm:5993":["All (g philosophical) derivation of that which is given in our cognition or can be given, is either physical or metaphysical or hyperphysical. The first from empirical principles of the nature cognized through experience; the second from the [faculty] prin|ciples\nof the possibility of our cognition *a priori* in general, independent of the (g empirically cognized) nature of things; the third from [de] the representation of objects beyond nature. The last way brings our cognition entirely outside the conditions of the use [the] our reason *in concreto*. The metaphysical way of explanation is objective, if it rests on the general conditions under which alone we cognize objects as such that can be given to us. It does not exclude the supernatural, but merely limits our reason to the natural.",[]],"reflm:5994":["The order of nature, i.e., the form of it according to rules, not merely according to the occurrences but according to the original predispositions, is according to its origin either automatic or organic. The former is grounded on the inner constitution of the individual, from which the interconnection in general arises; the second is grounded on an idea that makes use of the individual as an instrument for an arrangement that would not have arisen from the individual natural things according to general laws. The first order occurs through inner forces by means of physical influence; the second through a force different from nature according to a pre-determined harmony. The order of natural things according to pre-determined harmony is no order of original nature, but of supernatural art. E.g., figure of the heavenly bodies, proportion of their magnitudes and distances, plants. Even if we grant an origin to nature, and indeed from a being in which the idea contains the ground of causality, but also at the same time of the essence of things, the order is nonetheless physical and not arbitrary.",[]],"reflm:5995":["If I think to myself in nature, besides the material (not merely formal) mechanism, also life, [so] i.e., an activity of natural things according to laws of the faculty of desire, then the concept\n|\nof needs and of an organism arises, be it now grounded automatically or on pre-determined harmony. Since the things outside every living being do not act for themselves, but must necessarily originally be arranged to the need of the preservation of living natures, so the order of nature here can [it not suitably] hardly be grounded on anything other than on the idea of an author [s], hence be organism.\nThe causality of these living beings, i.e., the determination of their faculty of desire, is either autonomy or heteronomy; in the last case grounded through instinct as *organon* of the ends in the idea of another, in the second through freedom in the own idea of the beings. In the former case there is always only a formal mechanism of nature according to physical laws, in the second a spontaneity according to practical ones, and their nature is not (g merely) organic [but] and physical, but also morally determinable. Insofar these beings do not merely direct themselves according to their natural needs, i.e., an alien and impressed idea, but according to their own, which can arise *a priori* from themselves, and [it] their causality is freedom.\nThus all causality is either [mere] material mechanism or instinct or freedom.",[]],"reflm:5996":["Order of nature is for experience, it is also necessary for the acknowledgment of the world as a particular substance.",[]],"reflm:5997":["Neither through a miracle nor through a spiritual being in the world can a motion be brought forth, without effecting just as much motion in the opposite direction, consequently according to laws of effect and counter-effect of matter. For otherwise a motion of the *universi* in empty space would arise.\nBut also no alteration in the world (thus no beginning of that motion) can arise, without being determined by causes in the world according to laws of nature in general, thus not through freedom or |\noccasional miracle. For since time does not determine the order of occurrences, but conversely the occurrences, i.e., the appearances, according to the law of nature (of causality) determine time, an occurrence that happened or were determined independently thereof in time would [the] presuppose a change of empty time, consequently the world itself would be determined in absolute time as to its state.\nMotions can therefore not begin of themselves, nor through anything that was not itself [moved] previously moved; and freedom is not to be encountered in the phenomena, nor any miracles in them that would be occasional, but at most *miracula praestabilita* in a world that itself had no beginning.",[]],"reflm:5998":["The being that represents to itself alone time and itself in time cannot cognize itself as persisting; but only that being can that is outside it.",[]],"reflm:5999":["Immortality rests on this, that the soul in itself be a substance, an intelligence and a person.\nIf immortality is to be proven, then the spiritual and not the animal must be proven from transcendental and not psychological grounds.",[]],"reflm:6000":["Practical freedom is the consciousness of absolute self-activity,\nfreedom the faculty of the causality of pure reason.\nWhether the soul is a particular substance?",[]],"reflm:6001":["The soul is in the transcendental apperception *substantia Nou|menon*;\nhence no persistence of the same in time; and this can only be in objects in space.",[]],"reflm:6002":["The first substances, which lie at the ground of matter, must also be simple, but give no other than composite appearances. So perhaps also the soul.",[]],"reflm:6003":["The difficulty about the *commercium* rests on the concept of immateriality, not on the influence of thinking on motions.",[]],"reflm:6004":["If the question is whether the intelligible *substratum* of thinking is not one and the same as that of bodies, if these are regarded as *monadatum*: then the immateriality of the soul is not proved.",[]],"reflm:6005":["One cannot say that soul and body are not *heterogenea*, because I cannot compare them through one sense, namely the soul not through the outer, body not through the inner.",[]],"reflm:6006":["Leibnitz presumably aimed his *harmonia praestabilita* at this, that 1. all alterations in the corporeal world must happen according to laws of mechanism, because otherwise, if a spirit moved them, the *centrum gravitatis universi* would move itself and, if this spirit nevertheless brought forth the motions according to the law of *action* and *reaction*, an alteration in the world would nevertheless not happen according to the law of causality in the corporeal world; and in the spirit itself something would begin as it were in empty time, because time can be determined only through the relation to things in space.",[]],"reflm:6007":["We have no need to prove the actuality of freedom; for that lies, as psychological, in the moral law.\nNor the possibility, [for] but only to show that there is no contradiction in it.",[]],"reflm:6008":["*Quaestio*. 1. On the hope of future life, 2. on immortality; on the latter, on the perdurability of the substance as well as of the personality.",[]],"reflm:6009":["The hope of future life can be grounded on resurrection without soul-existence.",[]],"reflm:6010":["On the *communi sensorio* (common sensorium).",[]],"reflm:6011":["*argumentum* (argument) either empirical or rational or according to laws or the analogy of the laws of nature, 2. either metaphysical or moral. The proposition is transcendent.",[]],"reflm:6012":["Immortality is the necessary continued duration of personality.",[]],"reflm:6013":["It is necessary to prove that the future life is general and also everlasting with the identity of the person.\nImmortality (g impossibility of the end of life, of the animal, to be sure, but not the spiritual) of the soul is continued duration of the person.",[]],"reflm:6014":["(s *perduratio* of the substance, of the personality. Separation of the soul from the body.)\nFuture life either animal or spiritual. The former either with the body of this life* (or little body) or with another body** (*metempsychosis*). If it is spiritual: whether in this life we could already become conscious of this *commercium*.\n*(s resurrection)\n**(g *Palingenesis* (s either of resurrection or [*in reno substitutionis*] of *metamorphosis*; *metamorphosis* either of evolution or of migration.))\nState, either of the dreaming soul, or of the awakening one. Lethean cup.\nPlace in heaven or in hell.\nProgress either (g continuous) to infinity, or [*per*] natural or [*in inf*] through a leap.\n(s Great objection against immortality from the *epigenesis* of the soul. Moral teleology.)\n(s The separating of the soul from the body.)\n*Libertas* of any intelligence whatever: causality through reason or practical [p] faculty of pure reason.\nCannot be proved *a posteriori* nor comprehended *a priori*.\nIndependence from the causes of the phenomenal world determining the will.\n(s Freedom is the independence of causality from all determining causes in nature (the world of sense).)",[]],"reflm:6015":["Over and \nThe separating of the soul from the body is the ceasing of sensibility |\nand the beginning of the spiritual and intellectual life. The other world is the present one, intuited either through other senses or spiritually.",[]],"reflm:6016":["*theologia* is either rational or historical,\ndoctrine of reason or doctrine of faith. This presupposes that; the former breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6017":["*Theologia Naturalis*.\n1. The concept of the *ens [necessarii] originarium* as the being of all beings (quantity); that there are not two kinds of *entia originaria*: God and matter.\n2. Of the highest of reality — that he be not merely ground of all reality, but content.\n3. Of the highest of causality — cosmological proof — not Spinozism, inherence.\n4. Absolute necessity.\nThe latter, to prove not merely *per hypothesin*, but to have insight into synthetically, transcendental theology must do from the concept of an *ens originarium* [either] as content of all possibility (but then Spinozism becomes of it).\na. The existence of no thing can be derived from concepts [proved]; for it is a synthetic proposition. (ontological proof.)\nb. The absolute necessity from no effect, such as from the contingent existence of something; and even if it must be assumed in some thing, it gives no properties to cognize, [because from no] from which the absolute necessity would follow; thus it gives only a deistic proof.\nc. Not from the constitution of the actual world. For to that it would be required that we survey all possible worlds.\nd. All not physical-theoretical, not teleological, hence merely as practical presupposition, and also determination of the concept of the same for the latter purpose. (s *Immutabilitas* (immutability), *libertas* (freedom), *praescientia* (foreknowledge), *determinismus*, *voluntas antecedens et consequens* (antecedent and consequent will).)",[]],"reflm:6018":["*Subsistentia* (subsistence) different from [causality] the independence of the *ens a se*. Spinoza.\n*Omnipraesentia* (omnipresence). Not as *anima mundi*, not local.\n*Aeternitas* (eternity).",[]],"reflm:6019":["(g Logical possibility lies in concepts, the real in God.)\n*Physicotheologia*. 1. *Ens originarium* as cause of the world is a free cause (g because otherwise things caused would be in it): intelligent being. According to analogy: understanding (*intellectus*). Beatitude. Will.\nPrinciple of possibility, i.e. of essences, as the concept of the *ens realissimum* is such for us.\n*Scientia simplicis intelligentiae, libera, media* (knowledge of simple intelligence, free, middle).\nCreation of the world, not emanation, nor as an architect. Subtle atheism. In time conservation. Moral *concursus* toward human freedom. (s End of creation.)\nProvidence through nature, immediate direction.\nDecrees. Predestination. Absolutism with respect to freedom.\n(s The system of cosmological apotheosis, where God is the world according to Spinozism, or the world God: pantheism, or God the soul of the world: zootheism.)",[]],"reflm:6020":["The ground why we cannot think to ourselves any absolute necessity of a |\nthing is that we can represent nothing to ourselves without thinking, can cognize nothing through mere intuition. Hence we make a distinction between possibility and actuality. Since the latter is supposed to be something that is posited still beyond the thought in itself. If we could cognize through intuition, we would find no distinction between possibility and actuality (if the former is not thought through concepts). Only what is actual is possible.",[]],"reflm:6021":["The immutability of God must be inferred only from this, that his concept is a *conceptus Noumeni* and his existence cannot be represented as in time, consequently alteration is not a predicate of the same; for otherwise one cannot at all infer from alteration to the contingency of the thing and of its alteration.",[]],"reflm:6022":["God 1. as original being, 2. as author, 3. as supreme head of the *mundus intelligibilis*.\n*causa* (cause) —\n*conditor* (founder) —\n*summus imperans, monarcha, legislator* (supreme commander, monarch, legislator).\nWorld in general, world of sense — intelligible world.",[]],"reflm:6023":["*ens originarium* in the logical understanding is: that whose concept is not derived from the concept of any thing, which therefore must be thoroughly determined a priori (from concepts). It is *omnitudo realitatis*. [Log] Its existence is *originaria*, if it is necessary in itself, not as presupposition.",[]],"reflm:6024":["We have a concept of impossibility only through contradiction.\nOnly the possibility in appearances is thought before actuality, because sensibility precedes the object as condition; logically the concept precedes the predicate of the thing. The concept of existence is *subaltern*.",[]],"reflm:6025":["In the logical use of the understanding the concept precedes the thing, in the transcendental the thing precedes the concept.",[]],"reflm:6026":["The world is either *ens a se* or *causatum*. The cause of the world is God.",[]],"reflm:6027":["332'. E II 1590.\nM 332:\nThe ontological proof would have to be called thus: A highest perfection must exist in relation to possibility in general, but not: a being which we think to ourselves as highest perfect must therefore exist. For this latter does not follow.\nM 332':\nThe proposition that a being (under whatever [I his] concept I may think it) exists is a synthetic proposition and cannot be proved *per analysin*.",[]],"reflm:6028":["Originally we can think to ourselves no possibility other than merely the form of the same.\nIn content the real thing must be given to us.",[]],"reflm:6029":["Whether the highest perfection alone makes up the concept of an original being.",[]],"reflm:6030":["Transcendental theology has merely negative utility.",[]],"reflm:6031":["Concept of God an idea. *Unicitas* (uniqueness) — ideal. Yet only regulative.",[]],"reflm:6032":["Whether the ground of the possibilities, *essentiarum* (of essences)?",[]],"reflm:6033":["Something exists etc etc. *derivative* (derivatively); thus something must exist *originarie* (originarily), this must be necessary.",[]],"reflm:6034":["If the *ens originarium* is assumed as dynamically *realissimum* (all-sufficient as ground), i.e. that it does not contain all reality as determination in itself, for which [it] it is the sufficient ground (e.g. not precisely understanding, although the original ground of all understanding beings: Hume, or those who assume a world-creator proceeded from the original being. Tibet.): then this is entirely different from the concept in which it |\nis assumed as mathematically *realissimum* and all reality as homogeneous in one single.\n(g What matters is whether certain realities descend from others of a different kind or can only be *sui generis*, i.e. can only rest on homogeneous causes. Understanding on the highest understanding.)\nThe derivation of the possibilities of things through limitation of the *Realissimum* seems to contradict itself; for reality cannot be limited in a necessary being. But the limitation of the sequel or effect to manifoldness is possible.\nThat all reality should be encountered in a *compositum* (composite) is impossible, because precisely the combination in one substance is the form of the highest being. Absolute necessity is also not in the *composito*, but in the *simplici*.",[]],"reflm:6035":["The *infinitudo* must not be regarded as mathematical, hence as *commensurabilis* with the reality of the creatures. It is *ens illimitatum*.",[]],"reflm:6036":["God is unchangeable, because he is not in time at all. He thus does not begin an action or cease with one. If something begins in the human being that is ascribed immediately to God, then the human being must be the cause of the changed relation. He becomes a better human being, and God becomes gracious. He is more diligent, and God stands by him. In God there is always the same principle and *actus*.\nThat God provides extraordinary assistance signifies only that outside the divine order, which we know, a far more extensive one, which we do not know, is to be encountered, although both according to one order of nature from a single decree and principle. If God were deflected by the events of the world even in his decree before the world, he would not have the unchangeability of an original being. From this one should conclude, no miracle is possible through a single original being. |\nHe who denies the possibility of miracles could say: there is indeed force for it, but no ground that would deflect its exercise according to a principle from which natural order arises; for this ground would have to lie in nature and yet make an alteration of nature necessary.",[]],"reflm:6037":["The beginning of the world cannot be thought otherwise than as an existence before which a time precedes in which the thing is not. An absolute beginning of the world can be thought neither in the world alone nor in dependence on God; for in him there would otherwise have had to be a state in which the causality of the world was not. In the course of the world God can be the cause of a change without changing himself. For the changed state of things brings forth changed relations to the divine activity (which is always the same). But before the existence of the world nothing could change that had worked toward arising, except God himself.",[]],"reflm:6038":["It is a necessary hypothesis of [unity] reason as a principle of the unity of all our cognitions, to assume a single universal original being as principle of everything, to assume this being as understanding, because only thereby, that through understanding it is the cause of everything, is the world ordered according to rules, whereby it becomes an object for our understanding, finally as a cause through rational choice, so that it may be a principle of a rational will for us and of the universal unity of all our free actions. Theism is thus not a dogmatic assertion, but a necessary hypothesis of the thoroughly harmonious use of reason, especially of its self-sufficiency.",[]],"reflm:6039":["Only a deity can cognize the world as one that is worthy of a deity; for this would have to [all mo] contain the highest possible perfection of a world in general, and to that belongs the cognition of all possible worlds.\nThus physicotheology does have the advantage that it thinks the concept of God through [pra] *realitates in concreto* (understanding, will), but it can give no determinate concept with regard to the degree and the number of the [Urs] originators.",[]],"reflm:6040":["From the existence of the world one cannot prove that a God is, but that he is an intelligence.\nThe highest understanding, the highest self-sufficiency, the highest will.",[]],"reflm:6041":["The divine understanding is called the highest and pure [unbe] understanding, which cognizes things simply as they are in themselves. It is not sensibly conditioned. It is no receptivity, but absolute spontaneity. It is *intellectus originarius*, not *derivativus*. Its cognitions are intuitions, not concepts, but not sensible intuitions, but ideas, which do not presuppose the things but make them possible. *intellectus archetypus*. Not thinking. Not reason by a detour. One thinks it with regard to the world *secundum analogiam*, but only insofar as its concept is to be a regulative principle; but *per eminentiam* without any analogy, when it is spoken of absolutely.\nM 352':\nGod cognizes all things *a priori*, consequently his understanding is a pure understanding. In this there must be subjective conditions of the possibility of things (but not of their appearances, for his cognition is not sensible), consequently subjective conditions of the possibility of things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:6042":["On the heading »Intellectus Dei«:\nAccording to psychological concepts *in concreto*.\nThe living God.",[]],"reflm:6043":["Understanding and will, because these are the only properties that have absolute spontaneity.\nNot divine reason.",[]],"reflm:6044":["On the heading »Intellectus Dei« and M § 863:\nThe highest intelligence. Originator of the world. *causa libera*. (g *theologia naturalis qvoad conceptum* breaks off?)\nPhysical teleology does indeed lead to an *ens intelligens*, but does not give the determinate concept of an *ens summum*.",[]],"reflm:6045":["Physicotheology requires the transcendental: 1. in order to supplement it, 2. in order to cleanse or purify it; analogy.\nThe necessity of an originator distinct from the world is concluded from the contingency of the order, not of the existence. Hume: doubt that the divine perfection is just as contingent.",[]],"reflm:6046":["A [moral theology] theological morals is not fitting, because there morals makes the existence and the will of God the *principium* (g of morality); but a moral theology is good, because morals is made the *principium* of the belief in a God.\nIn the first case morals is derived from the existence and will of God, in the second (g the belief in) the existence of God and his will from morals.",[]],"reflm:6047":["Physicotheol. Faculty of cognition. Feel. of pleas. and displ. Desire. faculty.\nthe only wise — blessed\n— holy\nin theoretical relation; as the highest good the order is reversed, and wisdom is derived from blessedness (goodness) and holiness.",[]],"reflm:6048":["It is hardly to be comprehended how another intuitive understanding than the divine one should take place. For that cognizes in itself as original ground (and *archetypus*) the possibility of all things; but finite beings cannot cognize other things from themselves, because they are not their originators, unless it be the mere appearances, which they can cognize *a priori**. Hence we can cognize things in themselves only in God.\n*(s One believes, however, that all cognition *a priori* is cognition of things in themselves; but it is precisely the opposite, but always only of things as appearances, i.e. as objects of experience.)",[]],"reflm:6049":["The divine cognition is not empirical, not pure sensible *a priori*. Nevertheless he cognizes our sensibility.",[]],"reflm:6050":["355. E II 236. 306.\nOn philosophical enthusiasm.*\nPlato noted correctly that through experience we do not know the things as they are in themselves, but only learn to connect their appearances [according to deter] in a lawlike way. (Further he saw that to cognize the things according to what they are in themselves [an intuition a cognition require which not] would also require an intuition of the things in themselves, i.e. pure intellectual intuition, of which we are not capable.) He noted that, in order for our representation to agree with the object, it must be thought either as produced by the object or (g as) producing the object. The latter would be the original representation (*idea archetypa*), of which we humans, if it is to be original in all respects, are not capable. Thus the ideas can be encountered only in the original being. [Now he believed all] The ideas, however, of this original understanding cannot be concepts, but only intuitions, though intellectual ones. Now he believed that all cognitions *a priori* are cognitions of the things in themselves**, and since we partake of the former, so we also partake of the latter, and among these he reckoned mathematics. From ourselves, however, we cannot come to partake of them, consequently only through communication of the divine ideas. [Now] Since, however, we are conscious of them not as merely historically imparted and transmitted, but as immediately seen into: so they must be not implanted |\nconcepts, which are believed, but immediate intuitions, which we have of the archetypes in the divine understanding. [Thus they are Intu] We can, however, develop these only with effort. Thus they are mere recollections of the old ideas from the community with God. Now this would not yet be enthusiasm, but a mere way of explanation of the possibility of the cognitions *a priori*. But now there comes a conjecture to partake, even now, of this community with God and of the immediate intuition of these ideas (mystical intuition), indeed also to find therein the [Ob] immediate object of all his inclinations, which only from misunderstanding have hitherto turned to appearances as the types of them (mystical love of God). But since [geni] it is probable that between us and God there is a great [Rei] ladder of creatures, which extends from us up to him (g genii), astral spirits, aeons, one could first attain to community with these and to the prelude of intellectual original intuitions. Since, however, the original ideas are the cause of the reality of their objects, one could thereby still hope to exert a superior power over nature. and so the Neoplatonic school, which called itself eclectic, in that it pretended to find its wisdom in all the ancients, because it [such] read its reveries into them, was done with all the raving enthusiasm with which it has plagued the world. (g finally Spinozism. (Theosophy through intuiting.)) The Aristotelian philosophy drove out this delusion. One began from concepts, which we arrive at on the occasion of experience (*nihil est in intellectu* —). Now, however, |\nM 355: |\none arrived at cognitions *a priori*, without investigating how this is possible according to the above principle. These expanded, and, because everything that remains within the world of sense is always conditioned, reason drove the principles valid there higher up and beyond the world of sense, in the confidence that they would [always] give just as secure an acquisition as they had until then given explanation of what is at hand. Now the subjective conditions |\nof reason with regard to comprehensibility began to be held for objective conditions of the things in themselves, and, since the former is not satisfied until it has grasped the whole, conquests began to be made in the supersensible world. Because now no boundaries are to be indicated where one could stop here, one had finally, since all things had been deprived of their individual and separated possibility to exist, to take from them also separated existence and leave them only inherence in a subject. Spinozism is the true conclusion of dogmatizing metaphysics. Critique of propositions accomplishes nothing here; for the difference of the subjective from the objective with regard to their validity [richt] cannot be remarked there, because those subjective ones which are at the same time objective are not distinguished beforehand. The necessity of assuming them is once there, and one does not notice that it is merely subjective. That experience is possible merely through principles *a priori* occurs to no one. Only critique of reason itself can accomplish something here. Meanwhile men of bright reason, surveying the great domain of the use and misuse of opinions, hold up this decline into enthusiasm for a while yet. — If one does not want to take the path of critique, one must let enthusiasm go its way and laugh about it with Shaftesbury.\n*(g The great difference of intellectual from empirical cognition misled the ancients to this.)\n(g Sect in China: I am the highest being.\nSect in Tibet: God is the assembly of all saints.)\n**(g And it is just the opposite. For only of things as appearances can there be synthetic cognitions *a priori*. For the form of sensibility with regard to intuition can be cognized before all objects, for it is given in the subject. Space and time. In this intuition, however, many propositions can be given synthetically *a priori*, which then hold of all objects of possible experience, but also of none beyond, although the concepts of them as objects in general are merely intellectual, but are only monograms, which give nothing to cognize *in concreto*, but only cognition *in abstracto*.)\n(g Analytic cognitions one has, to be sure, *a priori*, if the concept of the object is given, be it an empirical or a rational one. But synthetic judgments *a priori* would not be possible without pure intellectual intuitions, which are encountered only in God. What humans are to cognize synthetically and indeed *a priori* must have an object of sensible intuition as object.)",[]],"reflm:6051":["354. E II 237. 238.\n M 354':\n* The origin of all philosophical enthusiasm lies in Plato's original divine intuitions of all possible objects, i.e. the ideas, since we intuit only them through their appearances, thus only passively. Now there is grounded on this first [the conjecture] Plato's opinion that all our cognition *a priori* [chiefly] (mathematics), chiefly that of perfections, descends from the recollection of these former intuitions and that we now (g only) must seek ever more to develop these; from this, however, arises the second step of mysticism, to intuit everything even now in God, which then makes all inquiry into synthetic cognition *a priori* unnecessary, in that we read them in God, third, since other beings may be nearer to God, we, so to speak, must perhaps first get to know those |\nideas through reflection, consequently associate with spiritual natures etc.\n*(s Already before Plato one distinguished the intellectual cognitions from the [sen] empirical [which one] and understood these when one called them sensible, and even made a distinction between intelligible and sensible things. One held all cognition *a priori* to be [sensi] intellectual, thus also mathematics; and since various |\nM 354: |\nsensitive and really only this can be cognized *a priori*, one had examples of a supposed intellectual cognition. But to find this distinction important, a need of reason was necessary to go beyond the empirical, because this is always conditioned and therefore cannot be thing in itself, which must at all times have its complete conditions.\nThe necessity of the hypothesis of such a something one held for insight into the necessity of these things.)\nM 354':\nThe highest degree of enthusiasm is that we ourselves are in God and [us] in him feel or intuit our existence. The second: that we intuit all things according to their true nature only in God as their cause and in his ideas as archetypes. The third: that we do not intuit them at all, but yet derive them only from the concept of the same and thus from our existence and our concepts of reason of things infer straight to the existence of God, in whom alone they can have objective reality. Now back from the lowest degree to the highest: Spinoza.",[]],"reflm:6052":["The cause of enthusiasm is the lack of the critique of reason itself; for, if I arrive from my own forces at the descent of all things from One and also at the qualities of this One, how do I come to that? 1. Through dissection of my concepts? Then the concept would have to be originally inspired in me. 2. Through the synthesis of the laws of experience? But there I remain in the world. 3. Through |\nprinciples that I take from neither of the two? These must then be secret intuition of the supersensible.",[]],"reflm:6053":["The superstitious religion is grounded on a principle of the subjection of reason under the delusion of perceptions.\nIn fanaticism the human being raises himself above humanity.",[]],"reflm:6054":["Delusion is the persuasion of the dispensability of the examination of one's judgments according to universal laws of nature.",[]],"reflm:6055":["On Plato's ideas as self-subsisting archetypes. The idea of humanity as the ground of the existence of the world. We must make our judgment conformable to these ideas.\n*Theosophia* and *Theurgia*. *Theologia Mystica*.",[]],"reflm:6056":["Anthropomorphism thought regulatively (g is the relation of an unknown cause [on the] according to laws of sensibility) is nothing but [the sche] the conditions of sensibility applied to divine actions as a schema of the application of the same in the use in experience. Anthropomorphism constitutive is the absolute representation of the divine attributes according to laws of sensibility. The latter gives opponents weak points; without the former we are deists.",[]],"reflm:6057":["God has given the human being not independence from him (God) himself, but from the incentives of sensibility, i.e., [moral] practical freedom. The actions of the same are appearances and insofar under merely inner conditions of humanity. To the same be|long\nalso punishments and rewards. What God did is all good, but does not lie in the world of sense as a mere schema of the intelligible. Thus space is nothing in itself and no thing as divine work, but lies in us and can also only take place in us. Likewise the agreeable and its distinction from the good. The appearances are properly not creatures, hence also not the human being; rather he is merely the appearance of a divine creature. His state of acting and suffering is appearance and rests on him like the bodies on space. The human being is *principium originarium* (original principle) of appearances.",[]],"reflm:6058":["Because the understanding is originally the faculty of considering itself in relation to objects, the causality of an original being (*entis originarii*), or what determines it, can consist in it alone.",[]],"reflm:6059":["The question is: what determines a self-sufficient being to causality outside it. It is content in itself.",[]],"reflm:6060":["The highest understanding of God, his highest self-sufficiency, this as all-sufficiency combined with the cognition of all other possible things outside him, the causality with regard to the greatest whole of the same, consequently the (g *summum*) *bonum originarium* (original highest good), the origin of the *summi boni derivativi* (derivative highest good).",[]],"reflm:6061":["The will is nothing other than the causality through the understanding. This is seen into by means of self-contentment in his all-sufficiency |\n. — Whether a physicotheology without a cause, without understanding and will, is possible?",[]],"reflm:6062":["*Intellectus* (understanding) — *actiones immauentes*\n*Voluntas* (will) —\n*transeuntes*.",[]],"reflm:6063":["The highest being is conscious of its all-sufficiency through understanding and has in this (in itself) the highest satisfaction. Therein consists now the causality of the same.",[]],"reflm:6064":["Under and \nThe consciousness of his existence as a (g in itself) sufficient ground of everything else is self-sufficiency.\nThe causality of representations with regard to the existence of objects is desire, the relation of the [objects] representations to the subject [and its], the force of the same or causality to determine, is pleasure.",[]],"reflm:6065":["A will without incentives from the object. Satisfaction, not allurement or need.",[]],"reflm:6066":["Divine feeling. Self-sufficiency with regard to the feeling of pleasure in his existence is blessedness.",[]],"reflm:6067":["The understanding determines the will in him immediately, not through the mediation of a pleasure or displeasure; the will of God is no faculty dependent on the feeling of the object.",[]],"reflm:6068":["Feeling of pleasure and displeasure in God is self-contentment; how is a willing of objects outside him possible therewith?",[]],"reflm:6069":["We cannot here bring out any interest that God would take in the existence of things.",[]],"reflm:6070":["How does the actuality of things outside him interest God? What has he as motive cause to create them? He has indeed an aim, but no motive cause. Omnisufficiency.",[]],"reflm:6071":["The divine understanding is a pure understanding, not affected by sensibility, hence also the will a pure will.\nThe divine will desires nothing.\nThe pleasure in the existence of the object does not precede. The pleasure in his all-sufficiency.\nA will that without the mediation of a feeling of pleasure in the object is determined immediately through the understanding, is free.",[]],"reflm:6072":["A manifold cannot agree with ends (which are concepts), except either this manifold must be the cause of the understanding or of thinking and of the representation of the ends, i.e., it must be able to think, or [d] a thinking being must be the cause of the manifold and of its purposive combination.",[]],"reflm:6073":["Cause of the world through freedom, i.e., author. Not indeed on account of stimuli through intellectual appetition (*appetitionem intellectualem*), but by [merely] merely objective grounds (*rationes*), determined through the understanding, subjectively through the sole acquiescence in himself (*acqviescentiam in se ipso*).",[]],"reflm:6074":["Opposite and also perhaps \nThe causality of the divine understanding with regard to the objects, which are in the greatest connection with it and among one another, [is] is the will itself. And the consciousness of this all-sufficient will the highest pleasure, which is thus not immediately in the object, but in it |\nself: as in a moral action the pleasure is not in the object, but his own good will brings forth the deed.\nNot cause of possibility, also not limited by external impossibility.",[]],"reflm:6075":["External effects of God, as in themselves contingent things, we can ascribe only to freedom, inner actions (as the cognition of God) only to natural necessity. In the former the idea of the best must lie at the ground, in the second the evil as well as the good is the object.",[]],"reflm:6076":["Opposite and \nThe negative concept of freedom is independence,\nThe positive concept: autonomy through reason.",[]],"reflm:6077":["Opposite and \nTranscendental freedom (of substance in general) is absolute spontaneity to act (in distinction from *spontaneitas secundum quid* (spontaneity in a certain respect), where the subject is yet determined *aliunde* (from elsewhere) through *causas physice influentes* (physically influencing causes)). Practical freedom is the faculty to act from mere reason.\nThe causality with freedom is *originaria* (original), although the cause is *ens derivativum* (derivative being).",[]],"reflm:6078":["The [divine] freedom of the divine will does not consist in that he could also have chosen something other than the best; for not even human freedom consists in that, but in that [he] the idea of the best necessarily determined him, which is lacking to the human being and therefore also limits his freedom.",[]],"reflm:6079":["M § 898 conclusion:\nThis *fatalismus theologicus* would take place, if God had created the world in |\ntime; for one would have to posit for it a fate of the determination of his force.",[]],"reflm:6080":["Divine blessedness.\nDifficulty from the freedom of the human being under the divine decree (*decreto*).\nPhysicotheological proof from the ends of nature does not alone suffice, but is in practical aim sufficient for sound understanding.",[]],"reflm:6081":["The divine will is with regard to the end either manifest or concealed. Presumption, to lay our connection in the willing of the deity underneath. Inscrutable are his ways. Even in the general of nature.",[]],"reflm:6082":["The will in the use of the means with regard to a presupposed end is indeed often manifest, but with regard to the end itself concealed; e.g., why the human being is formed thus or so is manifest, but concealed, for what the human race exists at all.\nIf once a human being is to exist, then it is manifest that he must be upright, wise, etc. etc. But that a human being (g and why he) must exist is concealed. What always remains concealed is inscrutable.",[]],"reflm:6083":["We cannot think of an end of God in the proper psychological understanding, because this indicates dependence of contentment, hence only an *analogon* of the ends in the world. The divine end is a satisfaction without interest, in which, then, existence would be indifferent, which contradicts itself.",[]],"reflm:6084":["The perfection, order and purposiveness of the world makes the relation to an original will, consequently physicotheology, necessary, which for its part extends the knowledge of the world as a system of ends.\nBut the incomprehensibility of the divine will, if one separates all anthropomorphism [and], makes it that one can derive this perfection also from the being of God as the origin of all beings and [the itself] thus from the being of the things themselves, because the will is only the self-contentment of God with himself as a being of all beings.\nBut the inscrutability of this will limits our physicotheology in the determination of the divine ends to nature. It is soothing.",[]],"reflm:6085":["We have brought the psychological attributes of the divine will to an end. It is incomprehensible. The ontological ones are *tacite* understood therewith — Physicotheology is possible.",[]],"reflm:6086":["At the very top of the page:\nOn moral theology.\nWhether the concept of God is a merely speculative or moral concept? How it arises from morals as a cognition of reason *a priori* (not for the benefit of experiences), and how it makes the concept of God also ontologically determined, which physicotheology does not do.",[]],"reflm:6087":["The reader feels a certain shy [fear] apprehension in engaging with the considerations and objections of Hume, and sees in it the expression of a presumption. On the other hand, something noble, sincere and unfeigned also shines forth from it, namely to undertake judging without slavish anxiety like Job, not in order to condemn God's ways |\nbut to confess his scruples openly to himself [and], without letting himself be misled by apprehension that one will thereby become irreverent into suppressing them and into flattering eulogies, like Job's friends. The government of God is not despotic but paternal. It does not say: do not reason, but obey, but rather: reason diligently, so that you can prove, from your own conviction, voluntarily and unafraid, the veneration to God, which [nothing] would be of no value at all if it were extorted. With one who believes slavishly and for just that reason also moves others tyrannically to this belief, nothing is to be begun. Whoever has love of peace does not begin it with them on grounds of reason.",[]],"reflm:6088":["The legislator must be benevolent; otherwise his command makes no obligation to obedience (He must also be omnipotent); but his law must, if he is wise, be holy. As legislator God is therefore holy, as ruler benevolent, and, since this is possible only under the condition of agreement with the former, for the sake of wisdom, so he is just as judge.",[]],"reflm:6089":["Benevolence under the limiting condition of the worthiness of the *subditi* is justice. It therefore presupposes an *imperium*. God as ruler is benevolent, but under limiting conditions of his holiness. Legislating holiness is the perfection of the will, not merely of the legality of the actions, but also of the morality. The divine will is holy; it demands morality.",[]],"reflm:6090":["Benevolence, insofar as it is limited to conditions of conformity to law, is justice.\n(s Is the wisdom of God in the limitation of his benevolence |\nto the condition of the agreement of rational beings with his holy law.)",[]],"reflm:6091":["370. E I 680.\nTo cognize the wisdom and benevolence of God in this world *in concreto* and at the same time in the whole interconnection, we are not in a position, because we know rational beings as [objects] subjects of all ends only on the earth. The new astronomy has produced great benefit for theology in this. For if we had to regard our earth as the whole theater of divine wisdom, great scruples would arise.\nOn our earth the arrangement is made that we should bring forth all good, both in ourselves and in our external state, even the knowledge of the good and the pleasure and liking in it, out of ourselves. Then it was impossible that we should be steered by agreeableness; for then we would have had to be able to foresee the good and also have pleasure in it (we were, however, to bring ourselves to our vocation through culture). Thus activity had to be the course of our vocation, but the spur of activity pain. In this, however, it also could not happen that each individual [its] reached the vocation of its existence [fulfilled]; thus deficiencies always remained. Only the species was to reach it. Elsewhere it may be otherwise; also in the future; belief.\nThe disproportion between our natural predisposition and its development in each *individuo* gives ground for belief in the future. We need not first assume God as benevolent, but only conclude according to the analogy of nature even without God, and so there will here always be encountered a guidance toward highest wisdom and a theology that flows from the firm resolve of progress toward perfection.\nM 370:\nHuman beings were even to bring forth out of themselves a system of the public good, given a general inclination to relate everything to private |\ninclination, from which nothing but strife and violence arises. They were to need from their own species a master or at least a supreme dominion, and many apart from one another. They were to invent universal peace.\nThe role of a human being is perhaps among all in this planetary system the most artificial, the most burdensome, but also in the outcome the most glorious. Happiness we also have here, but to be sure according to our concept of happiness not *in abstracto* but *in concreto*; for we cannot think any up without, in the striving to work oneself through hindrances, in the labor of dangers, in a word: in the reward of our force, bringing it out of the evil.",[]],"reflm:6092":["We speak of happiness as contentment, but [we] as of an idea that we have *in abstracto*, whose reality we cannot, however, set forth through any determination *in concreto*, not even once in fiction. If it is to be a complete satisfaction of all desires: why then desire, i.e. lack, in order to acquire it? For this indeed signifies that one was not happy beforehand. (Besides, the satisfaction of the desire increases the longing for more and thereby makes contentment entirely impossible.) If, on the other hand, we are to be in possession of well-being without desiring anything, this state seems to make the being entirely useless, because no activity is in it; besides, persistence in the same state is impossible for a being that drags its existence through time, [only] and yet with complete contentment, because a part of its existence always lies ahead of it, in regard to which it expects something different from the previous state.\nReligion can only be moral insofar as we venerate God in a threefold person. [So] As legislator he cannot be benevolent, i.e. his law cannot be subordinated to the aim at the welfare of the creature or mixed with it. It is directed to freedom and not to happiness: that the creatures become worthy of happiness, not that they |\nbecome happy; otherwise we represent his law as indulgent, accommodated to our weaknesses and yielding, but not as holy. As benevolent [he has the That] and for no other ground does he will the existence of the creature; but as holy he wills, if they exist, that they must conduct themselves in order to become partakers of benevolence. As just he indeed presupposes benevolence, but with limitation through the holiness of the law; justice, however, is not benevolent, it is also not (g merely) holy, but benevolence in conformity with the holiness of the law is distributive justice. As benevolent he would not punish, as holy he will not reward (for everything is duty), for he demands the deed regardless of the happiness of the state. Thus justice is the third personality.\nIn human beings the three personalities are distributed among three *individuis*, in God it is one threefold personality. This, however, is only in the concept of God in practical, not speculative, aim: an idea of the relation to human morality and freedom.",[]],"reflm:6093":["That is the holy symbol of moral theology, the monogram of his mysterious being, but in order to prevent theosophy and theurgy.\nThe threefold function in the relation must presuppose in God a threefold original principle of his activity; but this we cannot have insight into.\nNumerical identity is the unity of the *individuum*: of that which in different relations has been considered as many.\nThe [specula] consideration of God in this threefold personality is not theoretical but moral. Theology as theosophy is presumption and enthusiasm.",[]],"reflm:6094":["Theology is not theosophy, to cognize God according to his nature in itself, but only in relation to us and the morality of our will. Likewise religion is not theurgy, in order to have an [immediate] influence on God and his will through formulas, through exertions of spirit, purifications, penances, but to direct the cognition of it to the improvement of ourselves. The question is |\nonly: what makes us better human beings? The moral concept of God and of the threefold personality relative to our practical maxims, not in regard to his own nature. Through a good conduct of life one attempts not to work on God, but only to make oneself receptive to the divine benevolence.",[]],"reflm:6095":["The judge is in himself indifferent, he does not pronounce his judgment out of love, is impartial; he cannot be entreated, he can only [pro]nounce according to laws. This becomes very confused if we assume benevolence and justice in one person. But also if we take holiness and justice in one. For then he does not speak from the standpoint of a human being. Who can stand against him? Job. Besides the laws he has benevolence in advance and is ready to bestow happiness.",[]],"reflm:6096":["Threefold personality, which I think in the highest good. 1. I desire to be happy from inclination and relate [my] this wish to an author of happiness. 2. I recognize myself as subject to the moral law through reason and relate this obligation to the will of a highest being, which can obligate me because it has my fate entirely in its power. 3. Reason in me concludes happiness only insofar as I act in conformity with my duty. And this I relate to a third person, whose place conscience takes. [Al] A benevolent person thus represents my inclination, the second my reason, the third my conscience.",[]],"reflm:6097":["Considered subjectively my own person is the first. I would gladly have my happiness limited by no conditions at all. But objectively I must represent my happiness through another person, and reason is the first person, which prescribes the con|ditions\nof happiness. Subjectively (g from inclination) I would gladly have the application of the moral law to me happen according to benevolence [angewa]; but reason teaches that it happens not merely according to the holy law (g nor according to benevolence alone either), but [from] benevolence combined with holiness, i.e., according to justice.",[]],"reflm:6098":["The question is: whether the concept of God is a concept necessary in a physiological or in a moral aim. Because (g then) nature must be regarded as contingent, the inner necessity cannot be concluded from it, but is only a necessary hypothesis (namely among the properties that are to make up the concept of a deity), in order to indicate [the] for the phenomena the highest [sufficient] sufficient ground for them (not for all other questions of reason). For the perfection and magnitude of the world do not require the presupposition of a [full highest] all-sufficient being. Thus as a physiological basic concept it is not determined and its existence is only hypothesis.\nIf morality were grounded on physiological conditions, precisely the same would follow. We would have to presuppose the existence of God only with regard to our need and aim at happiness. But all morals is necessary from the essence of things, and that without which it would have no objective reality is necessary a priori, but only in practical relation. Also with regard to it God must be represented first as the highest good, and thereupon, so that he can be it, also as the highest being.",[]],"reflm:6099":["M 372':\n(g If it were possible to know that a God is, would morality then still remain in the human being.)\nIn moral theology\nGod is represented as the head in a necessary system of ends, not of a contingent system, which we assume hypothetically as universal and believe, but which we know, |\nnot through choice among the contingent ones and a will that arbitrarily establishes the ends, but whose will is one and the same with that which in the order of ends among rational beings is absolutely necessary, consequently not as despot, but as the highest good itself through the nature of things in general. God as *summum bonum originarium* (original highest good). The moral properties are not his [subordination] superior dominion over the beings according to laws of morality, but the self-subsistence of these laws themselves. Here we lay at the ground what we know with certainty, namely the necessary system of all ends, to which all others must be subordinated as to their *conditio sine qua non* (indispensable condition). Hence the errors of moral theology are properly pernicious, above all anthropomorphism, which can however be more easily prevented by regarding God not as author of the laws but of the obligation according to these laws.\nM 373':\nThereby moral theology is at the same time ontotheology. It is nevertheless a merely practical and subjective certainty, which is grounded on an interest, but on an objectively necessary one inseparable from the essence of reason, an interest subordinated in no aim. If morals were only a pragmatic system of prudence, belief in God would be merely hypothesis; now it is a postulate.\nThe highest wisdom in physicotheology is merely relative, and concerns the sufficiency with regard to the system of all ends; but in moral theology it is self-subsistent, the source of the possibility of the things themselves. Thereby we are led to derive everything from the order of things as necessary in itself and yet at the same time from God as the self-subsistent principle of all order, not to turn to God as the being who gives laws that are contingent in themselves, and from which he can dispense, but to expect, what is necessary in the nature of things according to laws of morality, also according to nature in accordance with physiological laws.\nAll moral properties of God are here to be determined only objectively according to our concepts, not subjectively — for in the latter case we have two correlates, whose connection we could represent anthropomor|phistically\nto ourselves: the moral order and God, who wills it. But his will is this moral order itself. He is benevolent and indeed infinitely, because all possible good flows from him in itself, but also holy, because it flows only according to the moral order, and just, because this benevolence does not establish the moral order, but is determined according to it.",[]],"reflm:6100":["Justice is limitation of benevolence through holiness. It is thus properly not rewarding (s for if the reward were merely in conformity with justice, we could expect little), but only benevolence rewards. For where [holiness] justice does not limit benevolence according to principles of holiness, there benevolence is effective.\nProperly we ourselves limit divine benevolence through our guilt, and punishments may not be regarded as positive.",[]],"reflm:6101":["How evil is possible under a good God rests on the question how freedom is possible in beings that are nevertheless entirely and wholly dependent as to their existence, hence how morality in general is possible in them; for if everything were nature, everything would also be, though only physically, good and there would be no morality at all. The possibility of freedom we cannot comprehend; but we must nevertheless presuppose it, for rational beings can act only according to the idea of it. Thus it is practically certain. The question how freedom [be possible] is possible in created beings is one and the same with how substance, which nevertheless exists only derivatively, is possible.\nWith regard to God, for whom the progress to infinity, which for the creature is never completed, is only an *intuitus* (intuition), there is no evil. This is only in the parts because they are not the whole. Evil seems to belong merely to appearances; in itself it is only a manifoldness of the good according to degrees. Yet for us appearances are the things themselves. We also really do not depend on ap|pearances\n(g in acting) on ourselves and are thus free. Nevertheless we necessarily represent ourselves as depending on them with regard to happiness [han] in the sensible world we depend on it; hence we are nevertheless dependent on it as to the object of the will.\nWhat holiness: benevolence: and justice are.",[]],"reflm:6102":["(g Without astronomy these objections would be important.)\nObjection against the holiness of God: that evil (vice) is in the world.\nObjection against the benevolence of God: that ill is in the world.\nObjection against the justice of God: that [both] happiness and ill are not distributed proportionally to the worthiness of rational beings. *Vide* p. 283.\n* We are to expect everything from nature, as well our own as that which surrounds us in this sensible world, so that we bring forth the good and happiness out of ourselves and do not exist as foster children. The conflict of animality with the principle of reason in us must be overcome through gradual culture; one generation forms the other. Falsity, laziness, cowardice are vices; but they are in the whole nevertheless as it were corruptions that serve for the generation of the good. The whole lies in the intelligible world, this is only appearance.\nIll only according to our idea of happiness is cause of dissatisfaction, but it is a goad to activity. We are not to want to become happy here, we must die.\nLack of justice. If it were as we wish, all morality would transform itself into self-interest.\n*(g Evil arises through our looking only at a part of the world and not at the whole of the course of the world, and is also only the imperfection that attaches to the part. It rests on this, that [the human] human being in each generation makes up only a part of the whole species.)",[]],"reflm:6103":["The good in the sensible world is progress toward, not possession of, perfection; so happiness is progress, not possession of contentment.",[]],"reflm:6104":["Next to the first half of M § 915:\nHow God is the cause of evil is precisely the question as breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6105":["The removal, i.e. the abolition of actual evil in the world through the progress to perfection is the *perfectio phaenomenon*, although evil is not a means to the good.",[]],"reflm:6106":["In all (g moral) properties of God that carry an obligation with them, i.e. a necessity of being [one]self subjected to the moral laws, the properties are considered only [after as] respectively to us. e.g. *iustitia non commutativa, sed distributiva*.",[]],"reflm:6107":["M 376:\n1. Morality subsists for itself (according to its principle) without presupposition of a deity.\n2. The highest good is not the determining ground, but the object of a will determined through the moral law, and the* possibility of it, consequently also of the existence of God, is practically necessary.\n3. To assume the existence of God is the consequence of the moral law and of the disposition according to it; for only through this does the highest good become object of the will.**\n*(g [The] Nature cannot, without a supreme author with a moral will, agree of itself with morality; for that would have to be a nature in which the good will of the creature were also the sufficient efficient cause of the happiness harmonizing with it; but this is not entirely in its power, though the disposition is.)\nM 376':\n**(g The subjective ground of taking to be true is here grounded on an objective practical law, hence objective, but sufficient not in logical but in practical aim.)",[]],"reflm:6108":["(g On theoretical belief [and its Veränd] as well as practical belief of reason and its unalterability.)\nThe sufficient taking to be true from subjective grounds [the] must [at the same time rest on objective grounds] be so constituted that the subjective ground is at the same time objective or necessary from an objective ground, i.e. that the moral law, which commands to promote the highest good, be at the same time maxim. Whoever has this is sufficiently convinced in practical aim of God and a future world; but he can convince no others of it, because he cannot presuppose the maxim in others. It is therefore a conviction in practical aim, a belief of reason, not a historical one, which would be merely an opining and would be combined with alterability.\nHow knowledge is harmful here. Minimum. *Qvantum est, qvod nescimus. qvantum est, cujus scientia non indigemus.* (How much it is that we do not know; how much it is whose science we do not need.)",[]],"reflm:6109":["M 377':\nIf we wanted to say: it is highly probable that a God is, this would be a judgment that would not at all be suited to the constitution of the cognition and also, if one admitted it, would say far too little. For probability can be thought only in a kind of connections |\nwhose possibility is otherwise certain, e.g. that planets are also inhabited. But the possibility of a causal combination can be valid only through experience, hence of causes in the world; but of a cause outside it and not one in nature, but of nature itself, we have no ground to infer according to the analogy of nature. Probability is only a judgment until closer acquaintance.\nM 377:\nBut it can be necessary to assume something, and indeed from subjective causes of the use of the understanding in general, although we do not know the object, e.g. that the weather and other occurrences, e.g. of whole states, happen according to laws of nature and from natural causes, because otherwise we could not make use of our understanding. That is a postulate of universal human reason. Here lies a practical postulate: we ought to think occurrences so that we can use our understanding on them. But this practical postulate always has only conditioned necessity, namely if we want our understanding to agree with itself according to principles. But there are absolute practical postulates of willing, and those are the moral ones. We ought to be faithful and honest without conditions of an arbitrary end. Whatever must always be assumed necessarily in accordance with this postulate with regard to certain objects is also a practical postulate. I must think it so, and it is not merely hypothesis.\nHere the ground is objective, but not theoretical, rather practical, entirely *a priori*; but the conviction is also only respective to the practical, not probability, but firm belief.",[]],"reflm:6110":["M 377':\nWe cannot say: we cognize with certainty, indeed not even: with probability, that a God exists, from objective grounds. For probability (g is approximation to that of which certainty is possible) requires laws that are subject to those of nature, which we know, and according to which we would have to assume here that the origin of nature itself too would have to be explained; we thus go over from the law of nature to an entirely other order (μεταβασισ εἰσ αλλο γενοσ) |\nand want the same rule to hold for things outside a certain genus that may hold for things of this genus, not to mention that [no on] no probability can entitle an inference from a limited effect to an unlimited cause and that, if one cannot prove a highest being, to prove the existence of any other is without use.\nBut we have subjective grounds of both [the] speculative and practical use of our reason to presuppose such an existence, because without that we find no satisfaction for our reason, also no |\nM 377: |\nthoroughgoing unity of it possible. Chiefly the practical laws, which are indeed objectively necessary, have no subjective force without that presupposition. Thus we have enough for rational belief, indeed even for it as a necessary presupposition; for in speculative philosophy it is not necessary that I attempt to explain the origin of nature (g There is no objective necessity to speculate, but certainly to obey the moral law); but moral laws I must acknowledge, hence also presuppose, as unavoidable hypothesis, that without which moral laws would have no binding force for rational beings.",[]],"reflm:6111":["The pragmatic interest from the principle of happiness as a subjectively universal end makes belief in God only a hypothesis; the moral from the principle of the system of all ends as the objectively necessary final end of rational beings makes of it a postulate, i.e. an absolutely necessary presupposition of pure reason. God as the highest [Gu] self-subsistent good, in which morality as the highest formal good alone can have reality, i.e. a ground of the existence of an intelligible world, i.e. [of] of a realm of ends.\nHappiness as a consequence of morality belongs to it [as a corollarium de] and to its transcendental truth; but as a ground of it, it cancels it.\nWere the cognition of God a knowledge and not merely a belief, would morality then still remain. To believe in God is already moral and has a worth. Whether one can say: devils believe too? They fear that one is; they do not need him, they do not want that one be.",[]],"reflm:6112":["If I am entitled only in moral (g and indeed *a priori* established) aim to assume something distinguished from nature, such as God, freedom and immortality of the soul, can demonstrate nothing of this through insight, nor may lay it at the basis in another respect, then hereby the field of hypotheses of reason is closed and all enthusiasm deprived of its escape.",[]],"reflm:6113":["(g Of the world as the object of the most perfect will as of the *summi boni originarii*.)\nThe divine end is not the mere happiness of the creature outside him, not the satisfaction of an inclination to honor in him, but that which is the necessary consequence of the highest original good, namely: the highest derived good. The best world — i.e. happiness under the conditions under which each is worthy of happiness. That one, as the benevolent will, is in itself unlimited and therefore infinite; this one, as the holy will, ties it to conditions. Thus the product of the most benevolent and holiest will is the highest good. Justice is the consequence of it. But in this consists the practical perfection of the highest being. The world as the *ectypon*, i.e. the effect, which testifies of the cause, thus has its greatest perfection in this, that it testifies of a (g practically) most perfect, not merely benevolent author, and its perfection is thus the honor of God, not that the creature honor him [but that] through high praise, but through action and deed. And thus be happy in the highest degree.",[]],"reflm:6114":["[With] Under a good principle evil must always be in vanishing, and this diminution and progress in the good is the perfection of the world of sense. Evil is not substantial, it cancels itself.",[]],"reflm:6115":["*Prosperitas summa*, the highest welfare is happiness; the highest self-contentment is blessedness.",[]],"reflm:6116":["Pleasure in one's state is welfare.\nPleasure in oneself is self-contentment.",[]],"reflm:6117":["Contentment with our whole existence is happiness; this requires in the human being also physical causes, i.e. welfare. Happiness independent of physical causes is blessedness. Thus only from self-contentment. God is thus the sole blessed one.",[]],"reflm:6118":["Concerning the author of the world we have 3 pieces to consider:\n1. Creation, 2. Preservation, 3. Government.\na. The causality either *per emanationem** or *libertatem*; *caussa libera est autor*. In the latter case God is either *creator* or *architect*. 1. *Creatio*. Whether from eternity or in time? b. *conservatio* 1. *omnipraesentia*, 2. (g The preservation of the form) government. The wisdom [as] with regard to all particular occurrences in the course of the |\nworld is either laid in the beginning of the world** and is called *providentia*, or is exercised in the duration and progress of the world and is called (g *concursus*) *gubernatio*. As *concursus* it is called *directio*, and indeed primarily with regard to freedom. The *directio* is either *ordinaria* or *extraordinaria*.\nThe *decretum* of providence is either *absolutum* (*praedestinatio*) or *hypotheticum*.***\n*(s The absolute necessity of existence as From its nature; a ground it can never be of the existence of other things, for this necessity of nature is isolated and can have only *rationata immanentia*.)\n**(s or causality with regard to individual ends: *concursus*)\n***(s Difficulty, how God can be *auctor* of freely acting beings and yet not of their free actions. The latter have difficulty only if these actions were not appearances.)\n===NOTES===\n- Urheber = author (Latin *autor*/*auctor* kept as written); Begebenheit = occurrence; Zweck = end; Erscheinung = appearance; Schwierigkeit = difficulty.\n- Erhaltung = preservation (*conservatio*); Regirung = government (*gubernatio*); Weisheit = wisdom.\n- The asterisks mark the three footnotes; (g ...) and (s ...) and | are carried over as in the edition.\n- 'Die letztere haben nur Schwierigkeit' is left as ambiguous as the German ('the latter' could be read as the actions or as the second case).",[]],"reflm:6119":["*Systema* 1. (g of natural causality or only of the phenomenon, to intuit everything in God) *inhaerentiae* or [*emanationis*] *caussalitatis* (Spinoza, the ancients); 2. of free causality (s or *emanationis*, this however either *architecti* or *creatoris*) either *architecti* or *creatoris*; 3. *influxus* either *per commercium* (*anima mundi*) or *providentiam*.\n===NOTES===\n- The Latin is mixed with German genitive/adjective forms ('natural causality', 'free causality'); I kept the language of each as written.\n- 'phaenomeni' is Latin in the source; 'phenomenon' given for it; 'intuiren' = to intuit (Anschauung = intuition).\n- Brackets, (g ...) and (s ...) are the edition's apparatus, carried over unchanged.",[]],"reflm:6120":["*Creatio* and *conservatio* go to the substance. (s *providentia* and) *Gubernatio* and *concursus* (s to the freedom of the world-beings. But as such they are not free) to the state. *Providentia* is the divine government, laid in the creation.\n===NOTES===\n- Zustand = state; Wesen = being (Weltwesen = world-beings); Regirung = government; Schöpfung = creation.\n- The grammar of the (s ...) insertion is left as in the German; 'they' (sie) is not resolved.",[]],"reflm:6121":["*creatio*; *conservatio*; *gubernatio*; *concursus*, not to nature, but to freedom. *providentia* either [through] and *directio ordinaria* and *extraordinaria*; *absolutum decretum*.\n===NOTES===\n- The text 'vel [per] et' is as in the source (bracketed word editorial); the sentence is evidently irregular and is left so.\n- 'ad' is supplied before 'libertatem' in English only because the preposition governs both; the Latin has it once.",[]],"reflm:6122":["Categories of magnitude. Unity, plurality, το παν. (pantheism.) 2. Reality. Substratum of all reality. or limited (g infinity). Categories of modality: possibility, actuality, necessity.\n===NOTES===\n- Größe = magnitude; Einheit = unity; Vielheit = plurality; Realität = reality; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Möglichkeit = possibility; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.\n- 'limitirt' = limited (cf. Schranke = limitation).",[]],"reflm:6123":["1. God as cause of the world: *nexus effectivus*,\n2. as author of the world: *nexus finalis*,\n3. as ruler of the world: *nexus moralis*.\nPantheism does not signify: everything is God, but: the All is God.\n===NOTES===\n- Weltursache = cause of the world; Welturheber = author of the world; Weltherrscher = ruler of the world.\n- bedeuten = signify.",[]],"reflm:6124":["*Systema* either *inhaerentiae* or *causalitatis*; *prius* Spinoza's, *posterius* of Deism.\n===NOTES===\n- 'spinozae' and 'Deismi' are Latin genitives in the source; 'prius'/'posterius' = the former/the latter.",[]],"reflm:6125":["The absolute necessity of nature is only the ground of immanent, but not of transient actions; to this belong representation of the object and freedom.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand = object; Vorstellung = representation; Grund = ground; Handlung = action.\n- 'dazu gehort' left ambiguous: 'to this' may refer to transient actions or to the ground.",[]],"reflm:6126":["*Caussalitas Dei respectu mundi* 1: in regard to breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- 'bricht ab' appears to be the editor's remark that the note breaks off; the fragment is cut off after 'in Ansehung'.",[]],"reflm:6127":["Divine causality through freedom, i.e. *auctor*.\n===NOTES===\n- The abbreviation 'i.e.' stands for 'i.e.' in the source.",[]],"reflm:6128":["how God could bring forth something outside himself, is not to be proved; Spinoza.\n===NOTES===\n- Beweis = proof ('zu beweisen' = to be proved); the lower-case beginning is as in the source.",[]],"reflm:6129":["either *per emanationem*: necessity of nature, or *per libertatem*: through freedom (s *autor*). This either as creator or architect. The architect is not complete author (s *demiurgus*); the matter also bears the guilt and merit.\n===NOTES===\n- Urheber = author (cf. Latin *autor* in the same passage); Materie = matter; Schuld = guilt; Verdienst = merit.\n- 'Dieses entweder' is left as in the German (the referent is not resolved).",[]],"reflm:6130":["In and \nWere matter not a creature, it would be a sum total of necessary beings. These can therefore not be in *commercio*. Besides, God would be limited by the nature of the subjects subsisting for themselves.\nOne cannot say that the essential imperfection of the creature limits God.\n===NOTES===\n- 'In und' stands thus in the source, apparently a broken-off beginning; left as is.\n- Inbegriff = sum total; Wesen = being; Unvollkommenheit = imperfection; eingeschränkt/einschränkt = limited/limits (cf. Schranke = limitation); Geschöpf = creature.",[]],"reflm:6131":["[In] Of God we can represent to ourselves no *actus successivos* with regard to the world, but certainly successive effects from one and the same *actus* of creation and conservation; e.g. that, when we improve, God becomes from an angered a gracious God. Should this, however, happen, that the various effects of God on the world must let themselves be developed out of a single *actus*, then that means as much as: they must let themselves be explained out of the *actus* of creation, hence of the effect of the same, i.e. of nature. For nature is nothing other than the single *actus* of the divine power, from which everything else in the world lets itself be explained according to universal laws.\nHence no successive creation lets itself be thought, because this would not be as occurrences developing themselves successively out of the single *actus* of creation according to the order of nature.\nMiracle would be something that indeed according to an order of nature, which however is possible only through the cognition of the nature of a world-whole as such, can be cognized according to its laws. Thus only insofar as the intelligible ground of appearances were known to us, which is always moral.\n(g Creation does not belong to occurrences in time. The world with its existence must be thought through this alone as possible.)\n===NOTES===\n- Wirkung = effect; Kraft = power; Gesetz = law; Erkenntnis = cognition; erkannt = cognized; bekannt = known; Begebenheit = occurrence; Erscheinung = appearance; Daseyn = existence; Zeit = time.\n- Wunder = miracle; Naturordnung = order of nature.\n- The sentence 'weil diese nicht als sich successiv ... Begebenheiten sey' is syntactically irregular in the source and left so; 'Als' in 'Also nur, so fern' is likewise not smoothed.\n- 'ihren Gesetzen' could refer to Naturordnung or to Natur; 'its laws' preserves this.",[]],"reflm:6132":["Moral theology has as its object God as the highest self-subsisting good, as author of the world, which is therefore the highest created good.\nThe principle of moral theology is: that the (g positive) idea of freedom as the ground of all morals is taken from the idea of the highest good, which alone constitutes the system of all ends, in which we think ourselves as members and ought to act from this point of view, because it is to be possible through us and our freedom. Now this system of all ends from freedom is nothing other than a world in which (g highest) happiness stands in proportion with the worthiness to be happy. Thus morals includes this idea in itself as principle. Such a world, however, is possible not merely through our freedom, but nature must [this] contain the agreement with this law. But this it can contain only if a God is.\n===NOTES===\n- Moral = morals (as in 'Grund aller Moral'); Moraltheologie = moral theology; Zweck = end; Glückseligkeit = happiness; Idee = idea; Gesetz = law; Urheber = author.\n- selbständig = self-subsisting (no glossary entry).\n- 'Gesichtspuncte' = point of view; 'wenn ein Gott ist' kept literally ('if a God is').\n- '[dies]' is the edition's bracket, carried over as is.",[]],"reflm:6133":["God according to his holiness wanted the moral worth of the ra|tional\nbeings as the condition of happiness. This consists, however, in this, that he can impute everything good to himself. The less, therefore, God gave predisposition to it, so that the human being from freedom could become author of it, the greater the moral worth. Great predispositions to the good would have diminished the imputation of the good. Now the evil arose precisely from the conditions under which alone the greatest moral worth could arise: namely a moral character acquired through one's own effort. The ills serve to put to the test and to exercise the strength of morality through resistance.\nThe non-prompt execution of divine justice in this world is also the best means to present morality purely. Thus all apparent objections are rather consequences of the necessary spontaneity, energy and purity of the moral character, which could be obtained only through this and toward which God has directed the highest aim in this life, as the highest condition of the good.\n===NOTES===\n- Bedingung = condition; Mittel = means; Absicht = aim; Vernunft-/vernünftig = rational; rein = pure (Reinigkeit = purity); Urheber/autor = author; Zurechnung = imputation.\n- Böse = evil; Übel = ills (two German words, two English words); Anlage = predisposition; Scheinbare = apparent.\n- 'durch dadurch allein' read as 'through this alone'; 'dadurch' has no determinate antecedent in the German and none is supplied.\n- 'Chrarakter' (source spelling) = character.",[]],"reflm:6134":["We assume in organized beings that everything is purposive, although we do not see it. Why not precisely this in all things of the world.\nWithout a best world there is no morals.\nThe world of sense is, as world of sense,* in the whole the best.\n*(g perfect namely as passage to the intelligible, since morality will govern nature and be in its stead. This is both in that ills also befall the virtuous, and that through vice there is progress to the good.)\n===NOTES===\n- zweckmäßig = purposive; Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Fortgang = progress; Übel = ills; Moral = morals, moralitaet = morality.\n- 'statt ihrer' left with ambiguous 'their/her' (ihrer could be nature or morality); rendered 'in its stead'.\n- 'Dieses ist' has no stated antecedent and is left so.",[]],"reflm:6135":["In an animated world the corporeal nature as means and conversely the thinking nature as end must agree with one another; otherwise it is not one nature, because it does not preserve itself. Here ends constitute the principle of existence.\n===NOTES===\n- Mittel = means; Zweck = end; Daseyn = existence; Princip = principle; erhalten = preserve.\n- belebt = animated (no glossary entry); 'eine Natur' read as 'one nature' (it could also be 'a nature').",[]],"reflm:6136":["Teleology. The principle that everything has its end and is either good in itself or for something, is the supreme natural principle of natural science, which has not been derived from the hypothesis of a rational author, but because otherwise we would have no standard for our idea of the good, namely what it is as means, if it were not nature. Anatomy, all things that hang together in a system. From this need arises that of the hypothesis of a rational cause — for the things in nature outside one another cannot be represented as acting on one another purposively of themselves. Hume.\n===NOTES===\n- Naturprincipium = natural principle (*principium* in German form); Naturwissenschaft = natural science; Hypothesis kept as written; Richtmaas = standard; Mittel = means; Zweck = end; Vorstellung = representation.\n- 'Aus dieser Bedürfnis entspringt die der Hypothesis' is awkward and left so ('that of' stands for 'the need of').\n- The sentence 'sondern weil wir sonst kein Richtmaas ... wenn es nicht die Natur wäre' is obscure and not smoothed.",[]],"reflm:6137":["By experience it cannot be proved that this world is the best, thus also not to conclude from it to the highest perfection of the author. One must lay this at the ground in order to conclude to that; but then this proposition can well be justified through experience, primarily teleology. For the ancients, who held the earth to be the sole end, it was more difficult. Hume's objection, taken from the ills of human beings; they are incurred through guilt. The guilt is itself an effect of our freedom and limitations, and the limitations a particular arrangement of the progress to perfection.\n===NOTES===\n- Erfahrung = experience; Beweis = proof; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Urheber = author; Satz = proposition; Zweck = end; Schranke = limitation; Wirkung/effect; Fortschritt = progress; besonder = particular.\n- 'schließen' = conclude (here 'to infer'); 'daraus auch nicht auf ... schließen' is an elliptical infinitive and left so.\n- 'verschuldet' (sie sind verschuldet) is ambiguous between 'incurred through guilt' and 'guilty'; rendered 'incurred through guilt'.\n- 'diese ... jene' left as in the German.",[]],"reflm:6138":["End of creation. By this is understood either the whole created or the *actus* of creation, thus either the effect or the determination of the cause to the effect. The end of the former is the whole of all possible ends in the *universo* (of the members\n|\nof the same), consequently the free will in a system and through the same happiness in a system. That is the objective ground of the divine will. The subjective or the incentive cannot be distinguished in God from the objective. We can state it only negatively in the self-sufficiency of a being as the supreme source of all good and in the possession of its infinite causality.\n===NOTES===\n- Zweck = end; Bestimmung = determination; Wirkung = effect; Grund = ground; Glückseligkeit = happiness; Wesen = being; Möglich = possible.\n- Triebfeder = incentive (not in the glossary).\n- Selbstgnugsamkeit = self-sufficiency; Qvell = source.\n- 'der Glieder | desselben' continues across the page break; the referent of 'desselben' is left unresolved.",[]],"reflm:6139":["The free will as harmonizing to a whole of all possible ends or from the principle of the possibility of a general system of ends.",[]],"reflm:6140":["The objective end of God is (s the absolute (unconditioned) good of the world together with what attaches to it as consequence), that the world be the greatest (g created) good in itself itself, not: that it be good for something.\nBut it is the greatest good in itself insofar as rational beings [gl] in it are happy and worthy of this happiness.\nAs a system of ends through freedom, and then also through nature. (s Honor: no subjective end.)",[]],"reflm:6141":["God has no subjective motivating cause, i.e. interest in creation.",[]],"reflm:6142":["The concept of God is no speculative concept, but a moral concept; not in relation to nature, but to the interest, and indeed the moral interest of human beings, |\nM 386': |\nhe is through this also determined as a perfect being.",[]],"reflm:6143":["(g *mundus optimus*, because otherwise a better will would be possible; the highest happiness is not the supreme end, for it is only conditionally good; nor is morality, for it is not the whole end.)\n*Summum bonum (finitum) derivativum*. That rational beings in the world be happy, insofar as they are not unworthy of this happiness.\nBelief in God through morality. This gives the interest. The moral disposition is impossible without this, although the estimation.\nHow is it possible that God has an end outside himself? Not as incentive, but that is in him and in the consciousness (of his highest fruitfulness) as *summi boni*.",[]],"reflm:6144":["Desire requires the existence of a thing; will goes only to action, which pleases in itself, because it produces something good.",[]],"reflm:6145":["The world may be as good as one likes, we still ask: what interest does God have in creating it. Its existence could after all be indifferent to him. Was he weary of solitude? Did he seek honor. Moral interest.",[]],"reflm:6146":["Over, in and \nNot in order that God be an object of our love, but of our veneration, i.e. as holy legislator to the morally good, and only thereby to the physical, has he created the world.",[]],"reflm:6147":["In and \nThe concept of the honor of God is nevertheless better than the concept of the happiness of the creatures; for that goes to perfection of the world, but it is nevertheless ambiguous.",[]],"reflm:6148":["In and \nEvery will has an object, but not always a motivating cause. The highest good and its existence has for a most perfect will no motivating cause, hence also no end distinct from the object.",[]],"reflm:6149":["1. God as world-cause. 2. As world-creator and preserver, author. 3. As world-ruler, and the world not only as a realm of nature, but also as a realm of ends. Moral theology. Existence. Belief.",[]],"reflm:6150":["End considered according to its objective constitution is the highest good; according to its subjective ground there is none at all, but an *analogon*. The former is immediate end.",[]],"reflm:6151":["The will of God has merely objective motivating causes, no subjective ones, hence no incentives.",[]],"reflm:6152":["*Attributa* firstly moral; in aid *theologia transscendentalis et naturalis*. Evidence: |\nM 386': |\nthe greatest practical conviction. World as *summum bonum derivativum*.",[]],"reflm:6153":["God has created the world to his honor, not for the sake of his honor. i.e. the objective, not subjective end; honor is objectively what determines in itself the rational judgment about [the worth] all worth of the person. God has therefore created the world for the sake of rational beings, and indeed insofar as they should be capable of the valuation of themselves and of their true worth.",[]],"reflm:6154":["The question still remains: 1. whether God has at all a subjective motivating cause of the creation of the world, i.e. has an interest in its existence, as the ground of the desiring that it be there; [or] 2ndly which it |\nmay be. If the first is denied, then the second drops out. The will is twofold: a, that which arises from the [Bed] own neediness of the subject, [and that] since this requires for its complete contentment the existence of other things; or b, from all-sufficiency, since the subject has a liking in itself [as a] and in its sufficiency for all good outside it. The self-contentment with himself as a ground, as a source of the possibility of all other things is at the same time the causality with regard to the existence of the greatest [and with] effect, which in form agrees with the form of his self-contentment. Hence a distinction between an *appetitio originaria*, which rests on no subjective motivating cause or incentive, and a *derivativa*.\nThe (g objective) honor of God refers to rational beings and not to the motivating cause that these get in the world, to love him, but to honor. (g\n|\nM 387: |\nGod is the highest good, insofar as [he] his will is a morally perfect and holy will and under this condition alone a benevolent one.) |\nM 387': |\nThis rests on his benevolence, [ver] limited and determined through his holiness, i.e. [on] on this, that the world be a stage of moral good conduct for them, in order to make themselves worthy of the benevolence of God. Thereby alone is he honored; and, because the good that is brought about thereby alone does not flow to him [se], but is immanent in the world, thereby God is indeed served, i.e. we act as in the service of God toward the best of the world, but God is not served upon, i.e. through some cultus some end [outside] or effect outside the world and in God is intended. Thus there is no immediate service of God, but only [through] thereby, that we carry out his commands in the world and thereby ourselves become 1. good, 2. worthy of the goodness of God.",[]],"reflm:6155":["Over, beside and \nWhether God has created the world from eternity or in time. I.e. Whether creation is a beginning of the world or a [world] causality with regard to the substance of the world without any relation to time. In this relation only the existence of the world appears to us. This appearance is only a way of representation for us, not a constitution |\nof the things in themselves, which appear to us so. Insofar the world is neither in itself from eternity, nor has it a beginning, but only the duration in time that our regress in it contains, which goes to infinity without a first therein, but no series as e.g. of generations must be regarded as actually infinite, but as in itself not determinable.",[]],"reflm:6156":["Creation refers either to a beginning of the world or to the existence of the world without relation to time as the form of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:6157":["To the end of God belongs that the rational creature be completely adequate to the will of the same (through holiness); but no creature attains that. Hence it is the *progressus* toward it to infinity, where, if the disposition is such that it lets one hope this firmly, this is in accordance with the divine end.",[]],"reflm:6158":["Respect for an unconditioned (personal) good is veneration, that for the highest *adoratio*.",[]],"reflm:6159":["The world is a whole of ends (g realm of God); God: creator as architect;\n[for] as rational beings (g containing) also a realm of ends; God: ruler (g *progressus* to the highest good).\nInsofar as they themselves are to cooperate toward the highest end. *Civitas Dei*. Judge.\n(g 1. as realm of nature,\n2. as realm of grace,\n3. as kingdom of heaven, since [everything] nature entirely under the condition of agreement with the moral in the highest breaks off.)",[]],"reflm:6160":["*actuatio initii — durationis*.\n*Creatio et conservatio* (g the opinion about the conservation of the form, because it could not be preserved through the nature of things) go to the substance. *Providentia* and *gubernatio* to the state of the world. (The former is the arrangement of the agreement of the world with the divine ends, insofar as it is already laid in the creation, the second, insofar as it belongs to preservation, thus also in the sequence of time.) *gubernatio est vel directio vel concursus*. The first [concerns] is the supplementing of the imperfection of things with regard to form, the second with regard to force.\n(g The determinations either of the forces of the things of nature or the supplementing of the same as means to particular ends.)",[]],"reflm:6161":["The principle of the estimation of all *actuum* of the *gubernatio* is: that they all belong to one *actus* of creation, hence also of preservation.",[]],"reflm:6162":["*directio* is the determination of the forces of nature [through d] as means to the divine intention, *concursus* the supplementing of the same through divine force.",[]],"reflm:6163":["The action of God, whereby he has laid the *nexum finalem* in creation, is providence; whereby he produces it in preservation, is *gubernatio*.\nOnly one *actus*.\n*Concursus. cooperatio*.",[]],"reflm:6164":["(g *Conservatio* is not a peculiar *operatio*, but only the respect of things differs.)\n*Omnipraesentia virtualis* — perpetual, intimate;\nnot *commercium* as *anima mundi*,\nnot as sole *substantia*,\nbut as *ens extramundanum*.",[]],"reflm:6165":["That the evils in the world (on our earth) do not refute divine wisdom and goodness, but let it remain possible to regard [them in] their permission as not unworthy of a deity: this manner of judging is much justified by the astronomical discovery of so many worlds. There are so many infinite steps (although so that every rational creature also attains to the higher ones).\nBut precisely the same discovery in turn strikes down all claims of the human being to provision and eternity, and annihilates him so in his own eyes that he does not credit himself with importance enough to be an end of creation.",[]],"reflm:6166":["*Concursus* to nature or to freedom.\nGod is the *autor* of all determinations of nature, whether they happen according to the order of nature or not according to it. (g Of moral ones he cannot be *autor*, neither of the good nor of the evil.)\n*Concursus* properly means accession, in distinction from the *causa principali*.\nIn the moral, in order that it can be said of God that he *concurs*, he must not be *autor*, i.e. *causa solitaria* of the good. He concurs to freedom.",[]],"reflm:6167":["The *nexus* of a *caussae remotae* with the *causa proxima* to a |\n*caussato* is not the *concursus*, but of *causarum immediatarum*, where one makes up the *complementum* to the *insufficientz* of the others. Now one cannot think that God concurs to natural effects as such (g for he is not *concausa* of nature, but *causa solitaria*), i.e. to the *insufficientz* of his own *caussalitaet* in creation. Thus God cannot concur otherwise than to the *caussalitaet* of freely acting beings to his moral ends in the world, for of their free actions he must not be regarded as *causa*. That which gives to the free actions the *complementum ad sufficientiam* to divine moral ends (of holiness) is called the Spirit of God. These, however, if the actions are nevertheless [themselves] to remain *imputabel*, must also not be *causata* of the Holy Spirit, but only removal of the hindrances of freedom. The Spirit of God is that which gives moving force to the moral laws, thus an inner moral life, which is not possible at all according to laws of nature, works in us. Everything morally good in us is effect of the Spirit of God, and it is imputed to us that we grant room to this.",[]],"reflm:6168":["We can say: everything morally good and evil comes from ourselves and is to be imputed to us, and at the same time: everything [comes] good comes from God. In order to prevent *concursus* and mysticism. The good comes according to the order of nature from God, the evil from freedom.",[]],"reflm:6169":["1. The conservation is no *concursus*, for this is the *nexus* of the *concausae* with a *caussa*; [so f] thus *caussa solitaria non concurrit*. 2. The *concursus* to the state of substances is either to nature in itself or to freedom in the world. To the first [is] is the divine effect also not *concursus*, e.g. God and the physician. 3. Thus only to freedom: of the worthiness to be happy and of happiness. For here God with regard to the world of sense is not *causa solitaria*. 4. Also with regard to this either *per providentiam* or *occasionalismum*; the *occasionalismus in nexu finali* is hyperphysical: thus *concursus per naturam*.",[]],"reflm:6170":["*Concausarum subordinatarum superior (nec inferior) non concurrit, sed est causa solitaria*. If the actions are regarded as natural effects, then God is to be regarded as *causa solitaria*: *non concurrit*.\nBut of actions from freedom God is not *causa*, and there he can concur to the highest good: 1. insofar as it contains happiness. 2. insofar as the freedom of the human being is under hindrances and God can remove these, [therefore] not insofar as he gives us hyperphysically forces, for then he would be *causa solitaria*. Lead us not into temptation.",[]],"reflm:6171":["The *concursus* to nature presupposes that nature has a causality independent for itself; for if God is cause of the same, then it is said: *causa solitaria non est iuvans*. God thus concurs [to] only to freedom; for nature is his own work, to that he cannot concur. But he concurs only to freedom, insofar as it has the highest good as end: either as [Würdigke] subjective condition of the same or as objective consequences.\n(g The *actuation* of the highest good in the successive execution is called the *providentz*.)\nThis *concursus* occurs either in the creation; there it is called providence and can, because it aims at freedom and its use as condition, only be general. All *providentz* is either in physical or moral aim; the latter is *concurrentz*. Or in the conservation, and then it is called *gubernation*, guidance. The *direction* [is], dispensation, is an *actus extraordinarius* of the *gubernation*, [not] which is directed at individual aim.\nIt is not necessary to assume any *gubernation*, but only *providentz* and extraordinary *direction*. The ordinary *direction* is the *providentz*, considered insofar as it agrees with all ends in particular cases, although it has been derived from the end in the whole.",[]],"reflm:6172":["General providence already includes the particular in itself, and this can thus not be contested if the former is granted. But whether providence goes from the general to the particular, (g i.e.) from the whole to the parts, or conversely, is the question. We can think of no lawfulness and harmony of the particular ends according to determinate laws except in such a way that they begin from that which [general] is common to all, and from the whole.\nMany ends cannot harmonize if they have not previously been compared with the whole of all ends. This idea of the whole contains according to our concept *generalia*, with regard to which special ends were excluded. To preserve the species and more species of animals, the others of nature had to be sacrificed.\nProvidence is not merely *generalis*, but also *specialissima*, but not to be thought according to the manner of our provision. For there a system of them cannot arise from many individual ends, and the whole we can determine only through the individual; or, if the idea of the whole precedes, we must set aside aims on the parts, as in a state various estates must do without freedom and well-being, so that in the whole [but not] there may be happiness, but not an absolute whole of happiness fitting every *individuum* and his merit. We must according to our manner thus ascribe to God merely provision for species, but not *individua*. Not everything is end, but side-consequence.",[]],"reflm:6173":["b.\nThe actuation of the beginning (g of the world itself in existence) of substance is creation. Of the continuance, conservation. With regard to the state [is the conser] of the things in the world the actuation of the purposiveness of the same in the continuance of the world is: 1. laid into the beginning: providence; 2. in the progression either as gubernation, insofar as the forces themselves are not encountered in the [nature] world, or direction, insofar as they are indeed in the world, but not the determining ground for the purposiveness.\nBoth, gubernation and direction together, are *concursus*.\n*concursus* is distinguished from providence.\n(s *Creatio, conservatio, gubernatio*.\nEvery extraordinary providence is a miracle. The direction a formal, the *concursus* a material miracle.\nGod is either the author of the perfection of the world through the order of nature or against the same. The first is the ordinary, the second, as *miraculum praestabilitum*, the extraordinary providence.\nThrough the order of nature, according to our concepts of it, the final end is not attained.)\nM 394b:\n(s Matter as an aggregate of substances can [not] no being necessary for itself exist. For necessary beings cannot be *in commercio*, consequently constitute nothing composite.)\nM 394aI:\n(s Moral proof. We find in the world ends; these give us [us] for our insight an indication of a being that, according to the analogy of an understanding one, is cause of the world. Through this, however, its concept is not determined, neither for theoretical nor for practical principles of our use of reason: because with regard to the former it explains nothing, with regard to the second determines nothing at all.\nBut reason gives us through the moral law a |\nfinal end to pursue. This cannot be attained through our forces, and we are nevertheless to have it as aim. It can only be effected in the world, consequently insofar as nature agrees to it. A nature, however, that agreed with the moral final end would be a morally effecting cause. Therefore we must assume a being outside nature as author of it, which as moral being, which as (g with) understanding and will endowed is cause of the world. That is now theism.\nWhat we merely must assume from subjective grounds of reason (respectively to our determination of the will), but cannot prove, is a matter of belief. Thus the existence of God is a matter of belief of reason, and through this proof alone a determinate concept of the original being is given. — With this then the natural concepts, omniscience etc etc., are combined. Creation and difference from preservation concerns not God, but merely determination of the world. To assume creations in the continuance of the world is contrary to the principle of the possibility of experience.)",[]],"reflm:6174":["With God the general signifies the all, which contains everything else in itself.\nWith human beings the general signifies the few, that is contained in everything else.\nDivine providence, if it is general, must also be special, it is not so with human foresight. For the general is with God the cognition of the whole, with the human being the cognition of the genus. Now what is contained in the single is not in the concept of the genus, but is indeed contained in the cognition of the whole, which however it is impossible for us human beings to grasp. The cognition of God determines every part in the whole, that of human beings the whole through the parts.\nThus with God the particular providence is de|rived from the general.\nThrough this it is also possible that, since genera and species can subsist only by *individua* being preserved, with God this as well as all other ends can be preserved according to an order of nature that finds the ground of possibility itself in him.\nParticular [provision] dispensation (direction) is that wherein a (g single) occurrence is regarded as end and the causes as means. It is thus not considered as possible according to the order of nature, because this goes from the general to the particular, but this lets (g from) the particular the general [to] follow.\nThe cause for assuming such a dispensation is that one cannot represent how else all single occurrences in the world could turn out purposive, since we can make no concept of how God goes in the ends from the whole [to the pa] and its end to the parts.\nThe *concursus* of God with freedom (g insofar as it is morally determined), in order to make nature agree with it and with its ethical law, can also be considered as lying in the order of nature and must be so considered.\nPresumption of the judgments with regard to the determination of occurrences as ends (g and of the causes as means) and of the particular direction of the divine will upon the plans of human wisdom.",[]],"reflm:6175":["The agreement of divine providence with the perfection of the world according to laws of nature is called a *concursus* to freedom and to morality. It imparts faculty, opportunities and incentives to the good. The extraordinary with regard to single good or bad actions is possible; we can however never make it out. The *concursus* of God to the *progressus* [in] of the good in the world is the essential here. *Auctor mali* God is not for the reason that the human being is free and God gives no incentives to evil; but he can be regarded as *auctor* of the moral [law] good, because he is *auctor* of the obligation according to this law.",[]],"reflm:6176":["Whether divine providence is merely general\nor also particular providence?\nThe highest good is not [an] (g as) aggregate of single aims, but is to be regarded as system of ends derived from one idea. For without deriving the former from the latter no unity of aim is possible, e.g. clock. In a system of ends the agreement to the end is possible only by that through which they agree together to the aim being derived from the idea of the final end, that however in which they do not contribute to the aim being only restricted to this, that it not hinder the aim. Thus not every part can be aim.\nOne can indeed say: *qvicqvid valet in genere (g omnibus), valet etiam in specie*, but not: *qvicqvid valet de genere (g toto)* etc etc. [For] What pertains to everything belonging to a genus etc etc., but not: what pertains to the genus as a whole etc etc. All human beings die, but the genus does not die. The particular providence would be that wherein in the creation every occurrence were already predetermined as means or end with aim. Then one would have to assume either that the [general] order of nature according to general laws necessarily led to such results as, even if one also considered them outside this *nexus*, would nevertheless have been necessary in themselves already in the *nexus* of ends. But then one would also have no need to assume for the supreme cause an understanding being, but only a *causam brutam*. Thus, if the order of nature cannot present every occurrence as end or means, then the special providence is possible only through supernatural effects and, since they already lie in the creation, *miraculum praestabilitum*.\nEverything stands also under the particular providence, but not everything happens through it.",[]],"reflm:6177":["If providence is regarded as the most special in the aim, i.e. that all occurrences happen through the divine particular providence, it cannot be regarded as *generalis* (but indeed as *universalis*); i.e. [the occurrences] it cannot be directed first and preeminently to the whole and be determined only in relation to it. For this is possible only through an order of nature (according to our concepts). |\nThe occurrences would all be supernatural, and [it] the world would be no *totum reale*, but merely ideal.",[]],"reflm:6178":["The intuitive generality is to be distinguished from the discursive. In the latter the particular is contained not in the general but under it, but indeed in the former. The general providence contains the particular in itself according to general laws of nature. The particular direction is distinguished from the providence that is contained in the creation. It asserts that something is not contained in the end according to general laws of nature. That is however possible, because God is a moral author.",[]],"reflm:6179":["How it is possible through the general dependence of beings on God that they are nonetheless, according to general laws, in accordance with his ends. e.g. tree with spreading of its branches. Twilight. Snow for plants. Snow-light in long nights. The moon in its waxing part is visible to the polar regions for half a month.\nIf rational beings are to have become subaltern authors of the good in the world, they must have nothing but the order of nature before them, so that the good could be ascribed to their freedom.",[]],"reflm:6180":["*Directio* (g guidance) is properly *concursus formalis*; the *concursus materialis* is a particular *actus* of creation, a material miracle.\nOrdinary or extraordinary direction. Everything stands under it, but does not happen through it.",[]],"reflm:6181":["*Concursus* either to nature or to freedom; the latter does not increase the moral good in the world, because it is not to be imputed to us.",[]],"reflm:6182":["*Gubernatio*: Because the world continues, we also distinguish in God a continuation of actions.\nDirection is *concursus formalis*;\nopposed to it is *concursus materialis*: cooperation.",[]],"reflm:6183":["Particular providence is that wherein a single occurrence is laid into nature as end and the cause as means.",[]],"reflm:6184":["To [that which is in] the perfection of the world as a moral whole, it seems God must concur. But it is said: The concept of rational beings of God, i.e. religion, must concur to it, in order to do good in belief; which belief we can have only through presupposition of God and his providence.",[]],"reflm:6185":["The extraordinary divine direction is not necessary with regard to the ends of nature, nor with regard to the ends of God with regard to our freedom (for through the former no moral worth would accrue to the latter); now it seems to be necessary merely for the sake of the harmony of freedom with nature, for rewards and punishments, because, as it seems, freedom is a kind of lawlessness, which disturbs the divine aim.\nWere everything in the world to reward virtue and punish vice, the moral worth would lapse and God's end would not be attained. Only in eternity can it happen, i.e. in the infinite.\nIt is contrary to moral authorization to determine extraordinary divine direction here or there.\nIt is contrary to the logical determination of our reason. For to assume a miracle means: to deny entirely the maxim of our reason, which suffers entirely through a single exception. Reason, where it is to judge of itself, hence according to its own nature, can cognize nothing as supernatural; for that would contradict itself. That a miracle is possible in itself may be conceded; but if it happens, then the conviction of it must be effected through this very miracle.",[]],"reflm:6186":["Whether God as lord or (g legislator) also as proprietor of human beings could sacrifice [to himself] one to the other as means to an end.\nWhether, if the latter is not so, God is limited by laws, consequently is not *dominus plenus*?\nHe has the right to obligate morally; but this presupposes freedom.",[]],"reflm:6187":["The necessitation of a rational being through the mere cognition of the law is obligation. The law can, however, necessitate a rational being only, and this being can cognize itself as obligated only, if it cognizes its universal validity for every will, hence also the necessary agreement of its will with it. Now the rational being can agree to no end in which it would not itself at the same time be used as end, but merely as means. Thus he also cannot cognize himself as obligated to it.\nThe authorization to dispose [et] of an object without its agreement to the law (g disponiren), as one wishes, is property. God is thus lord, but not proprietor; sovereign (*autocrator*), but not despot, who would dispose of subjects as his property, consequently would have disposed of them as of things according to a will that does not have this happiness as an end.",[]],"reflm:6188":["A despot is he who regards his subjects as his property. Now he can be kind, but without asking the will of the people, in which way they can want to be so. He thus does not regard them (a tyrant is a cruel despot) as ends in themselves, from whose will at the same time he alone may give laws, but merely as tools of his ends. (E.g. to sacrifice one as an example for others.)",[]],"reflm:6189":["It is very agreeable to observe the divine provision according to general laws of nature; for this gives the understanding unity according to rules. But it is something absurd to allege particular providence for individuals, because these are nothing but interruptions of the laws and, without making a relation to freedom to individual laws of providence, subjects everything to our liking. But hope in particular providence, yet without presumption to want to determine it, is in turn a law both of nature and of good conduct.\nWith regard to the history of human freedom we do not refer to a divine predetermination [wede], i.e. providence, neither according to general laws through ordinary nor through particular ordinances through extraordinary arrangement, but to divine direction; and this too it is beneficial for reason and morals to observe in history. It is the gradual development of the natural predispositions toward the determination of humanity.",[]],"reflm:6190":["The doctrine of predestination is grounded either on the concept of the [nature] necessity of actions in opposition to freedom, or on the concept of moral incapacity for the good, since it can never spring from ourselves, but can only be effected by God.\nHere the rational being is merely a machine for divine aims. Damnation is no punishment and blessedness no reward for good conduct.\nFanatical concept of the elect. To be merely passive with regard to the divine decrees, both externally with regard to death and life, and internally in the improvement of oneself.\nDread of ascribing the good to oneself. False humility.",[]],"reflm:6191":["In predestination the concept of freedom is violated. Since this is incomprehensible, those who assert it are to be excused.",[]],"reflm:6192":["The making known of that which was hidden is revelation.\nGod has hidden himself from us. Through morality he first gives impetus to seek him in nature. Enlargement. Yet he wants merely belief (practical), not to inquire speculatively.",[]],"reflm:6193":["God wants to be hidden from our speculative presumption with regard to [their] his decrees, even more from our intuition. — Nature is a mirror that has its own color, anamorphotic.",[]],"reflm:6194":["All revelation goes to practical cognition.",[]],"reflm:6195":["All religion, even the natural, is a matter of belief. For it is doctrine objectively and disposition subjectively.\nTo regard all duties as divine commands and the whole object of them, the highest good, as possible merely under a divine legislation and government, consequently to expect this outcome merely in belief. Whether there is a duty to believe in God, or not rather (g historical belief without rational belief does not improve) the cognition of our duty brings forth belief in God?\nThe divine will must first have been cognized through reason, for otherwise one cannot test any revelation. But thereby I cognize the will of God only from the moral side. With regard to [our] the pragmatic laws as sanctions it must have been revealed. But the moral alone can be a universal human duty. The relevation can, as discovery of that which is necessary merely for knowing, and through which no one becomes a better human being, not be for all human beings. But with regard to the moral it is not therefore unnecessary, because human beings can also see it through reason.\nWhat belongs to the introduction of a religion is not itself a religious prescription for all times.",[]],"reflm:6196":["Revelation contains *credenda* and *agenda*, but the former as belonging to religion insofar as they are directed to *agenda* as moral ideas. Sanctions bring forth observances, only laws general human duties.\nThere can be no obligation to belief; for belief is a consolation, not compulsion, one holds to it gladly, compulsion one hates.",[]],"reflm:6197":["(g An unbeliever is only he who does not) morally [un] believes, thus he who does not want to accept that which alone can strengthen moral.",[]],"reflm:6198":["Unbelieving is he who, what is not certain to him through his own experience, [to it] does not want to take to be true otherwise than through certainty a priori. Thus neither as hypothesis for the comprehensibility (g or the practical use) of other propositions certain a priori, nor on account of the experience of others. Disbelieving.",[]],"reflm:6199":["Belief in revelation is moral if it is duty; but only historical if it is not duty, but merely a means to duties.",[]],"reflm:6200":["Whether God reveals himself to us (g announces) through pure reason (the practical, not speculative) or through nature (he opens himself, but uncovers himself to us) or in a supernatural way through outer or inner experience?",[]],"reflm:6201":["Whether God can reveal himself through appearance?\nWhether through [immediate] speech, so that we know that it is God who speaks?\nWhether, if this is so, he communicates his will, which cannot be cognized by us through reason, or only the confirmation of the moral (g natural) will in our souls. Effects of grace.\nWhether he reveals himself only to him to whom he speaks, through the [generation] conviction effected at the same time in him, or also to others just as certainly through [this] that one's narrative, which as historical requires testing according to criteria of history.",[]],"reflm:6202":["athei. (g diagoras.) Strato. Epicurus. Anaximander.\nDiagoras.*Practici*.\nNatural theology is necessary:\n1. because according to its concepts we judge the genuineness of the revealed ones,\n2. because it is needed to preserve the latter in its purity.\nIts origin can be through revelation, but, when it is there, it must at all times be able to be seen into by reason by everyone.\nIn the presentation to look chiefly to the moral attributes of God.\nTo seek to prevent superstition, enthusiasm and unbelief.\nIn a word: empty rationalizing about God.",[]],"reflm:6203":["The believing way of thinking is either receptivity or spontaneity of belief. The latter is practical and consists in the principle of doing good in belief. With regard to the truths, with regard to whose acquaintance we must be merely passive, there he does not count this passive behavior toward obligation, but only toward the warrant to accept it, and denies neither what he does not know enough, nor presumes for its truth.",[]],"reflm:6204":["Healthy understanding is, like beef and mutton, a dish for burghers and peasants etc etc.\n(g The human being who accepts nature as principle, but permits himself as many exceptions as he wants, or thinks yet another nature, which is not experience, is unenlightened.)\nTo be enlightened means: to think for oneself, to seek the (g supreme) touchstone of truth* in oneself, i.e. in principles.\n*(g of my judgment. the ground of the taking to be true; for I must answer for it.)\nEnthusiasm: in (g spirit-) sensations*, which pretend the feeling of truth. Panegyrists of common human understanding.\n*(g these I do not have from myself. Only reason posits criteria for itself.)\nSuperstition: in *Factis* [alone]; for these still have a touchstone above them.\nUnbelief (g The blameworthy lack of belief or resolve not to believe): in theoretical reason alone. (g For practical reason needs for the incentive also hypothesis.)\nNaturalist: how much meaning. Whether there are atheists. Free spirit in historical meaning is a free thinker. Who submits himself to no statutes.\nThe morally unbelieving person is a free spirit.",[]],"reflm:6205":["Delusion: 1. of lazy trust in passive reason: mysticism; 2. of mistrust in all reason; 3. of the self-conceit of the speculative.",[]],"reflm:6206":["Th II:\nHuman reason has the particular property, [not only the Mo] besides what belongs to it, of making for itself, for a certain aim, a concept of a thing, not only this concept itself by means of everything that constitutes it, but also of completing the object of the concept in the kind of things to which it belongs. We are not content with what would be sufficient for the common use of words, to know distinctly the concept of a body, of a human being, of a plant; we seek [it] to become conscious of it in all its marks, and from this, when the law of parsimony is added, the definition arises. But we seek, moreover, when we have counted the object as belonging to a certain kind of things, |\nto think it for ourselves completely with regard to this kind. Body belongs to matter, and what in its extension is not matter is empty space; hence we make for ourselves the concept of a perfectly dense body. It has interconnection; we think for ourselves a perfectly hard one, without troubling ourselves about whether such a thing is actual or even (g only) possible. Thus the completeness of a thing of a certain kind serves us only as the standard of all the remaining concepts [things] that we could make of it, insofar as they are distinguished from one another merely according to magnitude. These magnitudes are variable; one must compare them with one that is invariable, i.e. that of a thing that contains everything that can be contained in the concept of it in relation to its kind.\nSome of these concepts of completion we can determine, in that we have a determinate and in experience [at least negatively to be indicated concept] of that which belongs to its completion (e.g. among all chords in the circle the diameter); others are so constituted that we only think the completion, but cannot ourselves complete the concept. The concept of wellbeing is empirical, but much can still be lacking [lack] in the contentment with one's state. Here, then, a concept is necessary that lacks nothing in content, i.e. the greatest and [best] everlasting wellbeing, i.e. happiness (g\n|\nTh I: |\nwhich we nevertheless can never think determinately). If this too does not depend on contingent external causes, but springs from ourselves: blessedness. Now we can still extend this concept of the satisfaction of a rational being, in that to what it enjoys we still [add] posit what it does, i.e. the contentment with its person and the morally good. The complete morally good is the highest virtue. If the same is itself free from all inclination to evil: holiness. Holiness of the will and blessedness of the state together therefore make the idea of heaven. On the other side, because that, [what not] whose concept consists in the relation of cause and effect, has a twofold opposite: a negative = 0 and a privative = –, one can think a state that leaves no contentment at all: misfortune, and insofar as the being contains the cause in itself: misery, likewise also a will that acts contrary to all moral laws with |\nintention, and thus the idea of hell arises. Hence\nheavenly joys and hellish torments heavenly virtue and hellish vices\n}\nideas.\nTh III:\nThese ideas are entirely necessary, even if [the] no object corresponded to them. It also does not matter whether there is such a thing, but only: how we can better compare our concepts of what is with |\none another, in that we hold them together with a third, although it is merely idea, as a common measure. (The Julian period goes back 532 years before the beginning of the world).\nIf we now think for ourselves an individual according to these ideas, it is the good or evil original being; for both are considered not according to what they contain in themselves, but according to that of which they are the cause. (Whether in the world, as much as there is of good on the one side, there is also as much of evil on the other, and whether, just as motion cannot be imparted in one direction without producing or taking just as much on the opposite side, *lex isonomiae* (law of equal distribution), then the principle would indeed be good, but the work of it necessarily contain just as much evil as good). Both would contain everything that is required for activity, but the one always the ground of reaction against the [activit] effect of the other. If the evil being is derived as to its existence, but yet, although the limited, cause of all ill: then it is the ill or the devil, an idea not *praecise* (precise), of which one does not know how far the influence and the effect of it go.\nIf we think for ourselves a completed being not according to determinate concepts of the good and evil, but as thing in general: then this is the transcendental idea of a highest being, which is also necessary in order for us to think the differences of all possible things in the thoroughgoing determination with regard to something and nothing in general. First everything is thought in One, in order afterwards to think more in one thing, less in another through mere limitation of the archetype. I always retain the same subject: a thing in general, and through different limitation of the being, which contains everything positive, I get out all things, insofar as they are something, and the differences rest, as it were, on the composition of something with nothing in the infinite space. The evil original being is also thought as infinite reality, but in the effect like the counterplay of all causality of the former, whereupon the world contains indeed no contradictions, but yet sheer conflict of aims.\nBesides the necessity of the idea of a most perfect being for |\nthe concepts of all others [counts] reason judges it [also] as necessary for the existence of things. These, considered merely as things, can distinguish themselves only through [reali] negations, hence only through limitations of a highest reality. Now the existence of limited things seems to be just as derivative as their concept, and, on the other hand, the existence of a being that has all reality seems alone to be able to be original. All shadows are only remnants of the infinite nothing, namely of the night, which without the all-illuminating sun would fill space. It seems therefore natural that not light mixed with shadow be there first, but arise only through greater or lesser limitation of the sunlight, which must go ahead. The highest reality can limit its own effect, whereby it manifests its existence externally; but limited reality has in itself no sufficient principle of its possibility; consequently the actuality of the same is also to be regarded as dependent. Further, from the principle of thoroughgoing determination there arises a thoroughgoing community of descent, hence kinship of everything possible, because it is possible only in one concept, because all negations are possible only through the bounding of the highest reality.",[]],"reflm:6207":["The *principium* [The du] *exclusi medii* (principle of the excluded middle), i.e. of determination, contains: that, if the concept is compared with one of two *oppositorum* (opposites), one of the two must pertain to it. The principle of thoroughgoing determination says that the concept of a thing in general, in order to make up the representation of an individual, must be compared with all possible *praedicatis oppositis* (opposed predicates), so that, if it has been determined with regard to one, it must in this determination be compared with other *praedicatis oppositis* and it can thus, as thing in general, be thought as determined through the relation to the *ente realissimo* (most real being) alone. Through this it happens that a general concept determines itself thoroughly and becomes a concept of an individual being.",[]],"reflm:6208":["The principle of thoroughgoing determination is a synthetic proposition; for it demands that the concept of every thing stand in community with the con|cepts\nof everything possible and thus that to every determination still more belong, which do not belong analytically in the concept of the thing, consequently that the general concept be regarded only as a part of the concept that contains the predicates for all possible ones.",[]],"reflm:6209":["The *principium exclusi medii* says only that of two *oppositis* not both can pertain to a thing or be *removed* from it, but not that it must necessarily be compared with all possible predicates [or also], i.e. exist only in relation to them and the determination according to the *principio exclusi medii*. This is the principle of thoroughgoing determination. The first is logical and signifies only the nature of disjunctive judgments, which contain the *oppositionem contradictionis*; the second is metaphysical and designates the derivation of the concept of each thing from the sum total of all possibility, namely of the highest reality.\nHuman understanding requires for a determinate concept of a thing (namely not of that which is common to many things, but of an individual one) that it let itself thereby be distinguished from everything possible, because it judges only through general concepts. It must therefore compare it with all possible predicates in the idea and thinks the thoroughgoing determination, which yet is possible for no understanding to carry out. It presupposes therefore: in order to cognize a thing completely, one must not only know what it contains, but moreover everything that it lacks, so that one also cognizes it in relation.\nThe *principium* of determination is the *exclusi medii*. The principle of thoroughgoing determination reads: every individual thing (i.e. insofar as it is distinguished from all others; for [the] a thing, considered generally as genus and species, is distinguished only from those that do not stand under this concept) is to be distinguished with regard to everything possible through its relation to the sum total of all possible predicates.",[]],"reflm:6210":["(g Theology. We can exhaust this object of cognition; for it is not given to us in itself.)\nThe reason of the human being needs a threefold completeness. 1. The completeness of the determination of a subject with regard to all possible predicates. 2. The completeness of the derivation of its existence from the existence of others. 3. The completeness of the derivation of the existence of all from one existence. I.e. of the community of all in one single cause. Three ideas.\n1. The completeness of the determination of a thing relative to a certain concept. e.g. the concept of figure, as of the rectilinear ones, if they are regular (as equilateral triangle, quadrilateral) or of the curvilinear: circle. The determination from the principle of the end with regard to all ends: morality, or also of an end that conflicts with all. Completeness of friendship.\n2. The completeness of the derivation. The independent existence, the first beginning, the cause that is not further *caussatum*. The idea of freedom as a kind of causality that is not externally conditioned. The first human being. The beginning of the world or of chaos (beginning of order), finally the first existence in general.\n3. Completeness of community. I.e. thoroughgoing determination of the many among one another through the dependence of their existence on One. e.g. Commonwealth under a lawgiver. Unity of the church under one head. The causality of many causes, insofar as it is thoroughgoingly determined through their dependence on one. The world and God.\nWe have 1. Only one concept of a thing in general, through which it is thoroughgoingly determined: *ens realissimum*; 2. [Only one concept which] Insofar as this concept is the only one that needs no other for its determination, also an existence that is no sequel from other existence, thus of an original being; 3. insofar as all other existence |\nderived and none but that one an original one, community of origin of all things, consequently complete concept of the combination of them with one another. Best world. — The all-sufficient being: 1. as the highest being as subject; 2. the original being as cause; 3. the being of all beings as cause of everything. Theology.",[]],"reflm:6211":["How can the *maximum* be a standard of our judgment, since the idea of it surpasses all our given concepts? For the reason that the restrictions are positive, which I leave out, all of them, in the *maximo*. Thus because it is the pure idea, just as the concept of totality arises from plurality without restriction.\n[Likewise]\nThus the divine understanding is a pure understanding, the divine will a pure will, the divine presence pure, eternity etc etc.\nThe idea of the *maximi* is the one that is most determined and therefore also determining.\nThe idea of the *maximi* alone is *praecisa*, e.g. perfect justice and morality.",[]],"reflm:6212":["The three problems (g of metaphysics): God, freedom and immortality fit the three latter antinomies, where (if one takes this series reversed) simplicity, (g absolute) causality, necessity can all together be applied to the intelligible. The one of the magnitude of the world in space and time does not come into account here, because it is entirely restricted to sensible conditions. Yet through its insufficiency for the unconditioned in general it can lead to the intelligible (the first beginner of the series).",[]],"reflm:6213":["From the above grounds of the need natural to reason, in |\nregard to the concepts that arise from itself, to ascend up to the [completeness] idea that completely determines that concept, there now arises the concept of a most perfect being. 1. As a thing in general, 2. as a perfect nature (s according to psychological concepts), 3. as the most perfect principle of the system of all ends. The transcendental, the natural and the moral perfection. The highest being [as] now, considered as the most perfect nature and at the same time as the highest good, is God.\n(g greatest transcendental, greatest perfection of nature, greatest practical.)\nThe system of our cognition of God is now a science and is called theology.\nQuestions. 1. How does theology as possible science relate to the faculty of our reason? Can one bring it to completeness, or does it remain, like others, always capable of an extension? Answer. The object surpasses by far human understanding, and what we could cognize of it, if it pleased it to reveal itself to us, reaches into the infinite. But what we can cognize of it through the light of reason can be [completely] exactly measured off and the natural theology possible to the human being completely presented, without an extension of this insight ever being hoped for.\n2. Of what kind will the cognitions be (g extending or rectifying) that such a science can supply us. As cognition of the common understanding (s as hypothesis, in order to complete the [Nat] chain of natural causes) it will contain various positive things, which however lie before everyone's eyes even without science. But as science its business is merely negative: a. to hold off errors, hence to prevent our contradicting ourselves in the concept of the highest perfection. b. in that a healthy critique shows distinctly the limitations of our reason, to moderate the wantonness of the conceited cleverlings, who through groundless presumption pretend to be able to penetrate so deeply into the nature of things that they think they can refute the rational belief [as ei] in such a being.\n3. What interest does reason have in this science? No speculative one, but merely a practical interest, and indeed of this alone the moral. This cognition is supposed to make us not into learned but better human beings or to give to our already previously good dispositions force and persistence. It is supposed to arise from morality and to act back upon it again. (s It belongs not to physics but to morals.)\n4. With this aim, will common understanding suffice for the cognition of God, which interests everyone, or will scholarship and deep speculation be required? Common understanding, for the cognition is supposed to be able to be demanded of everyone.\n5. What is the minimum of theology? That it is at least possible that a God is, and that no one could know so much as to refute us if we believe him.\n6. How far does knowledge go on this point? Merely that our concept agrees with itself and does not conflict with its own aim. The object is a mere idea, thus cannot be given according to concepts of experience, and our positive cognition here cannot be knowledge.",[]],"reflm:6214":["P. I:\n(s *theologia vel rationalis vel revelata* (theology either rational or revealed).)\n(s *Physicotheologia a primo motore*.)\nThe concept must be thoroughly determined *a priori*. This is only that of the *ente realissimo* (most real being). (g Hence this theology the foundation of the rest.)\n1. *Ens originarium* (original being) (s *Cuius nec conceptus nec existentia est derivativa*) as *ens summum*: *Theologia transscendentalis*. (s This compre|hends Ontotheology and Cosmotheology.) (s Benefit of transcendental theology.)\n2. *Ens originarium* as *summa intelligentia* (highest intelligence) (s living): *Theologia naturalis*.\n3. *Ens originarium* as *summum bonum* (highest good): *Theologia moralis*.\n(s The concept of God: 1. as* [isolated concept] *sibi sufficiens conceptus*, 2. as boundary concept, 3. as architectonic concept.)\n*(s Possibility from concepts, 2. necessity as ground of the series, 3. of the system.)\nHe who merely accepts the first theology asserts merely cause of the world.\n(s Anselmus:\nOntotheology.\nWolff:\nCosmotheology.)\nHe who also accepts the second theology asserts it as author of the world.\n(s Physicotheology.)\nHe who also accepts the third theology asserts it as ruler of the world (s lawgiver).\n(s Concept — nature — will of God)\n(s speculation or moral theology. Moral theology is not theological morals. In the latter morals is derived from the existence of God, in the first the existence and the concept of God is determined through morals.)\nDeism, theism. Relig: *Theismus Moralis*.\nThe world as a realm of nature, 2. of ends. 3. of graces.\nThe principle for proving the existence:\n1. From mere concepts or experience in general;\n2. From the object of particular experience, knowledge of the world;\n3. From our ends.\n(s *Ens realissimum* as ground is the principle of all beings and of their [material] real possibility.)\nCertainty is apodictic or physical (s hypothetical) or moral.\nKnowledge, surmising and believing.\n(s Benefit of transcendental theology: more negative.)\nProperties. 1. transcendental theology: Nothing but ontological predicates.\n1. *Possibilitas* (g *omnimoda*).(g *Essentia* (g *originaria*)) (g *Ens summum (entium)* as aggregate or as ground. I limit only the consequences.) (s *contra Atheistas dogmaticos*) 2. *Existentia* (g *necessaria*) *contra Atheistas*. 3. *Necessitas contra scepticos*. 4. *Substantia* (g *non unica*) *contra Spinozam* (g *mundus non aliud*). 5. *Immaterialitas contra Anthropomorphismum*. 6. *Vnicitas contra Polytheismum* (g *Manichaeism*). 7. *Extra Mundum contra Stoicorum Animam Mundi*. (s *Omnisufficientia contra independentiam materiae*.) 8. *Infinitum* (s *incommensurabile*) *contra Anthropomorphismum subtiliorem*. (s We |\nrepresent to ourselves the possibility of all *finitorum* as derivative.) 9. *summa perfectio* (g *formalis* as *consensus* to all reality outside it, thus not as *destructor*, but *conditor realitatis*) *contra Manichaeismum*. 10. *Aeternitas, Omnipraesentia, Omnipotentia* (*Omnisufficientia*) (g *non solum formalis, sed materialis*). (s *attributa operativa*.) (s *Causa, qvae non habet concausam, e.g. Materiam causam mali*.) *Immutabilitas. Impassibilis*.\n(s Whether the concept of God is a physically or morally necessary concept, necessary in order to rationalize better or to act? First determination of his idea as highest principle of all existence. Anthropomorphism. Either from the concept of the highest being the concept of the original being or conversely. From the order of nature contingency is inferred. The understanding of the author does not prove his necessity.)\n(s transcendental theology for the sake of the purity of the concept of God.)\nP. II:\n(g Moral proof of the existence of God. as a necessary hypothesis for speculative and practical interest.)\nSkeptical atheism is godlessness, the dogmatic: denial of God. The first disputes only the possibility of making for oneself a concept of it, the other the possibility of the being itself.\n(g All concepts except that of the *entis realissimi* are, with regard to the remaining predicates, undetermined.)\nOneness follows from the *omnitudo realitalis*, because in the concept of the *entis realissimi* as *Noumeni* space and time do not occur; otherwise two beings with the same properties could be in different places.\n(s The subjective necessity of reason to assume God is valid for us objectively.)\n(g from concepts or from *factis*)\nThe ontological proof is one and the same with the pure cosmological, only that there from the concept of the *entis realissimi* the necessity, here from the necessity of some being (as independent existence or highest ground for the actual) is inferred to the concept of the *realissimi*. The first errs in that it regards the proposition: *ens realissimum* exists, as analytic, since it is nonetheless synthetic and as such cannot be proved. The second: that it takes the concept of the relative necessity of determination (of a thing (g in general) with regard to the (g opposed) predicates that can pertain to a thing in general) for a concept of the absolute necessity of the thing itself*; that namely *ens realissimum* is thoroughly determined with regard to all its predicates and these pertain to it necessarily, be just as much as: it exist necessarily. The analytic necessity again confused with the synthetic.\n*(g The error is actually this, that one believes, [the real nec] from the logical necessity of the predicates of an *entis realissimi*, since namely only this concept is throughout necessarily determined, to infer to the real necessity. We ought only to say: through this we have a necessary concept of a being, not: a concept of a necessary being.)\n(g Contingent is that which exists only in a conditioned way. Thus only the unconditioned is necessary. We can know this only thereby, that it is the condition of everything else. Time and space are contingent forms of our own intuition.)\n(g To the moral proof anthropomorphism is most opposed.)\nThe theistic concept is a merely relative or regulative, not absolute and constitutive anthropomorphism. The concept of God as boundary concept of cognition of nature according to speculative laws of the entire reason.",[]],"reflm:6215":["Th 2':\nWe live in an age which has not yet had its equal in the history of the human understanding. To be sure, the human mind has perhaps already exhausted every possible folly and delusion of an erring reason [zwar], and one will probably always be able to find an example for every new nonsense in the old time; but that all kinds of absurdities and delusion show themselves at the same time and indeed publicly, while [the true investigation of its] reason conducts its business publicly and calmly: this is [the] to be regarded as an unavoidable misuse of the freedom to think that is only now first sprouting, which, just as in states that have thrown off despotism, must first bring forth anarchy and civil disorder, but finally nevertheless a lawful civil state. The [civil] authoritative standing does not mix in any disputes that concern merely (g public (written)) thinking, except solely insofar as this concerns religion, because this is really a powerful support of morality and by means of it of civil [peace] security and combination |\n. States that have an inner firmness do indeed allow these disputes free course, insofar as they remain among the learned; but as soon as separations and factions begin to be generated from them, which in matters of religion commonly produce either partiality toward some and hatred toward others: so they too become concerned about the outcome, for the balance is hard to maintain. [Were it] Best of all, however, is: they let these things go and favor reason and science; for in this way alone can lawful freedom of thought (instead of anarchy) and supremacy of reason (instead of despotism of orthodoxy) arise.\n(g Nothing is more harmful than impudent ignorance.)\nThe nonsense rests for the most part on the prattle about religion without a preceding determination of theology, and indeed first of that which has its origin from reason alone and which must also be laid as ground to every other, be it grounded on history or immediate inspiration, |\nas criterion of its correctness. — It is therefore especially in our times of the greatest importance to present a well thought-through [theol] and in its whole domain completely carried out theology of mere reason [to bring about], which latter can also be done, since it is not demanded to know everything that pertains to the object, but what human reason can cognize of God. When theology and religion are mixed together, a confusion of concepts arises, in which one regards theology as a necessary consequence and duty of religion and therefore already proceeds partially. With regard to the former [all] speculative reason must first be left alone and free.\nSo that we may determine beforehand the mode of thinking and hence the subjective causes of the discord of human opinions [here] and, when we sift and test these, at the same time discover the source from which the absurdities arise and thus be able to divide the history of the aberrations determinately, we must first of all oppose to the genuine use of reason the manifold delusion with regard to the use of reason in general and consider this preeminently.\nDelusion is the [maxim] opinion of a freedom in thinking without [the] regard to the laws of reason. Thus it has imagina|tion\nor illusion of the senses as ground. Imitation. Presumptuous ignorance.\nTo delusion the use of reason in experience is opposed. Not that it must take its grounds precisely from experience, but it must not think further than [to] so far as it [according to laws of] can be applied to experience according to laws according to which this itself is possible.\nTh 3':\nWhat is the use of our reason in experience?\nWe can give meaning to all our concepts and reality to our judgments only insofar as [that we] so far as (g they) agree with the principles of a experience possible thereafter (otherwise they are paraphysical). All principles to explain things of experience that are not in conformity with the rules of a possible experience, but [unab] transcendent breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6216":["Someone assumes (g [to]) [principles of experience the ... ein] experiences, in which no reason at all can be used, and is superstitious; or to the principle of reason, in which [all reliability] no connection of experience is possible, and is fanatical.\nOr someone rejects everything that is not demonstrable either through reason or experience, and he is unbelieving. He, on the contrary, who grants also something as a necessary hypothesis [zu volle], insofar as it is indispensable to the completion of the theoretical or practical use of reason, is believing.\nThe believer indeed assumes something that can be no object of experience at all breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6217":["He who therefore assumes an idea (g of reason) with which no laws of experience can agree is fanatical; he who [Erschei] assumes laws of experience with which no use of reason can subsist together is superstitious. He who, to be sure, cognizes the reciprocal agreement of reason and experience in [einem ob] the judging [de] of an object as necessary, but at the same time wants to grant no |\nother object than one whose existence is sufficiently demonstrable through reason or experience, is unbelieving. (Baar.)\nBelief of reason is [the necess] the principle (g maxim) of reason to assume that which [in the] for the completion of the series of grounds of a reason agreeing completely with the use in experience is unavoidably necessary. Now the first of this series can be proved neither through reason nor on the path of inferences of experience [proved] as a determinate object. Therefore belief of reason is unavoidably necessary for the complete use in experience (by which I understand the derivation of the maxims of reason exercised on objects of experience). Fanaticism is madness. Superstition stupidity and unbelief folly, i.e. frivolous abandonment of the chief end (namely of the completeness of the grounds for that which can in general be the end of speculation or praxis). Investigative use of reason, therefore that [se] which concludes with a belief of reason completing that, is wisdom.",[]],"reflm:6218":["The mode of thinking according to ideas of which one can make no use in experience, (g is fanaticism). God, freedom, immortality are ideas of which one can make a use in experience. But secret forces to pervert nature, spiritual intuitions are ideas of which one can make no use in experience, precisely because they make experience impossible.\nThe mode of thinking of principles from *facta* of which one can make no use in experience is superstition; e.g. presentiments, significant dreams, apparitions of the deceased.\nUnbelief is the principle of denying everything that cannot be an object of experience. For there are always still subjective grounds of taking to be true, when objectively sufficient ones are lacking. But the subjective ground with regard to the supersensible is the reason that never sufficiently satisfies itself through the sensible, in the theoretical and the moral.",[]],"reflm:6219":["The speculative concept of God is (g es) beforehand most necessary to |\nrectify; but its worth cannot be rated so high that the moral one would not have to give it the foremost determination. Even the unity of this being, if one does not at the same time ascribe to it a holy will, can yield no secure religion.\nThe way of thinking [of the] of a reason separating itself entirely from the use in experience is fantasticality. (Whimsy-chasing.) (g Chimera. Dreaming.)\nThis sentence has subsequently been altered by words or letters written over it as follows, without the words no longer fitting the new context having been struck through:\nThe way of thinking of a reason contradicting the principles of the use in experience is delusion.\n* Fantasticality is: 1. fanaticism, i.e. a [through] fantasticality that seeks to raise itself through higher reason (g by means of pure intuition) above the reason adhering solely to the use in experience (restricted to it). 2. Superstition: a fantasticality that by means of the (g empirical intuition) experience (one's own or that of others) wants to raise itself above (the use in experience of) reason. (g Among this experience are counted narratives of wondrous effects (which contradict all laws of experience) or wondrous occurrences united through fantasy as cause and effect.)\nThat is mad delusion (g madness), this is stupidity. The first is twofold: either of the delusion of immediate illumination or of extravagant speculation, e.g. of the astral nature-spirits. The supposed derangement.\n*(g The transcendent use of reason is that which ventures to judge without any [possibility] relation of the same to possible experience. Diametrically opposed to this is the disavowal of reason in that which deviates entirely from experience. Consequently disavowal of the use in experience as well as of the transcendent use of reason.)\nUnbelief is the maxim of conceding no other use in experience of reason (g hence no use of reason at all) than with regard to an object of experience. Thus it must either hold everything that |\nis not an object of the senses to be impossible (it can do this only if it regards the senses as the way of cognition through which alone objects are immediately represented as they are) or, contrary to the use in experience of reason and thus to the maxims of its use in general, at least as dispensable and entirely groundless, to assume such a thing, e.g. God [as], who is no object of experience, therefore to regard as nothing or at least (his presupposition) as entirely dispensable and unnecessary for reason. The former unbelief is the empiricist, the second the sophistical or rationalist, which [believes] supposes to explain everything [to be able to] or also to be able to provide all [moral] practical laws with sufficient moving power [thus an influence of the if] and asserts a use in experience of reason, namely its power to bring forth good actions, although it assumes merely objects of experience and no other actual things, but merely makes use of reason.\nBelieving* is he who concedes to his reason (g to assume) what for its completeness, be it in the theoretical or practical use in experience, is indispensably necessary, even though it cannot prove it.\n*(g The small number of believers who from moral interest are capable of a rational belief. They want to have cash: either in cognitions or the advantages of life.)\nThe belief without which it is impossible to make even the use in experience of reason, be it in the theoretical or practical, [complete] self-satisfying, is a pure rational belief.\nWithout a pure rational belief (g the use of reason becomes) either all-knowingness (pansophy) or misology, [self-] suicide of reason.\nTo become superstitious: to this human beings have a* natural propensity; they personify natural causes, do not yet know the lawfulness of nature, nor the importance of the use of reason solely under [the] presupposition of that lawfulness. They are therefore easily brought, when something is unusual or they violently desire some|\nthing (or when they dream), to imagine to themselves something that they do not see, which knows their wishes and can be moved or appeased by them. Superstition without determinate representations of their objects.\n*(g Fanaticism is boastful (presumptuous) ignorance and a convulsive state that is contagious through sympathy.)\nWe can make no other applied use of reason, i.e. one in which the object is given (g immanent use.), than insofar as we can bring forth in thought, according to laws of experience, what we think.\n===NOTES===\n- Erfahrungsgebrauch = \"use in experience\" (rendered thus throughout); Erfahrungs- sowohl als transscendenten Gebrauchs is kept as an elliptical pair, \"the use in experience as well as of the transcendent use\".\n- Phantasterey = \"fantasticality\"; Phantasie = \"fantasy\"; Schwärmerey = \"fanaticism\"; Aberglaube = \"superstition\"; Wahn = \"delusion\"; Wahnwitz = \"mad delusion\"; Tollheit = \"madness\"; Unglaube = \"unbelief\"; Vernunftglaube = \"rational belief\"; Gläubig = \"believing\" (here substantive: \"he who\").\n- Gegenstand = \"object\" throughout; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Begebenheit = \"occurrence\"; Erkentnisart = \"way of cognition\"; Allwisserey rendered \"all-knowingness\"; Grillenfängerey \"whimsy-chasing\" (uncertain, a literal rendering).\n- In the Unglaube sentence the construction after \"oder\" is syntactically broken (zuwieder … anzunehmen … ansehn); I kept it as it stands, so the sentence remains awkward in English; the bracketed words (\"[as]\", \"[believes]\", \"[to be able to]\", etc.) are carried over, with \"[to be able to]\" placed after \"explain everything\", which departs from the German order.\n- \"Der erste\" (\"The first\") and \"Jene … diese\" (\"That … this\") are left unresolved as in the German; \"er\" in the Unglaube sentences is rendered \"it\", but could refer to the Unglaube or to a believer.\n- Fragmentary or doubtful readings kept as printed: \"[Selbstver] Selbstmord\", \"[wenn]\", \"[also einen Einflus des wenn]\", \"es es\" (doubled in the original), \"gringe\", \"et|was\" (split across the line break). The \"*\" markers and \"(g …)\" insertions are Academy apparatus and carried over unchanged.\n- Gnugthuend rendered \"self-satisfying\" (sich selbst gnugthuend); \"einen*\" keeps the asterisk, where the English article is \"a*\".",[]],"reflm:6220":["The delusion of honor on account of posthumous fame after death.\nThe delusion of honor on account of the high esteem of that which has no inner worth.\nEthical perfection, although it cannot be entirely attained, is yet no delusion.\nFanatical and superstitious delusion. God is yet no delusion.\nThe use in experience of reason is the use of reason that [according to the rules] (g its reality) can prove its truth through presentation of its (concepts and) principles in experience, even if one assumes something that is not an object of experience. e.g. soul as incorporeal being or pure morality. If one even merely assumes something of this kind, without being able to prove it, still the assumption of it itself conforms to the use in experience of reason with regard to the form [of the Ver] of its application and of the principles, just as we proceed in general with regard to hypotheses.\nEvery use of reason that does not agree with the principles of the use in experience is delusion; e.g. to sense heavenly influences, to have influence on the realm of spirits.\nAll use of [pure] reason is either a use in experience, whose application to experience is possible, or a [übers] chimerical use of it. The maxim of the latter is delusion.\n[To the] To the former [is] is laid as ground either merely an object of experience or also an object of mere reason, but the application to objects of experience according to rules of the same. The first is the physical, the second the [pure] use in experience of pure reason.\nHe who denies the possibility of the last is unbelieving.\n===NOTES===\n- Wahn = \"delusion\"; Schwärmerisch = \"fanatical\"; abergläubisch = \"superstitious\"; Unglaube/ungläubig = \"unbelief\"/\"unbelieving\".\n- sittlich = \"ethical\", Sittlichkeit = \"morality\" (distinct from moralisch, not occurring here); Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Realität = \"reality\"; Warheit = \"truth\".\n- Erfahrungsgebrauch = \"use in experience\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\" while Principien (Latinate) is also rendered \"principles\", so one English word stands for two German words.\n- \"Das erste ist der physische, das Zweyte der [reine] Erfahrungsgebrauch\": the noun after \"physische\" is not repeated in the German; I left it elided, so \"the physical\" is an adjective without noun. \"der letzte\" and \"der letztere\" are rendered \"the last\" and \"the latter\"; their referents are left as in the German.\n- Doubtful or fragmentary readings kept as printed: \"[der Ver]\", \"[übers]\", \"[Dem] Dem\", \"[wird] wird\", \"[den Regeln nach]\"; Academy marks \"(g …)\" are carried over.",[]],"reflm:6221":["Th 5':\n(g The need of reason to think still more to itself about the things of experience, what cannot be experienced and has reason because of the ends, is the first cause of assuming gods.)\nHow did human beings first arrive at the opinion of the existence of invisible forces, which the usual course of experience could not teach them.\nEither 1. by the path of reason, or 2. of imagination; (g For objects that neither experience nor reason has given are possible merely through imagination;) on the first: a. through proof of reason, b. through belief of reason; on the second: a. through enthusiasm (g reason-intuition), or b. superstition.\nThe first path is not the one that human beings took at the beginning.\nOn the second path the guidance through enthusiasm is also not the first, for that presupposes attempts, also some beginnings of cognition of reason, which, however, do not satisfy the (g reason) imagination. Thus it is imagination without the guiding thread of reason, hence supported by apparent experience, without knowing (g demand) its (g constant) lawfulness (as in which the use of reason in experience consists), i.e. superstition, that first brought invisible forces or also powers onto the track.\n(g Belief of reason is earlier than proof of reason. Both are grounded on the striving of reason toward completeness.)\nThese could have been merely forces of visible things (g objects of experience), which however did not stand under the rule of experience: fetish, manitou, talisman or shades of the dead (g objects of past experiences), or even invisibly ruling beings. The latter took place only in the state of civil constitution under chiefs, and so the proper concept of gods arose. Whether they first represented them as fearful, because misfortune and dangers make superstitious, to which yearning hopes also belong, or the provision of nature as its work represented them as kind, cannot be the question.\nThat these gods were even the originators of nature could not occur to them; they held them themselves for natural things (g or former human beings.), which only had more might, but all natural things for necessary in themselves.\nThese gods had to be persons, otherwise they could not direct their superstitious wishes to them, but powerful without morality. Alone, however, they could not think a god, precisely because being alone seems a misfortune, hence polytheism. Finally it comes to moral concepts: a law and a lawgiver. Unity of God, and now all perfection. — Up to here sheer superstition and now reason. — One was not content with the influence on morality. — Now enthusiasm arose. Neoplatonic sects last as long as proofs of reason alone are supposed to count. — Only belief of reason with consciousness of its ignorance can hold off enthusiasm.\nTh 5:\nCognition of reason is either subjective, i.e. cognition of one's own reason, or objective: cognition of an object (g different from us) through reason. The necessary presup|position\nof a first ground in the series of things subordinated to one another is not a cognition of the necessity of this first in itself. This would be cognition of the absolute necessity of the same, of which we are entirely incapable. It is also not the cognized hypothetical necessity of the thing, but the cognition of the subjective necessity of the *hypothesis* according to principles of reason or rather of the assumption of an absolutely-necessary thing. This necessity of a thing determined through concepts that do not lie merely in pure reason, for the explanation of appearances or practical rules is not knowing, not opining (hypothetical), but believing.",[]],"reflm:6222":["God signifies to crude human beings a being that has reason and has more might than all forces of nature acting at will known in experience. Yet sometimes lifeless things, such as fetish and manitou, have been taken for it. Deceased human beings.",[]],"reflm:6223":["In and beside, perhaps also on Th § 4:\nReason needs very much in order to satisfy itself in the concept of a highest ground of things, above all not merely in pure use, but in what is applied to all experience as well as morals. Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:6224":["On Th § 2:\n[The] Religion is the consideration of moral laws as divine commands.\nMorality teaches how we become good human beings, [theology] religion: how we become human beings well-pleasing to God.\nMorality teaches how we may become worthy of the highest good, religion teaches how we can become partakers of it.",[]],"reflm:6225":["On Th § 2:\nTheology, insofar as it has influence on morals, is (moral) religion; insofar as it [itself a] a particular object of morals contains, is *cultus*. This would presuppose religion.\nFor religion it is enough to believe; for *cultus* one must know, otherwise it is hypocrisy.",[]],"reflm:6226":["On Th § 2:\nMoral religion is that which makes better human beings.\n*Cultus* is the religion that, if it is to be genuine, already presupposes good human beings, because they are supposed to take duty toward God himself to heart.\nThe mere possibility of divine existence is already sufficient for moral religion, yet not so much as belief.\nBelief is absolutely necessary for *cultus*, indeed hardly sufficient.\nFor actual divine service knowledge is required; otherwise one only worships in order to keep oneself safe in all cases, not from conviction.",[]],"reflm:6227":["On Th § 2 conclusion:\nReligion is the moral disposition insofar as it has the cognition of God as ground (not the pragmatic). There is indeed no religion without cognition of God; but this may not at all be knowing; it can be merely a pure idea of God, which is morally correct (although as speculation full of errors), and second contain only the conviction that it is nevertheless possible: there is a God, or moreover a firm belief. For the former morality is not required; but if it is there, it can, in combination with that problematic judgment [zus], give religion. For the latter morally good disposition is already required.\nWhich is the *minimum* of theology that is requisite and sufficient for religion? (s I. (g With regard to existence:) the possibility of assuming one. 2. With regard to the concept: the moral and the metaphysical combined with it. (The ancients had a *cultus* without religion (g moral), consequently also without theology from reason, but tradition.) Philosophical theology cannot extend that *minimum* with regard to the positive of God, but can nevertheless bring it to science through the negative, by which the errors that are unavoidable to common theology are kept off. — Philosophical (g rational) theology is not divinity; the *revelata*, as grounded on ancient documents and ancient languages, is alone divinity.)",[]],"reflm:6228":["On Th § 3:\nDoes our cognition of God have dignity, and can we occupy ourselves with nothing else more worthily? The object has the highest dignity, but our acquaintance with it is very wretched: it consists for the most part only of restrictions of what we are acquainted with, namely insofar as it is only creature. Thus our calling is not to search behind the divine nature, and this cognition is of no worth; but certainly: 1. To close and crown with it our cognition of nature and of morals, in that we consider everything as belonging to the infinite cause, hence in the highest interconnection, likewise represent our will as contained under the universal legislation in the realm of ends. 2. Not to inquire what God is, but: what we ought to be in relation to him, i.e. what the idea of him can benefit us.",[]],"reflm:6229":["On Th § 3 heading:\nSpeculative truth (logical) consists here merely in the possibility of this concept; practical in the necessity of the presupposition of this being with regard to all moral laws.",[]],"reflm:6230":["On Th § 3 »der eigentliche Gottesgelehrte«:\nAre there also divines of natural religion?\nAs for knowing, the philosopher here knows merely his ignorance, i.e. he is acquainted with the bounds of his reason; but to this science also belongs. The bounding of nature in every respect makes the concept of God necessary to us, but, since it goes beyond the concepts of nature, also unattainable as speculation.",[]],"reflm:6231":["In Th § 3 No. 1 Kant inserts after haben:\nthat I think something by it and can give them in examples.",[]],"reflm:6232":["Under Th § 3:\nAmong the Greeks: Thales *physicus*, Anaxagoras *theologus*, Anaximander (*physicus*), Xenophanes (*theologus*).",[]],"reflm:6233":["On »Irrthümer« in the heading of Th § 4:\nThose that are not practical are, insofar, also pardonable.",[]],"reflm:6234":["On Th § 5 beginning:\nThe actual difficulty consists in this, that the concept is transcendent and no property of it can be given in any possible experience, consequently no example can be added to the concepts.\nWe [must] should leave out everything sensible from it; but then nothing remains but concept without corresponding intuition, thus without examples and application *in concreto*, except only according to analogy and *symbola*.\nOn Th § 5 No. 1:\nThe limitations do not hinder us so much as the inclination to overstep them and the conceit of reason and presumption, to introduce false judgments and to pass off incomprehensibility for impossibility.\n===NOTES===\n- Zu Th: 'Th' left as in the Academy locus note; its expansion is not given in the passage.\n- Schranken = limitations; Begriff = concept; Anschauung = intuition; Urteile = judgments.\n- Eigendünkel = conceit, unbegreiflichkeit = incomprehensibility (not in glossary).\n- '[müss] sollen': the bracketed word is a deleted reading, carried over as '[must] should'.",[]],"reflm:6235":["Moral theology has as its principle: If the laws of duty stand firm *a priori*, consequently flow necessarily from practical reason, yet without presupposition of a rational morally perfect being as author of the whole of nature have no force to move the will: then this presupposition is inseparable from practical reason, and the idea of God must be arranged not in accordance with the observation of nature but with the need of morality: In this I am now relieved of all the circumlocution of speculation. I can declare it entirely incapable for this end and demand only that it still cannot prove that there is no God, and have enough in belief. The proposition has no difficulty, but the speculative attainment of it.\n===NOTES===\n- Zweke = end; Beobachtung = observation; Glaube = belief; Kraft = force; Vollkommenen = perfect.\n- Umschweif = circumlocution, Bedürfnis = need, Erlangung = attainment (not in glossary).\n- 'Satz' is rendered 'proposition'; 'Der Satz' may refer to the principle stated at the opening or to the proposition that God is; the German does not say, and the English leaves it open.",[]],"reflm:6236":["In moral theology [can] it is enough to presuppose that it is still possible that a God is, and that no one could ever prove the non-being of the same; hence we are then entitled, to a practical and indeed for the sake of necessary laws [um] to lay this existence at the ground by hypothesis. For these laws are absolutely necessary, but cannot become practical subjectively without that presupposition.\nThe practical indifferentism of theology makes speculative theology the only possible one, and, if the critique of reason is not favorable to it, leads to skeptical atheism. Moral theology, however, permits a theism that can at the same time be critical with regard to speculative theology.\nTheology as principle of virtue serves only to remove from morality the hindrances that could be taken from the objection of an empty ideality. As principle of religion it is, through duty toward a higher being, itself a motive to virtue. As principle of a divine service it is the ground of actions whose effect is directed immediately at God. — A subjectively necessary hypothesis is when I see no other ground of explanation; an objectively necessary one, when I see that no other is possible for human reason.\n===NOTES===\n- First sentence: the construction after 'befugt seyn' ('einer practischen ... [um] ... zum Grunde zu legen') is broken, with a deleted '[um]'; kept as it stands, no noun supplied for 'einer practischen'.\n- Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Bewegungsgrund = motive; Wirkung = effect; Erklärung = explanation; Grund = ground.\n- Gottesdienst = 'divine service' (not in glossary); Einwurf = objection.",[]],"reflm:6237":["The atheism of doubt: skeptical (g which denies all cognition and conviction of God).\nThe dogmatic atheism of assertion (g which denies the existence of God).\nThe critical (with regard to the faculty of pure and speculative reason) deism is moral.\nThe physicotheological deism is in itself indeterminate and a source |\nof all theological errors contrary to religion, polytheism and anthropomorphism.\nIn physicotheology I infer from the contingent interconnection of ends to the existence of God, and this concept can therefore not be determined.\n===NOTES===\n- '(g' marks the Academy sign for a later addition; carried over as it stands.\n- Erkentnis = cognition; Überzeugung = conviction; Behauptung = assertion; Vermögen = faculty; Zusammenhang = interconnection; zufällig = contingent; bestimmt = determined.\n- Daseyn = existence.\n- The sign | is carried over where it stands (mid-sentence, after 'Qvell').",[]],"reflm:6238":["Even if neither reason nor nature proved to me the existence of the most perfect, reason would still need a concept of it, in order merely [zu] to estimate the derived in accordance with it.\n===NOTES===\n- 'blos [zu] das Abgeleitete darnach zu schätzen': the bracketed 'zu' is a deleted reading, carried over as '[zu]'.\n- Vollkommensten = most perfect; Begriff = concept; schätzen = estimate (not in glossary).",[]],"reflm:6239":["The highest concept, (g which) lies at the ground of all things.\n1. as complete concept of a thing in general,\n2. as complete concept for nature as ground of explanation,\n3. as complete concept for the ends of rational beings.\n1. God as the highest being.\n2. God as the highest universal cause.\n3. God as the highest good.\na) For pure speculative reason,\nb) for the empirical use of reason with regard to nature,\nc. for the practical.\nAlways the unconditioned and at the same time complete.\n===NOTES===\n- Begriff = concept; Ding = thing; Wesen = being; Grund = ground; Erklarungsgrund = ground of explanation; Zweke = ends; Gebrauch = use; Unbedingte = unconditioned.\n- The lettering a), b), c. is as in the source.",[]],"reflm:6240":["The belief of a thing and [its] of a being is distinguished from belief in this being. The latter is the presupposition of its existence as highest practical ground, thus in which I take a moral interest.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Glaube eines Dinges' / 'Glaube an dieses Wesen': 'belief of' versus 'belief in' follows the German genitive and 'an'.\n- '[seines]' is a deleted reading.\n- 'an welchem' is rendered 'in which', leaving open what it refers to (ground or being), as the German does.\n- Daseyn = existence; Grund = ground.",[]],"reflm:6241":["First the need of (g pure) reason with regard to (g the concept of) a complete thing in general: to serve all others as foundation and measure. Second the need of reason with regard to an existence: to serve all remaining existence as ground. Third the need of reason with regard to an |\nexistence that is possible only according to concepts, namely of the actually present ends (of nature) or of the merely possible and as such yet at the same time necessary ends (of freedom).\nFor all this reason needs unity. 1. of the [Besti] thoroughgoing determination (g of each thing for itself), 2. of the connection (g thoroughgoing) in a world, 3. of purposiveness.\nThis Refl. has subsequently been greatly altered from the 1. dienen on through additions and deletions, so that it now reads as follows:\nand with regard to an existence: to serve all remaining existence as ground. Second the need of reason with regard to an existence that is possible only according to concepts through nature, namely of the actually present ends (of nature). Third: to serve as principle of that which is possible only according to concepts through [Fr] our freedom* (necessary ends (of freedom)).\n(* g A.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to those (g of the first of the things) that are possible only as *causata*. *Primus Motor*. B.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to (g the first) those that are possible only (g as *caussata*) [through] according to concepts (purposiveness). (g of the first of the causality according to concepts) C.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to that which outside of nature, thus of freedom is possible only [through] according to concepts, but in harmony with nature. )\n===NOTES===\n- Bedürfnis = need; Daseyn = existence; Verknüpfung = connection; durchgängig = thoroughgoing; Bestimmung = determination; Zwekmäßigkeit = purposiveness; Welt = world; Einheit = unity.\n- '[Besti]' and '[Fr]' are broken-off deleted fragments, left untranslated. '[durch]' = '[through]', a deleted reading.\n- The passage is as transmitted, including the editorial remark about the revision, which is translated. In the revised version the placement of 'zum princip zu dienen*' (before the asterisk-marked parenthesis) follows the German order only roughly; the German is difficult here.\n- 'caussata' / 'caussalität' are Kant's spellings; 'causata' is also in the text.",[]],"reflm:6242":["God is a concept (idea) of the first of all beings, which is the highest cause of everything remaining.\n===NOTES===\n- Begrif = concept; Idee = idea; Wesen = being; Ursache = cause.",[]],"reflm:6243":["The last end is moral theology. Thus concept of God must be sufficiently (g determined) for it.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Der letzte Zwek' = 'the last end'; Zwek = end.\n- hinlänglich = 'sufficiently' (not in glossary; the glossary's 'zureichend' is also 'sufficient').\n- 'Also Begrif von Gott' has no article in the German; none supplied.",[]],"reflm:6244":["Minimum of rational theology for the sake of morals, since it makes better human beings.\nThat a God is possible is sufficient for religion, but not for *cultus*; for that presupposes not merely belief, but knowledge. The proposition: one ought to believe, presupposes that he who says it knows what (g he) wants to have believed.\n(g The minimum of rational theology is a concept of God agreeing well with itself and with the need of reason in principles, above all the practical, and the possibility of his existence, consequently the entitlement to assume it (g to opine). The maximum is knowledge, i.e. the (g complete) taking to be true, insofar as it grounds itself on proofs. The middle, [appropriate] to the least capacity and appropriate to the best will, is believing, which is the acknowledgment of the necessity of such a hypothesis either for theoretical or practical own use (theoretical and practical belief).)\n(g For virtue [belongs] is the *minimum* of theology (g sufficient for virtue), namely mere opinion; for fear of God (g religion) *majus*, namely belief; for *cultus* (divine service) the *maximum* of cognition, namely knowledge.)\n===NOTES===\n- Vernunfttheologie = rational theology (literally 'reason-theology'); Glaube = belief; Wissen/wissen = knowledge; Meinung = opinion; Meynen = to opine; Fürwahrhalten (spelled 'Vorwarhalten') = taking to be true; Beweise = proofs; Fähigkeit = capacity; Erkenntnis = cognition.\n- hinreichend = 'sufficient' (as in no. 6243, not in glossary).\n- '[angemessene]' and '[gehört]' are deleted readings; the nested '(g' signs are carried over as they stand, and the first '(g Das Minimum' parenthesis is not closed before the following '(g Meynen)'.\n- Gottesfurcht = fear of God; Cultus/Gottesdienst = 'cultus' / 'divine service'.",[]],"reflm:6245":["Because the concept of the original being is entirely negative, namely that it according to its possibility and existence descends from no other ([and] is not derivative), through which, however, one does not know what it is, here the concept of the original is to lead to a concept thoroughgoingly determined *a priori*.\nBut the concept of an original being is entirely arbitrary if one does not presuppose that something exists; in the latter case, however, it is necessary to assume such a one. But because then some existence is presupposed, the proof is not ontological, but cosmological, but yet transcendental.\nIf the existence of the original being is to be carried out through ontological grounds of proof alone, then the existence must be concluded from mere concepts and not precede *a posteriori*. In the ontological way, therefore, there must be found from mere concepts of possible beings that one which includes existence in itself, and this [is the] seems to be the concept of the *ens realissimum*.\nThis has the peculiarity in itself that through a universal concept the object is at the same time thoroughgoingly determined and insofar similar to the concept of an existing one. Only since outside the concept in its thoroughgoing determination something else, namely no new determination, but the position of the thing in itself outside the concept must come to it, i.e. existence, the thoroughgoing determination cannot yet include existence, and the proposition: *ens realissimum* exists, is no analytic, but synthetic proposition; hence it cannot be found through mere analysis.\n===NOTES===\n- Urwesen = original being; Ursprünglichen = the original; Beweisgründe = grounds of proof; Beweis = proof; durchgängig = thoroughgoing; Bestimmung = determination; Begriff = concept; Satz = proposition.\n- Daseyn and Existenz are both rendered 'existence' (Daseyn in most places; 'Existenz' only in 'i.e. existence' and 'existirt'/'existirenden' forms).\n- 'position' stands for German 'position' (Latinate, cf. positio); setzen would be 'posit'.\n- '[und]' and '[ist der]' are deleted readings; 'scheint' follows the deletion.\n- 'dem Begriffe eines existirenden ähnlich': 'existing one' leaves the noun open as the German does; 'führen' (geführt) is rendered 'carried out'.",[]],"reflm:6246":["But why, if the all of reality is to be laid at the ground of the concepts of all things, precisely all reality in one being and not in many? Because the multiplicity of things as things in general takes place only through the different limitation of the unity, hence of the all of reality, consequently each of these beings is possible only ever through a greater [alone], the greatest therefore alone can contain original (not derivative) possibility. So many beings, which have shadows, presuppose a foreign light, through which they are illuminated; only that which is [no] light without any possible shadow, i.e. the original light, presupposes no foreign one.",[]],"reflm:6247":["It is the question whether the abso|lute\nnecessity of its existence is also required for the concept of the *ens originarium*. Can one not perhaps say: it is neither necessary in itself nor contingent in itself? For both go only to relative position and to the existence of the inhering, i.e. of an incomplete being, which itself is no thing; the subsisting can perhaps indeed be thought according to its non-existence, but non-being can never be something that can befall it, once it is considered as existing, indeed also not its arising.\nAll proofs of the existence of God and of the determination of his concept rest in the end on the transcendental proposition: That the most perfect being is an *ens originarium* (s *possibilitas originaria* and *existentia originaria*) or: that conversely the *ens originarium* is a most perfect being, consequently there can be no more of them, and everything else is also derived from it. The first of these two propositions is the ground of ontotheology, and there existence is inferred from the concept of its properties. The second is the content of cosmotheology (s *theologia naturalis*), and there conversely its concept [to the] is concluded from the existence of something in general. [The physi] Thus all theology is actually built upon the transcendental concept of the *ens realissimum*. Physicotheology makes use of the contingency of the |\nthings of this world (primarily from the ground of their purposiveness), in order to infer a being distinguished from the world, but afterwards pursues the transcendental path, in order to conclude from it the concept and the existence of the most perfect being; so far it has no advantage over transcendental theology. Now, however, it infers from the particular properties of this world to the (g psychological) properties of the highest being as a highest intelligence and transforms deism into a theism. Moral theology adds to the realm of ends as a realm of nature still the concept of a sovereign in the realm of morals and makes of the highest being the highest good.\nIf ontotheology does not succeed, then neither does cosmotheology succeed; if both, i.e. transcendental theology, do not succeed, then neither does physicotheology, consequently knowledge not at all. So there then still remains belief. Cosmotheology gives God as first cause of the contingent and has in this a preference over ontotheology, because it gives an indication of free will (*summa intelligentia*). Physicotheology gives an indication of a will governing the world: *summus imperans*.",[]],"reflm:6248":["The transcendental concept of God is no cognition, because one can posit no object corresponding to it, e.g. There exists a most real being, through mere logical concepts, but not categories, because these require intuitions, to which those can be related.\nThe *omnitudo* in the concept of the *realissimum* is not one and the same with logical universality (one in all); for this is distributive, that, however, collective (many, which make up one), namely synthetic unity. Whether now objective reality pertains to this concept, I cannot guess from my thoughts; I must adduce intuition, e.g. circle. But this is always sensible and is not supposed to be applied to the *ens realissimum*, and another intuition I do not have; therefore I cannot procure objective reality for it, i.e. my thoughts cannot be called cognitions of the object. Reality is that whose representation in itself contains a being; [whether such] what that is supposed to say, I do not even rightly understand, although I understand the logical being in the relation of concepts. I want an example, that is sensation (of the senses); but this cannot serve as an example for God; consequently I cannot give it objective reality.",[]],"reflm:6249":["To assume the existence of the unconditioned [is], reason has an objective ground of cognition. This cognition is objectively certain. But |\nhow is it to be determined. All concepts of possibility besides the *realissimum* are conditioned; for everything is either *realissimum* or *limitatum*, and with us the concept of the *limitatum* is at all times derivative with regard to the *realissimum*. This alone is *conceptus originarius*, namely a *principium cognoscendi non derivativum*, and, what is noteworthy, reason must, in order to represent to itself a possibility according to thoroughgoing determination, thus as complete, conceive the principle of all possibility in the *realissimum*; but on that account the *ens originarium* is not straightaway *principium essendi* of all things.\nOf the *ens originarium* [shall b] it is thought that it is not merely no *derivativum*, but itself *principium* (*non principiatum*). That is its [negative] general concept as of the unconditioned principle. In order to determine it, I must think it as *realissimum*, i.e. a subjective ground, i.e. need of human reason and necessary hypothesis, merely to make the possibility of things thinkable. But if the practical need, through which I am necessitated (g it) to think, is added to this, then a belief comes of it, since theoretical and practical reason agree and nevertheless there is no insight.\nThus 1. the subjective condition of thinking possibilities is taken for the objective condition of the possibility of the things themselves as dependent on an *ens realissimum*.\n2. The concept of the *realissimum* as all-sufficient ground or as a being containing all reality [be] in itself.\n3. The determination of this concept through predicates of intuition (because this is sensible and not adequate to it) is impossible. Thus I can think an *ens realissimum* through logical functions, but through theoretical reason cannot bring it under categories, because these relate merely to intuition.\nThe proposition that the unconditioned must be not only the condition of a |\nkind of cognition, but at the same time of all, follows from this, because otherwise it itself would be limited and thus its possibility would again be conditioned.",[]],"reflm:6250":["On Th § 9:\nWhether it is an invention, to ascend to the concept of a most perfect being.\nWhether a determination belongs to the concept of the most real being, one can never know with reliability. Is it extended or not? There we can only say that extension does not belong to it, because it comprises in itself restrictions, being and non-being, consequently non-being belongs to its [Re] determination. In this, however, we know nothing real. Where we believe to cognize something merely real, e.g. cognition, there |\nit is uncertain whether it does not stand in real conflict with another real, which is still more fruitful, i.e. whether the grounds of it do not cancel one another in the same subject or the consequences reciprocally annihilate.",[]],"reflm:6251":["On Th § 9 heading:\n(g independent:) The original being,\n(g perfect:) highest being,*\n(g all-sufficient:) being of all beings.\nThe word infinite can follow first of all.\n*(g This concept is of such a kind that it thoroughly determines its object, and indeed with regard to that which lies at the ground in all opposed predicates, namely the real.\n*Ens summum est igitur ens entium*, for all manifoldness is that of limitations.\n*ideoque ens summum non est derivativum, sed originarium*. An idea as *prototypon*.)",[]],"reflm:6252":["On Th § 9:\nEither we begin from the *realissimum* or *necessarium* and infer from the former concept to the latter or conversely.",[]],"reflm:6253":["The concept of a negation is a derived concept, for it presupposes that of reality. But it is also not for itself the concept of a thing (because every *ens* must be something real and the negation thus only the determination of a real). It is thus only the limitation, and, since that of a thing already thus *partim* negative presupposes precisely the same sort of limitation, the original concept, on which all thoroughgoing determination |\n(g of a thing as thing in general) rests, is that of an *ens realissimum*. Thoroughgoing determination is an idea, which cannot be carried out *in concreto* (g as *limitati*), but of a thing as thing in general it would be that of a thing as *realissimum*. For the complete is the ground of the possibility of determining everything incomplete [as thing].",[]],"reflm:6254":["The concept of an *ens perfectissimum* contains not so much (g and is also not so determined) as the concept of the all-sufficient being (*omnisufficiens*); for even if no being were sufficient for everything possible, one could nevertheless be a sufficient ground for most of what is possible. Some moral concepts already contain the relation to this *omnitudinem*, without containing a *superlativum*. These are the only wise, holy and blessed, because all three contain [the highest] relation to the highest good for the cognition, for the [feel] will as legislating and for the same as good.",[]],"reflm:6255":["A most perfect being, of whatever kind it may be, is thoroughgoingly determined* through this concept, and if it is thought only according to metaphysical perfection, then it is also thoroughgoingly determined through this concept (although the concepts of place and time cannot be encountered in it). Now we call something, insofar as it is still undetermined through its concept with regard to many predicates, to that extent merely possible; for the actual is thoroughgoingly determined, although not for that reason actual. For although I can say: everything actual is thoroughgoingly determined, I nevertheless cannot say: everything thoroughgoingly determined is actual.\n*(g Hence Plato's ideas are represented as individual beings which, unchangeable and eternal, are the self-subsistent archetypes of things.\nAs a thing in general (*metaphysice*) only one being is thoroughgoingly |\n[deter] determined through its concept. All other things can be considered as if, through the omission of some determinations, they gave all possibilities.)",[]],"reflm:6256":["It is impossible to determine a concept thoroughgoingly; but it is subjectively necessary to represent to oneself the thoroughgoing determination of it as the idea of the completeness of the concept, i.e., to represent the object to oneself without this subjective restriction, and so we think to ourselves every object, insofar as it is posited in itself, namely in the completeness of the determination of the concept of an object in general. If this concept is at the same time that of a real object, i.e., of a thing (g consequently the concept of an object insofar as already more closely determined), then the complete determination of it is the concept of the *ens realissimum*. No other concept offers a rule for determining the object with regard to every given predicate. Thus it is the condition of the application of the principle of thoroughgoing determination to an object thought *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:6257":["On Th § 10:\nWhether one can really think objectively many realities or one without limitations?\nThe greatest reality as ground is at the same time combined with all others. Whoever posits one attribute of God posits all.",[]],"reflm:6258":["On Th § 10:\nWe seek to cognize true reality (g not phenomenal) only by this, that we try whether it could pertain to God.",[]],"reflm:6259":["On Th § 10:\n*realitas phaenomenon* is (g not) pure reality and does not pertain to the *ens realissimum*.\nThe *prototypon* ideal [all] as *logicum*. Everything positive requires the determinations of the understanding of things. For we take only that from the real of our (g possible) experience which belongs to the concept of the understanding.\nDependence alone makes up the certain mark of a *realitas phaenomeni*. Now the question is whether we can ascribe to it even a single one besides the transcendental ones, which are properly only the titles for concepts, or whether all those that we can give *in concreto* can be ascribed only *per analogiam*.",[]],"reflm:6260":["On Th § 10 Remark, proposition 3: »Ein endlicher — Grössen«:\nwere that so, we would not be able to use the concept from our understanding, through elevation to completeness, in order to represent the divine one to ourselves.",[]],"reflm:6261":["On Th § 11, proposition 2: »widerspreche«:\nbut surely: that it could not subsist with it in one subject, namely could follow from a common ground.",[]],"reflm:6262":["On Th § 11:\nAll predicates of God are derived either from his concept as *ens originarium* (g of his independence) or as *realissimum*. The first, which are at the same time those of absolute necessity, are the surest.* (g e.g. simple — immaterial.) Unity from the concept of the *realissimum*.\n*(g for that the *ens originarium* is a *realissimum* is itself only a *hypothesis*, to infer from subjective conditions of reason to objective ones.)\nOneness says that the highest reality cannot be distributed among many things, nor pertain wholly to many things. The first follows from the simplicity of the *originarium*.\nWe indeed cannot see the possibility [of God of the concept of] of God as sum total of all reality [but] (for the possibility of the concept, since realities do not contradict one another, is not enough); [for] but yet we can be certain that no one could make the opposite even comprehensible; for realities that conflict with one another in a being must throughout be *realitates phaenomena*, and those are not in God.",[]],"reflm:6263":["On Th § 11? On Th § 12?\nIt is a great difference whether one regards the *ens realissimum* with regard to all reality as its subject or as ground. According to the latter idea, understanding beings could come from it without its itself having understanding. For the cause does not need to have the quality of the effect. The expression: »God is the highest reality« is the genus, under which those two concepts stand as species.",[]],"reflm:6264":["On Th § 12 beginning:\nThe highest reality: 1. as content of the thing, 2. as ground.\nAll reality and the All of reality [In one] as a single being.\nThe All is the standard of mere reason, which cognizes no limited unity otherwise.",[]],"reflm:6265":["On Th § 12 beginning:\nWhether I should not rather say: a reality in its highest degree?",[]],"reflm:6266":["On Th § 12, Remark 1, proposition 3:\n[The] Infinity is a mathematical concept.\nThe magnitude of the given mathematically infinite is also determined through this concept, but only relative to the human understanding. It is that which in relation to the unity as measure is greater than every number.\nThe metaphysically infinite is that which contains everything, hence the whole possible magnitude of a certain reality.",[]],"reflm:6267":["On Th § 12, Remark 3, concluding sentence: »bestimmbar — finitam«:\n*definitam*",[]],"reflm:6268":["On Th § 13:\ninner infinity: extensive, intensive infinity.\nInfinity in real relation: just as before.",[]],"reflm:6269":["On Th § 13:\nGod as *ens summum*. Deism.\nThe concept of the *ens realissimum* as *ens summum* determines itself thoroughgoingly (g with regard to the (g ontological) predicates), but not that of the *infinitum*.\nBecause all negations are here regarded as limitations, the *ens realissimum* is *ens illimitatum*.\nWhether not every reality, insofar as it is represented (in relation to all possible reality) as absolutely complete, could furnish a central concept of the *ens realissimum*, and the highest understanding, the highest power, the eternal one, the all-blessed one, each particularly [the Go] not make up the complete concept of God.\nIf his existence can be seen *a priori*, then the *ens summum* is a necessary being.\nIf a determinate concept of the absolute necessity of a being is possible, then thereby the existence of the same is also proved.",[]],"reflm:6270":["Probably with reference to Th § 13 Remark 2 No. 4:\nPossibility and actuality are not distinguished in the things in themselves; rather [through] in relation to the concept the thing is merely possible, in relation to a completely determined intuition actual. — If I say: a thing is possible, it means: it can be (but not: because it can be, so it is). (g Our concept of possibility goes |\nmerely to relations; the absolute permits no concept of possibility that would be distinguished from actuality.)\nWe can ask of every thing why it is, because we cannot derive its existence from any concept; but on that account it is not contingent.\n[Possibility] Necessity and contingency are therefore only relations of things to the concept. In themselves things are neither necessary nor contingent; they [are] exist, and their non-being can very well be united with their concept alone; but under the condition of the connection with another existence [it] the non-being of the same is impossible, i.e. they can be regarded as necessary in a conditioned way and at the same time as internally contingent according to concepts. In itself, however, no such separation from the conditions takes place, thus neither conditioned nor unconditioned necessity. For possibility is distinguished from actuality only in relation to concepts.\nOnly with regard to the idea of freedom is possibility distinguished from actuality. Actions as appearances are determined in space and time (the determining ground of actuality), and there actuality is one and the same with possibility, i.e. necessity. By contrast these very same actions are, respectively to the intellectual subject, which determines itself merely according to concepts, free, because in themselves they are nothing and are possible only respectively to the activity according to concepts as ways of representing this activity sensibly; thus the actions as [appearan] determination of the intellectual subject are neither [necessar] sensibly neces|sary\nnor contingent, but not at all sensible and so far free from natural necessity.\nThe absolute possibility of a thing we cannot comprehend, i.e. cognize and have insight into a priori, for that is already itself the insight into possibility, without something being given; but possibility is supposed to be cognized without actuality and before it, and the same is supposed to be synthetic. Synthetic impossibility can never be comprehended, e.g. of a figure, where a synthesis in our sensible intuition [i.e. a] is not laid at the ground beforehand, through which the object is given a priori respectively to our sensibility. Hypothetical possibility can be comprehended if something has already been given as actual and the law of effects and causes. The absolute necessity of a thing, however, is entirely beyond our concept; yet we have insight that possibility goes before actuality only respectively to our concept, and that in itself the one is not separated from the other.",[]],"reflm:6271":["On Th § 14:\nEverything that belongs to its determinations belongs to its essence, for it is thoroughly determined through its concept.\nIts possibility is not distinguished from actuality, thus also the essence not from its nature.\nYet here there is still no talk of existence, but only of the concept of the *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:6272":["On Th § 14 sentence 3-5:\nThe transcendental concepts are pure only then, if one leaves out the conditions of their empirical use and in general all conditions of sensibility, e.g. presence or cause, upon which something follows.",[]],"reflm:6273":["On Th § 14 sentence 3: »ohne Bedenken beilegen«:\nFor thereby we determine only a thing in general, without particularly limiting it and distinguishing it from the most real.",[]],"reflm:6274":["On Th § 14:\nFirst it is a matter of showing what lies in the concept of the *ens realissimum* with regard to the ontological predicates, [as] so far as it is considered according to the content or as ground. Afterwards the existence.\n1. Analytic predicates. Ontological except existence.\n2. Synthetic: first* the absolute, secondly relative as cause of a given world.\n*(g of its absolute or relative existence [of the] in relation to a world. In Spinozism God has no relative existence at all.)",[]],"reflm:6275":["On Th § 14:\n(g The atheist posits no existence: the dogmatic one (denier of God) denies the possibility, the skeptical one (one without gods) all proof of actuality. The former the concept, the latter the ground of proof.)\nThe atheist must nevertheless concede the analytic predicates of God (g for the possibility of such a being one cannot contest).\nThe deist also concedes the synthetic ones, but purely transcendental ones, not the physiological, from fear of anthropomorphism. He therefore also has no moral use of the cognition of God.\nSpinoza was no atheist in the transcendental sense of the understanding, also no deist, for he denied only the cosmotheology.\n[He] His error arose, however, from a false ontology, in that he set the concept of a substance such that there would be only a single one.\n(g One could say with much illusion for Spinozism: if all forces and faculties of a substance created and preserved by God are merely divine actions, if apart from these nothing can be thought by us in them, then one cannot at all have insight how the subject of the same is then to be posited outside God. On the other hand, however, if we perceive in ourselves effect and with regard to other things counter-effect, then again it cannot be seen how we should be accidents, which can never be subjects of acting and suffering.)\nThe [is] deist is it often from modesty, the theist is it (g often) from arrogance.\nThe [reality] relative existence of God as the world-creator, not as the world-soul.\nThe synthetic predicate of absolute existence,\nof relative existence.\nUnity. *conceptus singularis*.\nMany gods as necessary beings would not stand in *commercio*. Manichaeans.\nCosmological argument. We cannot from alteration |\ninfer to contingency, but, if one does not admit the infinite series, only to the necessity of a first, but also cannot make the first action of the same comprehensible.\nOntological argument. From the concept of the *realissimum* the necessity of existence,\nCosmological argument. From the concept of a necessary being to infer its quality as highest reality.\nI infer in the cosmological proof either from the concept of the dependence of everything that is alteration to a first that is without alteration (*primus motor*), or from that of contingency to a necessary, and then I ask: what properties does a necessary being have. The necessity of existence, however, can be derived from no properties at all and is absolutely incomprehensible.",[]],"reflm:6276":["Through the predicate of existence I add nothing to the thing, but rather the thing itself to the concept. I go beyond the concept, therefore, in an existential proposition, not to another predicate than what was thought in the concept, but to the thing itself, precisely with the same, no more, no fewer predicates, only that [it as added] the absolute position over the relative is also thought in addition (*complementum possibilitatis*). The ground of the illusion lies in this, that the concept of the *ens realissimum* contains the *omnimodam determinationem*, but all others leave the object manifoldly undetermined.",[]],"reflm:6277":["At the lower margin under Th § 15, but yet probably still on Th § 14:\nThe whole difficulty of transcendental theology rests on this, that it is not possible to determine the concept of the absolute necessity of a thing, i.e. to say on what its thinkability rests. |\nNecessity of propositions is absolute only if it is analytic; but the absolute necessity of things is a synthetic proposition.*\nThe deduction of this concept yields: that it is a necessary hypothesis.\n*(g one can adduce no case of it, everything is logical necessity. The opposite of no thing contradicts itself.)",[]],"reflm:6278":["According to Mendelssohn God [indeed] cognizes the contingency of all things outside himself even so far as in relation to his nature, thus the thoroughgoing contingency of nature or theoretical [necessity]. Only at the same time the practical necessity of the same through his will as the best, and thus the contingency of existing things is a proof of the existence of an understanding cause, without which they could not exist. His own necessity he cognizes absolutely (without our being able to comprehend it). But since of the way in which possible things can become actual otherwise than through nature we have no other concept than through a will, we lay this concept, which is taken from experience and has only subjective validity, at the ground of things in themselves. If we substitute for the concept of contingency the concept that we can think the opposite, not the conditioned, but the unconditioned, then the argument infers thus: what we cannot think otherwise than so, not perhaps on account of contradiction, but because otherwise no rule of thinking is given to us, that is so necessary, thus we see that everything is merely subjective presuppositions.\nIn the cosmological *argumentum* (*a contingentia mundi*\\*) one would have had to infer the contingency of the things of the world not from the alterations but from the limitations of them; but then one would have had to presuppose [proof] what was to be proved, namely that the *ens realissimum* alone exists necessarily.\nBut if someone assumes that time and all alteration is not a determination of things, but only a particular form of their sensible intuition, then the world could nevertheless be necessary.\n*(g This argument can, [only] if it is conducted from the alterations of the world, serve only to demonstrate that the world cannot be the *ens necessarium*. Otherwise it can be conducted thus: If something exists, then a necessary being exists. *Atqui*. *E.* [The Ne] But no limited being can be necessary (because it is not thoroughly determined through its general concept, consequently it is contingent whether it is limited so far and not less or more); therefore the necessary being is unlimited.\nThe alterations in the world lead after all in the end only to contingency and a first as necessary being. Thus it comes only to this: what properties belong to it, in order to cognize from the concept of such a being at the same time its necessary existence. Or is the possibility of an absolutely necessary being to be derived from some other concept? (It is a synthetic proposition and can therefore not at all be derived from mere concepts). Absolute necessity is a boundary concept, at which we, as everywhere, must necessarily come out at the first, [without] and which can be [can] assumed only for the sake of the consequences, but in itself is not seen into or comprehended.\nTo infer from effects to the existence of the *ens realissimum* as cause proves this only as *realissimum tanquam causa*; but from possibilities of things, which are regarded only as determinations of a single general possibility, namely of the highest being, proves the existence of the *realissimum* as sum total, consequently also, if understanding [is] reality, that it is understanding.\nAll error consists in this, that we hold our way of determining or deriving or dividing concepts for conditions of things in themselves. — One can use Spinozism to overthrow dogmatism. The critical and practical philosopher fears nothing from such enthusiasms.)",[]],"reflm:6279":["If we want to represent to ourselves a thing, whichever it may be, according to a certain concept and to represent this concept completely [so] with everything that also belongs to the thing outside the understanding, then we lay the thoroughgoing determination as the schema of every complete concept at the ground. The concept of a thing in general as determinable according to it or as the mere subject of the determination is merely possible, as congruent with this schema outside the understanding is actual, and, could it be considered through the mere concept also as posited outside the understanding [through], necessary. Contingent is called in a thing everything in regard to which this is undetermined through its concept. Hence [are called the determ- we call] that seems to us necessary which is thoroughly determined through its concept.",[]],"reflm:6280":["On Th § 15? Th 21':\nThe inference *per metabasin* *εισ αλλο γενοσ* is impermissible in logic, where I use what holds of one species of things or of cognition as a |\nprinciple for another species of things or cognitions; but the passage over to another species in inferring can nevertheless be permitted, indeed necessary, namely from objective principles of reason to subjective ones: 1. for the sake of the completion of the grounds of reason in their derivation; 2. for the sake of the separation of the grounds of reason from all determining grounds of sensibility and on account of their independence, to a procedure that is to determine concepts entirely a priori, as in the moral. In such a *metabasis* no probability holds, but also no knowledge, but instead of the former is necessity of the idea, instead of the second: hypothesis, which indeed has analogy with theoretical presuppositions, in that I something, which [I not] neither nor the like similar to it I know nor can represent determinately to myself, yet must presuppose as necessary, in order to obtain completeness or even only security of the empirical use for my use of reason. I step over properly not to another thing, but to another kind of use of reason, and the necessity of the same, so far as it is to be practical, justifies the theoretical. Without morality the hypothesis would always be groundless and [a] the purposiveness in the universe would at most lead to a Spinozism or emanation. But morality has without such a presupposition no prospect of the connection of the objective principle of the will (good) with the subjective (of happiness). To the system of reason (g and freedom) corresponds no system of nature, and thus the moral concept would concern a mere *ens rationis*, which would come to nothing.\nThe existence of a merely happy being without morality has indeed for this being, but not for a mere spectator, some worth. The existence of a merely ethical being without happiness has indeed for a spectator the greatest worth, but not for the subject itself. The worth of existence, however, must be determined |\nand certain objectively as well as subjectively. For I can well say: I must be truthful, even should fortune deny me all favor; but this holds only so far as I am there and live, I exist as a good being. But I do not know why I should be there merely for acting. The very same will (in the idea) that commands me this, why did it command my existence. I cannot agree to that. Thus morality is conditionally necessary, but the condition (my existence) is then not merely con|tingent,\nTh 22': |\nbut impossible for my wish. The will thus agrees in respect of the matter not with that in regard to the form. — A necessary hypothesis of reason, which however in transcendental theology is itself only hypothetically necessary, namely under the subjective condition that I want to explain. Morals gives objective condition.",[]],"reflm:6281":["Morality is clear for itself, if the rational being exists; but its existence itself has for him no worth, if good conduct is not rewarded.",[]],"reflm:6282":["The concept of the unconditionally necessary being is the most unavoidable and yet the most unattainable concept of human speculative reason.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* was only subjectively necessary, that of unconditioned existence is objective. To cognize this through reason means: to think it to oneself as necessary [repres-]. — The necessity of the hypothesis is distinguished from the necessity of insight.\nOne can distinguish the necessity of the thing thus: 1. in that the opposite of being*, or 2. in that the possibility of all other things would thereby be canceled.\n*(g The possibility of the same thing; to have insight into the possibility of an *ens necessarium* means: to prove its actuality.)",[]],"reflm:6283":["[Die Exi] The concept of an absolutely necessary being is a necessary hypothesis of our reason: first in the series of the dependent to the independent (negative concept), second in the series of the parts to the complete (*ens realissimum*), in order to derive everything limited from the unlimited. The latter is only a greater facility, but not subjective necessity. In morals, on the other hand, this hypothesis is necessary for the agreement of nature with freedom, because otherwise the latter is a transcendent concept and thus the laws of it could also be transcendent, thus not subjectively practical.\nThe difficulty of comprehending the absolutely necessary, and yet the (g subjective) necessity of assuming it, is common to the theologian |\nand the atheist; hence the concept of the *ens realissimum*, as that which is least opposed to him, is a permitted hypothesis. — From this follows unchangeability, not so much in the whole of time (because he then remains the same) as that he is not in time: eternity. (Duration) without time. *impassibilitas*. *Solitudo*. Nothing but concepts from absolute necessity. Now those from the highest reality follow. In transcendental theology there is no anthropomorphism, and it is therefore not necessary to attribute anything to God [secund] *per analogiam*.\n(g The warrant, indeed even the subjective necessity of reason, to assume an *ens realissimum* rests on the presupposition, unavoidable even for the atheist, of an *ens necessarium* as independent ground of that which exists. Now such a being must nevertheless have certain properties, and indeed such as are connected with its complete independence and at the same time with its adequacy to be the highest ground of the contingent. We can indeed not give this concept as objectively valid. But in our reason there is a single concept that alone is originary for it with regard to the thoroughgoing determination of a thing and cannot be derived. This is the concept of the *realissimum*. It is moreover a *conceptus singularis* and is suited to complete derivation and at the same time thereby to the affinity of all possibilities. Thus we have [subj] a subjectively sufficient ground for assuming such an existence. This is a rational belief, which objectively is neither certainty nor probability. The rational unbelief has nothing to say against this assumption as a hypothesis. Even the advantage of the unity of reason condemns its maxim.)",[]],"reflm:6284":["The relative predicates of God (g with regard to the existence of other |\nthings) are not at all in Spinozism, if by relation an external relationship is understood. But if an inner *respectus* is also meant by it, namely of the accidents to a substance and conversely, then relative predicates are attributed to God there too. Thus relative predicates of God with the world: either 1. Of inherence: Spinozism. 2. Of dependence or causality according to the system of creation. 3. Of *commercium* according to the theory of the world-soul. The system of causality (g according to substance) is either according to emanation or according to creation. That of causality according to form [des Ursp] either of the coming-into-being of the same or of mere modification of the same (thus mixed cause: matter and God. *fatum*).\nThe relative predicates of God with regard to the state of other things. 1. Preservation. 2. Omnipresence. 3. Cooperation.",[]],"reflm:6285":["On Th § 17 conclusion:\nThe things in space are all bound to the condition of space *a priori*. Were this something in itself, it would also be necessary, and God would also be bound to existence in it. God must |\nbe present to the things in themselves, and so also to the thinking beings, and in them thereby make the idea of space the necessary condition of their outer intuition.",[]],"reflm:6286":["On Th § 19:\nThe deist attributes to the *ens summum* only all reality *in abstracto*, but none *in concreto*. How, now, is the theist to proceed, in order to attribute them *in concreto* to God?\nWe proceed with the choice of the realities *via* (g *tam*) *negationis quam eminentiae*, but in the manner in which we attribute the realities *in concreto* to the highest being, *secundum analogiam*.\n*per analogiam*.\nRealities cannot be thought *in concreto* through mere understanding, but are always affected with conditions of sensibility; first, therefore, I shall *via reductionis* free, as far as possible, the reality from that which belongs to it as *phaenomenon* (*adhaerentibus sensitivis*), for otherwise anthropomorphisms result. But thereupon [das] raise it as *realitas noumenon* (even if all particular determinations *in* |\n*concreto* were to fall away) infinitely *per eminentiam*. (Before the reduction the path of *eminentia* must not be taken; for human perfection too could grow into the infinite, without being different in species.) But because the removal of everything sensitive also removes the concept *in concreto*, which would transform all theism into a mere deism, there remains the path of application according to analogy, according to which I confess not to know how the divine properties are constituted in themselves, but only that they are thought in just the same relationship to the world as human properties to their products.",[]],"reflm:6287":["On Th § 20:\n1. Possibility (g against dogmatic atheism) of the *ens rea*|*lissimum*:\nthat all reality could be contained in a common ground, i.e. the real possibility we do not have insight into, but merely the logical.*\nThis is not directed against skeptical atheism, for it doubts only proofs of actuality.\n*(g whether all perfections can be grafted onto one stem and can spring from an inner principle of one and the same thing, we do not have insight into (though also not the opposite); indeed different perfections can hardly be united in human beings, e.g. great activity, zeal with cautious examination and investigation etc. etc.\nAgainst dogmatic atheism it is sufficient that we show that the impossibility of a highest being cannot be proved, because that would have to arise through the contradiction of this concept with itself; but we nevertheless do not therefore have insight into its possibility.\nAgainst skeptical atheism it is enough to show that thereby not yet every way of arriving at conviction is cut off, |\nbecause that through speculation does not provide it (for so the skeptical atheist concludes) (Ohngötter). Because, even if speculative conviction does not take place, moral conviction is nevertheless possible.\nThe speculative doubter is he who asserts that a thing may not be assumed for the reason that its existence cannot be proved. This certainly holds in all speculative cognition. But the very same, if he is a doubter in every aim with regard to the existence of God, is a skeptical atheist; to this, however, he is by no means entitled, unless he must hold all moral laws to be empty imagination, for then he also does not need to assume a God in a practical aim. He who asserts that virtue rewards itself has no need to assume a God.)",[]],"reflm:6288":["On Th § 21:\n*Ens realissimum est ens originarium*, if it can be proved that all possibility takes place only in and through it.\nExistence from mere concepts.",[]],"reflm:6289":["On Th § 21:\nOne could conclude in the same way that the most perfect of every being exists in its species, because [es nothwendig] existence is also perfection.",[]],"reflm:6290":["On Th § 21: Th 30':\nThat something is actual because it is possible according to a general concept, does not follow. But that something is actual because through its concept it is thoroughgoingly determined among everything possible and is distinguished from everything possible as one, signifies as much as: it is [no longer] merely a general concept, but [the concept] the representation of a single thing thoroughgoingly determined through concepts in relation to everything possible. This relation to everything possible according to the principle of thoroughgoing determination is precisely the same according to concepts of reason as what the Somewhere or Sometime is according to conditions of sensible intuition.* For space and time determine not merely the intuition of a thing, but at the same time its individuality through the relation of the place and of the point of time, because |\n(g bey) space and time possibility cannot be distinguished from actuality, because both together contain all possibility in the appearance [consequently all] in themselves as substrata that must be given beforehand.\nFrom this it follows only that the *ens realissimum* must be given beforehand for the real concept of all possibility, consequently, just as space cannot be thought beforehand as possible, but as given; but not as [something] an object actual in itself, but a mere sensible form, in which alone objects can be intuited, consequently also the *ens realissimum* not as object, but as the mere form of reason, in its thoroughgoing determination, of thinking for itself the distinction of everything possible, consequently as idea, which is actual (subjective), before anything is yet thought as possible; from which, however, it does not at all follow that the object of this idea is actual in itself.\nNevertheless one sees that, in relation to the nature of the human understanding (g and its concepts), a highest being is just as necessary as space and time in relation to the nature of our sensibility and its intuition.\nTh 30:\n*(g Something whose relation (g to everything possible) is determined in absolute space and time is actual. Just so that whose relation to everything possible is determined in the absolute representation of a thing in general is actual. Both belong to the thoroughgoing outer determination with regard to possibility in general and [therefore] thereby also make the thoroughgoing inner determination of an *individuum*.)",[]],"reflm:6291":["Formal ground of possibility — *principium contradictionis*.\nMaterial ground of possibility — *principium omnimodae determinationis*. As sum total.\nThe first concept of the absolute necessity of a being is (g logical, i.e.) this: that [being] its non-being contradicts itself.\nThe 2nd concept of the absolute necessity of a being is metaphysical: that its non-being would cancel the possibility of all things.\nProof of a common ground even of the possibilities of things from the essential purposiveness.",[]],"reflm:6292":["Th 30':\nThe contingent agreement with ends can perhaps, as the atheist wants, also be explained through mere chance. But the necessary agreement with them cannot be thought at all if I assume the essences of things as given independently for themselves; for then it is impossible that they should agree together necessarily to the unity of the end (e.g., to draw geometrical proportions). For this one must necessarily lay at the basis the unity of the being, from which all possibility is derived, in which possibility and actuality are not distinguished; for only then do all agree necessarily with one another to unity, because they descend from such a one.*\nTh 30:\n*(g One must therefore infer not from the concept of the *ens realissimum* to its necessity (inner impossibility of non-being), but from that of absolute necessity (as of a being whose cancellation cancels everything possible, consequently the possibility of an *ens summum* as highest ground, *ens entium*) to the complete reality.)",[]],"reflm:6293":["On Th § 22:\nIf through this proof the objective necessity of the highest being is indeed not seen into, yet the subjective necessity of a hypothesis of it as substratum of all possibility (of the |\nthoroughgoing determination of each thing in general) is seen into in our reason itself in the speculative use, although this use is not in itself necessary.",[]],"reflm:6294":["On Th § 23:\nIf the highest |\nreality of a necessary being could be derived from its concept, then among all possible beings one would be determined as absolutely necessary, and I would not need here to lay experience at the basis.",[]],"reflm:6295":["On Th § 23 heading:\nCosmological",[]],"reflm:6296":["If it is impossible to prove the *necessarium* from the concept of the *realissimum*, then it is still much less possible to prove its properties (realities) from the concept of the *necessarium*.",[]],"reflm:6297":["On Th § 23. Th 34':\nProof from the indication of existing things to an *ens originarium* and from this the inference that an *ens originarium* is also an *ens realissimum*.\nThis proof assumes that an *ens originarium* is an *ens* (g absolutely) *necessarium*, and concludes that the absolute *necessarium* can be no other than the *realissimum*. All of this happens by our first applying the concepts of contingency and necessity to the existence of all things (not merely the conditioned) and therefore thinking an *ens originarium*, whose concept is merely negative, through a positive predicate, namely that of the absolute necessity of its existence.\nIf we separate possibility from actuality, this is a sign that we have not yet thoroughgoingly determined our concept of the thing; for then the object is thought only as something that can be determined in one way or another among all [possible] predicates, with regard to [which] these, however, it must be thoroughgoingly determined if it is to exist. The distinction of possibility [is al] and actuality is therefore no distinction of things, but of concepts. in so far as that which is regarded in every aim as still undetermined makes up the concept of a merely possible thing, but that which is regarded objectively as determined, that of an actual thing. [The *ens origin*] Conditioned is the existence that cannot be cognized a priori otherwise than under the presupposition of a cause; unconditioned: that which can be cognized either a priori, without nevertheless (g being) *rationatum*, or, even if it may not be cognized a priori at all, consequently also not as *caussatum alterius*, nevertheless must itself be presupposed as cause.\nThe cosmological proof presupposes the ontological. For if from something's being an *ens necessarium* it follows that it is also *realissimum* (because this can be only a single being and the concept of the absolute *necessarium* must be thoroughgoingly determining, consequently not many kinds of beings are to be understood under it), then conversely it must also be possible to conclude that the [*absol: necessarium*] *ens realissimum* is an *ens necessarium*, for they are reciprocal concepts. I cannot convert the proposition: all triangles are figures, because the predicate fits more things than triangles. But if I find that the predicate is suited only and solely to this subject, |\nTh 34: |\ne.g.: is enclosed in three sides, then I must be able to convert it. [Now goes the infer] and since the proposition is certain a priori, its converse too must be certain a priori in itself, i.e., in our case the necessity must flow from the concept of the |\n[abs: nec:] *realissimum*. Chiefly, however, the inference goes from the *necessarium* to the *realissimum* by the *necessarium*'s being determinable in no more than one way, consequently only a single one, and this single one must already be distinguished through its concept from all contingent beings.",[]],"reflm:6298":["On Th § 23:\nWe can ask: why anything at all is actual; for possibility we presuppose here as independent of existence. But is the concept of possibility then also independent of all actuality and experience? The logical of possibility, the proposition of contradiction, it is, namely the possibility of the concept. But the possibility of the things themselves demands more, namely synthesis. At the basis of this lies the actual nature of our sensibility and of our understanding, and it goes no further than to objects of possible experience. But even there the possibility, e.g., of a building, where none is there, is nothing but the determination of a concept through various predicates, which, however, is not yet extended to thoroughgoing determination, consequently also not with regard to the relations. The complete possibility we can never have insight into, [but] because we cannot determine a concept thoroughgoingly and in detail *in concreto*. For if we could do this, predicates would belong to it that cannot be taken otherwise than from experience, and whose meaning also consists only in the relation of the faculties of cognition to possible experience.",[]],"reflm:6299":["On Th § 23:\nThe cosmological proof: If something exists, then some- |\nthing exists absolutely necessarily, i.e. it must be impossible that it not exist. If this means as much as: it must contradict itself, then no being is absolutely necessary. If it means as much as: I must presuppose its existence according to laws of reason as necessary (not as necessary), then it is necessity of the *hypothesis* (hypothesis). Synthetic propositions *a priori* (a priori) that relate to no object [de] of possible experience can never have objective necessity. But subjective [necessity] of the presupposition of reason of a *realissimum* (most real [being]) as *substrato* (substratum) of all possibility [bey] in the principle of thoroughgoing determination they may well have, and that is sufficient for us.\nReason always ties one existence to another and can posit nothing for itself; it is thus only the faculty of connections *a priori*, not of cognizing absolute necessity in existence. Nevertheless absolute necessity is an unavoidable presupposition of reason for its totality. Thus it is subjectively, not dogmatically necessary.\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn and Existenz are both rendered 'existence'; here Daseyn, Existenz (once). Gegenstand = object; obiect(ive) = objective.\n- 'nothwendig (nicht als nothwendig)' kept as in the German, with its parenthesis.\n- 'Allein Subiective der Voraussetzung...' is elliptical (no noun after 'subjective'); I added '[necessity]' in brackets only to show the ellipsis; the German has no noun. 'können sie' refers back to the synthetic propositions.\n- Bracketed [de], [bey] are deleted words/fragments; [de] is a fragment, left as is. 'Bestimung' (sic) = Bestimmung, determination.\n- 'knüpft' rendered 'ties' (cf. Verknüpfung = connection).",[]],"reflm:6300":["On Th § 24:\n*Theologia Naturalis* (natural theology): God as cause of nature, thought according to the analogy with natural causes, i.e. psychological predicates.\nPhysicotheology. Spinoza assumes two attributes of the Godhead, extension and thinking: the modification of the former is motion. But he wants the motion not to come from thinking, but immediately from the nature of the being, and then God is no being distinct from the world.\nTo infer from cosmotheology to the intellectuality of the original being would go thus. The world is a series of states succeeding one another. A being can make the first beginning only through freedom; but freedom presupposes understanding, consequently the *ens originarium* (original being) is *summa intelligentia* (highest intelligence). *Primus Motor* (first mover). This proof is better than that *intellectus* (intellect) belongs to the highest reality. — Prevention of anthropomorphisms in theism — *secundum Analogiam* (according to analogy) and *via reductionis* (by way of reduction). Incomprehensible. — Of him no example, not even anything similar.\nLiving God. *Summa intelligentia*. *summa sibisufficientia* (highest self-sufficiency) (g blessedness). *summum Numen* (highest deity) (g will). First only from the physicotheological concept, then from existence.\n===NOTES===\n- Eigenschaft = 'attribute' (not 'property', which is reserved for Beschaffenheit). Daseyn = existence.\n- 'Unbegreiflich' = incomprehensible (begreifen = comprehend). 'Verstand' = understanding; 'intellectus' is Kant's Latin.\n- (g ...) is the Academy's sign for a later addition, kept as is.\n- Spelling 'analogie', 'Beyspiel' etc. are Kant's; English normalized only.",[]],"reflm:6301":["On Th § 24:\nThe emergency aid, in the case of the highest and in itself necessary being, which can be cognized through nothing given in experience (as cognition through appearances), of nevertheless making for oneself some concepts of nature that can have their examples *in concreto* (in the concrete), rests not on its unfathomability, but its incomprehensibility. The first it has in common with all things, the latter is peculiar to all its attributes. We consider its relational attributes only according to analogy, but do not transfer the absolute to it. These relations are thought through |\npure categories; but one cannot say: the *analogon* (analogue) of these world-attributes belongs to it, for this would prove a similarity in the constitution of the thing; we want, however, only to say that, according to our way of representing to ourselves the possibility of things, we must think its attributes according to the same relations as we represent those in the world to ourselves.\n===NOTES===\n- Unergründlichkeit = unfathomability; Unbegreiflichkeit = incomprehensibility. Eigenschaft = attribute; Beschaffenheit = constitution.\n- 'sich ... zu machen' depends on 'Nothhülfe'; the long subject is kept as in the German. 'dieses' and 'denselben' kept without nouns supplied beyond the German.\n- 'in der Sache Beschaffenheit' rendered 'in the constitution of the thing' (Sache and Ding are both 'thing').",[]],"reflm:6302":["On Th § 35:\nThe preservation of the species can either be regarded as entirely natural or requires a supernatural influence. In the first case the origin of the species too would perhaps have to be regarded as natural: for every generation is to be regarded as a new origin insofar as there are so many foreign causes that can modify and alter the formative force that, if there were not a counteracting principle according to universal laws, the regularity in propagation could not be explained from a predisposition once created. Chiefly if one assumes epigenesis.\n===NOTES===\n- Art = species here (Arten). 'Kraft' = force; 'Anlage' = predisposition (not in glossary).\n- 'so fern anzusehen, daß so viel fremde Ursachen seyn' kept in its loose construction.\n- 'wohl' rendered 'perhaps'.",[]],"reflm:6303":["Of moral theology. Here morals is laid at the ground of all theology.\nConscience is 1. the faculty of becoming conscious of the lawfulness or unlawfulness of one's own actions. 2. The inner standing of this judging faculty as a judge, to call us to account for the warrant of our actions.\nThe supreme principle of conscience is: that nothing is permitted to do of which the agent is not completely certain that it is (generally) permitted to do. We can undertake nothing at the risk of acting wrongly.\nA hypothesis, the contesting of which carries with it the danger of doing wrong, (g through) the assumption of which (g we) however can never do wrong, is morally certain, and [ein Geg] the presupposition of it with a view to the confirmation of morality is moral belief. Moral belief is thus not taken from the agreement of our judgment with the object, but with our conscience.\nMatters of belief are those in which the morality of taking to be true is the essential. The existence of God and of the future world are matters of belief of mere reason. With regard to speculation they are of no great importance, nor can they be proved apodictically. But if morality is laid as the ground, they are indispensable hypotheses to set them to work.\nThe guideline of conscience here, with a morally good intention, is not to contrive more conviction than we are capable of, so that we are certain of not doing wrong in this cognition.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Vorwarhaltens' (sic) read as Fürwahrhalten, 'taking to be true'. Existentz/Existenz = existence.\n- 'sie ins Werk zu richten': 'sie' could be 'them' (the hypotheses) or 'it' (morality); I kept 'them' without resolving.\n- 'Glaube' = belief (also 'faith'); Überzeugung = conviction; Absicht = intention.\n- '[ein Geg]' is a deleted fragment, left in German. 'Ansehen' rendered 'standing' (doubtful; could be 'authority' or 'appearance').\n- 'Rechtmäßigkeit/Unrechtmäßigkeit' = lawfulness/unlawfulness; 'Befugnis' = warrant.",[]],"reflm:6304":["On Th § 49 Note 2 »Spinoza«:\nεν και παν.\n===NOTES===\n- Greek as in the edition (ἓν καὶ πᾶν), without accents in the source; not translated.",[]],"reflm:6305":["On Th § 71:\nOf the human-likeness in the expressions, which nevertheless must be understood Θεοπρεπωσ.\nOf the God-likeness in the idea of a perfect human being.\n===NOTES===\n- Greek Θεοπρεπωσ (theoprepōs) left as written. 'Menschenähnlichen' and 'Gottähnlichen' are substantivized adjectives; hyphenated renderings are literal.\n- Ausdruck = expression; Idee = idea.",[]],"reflm:6306":["On Th § 71 Note 1, sentence 2 »als Mittel«:\nnever merely\n===NOTES===\n- 'blos' = 'merely'; the lemma is kept in the German as printed.",[]],"reflm:6307":["On Th § 71:\nOf God as the world-cause. The ideal of humanity in its whole perfection is his first-born son, the reflection of his glory, in him and through him are all things made [he himself is from |\neternity]. Hence he is also called the self-subsisting or original Word* (the ground of becoming). In him merely he loves (g alone) the world, and in relation to him the world-creator is also called father of human beings in his son, i.e. as his *ectypo* (ectype). To bring human beings to this agreement with the ideal of the son, the sanctifying spirit is in him from eternity, which unites the deficient creature with the holiest will through the striving to become similar to the ideal of the son, and through supplementing the deficiency of justice.** He is the judge in us, who holds the holy law before us, judges accordingly, but also supplements that which we lack in justice, through the ideal of humanity, if we are on the way to come ever nearer to it, and brings us in the infinite, uninterrupted progress nearer to the same and at the same time to blessedness.\nIn the [image] consciousness of the first Adam, in whom we all have sinned, and [with] the humble consciousness of the frailty of every (g human) virtue, which can be overthrown by contingent tempting circumstances, however great it may be, and in belief in a second Adam, i.e. a human being who withstands all such temptations, even the hardest through disgrace and suffering, and thereby proves the practicability of what the holy law demands, hence that it is not an empty but a practical ideal: in the ever-progressing [Besse] approximation to this example of perfection in this spirit of humility and at the same time of hope we can become the objects of the love of God in his son thereby, that we are made similar to him and justified with him. Here there are no feelings and super|natural influences nor historical sources of cognition, but mere human reason.\n*(g He is the uncreated, but yet the archetype of the most glorious that can be encountered in creation; the human being who is similar to him, as much as the restrictions to which he as creature is unavoidably subject permit, is united with him in one person.)\n**(g He proceeds from the father and is sent by the son. For because no creature is equal to the ideal of humanity, in that it cannot be holy through creation-in, but through freedom alone: so the holy law has the influence of this law (g in itself) itself on the will of creatures as its aim, the kindness of the father with regard to the son however the will to take up the creature through him into his sonship. Thus a morally [erh] enlivening and sustaining force of God is necessary, to bring the human being nearer to the ideal of humanity, to direct him inwardly accordingly and to ground and confirm belief in the same for the striving of an ever-continuing approximation to it. This spirit is thus the means to bring the object of creation, humanity, [in the] well-pleasing to God into actuality, namely the founding [of the community] of a kingdom of God under laws, a kind administration and a just judgment, and indeed under the only possible paternal one through sonship of the subjects.)\n===NOTES===\n- Words in square brackets are Kant's deletions; whole words are translated within the brackets, fragments ([Besse], [erh]) left in German. '[Bilde]' = 'image', '[mit]' = 'with'.\n- 'Bewustseyn' = consciousness; 'Gerechtigkeit' = justice; 'Gericht' (judgment) is distinct from Urteil, both 'judgment'. 'richtet' (judges accordingly / directs) is ambiguous: 'darnach richtet' could be 'judges us by it' or 'directs us by it'; kept 'judges accordingly'. In the second footnote 'richten' rendered 'direct'.\n- 'Thunlichkeit' = practicability (not in glossary). 'Anerschaffung' rendered literally 'creation-in'; sense doubtful.\n- 'Er' in 'Er ist der Richter in Uns' and the footnote's 'Er' are left without noun. 'Wohlgefällig' placement ('[in der] Gott wohlgefällig') kept ambiguous: 'well-pleasing to God' or 'God, well-pleased'.\n- Footnote markers * and ** follow the source; 'ectypo' is Latin in the German.",[]],"reflm:6308":["On Th § 73:\nIn religion the requirement is objectively orthodoxy (g orthodoxie), subjectively however conscientiousness, i.e. [pure] examined sincerity in the confession of that which is taught as orthodox.\nIf someone, according to his best capacity, [that as] [to] chooses what now seems to him orthodox, he can have religion with complete conscientiousness, and in fact only with pure conscientiousness is there religion. Where now there is no freedom of [choice is] public examination, where either the anticipation with impressed prejudices or compulsion hinders the examination, there is unconscientious religion, i.e. priestcraft [or the submission power de], slavish [of God] or hypocritical submission under the pressure of pious observances. One should not call that religion: it is priestcraft. For re|ligion\nmust be conscientious, and to conscience freedom belongs. One cannot say that he, [so] who even accepts a true religion by compulsion, proceeds conscientiously; for he must know and himself see that something is his duty, and cannot rely on the assurance of others. What he does herein according to moral laws of reason: the effort to establish himself firmly in integrity etc etc., that alone can be credited to him as good; the rest is lost work and moreover risked, hence impure and mere courting of favor. Priestcraft is everywhere one and the same: Catholicism and Protestantism are essentially distinguished. Thus there are arch-Catholic Protestants and probably also Protestant Catholics. If one once assumes [the least] that among what we can do to please God there is something more than a good conduct of life, then there are no bounds.\nIf in one respect the character of the people is corrupted, this extends also to several others. Certain observances still seem necessary for religion-[practice no]-formation, and in this there must be a mechanism not easily altered; but [that it with that which] they must be so constituted that, even if they were unnecessary or grounded on false histories or alleged revelation, they would still at least be morally indifferent [would be] are. But confessions of belief as to their truth are [the] a burden for the conscience. What I [need] merely believe, I cannot [to] (g as true) swear, and to swear as believed by me is indeed for now a warranty, but I cannot swear that I will always believe it, [wei] consequently cannot make myself liable to it, and a sovereign of the land, who from his servants |\nyet not because they do not want to act against conscience takes the office (unless it be that of a teacher), always does wrong.",[]],"reflm:6309":["On Th § 73:\nOn sincerity, probity and integrity.\n(g The opposite of sincerity is impurity, in a higher degree falseness, in the highest unconscientiousness, which is distinguished from wickedness.)\n(g Outward sincerity (g in utterances toward others) is truthfulness, the inner is probity, both together, so far as they [on] examined [are], are secure, integrity.)\n*Integer vitae scelerisqve purus* (blameless in life and pure of crime). This seems to be the easiest and that which is in every human being's [force power] capacity. For it signifies nothing other than: never with consciousness that it is wrong, or with (g the consciousness) of a surmountable ignorance whether it is right, to do something. Nonetheless this is the proper impurity rooted in our nature, which is put off the latest, and everyone seeks to deceive himself and others. Truthfulness is what is demanded first. What is true is often hard to make out; but truthful one can be in all cases, if one only seriously wills. The propensity to illusion, which the form-servants nourish, is the effect and in turn the cause of this corruption of character. Formality is very necessary in serious actions, but the end (g and disposition) must be truthful. At most it can (g then) become pedantry. But to posit formalities as a surrogate of dispositions corrupts the minds in civil transactions, in religion, even |\nin intercourse. Someone can be honest, but for that reason he is not also of probity, i.e. honesty from principles (toward others); integrity is still more, namely sincerity with regard to oneself in the sharpest self-examination. Job. The harm that arises from it for the sciences, if one is not of probity, for religion, if one is not of integrity. What is truth? is a logical question in religion, and there orthodoxy is different. What is truthfulness and integrity? is a practical (moral) question, and there one can easily see that each must follow his reason and conscience. The material of religion is: to be a better human being, the formal: to adopt the manner and way of a disposition devoted to God through actions in which in themselves there is no moral content. Thus formalities as final end or as essence of religion.\nOaths are formalities for awakening the conscience; otherwise, as asseverations, as Christ indicates, they are absurd. But they have great harm, like the signatures of the English clergy under the 39 articles. They make the lie permitted, if only it is not sworn. The first item of education.\nThe *argumenta a tuto* (arguments from safety) (nothing is safe except what is conscientious), *ab vtili* (from utility), used as proofs of truth, corrupt the character. How many a one conceals his own uncertainty and speaks loudly as decided, because he thinks to be useful. To lay into scripture a sense of which one is not certain that it is in it, if this sense is only moral, means: to ascribe to it a good aim, which we are always obligated to do, so long as the opposite is not proved, and which is also the most important. Those who always ask: what God does in order to make them blessed, not: what they must do in order to become partakers of blessedness, always believe thereby, in that they confess and highly praise the former, to do everything on their part in order to become partakers of it, without being worthy of it, thus to acquire heavenly favor through ingratiation, and corrupt the character.\n(g Solemnities in matters of religion are very precarious means. They are formalities as business. Formalities as method are good in the |\nadministration of justice and religion. They must however not displace the realities, are honestly meant.)\nLook of a human being who is not sincere (he squints, staring look).\nFreedom in thinking alone can make sincere human beings, compulsion in religion makes either hypocrites or at least such as, in that they examine nothing, since (g it) helps them to nothing anyway, in that they have no choice, are unconscientious. The very freedom to be sure makes also windbags and frivolous people.",[]],"reflm:6310":["Of the Son of God. Every creature has duties. For all of these it must have a model.\nThis it can find in no creature (g for that is not wholly adequate to any duty, because before it a temptation is still to be found, which overthrows its good will), also not in the creator, for he has no duty, |\nthus only in that which proceeds from God, serves humanity as archetype, so far therefore is human being, but yet can exist only in God, thus [to] is combined with his essence as the Son of God, i.e. in the idea of humanity in the divine understanding. (No one is good but the one God.) that is thus the God-man, and whoever is in progress toward this archetype [is] is taken up by him.\nBut if the creature also has this model: whence does it take the capacity to be adequate to it? from itself he must take it, for otherwise it could not be imputed to him; but since no creature at any point of time can be wholly adequate to it, there must be something that can serve for guidance and give courage in respect of this progress into the infinite [i.e. the God-man]. This confidence the creature also cannot grasp from itself. Thus only in that he consoles himself with the holy, but at the same time also benevolent providence, to the measure that he makes use of his forces, also in progression to be equipped with new (g greater) capacity for the future or else, in the opposite case, to be abandoned by it. That is the sanctifying spirit, the source of moral life, which at the same time judges.",[]],"reflm:6311":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 265-7. Hb. IV S. 502f. Ki. L S. 197f.\nRefutation of problematic idealism.\nOne divides idealism into the problematic (that of Cartesius) and the dogmatic (that of Berkeley). The latter denies the existence of all things except that of the one asserting, the former, on the contrary, says merely that one cannot prove the same. Here we want to restrict ourselves merely to problematic idealism.\nThe problematic idealist admits that we perceive alterations through our inner sense, but he denies that one can therefore infer to the existence of outer objects in space, because the inference from an effect to a determinate cause is not valid. — Alteration of inner sense or inner experience is thus admitted by the idealist, and if one therefore wants to refute him, this cannot happen otherwise than that one shows him that this inner experience, or what is the same, the empirical consciousness of my existence, presupposes outer perception.\nHere one must well distinguish transcendental and empirical consciousness; the former is the consciousness I think and precedes all experience, in that it first makes it possible. This transcendental consciousness, however, supplies us with no cognition of ourselves; for cognition of ourselves is the determination of our existence in |\ntime and if this is to happen, I must affect my inner sense. I think, e.g., about the Godhead and combine with these thoughts transcendental consciousness (for otherwise I would not be able to think), without nevertheless representing myself to myself in time, which would have to happen if I were conscious of this representation through my inner sense. If impressions occur on my inner sense, this presupposes that I affect myself (although it is inexplicable to us how this comes about) and thus the empirical consciousness presupposes the transcendental.\nIn our inner sense our existence is determined in time and thus presupposes the representation of time itself; but in time the representation of alternation is contained; alternation presupposes something persistent, on which it alternates and which makes it that the alternation is perceived. Time itself is indeed persistent, but it cannot be perceived alone, consequently there must be something persistent, on which one can perceive the alternation in time. We ourselves cannot be this persistent, for we are precisely as object of inner sense determined through time; thus the persistent can be posited merely in that which is given through outer sense. Thus possibility of inner experience presupposes reality of outer sense. For supposing one wanted to say that even the representation of the persistent given through outer sense is merely perception given through inner sense, which is represented only through the imagination as given through outer sense, it would nevertheless have to be possible in general (even if not for us) to become conscious of the same as belonging to inner sense; but then the representation of space would be transformed into a representation of time, i.e., it would be possible to represent space to oneself as a time (according to one dimension), |\nwhich contradicts itself. Thus outer sense has reality, because without it inner sense is not possible. — From this it seems to follow that we always cognize our existence in time only in *commercio* (commerce).",[]],"reflm:6312":["S. II:\nBy what do we cognize the being simultaneous of things, since our representations in apprehension nevertheless follow one another? Thereby, that we can apprehend the manifold there forwards and backwards. Since now in inner sense everything is successive, hence nothing can be taken backwards, the ground of the possibility of the latter must lie in the relation of the representations to [a S] something outside us, and indeed, what itself is not in turn mere inner representation, i.e., form of appearance, hence thing in itself. The possibility of this cannot be explained. — Also the representation of the persistent must go to that which contains the ground of the determination of time, but not with regard to succession, for in that there is no persistence; consequently only in the [simultaneous], what is simultaneous, must the persistent lie, or in the intelligible, which contains the ground of appearances.\nThat even the empirical determination of his own existence in time is not possible without the [his] consciousness of his relation to things outside us, constitutes the ground why this is the only possible refutation of idealism.\n––––––––––\nWhether the objects (g outside us) or their representations affect us, (of which the first the reality of the [Materi] outer sense, the second the mere —), [under] one can distinguish thereby. We need space in order to construct time, and thus determine the latter by means of the former. Space, which represents the outer, thus precedes the possibility of the determination of time. Since now with regard to time we are affected only by representations, not by outer things, |\nnothing remains but that we must be conscious of ourselves in the representation of space as affected by outer things. This we cognize not through an inference, but it lies in the way in which we affect ourselves, in order to construct time as mere form of the representation of our inner state, whereby there must nevertheless always still be given to us something else, but not belonging to this inner state (i.e., something outer, [in] of which the construction[jene Zeit s] at the same time contains the intuition of time and lies at its ground).\n––––––––––\nIn order that something be able to seem to be outside us, something must really be outside us, although not constituted in the way in which we have the representation of it, in that other ways of sense could supply other ways of representation of the same thing. For the representation of something outside us could otherwise never come to us in thought, because we are conscious only of our representations as inner determinations and have for the object of them inner sense, which we, however, carefully distinguish from the outer.\n===NOTES===\n- reflm:6311: Daseyn = existence; Wahrnehmung = perception; Schluß/schließen = inference/infer; Bestimmung = determination; Erkenntniß = cognition, erkennen = cognize; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Eindruck = impression; Beharrliche = persistent (substantivized).\n- reflm:6311: Veränderung = alteration, Wechsel = alternation (neither in the glossary; two German words, two English words). *Commercio* is printed in Latin type in the original and is italicized here; the English is added in parentheses.\n- reflm:6311: The opening line (Sch., Hb., Ki. references) is the Academy Edition's apparatus and is carried over unchanged. The sentence beginning 'Denn gesetzt man wollte sagen' is kept as one sentence; the antecedents of 'derselben' and 'welches' are left as in German.\n- reflm:6312: Zugleich seyn = 'being simultaneous'; the substantivized 'Zugleich' = 'simultaneous'. 'Sache an sich' is lower case in the manuscript; Sache is rendered 'thing' as for Ding.\n- reflm:6312: Bracketed words ([einen S], [Zugleich], [sein], [Materi], [unter], [in], [jene Zeit s]) are the Academy's editorial marks, kept in place; the German fragments 'S', 'g', 'Materi' are truncated and left as they stand. 'unter' is rendered 'under' and its sense is uncertain; the sentence 'Ob uns die Gegenstände ... kan man daran unterscheiden' has no verb for the parenthesis and breaks off at '—'.\n- reflm:6312: 'ihr zum Grunde liegt' ('lies at its ground'): 'ihr' may refer to the intuition or to time; left ambiguous. Sinnesarten/Vorstellungsarten = 'ways of sense'/'ways of representation' (Art = way). Auffassung = apprehension; construiren/construction kept as cognates; Spelling variants (Moglichkeit, Bewustseyn, obiect, Wiederlegung) are Kant's own.",[]],"reflm:6313":["P. I:\nAgainst idealism.\nIt can be refuted 1. thereby, that one shows that the representation of outer things must not lie in the imagination, but in an outer sense, because the form of the representation in time, without taking along with it that in space, would make possible no empirical consciousness of his own existence in time, hence no inner experience.\n2ndly thereby, that the matter of the representations in space would be impossible to take place in the mind without an outer sense. For the imagination can procure a representation of the outer only thereby, that it affects the outer sense (in the inside of its organ), and there would be no material for outer representations in the imagination, were there not an outer sense there. Now, however, it is also not demanded that we be able to give of every object of outer senses and its actual|ity\na certain general mark, but it is sufficient to have demonstrated that there is an outer sense.\n3. Because the imagination (g and its product) is itself only object of inner sense, the empirical consciousness (g apprehensio) of this state can contain only succession [the time-cond]. But this itself cannot be represented otherwise than through the persistent, with which that successive is simultaneous, [determined]. This persistent, with which the successive is simultaneous, i.e. space, can now not again be representation of the mere imagination, but must be representation of the sense, because otherwise that abiding would not be in sensibility at all.\nNB. I. The simultaneity [of the representation] of A and B cannot be represented at all without a persistent. For strictly all apprehension is successive. But insofar as the succession can occur not merely forward from A to B, but also (g as often as I like) backward from B to A, it is necessary that A continue to endure. The representations of the senses A and B must therefore have another ground than that in inner sense, [hence] but yet in some sense, hence in the outer sense; consequently there must be objects of the outer senses (and as concerns the dream, this object, which effects the deception about the presence of several outer objects, is the body itself).\nNB. II. Because we thus could not perceive even the succession in us, hence could make no inner experience, if we could not [at the same time] also become empirically conscious of simultaneity, [but this is possible only through the persistent in the object of the representations] but this latter is possible only through an apprehension made forward and backward, which does not take place with regard to the objects of inner sense, so even inner experience can be thought only by means of the relation of our sense to objects outside us. (g Inner sense would otherwise have to represent us ourselves as outside us etc etc.)\nIf our cognition of outer objects had to be a cognition of them (g and of space) as things in themselves, we would |\nnever be able to prove their actuality from our representation of sense of them (g as outside us). For only representations are given to us, the cause of them can now (g either) be in us or also outside us, about which the sense decides nothing. But if the representations of inner sense as well as of the outer are mere representations of things in appearance and the [inner sense] determination of our consciousness for inner sense is itself possible only through representations outside us in space breaks off.\nP. II:\n*verte*. But if the soul is itself only appearance, hence its empirical intuition only the sensible form of the way in which its own subject is affected through the apprehension of the manifold of a given intuition, then this latter must be an intuition other than inner, i.e. an outer one, so that precisely this alone is immediate.\nIn the difference of idealism and dualism one is to distinguish the transcendental consciousness of my existence in general. 2. of my existence in time, consequently only in relation to my own representations, insofar as I determine myself through them. This is the empirical consciousness of myself. 3. The cognition of myself as a being determined in time. This is the empirical cognition. — That the latter can be only [as] the [Dase] of myself as a being existing in a world, and indeed for the sake of empirical consciousness and its possibility, insofar as [it] I am to cognize myself as object, is proved in the following way. — I cannot cognize time as determined beforehand, in order to determine my existence therein (thus only insofar as I, according to the law of causality, [the] connect my alterations at the same time). Now in order to determine that [an de] empirically, something persistent must be given, in whose apprehension I can cognize the succession of my representations and through which the simultaneity of a series, in which each part passes away while the other arises, alone can become a whole, in which I posit my existence.",[]],"reflm:6314":["P. I:\nOn idealism.\nWe cannot represent to ourselves any number [without] except through successive counting in time and then the taking together of this plurality into the unity of a number. This latter, however, cannot happen otherwise than by my placing them next to one another in space; for they must be thought as given simultaneously, i.e. as taken together in one representation, otherwise this many makes up no magnitude (number); but being-simultaneous is not possible to cognize by anything other than that, outside my action of composing, I can apprehend the plurality forward and backward as given (not merely think it). Therefore an intuition, in which the manifold is represented outside one another and [simultaneously represented] next to one another, i.e. the intuition [of space], which makes the representation of space possible, (g in perception) must be given, in order to determine [the existence] my own existence in time, i.e. an existence outside me lies at the ground of the determination of my own existence, i.e. of the empirical consciousness of myself. Therefore [it is just so] I must, just as well as I am conscious (g of my) existence in time, also become conscious of the existence of outer things, although only as appearances, yet as actual things. No one can have inner sense alone, namely for the sake of the cognition of his inner state, and yet idealism asserts this.\nThe composition in the representation of the composite is always merely our own work. How can we now say that the object agrees with it. This agreement can after all (g not consist in the fact that) the quality of the composition [not] similar to the composite, but that one of the two is the other's ground or sequel (the latter it is, if the object is merely appearance). That (g in the representation) which is related to the object of the senses in itself [belongs] is sensation; but there the representation is related merely to the subject (according to its quality), the object is a mere something in general. If I leave out this something (g sensation) and at the same time the composition, then there remains the form of intuition and the object |\nas appearance. (The sensations, related to the object, make the illusion.)\nP. II:\nCritical philosophy brings about this, that we may not ask of what properties God is, in order to know what we have to do in the world, but may only consult in ourselves the voice of reason, which teaches us immediately what we have to do, and[the will] assumes in the supreme cause of our existence such a will, of which those laws of reason are the commands, with which then also all promises for our wishes are, which the same reason in us represents as agreeing with such conduct.\nWe will not learn his will first from [the Be] his revelation, as much in his works as in scripture; for these can be interpreted in many a way, and only that sense which we read into them by virtue of our moral vocation is indubitably the morally correct one, since then those revelations serve to strengthen this in us.\nWe will not need to engage ourselves in combat with the [Zweif] theoretical doubts against those theoretical dogmas of the divine nature and its aims or of the doctrine of immortality. For because we can convict the opponent that he, in order to pronounce negatively on this, understands precisely just as little of these objects as (g others) do affirmatively, we will stand firm in our place, without wavering; for [our] the law of our conduct, the hope of being able to accomplish it, because we ought, the disinterested conjecture of a state of things that on the whole agrees with morality, which before reason is the [wich] highest condition of the possibility of a world according to rules of wisdom, will [a] raise us to the idea of a deity [in] governing the world wisely and at the same time provide this with the properties that strengthen and uphold our morality.",[]],"reflm:6315":["P. I:\nOn idealism.\nExperience is cognition of the objects that are present to the senses. Imagining is intuition even without presence of the object, and [this intu] the object is then called a *phantasma*, which can be a production (fiction) or reproduction (recollection) of an intuition previously had. — The assertion that we can never be certain whether all our supposed outer experience is not mere imagining is idealism. It is thus not precisely an assertion that it is so, but only that we can bring forth no proof of it, consequently [always] (g the reality [of the] of an outer experience taken for such always) can still doubt it.\nThe idealist thus assumes that it is possible that we have [actu] no outer sense, but with regard to outer intuitions only imagination. — Now, however, the Critique proves that this is impossible. For the form of the intuition of inner sense is time, which contains only one dimension of sensible intuition. [All outer objects thus would have to thus with it] So in order that my intuition have three dimensions (g as space contains them in itself), we would have to think this representation of ours (g inner) as located outside us, which contradicts itself. — To take imagining of outer objects for perception (to dream) is indeed possible, but only under the presupposition of an outer sense, i.e. that [it actually] our outer intuition relate itself to objects actually located outside us, because otherwise all these intuitions, being at bottom merely inner, would have the form (g and dimension) of time and not (g that) of space, and this form is not thought, but intuited, i.e. immediately related to an object, even if we do not know what this is in itself, but only how it | appears to us. Were this not so, we would also have no imaginings; for these are only, in respect of form, reproduced intuitions of the senses of outer objects, which indeed can be fictions, but not with regard to their having no outer objects at all. We are ourselves beforehand an object of outer sense, for otherwise we would not perceive our place in the world and be able to intuit ourselves in relation with other things. — Hence the soul, as object of inner sense, cannot perceive its place in the body, but it is in the place in which the human being is. — Leibniz's *harmonia praestabilita* necessarily carries idealism with it: because, there, each of the two subjects is for itself, without the influence of the other, in the play of alterations, one of them is wholly unnecessary for the determination of the existence and the state of the other. — But the inner alterations too cannot be comprehended, as to their possibility, without something outer that contains the ground.\nP. II:\n# vid. P. 2. C. below.\nNevertheless this doubt, which when it is presented dogmatically one calls idealism, besides being a stumbling block for metaphysics, of which one expects that it, which promises so many still unopened prospects, could yet have an objection against a matter laid so near to us: so it can also well furnish a hindrance to advancing to that which constitutes the final end of metaphysics, the supersensible, if everything sensible is posited merely in us.\nWe have two kinds of intuition: intuition of the senses, [and which the] for which the object must be represented as present, and imagining as intuition without presence of the object. Imagining, if one is conscious of it as such [and], can also be considered as [intu] (g inner) intuition of the senses.\nNow the problem is: whether [and how] intuition of the senses can be distinguished from imagining (g of outer objects); the idealist denies |\n[the former consequently also and so falls the] this, without doubt on the ground that we immediately perceive our representations (g as inner determinations of the mind) only through inner sense, but not their cause, which we only infer, the inference, however, from an effect to a determinate cause is not certain, in that to one and the same effect more than one cause can be, as here (g either) the outer object or the subject itself can be the cause, which latter intuition would then be imagining. Of the latter dreaming or also madness is the example, from which, then, the [sense] outer representation of sense as such could not be distinguished.\nNow I say: outer intuition of the senses is distinguished with complete certainty from the inner by the mere form of the former, space, in which we posit outer objects, in contrast to the other, whose object we only in time breaks off.\nIf we cannot distinguish them immediately, then it will also not happen mediately through inferences to their cause, for [if we]. I myself am an object of my outer intuition in space and without that could not know my position in the world. Hence the soul cannot know its place in the body, because it would have to perceive itself through outer [place] sense, thus as outside itself.\nMy representations cannot be outside me and an outer object of representations cannot be in me, for that would be a contradiction. But although the representation is in me, the object of it can without contradiction be outside me or also [the object] the representation together with the object be in me. According to idealism it is asserted that it is not possible to distinguish whether [not] together with the representation [of a] the object of it is at the same time in me, even if this is represented (g in the intuition) as existing outside me. — Against this the realist of outer intuition asserts that this is possible, and indeed rightly, on the following ground. [That my representation cannot exist outside me (g the subject) What I to myself] What I represent to myself as spa|tial cannot be counted among the representations of inner sense, for its form is time, which has only one dimension. Likewise what is mere representation I cannot make into the object of outer sense, for its form is space. — Now the question arises: whether [the its f] that intuition which has the form of outer sense, as imagining is (in dreams or in fever), is so one and the same with that which also has an object of outer sense, that both cannot be distinguished from one another. The answer is: that in this state of imagining they indeed cannot be distinguished, for this is a deception of the power of judgment; but the question is properly: whether they cannot be distinguished at all, i.e. whether one cannot become conscious that the one is an intuition of the senses, the other indeed sensible intuition, but only in imagining, for which the object outside the representation is not present. The answer is: consciousness can accompany all representations, hence also those of imagining, that and whose play is itself an object of inner sense and of which it must be possible to become conscious as such, because we actually distinguish such as inner representations, hence existing in time, from intuition of the senses.",[]],"reflm:6316":["P. I:\nAgainst idealism.\n1.) That the ideality of space and time, which is merely formal, does not contain the real idealism, which alleges that to the perception of things in space no object at all outside the representation is given. but that to this object [because] or to these outer objects (which remains undecided) only the same form of space does not belong in itself, under which we intuit it or them, because it belongs merely to the |\nsubjective way of our faculty of representation in perception, which can be concluded [from the] from this, because space contains nothing in itself that could be representation of a thing or of the relation (g of different ones) of the same to one another in itself, and, if it is considered as such a determination, [to the *entibus*] as *ens imaginarium* is a *non ens*.\n2.) That (g the representation) of the object outside us in space is not illusion, i.e. seems merely to be something outside us, but is outer, not merely inner perception, that (g it) however seems to us to contain this form of space in itself, because we cannot cognize otherwise than through inferences that it is merely appearance.\n3.) That this form of things in the appearance is sufficiently distinguished from every other, which contains that which adheres not to the objects outside us, but merely to our way of representing, by this, that through it we can determine the appearances a priori, which we cannot say in the case of a tone, in distinction from sound, in the case of heat, in distinction from the perception of [the] a [the] fluid penetrating all other matter and expanding it, consequently the form of the appearance concerns (g outer) sense in general and not certain particular ways of sensing and (g immediately) perceiving.\n4.) That therefore with regard to this universal the sense can (g and also must) be distinguished from the imagination through a correct inference, [because] unless the objection, taken from the possibility of this confusion in particular representations of the imagination in madness or in dreams, is to count as a proof that we perhaps might even exchange these faculties (of sense and of imagination) themselves, and indeed without means of getting out of this ambiguity: that in the realism of outer sense nothing else is asserted than that no fancy itself could [perhaps] make things in general representable to us as objects of the senses outside us as such, were there not actually such a sense, consequently that we distinguish this as a faculty different from the imagination, though not through sensation alone, yet through a certain inference |\nand that something outside ourselves lies at the ground of the fancy (even if (g it) is not [the] contained in the now given (g supposed) perception).\nIn the margin next to No. 4:\nIf we cognize a law a priori, then we prescribe this law to the object. to nature, if it is a law of nature, to freedom, i.e. to ourselves, if it is a moral law, but not arbitrarily, rather as necessary.",[]],"reflm:6317":["P. I:\nN. I. |\nOn the Critique with regard\nto Theology.\nIn order to prove that it is unavoidable for reason to assume an existence of God, and indeed according to a concept that is sufficient for the theoretical as well as practical use of our reason, insofar as it proceeds to the last principles *a priori*, I had to prove that speculative reason can neither give his concept in agreement with itself nor [prove] such an existence or even demonstrate the reality of this concept. — For had I conceded the latter, I would (g either) have had to come to the use of reason with regard to the objects of experience and, since I would have had to hold these for things in themselves, I would first have run into antinomies, in which all speculative reason founders, and finally I would have sensified and anthropomorphized the divine being; or I would have had to hold everything for appearances and seek the deity only among things in themselves through pure ontological concepts, where then no cognition at all would have remained for me. I therefore had to demonstrate the incapacity of the |\nmerely theoretical use of reason in this, whereby it still remained that it did not contradict the concept of God and his existence, instead of which otherwise entirely false concepts of God and in the end the impossibility of thinking such a being would have come out.\nOn the possibility of cognition *a priori*.\nIt is asked whether, if the causal combination were not given to me in experience, I would have to have insight into it *a priori*? If a manifold existence follows one upon another, then the time in which it follows must also be perceived. But this is possible only if something is there with which all this manifold, which succeeds among itself, nevertheless is at the same time.\nOn the way of proof in relation to possible experience.\nIf the forms of things according to their general properties as condition of the possibility of these things are [given] given *a priori*, then I can cognize immediately what these things themselves are with regard to the categories, e.g. of magnitude. But if I want to cognize things in general, not merely their form, through categories, then I can cognize this *a priori* only in relation to possible experience [proof]. e.g. That all things have a magnitude can be proved only so, that it is demonstrated that only through their being given in space and time can they become objects of experience, but under this condition their *apprehension* is always a magnitude, hence also the object.\nLaw of continuity.\nBetween a and –a (attraction and repulsion e.g. in the magnetized rod) there is a point where the predicate of the thing vanishes, becomes =0. Thus an acute as well as an obtuse angle can be held for a right one that is only infinitely little smaller or greater; but an acute cannot count for an obtuse. Usefulness can be regarded as an indifferent matter if it is very |\nsmall, but not as harmfulness. Hence it is impossible that virtue be the mean between two vices (opposed to each other in degree). For the vanishing vice is innocence.\nOn the occasion of the Critique.\nWe have cognitions *a priori*, which we extend without experience: it is asked whether we can also extend them beyond experience (g and its objects). If we attempt the latter, then we cannot discover our errors through experience, [because] and possibilities of thinking something always remain without reality too. Further: we cannot, even with the propositions that seem necessary, distinguish the objective necessity of the propositions from the subjective necessity of thinking the objects thus and not otherwise in accordance with the particular nature of our powers of cognition with regard to given sequels, without a critique of reason. — But nothing depends on all of this; for if we have erred, this is merely the missing of an aim at speculative cognition, by which we wanted to extend ourselves beyond the boundary of possible experience. We can at most err if we say: the objects are actually so, e.g. the supreme original ground of things an intelligent being, because according to the constitution of our faculty of cognition we cannot make the *phaenomena* comprehensible to ourselves otherwise; but that is of no detrimental sequels. — But there is in us a property or reason attributes it to us as practical beings, which (g are) entirely distinguished from the natural properties and their laws, entirely from natural laws, indeed even are contradicted by these: freedom and under this concept the law of morality in our independence from nature. Here we now have two laws certain *a priori*, which contradict one another in the same subject, if I represent to myself the subject according to the theoretical principles in the same meaning. Hereby I am necessitated to think my own subject as object of the senses and (g at the same time) of reason as existing in two different ways: 1. as object of the senses, 2. as a being [which] insofar as it is not at all an object of the senses. This necessitates me to a critique of sensibility. But that too would accomplish nothing, were empiricism and predeterminism not contrary to all morality. |\nThus morality runs danger through speculative reason without critique. But even here the power of the moral disposition could outweigh speculation. But the same practical reason necessitates us to assume those laws as divine commands, because otherwise they would be without the lawful effect and the course of nature as principle of practical laws, insofar as they went to one's own happiness, would not correspond at all to the course of nature in relation to moral ones. Thus I must think a God to myself and assume him, but I cannot prove his existence and cannot comprehend him.\nP. II:\nNow it becomes interesting not to make the conditions of the cognition of things possible for us into conditions of the possibility of the things; for if we do this, freedom is abolished (g and) immortality, and we can get no other than contradictory concepts of God. This now necessitates determining exactly the possibility, the domain and the boundaries of our speculative faculty of cognition, so that Epicurean philosophy does not take possession of the whole field of reason and ruin morals and religion, or at least not make human beings inconsequent.\nMoreover, space and time are such necessary determinations *a priori* of the existence of things that they would have to be made not only, together with all the sequels attached to them [of the limitedness], conditions of the [God] existence of the deity, but, on account of their infinity, absolute necessity and necessity, even into divine properties, were they determinations of things in themselves. For once one has made them into this, there is no ground why one should restrict them merely to finite beings. Theology, so that it not contradict itself, sees itself necessitated to make both only into the form of our sensibility and to lay at the basis of all things that can be cognized by us, as *phaenomena*, *noumena*, which we do not know, but with regard to which the unconditioned alone obtains. Since now the conflict between the principles of the unconditioned in synthesis and the principles of the conditioned in space and time [schle], hence the antinomy of\n|\nreason, cannot at all be settled without making this distinction among the objects and their representations, theology leads to the aesthetic critique.\nWith regard to the theoretical cognition of the objects of possible experience the critique nonetheless has the use that it resolves the antinomy between the principle of the unconditioned according to mere concepts and of the always conditioned according to conditions of intuition, by showing that this, however pure it may be, is always only sensible and represents the object not as thing in itself, but merely in appearance [incidentally], e.g. antinomy of the beginning of the world, of the world-whole with regard to space, of absolute and unconditioned causality and of the unconditioned necessity [or Zuf] of things. Meanwhile it is nonetheless necessary against skepticism, which aims precisely to rob reason of trust in itself through contradictions among the clearest convictions of reason — idealism is indeed not so dangerous, but it nevertheless restricts the field of experience very much and gives a belief claim even to our empirical cognitions. — But if it is shown that the determination of our own existence in time presupposes the representation of a space, in order also to be able to represent the relation of the determinations of inner intuition to the abiding object, and we space, which is merely a form of intuition, yet cannot be the form of inner intuition, precisely because the inner is not space but time, then reality (as things in themselves) can be secured to the outer objects (g the) precisely by one's not assuming their intuition as that of a thing in itself; for if it were this and the form of space the form of a thing, (g which) even without the particular constitution of our subject adhered to it in itself, then it would be possible that we had the representation of such a thing even without its existing. But if it is a particular way of intuition in us, which cannot represent that which is in us, hence what exists in the change of time, because it can then as mere representation be thought only in |\ntime relations, then such an intuition must subsist in actual relation to an object outside us and space signifies actually something that, represented in this form of intuition, is possible only through relation to an actual thing outside us. — Thus refutation of skepticism. idealism. Spinozism. likewise also of materialism, predeterminism.\n|––––––––––\n[Since] Some judge that it is difficult to make comprehensible the content of the critique and by means of it also of metaphysics (g together with its final aim) from a single point of view, moreover [probably] also even by those who (g are fair enough) not (g to) mistake its good intention, hold it for an entirely useless *chicane* with the [hitherto] merely speculative proofs of reason [of God] of the most sublime [belief] ideas, on which the reassurance of human beings rests, since in the end the critique nevertheless leads to just the same, though only as matters of belief of pure reason, whereby still that which one apostle says to the other, that namely things [are] encountered therein which are too high for many and confuse the simple. [To that end] As for the first, the very ease of surveying the whole plan is a strong recommendation of this system and a confirmation of the unity of its principle, and, as for the confusion, such a critique is not drawn up to be presented to the simple, but to the most subtle rationalizers, who opined no matter to be too high [thought] for them. Since then this critique is to prove precisely that these are much too high for them [wirkl] and everyone [for the S] for speculative insight, and to confuse them in the delusion of the latter (since it is their own fault to persist therein), [is the preparation] in order to condescend to the same low step of which all human beings are capable, for which |\nthose matters of belief must be just as accessible as to the most subtle investigation.\nThere are three supersensible objects with which human reason has been unceasingly and at all times occupied and will remain: God, immortality and freedom. Of the last alone we have an immediate conviction of its actuality, without being able nonetheless to [geh] have insight into it. It is natural to proceed from this, in order to assess accordingly our possible cognition of the rest.",[]],"reflm:6318":["On psyschologie. We cannot cognize the nature of the soul as of a spirit, consequently also not immortality, because we can never free it from corporeal influence. — We cannot cognize God as *realissimum*, because we can attribute to him neither understanding nor will, without transferring our limitation to him.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* is *conceptus originarius*, because the negations presuppose reality and are removals of the same, thus derivative. These negations also can only be represented as limitations of the *realissimum*. Hence the representation of the highest reality as the |\nmatter of all things, so that all evils are supposed to stem only from the form, which consists in negations. — But from the necessity of presupposing that concept it does not follow that a *realissimum* is possible.",[]],"reflm:6319":["R II 254f\nS. I:\nIdealism.\nA specifically different imagination must have a different sense lying at its ground, for the imagination is only an inner determination of the sense to the same intuition that it has as sense.\nOn the Critique of Pure Reason.\nThe two difficulties in it consist in this, that it be shown that it does not contradict itself: I. The soul cognizes itself theoretically only as *phaenomenon*, consequently it cognizes itself, but only as appearance. — The resolution is this: it does not cognize itself through concepts, which are merely the simple actions of synthesis, which belong to cognition in general, namely not through the consciousness of these concepts, for that would be a contradiction, since it is supposed to cognize itself as object, but only by means of the application of the same to inner intuition. But time it cannot determine in itself without the representation of space and the product in the same through the imagination. Space, however, lies in its outer sense, which the imagination must affect in a certain way, and through this the inner sense too, with regard to the inherence of this representation, even the feeling of pleasure etc etc., is affected. But also the empirical consciousness of the representations of reason or also of the categories and of thinking in general still always belongs to appearance, because it is occurrence, and nothing intellectual remains except the I — but practically freedom together with its law as cognition.\nII. How we can speak of the intelligible, e.g. God, through categories, notwithstanding that these are valid only for *phaenomena*, in order to yield cognition, thus of a being that cannot at all be represented as *phaenomenon*.\nS. II:\nOf the soul in the birth, the life and the death of the human being. About which we have no experience, thus would have to either conclude from experience or prove it a priori from the mere faculty of thinking in life or from freedom as presupposition for the practical [Verhalt] use of reason. But since the first would always be concluded from the sensible and the latter merely from the supersensible, which is given to us breaks off?\nThe identity of the person concerns the intelligible subject amid all diversity of empirical consciousness. The latter can be greatly altered. But insofar as it remains interconnected, it is the cognition of itself as the same person and is required for imputation.",[]],"reflm:6320":["S. I:\nOn the cosmological proof.\nIt is grounded on this, that (g the concept of) a necessary being is a single concept, just as that of the *ens realissimum*; hence these concepts meet and must also meet, if it is true that the concept of a necessary being is not the concept of a species of things, but of a single one and thoroughly determined. The proof is supposed to be this: that, if there were several, then [they on] one necessary being would be determined in a certain way, another in another, i.e. opposite way, so the first too could, without prejudice to its necessity, be de|termined\nin the opposite way, i.e. the opposite of it would be possible, so the thing would be contingent and yet necessary, which contradicts itself. — But from the [other way] possibility of being determined in another way, only a contradiction with the concept of the necessary would follow, if [it is presupposed that the thoroughgoing determination must follow from the concept or the necessary existence (which at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination) is an existence which can already be concluded from the concept of a thing. But no such concept is possible from which existence can be concluded (hence the concept of a necessary being is also a merely problematic concept)] the very same being were thought as determinable in another way. For that one necessary being is determined in a certain way, another necessary one in another way, proves only that the necessary being is not at all determined through this its concept [be] as to what it is, for existence is no particular determination of it.\nS. II:\n1.\nïï [If] The most perfect being of all must necessarily (g i.e.) exist on account of this concept; for if it did not exist, a perfection, namely existence, would be lacking to it.\n2.\nïï The necessary being must* contain all perfection. For if it did not contain them all (already through its concept), it would through this concept be undetermined with regard to one or another predicate, consequently that it is (g as) a [as] such one and not (g as) another, contingent, i.e. it would not be necessary.\n*(g through this mere concept already as the most perfect be cognized; |\nS. III: |\nfor if it were not as being in general determined through all reality, [so it would be in An] but also only with regard to one undetermined, then [it its opposite], as it is, its opposite would be possible, so it would also not be necessary. That does not follow, however, for a necessary being with a and also one with non a would be possible; i.e. not the opposite of the existence of this being, but of its predicates, without prejudice to the existence of the subject, would be possible. — In the Cartesian proof one made existence into a |\nparticular predicate, — in the cosmological one makes existence into the subject. In both the concept of an all of reality is always laid at the ground as the only concept that a thing[in its] as thoroughly determined a priori, and hence as the only non-derivative concept.)\nS. II:\nIf even the best thinker hears this proof, he may well, as long as he has it in thought, think to feel [a] conviction; but if he has only forgotten the words, he is wholly empty, as if he had heard nothing. He must orient himself through healthy reason.\nBoth must be analytic judgments, although the first is evidently synthetic. But if the second were analytic, then the all of perfection would be derived from the concept of necessary existence, since this, however, as mere being contains no concept of the subject of the judgment, consequently, just as in the first proposition the synthetic predicate is lacking to the subject [the], so here the concept of a subject as a thing of particular quality would be lacking to the predicate and merely being be taken for it.\nS. IV:\nCosmological proof.\nThe proposition that the necessary being must have all reality is conducted synthetically, although it can only be conducted analytically. It namely does not say: a necessary being has in its concept all reality, but: if we want to make a concept of it, then we must think it under the conditions under which only a single being can stand. Now, however, the first (to make a concept of it) is impossible; for that could be none other than that a being of certain determinations would be thought in such a way that according to these its non-being would be impossible, i.e. would be self-contradictory.",[]],"reflm:6321":["*Ens necessarium*.\n(g Its *conceptus* is not generic, but *singularis*.)\nOne says: The concept of an *ens necessarium* is only *conceptus singularis** (because this must through its concept be thought as thoroughly determined). Now, however, it does not follow that, if a thing also exists necessarily, this existence must also be able to be derived from its concept (rather, from the *omnitudine realitatis* existence can never be concluded), and it would thereby also not become contingent, if besides it still several things *partim realia, partim negativa* were assumed as necessarily existing.\n*(g i.e. there cannot be necessary beings internally differing from one another.)\nA being that is already thoroughly determined through its concept (g identical proposition) (can exist only in one way, if it exists), contains all reality (as aggregate). Granted. But not: give me such a concept, that carries absolute necessity with it. You can name only one of which it is problematic whether such a thing is even possible.",[]],"reflm:6322":["P. I:\nfrom transcendental *contingentia* — in contrast to the natural.\nThat an absolutely necessary being must contain all reality would have to be an analytic judgment, because it was drawn from the concept of the absolutely necessary. Thus the predicate is identical with the concept of the subject, but not conversely; for otherwise they would have to be *conceptus reciproci*. [Now are] Thus it is not the concept of all predicates that belong to the *necessarium*. Thus the concept of the object was not one that contained the thoroughgoing determination [because existence was not among them. — If the concepts were not wholly identical, then some most real beings would not be necessary beings — Thus there would be more than one *realissimum*], which yet was demanded in the proposition [contained], that the necessary being could be determined only in one way; for the concept of the whole of reality does not, according to this admis|sion,\ncontain the thoroughgoing *determinatio*. Thus the proposition that [a being] from absolute necessity could indeed be inferred to thoroughgoing reality, but not conversely from this, is a *contradictio in adiecto*.\nRather one must say: the concept of the *realissimum* is a transcendental original concept, i.e. one can [after] with transcendental concepts fabricate no other things than by laying the *realissimum* at the ground, [there] which contains as it were the material of all things, and now posits the manifoldness only in the form, consequently thinks all other concepts as derived, but, to be well understood, through mere ontological concepts.\nThe analytic *oppositum* of a is =0, the synthetic\n=a–a=0; the aim is to designate these two ways of *remotion*.\n––––––––––\nThe proposition: an existing being must at the same time be represented as a thoroughgoingly determined being, i.e. it is thoroughgoingly determined, is an analytic proposition. — But the proposition: (g any) existing being must be able to be thoroughgoingly determined through the concept of its existence, is not only a synthetic proposition, because it goes [still] beyond the concept of existence, which contains the thoroughgoing existence, and says that the thoroughgoing determination of the same as of a single being is possible through the mere concept of its (necessary) existence, but also a false proposition; for from the (g mere) concept of [mere] existence this concept of a thing cannot be determined at all, even if I assume it as necessary: rather the proposition wants only to say this much: if I were to be able to cognize its absolute necessity, I would have to cognize (g it) from the mere concept of it, which [sch] latter is absolutely impossible. — The second proposition goes thus: [if I] I can cognize the thoroughgoing determination through the concept of it as an All of reality. [But that is already again refu] But thereby I do not cognize the existence of this thing. [For] That is already refuted: for if I could do this, I would need no other proof than the Cartesian one.\nThe proposition: an existing being must be represented as thoroughgoingly determined through the concept of its |\nexistence; but from this it does not follow that, if it is also thought as necessarily existing, it could be thoroughgoingly determined through this concept, rather [is] the concept of existence leaves the determination of the thing, i.e. with which predicates it exists, wholly undetermined, except only that something real is in it, because otherwise it would be no thing.\nThe concept of an *ens realissimum* is a *conceptus singularis*: it [is] contains nothing that can be common to different things.\nThe cosmological proof goes thus:\nThe necessary being must be a most real thing. For (g as necessary being it must be determined through its concept with regard to existence, hence as most real) were it not such, it would not be thoroughgoingly determined through [the] its concept [of a thing] [(because the concept of the most real is the only one that at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination in itself)], thus not thought as existing. This inference is false; for through the concept of existence I think to myself the thoroughgoing determination, but not that through this concept the thing (g must) be able to be thoroughgoingly determined (as the concept of the *realissimum* carries it with it). For the concept of the thing is not determined at all through the (g concept of) existence [even if it] (except that something real is thought in it), but only that it is given outside my concept [posited].\n(g The proposition that the absolutely necessary being has all reality is just as well synthetic. For the concept says only that the non-being of it is impossible, whatever determination it may have. Thus the proposition goes beyond this concept, in that it determines it.)\nIf from the concept of a necessary being [at the same time] the *omnitudo realitatis* of it followed, then I would not [be allowed to prove] be allowed to ground the existence of a necessary thing on experience, |\nbut among everything possible there is also an absolutely necessary one, i.e. such a one exists.\nThe common proof goes thus: If [it not] a necessary being were not the most real, then [it through] its concept would be alterable, hence this being itself alterable, thus contingent. But this does not follow.\nP. II:\nAccording to the Wolffian philosophy one holds that, even if all our actions, indeed every use of reason in time were predetermined, nevertheless, if through [the last] reason the concepts according to which we determine ourselves to action were distinct, they would nevertheless be free. The Critique has the peculiarity that it does not do this.\nPerpendicular to the preceding:\nOn divine causality with regard to human beings.\nHow a *caussatum alterius* can nevertheless be a free being, capable of imputation, since it is yet thoroughgoingly determined by another with regard to its own causality, cannot be comprehended. Thus also not how God himself can know the present free actions. — The ground of this incomprehensibility lies in this, that we have no concept for it, that a substance can yet at the same time exist and endure only as *caussatum* of another being. For persistence in itself is the character of all substance as phenomenon.\n\n===NOTES===\n- Brackets, parentheses with g, and | are carried over as in the Academy text; \"(g\" marks a later insertion in the apparatus and is left unexpanded. \"S. I / S. II\" rendered \"P. I / P. II\" (Seite).\n- Doubtful/truncated readings: \"wiederl\" (rendered \"refu\", i.e. wiederlegt abbreviated) and \"[schl]\" (rendered \"[sch]\") are broken-off words and left so; \"die [letzter] Vernunft\" is a deletion fragment, kept as \"[the last] reason\".\n- \"Ge|standnis\" (Geständnis) is split by the page/line break; rendered \"admis|sion\".\n- bestimmen/Bestimmung = determine/determination (determinatio); durchgängig = thoroughgoing(ly); Daseyn and Existenz both = existence; Realität/Realitaet = reality; Begrif = concept; Satz = proposition; Urteil = judgment; Schluß = inference.\n- erkennen = cognize; begreifen = comprehend; Beharrlichkeit = persistence; fortdauren = endure; Absicht = aim; Art = way; Beweis = proof; Vernunft = reason.\n- \"Urbegrif\" rendered \"original concept\" (not in glossary); \"dichten\" = fabricate; \"Zurechnung\" = imputation; \"Phänomen\" = phenomenon.\n- Ambiguity kept: \"aber nicht umgekehrt\" and \"dem necessario zukommen\" left as in German; the subject of \"verlangt wurde\" (welches) is left open as in the German, referring back to the bracketed clause.",[]],"reflm:6323":["P. I:\nCosmological proof of the existence of God.\nThe proposition goes thus: if [a necess] the necessity of the existence of a being consists in this, that [is] it is thoroughly determined through a single concept, then it has all reality. —\n*idem aliter*. If the necessity of the existence of a being can be cognized from concepts, then it must be cognized as the most real being. — But the necessity [through] of a being can never be cognized through concepts of it.\nOr conversely: If the most real being is to be cognized as a necessary being, then its existence must be cognized from concepts. Now the latter is false, therefore also the first. — For if in the *Antecedens* breaks off.\nOr if a [be] necessary being [is to be cognized as such] is, then [its] its thorough determination must [cogn] follow from a concept of it (but not to be inferred conversely). Here it must be noted that this thorough determination follows from the concept of a necessary being and not from other concepts, which is false.\nïï Or if a being is absolutely necessary, then it must be thoroughly determined through its concept. — [The consequence is not evident. But if one wanted to say it must be thoroughly determined (although not through its concept), then it would not have to be the most real.] Should read thus: if it is to be cognized as such. For if it also [as] is necessary, but [as] this absolute necessity permits no cognition of the being as such, then one can have no concept of it that would determine [it] that [concept] problematic.\nIf one admits that from (g the concept of a being of) the highest reality it cannot be concluded that it [necessary] therefore (from such concepts) exists, but yet, if a necessary being is assumed (which is already a kind of contradiction), the highest reality can be concluded, then the concept of a *realissimum* must be a wider concept, which does not merely contain the concept of the *necessarium* [in] under itself, but still more things. But then through the concept of |\nnecessity the being is not thoroughly determined with regard to its constitution (according to reality), which yet should have happened in the concept of reality.\n(g The concept of a necessary (g modality) being is entirely indifferent toward every possible determination that merely does not contradict itself.)\nA necessary being must have all reality; for if it did not have [in its concept] all reality, it would not [be] thoroughly determined through its concept, hence would be no being necessarily constituted as it is.\nSince we now can find no concept from which the necessity of its object could be cognized, the *omnitudo realitatis* will be only the *conditio sine qua non* of the concept of a necessary being, without which a thing indeed cannot [be] be, through which however it does not become all that it is, i.e. through which we cannot cognize its existence, although we think everything that it is thereby.\nP. II, left of Motherby's communication, crosswise to it:\nThe real is opposed to the\nnegative\nalso— — —\nideal\n— — — — —\nformal.\nThe merely formal ideality of the objects of the senses is proved in the transcendental aesthetic.\nThe material ideality of the objects of outer senses, that namely no outer object corresponding to them exists at all, is refuted there|by, because otherwise we would ourselves have no inner sense and our empirical consciousness in time, since time as magnitude can be cognized only in outer [alterations] objects.\nOnly space and time have a formality, from which the properties can be indicated synthetically a priori (not so with the colors).\nOn the supposed necessity of indicating something real (pleasure or displeasure lying at the ground) as incentive of the will in the moral law.\n––––––––––\nIdealism.\nThe impossibility of determining one's existence in the succession of time through the succession of representations in us, and yet the actuality of this determination of one's existence is an immediate consciousness of something outside me, which corresponds* to these representations, and this intuition cannot be illusion. — The possibility of this consciousness of an object as lying outside us lies in the simultaneity of the manifold of intuition, because I can undertake the successive taking together forward and backward, which cannot happen with the representation of the manifold in time without limitation of space.\n*(g and what exists not merely in my representation (g but (g as thing) in itself), because otherwise from this representation itself no determination of time of my existence would be possible.\nThe thought of which can indeed be possible, e.g. a relation of the things outside us according to (g more than) three dimensions, but it is thereby not yet clear that such an object too is possible, at least it would not be space. There one says: the proof of its objective reality is lacking to that thought, although it is possible considered logically.)\nRight of the letter, crosswise to it:\nOn God as the greatest aggregate of reality: *ens maximum*, or the highest ground: *ens summum*, *ens entium*.\nComplaints about the mischief that metaphysics does in matters of the state |\nand of the church. To introduce anarchy in both. To restrict the supreme power so much that finally none at all is valid, or to change in religion so long and so often until none is left. Importance that one thereby gives to metaphysics.\n1. reality of the empirical way of thinking against the ideality of the rational. Palliation of [the last through] the first through the last.\n2) reality according to matter, in distinction from the mere form, as the filled space and space not as property of things, but merely of the way of representing.",[]],"reflm:6324":["S. I:\nCosmological proof.\nIf someone were to ask whether we can prove from concepts a priori (g of any being) that it is necessary, we would have to concede that this does not work, because, the concept may contain much or little, if only it does not contain change in itself, it would be just as easy to cancel it (g without contradiction), [it may] if it contained all reality. — But if someone said: experience teaches that something is, and reason that some thing or other is necessary (s One could thus perhaps contrive out of being necessary the concept of such a being, e.g. all reality), then the latter would also have to teach that everything is necessary; for it is either necessary for itself or through a supreme cause. Thus one has given no proof of any being that therefore has something particular, but rather everything that is, is necessary.\nIf that alone is necessary whose concept can be determined only in a single way, then nothing is necessary. For a thing can be determined as the most real of all or as the less real etc etc. But if it is said that, if a thing is the most real of all, then it can as such (not as determinable concept of a thing in general) be determined only in a |\nsingle way, then one must assume what is to be proved: that the necessary being is the most real of all. — On orienting.\nIf I cannot prove from the mere concept of a thing that such a thing exists, then I can never cognize the [Necessary] existence of it as necessary; for just the cognition a priori proves [that] the necessity.\n(s Proposition. A necessary being must have all reality. For if it does not have it, there would be lacking to it one that could yet belong to it, thus the opposite of its determinations would be possible; it would be possible that a being with the given determinations not be, i.e. it would be contingent. — But if I have once assumed it as a necessarily existing being just as it is, then I cannot say that a reality that could be in it is lacking to it, but that a certain reality necessarily is lacking in it (or that one that is in it cannot exist). But had I not added this, then I could not say either that it is contingent, because it cannot exist otherwise than with this lack. — But from my concept of an existing being in general it cannot be thought as thoroughly determined otherwise than through that of the *realissimus*. But from this thoroughgoing determinability existence does not follow at all, and I cannot at all, if something exists, determine through the mere concept of its existence, [whether and ho] how much reality it has.)\nIf one draws from the Critique of Pure Reason the (g theoretical) propositions to which it leads, and compares them (g for themselves) alone with their dogmatically [herausge] assumed opposites that are in vogue, without first setting out the derivation of the former from their grounds, then one can, as the rector of Schlottheim, Ludwig, opined, set out absurdities that strike the eye.\nS. II:\nBeing and non-being are the simplest concepts, if they express the logical relation of the subject to the predicate in a categorical judgment. But the concept of a thing, which in itself contains the representation of a being, and the opposite: the concept of that which in itself [only] is the representation of non-being, are the simplest representations of things.\n1.) *Realitas* (g as the material) in the object in distinction from the mere form, which represents only the way of composition and separation. E.g. since in the moral law one thinks the determining ground [sich] merely as lying in the possibility of serving as law, and in the form of the law, — but others in some object of choice as incentive.\n2. *Realitas* in opposition to ideality. For even the form can lie in the object itself or merely in the representation of the subject.\n3. *Realitas*: In opposition with the nullity of a thought, which can well be thought, but has no object (g for cognition).\n4. *Realitas* [in opposition] as ground, although its sequel is a nega|tion\nSo –a is for itself something real, but [only a ground] in combination with a, a–a is a negation =0.\n5. The real to be regarded as the good (metaphysical perfection) and the evil merely as the formal. Consequently, by taking together all reality in general in the idea of a being, merely through limitation to make for oneself the concepts metaphysically of all possible things. [De] Thus not to make for oneself a God out of that which we cognize [as real] as real through moral incentives, but only to seek supporting evidence for the concept of reality. Existence of God concluded from that. (g That which is opposed *realiter*. — The aggregate of all reality as sum total: God, or as ground.)\nAccording to Aristippus pleasure and displeasure alone are true realities, everything else are only representations of relations.\nNow follows a preliminary work for the Gemeinspruch. At the very bottom of the page there still stands the following s-addition, from the same time (ω2) as the earlier ones:\n(s *Realitas*: whose concept already contains a being, signifies (in opposition of negation as *aliqvid oppositum nihilo*) that whose concept is existence, and there I can distinguish the possibility, actuality and necessity of existence. In objects of the senses *realitas* (*phaenomenon*) is that which corresponds to sensation. *sensibile qvoad materiam* (*qvoad formam* is space and time, which are indeed something actual, but nothing existing). All appearances, i.e. objects of possible experience, contain in themselves space, time and the sensible in general. — One could also call the latter *apprehensibile*, the graspable, seizable, of the intuition of the senses, which would otherwise be empty.)\n===NOTES===\n- Daseyn and Existenz are both rendered 'existence'; Erkentnis = cognition; Beweis = proof; Wesen = being; Begrif = concept; Bestimmung/bestimmen = determination/determine; durchgängig = thoroughgoing.\n- Veränderung = 'change' (not in glossary); aufheben = 'cancel'; herauskünsteln = 'contrive out'; Ungereimtheiten = 'absurdities'; angehe ('does not work') is literal 'goes/proceeds'.\n- Willkühr = 'choice' (not in glossary); Triebfeder = 'incentive'; Bestimmungsgrund = 'determining ground'; Belege = 'supporting evidence'; Einschrankung = 'limitation'; Folge = 'sequel'; Inbegrif = 'sum total'.\n- Wirkliches = 'actual', Existirendes = 'existing'; Empfindbare = 'the sensible' is a gloss on 'what can be sensed' (sinnlich is also 'sensible'); Ergreifliche, -baare = 'graspable, seizable' (rendering of the German, not of apprehensibile).\n- 'Gemeinspruch' left in German (editorial remark); the sigla (g, s, ω2) and bracketed fragments such as [herausge], [De], [ob und wi] are carried over as printed.\n- 'Verhaltnisse' = 'relations'; Lust/Unlust = 'pleasure/displeasure'; 'Nothwendig' in the first (s-)passage is a deleted reading in brackets as printed.\n- 'Rector von Schlottheim Ludwig' is given as in the text; the syntax of 'wie ... Ludwig meynte' is kept as is.",[]],"reflm:6325":["S. I.:\nA necessary being must have all reality.\nFor if one were lacking to it, it would nonetheless be able to have this (g as a thing in general); it was thus not necessary, just as it is. — We can from the concept a priori of no thing in general get the existence, and from the [Voraus] a priori given [concept of] existence no concept of the thing. Concept (thoroughly determined) gives no existence and existence given a priori gives no concept.\n===NOTES===\n- The German has masculine 'er' for the neuter Wesen ('so würde er doch diese haben können'); rendered 'it' here.\n- 'es war also nicht nothwendig' keeps the German past tense 'was'.\n- Wesen = being; Ding = thing; Begrif = concept; Existenz = existence; durchgängig = thoroughly.\n- Brackets [Voraus] and [Begrif der] are deletions/insertions as printed.",[]],"reflm:6326":["S. IV:\n(Ontotheology.) The thoroughgoing determination of a thing in general through a concept is, either to assume it as thoroughly negative or real-determined. The first gives no thing, thus only the second. The most real of all is the concept of an individual, not of a genus of things, which could still be A or non A with regard to some predicate.\n===NOTES===\n- Allerrealste = 'the most real of all'; einzelnen = 'individual' (also 'single'); Gattung = genus.\n- 'real-bestimt' kept as a hyphenated compound; 'Ansehung' = 'regard'.\n- 'Ontotheologie' is given in parentheses as printed.",[]],"reflm:6327":["If I say: a [quiet] body (g that is at rest) is moved, then the predicate moved can go to rest or also to body. It is thus designated thereby that it is meant here as synthetic predicate, and indeed with regard to the concept of the body. But if it says: a body at rest is moved, then that means: as well, in so far as I think it as at rest, it is moved, and the judgment would be analytic and false.\nThe two propositions: a body that is at rest is not at rest,\nand: a body at rest is not at rest,\nare indeed logically, i.e. *formaliter* (according to the relation), one and the same, but not *materialiter* (according to the content). — For in the latter [case] it is a relation according to concepts, in the former a relation [to] expressed in time. A body that exists as at rest (in [the] one time) exists in another as not at rest. Rest is here posited in relation not with the concept of the body, but with the existence of it, which is indicated through the separation of the predicate from the subject, where the first judgment in the relation of two judgments: A body is at rest and just the same body is not at rest, is posited as assertoric judgment; if therefore the body is not at rest, while that the body remains, then that one can nonetheless also be at rest, but at another time.\n===NOTES===\n- 'ruhig' is rendered 'at rest' (in the adjectival 'quiet' only in the deleted bracket [ruhiger]); Ruhe = rest.\n- 'so wohl, in so fern' is doubtful (possibly 'sowohl' or 'eben so wohl'); rendered literally as 'as well, in so far as'.\n- 'indes das der Körper bleibt' appears corrupt or miswritten (perhaps 'indes der Körper bleibt'); kept literally. 'der' in 'so kann der doch auch ruhig seyn' is a demonstrative pronoun ('that one').\n- Verhältnis = relation; Inhalt = content; Urtheil = judgment; setzen = posit; Begriff = concept; Existenz = existence.\n- 'formaliter'/'materialiter' are Kant's Latin; the parenthetical English is Kant's own German glosses.",[]],"reflm:6328":["S. I:\nThat a thing can be another than a certain given thing, although it is internally determined in just the same way — is again the *principium identitatis indiscernibilium*.\nBut that a thing can be just the same, although it is internally determined in a wholly opposed way, is again the *principium diuersitatis discernibilium*. — According to the latter the proposition: Caius, who is (was) unlearned, is (now) learned, is a proposition that expresses the identity of the subject with (g one another) opposed predicates of it in time, hence a proposition of the possibility of change, where thus the predicate (learned) is valid neither of the thing nor of the concept of it, but according to the state in which I it propositions, which (g in itself) is no concept of the thing.\n===NOTES===\n- The last clause is corrupt or broken: 'darin ich es Sätze, der' ('Sätze' perhaps for 'setze'); rendered literally as 'I it propositions' and not emended.\n- 'der' at the end could refer to Zustand or to Satz; left ambiguous ('which').\n- 'bey (g einander) entgegen gesetzten praedicaten': the insertion 'einander' placement kept as in German.\n- Veränderung = 'change'; Zustand = state; Gegentheil/entgegengesetzt = opposite/opposed; gilt = 'is valid'; an sich = in itself.\n- Latin principles left in Latin, 'diuersitatis' spelled as in the source.",[]],"reflm:6329":["In the proposition: Cajus, who is learned, is unlearned, there is no contradiction, because the »is« is only the *copula* in the relation of two representations and I connect the »unlearned« not with the [Cajus] learned one, but with the [who is learned] Cajus. But if I said: the learned Cajus is unlearned, then [the em] the subject makes up only one concept, and there the predicate conflicts with it. In the first case the »*est*« signifies an existence in time, yet in such a way that time itself remains undetermined, whether it be the earlier or the present one.\nIf I say, then: Cajus, who is learned, (g is) unlearned, these are two opposed predicates, combined in one thing (g namely Cajus, according to his existence as alterable), but not in one concept; but if I say: the learned Cajus is unlearned, then I combine both in one concept, and there they contradict each other. — This has only the use of showing*, how little we have insight into the possibility of objects, e.g. of alterations: *a* and *non a* in one thing.\n*(g That time absolutely cannot be brought to concepts of the understanding; Because (therein) *conjunctio praedicatorum oppositorum in eodem subjecto* (combination of opposed predicates in the same subject) would occur in the concept of alteration, the possibility of which can be thought only under the presupposition of time.)\n(g In the proposition:) A man who is learned is unlearned, the concept of the learned is not a partial concept of the subject; for the »is« here signifies not the *copula* in a judgment, but merely the existence of the man, represented in a determinate way.",[]],"reflm:6330":["1793-Jan. 1794). L Bl. F 19. S. I. R II 357.\nThe infinite divisibility of matter proves that it is merely appearance. For according to concepts of the understanding a whole cannot contain the ground of the possibility of the parts, but conversely; the first ground of the same can therefore lie only in the simple.",[]],"reflm:6331":["1793-Jan. 1794). L Bl. C 1. S. I-III. R I 121-124.\nS. I:\nCausality as ground of existence\nCan also be used of the supersensible, like all categories, because it goes merely to synthetic unity (here of the relation), but does not determine the way in which such a one is given in experience, e.g. not as that on which something else necessarily follows. Whether such a thing is possible or not is not settled theoretically, but only assumed practically. — To consider God now not as sum total, but as ground of all reality, again preserves the otherwise unavoidable anthropomorphism, if we want to lay this example beneath.\nIn the margin at right:\nThat metaphysics be recommended to all through the supersensible.\nWhether there is probability in metaphysics.\nAbove this marginal note Kant has noted two book titles:\nRayd. F. F. C. de aeqvitate etc etc. Lingae apud F.A. Julicher.\nWild: de rotatione annuli Saturni. 4. Hannover. apud fratres Hahn.\nS. II:\nAlteration.\nContingency is supposed to be cognized from the alterability of a thing, thus also [if it] its necessity from the unalterability, which is encountered only in the concept of the highest reality. But that is only the logical unalterability of a concept with regard to its determinations in thinking, but not the real one of the thing itself.\nAlteration in general.\nThat a *realissimum* is necessary, [no objective] i.e. [from] in its concept existence does not lie; so too that a *necessarium ens realissimum* is, cannot lie in the concept of the *necessarii*. For in the first case the concept of the *realissimi* would be a *conceptus latior* than that of a necessary being, this therefore *angustior*; in the second case that one would be |\n*angustior* than that of a necessary being and contained under it. They must be *conceptus reciproci*, because they go to the thoroughgoing *determination*, which is identical of both parts.\nS. III:\nAlthough one can make for oneself no concept at all of a necessary being, i.e. although no concept of a being suffices to be able to conclude from it that such a being must necessarily exist, one nevertheless believes that if once the necessity of some thing of which one has no concept at all is assumed, [the constitution] the concept of what constitution this thing has could well be developed from it. Now the concept of a necessary existence [quite simple] is a concept of no object at all, but merely of the existence of the same and of the impossibility of not being. Existence, however, and not being, likewise possibility and impossibility, are concepts of mere modality, which leave the object itself entirely undetermined as to content. Thus it is in vain to hope to find the properties of a necessary being on this path. It is as with a point, which can be represented only as boundary of a line.\nThe proposition: a sick human being is healthy, is a logical judgment, because the »is« signifies merely the *copula* and by means of it contains a contradiction. The proposition: a human being who is sick is healthy, would say just the same, hence contain a contradiction, if the »Is«, which stands twice, the one time belonged to the subject, the other time to the *copula*, were considered in both cases as *copula*. But the first time it is not the *copula* (of the categorical judgment), but determination (synthetic) of the concept of the subject, and the judgment is considered as synthetic: that I namely, when I have affirmed something of the subject, do not deny of it just the same thing, but something other. Namely he is [healthy] sick at one time and healthy at an |\nother. This is expressed thus: a sick human being becomes healthy. This »Becomes« however is not the mere *copula*, but a representation of the existence of an object in time.",[]],"reflm:6332":["Astrology, Cabbala, alchemy, treasure-digging, animal (g magnetism) electricity, incantation formulas.",[]],"reflm:6333":["The consciousness of the presence of an object is perception. — The subjective of perception is sensation, the objective, i.e. the concept of the sensed, is reality.",[]],"reflm:6334":["But not merely this negative ground of the deception with supposedly all bounds of sensibility overflying insights, but the great good fortune which reason [*a priori*] makes in the expansion of its cognition *a priori*, chiefly in mathematics, is [it] that which [hope makes courage also with] on the wings of mere [reas unders] concepts, without needing the construction of concepts, [which de to need] which gives mathematics its whole firmness, instills the courage to venture, for its part (g it on), an adventure [of the same] of a similar kind. For if mathematics, without borrowing anything from experience, yet gives experience itself the rule *a priori*, why should this not [succeed] for philosophy in that part of it which is called metaphysics, hereby not just as well succeed? and it also actually succeeds for it in various principles belonging to natural science. If it succeeds |\nfor it, however, independently of experience: why should it not succeed beyond all bounds of possible experience (within which mathematics still keeps) up to the attainment of that El Dorado (g i.e.) of the supersensible. — Before the appearance of the Crit. d. r. V. everyone thought so, hence also so many metaphysical systems and in fact how — the Organon breaks off.\n|––––––––––\nIt seems that, if one grants that the soul is substance, persistence must also be granted to it, as to bodies. We can, however, cognize [in] on the soul nothing at all persisting, as e.g. in the body weight or impenetrability. — Thus the concept of the soul as substance is only the concept of a mere category of the subject, in distinction from the inhering *accidens*, a concept [of that] of an object, whose possibility is not cognizable *a priori* at all.",[]],"reflm:6335":["S. I:\nOutline of Metaphysics. The investigation of the laws of nature, which constitutes the beginning of all philosophy, could not take place without one's becoming conscious, in the case of many of them, also of their necessity, besides actuality and empirical universality, without however carefully separating these, as cognitions *a priori*, from the others that are of empirical origin, and setting them up, arranged together in a system of their own. To what end would this also have been necessary, if one, as still happens in the investigation of nature, could get by with those laws *in concreto*, without just examining and appreciating them in their abstract form. Thus Newton proceeded with the proposition of the equality of action and reaction — similar to the orators who long followed the grammatical rules before they thought of a grammar, who by their practice rather taught the gram|marians\nthan learned their art of speaking from them. — Such a natural study, united with mathematics, could also make great progress with consciousness of the necessity of its propositions, without rationalizing about the possibility of this progress of theirs, because their certainty laid itself immediately before their own eyes. — What then may have moved an Aristotle to separate concepts that are of constant use in common life and in experience, and just such principles, from judgments of experience, and where possible as it were to measure out everything of our cognition, [which] of which we are masters *a priori*, if he had not intended something other than natural science, namely something that reaches beyond it. This science reaching beyond nature, resting on mere concepts *a priori* and therefore called metaphysics, is as it were the India that promises to the human being far greater and more glorious possessions than the paltry sense-fatherland, in which he, despotized here by nature, finally, after [long vain cultivation to] constant deception [with] through the feigned attainment of a final end, sees before him nothing but death.\nFrom the interior of human reason there now develops a need for a final end that no nature grants, indeed does not even promise, because it lies outside the field of sensibility, and he looks out toward this land.\nS. II:\nThe addition (to the, [everything is] there is no freedom, and everything is natural necessity) makes it that in the opposition actually less is said than is required for contradictory *opposition*. For natural necessity signifies [a] the causality of a thing as a being of sense. This can now always take place, because freedom is the causality of a being of understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- Wirklichkeit = actuality; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Erkentnisse = cognitions; Begriffe = concepts; Grundsätze = principles; Sätze = propositions; Verstandeswesen = being of understanding; Sinnenwesen = being of sense.\n- Nachforschung and Naturforschung both rendered 'investigation' (the first of laws, the second of nature); Naturkunde = 'natural study', Naturwissenschaft = 'natural science'.\n- Gegensatz and *opposition* (Latin-derived) both rendered 'opposition'; the Latinate member is the one in 'contradictorischen opposition'.\n- Bewusstsein/bewust wurde = consciousness/becoming conscious; belehrten = 'taught' (cf. Belehrung = teaching); vernünfteln = 'rationalize'.\n- 'welches' is deleted and 'dessen' follows, so the clause 'everything of our cognition, [which] of which we are masters' is left as the deletion leaves it; the sentence beginning 'What then...' has no finite closing main clause in the German (it ends with a conditional), left unemended.\n- 'Gram|marians' reflects the page break in the middle of the word; Sinnenvaterland is Kant's compound ('sense-fatherland'); 'despotisirt' = 'despotized'.\n- The parenthesis 'zu dem, [alles ist] es ist keine Freyheit...' is grammatically rough in the German; kept so.",[]],"reflm:6336":["(2nd half of June–beginning of August 1795) L Bl. F 4. S. I. R II 281.\nThe affinity according to laws of vitality (g of attraction and aversion, wherein however I fear Doctor Akakia), not according to chemical ones, makes bastards. — Laws of animal affinity in the generation of bastards prove the sensing principle as an organized or self-organizing fluid, for otherwise they could not penetrate one another.\n===NOTES===\n- The first line is the editors' dating and location note, rendered as it stands; abbreviations (L Bl., S., R) left as in the source.\n- 'g' marks the Academy Edition's sign for a later addition; carried over unchanged.\n- Zuneigung/Abneigung = 'attraction'/'aversion'; Verwandtschaft and affinitaet both rendered 'affinity' (the second is Latinate).\n- The pronoun 'sie' in 'denn sonst könnten sie sich nicht durchdringen' has no explicit antecedent; left as 'they'.\n- Bastarte = 'bastards' (not 'hybrids'); Princip = 'principle'; Fluidum = 'fluid'.",[]],"reflm:6337":["One could not have hit upon the [dyna] mathematical antinomies if one had not taken the things in space and time for things in themselves instead of appearances. For how could one assume a world-whole whose parts, as conditions, were yet always to be [un]conditioned. But in space it is so. Of appearances, however, there is admittedly nothing unconditioned, because they are mere representations. (Besides, with a given whole one would also have to let go of the unconditioned, which would be infinite.) — But if we assume a boundary of the world, then we have a position of the world in empty space, i.e. a relation and alteration of the position without objects to which it is the *relatum*. Both are false, because space is not thing in itself but form of intuition, which signifies space only so far as we draw it, thus merely as process in our representation. — Just so infinite elapsed or beginning time. = The infinite division is also possible only under the presupposition of the ideality of space, in that the bodies or alterations in time are only appearances, consequently in themselves undetermined with regard to the multitude of parts, the *regressus* therefore goes to infinity. — But they are both false, because they contain [cont] not logically opposed but *realiter opposita*. *Contraria* thus say more than etc etc.\nThat everything that happens in the world stands under the law of *praedetermination* is true, because they are *phaenomena*, thus objects of possible experience, which without those laws could not be so. — Yet just this also proves that an intelligible ground of the same, so far as human beings could be regarded as *noumena* (which the moral law must afterwards demonstrate), can be thought at the same time, which determine just the same occurrences, although they are not [not de] dependent on the chain of causes and effects in the world of sense and this therefore does not *praedeterminate* them. Thus both can be true, because they contain less than is required for opposition. — Just this holds of the possibility of a necessary being, although all *phaenomena* connected with the world as cause and effect are contingent. — For the dynamical categories permit that the conditions are of another kind than the conditioned; not as the mathematical, which assume merely the homogeneous as condition.\n(g The I, which composes and separates. — 2. I as the composite of inner intuition.)\nWe can have a (g dynamical) cognition of a *noumenon*, but only in practical regard, if we cognize for human beings a practical law, which lays a supersensible condition of the same [thereof] at the ground.\n|––––––––––\nThe proposition: all [effects] occurrences in space and time stand under the law of natural necessity, is true. — The other: they do not stand under this law, because the ground of the same can also be supersensible, since in the former case the relation of the ground to the sequels is only a sensible one and [...] goes to possibility of experience. — Just so: there is no necessary being in the world of sense, but it can yet be in the intelligible one.\n===NOTES===\n- Sachen an sich / Dinge: 'things in themselves' and 'things' both render Ding/Sache (Sache a second German word behind 'thing'); Gegenstande/objecte/Object all 'object(s)'; the Latinate *objecte* is in the second paragraph's 'objects of possible experience'.\n- Stelle = 'position' (Ort would be 'place'); Veränderung = 'alteration'; Begebenheiten = 'occurrences' (glossary); Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary Folge), left unsmoothed.\n- Gegenstande ('without objects to which it is the *relatum*'): the German 'wozu sie das Relatum ist' leaves the antecedent of 'sie' open (Relation/Veränderung/Stelle); kept as 'it'.\n- 'welche eben dieselbe Begebenheiten bestimmen' : the relative 'welche' (plural) could refer to the grounds or to the human beings; kept as 'which'. Likewise 'diese' ('this') in 'diese sie also nicht praedeterminiren' left unresolved.\n- 'Both are false' / 'sie sind aber beyde falsch': both left unresolved as to which pair; 'contraria' and 'opposita' kept in Latin; 'etc etc' as in source.\n- 'laßen' in 'von dem Unbedingten ... laßen' is read as 'let go of' (lassen von); doubtful, the construction is elliptical.\n- Doubtful/deleted readings: '[dyna]', '[un]', '[Gr G]'-type deletions and '[cont]', '[not de]', '[...]' are carried over as in the edition; 'Prozessus' rendered 'process'; Reflexion text breaks at the | before the last paragraph as marked.",[]],"reflm:6338":["S. II:\nA proof of the ideality of space is that it is a magnitude that can always be represented only as a part of a still greater one |\n(i.e. is infinite). — A second is this, that, however small a space we may represent to ourselves, I can think all given things in a still smaller space, without its being required that, considered internally, the things in the one be in the least different from those in the other. — It is thus not sum total of things in themselves. — Just so in time.\n===NOTES===\n- Beweis = proof; Idealität = ideality; Größe = magnitude; Inbegrif = sum total; Dinge an sich selbst = things in themselves.\n- The sign | falls in the German between 'Vorgestellt werden' and 'kann'; English word order forces its placement after the clause, so its position is only approximate.\n- 'ohne daß ... unterschieden seyn dürfen' (literally 'without... being allowed/needing to be different') is ambiguous between permission and requirement; rendered 'its being required' as the nearer sense, the modal 'dürfen' is a doubtful point.\n- 'Er ist also nicht Inbegrif' has no article; kept as 'sum total'.",[]],"reflm:6339":["A great ground for assuming the distinction of the objects as *Noumena* and *Phaenomena* [as] also as necessary hypothesis is that without this freedom cannot be defended at all, but without presupposing this, no morality.\n===NOTES===\n- Objecte = 'objects'; Freyheit = 'freedom'; Moralitaet = 'morality'; Hypothese = 'hypothesis'.\n- The final clause ('ohne diese aber Vorauszusetzen, keine Moralitaet') has no verb in the German; nothing supplied. 'diese' (feminine) could refer to Freyheit or to the distinction (Unterschied is masculine, so Freyheit is likely); left as 'this'.\n- 'dieses' in the first 'without this' (neuter) is left unresolved.\n- '[als] auch' : deleted 'als' retained in brackets.",[]],"reflm:6340":["S. II:\nOn the utility from the principle of making every progress of cognition by going back to principles, as if they were now for the first time to be discovered, because thereby the one-sidedness is avoided |\n(*fallacia systematis*), of holding this itself to be free of error on account of the plausibility of the sequels from an assumed system. If one examines every proposition as if the center of the system had not yet been found out, one can often discover errors in this, which make necessary a new examination of the system and often the overthrow of it.\nS. I:\nDeutschland, 7th issue, 1796. Berlin. p. 10: »on the concept of republicanism« by Friedrich Schlegel.\n===NOTES===\n- Fortschritt = 'progress'; Erkentnis = 'cognition'; Grundsätze = 'principles'; Satz = 'proposition'; Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary Folge).\n- The sentence beginning 'Von dem Nutzen' is a fragment with no main verb; kept so. The German places | between 'vermieden' and 'wird'; in English the sign is placed after 'is avoided' for idiom, so its position is approximate.\n- 'dieses selbst' (neuter) refers back to the system ('System'); 'in diesem' in the next sentence is likewise left as 'in this'.\n- Scheinbarkeit rendered 'plausibility' (from Schein = 'illusion'; 'apparentness' also possible).\n- Stück = 'issue'; the Schlegel title is translated, the periodical title Deutschland kept.",[]],"reflm:6341":["g-addition in the margin at upper right next to lines 15-21:\n(g On the identity of the person in manifold alterations. The I as that which does not arise, pass away, or even merely persist.)\nWhether, if a thing (a simple substance or also a composite of such), if it has ceased to be, then just the same could be named (*per palingenesiam*), if a perfectly equal and similar one followed upon it? Or whether, like the *cyclus* of the Platonic year, the previous state must always be the cause of the following and, should this causal nexus once be broken off, the restoration (*apocatastasis*) would in fact be an entirely new generation, and so also with the future resurrection. — A contradiction lies hidden in the question itself. For alteration of just the same is thought, where only exchange with another should be thought.\n––––––––––\nHe who philosophizes not school-wise but genius-wise manages from the full, which then lets a near bankruptcy be prophesied |\n(*noris quam sit tibi curta supellex*). The critical philosophy is that [which] reason-economy which first examines its state of assets, in order to know how far it can go in expenditures, and looks like a brush against the spirited head, which [so] like a certain minister boasts of his state administration: the more debts he makes, the richer he becomes.\n===NOTES===\n- Veränderung = 'alteration'; Zustand = 'state'; Wiederspruch = 'contradiction'; Ursache = 'cause'; Vertauschung = 'exchange'; beharrlich = 'persistent' (cf. Beharrlichkeit = persistence); Vermögenszustand = 'state of assets' (Vermögen here in the sense of property, not 'faculty').\n- Both Zusatz and the notation 'g' are the editors'; 'Z. 15-21' = 'lines 15-21'.\n- 'wenn ein Ding ... wenn es aufgehört hat' repeats 'wenn' in the German; kept. The question 'Ob ... Oder ob ...' is left without a main clause.\n- Pinsel ('brush') is colloquially also 'simpleton'; both readings are possible and the single English word does not decide; geistreich = 'spirited' (Geist = mind/spirit).\n- Wirthschaft/wirthschaftet rendered 'economy'/'manages' (related to household management); 'aus dem Vollen' is an idiom rendered literally ('from the full').\n- 'schulmäßig' / 'geniemäßig' rendered 'school-wise' / 'genius-wise'.\n- The quotation *noris quam sit tibi curta supellex* (Persius) is left in Latin; '[welch]' and '[so]' deletions retained in brackets, which leaves 'that [which] reason-economy' awkward as in the source.",[]],"reflm:6342":["We could not cognize things *a priori* [would be] if the subjective of our power of representation, hence the way in which they appear to us, did not lie for us *a priori* at the ground as the condition under which alone they can occur to us thus and not otherwise.\nWe can cognize things as they are in themselves only through perception. But then w — breaks off. If space and time were the forms of things in themselves, we would cognize them only through perception, hence not as necessary.\nWe can cognize things according to what they are in themselves (*noumena*), and in general *a priori*, only insofar as we make them ourselves.",[]],"reflm:6343":["p. I:\nThe final aim of all metaphysics is to ascend from the cognition of the sensible to that of the supersensible. The Critique of Pure Reason now proves that this can never be accomplished in a theoretical, but indeed in a morally-practical a|im\nby means of the transcendental concept of freedom, which [in aim] with regard to the theoretical faculty of cognition is [wholly] transcendent and [wholly] absolutely inexplicable and unprovable, but with respect to the pure practical (determinable through pure reason alone) faculty, however, has through the categorical imperative [wholly] undoubted reality. — The reality of the concept of freedom, however, inevitably draws after it the doctrine of the ideality of the objects [insofar as they] as objects[their] of intuition in space and time. For if these intuitions were not merely subjective forms of sensibility, but of the objects in themselves, then the practical use of them, i.e. the actions would [the] absolutely depend only on the mechanism of nature, and freedom together with (g its sequel) morality would be annihilated.\np. II:\nAccording to the letter, not the supposed spirit.\nIn the reverse direction:\nwhat the very same man says in the interconnection.\nAn uprising of the dogmatists in mass.",[]],"reflm:6344":["p. III:\nAll objects are: 1. the *sensibile*, 2. the *aspectabile*, 3. the *intelligibile*.\nIn the margin:\n(g On the way of writing: Niemand, ein Mal, Muse, concludiert.)\nThere are 2 cardinal principles of the whole of metaphysics: the ideality of space and time and the reality of the concept of freedom. If one does not concede the former, then there are no synthetic propositions *a priori* for theoretical cognition; if the second is not, then there are no such unconditionally practical ones, i.e. no laws of duty. But if there are none of the latter, then there is no ground to think the concepts of God, freedom and immortality as [concept] ideas of the supersensible. — Mathematical and dynamical potencies. — Between the two that of the power of judgment of purposiveness in the objects, which is [merely] subjective and thereby an objective principle.\nA *quantum*, in comparison with which every other alleged (*dabile*) one can be thought only as a part of a still larger *quanti*, is infinite. But the *quantum* that, in comparison with every other assignable *quantum*, can be considered only as a part, is infinitely small. That all extended beings in the world can be brought into a drop of water or into the infinite still smaller space proves the ideality of space, when everything is always considered as relative, never absolutely large or small.\np. IV:\nIn order also still others, who have allied themselves in the same aim, |\nI also posit here the notice for them, that the one whom I regard as the one who understands me best etc etc.",[]],"reflm:6345":["p. I [also: XXIII 357,3-8 and XXIII 359,8-11 and 25-27]:\nNature and freedom. In both cognition *a priori*. Mathematical and dynamical faculty of reason in the sensible and supersensible.\n(Against idealism). Whether we could indeed think things as existing at the same time, if they merely represented that which is [is] in us and in our mind. The thoughts in me are not at the same time.\n(Schletwein and Hufeland.)\np. II:\nHow are synthetic propositions* of the supersensible possible? As regulative principles of practical reason. Those of the sensible as constitutive concepts of the theoretical.\n*(g possible *a priori*, the former as theoretical objectively determining the objects as appearances. The second as practical merely subjectively determining the objects as things in themselves.)",[]],"reflm:6346":["Two times are not at the same time and two spaces not one after another. But since there nevertheless are two different series (g of existence) in one time and just so different sums total in one space, one can consider both not as properties inhering in the objects of the senses, but only as forms of the composition of the manifold in a sensible intuition.\nSince the conditions of space and time, which lie at the ground of all representations of experience, carry necessity with them, hence lie *a priori* in the faculty of representation of the senses, this can happen in no other [possible] way than so that they lie in the subject and its sensible form of intuition; for the [goes] is given alone before all experience.\nSuppose we cognized the objects of the senses as they are in themselves, when we are immediately conscious of them, then this would still be no cognition *a priori*, but mere perception, which carries no necessity with it, but would contain only that it be so, not however that it must be so necessarily.\nThis ideality of the intuitions *a priori* (g in the representation of the senses), the (g form of) composition of the manifold of intuition in an apperception [and a] through the understanding together with the schematism of the power of judgment, — finally that through reason in the practical.\nThe infinity of the actual space proves that it is merely the form of appearance. For infinite is the [Geg], what [wholly given] given, what however cannot exist otherwise than as a part of a whole. Now the *quantum* that can exist only as a part of another *quanti* is not a thing that is objectively given.\nFurthermore, however large the given world may be, it can nevertheless be contained [in] in the space of a drop of water, pinhead etc etc.; because of the infinite divisibility. Thus it cannot at all be composed of things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:6347":["On the something and nothing, things and non-things, — members of the division, the concept of the object is what is divided. — I cannot live through my death, for that is contradiction, but indeed the death of another.",[]],"reflm:6348":["(Summer 1797.) L Bl. E 21. p. I. R II 89f.\nThe form of intuition (not the form of thinking) of the objects in space and time, because it is represented *a priori* and as necessary, proves their subjectivity, that namely they do not belong to the constitution of the objects, but must lie *a priori* in the sensibility of the subject, with which one can of course know *a priori*, [how] in which form they will be intuited by us and how they will therefore appear to us: But at the same time since nevertheless also, that a kind of representations, namely that of how the things are, must at least be thought.\nSecond, freedom is an attribute of which we are not immediately conscious through inner sense, but it is proven apodictically through the concept of duty (namely negatively). This attribute represents to us a being like the human being, not as he [as] appears, but as he is. — This principle of cognition *a priori* is however merely practical.\nBoth together are the *cardines* of critical philosophy, and all metaphysics has them as its end. This together with the doctrine of taste make the whole of the principles [of the] breaks off?\nThe forms that we cognize through experience cannot be cognized *a priori*, because otherwise the character of necessity would be lacking to them.\nTranscendental concepts can be called sense-free ones. Transcendental philosophy also sense-free. Everything in which there is nothing empirical, but yet universally valid. [Transcendent] e.g. the categories. Transcendent: what is not at all a piece of cognition.",[]],"reflm:6349":["S. I:\nIf the appearances of the senses were the things in themselves, then they would all have necessity in the chain of effects and causes and there would be no freedom, and hence also no categorical imperative, which yet commands unconditionally necessarily through reason.\nWere the object of inner sense, the soul, not mere appearance of itself, but a pure consciousness of its thinking, then we could not have the being-together of representations in time, consequently in a form that is thought synthetically *a priori* [in us], in |\nus, thus have no (g experiential) cognition of ourselves. For what contains representations combined in relations of space and time is merely appearance.\nOn Aenesidemus and the *Dialele*. — Tieftrunk to be answered. — How can a subject [itself] intuited by itself nevertheless cognize merely as appearance?\nOne can [for oneself] have no cognition at all of an object through the mere category: 1. that many together make up one; 2. that reality has a degree; 3. that something is so constituted that something else is the necessary sequel of it; 4. that a thing's existence is absolutely necessary for itself.\nLikewise one can have no cognition through intuitions alone either and, if they are empirical, no cognition *a priori*, if the rule does not already precede.\nThe ideality of space and time (consequently the concept of all objects of experience as appearances) and the [R] practical reality of the concept of reason of freedom are the two cardinal points of metaphysics.\nOn the spectacle that one makes with the proposition, to be only appearance to oneself in inner intuition, not the thing itself. It signifies only as much as: »inner experience is nothing further than [Erk] empirical cognition of oneself only according to the subjective of the way of the intuition of ourselves in time (of the apprehension of inner representations), how we represent ourselves inwardly, not how the subject is in itself; how it is given to us inwardly, not how it is thought by us.« The difficulty is directed only to how the subject in itself could conduct experience. It must not merely perceive sensations in itself |\n, but excite and synthetically connect them, consequently affect itself. Thus it is not a thinking but an intuiting of itself.\nTo HE. Collegiate Councillor and Director Euler at the Russ. Imp. Academy of Sciences at St. Petersburg.\nThat space and time yield synthetic propositions *a priori*. That this is not possible otherwise than merely if they are subjective forms of sensibility; for otherwise they would be synthetically empirical propositions of the object.\nS. II:\nThat synthetic propositions *a priori* are actual, consequently also possible, mathematics proves. That these [possibility] however are not possible through perception of the objects of intuition as things in themselves, is to be seen from this, because otherwise they would be empirical and would [be] contain no necessity, which is proper only to cognitions *a priori*. That [therefore] they therefore indicate only [through] the subjective constitution of our sensibility, which even before everything empirical, (g therefore) *a priori*, furnishes the form of intuition, and hence can contain the cognition (g objects) of the senses merely as appearances, follows from this unfailingly. That it must however also be so, follows, if we proceed from the practical-supersensible of freedom. [For the categorical imperative could not be valid, if the actions were determined through natural causes, and no freedom would be possible, if determining nature represented things in themselves. The human being (g as object in) himself (g himself merely) as natural-laws (g in accordance with) determinable would think. He must, insofar as he is to be effective in himself, think himself, considered as *phenomenon*, solely under natural laws, for so he can think himself as *noumenon* independent of condition of time determining himself:] Conversely if our actions are free, then the [sense] inner sense can give us only appearances, not cognition of our substance as thing in itself. For were the latter [the case], then* all |\ninner determinations of the soul would be physically necessary, like everything that happens according to the law of cause and effect, and there would be no freedom.\n*(g The consciousness of all determinations of the soul (of inner sense) be empirical, thus no practical principle of the same *a priori*, as that on which all unconditioned necessity, thus also that of actions of duty rests, be possible.)\nThat the concept of freedom and the reality of it is a sufficient ground to assume the existence of God and a future life. — The moral imperative contains an ought and precisely therefore a faculty, in conflict with the whole of nature on account of its causality, nevertheless to overcome it (to resist it). This faculty refers necessarily to the faculty of a being, to make these [decret] decrees of practical reason also in agreement with nature or conversely. Such a faculty is however in God.",[]],"reflm:6350":["S. I:\nUnder presupposition of the [exter] subjective form of inner and outer intuition synthetic propositions *a priori* are alone possible, for the objects must be in accordance with this. This is the principle of the ideality of space and time.\nIf the subject [in the] affects itself to representations of inner sense, then these are only [Ersch] ways how the subject appears to itself, and experience is only the composition of the representations of inner sense ordered according to the categories.\nMyinda, the game of blind man's buff, is more amusing than [the k] the brawl with bound eyes of the *andabatarum*.\nThings [in themselves], as they are in themselves, not considered as appearances, qualify themselves for no theoretical cognition, [but] for they are merely ideas. But to behave in a way adequate to these ideas [goes] |\nis a real concept of practical reason. Myinda *metaphysica* or anticritique of pure reason.\nOne can never make an experience of a supersensible nature: God, freedom, spiritual being; for between both an *action* and a *reaction* would have to take place.\nWhat is object? That whose representation is a sum total of several predicates belonging to it. The plate is round, warm, of tin etc etc. Warm, round, of tin etc etc. is no object, but surely the warmth, the tin etc etc.\nObject is that in whose representation various others (g as) synthetically combined can be thought.\nIn every judgment there is subject and predicate [and and object which]. The subject of the judgment, insofar as it can contain various [Pra] possible predicates, is the object. The predicates all depend on the subject, as warm on warmth.\nWarm, four-cornered, deep are predicates. — The warmth, the quadrangle, the depth are objects. — Just so rational and reason. The determinable in a judgment, the logical subject, is at the same time the [object] real object.\nWhat is virtuous, I can explain analytically; but virtue itself is [thereby] afterward synthetically composed from it.\n(g Remark. Principle of synthetic universality, insofar as it flows from the analytic. — 2 parallel lines, cut by a third breaks off?)\nS. II:\nThe subject of a judgment, whose representation contains the ground of the synthetic unity of a manifold of predicates, is object.\nThat which contains the synthetic (real) universality of a concept is the object. — What contains merely the analytic [contains is] (logical) is the logical (subject). Reinhold.\nThe concept that contains the synthetic unity of the apperception of the mani|fold\n(what might come to it) is the concept of an object. It is also the subject of a judgment that has many predicates.\n[That which is represented in] That which is thought in a judgment is the object (g matter); the thinking of it through the predicate is the form, how I think it. [A quadrangle] Thus the concept [virtuous happiness] wise is the predicate in a judgment, e.g. [This] The virtuous is wise.\nAt the bottom of the page, separated from the immediately preceding by a *spatium* of about 1 cm:\nTo the Society of Artists for the mechanical reproduction of paintings in Duisburg.\nOn the words ein Mahl, Niemand, Jemandt.",[]],"reflm:6351":["1797). Loose sheet. Berlin State Library. No. 21. pp. I, II.\np. I:\nBerl. Gazette, September 30th 1797.\n»The Royal Academy of Sciences is not of the opinion of those who regard it as mathematically proven that there are pure subjective representations; it is rather convinced: that there are essential counter-grounds to which no satisfactory answer has yet been given, and that there is no lack of strong grounds for the universal empirical origin of all our cognition, which perhaps have only not yet been put into their full light.«\nNB. The prize question has been set by the Phil. Class of the Academy of Sciences until June 1, 1799. The director is Mr. Selle.\nSuch a ground of proof is a contradiction in itself. For, |\nif it is to be universally valid, then it must also contain unconditioned necessity, consequently a priori [gefüh], hence also be able to be conducted independently of everything empirical. For (g only) by necessity does one cognize universality strictly (stricte).\np. II:\nThere is no hesitation about this, that if a physician had had the commission from the Academy [had] to draft the formula of this problem, (g he) could have adduced for the universal [Urs] empirical origin of all his (medical) cognition not only strong but also incontestable grounds. — How, however, such a problem [for the philosophical] could then have passed over into the philosophical field and not into the medical, is not to be comprehended.\nOf synthetic practical cognition a priori it is also said that it is merely subjective; (of the will) freedom is the first — and the transcendental concepts of God and immortality go only to the principles of my actions. I ought to act as if there were a God and a future life.\nHow are synthetic propositions of the supersensible possible? As regulative principles of the practical, not as constitutive of theoretical cognition.\n(g The supersensible that is given is the concept of freedom; consequently no synthetic-theoretical proposition, hence none that would be objectively transcendent, is possible.)\nOf objects as things in themselves. That there must be such in our reason is to be seen from the opposite of the sensible in general.\n===NOTES===\n- Beweisgrund = ground of proof; Gegengründe = counter-grounds; Gründe = grounds (Grund = ground throughout).\n- Erkenntnis = cognition; erkennt = cognizes; Vorstellungen = representations; Aufgabe = problem; Begreifen = comprehend.\n- Principien (Latinate) and Grundsatz/Satz would all be English 'principle'/'proposition' per glossary; here Principien = principles, Sätze = propositions.\n- Allgemeinheit = universality; allgemeingeltend = universally valid; Übersinnliche = the supersensible.\n- Fragments [gefüh], [Urs] are broken-off deletions in the Academy text; left untranslated. '(g ...)' is the Academy's sign for a later insertion, carried over.\n- 'Wiederspruch', 'Erkentnis', 'Begrife' are Kant's spellings; 'stricte' is Latin in the source, italicized as foreign.",[]],"reflm:6352":["p. I:\nOf the analogy between the parallels and the principle of the universal empiricism of Selle.\n===NOTES===\n- Princip = principle; 'die Parallelen' = 'the parallels' (noun left as in the German, no supplied noun).",[]],"reflm:6353":["p. I:\nWhat if Mr. Bek were to begin from the categories, which have no significance in themselves, but are nevertheless concepts a priori, then proceed to intuitions a priori that correspond to them, and so arrive at space, time and reality.\nThe system of the Critique of Pure Reason turns on 2 cardinal points: as system of nature and of freedom, one of which leads to the necessity of the other. — The ideality of space and of time and the reality of the concept of freedom, from one of which one is inevitably led analytically to the other. According to the one to synthetic-theoretical cognition a priori, according to the other to synthetic practical, likewise entirely a priori. The nature of the human being in himself cannot be determined a priori without this determination (to actions) presupposing freedom; for otherwise this determination would not occur a priori. Something supersensible must therefore be assumed, under which the sensible can be considered as determinable by it, and conversely something sensible a priori, [unter] according to which the supersensible [empi zur Thätigkeit] determines sensibility in deeds. The (g 1st) principle: all objects of our cognition, if they are to [werden] give experience, can be considered only as appearances. The 2nd principle is the categorical imperative: all actions from freedom can be thought only according to the principle |\nof agreement with the universal validity of their maxims for universal legislation.\n===NOTES===\n- Bedeutung = significance; Realität/realitaet = reality; Gegenstände = objects; Grundsatz and Princip both = principle.\n- Bestimmung = determination; bestimmbar = determinable; Erscheinungen = appearances; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.\n- 'für sich' rendered 'in themselves' (could also be 'for themselves'); 'Hr. Bek' kept as 'Mr. Bek'.\n- 'Natur'/'Nothwendigkeit' = nature/necessity; 'Allgemeingültigkeit' = universal validity.\n- Bracketed words/fragments ([unter], [empi zur Thätigkeit], [werden]) are deletions carried over as in the Academy text; '(g' marks a later insertion.",[]],"reflm:6354":["p. I:\nInner sense is not yet [the consciousness wod] cognition of myself, but [only representation of the inner appear] first we must have appearances through it, afterwards for the first time make for ourselves, through reflection on them, a concept of ourselves, which then has as its sequel empirical cognition of myself, i.e. [Erfahr] inner experience. — That, however, even this experience again lets us judge ourselves, in another relation, merely as appearance, not as we are simply in ourselves, amounts to this, that the form of inner sense-intuition may also derive entirely from the specific constitution of the subject, because we must after all be affected by something [auss], at any rate through attention by ourselves, and thus all inner intuition is passive. Such a form we also encounter in our sense-intuition (the inner), namely time, which could never be [represented] thought a priori (as nevertheless really happens) and thus as necessarily proper to the subject. This representation of time also cannot be derived from concepts, but only as belonging to the appearance of the subject with regard to myself. This now makes up a double I, but not of consciousness (I appear to myself, I am to myself even in this empirical consciousness nevertheless the observed and at once observer, which breaks off.\n===NOTES===\n- Sinn = sense; Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinung = appearance; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Vorstellung = representation; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Erkentnis = cognition; Erfahrung = experience.\n- 'beurtheilen lasse' (subjunctive, no stated subject for 'lasse' beyond 'Erfahrung'): kept as 'lets us judge ourselves'; the German could also be read as 'lets itself be judged', not resolved.\n- 'als Erscheinung' etc. Sinnesanschauung rendered 'sense-intuition' (Sinn + Anschauung).\n- Fragments [wod], [auss], [Erfahr], [inner appear] are broken-off deletions, left as in the Academy text.\n- 'der bricht ab' is an editorial remark on the breaking-off of the text; its grammatical subject is unclear, rendered 'which breaks off'. The opening parenthesis '(ich erscheine ...' is never closed in the source.\n- 'Attention' is a Latinate word in the source; rendered 'attention'.",[]],"reflm:6355":["p. II:\nThat synthetic [sätze] propositions a priori are possible only through the subjective form |\nof sensibility, consequently can represent their objects only as appearances, is to be seen from this, because they carry necessity with them, but not from the concept through analysis. — For suppose we could perceive the things in themselves, then necessity and universality would be lacking to such propositions. But if they are merely appearances, then we can know a priori how they must appear to us; for they can have no other intuitions than the subjective constitution of our sense permits. — That, however, has no kinship with this, that, as far as color is concerned, each may have his own manner of sense. For this is sensation, consequently not objective, but merely subjective and carries with it no universality [for everyone] and necessity.\n===NOTES===\n- Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Sinn = sense; Sinnesart = manner of sense (Art = manner); Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance; Anschauung = intuition.\n- warnehmen = perceive (cf. Wahrnehmung = perception); wissen = know; Beschaffenheit = constitution.\n- 'Das hat aber damit keine Verwandtschaft, daß ...' kept in its construction; 'Daß ... ist daraus zu ersehen, weil ...' kept as is.\n- [sätze] and [für jeden] are deletions carried over.",[]],"reflm:6356":["p. III:\n1. How are synthetic propositions a priori about things that can be objects of experience possible? 2. How are synthetic propositions a priori about things that cannot be objects of experience, [possible] i.e. about [mere ideal beings] objects of [blo] pure reason possible? — The former only in that the objects (outer as well as the inner object of inner sense) can be cognized merely as appearances in theoretical cognition: the latter merely as objects which correspond to the final end of reason [determine] to (g pure) practical cognition [in us s] of ourselves.\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand = object (Object also = object, none occurs here); Erscheinung = appearance; Erkentnis = cognition; erkannt = cognized; Endzweck = final end (Zweck = end).\n- Vernunft = reason; rein = pure; Wesen = being.\n- 'Jene'/'diese' rendered 'the former'/'the latter'; 'welche ... correspondiren zum' keeps the dative construction ('correspond to').\n- The sentence's second clause has no finite verb in the source (elliptical); kept elliptical. [blo], [in us s] are broken-off deletions.",[]],"reflm:6357":["p. III:\nBecause merely the subjectivity of the form of sensible intuition can make synthetic propositions a priori (as going merely to objects in appearance) |\npossible, it can also be comprehended why they are contradictory to the principles of the pure understanding and the concepts made in accordance with them. E.g. that space consists neither of infinitely many parts nor of a finite number of them. Why the given space nor the elapsed time is neither finite nor infinite; because namely here not the object in itself, but the making of it through the putting together of the manifold in appearance [zu] lies under, or is laid under, the concept.\nIs it all one to say: that space is infinitely divisible, or to say: that it consists of infinitely many parts. Just so in what concerns the infinity of space and of time, which are represented as given.\n––––––––––\n[Auch:XXIII 462,18-19]\nOnly through and for the moral law do the theoretical ideas of God and immortality get their (practical) reality.\np. IV:\nHow are synthetic propositions a priori with regard to the faculty of cognition, the feeling of pleasure and displeasure and the faculty of desire, with regard to the sensible objective, with regard to the supersensible subjective, possible? — Of the centering of the power of representation, in that the [Subj] objective is thought on the one hand as appearance, on the other as in itself.\n===NOTES===\n- Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding; Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); Zahl = number; Erscheinung = appearance; an sich selbst = in itself; Vorstellungskraft = power of representation (Kraft = power).\n- 'Warum der gegebene Raum noch die Verflossene Zeit weder endlich noch unendlich sey': the 'noch ... weder ... noch' is awkward and kept awkward ('nor ... neither ... nor'); 'noch' may be a slip for 'weder'.\n- 'dem Begriff unterliegt oder untergelegt wird': dative 'the concept' kept; 'Zusammensetzen' = putting together.\n- 'in Ansehung des Sinnlichen objectiv, in Ansehung des Ubersinnlichen Subjectiv' is elliptical and kept so; 'moglich' stands at the end.\n- '[Auch:XXIII 462,18-19]' is Academy apparatus carried over unchanged; [zu], [Subj] are deletions.",[]],"reflm:6358":["p. II:\nThe whole of critical philosophy.\nFirst the superior division according to the categories.\na. Mathematical and dynamical potencies. Constitutive and regulative principles: those of the cognitive faculty, these of the faculty of desire. — A. All representations given [in] intuition as appearances, |\nB. as things in themselves. (Appearances not empirical, e.g. of colors, but intuition-representations given a priori). — The sensible as such, considered generally, points toward a supersensible.\nPrinciple: Synthetic propositions a priori (g the theoretical) represent all objects to me only Vor as appearances. — The things in themselves thought a priori relate to the supersensible.\nb. dynamical potencies. 1. Freedom: basis of the constitutive principle of the supersensible. 2. Necessity: basis of the regulative. Theoretically transcendent — practically immanent. α. God, β. immortality of the soul. — Freedom: ideality according to the subject and reality with regard to its causality. Concepts of reason and ideas. — Apparent antinomy of reason with regard to the totality of the conditions, which are all contingent and conditioned in comparison with the unconditioned, which is merely in pure reason, i.e. is in the idea. Practical idea as a regulative principle, to act as if there were a God and another world. — The scholastic systematic (*simplex et vnum*), the popular fragmentary∗.\np. III:\n∗ I do not see why one should not begin with Mr. Bek from the categories, but so that it would at the same time be admitted that these pure concepts of the understanding, without laying sensibility under them as *materia circa quam*, can bring forth no cognition at all. E.g. quantity (how one in many), quality (how many in one) is thought as contained; relation: how, if something is given in perception, something *realiter* different from it is also given (g a priori) as sequel; modality: how the form of the connection of all perceptions (in one experience) makes the actuality of this manifold in one experience [best] cognizable a priori. — Here |\nMr. Beck will only remark that these categories lack objective reality, namely one does not see whether something of this sort is possible or not. Now he would apply the latter to the appearances in general in space and time as intuitions and so follow the synthetic method, which attempt would nevertheless have within it another standpoint.\np. IV:\nTo empirical cognition (of experience) belongs: (g 1.) intuition, i.e. representation, through which an object is given, 2. concept, through which this is thought, 3. composition of the manifold of intuition, 4. unity in the consciousness of the same.\nThe empirical in the consciousness of an intuition is called perception (*animadversio*) and has a degree, i.e. intensive magnitude; pure intuition merely extensive magnitude (space and time). Space and time, in which nothing is perceived (no empirical representation of the object is encountered), are empty.\nTo the composition of the manifold of intuition in accordance with the rule that is contained in the concept of the same belongs the synthesis of the manifold of intuition into a concept (category) and then that of the perceptions toward the possibility of an experience.\n(g From the moral-practical to ascend to ideas, and indeed of the theoretical: God and immortality, and so in turn downward to the sensible-practical: religion, state and private happiness. Here too the subjective of practical reason is determining ground of the objective in appearance, upon which synthetic-practical propositions a priori can be grounded.)\nOn the cognition of the sensible and the supersensible.\nSynthetic propositions a priori can give us the objects to cognize only as appearances, not according to what they are in themselves. — For because through such a synthetic proposition I say more of the object than is contained in my representation of the object, I assert of it something that does not belong to it, i.e. it is without truth. (Spontaneity of an empty judgment.). — It is not possible otherwise to go beyond my given concept a priori than only through a judgment which contains the relation of the object to the constitution |\nof the subject and its faculty of representation [sub] or rather first of its receptivity, to be affected by the object in a certain way, and hence says only how it appears to me, not how it is. [With] With this limitation I can say a priori how the object will and must appear to me.\np. III:\nOne can think no way of representing as limited with regard to a certain principle without opposing to [it] it another, which is general with regard to the same. That is, if I distinguish a cognition by its being limited to the sensibility of the subject, then I must think in opposition to it a cognition of the supersensible and can afterwards investigate whether and how (of theoretical or practical use) reality could be procured for it.\nTo explore the supersensible [is] is therefore an unavoidable problem of reason, what outside me or in me [necessarily] must be assumed as necessarily existing, if not in a theoretical, yet in a moral-practical respect. God and immortality, which through the law of freedom (morality). All philosophy comes out at these 3 points, namely to strive from the sensible to the supersensible (metaphysics).",[]],"reflm:6359":["and 11 Dec. 1797). L Bl. C 2. p. I, II. R I 128-131.\np. I:\n1. A note, which (to the treatise on the false subtlety of the 4 syllogistic figures) would state in all brevity the essential difference of the |\nprocedure of the understanding in its logical function from that in its transcendental function; 2. a note, which (to the treatise on the use of teleological principles in philosophy — at the end) would state briefly the difference between exposition and deduction of the categories, whereby it could at the same time be remarked how the proposition in the Critique of Pure Reason p. 177 is to be understood: that through the transcendental time-determination (because it is homogeneous with the appearances and with the categories) the application of the categories to the appearances is mediated and made possible. The difficulty seems to be, because the transcendental time-determination itself is already a product of apperception in relation to the form of intuition and so also itself raises the question how the application of the category to the form of intuition is possible, since categories and form of intuition are heterogeneous. In general the schematism is one of the most difficult points. — Even Mr. Beck cannot find his way into it. — I hold this chapter to be one of the most important.\nNB. The intuition of time is not homogeneous with the categories, but the time-determination, but the unity of the representations in the synthesis (composition) of the given intuition breaks off?\n(g The desire to rule is as a rule in the faculty of jurists.)\nNB. 1.) That the categories in themselves and for themselves alone have no object (g or sense) at all (because they are mere forms of thought) and the possibility, e.g., of a composite (since many together make up one) cannot be explained.\nNB. 2) When it comes with the philosophical principles of cognition a priori to metaphysics, one can no longer judge fragmentarily, |\nbut must judge systematically; a critical-dogmatic philosophy is a non-thing.\np. II:\nThe categories [are] as functions (subjective possibility) of the composition of the manifold, insofar as the many in the representation of something (*entis*) makes up one. a) Of magnitude in (g pure) intuition, b) of sensation, quality of the empirical or of perception [c.)] of the homogeneous, c) of the real ground and the sequel of sensations, i.e. of causality, d) of experience, empirical cognition, consciousness of the existence of an object of given perception.\nThe composite [can] as such cannot be given (g be) in intuition, but only through the composing of the manifold, hence cognized as composite through the concept of synthesis.\nThe merely subjective in intuition [is] as representation of an object is appearance. The form of an appearance, insofar as it is representation a priori, not empirical, is called pure intuition. Thus [light and] representations of colored objects are not pure intuitions.\nThe (g again) finding of what is thought is the (g choice)memory. The holding together of the same in looking back at what was thought and still prospected is the power of grasping. The power of judgment (namely the negative) lasts longest in growing old. If this dwindles, age makes childish.\nN.B. That time is expressed through a line (which is nevertheless a space) and space through a time (an hour of going), is a schematism of the concepts of the understanding. *Compositio.*",[]],"reflm:6360":["—\nS. I:\n1.) The doctrine of the objects of the senses, of the outer as well as of the inner, as appearances. 2. The doctrine of the supersensible [as] (unconditioned) as regulative principle, not constitutive, and thus as practical thing in itself, namely not starting from the concepts of God and immortality toward morals, but proceeding from morals to those concepts.\nA quantum, of which every magnitude that can be given can always only be thought as part of another homogeneous one (g can), is infinite. — That an object, however, can exist always only as part, proves that it is no true thing, but only a thought-entity. That we do not intuit it as composed, but are conscious only [of the Z us] of the act of our composition according to a certain form of intuition. What is infinitely small. — Through categories alone, because they contain merely thinking, we cognize no object (matter). [they] One cannot even have insight into the possibility of an object according to them, e.g. how much of the homogeneous together makes up one, or how something positive could yet with regard to the same predicate also be determined negatively, or how, if something actual is given, another actual must necessarily follow upon it, or finally how the existence of a thing could be absolutely necessary. Intuition of space and time tied to the real (to what can be sensed) alone gives us cognitions that in the end (even pure mathematics) [ih] can prove the reality of their concepts merely through their agreement with the possibility of experience, whereby, however, the intuitions can then only deliver cognitions a priori if they are not assumed as things in themselves, but must always only be assumed as appearances, i.e. as the subjective form of our sensibility, because one can know this by itself alone, |\nthe objects may be whichever they like, according to its constitution, consequently those are given a priori.\n––––––––––––––––––\nThat a human being can also be represented to himself from one side also as appearance is no harder to see than that it takes place with outer objects; if one [the] lays at the ground the possibility of representing oneself as object, which cannot be proved further. For he is to himself partly object of his sense, or of his faculty of thinking. In the former case he is conscious how he empirically affects himself, and puts these impressions into the form of time. In the second he is conscious how he affects the subject and is to that extent in the act of spontaneity.\nOne cannot become conscious of the intuition of a composite as such immediately, but only of the composition (*synthesis*), i.e. of the self-activity of composition. Hence the categories.\nOf pressure, pull, and both together in the beginning of interaction: impact.\nS. III:\nPhilosophy (as doctrine of wisdom) is the doctrine of the vocation of the human being with regard to [his] the final end proceeding from his own reason.\nA. To this belongs, as science of the (g theoretical) object of this vocation in general, to find through reason the supreme condition for the conditioned in general (the totality of conditions), and, since this, if the representation of objects is sensible, always stands in contradiction with itself (antinomy), yet an unconditioned must nevertheless be assumed, to arrive at the inference: that the cognition of things through senses never gives anything more than mere appearance: never the things in themselves.\nB. That practical cognition from reason can never command unconditionally (conse|quently\nonly through the concept of duty), which grounds the concept of freedom, which can be cognized only through this imperative. — With this now doctrine of right (and the doctrine of state right and of international right to be found in it) and doctrine of virtue — both as pure cognition of reason from concepts — are grounded.\nC. That from this non-sensible a step to the supersensible still stands open, namely to the concepts of God and immortality, which, taught dogmatically, contain only transcendent attempts, but critically [with] after a preceding critique of one's own faculty of reason do not give those objects to be cognized [but] (as they are unattainable for us), but whose idea yet furnishes a regulative principle (not constitutive), to act as if those ideas at the same time had objective theoretical reality.\nNow still the doctrine of taste as belonging to the power of judgment according to principles. — —\n===NOTES===\n- Gegenstand / Object: both rendered 'object'; here Gegenstände (first paragraph) and Object / objecte (later) are not distinguished in English.\n- Zusammensetzung = composition, Zusammengesetzt = composed, composite; Synthesis is Kant's Latin/Greek form in parentheses.\n- Empfindbaren = 'what can be sensed' (not in glossary; cf. Empfindung).\n- Wiederspruche (sic) = contradiction; Schlusfolge = inference; Pflichtbegrif = concept of duty.\n- Doubtful/broken readings: '[der Z uns]', '[ih]', and the sentence 'daß ... die Objecte mögen seyn ... wissen kann' (after the | break) are syntactically broken in the German; left as they stand. 'Was ist unendlich klein' is given as a statement, as in the German (no question mark).\n- 'wenn man [das] die Moglichkeit ... zum Grunde legt': 'lays at the ground' keeps 'zum Grunde legt'.\n- Ubersinnlichen = supersensible; Nichtsinnlichen = non-sensible.\n- 'Erkentnis' (sic) = cognition; 'Erkenntnisse' = cognitions.",[]],"reflm:6361":["S. I:\nHow are synthetic cognitions (judgments) a priori possible according to the three categories of modality, of possibility, actuality and necessity of the judgments about the object. (of the problematic, categorical and apodictic.) the faculty of cognition, feeling of pleasure and displeasure and faculty of desire. Understanding, power of judgment and reason. Logic, aesthetic and morals. — Cognition a priori indicates necessity. The necessity *simpliciter* or *secundum quid*. Skill, prudence, wisdom. Philosophical or historical history of philosophy. — Ascending from the sensible of intuition through concepts of reflection (of the end) to the supersensible.\n===NOTES===\n- Wirklichkeit = actuality (glossary); Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Moglichkeit = possibility.\n- Erkentnis Vermogen = faculty of cognition; Begehrungsvermögen = faculty of desire (Vermögen = faculty).\n- Zweks = end; Reflexionsbegriffe = concepts of reflection.\n- The passage is a list of fragments without connecting syntax; the first sentence has no question mark in the German.\n- *simpliciter*, *secundum quid* are Kant's Latin.",[]],"reflm:6362":["S. I:\nXenophanes. Empedocles, Parmenides, Pythagoras. Thales: whether they were the first is uncertain.\nNB. The composite as such I cannot (g im|mediately)\nintuit, but only become conscious of with the composition in [space and] time [consequently] and so get a concept of the intuited object, so that inner sense to this extent also contains the consciousness of outer intuition.\n===NOTES===\n- 'mir der Zusammensetzung' is the printed reading; 'mir' is presumably 'mit', and is translated 'with' (doubtful reading).\n- The placement of the insertion '[Raum und]' and the deletion '[folglich]' is kept as in the German.\n- Zusammengesetzte = composite; Zusammensetzung = composition; Anschauen = intuit; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Gegenstande = object.",[]],"reflm:6363":["S. II:\nOn the Metaph. (g first grounds) of the doctrine of nature. The (g empirical) progress in space goes to the infinite, without my thereby being permitted to assume an infinite space: [for] Because I can experience no boundary; for that would have to happen through perception of empty space, which is impossible. But where [I] there is no boundary for me, there my progress goes to the infinite.\n===NOTES===\n- Anfangs Grunde = 'first grounds' (Grund = ground); Naturlehre = doctrine of nature.\n- Fortschritt = progress; Grenze / Grentze = boundary; Warnehmung = perception.\n- 'Weil' after the colon is capitalized in the German and is kept; '[denn]' and '[ich]' are deletions.",[]],"reflm:6364":["S. I:\nGeneral Geographical Ephemerides March 1799 p. 276. where the difference between theoretical and practical astronomy is spoken of and it is said: »what would astronomy be without observation? Perhaps a Kantian astronomy a priori? Theory and practice must go hand in hand« (but which goes ahead in the mathematical?) — In the review of the theoretical astronomy by Friedrich Theodor Schubert, member of the Russian Imperial Academy of Sciences at St. Petersburg.\n===NOTES===\n- Beobachtung = observation; Wissenschaften = sciences.\n- 'Kant'ische' rendered 'Kantian'.\n- The quotation marks » « are the source's; the passage is a bibliographic note with a quotation from the reviewed journal.",[]],"reflm:6365":["11. S. II R.-Sch. XI 2 p. 164.\nOn the teaching method, of treating the way of thinking of human beings like an *ars veterinaria* (g cattle-medicine-art), e.g. through frightening with purgatory and hell, and in general working on senses, not on reason. — One can also treat mathematics philoso|phically,\nbut not conversely: namely in order to determine the boundaries of its possible use and of the end of the same.\n===NOTES===\n- Lehrmethode = teaching method; Denkungsart = way of thinking; Zweks = end; Gebrauch = use; Grenzen = boundaries.\n- 'R.-Sch. XI 2 S. 164' is an editorial reference, left as is.\n- 'Vieh-Arzney-Kunde' = 'cattle-medicine-art' (literal, a later gloss marked g); *ars veterinaria* is Kant's Latin.\n- 'nämlich um ... zu bestimmen' is read as attached to the whole preceding clause; the German is elliptical.",[]],"reflm:6366":["S. III:\nBetween philosophical cognition and philosophy there is a difference. Being able to philosophize and being a philosopher.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkenntnis = cognition; Philosophiren = to philosophize (the glossary's Weltweisheit = philosophy is not the word here).\n- The second and third sentences are infinitives with no finite verb; kept as fragments.",[]],"reflm:6367":["1800). L Bl. K 7.\nS. II:\nThe consciousness of oneself as a being residing in the body, touching the matter on which it acts, which perceives itself therein, is contradictory; for it would then be an atomistic principle that so acted, which however is entirely canceled by the dynamic.\n===NOTES===\n- Bewustseyn = consciousness; Wesen = being; Materie = matter; warnehme = perceive.\n- 'wiedersprechend' = contradictory (Widerspruch = contradiction).\n- 'was sich darin warnehme' could attach to 'Wesen' (the being that perceives itself in it) or to the consciousness; the English keeps the relative clause in the same place.\n- 'durch das dynamische' lacks a noun ('principle' not supplied); 'aufgehoben' = canceled. The opening '1800). L Bl. K 7.' is an editorial fragment.",[]],"reflm:6368":["1800-1801). L Bl. Kemke 2. S. II.\nS. IIa:\nWhat are the *reagentia* as trial experiments in morals and what the mixtures that cannot be set apart from one another any further in chemistry?\nMathematics is the most reliable of all sciences, because it itself makes everything that it offers for cognizing, and exhibits pure intuitions in space and time.\nS. IIb:\nTo make the subjective in our way of representing objective.\nS. IIc:\nTheologians and jurists are the 2 scholars of reason on which the highest principles, i.e. those of the use of reason with regard to morality, rest.\n===NOTES===\n- *reagentia* is Kant's Latin; Prüfungsversuche = 'trial experiments' (not in glossary).\n- Wissenschaften = sciences; Erkennen = cognizing; Anschauungen = intuitions; Vorstellungsart = way of representing; Vernunftgebrauch = use of reason; Moralität = morality.\n- 'sich nicht weiter aus einander setzen lassen' is rendered literally; the sense of 'aus einander setzen' (separate / resolve) is ambiguous.\n- 'die 2 Vernunftgelehrte' kept with the numeral as written; 'auf welchen' could refer to the scholars or to reason ('on which'); left ambiguous.",[]],"reflm:6369":["Remarks of Kant in his hand copy, interleaved with octavo leaves, of volume II of the Miscellaneous Writings of G. Chr. Lichtenberg, edited by L. Chr. Lichtenberg and Fr. Kries (1801; preface dated August 1800; announced as finished in the catalogue of the Michaelmas Fair 1800).\nOn p. 13 »Mir kommt es immer vor, als wenn der Begriff seyn etwas von unserm Denken erborgtes wäre, und wenn es keine empfindenden und denkenden Geschöpfe mehr gibt, so ist auch nichts mehr« [It always seems to me as if the concept being were something borrowed from our thinking, and if there are no more sensing and thinking creatures, then there is also nothing more], Kant remarks on p. 13':\npure idealism.\nPp. 28/9. »In the drives of insects there is much that must make us believe that they are guided more by the future than by the past.« On »Insecten« p. 28':\nWhich make their bed for the winter sleep, since they did not know beforehand that they would get up again in the future.\nPp. 32/3. Next to the beginning of the sentence: »I believe from the ground of my soul and after the ripest reflection that the doctrine of Christ, cleansed of priestly scribbling, and properly understood according to our way of expressing ourselves, is the most perfect system that I at least can think, for promoting rest and happiness in the world most quickly, most powerfully, most surely and most universally« there stand, written by Kant in red pencil, the words:\nI too.\nP. 33: »Christ accommodated himself at the same time to the material, and this extorts admiration even from the atheist. (In what understanding I take the word atheist here, every thinker will feel.)« On the underlined word »Atheist« p. 33':\nGodlessness and denial of God.\nP. 74: »Caring for the future must, for creatures who do not know the future, suffer peculiar limitations«. Opposite, on p. 74':\nCaring for the future (being concerned) is commonly that which does not let the intended happiness arise.\nP. 76 at the bottom:\n*verte*\nP. 92: »it is impossible that a being can receive the influences of another without the principal effect appearing mixed«. After »empfangen kann« Kant makes in the margin the addition:\nwithout for its part taking them up.\nP. 137: »To make man as religion wants to have him is like the undertaking of the Stoics; it is only another step of the impossible«. »die Religion haben« underlined with red pencil, besides this a stroke with red pencil next to the whole sentence in the margin, and next to the beginning (up to »Unternehmen«) a large question mark (also red pencil).\nP. 234: »The saddest thing that the French Revolution has brought about for us is indisputably this, that one will regard every reasonable demand, to be demanded by God and by right, as a germ of rebellion.« Next to the last words in red pencil:\nNot happened!\nP. 269: »I have never been able to find a genus that had so much in common with the tinker as the people who, under the illusion of practicing a useful trade, go around in order to deceive and rob the people«. Next to it in red pencil:\nLavater.\nP. 415: »Something very good could certainly be written about superstition, namely in its defense. Everyone is superstitious. I with my lights; I do not believe in these things, but it is nevertheless pleasant to me when they do not turn out adversely.« Next to the words »nähmlich — Lichtern« in the margin a stroke and question mark (red pencil).\nOn the last empty leaf stand, written in red pencil, the following words, of which the last is wholly uncertain and of all of which it is doubtful whether they derive from Kant:\n*Patria* Fr. II *Patri*.\n===NOTES===\n- Editor's heading, not Kant's text; Kant's own words are the marginal entries. »Verstande« in 'In welchem Verstande' rendered 'understanding' (Verstand), not 'sense'.\n- Schein = illusion; Überlegung = reflection; Stoff = material; Gattung = genus; Glückseligkeit/Glückseeligkeit = happiness; Welt = world; Trieb = drive.\n- 'Lichtern' (Lichtenberg's quotation) rendered 'lights' literally; the final words 'Patria Fr. II Patri' are flagged by the editor as uncertain.",[]],"reflm:6370":["Over and \nHypothetical possibility is problematic. If certain conditions are there, e.g. the equality of the angles of a triangle. Possibility *in hypothesi* is categorical. The conditions are.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Über und ' breaks off in the German after 'und'; nothing supplied.\n- Möglichkeit = possibility; Bedingung = condition; *in hypothesi* (Latin) kept in italics.",[]],"reflm:6371":["*Conditio est, qvo non posito non ponitur aliud* (a condition is that, which not being posited, nothing else is posited). It is therefore not the ground. The hypothesis can be negative.\n===NOTES===\n- Latin sentence is Kant's; English in parentheses is added beside it. Grund = ground.\n- 'hypothesis' is in Latin script in the German; kept without italics here only as a plain English cognate.",[]],"reflm:6372":["Something is possible *in hypothesi*: signifies more than internally possible.\n—\n—\nonly in it\n—\nWhat is possible only under a *hypothesi* is not absolute.\n===NOTES===\n- The dashes and 'nur in ihr' stand as in the source; their placement among the lines is not clear. 'ihr' (feminine) is left without an antecedent.\n- bedeuten = signify; innerlich = internally.",[]],"reflm:6373":["Something can be possible *in thesi* and yet it is not so *in hypothesi*. Hypothetical possibility is at all times restrictive *sub conditione suspensiva* (under a suspensive condition).\n===NOTES===\n- English in parentheses added beside the Latin term. Moglich = possible; Möglichkeit = possibility.",[]],"reflm:6374":["I cannot say: it is absolutely possible (i.e. without any restric|tive condition) that the square of one side of the triangle be equal to the squares of the remaining ones; but in itself it is nevertheless possible.\n===NOTES===\n- 'restric|tive' carries the Academy line break. an sich = in itself; 'Gleich sey' (subjunctive) rendered 'be equal'.",[]],"reflm:6375":["The absolute is opposed not to the hypothetical, but to the restrictive and conditioned.\n===NOTES===\n- bedingt = conditioned; entgegen gesetzt = opposed.",[]],"reflm:6376":["One can say: something is possible not merely in itself (internally), but also hypothetically. But also: something is only hypothetically, not absolutely possible. What is possible in every *hypothesi* is actual; for it is absolutely possible.\n===NOTES===\n- 'absolutmöglich' is one word in the German; rendered 'absolutely possible'. wirklich = actual.\n- an sich = in itself.",[]],"reflm:6377":["According to the *principio rationis* one and the same state is always accompanied by one and the same sequel; because we can determine the state [through] only through the sequel, the state determines the sequel. We cannot see into how something must follow on something else; but that one and the same *antecedens* must have one and the same *consequens* flows from this, because the state itself is determined through the sequel and otherwise there would be no *continuum* of the *phaenomenorum* without interconnection of the members and they would thereby also alone be represented in a series.\n===NOTES===\n- Folge = sequel (Folgerung would be 'consequence'); Zustand = state; Zusammenhang = interconnection; einerley = 'one and the same'.\n- '[durch]' is an editorial bracket carried over (translated); the sentence is syntactically doubtful ('den Zustand nur durch die Folge bestimmen können'); 'auf etwas anderem' kept with the dative as in the German.\n- 'und sie dadurch auch allein' is ambiguous as to what 'sie' refers to; left as is.",[]],"reflm:6378":["The sequel is posited, in time, after the ground, although this |\ntime is vanishing. But if there is a series of sequels, and indeed one that is infinite, then the succession becomes noticeable. The immediate ground is the starting point of a series of sequels.\n===NOTES===\n- 'der Zeit nach nach dem Grunde': first 'nach' = 'in respect of', second 'after'; 'diese' (feminine) is taken to refer to 'Zeit' across the line break.\n- Folge = sequel; Grund = ground; Reihe = series.",[]],"reflm:6379":["In and \nThat everything contingent or what arises has its ground flows from this, that without a *prius* there would be no *continuitaet* of the *phaenomenorum* and without rule no identity of them.\n===NOTES===\n- 'In und ' breaks off after 'und'; nothing supplied.\n- zufällig = contingent; Grund = ground; *continuitaet* is Kant's spelling; *prius* = 'what is prior'.",[]],"reflm:6380":["In, under and \nThe ground determines the sequel, but the latter does not determine the ground conversely.\n===NOTES===\n- 'In, unter und ' breaks off after 'und'. Grund = ground; Folge = sequel; 'diese aber nicht umgekehrt' is rendered literally ('but not this conversely') in sense: 'diese' = the sequel.",[]],"reflm:6381":["14. E II 1662.\nExistence contains at the same time the concept of a thoroughgoing determination in itself; but thereby (g alone) the concept of the thing is not determined at all. Now we have a concept that contains at the same time the thoroughgoing determination of a thing: the concept of the *realissimum*; and these determinations (it seems to us) we can also specify *in concreto* [gege], e.g. wisdom, power etc. etc. Only the existence of the thing (because this is no determination) is not thereby given as following necessarily from this concept. But if the necessary existence of some thing is also assumed, then I cannot say that the thoroughgoing determination with regard to its quality is given through the concept of necessity, but it is given only through that of existence, where the predicates remain undetermined. There could be several necessary beings different from one another; but admittedly then none of them [existence] would be determinable from the concept of necessity (as to how it is constituted). One could only say: |\nM 14: |\nwe have no concept for its thoroughgoing determination other than that of the *realissimum*, but not: this concept is con|tained in that of its necessity.\nIt is only a subjective [necessity] logical necessity of the condition, to make ourselves a concept of its determinations; but that if from the concept of the same (of a *non realissimi*) this idea it is determinable *duplici modo*, then this signifies only: we can think many kinds of beings as necessary, not: the same being could also be determined otherwise, for we have here only concepts.\n===NOTES===\n- Existenz and Dasein: only Existenz occurs here ('existence'). Bestimmung/determination (Latin-script 'determination' in the German) both = determination; bestimmbar = determinable; Wesen = being; Begriff/Begrif = concept; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.\n- Bracketed words '[gege]', '[Existenz]', '[Nothwend]' are carried over as in the edition; '(g allein)' is as printed and unclear. The line breaks and 'M 14:' follow the edition.\n- The sentence beginning 'aber daß wenn aus dem Begriffe...' is grammatically irregular ('diese Idee es duplici modo bestimbar ist'); the awkwardness is kept. *duplici modo* = 'in a twofold way'. 'non realissimi' is as written (genitive).\n- 'qvalität' (Latin script in the source) rendered 'quality'.",[]],"reflm:6382":["Through existence no predicate is posited in addition to the thing, but the thing with all its predicates is posited [in addition] outside the concept (to intuition).\n===NOTES===\n- hinzu setzen = 'posit in addition'; the bracketed '[hinzu]' is an editorial deletion/insertion mark carried over (translated). Anschauung = intuition. 'außer dem Begriffe (zur Anschauung)' kept as is; the parenthesis could qualify either 'posited' or 'outside the concept'.",[]],"reflm:6383":["If I posit something only with respect to my concept, then it is manifoldly undetermined; but if it is posited absolutely, then according to the *principio exclusi medii* it is thoroughly determined; but I cannot infer conversely.\n===NOTES===\n- 'respectiv auf' rendered 'with respect to'; 'vielfältig' = manifoldly; setzen = posit; schließen = infer.",[]],"reflm:6384":["In and \nI can indeed say: everything actual is thoroughly determined, but not: everything thoroughly determined is actual. — It is only a thought.\n===NOTES===\n- 'In und ' breaks off after 'und'. wirklich = actual; durchgängig determinirt = thoroughly determined (Latin-derived 'determinirt' and 'bestimmt' both = 'determined').",[]],"reflm:6385":["The objective reality of the concept of a *quantum*, that namely many make up One, is not clear of itself, but only in space and time, since namely many times cannot exist, as it were, separated from one another (nor many moments), but only so that they make up one time. Just so many spaces. — Thereby the concept of a |\n*quantum* becomes a principle of possible experience, namely of perceptions so connected that many together represent one (homogeneous) object, which is One not in Many, but represents Many as contained in One. *Praedicabilia quantitatis*.\n===NOTES===\n- 'welches Eines ist nicht in Vielen, sondern Vieles als in Einem enthalten vorstellt' is ambiguous (the relative 'welches' may be subject or object; 'Eines' may be predicate or object of 'vorstellt'); the German word order is kept as closely as possible.\n- Realität = reality; Warnehmung = perception; Raum = space; Zeit = time; Augenblike = moments; Erfahrung = experience; object (Latin-script) = object. 'Eben so viel Räume' kept elliptical.",[]],"reflm:6386":["The transcendental propositions a priori are all together analytic and drawn from the concept of a thing in general, the essential piece and the thoroughgoing determination belonging to its existence: the unity of the concept; the truth in the combination of the manifold, what pertains to it, i.e. that everything belonging to it be grounded either sufficiently for existence or at least for its possibility, and because every common ground of the manifold [combined] composite is properly a ground [is according to] for me to compose them, so this composition stands, as to the way, under a rule, according to which they are coordinated (*essentialia*) or subordinated ([attribu] *affectiones*); — perfection is the [Zus] suitability of this manifold to its concept — *totalitas*. *Unum, plura conjuncta, totum*.\n===NOTES===\n- Vollkommenheit = perfection; Verbindung = combination; Bestimmung = determination; Begriff = concept; Einheit = unity; Mannigfaltige = manifold; zureichend = sufficient; Daseyn = existence; Allheit/totalitas = totality.\n- Stück rendered 'piece' (not in glossary). Angemessenheit rendered 'suitability'.\n- The syntax of 'weil ... ein Grund [ist nach] für mich ist sie zusammenzusetzen' is awkward and left so; 'der Art nach' could attach to 'steht' or to 'Zusammensetzung' ('as to the way'). 'Zus' is a broken-off deleted word.\n- Brackets and '(g' (Academy sigla) are carried over as they stand.",[]],"reflm:6387":["Transcendental perfection consists in this, that all pieces belonging to the essence together make up the whole essence.\n===NOTES===\n- Vollkomenheit = perfection; Wesen = essence (or 'being'; here 'essence' by context of 'zum Wesen gehörige'); Stück = 'piece'.",[]],"reflm:6388":["That all combination, as composition, must not be perceived as (g outside me), but thought as happening through me.\n===NOTES===\n- Verbindung = combination; wargenommen (= wahrgenommen) = perceived (Wahrnehmung = perception).\n- The sentence is a fragment (dependent clause only); translated as it stands.",[]],"reflm:6389":["26. 25'. E II 1670II. 1669.\nM 26':\nAssume a necessary being. Then you represent to yourselves a being whose non-being is impossible. But you have no concept at all of such a one; for the impossibility you can cognize only through a contradiction; the non-being of a thing, however, never contradicts itself, because that a thing is, is no analytic proposition following from its concept, but a synthetic one. — Now you can well say that, because everything existing must be thoroughly determined, if you are to give any concept of the necessary being, [from which its existence] that determines it thoroughly a priori, it can only be the concept of the *realissimum*. — But I cannot say that, if I did not think the [*realissimum*] *necessarium* under the concept of the *realissimum* (g substances we never represent to ourselves as necessary according to their existence, but only the *accidentia*), a contradiction with the concept of the *necessarium* would then arise, since, if I presuppose a *realissimum*, the non-existing of this thing |\nM 26: |\ncontains no contradiction (such a relation of concepts is only possible if the concept of the *realissimum* contains only a partial concept of the *necessarium*; for then |\nI can well infer from the *necessarium* to the *realissimum*, but not from this also to that necessity; but the concept of the *realissimum* itself, according to the presupposition, makes up the whole concept of the *necessarium*, because it is assumed [therefore necessarily] precisely for this, in order to represent to oneself the *necessarium* in its thoroughgoing determination); for necessity can be concluded just as little from a concept of a being determinable in two ways (g the contingency of it) as from one that is determinable in only one way, because the existential proposition is always synthetic and from concepts, be it from that which contains a thoroughgoing determination, or from that which leaves it undetermined in many pieces, it cannot be inferred whether it can be or not be, hence the objective reality of it can be neither affirmed nor denied.\nNot the necessary being is determinable in only a single way, for it could be that, even if a non A were beside the A among its determinations, but rather, if the existence of a thing is to be cognized from its concept, then this concept must already contain the thoroughgoing determination in itself; e.g. [If] from the concept of the *realissimum* this alone could happen, if in general the existence of a thing could be cognized from its concept; for this is the only concept which [all da] represents an *ens singulare*.\nBut from the concept of no thing can the absolutely necessary [being] existence be derived; thus [is it also] if it is thought by us as a necessary being, which is the modality of its position, then it is, with regard to this concept, entirely undetermined which predicates it contains.\nThere is no ground why the *partim negativum* should not exist just as necessarily.\nM 25': |\nThe concept of a necessary being is (g 1.) the concept of a thing that is undetermined with regard to none of all possible predicates A or non A, i.e. of an *individuum*; but through this con|cept\nit is 2.) undetermined; consequently it is logically contingent whether I attribute to it a or non A. 3. from the necessity of its existence it does not follow that it is thoroughly determined through its concept, i.e. is an *ens realissimum*.\nThe proof of the (g necessary) existence of a *realissimum* is this: if it did not [all perfection ha] exist, it would not have all perfection, for existence (as thingly perfection) would be lacking to it. — The proof of the highest reality as predicate of the necessary being is: If this did not have all perfection, then it would be (g in itself) (through its concept) undetermined whether it has a certain perfection or does not have it, consequently, however it may ever exist, possible that it nevertheless did not exist as such a one, hence [a contingent being] its existence (the thoroughgoing determination) contingent.\nOne opines here that, since the first proposition would have to be synthetic and a priori, the second is merely analytic: for there I go from possibility (but is even this given?) to actuality, consequently beyond the concept — here, however, from actuality, which [aber ihre] is at the same time determined as necessary, to the [einzi] concept that alone contains thoroughgoing determination (which must always be thought in existence) and seems to be identical with the previous concept, hence through an analytic proposition not beyond the concept, but to what is contained in it. — But the proposition is nevertheless synthetic; for I go beyond every concept that I may have of a necessary being as such, if I make the concept that for me is the only one that contains thoroughgoing determination into the condition of the possibility of the thoroughgoing determination of the object itself (in intuition) or infer to it; for in intuition it can, without prejudice to its necessity, contain many negative predicates [contain] in its thoroughgoing determination, only that I cannot derive them from a single concept or grasp them together in it.\n===NOTES===\n- Wiederspruch = contradiction; erkennen = cognize; Anschauung = intuition; bejahen = affirm; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Existenz/Daseyn both = existence (Existenzialsatz = 'existential proposition').\n- schließen = infer; folgern = conclude; Beweis = proof; Gegenstand/object: 'object' here is Latin-form 'object' (objects, objective).\n- Latin cases (*realissimi*, *necessarii*, etc.) are rendered with the nominative/neuter form in the English, since the German often uses them as nouns; Latin kept in italics.\n- 'Stück' = 'piece'; 'dingliche Vollkommenheit' = 'thingly perfection'; 'Weise' = 'way'.\n- 'in Ansehung keines ... unbestimmt' kept as the double negative-like construction ('undetermined with regard to none').\n- Bracketed words and '(g', '|', 'M 26', 'M 25'' are Academy apparatus, carried over; some bracketed fragments (aber ihre, einzi, all da, ha) are broken-off words.",[]],"reflm:6390":["»A necessary being can only [with] exist as one that is already thoroughly determined through its concept; for if it were not determined through it, it could (g *salvo hoc conceptu*) be A, but also non A, i.e. its opposite would be (g objectively, i.e. in itself) possible, i.e. it would be contingent.« — That is the *argumentum cosmologicum* for proving the existence of an *ens realissimum*. For this concept is the only one that at the same time contains in itself the thoroughgoing determination of a thing as a thing in general. But through the concept of a necessary being [this] no thing at all is determined as to what it is, [for if] i.e. it remains entirely undetermined whether it is A or non A with regard to any synthetic predicate, without therefore being contingent. For for the concept of a body it is contingent to be [warm or] heavy or [to be cold] not; but it does not follow from this that what that concept leaves undetermined does not belong necessarily to the body itself.\n===NOTES===\n- zufällig = contingent; durchgängige Bestimmung = thoroughgoing determination; bestimmt = determined; Pradicat = predicate; Körper = body.\n- 'objectiv' = 'objectively' (Latinate form of 'object').\n- 'Schweer zu seyn oder [kalt zu seyn] nicht' is syntactically loose; kept as it stands. 'Dies' in brackets is deleted.",[]],"reflm:6391":["We can think no existing thing as contingent in every aim. (For then it would be, as object of experience, something, but for reason nothing.)\nBut in relation to our concept of a thing all things are contingent.\nWe must think at least one thing as necessary, but we can make for ourselves no concept of this thing as such a one; i.e. respectively to our concept everything is contingent.\n===NOTES===\n- 'in Aller Absicht' = 'in every aim' (Absicht = aim); the phrase could also be read idiomatically as 'in every respect'; left as the literal 'aim'.\n- Gegenstand der Erfahrung = 'object of experience'; Vernunft = reason; Begriff = concept; 'respectiv' = 'respectively'.",[]],"reflm:6392":["Everything determined is necessary, the undetermined contingent.\n===NOTES===\n- Bestimmte/Unbestimmte = 'determined'/'undetermined'; notwendig = necessary; zufällig = contingent.",[]],"reflm:6393":["Over, beside and\nAbsolute Necessarium Ens. That something is possible, we cannot cognize through the mere non-contradicting of the thought, but well that something is impossible, from the contradiction.\nThat a thing is necessary, however, we can see only through the contradiction of the opposite (either where the concept contradicts itself, or the existence contradicts other given laws); where therefore nothing of the sort can be demanded, e.g. that the non-being of a thing contradict itself, there we cannot see the impossibility of non-being, hence the necessity. Were we to cognize the possibility of all things intuitively, we would cognize the impossibility of something also without contradiction, by our not encountering the thing at all in the field of possibility. Absolute necessity would be nothing but the intuition of a thing a priori.\nWe are compelled to assume all things as necessary (as absolute or hypothetical). The ground is st, because we must represent everything true at the same time logically as necessary (*principium exclusi medii*), in that we determine things only through judgments, and judgments a posteriori, without being confirmed through such a priori, are not objective.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Über, neben und' (first line) is a marginal locator; its sense is unclear and it is translated literally.\n- erkennen = cognize; einsehen = 'see' (not in glossary); anschaulich = intuitively; Anschauung = intuition; Urteil = judgment; determiniren = determine; Gesetz = law.\n- 'Der Grund st' is as printed (apparently a broken-off or slipped word, perhaps 'ist'); 'zuglelch' is a slip for 'zugleich'.\n- 'wo also dergleichen nicht verlangt werden kan' and the clause following are kept in the German order, so the 'not' attaches to 'see' with the objects 'impossibility ... necessity' as in the German.",[]],"reflm:6394":["From the concept of an object I can never cognize the necessity or contingency of its existence.",[]],"reflm:6395":["If there is no ground for a thing, then it is contingent for me.",[]],"reflm:6396":["We can think an occurrence that is no alteration of a thing, e.g. arising or perishing of the substance. Of such a one I do not say that it has a cause, but that such an arising would not be an occurrence at all, i.e. could not come to pass.",[]],"reflm:6397":["(g Quantum) *Absolute minimum non foret quantum; ergo est im|possibile.* (An absolute minimum would not be a quantum; therefore it is im|possible.)\n*Absolute maximum non foret dabile quoad quantitatem; ergo ut obiectum intuitus est impossibile.* (An absolute maximum would not be givable as regards quantity; therefore as an object of intuition it is impossible.)",[]],"reflm:6398":["All magnitude in appearances is merely comparative, in things in themselves absolute. The whole solar system could be contained in a drop of water, and thus in neither of the two is there a determinate number of parts. But in a *mundo noumeno* (noumenal world) there must be a determinate multitude of parts. One drop of water is not to be distinguished from another in itself by anything, but only by place. But in the *substantia noumenon* (noumenal substance) the difference is actual. Just so the successive Platonic year-revolutions. In the soul a minute can contain what a century does in another. But in itself there must nonetheless be more reality in the one than in the other. This proves *animam ut phaenomenon* (the soul as phenomenon).",[]],"reflm:6399":["The magnitude of a thing as aggregate is extensive, as ground is intensive.",[]],"reflm:6400":["Magnitude of space and degree of extension.",[]],"reflm:6401":["Contingency is either (g merely) internal or (g also) external, the latter is either in particular or in all *respectu* (with respect to) possibility of the opposite; we represent to ourselves everything that is actual also as necessary.",[]],"reflm:6402":["Possibility not merely *in thesi*, but also *in hypothesi*, is greater; but that *sub hypothesi* smaller.",[]],"reflm:6403":["In every alteration the substance persists, because alteration is the succession of determinations of one and the same thing. This is a merely logical proposition according to the rule of identity. But it does not say that substance in general does not arise or perish, but only that it remains during the alteration.\nThat proposition holds (g however) also only of (g corporeal) substances in space. For there arising or perishing is always an alteration of the non-substantial, namely of space, which remains, but is no object of experience at all, consequently that alteration too would be no object of experience, hence no alteration. The persistence of matter through all change of the relations [is] is proven thereby, that matter itself is a mere sum total of relations, which cannot drop away without all other relations also dropping away, hence all possible outer experience.",[]],"reflm:6404":["Because the reality that is encountered in all things in part is thought together in God, there arises from it Spinozism: that all things inhere in God, because their essence is possible only as part of his essence. *Non indiget existentia alterius.* (It does not need the existence of another.)\nInherence, dependence, and real whole. In so many ways do realities belong to one another according to existence.",[]],"reflm:6405":["Spinoza's proposition, that that whose idea needs no other is substance, is the same as that the *realissimum* alone is substance, hence alone is the necessary being, in that everything else inheres in it.",[]],"reflm:6406":["Over and \nEvery alteration has its cause; substances do not arise and perish.",[]],"reflm:6407":["*Principia:* (Principles:)\n1. *Inhaerentiae.* (Of inherence.) No substance perishes.\n2. *Causalitatis,* (Of causality,) of alteration.\n3. *Commercii.* (Of commerce.)",[]],"reflm:6408":["Contingent is that which is possible only in a conditioned way (hypothetically) (whose non-being is therefore possible in itself).\nOne cannot begin with this, that the opposite is possible, i.e. that it does not exist. For since we cognize impossibility only through a contradiction, but the non-being of a thing never contradicts itself, everything would be contingent. Everything that happens is contingent, i.e. possible only in a conditioned way: *principium causalitatis* (principle of causality). Hence substances, which persist, are not to be regarded as contingent, although their state is contingent, hence the alterations of substances do not prove their contingency.",[]],"reflm:6409":["What happens is possible only in a conditioned way. For before it a time goes before, which contains the condition of the existence of the occurrence.",[]],"reflm:6410":["Contingent is not that whose non-being does not contradict itself, but is ungrounded.\nNow everything that exists is grounded; consequently nothing existing is contingent. Being grounded here pertains to experience, not to objective principles.",[]],"reflm:6411":["Necessity and contingency are mere concepts of reason, so they certainly cannot be cognized in any object.",[]],"reflm:6412":["Of the contingency that is attributed to a connection on account of its purposiveness without a purpose.",[]],"reflm:6413":["94. 95'. E II 1089.\nEvery synthetic predicate, i.e. determination, has its ground, namely something else, through which it is combined *a priori* with the concept of a thing. For otherwise the determination would not be objective. [Thus] But the reality of an *ens realissimum* (most real being) is no determination. Yet the existence of a thing is a synthetic predicate of our representation of things, and one cannot say: it has a ground [but rather] (*rationem existendi* (ground of existing)), but only: *rationem cognoscendi* (ground of cognizing), and this not merely *a posteriori*, but also *a priori*.\nM 94:\nThe concept of cause holds only of objects of experience; for that something exists can be cognized only by means of experience, and of that which is not an object of experience, only [according to the] insofar as it is represented according to the analogy of experience, can be thought. But that something is contingent in such objects cannot be cognized from concepts and the possibility of the opposite, i.e. the agreement of the opposite with concepts; for contingency here concerns existence as the predicate of a synthetic judgment. Thus only the opposite of that which belongs to existence is possible, which is determined according to [the] (g no) law of experience and according to no analogy thereof. Hence nothing is contingent, except (g what) happens, |\nbut without cause or respectively without outer cause. Motion inheres in a body *accidentaliter* (accidentally) [or]. It arises *accidentaliter*. It is a contingency, i.e. event, but not according to concepts, i.e. absolute |\nM 94': |\npossibility of the opposite. For everything that is determinable through experience is necessary, because otherwise the combination of perceptions would not be objective. Logical contingency is yet physical necessity.\nM 95':\n*Omne contingens, h.e. quodcunque contingit, est rationatum alterius* (Every contingent, i.e. whatever happens, is the *rationatum* of another), every event is [an] effect of a cause: can be understood in two ways, either that by it is understood merely the determination of a thing, or the existence of it as substance. In the first meaning it is a possible concept of experience, and the proposition a principle of nature; in the second it is a problematic concept, which is wholly empty, since namely the existence of the subject itself is supposed to be an event and the existence itself is supposed to follow upon nonexistence — empty —. This contingency of the thing can no more than necessity be seen into. *Contingentia absoluta* (absolute contingency) is just as *necessitas absoluta* (absolute necessity) uncognizable.",[]],"reflm:6414":["Proper metaphysics is the application of transcendental philosophy to [that which] concepts given in reason (which are necessary to it), to which, however, [in the] no corresponding objects can be given in experience (consequently to the supersensible).\nThis can therefore only be the unconditioned, for that is the sole theoretical idea of reason. Thus metaphysics goes 1. to that of which only |\nthe whole is to be represented as absolutely unconditioned; 2. to things, insofar as they are in themselves sensibly unconditioned.\nThe first part is therefore cosmology, the second rational doctrine of the soul [and] as pneumatology and theology.",[]],"reflm:6415":["Proper metaphysics: the application of transcendental philosophy to things that cannot become objects of experience, either to a whole of the objects of the senses or to supersensible things.",[]],"reflm:6416":["Cosmology. Is transcendent. For it goes to the whole of all conditions, which is unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:6417":["The world of sense is a mere *totum ideale* (ideal whole), because space is a mere form of intuition; thus the synthesis is never total; time does not make a measure of the magnitude of the world, but the whole of its states.",[]],"reflm:6418":["All antinomies come from this, that one seeks the unconditioned in the world of sense.",[]],"reflm:6419":["That both *opposita* (opposites) in the first 2 antinomies can be false comes from this, that they stand opposed to one another not *contradictorie* (contradictorily), but also *contrarie* (contrarily) |\n. The world has a beginning — It has not — It is infinite.",[]],"reflm:6420":["Were space and time [as infinite] something given in itself, they would have to be considered as infinite magnitudes. Now they are nothing but forms, to enlarge or to diminish into the infinite.",[]],"reflm:6421":["In the mathematical antinomies both propositions are false, because the unconditioned is supposed to be a part of the appearances and yet [it] as such can never be unconditioned. In the dynamical ones both can be true, because the unconditioned is laid at the ground of the appearances, but is not a part of them, and the one proposition holds of the things in the appearance, the other of their relation to the intelligible ground.\nThe first two antinomies are grounded on the unconditioned totality of the conditions — the other two on the unconditioned ground of the existence of the conditioned. Hence the first 2 are false; the others can be true.",[]],"reflm:6422":["Freedom is a (g sensibly unconditioned) faculty of a substance, to determine itself to acting.",[]],"reflm:6423":["*Non datur fatum.* Everything in the world is composed, consequently contingent.",[]],"reflm:6424":["Idea. A pure concept of reason must be a [through] subjectively necessary concept of reason, and this is that of the unconditioned, consequently of the absolute totality of the conditions (because reason is a faculty for deriving the particular from the universal). Now the world as *quantum* is composed, and the possibility of a *quantum*, which itself is still a part, is always still conditioned; thus only the concept |\nof the absolute totality of the whole of substances is in conformity with the concept of reason of a world. Now, however, contradictions arise, whether one says: the world of sense as an absolute whole of things is infinite (given), or also: it is given as finite. That is because it is a contradiction that a world in space and time is given [a] in itself whole, because it is no thing in itself. The world of sense is infinite, means [only] not: it is actual as such. For, because it exists merely in the representation, not in itself, as one thinks it: so it is given only as far as the *progressus* reaches, consequently never whole; i.e. the last goes into the infinite.",[]],"reflm:6425":["One can say: matter is divisible to infinity, but not: it consists of infinitely many parts: somewhat just as Euclid: two parallel lines [become], drawn to infinity, cannot meet, is not the same as the proposition: they meet at an infinite distance. For the latter would yield a triangle whose angles contained more than 2 right angles. It stands just so with the proposition: the (g world) space can be pursued to infinity, and with: it is infinite (the latter we can say of pure space, but only as idea). One can also conduct a proof of this, that, if [the] (g a given) space consists of infinitely many parts, the world-space is infinite.",[]],"reflm:6426":["Nature of the things of the world is either under laws of the determination of their existence in space or merely in time: corporeal and thinking nature.",[]],"reflm:6427":["Beside and \nBecause good conduct in life (g him) makes worthy of an enlargement of his natural predispositions, the progression in the suitability to his duty must also bring the human being in the future to a greater step of his natural rank. But because this in turn draws greater duties after it, the progress to the fulfillment of these will also continue, and so in eternity.",[]],"reflm:6428":["The concept of freedom is a concept of reason, whose reality cannot be cognized theoretically dogmatically; but also the only one that is not only capable of a practically-dogmatic [not] proof, but also necessarily combined with it. — From the same, however, there also flow God and immortality. Thus these too are merely practically dogmatic.\n(s κατ' ανθρωπον to prove *a priori* means: to prove sufficiently for the subject, but only in practical aim.)",[]],"reflm:6429":["If space were the form of the outer relationships in itself, there could be more *entia realissima*.",[]],"reflm:6430":["*immensum*, [infinit] *incommensurabile*, What is analogy without similarity.\nThat there is no probability of the existence of God.\nThe divine nature is for our reason unreachable, transcendent. The cognition of God as a moral being is immanent. The categories in the moral concept are concepts that remain |\nover, if I omit all theoretical relation of the same — of a thing in general.\n*natura divina est imperscrutabilis.*",[]],"reflm:6431":["2) God not merely as first cause, but also as author.\nProof of a living God:\n1. as *primus motor*,\n2 — architect,\n3 — creator.",[]],"reflm:6432":["Of God and immortality.\nIf the moral law, in order to bind us, needed God and a future life, it would be absurd to ground upon such a need the belief in the actuality of that which can satisfy it.\nNow, however, the moral law stands firm for itself independently of all theoretical presupposition; indeed it also has, in place of this, something supersensible, which surpasses all our insight, freedom, as ground (which itself lies beyond nature in another field). This law as principle of wisdom leads our reason in its practical use to the final end of a highest wisdom: the greatest happiness combined with virtue as the final end of all things, which, however, as far as we have insight, makes necessary an eternity [in] of our existence and a moral author of the world for the effecting of the world-constitution [geho] required for it.\nEven if one could [assume] believe neither immortality nor the existence of God, the moral law and the end of a will conforming to it (the highest good) would nonetheless just as well have their powerful influence on the will; we would nonetheless not take such a scandal into our principles in a practical aim, but would, as (g permitted) hypothesis [our], promote the contentment with our existence and the world which we have in prospect.\nThe *argumentum a tuto* is good for actions, but very bad (g itself) for the [determining gr] (g inner) confession of belief.",[]],"reflm:6433":["340. E II 1676. 873.\nAll rational theology is either that of deism (g transcendental) or of theism (g psychologically determined). The first either ontotheology or cosmotheology. — That of theism either physico- or ethicotheology.\nThe cosmotheological proof wants to conclude from [the] a given, absolutely necessary existence (without being able to derive this itself from any [determinate] concept of a thing, but merely because it is unconditioned) to the [concept] highest reality of this being. For, it is said, if we do not think it as *realissimum*, then we have no concept by which it would at the same time be cognized according to its thoroughgoing determination. consequently [they] we would be able to think its concept through a or also non a, i.e. the concept that we made of it would be only contingent; thus the thing itself contingent.\nM 340:\nIs the concept of a necessary or contingent substance, however, thinkable at all? At the least [are] changes of substances do not prove their contingency. These concepts want only to indicate the possibility of the cognition of the existence of things fully *a priori* or the impossibility of the same, and hold therefore not of objects, but of the relationship of our concepts of them to our faculty of cognition. The former is impossible. Instead of these concepts one should make use of those of unconditioned- [cognizable] possible existence.",[]],"reflm:6434":["Irrational concepts (g *conceptus surdi*) are to be distinguished from ideas; namely they are such concepts of the understanding from which one nonetheless withdraws everything that is required for example and application *in concreto*, which therefore can have no significance, although they are without contradiction. |\ne.g. (g 1.) God is eternal, i.e. his existence has a magnitude, but not of time. Now, however, we cannot think for ourselves any magnitude of existence, i.e. a duration, except in time. 2. His presence has a magnitude with regard to the All of things outside one another (in space). But it is nonetheless not to be taken as in space, for then God would be outside himself. 3. He is cause of substance. But the way of being cause must be different from that which we know; for then nothing can be the cause of a substance.",[]],"reflm:6435":["Concepts of irrational relationships are those that cannot be exhausted by any approximation; they are transcendent, however, if in this relationship an object has no standard of measure.",[]],"reflm:6436":["1. A (g Most perfect of all) being, which contains all reality (be it as ground or as aggregate), is (g necessarily) actual. For were it not actual, a perfection (metaphysical), namely existence, would be lacking to it.\n2. A being that exists necessarily must contain all reality (i.e. if existence can be concluded from its concept, for only by that can one cognize the necessity). For if it did not contain all reality, it would not be thoroughly determined through its concept, which thoroughgoing determination must be attributed to every existing thing [noth]. But one can indeed think a necessary being, but cognize it by nothing at all, because every thing can be canceled in thought, even all reality, unless one had to take existence [al] for a particular reality, which contradicts the first argument.",[]],"reflm:6437":["That from God no [theoretical] cognition (g in speculative aim) is possible for the human being, because from our concepts of things we must so abstract everything that makes the things cognizable to us thereby, hence nothing remains to us for giving them reality. The categories\n|\nare concepts of things whose objective reality is problematic. Through them we can think a being, but not cognize it.\n===NOTES===\n- Erkentnis = cognition; erkennbar = cognizable; Absicht = aim; Realitaet = reality; Begriff = concept; Wesen = being.\n- *dadurch* may mean 'thereby' or 'through them' (the concepts); I kept 'thereby'.\n- 'g' is the Academy's sign for a later insertion; carried over as it stands.",[]],"reflm:6438":["Belief in the existence of God is not probability. The cognition of God in practical respect is merely representation of him according to the analogy in theoretical [respect], whereby he is thought only according to the analogy with an object of the senses. Anthropomorphism.\nUnderstanding — but not human in thinking — feeling of pleasure and displeasure, but not similar to the human — faculty of desire or will, but not human. Yet according to the analogy.\n===NOTES===\n- Glaube = belief; Daseyn = existence; Warscheinlichkeit = probability; Vorstellung = representation; Gegenstand = object; Begehrungsvermogen = faculty of desire.\n- 'in theoretischer' is elliptical in the German; the bracketed 'respect' is my supply, marked by brackets, and the ellipsis is otherwise preserved.\n- *desselben* refers back grammatically to 'God' (or to 'cognition'); I kept 'him'.",[]],"reflm:6439":["In and \nThe psychological concept of God as *summa intelligentia* is that of a living God with understanding and will. The proof of such a one is teleology, not the concept of reality. But ontotheology as well as physicotheology give no cognition of God without anthropomorphisms.\n===NOTES===\n- The first line ('In und ') is broken off in the text as given; nothing is supplied.\n- Beweis = proof; Verstand = understanding; Begrif = concept; Realitaet = reality; Erkentnis = cognition.",[]],"reflm:6440":["From the existence of the world one can indeed infer, according to laws of causality, to supreme causes, but not to their absolute necessity, unity and infinity.\nInference according to the analogy of nature from contingent effect to free cause.\n===NOTES===\n- Schließen / Schlus = infer / inference; Zufälliger = contingent; Wirkung = effect; Einheit = unity.",[]],"reflm:6441":["NB. Inference according to the analogy of the cause of alteration, the first of which is always free action.\n===NOTES===\n- Veränderung = alteration (not in the glossary); Schlus = inference.\n- 'deren erste' may refer to the alterations or the causes; the German is ambiguous and the English keeps 'the first of which'.",[]],"reflm:6442":["One has cause to represent God according to the analogy with an understanding |\nthat is, that just as a clock, a ship etc etc. relates to its cause (an intelligent being), so the world to the unknown, which we call an infinite understanding, although it has nothing in itself similar to it. — But we cannot infer from the causality of things through understanding to a world-cause (g of the same kind, i.e.) that has understanding, just as little as from the artworks of the bees that they have understanding; because, just as with these the causality is of a far lesser species, so there it can be of a far higher kind than that which we indicate through understanding.\nIt is also not perhaps probable that such a cause is. For everything probable must lie on the way to certainty, insofar as this is attained through the mere supplementation of what is lacking in the progress. But here the data are of such a kind that they do not lead there at all.\n===NOTES===\n- *Ursache haben* = 'have cause' (Ursache is also 'cause' elsewhere; one English word for both senses).\n- verständig = intelligent; Verstand = understanding; Fortschritt = progress; Gewisheit = certainty.\n- 'Unbekanten' is capitalized in the German; I rendered 'the unknown'. 'dort/bey diesen' (there/with these) are kept without resolving their referents.",[]],"reflm:6443":["That our idea of a highest being as an intelligent being has objective reality can be concluded only from the (g objective) reality of the concept of freedom, which makes it necessary for us to direct our actions to [the] a highest [G] good in the world possible through our cooperation, because we thereby assume the possibility of the same, at least must proceed as if we assumed it (in that we not only act in agreement with it, as is required for a best world, but must also make it our incentive), from which it then follows that we, since our faculty alone does not suffice for this, must assume a highest original good, consequently the concept of the latter has objective practical reality, although in theoretical relation it is transcendent, hence only an object of belief.\nThe mere categories (of substance, cause, community) give mere ways of making for ourselves concepts of things in general, i.e. of the synthetic unity of a manifold that may be given to us, in one consciousness, which must be in us a priori for this reason, because a *compositum* as such cannot be perceived by us but must always be made, and the action of making such a one, i.e. of giving to the |\nmanifold unity of consciousness in one representation, must inhere in us a priori. These categories, however, have by themselves alone no objective reality, because they do not contain the way in which a manifold may be given to us. Since this now cannot be given to them before the theoretical faculty of cognition, i.e. as (g to) belonging to a cognition of the nature of the objects: so there remains only the practical reality of the same with regard to the concept of freedom (which presupposes merely the form of unity, not the intuition itself) as immanent for our reason, in order to think the highest being according to the analogy with those that we cognize in nature according to their spontaneity.\n===NOTES===\n- Folgern = conclude; Zureicht = suffices (zureichend = sufficient); Triebfeder = incentive (not in glossary); Gegenstand = object; Object (in 'objecte') = object.\n- 'ein ... mögliches hochste [G] Gute': bracketed deletions/insertions kept as in the text; word order adjusted minimally for English.\n- 'welche ... in uns seyn muß' could refer to the unity or the manifold; 'dieses' ('this', before 'ihnen') left unresolved.\n- Mannigfaltiges = manifold; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Anschauung = intuition; Erkentnisvermögen = faculty of cognition.",[]],"reflm:6444":["Physicotheology is the inference, from experience [in] of this world to infer to an intelligent supreme cause:\n1. From the motion of matter as something lifeless to a first mover as a living one.\n2. From the art in this world to an [first] artificer who has understanding.\nMoral theology:\n1. From moral wisdom as a [rule] legislation in rational beings for this world, although nothing conforming to this wisdom can be shown in experience, to infer to an intelligible world in which this is.\n2. From the idea of such a [to a] as the highest good possible only through a moral author to the existence of such a one.\n===NOTES===\n- Schluß = inference; Erfahrung = experience; Materie = matter; Vernünftig = rational; Existenz = existence (Dasein elsewhere also 'existence').\n- 'einer[Regel] Gesetzgebung' and 'einer solchen [auf ein]' are as the text stands; the latter has no noun after 'solchen' and the sentence is left elliptical.\n- 'Weisheit' = wisdom; verständig = intelligent.",[]],"reflm:6445":["We can indeed unite divine freedom with reason, but not the arising of the effect from this freedom.\nThe transition from the intellectual as cause to that which is merely appearance cannot be comprehended. Thus, even if we were to abolish the freedom of the human being, we could still not resolve the difficulty of how the determining causes of the will could first have begun.\n===NOTES===\n- begreifen = comprehend; Erscheinung = appearance; Wirkung = effect; bestimmend = determining; Ursache = cause.\n- 'Entstehen' = arising; 'Ubergang' = transition; 'aufheben' = abolish.",[]],"reflm:6446":["Human actions cannot be considered merely as *phaenomena* [according to] and hence as determined according to empirical laws; they are at the same time *noumena* and have a relation to the causality of the (g pure) understanding and of pure [understanding] reason. What arises from this as a cause is good. The possibility of acting from reason, i.e. from the motive of the good in itself, is freedom. This possibility is (g to be found) in all actions, because the incentives of sensibility never determine, not even the evil actions, and no action is empirically certain according to laws of the *phaenomena*. The higher choice is therefore at all times free (*a stimulis*), and between its motives and the empirically determined choice there is a connection, which can be represented only according to the analogy of the connection of appearances and which makes up no series. Thus it remains incomprehensible how the very same reason that produces certain actions could have produced others in their place. With regard to these actions as *phaenomena*, freedom is a necessary hypothesis for the human understanding, which does not comprehend the transition from the intellectual to the sensible and the first ground of arising. Among the *noumena*, however, the *effectus* of freedom are not according to what happens in time, but in itself; in the soul it is to be assessed differently, and what was evil there can be good here on the whole.\n===NOTES===\n- Willkühr = choice (not in glossary); Bewegungsgrund = motive; Triebfeder = incentive; Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung not used here.\n- 'Die obere Willkühr' = 'the higher choice'; 'in der Seele' left as it stands, with 'dort/hier' unresolved.\n- 'vor den Menschlichen Verstand' is placed before 'der ... nicht begreift'; the clause 'In Ansehung ... ist ... die Freyheit eine nothwendige Hypothesis' is reordered only slightly for English.\n- 'unbegreiflich' = incomprehensible (begreifen = comprehend); 'beurtheilen' = assess; 'an sich' = in itself.",[]],"reflm:6447":["Theodicy or justification of God on account of the ills is easy, because they can be a partial good; but on account of the evil (the moral), because it absolutely should not be (cannot belong even to the negations |\nas necessary limitations of the creatures), neither an accusation nor a justification can take place, because all evil in the world takes place in it only as *phaenomenon* and, as concerns the actual world as noumenon, we cannot at all see the possibility of the same in accordance with and contrary to the moral laws.\n===NOTES===\n- Ubel = ills; Böse = evil; Schranken = limitations; wirklich = actual; Möglichkeit = possibility.\n- 'sie' (they) = the ills; 'derselben' ('of the same') is ambiguous (the world or the evil); left as 'the same'.\n- 'noumenon' is printed in the singular, unitalicized here for 'phaenomenon' as in the source; both set in italics/Latin form as technical terms.",[]],"reflm:6448":["The transcendental ideas of reason go to the totality of the series of conditions *a parte priori*, consequently to the absolutely unconditioned in the synthesis. The absolutely whole [time] elapsed time (beginning of the world), the absolutely whole division (the simple), the absolutely whole series of determining causes (freedom) and the postulate of the presupposed (g or absolute presupposition.) (Necessity).\nAccording to the schema of sensibility this is impossible (because the synthesis of the same cannot be whole, nor empirically bounded); but intellectually it is possible. These transcendental ideas cannot be used for the explanation of appearances, but have their meaning only in the pure system of reason.\n===NOTES===\n- Allheit/totalität: German 'totalitaet' = totality; Bedingung = condition; unbedingt = unconditioned; Einteilung/Teilung = division; Einfache = simple; Erklärung = explanation; Bedeutung = meaning.\n- 'desselben' in 'die synthesis desselben' has no clear antecedent; left as 'the same'.\n- 'Nothwendigkeit' appears in parentheses after the postulate clause; the sentence is a fragment without a verb, left as is.",[]],"reflm:6449":["M 381':\n*Ens necessarium.*\nHe who does not know how to derive from the concept of a thing its existence (g as necessary) also does not know which determinations a thing must have in order [from it] to be absolutely necessary, if he assumes such an existence.* For for human beings this would have to happen from concepts. Now if existence is not to be derived from concepts, nothing further can be cognized of the same than that its concept is thoroughgoingly determined, without cognizing in what these determinations consist; so he would only be able to say that it as also |\noutside the understanding is represented as posited, for (g only) for that reason a thing is called thoroughgoingly determined; so he has said nothing than breaks off.\n*(s The cosmological proof demands that the thoroughgoing determination of an *ens necessarium* must lie in its concept, which, however, is only that of the highest reality; consequently the properties of the *ens necessarium* can be derived from the mere necessity of existence. Yet it concedes that [from] conversely the existence of the thing can be derived from no concept. It would therefore have to |\nM 381:\n[say] not say: the *ens necessarium* has by virtue of its concept the highest reality, but rather: we cannot determine it from concepts otherwise than through nothing but reality (for as (g necessary) existing it must be represented *a priori* as thoroughgoingly determined). But if the inference from the object itself is to be valid, then the concepts of necessity and of the *realissimum* must also be *conceptus reciproci*; for we have here to do only with concepts, not with intuitions, in which more can be contained than in the concept of them.)",[]],"reflm:6450":["On creation in time or from eternity.\nAn existence upon which the whole duration of a given thing follows (the beginning of the same) is quite well thinkable, and such is the birth of an animal. But an existence upon which all time in general follows, (g as definition of the absolutely first beginning) is a contradiction; for time can only follow insofar as something precedes (which was therefore not in it). [Therefore the beginning belongs to the Ver] But the preceding itself presupposes a time. Therefore not all time can follow upon a given existence; for that upon which a certain whole time (which contains the beginning of the thing) follows is a preceding time.\nThe definition of the absolutely first beginning is the existence before which a time preceded in which as yet no thing at all was.\nIf we assume that a beginning can be thought before which no time at all precedes, then an end will also (g be able to) be thought, upon which no time at all follows. But then one would have to say that a world was and now is no more, which, since it nevertheless assumes a time that follows upon the duration of the world, would be a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:6451":["On moral theology.\nThe final end of all things can be known, because it is moral, namely the highest (g contingent) good. This gives the concept of a highest original good, i.e., of a moral author of the world, and this concept determines the same as the most perfect, whereas the physico-theologian could prove only a being of great perfection. Strictly speaking morals gives objectively no proof, but only subjectively a morally necessary assumption of the conditions under which the object of morals, the highest good, on the side where it is not in our power (namely that of happiness), is alone possible.\nFirst the representation of the world as a system [of the caus] of the *nexus finalis physici* (*causarum finalium physicarum*, among which the human being must also be). Thus an intelligent original being, but not yet God, because for that the concept of the perfection of the world from experience is not sufficient. Now the representation of the world as a *systema causarum finalium moralium* toward the highest good. For the human being, who in the *nexus finalis physico* is a member, but in himself encounters a principle of a higher *nexus finalis*, will [himself] also with regard to this relate his existence to [an Ve] the same intelligent author; but the concept of the same is as of a being as author of the highest good, because this alone is appropriate to the relation to ends of the moral human being. Through this the assumption of a living God as a moral being and hereby also as an all-sufficient being is assumed.\nThis is no objectively sufficient theoretical proof, but a subjectively-practically sufficient moral proof. The assumption of God is not necessary in order to fulfill a duty in individual actions, |\nbut in order to work toward the object of the extended moral disposition, the highest good, without which the moral disposition indeed has incentives of the action (from the form of morality), but no final end (with regard to the matter, i.e., the objects of rational choice). Artistic wisdom we see in corporeal things, moral wisdom we find in ourselves as rule.",[]],"reflm:6452":["The end is (g either) the object of the will or the incentive of the action for the production of this object. The final end is the [selb] immediate end.\nThe highest good is the final end, but not as an incentive found outside God.\nThe honor of God consists in respect for his command. This, however, is the supreme condition of the highest good, consequently the final end.",[]],"reflm:6453":["If the world were considered only as created for the end of human beings, their own happiness, it would be created a proof of God as a being whom one could love; but that is only a relative perfection in relation to the wish of human beings. Only the absolute, if it is encountered in the world, makes it so that we can honor the author, namely in that one cognizes him not merely as benevolent but as wise. Wisdom, however, is the benevolence that subordinates its exercise to the condition of the objects, of being worthy of it. Therefore God created the world for his honor, not in order to be loved.",[]],"reflm:6454":["Moral theology.\nThe moral need to assume a highest moral good is not a (g pathologically) conditioned one, in order [because of] to the good actions that the moral law commands still incentives of self|love\nto have; for it is a moral need to assume a just judge himself, thus not a being of whose goodness we hope, but whose holiness we must fear. Even the thought of this, given the uncertainty of the purity of his actions, is more fearful than [einschm] enticing. — But for our legislating reason, if we do not even represent ourselves as standing under laws, but as projecting the highest good for the world according to moral laws, everyone will want virtue to be happy and vice to be punished. This wish is alone purely moral, not in the least selfish and unavoidable for the rational human being, and that makes it a necessity to assume a living God as moral author and ruler of the world, insofar as we relate our idea of the world not to theory but to our practical determination. Hence the wavering when one weighs this matter theoretically and then in turn looks back to the satisfaction of our practical incentives.\nIt is no passive interest, but an active one in the idea of a merely rational being, who considers himself as morally legislating.\n1. On the end of creation,\n2. the best world,\n3. the end of God with this object.",[]],"reflm:6455":["The *potestas legislatoria divina* rests on benevolence. For only he can obligate whose will agrees with that which is the essential property of the will of others. This is only the desire of a finite being for happiness. Therefore he must be benevolent, but also omnipotent, omniscient etc. etc. Only benevolence is the moral condition, but under the supreme one of his holy will. Benevolence is a sequel of blessedness. Wisdom, because it is merely restrictive [in Anseh] for benevolence, is not rewarding; therefore it is justice.",[]],"reflm:4843":["Wisdom is the relation of his choice to the whole (g unification of his intention with the whole) of all ends; consequently wisdom is a moral perfection, not mere natural perfection.\nWisdom is in itself good. Omniscience not immediately.\nJustice [belohn] is not rewarding, but punishing; thus goodness is limited through wisdom. Goodness in proportion with the morality. Not justice of revenge, although avenging. [*justitia*] *poenae vel vindicativae vel correctivae* (justice of punishment, either vindicative or corrective) (*hae vel animadversiones vel poenae exemplares*: these either reprimands or exemplary punishments).",[]]};
